window.GIQ={"meta":{"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Chapter","lede":"","top":300},"lectures":[{"id":"PHYS-C1","t":"The GI wall and its control"},{"id":"PHYS-C2","t":"GI hormones and other messengers"},{"id":"PHYS-C3","t":"Motility"},{"id":"PHYS-C4","t":"Saliva"},{"id":"PHYS-C5","t":"Gastric secretion"},{"id":"PHYS-C6","t":"Pancreatic and biliary secretion"},{"id":"PHYS-C7","t":"Digestion and absorption of carbohydrates and proteins"},{"id":"PHYS-C8","t":"Digestion and absorption of lipids"},{"id":"PHYS-C9","t":"Water, electrolytes, regional absorption, diarrhea and the liver"}],"groups":[{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":0,"label":"Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-7.jpg","cap":"Wall cross-section: mucosa = surface epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosa.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/D061-30.jpg","cap":"Layer table: mucosa = epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosa; faces lumen.","where":"2023 TA review, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-22-1","s":"Which of the following makes up the mucosal layer of the GI tract?","o":["A. Muscularis mucosa, epithelial cells, lamina propria","B. Muscularis mucosa, circular muscle, longitudinal muscle","C. Muscularis mucosa, submucosa, serosa","D. Muscularis mucosa, epithelial cells, submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis mucosa, epithelial cells, lamina propria","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>mucosa</b> has three parts: <b>epithelial cells</b>, the <b>lamina propria</b> (connective tissue under them) and the <b>muscularis mucosae</b> (a thin smooth-muscle sheet).<br>B. Circular and longitudinal muscle: the muscularis externa, outside the submucosa.<br>C. Submucosa and serosa: separate layers, not part of the mucosa.<br>D. Submucosa: its own layer, just outside the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":22,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":0,"label":"Muscularis mucosae is smooth muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-7.jpg","cap":"Muscularis mucosa drawn as a thin muscle band at the base of the mucosa.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G038-5.jpg","cap":"Micrograph labelling muscularis mucosae between mucosa and submucosa.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-30-1","s":"Which of the following layers consists of smooth muscle cells?","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Muscularis mucosa","C. Submucosal layer","D. Serosa"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis mucosa","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>muscularis mucosae</b> is a thin layer of <b>smooth muscle</b> at the base of the mucosa.<br>A. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue with blood and lymph vessels; no muscle sheet.<br>C. Submucosal layer: collagen, elastin, glands and blood vessels.<br>D. Serosa: a thin connective-tissue sheet on the outside, facing the blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":30,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":0,"label":"Mucosa faces the lumen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-4.jpg","cap":"Cross-section ring: mucosa lines the central lumen; serosa is the outer layer.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/D061-30.jpg","cap":"Layer table: mucosa 'faces lumen', serosa 'faces blood'.","where":"2023 TA review, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-41-1","s":"The mucosal surface of the GI tract faces ______ :","o":["A. The lumen","B. The blood"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The lumen","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>mucosal surface faces the lumen</b>: it is the innermost layer and touches the food. The <b>serosal surface faces the blood</b> side (the outside). Order outward: mucosa, submucosa, circular muscle, longitudinal muscle, serosa.<br>Common mix-up: the serosa, not the mucosa, is the outermost layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 4","L49 slide 10"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":41,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":0,"label":"Wall layers in order, lumen outward","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-3.jpg","cap":"Layer stack: mucosa, submucosa, submucosal plexus, circular muscle, myenteric plexus, longitudinal, serosa.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G038-6.jpg","cap":"Ring diagram labelling every layer and both plexuses, mucosa to serosa.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-41-1","s":"Select the correct order of the GI wall structural organization starting from the lumen and moving towards the blood:","o":["A. Mucosal layer, submucosal layer, circular muscle, longitudinal muscle, serosa","B. Mucosal layer, submucosal layer, longitudinal muscle, circular muscle, serosa","C. Serosa, longitudinal muscle, circular muscle, submucosal layer, mucosal layer","D. Mucosal layer, serosa, longitudinal muscle, circular muscle, submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucosal layer, submucosal layer, circular muscle, longitudinal muscle, serosa","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> From the lumen outward: <b>mucosa → submucosa → circular muscle → longitudinal muscle → serosa</b>. Circular muscle is the inner muscle coat; longitudinal is the outer.<br>B. Swaps the two muscle layers: longitudinal lies outside circular.<br>C. Right layers, but listed from the outside in (blood → lumen).<br>D. Puts the serosa second; the serosa is the outermost layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":3,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":41,"f":"D040"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":36,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"},{"id":"D061-28-1","s":"An ulcer in the stomach erodes through all layers of the GI tract. In which order does it erode?","o":["A. Mucosa → Circular Smooth Muscle → Myenteric Plexus → Longitudinal Smooth Muscle → Serosa","B. Serosa → Submucosal Plexus → Myenteric Plexus → Longitudinal Smooth Muscle → Mucosa","C. Mucosa → Circular Smooth Muscle → Longitudinal Smooth Muscle → Submucosal Plexus → Serosa","D. Serosa → Longitudinal Smooth Muscle → Myenteric Plexus → Circular Smooth Muscle → Mucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucosa → Circular Smooth Muscle → Myenteric Plexus → Longitudinal Smooth Muscle → Serosa","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> An ulcer digs from the lumen outward: <b>mucosa → (submucosa) → circular muscle → myenteric plexus → longitudinal muscle → serosa</b>. The myenteric plexus sits between the two muscle coats. A leaves out the submucosa but is the only correct order.<br>B. Starts at the serosa (wrong direction) and puts the submucosal plexus next to the serosa.<br>C. Puts the submucosal plexus outside the longitudinal muscle; it lies inside the circular muscle.<br>D. The right layers, but listed from the serosa inward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":28,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 4"},{"id":"D035-71-1","s":"5. The GI system has layers of muscle and plexi of nerves. Which of the following is the correct order of these muscles and nerves going from most external/superficial to most internal?","o":["A. Mucosa → myenteric plexus → longitudinal muscle → Aurbach plexus → circular muscle","B. Longitudinal muscle→ Submucosal plexus → circular muscle → myenteric plexus → mucosa","C. Mucosa → Meissner’s plexus → circular muscle → Myenteric plexus → longitudinal muscle","D. Mucosa → Aurbach plexus → longitudinal muscle → Myenteric plexus → circular muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucosa → Meissner’s plexus → circular muscle → Myenteric plexus → longitudinal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The order is <b>mucosa → Meissner (submucosal) plexus → circular muscle → Auerbach (myenteric) plexus → longitudinal muscle</b>. The stem says outside-in, but this list runs from the lumen outward; C is still the only correct order.<br>A. Lists the myenteric plexus twice (Auerbach = myenteric) and swaps the muscle coats.<br>B. Puts the submucosal plexus between the muscle coats; that is the myenteric plexus's place.<br>D. Again lists Auerbach and myenteric as two plexuses and swaps the muscle coats.","ex":"","fl":["Stem wording error: the keyed order (mucosa -> ... -> longitudinal muscle) runs from INTERNAL (lumen) to EXTERNAL, not 'most external/superficial to most internal' as the stem says; C is still the only correct sequence."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)","L49 slide 8 (Georg Meisner (1829-1905))","L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":71,"f":"D035"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":71,"f":"G066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":0,"label":"Submucosal vs myenteric plexus location","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-3.jpg","cap":"Layer stack: submucosal plexus beside circular muscle; myenteric plexus between the muscle coats.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G038-7.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: Meissner's plexus in submucosa; Auerbach's between inner circular and outer longitudinal.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G038-15.jpg","cap":"Micrograph: Auerbach's plexus ganglia between inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-27-1","s":"Where is Meissner’s plexus location?","o":["A. Between the submucosa and the circular muscle","B. Within the circular muscle","C. Between the circular muscle and longitudinal muscle","D. Within the longitudinal muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Between the submucosa and the circular muscle","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Meissner's plexus</b> (the submucosal plexus) lies <b>between the submucosa and the circular muscle</b>. Memory aid: Meissner sits on the mucosa side.<br>B. Within the circular muscle: no plexus lies inside a muscle coat.<br>C. Between circular and longitudinal muscle: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.<br>D. Within the longitudinal muscle: no plexus lies inside a muscle coat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 8 (Georg Meisner (1829-1905))","L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":27,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"},{"id":"D040-43-1","s":"Which plexus lies between the submucosa and the circular muscle layer of the GI tract?","o":["A. Myenteric plexus","B. Celiac plexus","C. Meissner plexus","D. Lumbar plexus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Meissner plexus","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>Meissner (submucosal) plexus</b> lies between the <b>submucosa and the circular muscle</b>.<br>A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus: between the circular and longitudinal muscle.<br>B. Celiac plexus: an extrinsic sympathetic plexus around the celiac trunk, outside the gut wall.<br>D. Lumbar plexus: somatic nerves to the lower limb, not part of the gut wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)","L49 slide 8 (Georg Meisner (1829-1905))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":43,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 6"},{"id":"D015-55-1","s":"THE SUBMUCOSAL PLEXUS IS BETWEEN WHICH TWO LAYERS?","o":["A. MUCOSAL AND LAMINA PROPRIA","B. LAMINA PROPRIA AND MUSCULARIS MUCOSAE","C. SUBMUCOSA AND CIRCULAR MUSCLE","D. CIRCULAR AND LONGITUDINAL MUSCLE"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"SUBMUCOSA AND CIRCULAR MUSCLE","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> lies <b>between the submucosa and the circular muscle</b>.<br>A. Mucosa and lamina propria: the lamina propria is part of the mucosa, so this is not a border between layers.<br>B. Lamina propria and muscularis mucosae: both inside the mucosa; no plexus there.<br>D. Circular and longitudinal muscle: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)","L49 slide 8 (Georg Meisner (1829-1905))"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":55,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 7"},{"id":"D033-3-1","s":"The Myenteric plexus is located between which 2 layers?","o":["A. Submucosa and mucosa","B. Submucosa and circular muscle layer","C. Circular and longitudinal muscle layers","D. Longitudinal muscle layer and serosa"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Circular and longitudinal muscle layers","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> sits <b>between the circular and longitudinal muscle</b> layers.<br>A. Submucosa and mucosa: no named plexus; the muscularis mucosae marks this border.<br>B. Submucosa and circular muscle: the submucosal (Meissner) plexus.<br>D. Longitudinal muscle and serosa: no plexus; the serosa is the outer covering.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":3,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":38,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":38,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":206,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"3"},{"id":"D036-25-1","s":"Where is the myenteric plexus location?","o":["A. Between the submucosa and the circular muscle","B. Within the circular muscle","C. Between the circular muscle and longitudinal muscle","D. Within the longitudinal muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Between the circular muscle and longitudinal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> lies <b>between the circular and longitudinal muscle</b>, where it can drive both muscle coats.<br>A. Between the submucosa and circular muscle: the submucosal (Meissner) plexus.<br>B. Within the circular muscle: no plexus lies inside a muscle coat.<br>D. Within the longitudinal muscle: same error; plexuses lie between layers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L49 slide 2 (49 Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":25,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 8"},{"id":"G067-38-1","s":"Which of the following most accurately describes the location of the plexi","o":["A. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus is between the muscularis mucosa and submucosal plexus","B. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus is between the circular muscle and longitudinal muscle","C. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies between the submucosa and the circular muscle","D. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus is between the submucosa and circular muscle"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies between the submucosa and the circular muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>B and C are both true.</b> The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> lies between the <b>circular and longitudinal</b> muscle. The <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> lies between the <b>submucosa and the circular muscle</b> (lecture layer diagram).<br>A. Nonsense: it names the submucosal plexus as its own border.<br>D. Gives the myenteric plexus the submucosal plexus's position.","ex":"","fl":["B is the unambiguously correct statement (myenteric/Auerbach plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers) and is exactly what the slide's own explanation line says. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies within the submucosa (between muscularis mucosae and circular muscle), so C misplaces it; the key contradicts its own explanation.","Source keyed C and the page accepted only B; both are true: the myenteric plexus lies between circular and longitudinal muscle, and the submucosal plexus between the submucosa and circular muscle (G038:3)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L49 slide 8 (Georg Meisner (1829-1905))","L49 slide 13 (Leopold Auerbach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":38,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 9"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":1,"label":"Enteric nervous system = myenteric + submucosal plexi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-28.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system = myenteric and submucosal plexi.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G038-27.jpg","cap":"Celiac, mesenteric and hypogastric ganglia are sympathetic, outside the bowel.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-36-1","s":"Which of the following is a component of the enteric (intrinsic) nervous system?","o":["A. Submucosal plexus","B. Celiac ganglion","C. Pelvic nerve","D. Hypogastric ganglion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>enteric (intrinsic) nervous system</b> is the <b>myenteric and submucosal plexuses</b> inside the gut wall. These ganglia can run the gut on their own.<br>B. Celiac ganglion: sympathetic, outside the gut.<br>C. Pelvic nerve: an extrinsic parasympathetic nerve.<br>D. Hypogastric ganglion: sympathetic, outside the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":36,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 10"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":1,"label":"Enteric nervous system runs the gut alone","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-28.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic innervation: enteric nervous system, myenteric and submucosal plexi, all interconnected.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/D033-6.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system can direct all gut functions without extrinsic innervation.","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-22-1","s":"What is the function of the enteric plexus?","o":["a. Distribute parasympathetic activation of the GI tract","b. Distribute sympathetic activation of the GI tract","c. Act as the pacemaker for slow waves","d. Autoregulation or intrinsic regulation of the GI tract"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Autoregulation or intrinsic regulation of the GI tract","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> The <b>enteric plexus</b> is the gut's own nervous system: it provides <b>intrinsic regulation</b> and can run the gut by itself.<br>A. Parasympathetic signals reach the plexus, but passing them on is not its main job.<br>B. The same is true for sympathetic signals.<br>C. Slow-wave pacemakers are the interstitial cells of Cajal, not enteric neurons.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":22,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"4"},{"id":"D040-47-1","s":"The intrinsic/enteric nervous system can direct all functions of the GI tract, even in the absence of extrinsic innervation.","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"True","a":"A","why":"<b>True.</b> The <b>enteric nervous system</b> (the myenteric and submucosal plexuses) can direct <b>all gut functions with no extrinsic nerves</b>. Sympathetic and parasympathetic input only adjust it.<br>Common mix-up: extrinsic nerves fine-tune the gut, but the gut does not need them to work.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":7,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":47,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-22-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":1,"label":"Submucosal plexus secretes; myenteric plexus moves","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D033-6.jpg","cap":"Enteric wiring diagram: submucosal plexus → gut secretions; myenteric plexus → movements.","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-49-1","s":"What controls secretions in the GI tract?","o":["A. Myenteric Plexus","B. Submucosal Plexus","C. Circular Muscle","D. Longitudinal Muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal Plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> controls <b>secretion</b> and blood flow; it lies right under the mucosa's glands.<br>A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus: controls motility, lumen size and peristalsis.<br>C. Circular muscle: a muscle coat that narrows the lumen; it does not control secretion.<br>D. Longitudinal muscle: shortens the gut segment; no secretory role.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 3","L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":5,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":49,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"},{"id":"D010-42-1","s":"What controls the fluid levels in the GI tract?","o":["A. Serosa","B. Myenteric Plexus","C. Submucosal Plexus","D. Lamina Propria"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal Plexus","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> controls gland and <b>fluid secretion</b> and absorption, so it sets fluid levels in the lumen.<br>A. Serosa: the outer connective-tissue covering; no control role.<br>B. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus: controls motility, not fluid.<br>D. Lamina propria: connective tissue of the mucosa; it holds vessels but does not control secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 3","L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":42,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":42,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 12"},{"id":"D008-40-1","s":"Which of the following describes the major function of the Myenteric (Auerbach) Plexus?","o":["A. Regulates motility","B. Regulates blood flow","C. Regulates secretions, and absorptions","D. Changes shape and surface area of absorptive portion of the epithelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Regulates motility","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> lies between the two muscle coats and mainly <b>regulates motility</b> (contraction, lumen size, peristalsis).<br>B. Blood flow: a job of the submucosal (Meissner) plexus.<br>C. Secretion and absorption: also the submucosal plexus.<br>D. Folding the epithelial surface: done by the muscularis mucosae, which the submucosal plexus controls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":40,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":40,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":208,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"},{"id":"D060-43-1","s":"What controls the size of the lumen in the GI tract?","o":["A. Serosa","B. Myenteric Plexus","C. Submucosal Plexus","D. Lamina Propria"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric Plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> drives the muscle coats, so it controls <b>motility, lumen size and peristalsis</b>.<br>A. Serosa: the outer covering; no control role.<br>C. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus: controls secretion and blood flow.<br>D. Lamina propria: connective tissue inside the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":43,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":40,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":40,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 13"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":1,"label":"Achalasia: lost myenteric (Auerbach) neurons","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-15.jpg","cap":"Auerbach's (myenteric) plexus ganglia sit between inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/G039-14.jpg","cap":"Achalasia: lower esophageal sphincter too tight, dysphagia; bird's-beak barium X-ray.","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-50-1","s":"58-year-old female patient comes into the office with difficulty swallowing foods and liquids. Testing reveals increased lower esophageal sphincter tone and biopsy shows shows lack of ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscle of the muscularis propria. Achalasia is diagnosed in the patient. What plexus is in question and what is its function?","o":["A. Meissner plexus - primarily controls motility of GI smooth muscle","B. Auerbach plexus - primarily controls secretion and blood flow","C. Auerbach plexus - primarily controls motility of GI smooth muscle","D. Meissner plexus - primarily controls secretion and blood flow"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Auerbach plexus - primarily controls motility of GI smooth muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Neurons between the circular and longitudinal muscle are the <b>Auerbach (myenteric) plexus</b>, which <b>controls motility</b>. Their loss in <b>achalasia</b> keeps the lower esophageal sphincter tight.<br>A. Meissner is the submucosal plexus, and it does not mainly control motility.<br>B. Right plexus, wrong job: secretion and blood flow belong to Meissner.<br>D. Meissner does control secretion and blood flow, but it lies between the submucosa and circular muscle, not where the neurons were lost.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":50,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":47,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":47,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":22,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"},{"id":"D007-51-1","s":"A 47-year old female patient comes into your practice complaining about difficulty swallowing. Testing shows an increase in lower esophageal sphincter tone, while a biopsy shows a lack of ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscles of the muscularis propria. What plexus is damaged in this patient?","o":["A. Myenteric Plexus","B. Submucosal Plexus","C. Longitudinal Muscle","D. Batson Plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric Plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Ganglion cells <b>between the circular and longitudinal muscle</b> are the <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b>. Losing them in <b>achalasia</b> leaves the lower esophageal sphincter unable to relax, so swallowing is hard.<br>B. Submucosal plexus: lies between the submucosa and circular muscle; it controls secretion.<br>C. Longitudinal muscle: a muscle layer, not a plexus.<br>D. Batson plexus: a vertebral venous plexus, unrelated to the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 3","L49 slide 15 (Auerbachs (Myenteric) Plexus)","L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":51,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 14"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":1,"label":"Vagal mechanoreceptors sense stretch","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-23.jpg","cap":"Vagus afferents: mechanoreceptors pick up stretch; chemoreceptors pick up acid.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-67-1","s":"4. Which of the following is the greatest stimulation for the GI system and what kind of receptor is responsible for sensing this input?","o":["A. Stretch; mechanoreceptor","B. Stretch; chemoreceptor","C. Acid / Base changes; chemoreceptor","D. Acidity; thermoreceptor"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stretch; mechanoreceptor","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Stretch (distension)</b> is the main stimulus, and <b>mechanoreceptors</b> sense it; their signals travel in vagal afferent fibers.<br>B. Chemoreceptors sense chemistry (such as acid), not stretch.<br>C. Acid–base change is sensed by chemoreceptors, but it is not the main stimulus.<br>D. Thermoreceptors sense temperature, not acidity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 23 (Vagus Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":67,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":67,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":2,"label":"Parasympathetic constricts and secretes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-18.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic effects: 'Constricts & Secretes'; acetylcholine makes you juicy.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G038-26.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic opposite: slows motility, dilates muscle, decreases secretions.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-32-1","s":"Which of the following occurs in the GI tract with activation of the parasympathetic nervous system?","o":["A. Constricts smooth muscle in the GI tract","B. Slows GI motility","C. Dilates muscle","D. Decreases secretions"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Constricts smooth muscle in the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The parasympathetic system <b>constricts and secretes</b>: through acetylcholine it contracts gut smooth muscle (constriction = motion), opens sphincters and raises secretions.<br>B. Slows motility: a sympathetic effect.<br>C. Dilates muscle: sympathetic ('dilates and dries').<br>D. Decreases secretions: sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 18 (Parasympathetic Effects)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":32,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-4-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 15"},{"id":"D066-4-1","s":"In regards to the GI tract, the parasympathetic nervous system ____ motility, _____ sphincters, and ____ secretions.","o":["a. Does not change, closes, increases","b. Increases, closes, decreases","c. Increases, opens, increases","d. Decreases, opens, decreases"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increases, opens, increases","a":"c","why":"<b>c. Increases, opens, increases.</b> Parasympathetic nerves release ACh (acetylcholine). It contracts the wall muscle, so <b>motility rises</b>. It relaxes the sphincters, so they <b>open</b>. It also <b>raises secretion</b>. Short form: constricts and secretes.<br>a. Does not change, closes, increases: motility rises and the sphincters open.<br>b. Increases, closes, decreases: closed sphincters and less secretion are sympathetic effects.<br>d. Decreases, opens, decreases: slower motility and less secretion are sympathetic effects (norepinephrine dilates and dries).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 21 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 18 (Parasympathetic Effects)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":4,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"},{"id":"G067-40-1","s":"Which of the following neurotransmitters of the enteric nervous system has a major role in increasing intestinal secretion?","o":["A. NO","B. Enkephalins","C. Neuropeptide Y","D. Substance P","E. Ach"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ach","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Acetylcholine (ACh)</b> is the parasympathetic transmitter that <b>raises secretion</b>: 'acetylcholine makes you juicy'.<br>A. NO (nitric oxide): relaxes smooth muscle; no secretory role.<br>B. Enkephalins (opiates): decrease intestinal secretion.<br>C. Neuropeptide Y: decreases intestinal secretion.<br>D. Substance P: contracts smooth muscle and raises salivary, not intestinal, secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 18 (Parasympathetic Effects)","L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":40,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-4-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":2,"label":"Acetylcholine relaxes sphincters, contracts the wall","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-21.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic: increases motility and opens sphincters.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/D060-49.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.1: acetylcholine contracts wall muscle and relaxes sphincters.","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-47-1","s":"Which of the following will contract smooth muscle and relax sphincters?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Epinephrine","C. VIP","D. Neuropeptide Y","E. Gastrin-releasing peptide"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Acetylcholine","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Acetylcholine (ACh)</b> <b>contracts the wall's smooth muscle and relaxes the sphincters</b>: the parasympathetic 'move it along' pattern.<br>B. Epinephrine: sympathetic; relaxes the wall and tightens sphincters (the opposite).<br>C. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): relaxes smooth muscle, sphincters included, but does not contract the wall.<br>D. Neuropeptide Y: relaxes smooth muscle and cuts intestinal secretion.<br>E. Gastrin-releasing peptide: from vagal neurons; it releases gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 18 (Parasympathetic Effects)","L49 slide 21 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":47,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-6-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 17"},{"id":"D019-6-1","s":"Which of the following relaxes gastrointestinal sphincters?","o":["a. Acetylcholine","b. Substance P","c. Enkephalins","d. Norepinephrine","e. Epinephrine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Acetylcholine","a":"a","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Acetylcholine (ACh)</b>, the parasympathetic transmitter, <b>opens (relaxes) sphincters</b> while it contracts the gut wall, so food moves on.<br>B. Substance P: contracts smooth muscle and raises salivary secretion.<br>C. Enkephalins (opiates): contract smooth muscle and cut intestinal secretion.<br>D. Norepinephrine: the sympathetic transmitter; it contracts sphincters.<br>E. Epinephrine: sympathetic; it also tightens sphincters and slows the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 21 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":6,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":2,"label":"Sympathetic dilates and dries, slows motility","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-26.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic: norepinephrine slows motility, constricts sphincters, dilates muscle: 'Dilates and Dries'.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-64-1","s":"3. Your patient travels back in time to visit the dinosaurs. He is walking around admiring the dinosaurs, when one decides to start chasing him. He forgot to use the bathroom before time traveling and doesn’t have time to find a bathroom before running away from the dinosaur. How is his nervous system allow him to avoid needing to have a bowel movement while running away from the scary dinosaur?","o":["A. Sympathetic nervous system contracts the muscles of the GI tract with the neurotransmitter acetylcholine","B. Parasympathetic nervous system relaxes the muscles of the GI tract with the neurotransmitter acetylcholine","C. Sympathetic nervous system relaxes the muscles of the GI tract with the neurotransmitter norepinephrine","D. Sympathetic nervous system contracts the muscles of the GI tract with the neurotransmitter epinephrine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic nervous system relaxes the muscles of the GI tract with the neurotransmitter norepinephrine","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Fight or flight is <b>sympathetic</b>. Its transmitter, <b>norepinephrine</b>, <b>relaxes the gut wall</b> and <b>tightens sphincters</b>, so stool is held.<br>A. The sympathetic system relaxes, not contracts, the wall, and uses norepinephrine, not acetylcholine.<br>B. The parasympathetic system contracts the wall and opens sphincters.<br>D. The sympathetic system relaxes the wall; norepinephrine, not epinephrine, is its nerve transmitter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":64,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":64,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"},{"id":"D066-8-7","s":"Which neurotransmitter of the gut's nerve supply slows GI contractions, contracts the sphincters and constricts blood vessels?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Norepinephrine","C. Substance P","D. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)","E. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Norepinephrine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Norepinephrine.</b> It is the sympathetic transmitter. It relaxes the wall muscle, so contractions slow. It <b>contracts the sphincters</b>, narrows the gut's blood vessels and cuts secretion. Short form: dilates and dries.<br>A. Acetylcholine: the opposite; it speeds contractions and opens sphincters.<br>C. Substance P: released with acetylcholine; contracts wall muscle.<br>D. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): relaxes muscle, opens sphincters and widens blood vessels.<br>E. GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide): makes G cells release gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-64-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 18"},{"id":"D008-53-1","s":"There is a common theme seen in the GI tract when it comes to innervation. Which system decreases contraction of the GI tract and decreased peristalsis?","o":["A. Sympathetic","B. Parasympathetic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>sympathetic</b> system <b>decreases contraction and peristalsis</b>: 'dilates and dries', fight or flight. Its transmitter, norepinephrine, relaxes the wall and tightens the sphincters.<br>Common mix-up: the parasympathetic system does the opposite. It constricts and secretes, raising motility (rest and digest).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)","L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":53,"f":"D008"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-64-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 19"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":2,"label":"Norepinephrine still increases saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-26.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic norepinephrine: slows motility, constricts sphincters, dilates muscle, decreases secretions.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Salivary glands: only gland where parasympathetic and sympathetic both increase secretion.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-10-1","s":"What is the effect of norepinephrine on the GI tract?","o":["A. Contraction of smooth muscle in wall","B. Increased pancreatic secretions","C. Relaxation of sphincters","D. Increased salivary secretions"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased salivary secretions","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Norepinephrine (NE)</b> slows the gut, yet it still <b>increases salivary secretion</b>. Salivary glands are the one place where sympathetic and parasympathetic input both raise secretion.<br>A. NE relaxes, not contracts, the wall muscle.<br>B. NE does not raise pancreatic secretion; sympathetic input dries the pancreas.<br>C. NE contracts sphincters.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)","L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":10,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2023 TA weekly","n":"GI physiology weekly TA presentation","sl":12,"f":"D048"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":3,"label":"Vagus upper tract; pelvic nerve past ascending colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-19.jpg","cap":"Vagus: upper third of esophagus, stomach, small bowel, ascending colon.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G038-20.jpg","cap":"Pelvic nerve: everything past ascending colon: transverse, descending, sigmoid colon.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G038-16.jpg","cap":"Colon diagram: ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid segments labelled.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-2-1","s":"Which of the following is the innervation of the muscle layers of the sigmoid colon?","o":["a. Vagal afferents","b. Vagal efferents","c. Pelvic nerve","d. Lamina propia","e. Cervical roots 3-5"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic nerve","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> The sigmoid colon lies <b>past the ascending colon</b>, so its parasympathetic supply is the <b>pelvic nerve</b>.<br>A. Vagal afferents: sensory fibers from the upper tract.<br>B. Vagal efferents: motor fibers that stop at the ascending colon.<br>D. Lamina propria: connective tissue of the mucosa, not a nerve.<br>E. Cervical roots three to five: form the phrenic nerve to the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":2,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"},{"id":"D007-44-1","s":"Which nerve provides parasympathetic innervation to the sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Vagus (aka Cranial Nerve X)","B. Femoral Nerve","C. Pelvic Nerve","D. Myenteric Plexus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic Nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>pelvic nerve</b> gives parasympathetic supply to <b>everything past the ascending colon</b>: transverse, descending and sigmoid colon.<br>A. Vagus (cranial nerve ten): upper tract only, from esophagus to ascending colon.<br>B. Femoral nerve: a somatic nerve to the thigh.<br>D. Myenteric plexus: the gut's own (intrinsic) network, not an extrinsic nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)","L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":44,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 20"},{"id":"G066-59-1","s":"A 24 y/o patient presents with GERD symptoms. She has been experiencing epigastric pain for the past 5 days but it worsened this morning. You take a detailed history and present the case to your attending. They want to make sure you remember some important facts from anatomy. They ask you, what is the extrinsic parasympathetic innervation of the upper and lower GI tract?","o":["A. Pelvic nerve - Upper GI tract; Vagus Nerve - Lower GI tract","B. Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve - Upper GI tract; Splanchnic Nerve - Lower GI tract","C. Vagus nerve - Upper GI tract; Pelvic Nerve - Lower GI tract","D. CN IX - Upper GI tract; Splanchnic Nerve - Lower GI tract"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve - Upper GI tract; Pelvic Nerve - Lower GI tract","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Vagus</b> (cranial nerve ten) = <b>upper</b> tract: esophagus, stomach, small bowel, ascending colon. <b>Pelvic nerve</b> = <b>lower</b> tract: transverse, descending and sigmoid colon.<br>A. Reversed.<br>B. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies the larynx; splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers.<br>D. Cranial nerve nine (glossopharyngeal) supplies the parotid and pharynx, not the gut tube; splanchnic nerves are sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":59,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":59,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 21"},{"id":"D015-57-1","s":"THE VAGUS NERVE INNERVATES ALL THE FOLLOWING STRUCTURES EXCEPT:","o":["A. TRANSVERSE COLON","B. WALL OF STOMACH","C. SMALL INTESTINE","D. ASCENDING COLON"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"TRANSVERSE COLON","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The vagus reaches only <b>down to the ascending colon</b>; the <b>transverse colon</b> and everything after it (descending and sigmoid colon) get the <b>pelvic nerve</b>.<br>B. Wall of stomach: vagal.<br>C. Small intestine: vagal.<br>D. Ascending colon: the last vagal segment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":57,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 22"},{"id":"D060-53-1","s":"A person comes in with a vagotomy (removal of some or all of the vagus nerve). What portion of the GI tract is spared?","o":["A. Ascending colon","B. Descending colon","C. Stomach","D. Esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The vagus reaches only <b>down to the ascending colon</b>. The <b>descending colon</b> gets parasympathetic supply from the <b>pelvic nerve</b>, so a vagotomy spares it.<br>A. Ascending colon: the last vagal segment, so it loses input.<br>C. Stomach: a classic vagal target.<br>D. Esophagus: vagal territory.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":53,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":44,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":44,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":3,"label":"Vagus synapses in the gut; sympathetic outside","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic synapses directly in the gut: vagus onto the myenteric plexus.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G038-27.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic synapses outside the bowel: celiac, mesenteric and hypogastric ganglia.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/D033-5.jpg","cap":"Extrinsic wiring: vagal synapses inside the plexuses; sympathetic ganglia outside the gut.","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-7-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the intrinsic parasympathetic system in GI tract?","o":["A. Postganglionic nerve fibers release norepinephrine","B. Long preganglionic fibers that synapse within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","C. Short preganglionic fibers that synapse outside the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","D. Preganglionic fibers are either cholinergic or peptidergic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Long preganglionic fibers that synapse within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Parasympathetic fibers have <b>long preganglionic fibers</b> that run all the way to the gut and <b>synapse inside it</b>, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.<br>A. Releasing norepinephrine: sympathetic postganglionic fibers.<br>C. Short preganglionic fibers that synapse outside the gut: the sympathetic pattern.<br>D. The postganglionic parasympathetic neurons are cholinergic or peptidergic; preganglionic fibers are all cholinergic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 24 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 27 (Synapses OUTSIDE the bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":7,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2023 TA weekly","n":"GI physiology weekly TA presentation","sl":10,"f":"D048"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"},{"id":"D036-34-1","s":"Where does the vagus nerve synapse?","o":["A. Meissner’s plexus","B. Smooth muscle","C. Outside of the GI tract","D. Myenteric plexus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric plexus","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Parasympathetic fibers <b>synapse directly in the gut wall</b>: the <b>vagus synapses onto the myenteric plexus</b>.<br>A. Meissner's (submucosal) plexus: the lecture's target is the myenteric plexus, which then relays to the submucosal plexus.<br>B. Smooth muscle: the vagus acts through enteric neurons, not straight onto muscle.<br>C. Outside the gut: that is the sympathetic pattern (celiac, mesenteric, hypogastric ganglia).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 24 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 27 (Synapses OUTSIDE the bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":34,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D033-7-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 23"},{"id":"D061-31-1","s":"2. The vagus nerve innervating the stomach synapses where in the GI tract and is part of which nervous system?","o":["A. Circular muscle, parasympathetic nervous system","B. Myenteric plexus, sympathetic nervous system","C. Submucosal plexus, sympathetic nervous system","D. Myenteric plexus, parasympathetic nervous system","E. Submucosal plexus, parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric plexus, parasympathetic nervous system","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The vagus is <b>parasympathetic</b>, and it <b>synapses directly onto the myenteric plexus</b> inside the gut wall.<br>A. Circular muscle: the vagus acts through enteric neurons, not straight onto muscle.<br>B. Right site, but the vagus is parasympathetic, not sympathetic.<br>C. Submucosal plexus and sympathetic: both wrong.<br>E. Parasympathetic is right, but the lecture's synapse site is the myenteric plexus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 24 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":31,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D033-7-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 24"},{"id":"G067-42-1","s":"Which of the following is true about GI tract autonomic nervous system?","o":["A. The pelvic nerve innervates the upper GI tract and innervates the wall of the stomach","B. Vagus nerve innervates the lower GI tract and innervates the transverse colon","C. The enteric nervous system needs extrinsic innervation to direct all functions of the GI tract","D. Vagus nerve synapses at the myenteric plexus","E. Sympathetic nervous system increases GI motility by relaxing sphincters"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve synapses at the myenteric plexus","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The <b>vagus synapses directly onto the myenteric plexus</b> in the gut wall.<br>A. Pelvic nerve: lower tract (past the ascending colon), not the stomach.<br>B. Vagus: upper tract; the transverse colon gets the pelvic nerve.<br>C. The enteric nervous system can run all gut functions with no extrinsic nerves.<br>E. The sympathetic system slows motility and tightens sphincters.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 24 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 20 (Pelvic Nerve)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":42,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D033-7-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 25"},{"id":"D019-4-1","s":"Which of the following synapse outside the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["a. Myenteric plexus","b. Vagus nerve","c. Pelvic nerve","d. Sympathetic nerves","e. Submucosal plexus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic nerves","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Sympathetic</b> preganglionic fibers <b>synapse outside the bowel</b>, in the celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric ganglia. Postganglionic fibers then enter the gut.<br>A. Myenteric plexus: inside the wall.<br>B. Vagus nerve: parasympathetic; synapses directly in the gut, on the myenteric plexus.<br>C. Pelvic nerve: parasympathetic; also synapses inside the gut wall.<br>E. Submucosal plexus: inside the wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 27 (Synapses OUTSIDE the bowel)","L49 slide 24 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":4,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":3,"label":"Vagus: 75% afferent, 25% efferent","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-23.jpg","cap":"Vagus nerve: 75% afferent (sensory), 25% efferent (motor, secretion, hormones).","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-46-1","s":"Which of the following is a true statement?","o":["A. There are 4 ganglia that provide GI parasympathetic innervation","B. The vagus nerve is 25% efferent and 75% afferent innervation","C. Postganglionic sympathetic neurons are either cholinergic or peptidergic","D. The GI tract can’t function without extrinsic innervation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The vagus nerve is 25% efferent and 75% afferent innervation","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>vagus</b> is mostly sensory: <b>seventy-five percent afferent, twenty-five percent efferent</b>.<br>A. Four ganglia (celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric, hypogastric) are the sympathetic supply.<br>C. Cholinergic or peptidergic describes parasympathetic, not sympathetic, postganglionic neurons.<br>D. The enteric nervous system can run the gut with no extrinsic nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 23 (Vagus Nerve)","L49 slide 27 (Synapses OUTSIDE the bowel)","L49 slide 28 (Intrinsic Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":9,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":46,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"},{"id":"D010-50-1","s":"Which is a true statement?","o":["A. The primary effect of the parasympathetics is to dilate and dry.","B. The primary effect of the sympathetics is to constrict and secrete.","C. The vagus nerve has 75 percent afferent and 25 percent efferent innervation.","D. The primary neurotransmitter associated with the sympathetics is Acetylcholine."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The vagus nerve has 75 percent afferent and 25 percent efferent innervation.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>vagus nerve</b> is about <b>seventy-five percent afferent (sensory)</b> and <b>twenty-five percent efferent (motor)</b>.<br>A. Reversed: the parasympathetic system 'constricts and secretes'.<br>B. Reversed: the sympathetic system 'dilates and dries'.<br>D. Sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine; acetylcholine is the parasympathetic transmitter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 23 (Vagus Nerve)","L49 slide 18 (Parasympathetic Effects)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":50,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":50,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":25,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-46-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"Parasympathetic: cholinergic and peptidergic neurons","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-22.jpg","cap":"Not only acetylcholine: peptidergic VIP, gastrin-releasing peptide, neuropeptide Y, substance P.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-61-1","s":"2. Parasympathetic nerves have multiple neurotransmitters involved in signalling. Which is the correct pair of nerve type and neurotransmitter involved in the extrinsic parasympathetic nervous system of the GI tract?","o":["A. Cholinergic - Substance P; Peptidergic - Ach","B. Cholinergic - VIP, Peptidergic - Substance P","C. Cholinergic - Ach; Peptidergic - Somatostatin","D. Cholinergic - Ach; Peptidergic - Substance P"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholinergic - Ach; Peptidergic - Substance P","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Parasympathetic neurons are <b>cholinergic</b> (release <b>acetylcholine, ACh</b>) or <b>peptidergic</b> (release peptides such as <b>substance P</b>, vasoactive intestinal peptide, gastrin-releasing peptide and neuropeptide Y).<br>A. Reversed: ACh is the cholinergic transmitter.<br>B. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) is a peptide, so it is peptidergic, not cholinergic.<br>C. Somatostatin: a paracrine from D cells, not one of the lecture's parasympathetic peptides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":61,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":61,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"Substance P: released with ACh, contracts muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D060-49.jpg","cap":"Substance P: co-secreted with acetylcholine; contracts smooth muscle, raises salivary secretion","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G038-22.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic nerves also use peptides: VIP, GRP, neuropeptide Y and substance P","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D066-8-5","s":"Which enteric neuropeptide contracts GI smooth muscle, increases secretion and is often co-released with acetylcholine?","o":["A. Neuropeptide Y","B. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)","C. Enkephalins","D. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)","E. Substance P"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Substance P","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Substance P.</b> Enteric neurons release it together with ACh (acetylcholine). Like ACh, it <b>contracts</b> smooth muscle and raises secretion (salivary secretion).<br>A. Neuropeptide Y: travels with norepinephrine; relaxes muscle and lowers intestinal secretion.<br>B. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): raises secretion but relaxes muscle.<br>C. Enkephalins: contract muscle but lower intestinal secretion.<br>D. GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide): makes G cells release gastrin; it does not contract the wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"Enkephalins and neuropeptide Y cut intestinal secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D060-49.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.1: enkephalins and neuropeptide Y decrease intestinal secretion.","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G038-26.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic norepinephrine: decreases secretions, 'dilates and dries'.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-34-1","s":"3. All of the following work to increase secretions except:","o":["A. ACh","B. NE","C. VIP","D. Substance P","E. Neuropeptide Y"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Neuropeptide Y","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Neuropeptide Y</b> relaxes smooth muscle and <b>decreases intestinal secretion</b>; it never raises secretion (Costanzo Table 8.1).<br>A. ACh (acetylcholine): raises salivary, gastric and pancreatic secretion.<br>B. NE (norepinephrine): cuts gut secretion but raises salivary secretion.<br>C. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): raises intestinal and pancreatic secretion.<br>D. Substance P: raises salivary secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":34,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"},{"id":"D066-8-2","s":"Which enteric neuropeptide relaxes GI smooth muscle, decreases intestinal secretion and is often co-released with norepinephrine?","o":["A. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)","B. Substance P","C. Enkephalins","D. Neuropeptide Y","E. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Neuropeptide Y","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Neuropeptide Y.</b> It <b>relaxes</b> gut smooth muscle and <b>lowers intestinal secretion</b>. Sympathetic nerve endings store it with norepinephrine and release the two together.<br>A. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): also relaxes muscle, but it raises intestinal and pancreatic secretion.<br>B. Substance P: released with acetylcholine; contracts muscle.<br>C. Enkephalins: lower intestinal secretion too, but they contract muscle.<br>E. GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide): vagal fibers release it onto G cells to release gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 28"},{"id":"D040-49-1","s":"Enkephalins (opiates) are secreted by neurons of the enteric NS. What effect do they have on the GI tract?","o":["A. Relaxation of smooth muscle and increase in intestinal secretion","B. Contraction of smooth muscle and increase in intestinal secretion","C. Relaxation of smooth muscle and increase in pancreatic secretion","D. Contraction of smooth muscle and decrease in intestinal secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contraction of smooth muscle and decrease in intestinal secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Enkephalins (opiates)</b> <b>contract</b> smooth muscle (circular muscle, without coordinated peristalsis) and <b>decrease intestinal secretion</b>. That is why opioids constipate and why opioid drugs treat diarrhea. Not on the 2026 slides; source is Costanzo Table 8.1.<br>A. Relaxation plus more secretion: that is the VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) pattern.<br>B. Opiates cut intestinal secretion; they do not raise it.<br>C. Relaxation and more pancreatic secretion: also VIP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":12,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":49,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"},{"id":"D066-8-3","s":"Which enteric neuromodulator acts on opioid receptors and decreases intestinal secretion?","o":["A. Neuropeptide Y","B. Substance P","C. Enkephalins","D. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)","E. Norepinephrine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Enkephalins","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Enkephalins.</b> They are the gut's own opiates. They bind <b>opioid receptors</b>, lower intestinal secretion and contract smooth muscle in a way that slows transit. That is why opioid drugs constipate and treat diarrhea.<br>A. Neuropeptide Y: also lowers intestinal secretion, but through its own receptors, and it relaxes muscle.<br>B. Substance P: contracts muscle and raises secretion.<br>D. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): raises intestinal secretion.<br>E. Norepinephrine: acts on adrenergic (alpha and beta) receptors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 29"},{"id":"D015-68-1","s":"WHICH NEUROTRANSMITTER DECREASES INTESTINAL SECRETION?","o":["A. ACETYLCHOLINE (ACH)","B. NOREPINEPHRINE (NE)","C. VASOACTIVE INTESTINAL PEPTIDE (VIP)","D. ENKEPHALINS (OPIATES)"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"ENKEPHALINS (OPIATES)","a":"D","why":"<b>B and D.</b> <b>Enkephalins (opiates)</b> <b>decrease intestinal secretion</b> (Costanzo Table 8.1; one reason opioids constipate). <b>Norepinephrine (NE)</b>, the sympathetic transmitter, also <b>decreases secretions</b> ('dilates and dries').<br>A. Acetylcholine (ACh): raises secretion ('makes you juicy').<br>C. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): raises intestinal and pancreatic secretion.","ex":"","fl":["Ambiguous vs 2026 deck (key follows Costanzo Table 8.1, where enkephalins and NPY are the ones listed as decreasing intestinal secretion): G038:26 says sympathetic/norepinephrine 'Decreases secretions', so NE is also defensible from the 2026 slides."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":68,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"VIP relaxes gut smooth muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-25.jpg","cap":"VIP relaxes sphincter smooth muscle.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D060-49.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.1: VIP and nitric oxide relax smooth muscle; substance P contracts.","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-8-32","s":"Which of the following is the inhibitory neurotransmitter responsible for relaxation of gastrointestinal smooth muscle?","o":["a. Dopamine","b. Vasoactive intestinal peptide","c. Somatostatin","d. Substance P"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> is the inhibitory enteric transmitter that <b>relaxes gut smooth muscle</b>, sphincters included (with nitric oxide).<br>A. Dopamine: not on the lecture's or Costanzo's list of enteric transmitters.<br>C. Somatostatin: a paracrine that inhibits hormone and acid release.<br>D. Substance P: contracts smooth muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 25 (VIP)","L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"21"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"VIP: watery intestinal and pancreatic secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G038-25.jpg","cap":"VIP: relaxes sphincters, raises pepsinogen, water and electrolytes from pancreas and intestines.","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D060-49.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.1: VIP relaxes smooth muscle, raises intestinal and pancreatic secretion.","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-45-1","s":"Which of the following neurotransmitters increases intestinal AND pancreatic secretions?","o":["A. Nitric Oxide","B. Gastrin-Releasing Peptide (GRP)","C. Neuropeptide Y","D. Substance P","E. Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> <b>raises water and electrolyte release from the pancreas and intestines</b>. It also relaxes sphincters and raises pepsinogen.<br>A. Nitric oxide: relaxes smooth muscle only.<br>B. Gastrin-releasing peptide: releases gastrin from G cells.<br>C. Neuropeptide Y: decreases intestinal secretion.<br>D. Substance P: contracts smooth muscle; raises salivary secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":45,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"},{"id":"D010-53-1","s":"40 year old man presents to his primary care physician with complaints of excessive watery diarrhea and facial flushing. On further testing, the patient is found to have hypokalemia and a higher than normal pH within the stomach. A tumor of the non-pancreatic beta cells is suspected. What is the hormone that is being secreted in excess in this patient?","o":["A. VIP","B. CCK","C. GIP","D. Gastrin","E. Insulin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Watery diarrhea, low potassium and low stomach acid (high gastric pH) = <b>VIPoma</b>, a non-beta islet-cell tumor making <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b>. VIP pours water and electrolytes into the gut, inhibits acid and flushes the skin.<br>B. CCK (cholecystokinin): gallbladder contraction and enzyme secretion.<br>C. GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide): raises insulin.<br>D. Gastrin: raises acid, so gastric pH would fall.<br>E. Insulin: from beta cells; causes low glucose, not diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":25,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":53,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":53,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":28,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"Paracrines act locally","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-7.jpg","cap":"Diagram: hormone via circulation, paracrine by diffusion, neurocrine by action potential","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G040-5.jpg","cap":"Paracrines: from GI endocrine cells, act locally, diffuse short distances","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-24-1","s":"Which best describes Paracrine regulation?","o":["a. Regulators secreted from endocrine cells that act locally","b. Regulators secreted from endocrine cells that act on distal tissues","c. Regulators secreted from neurons that act locally","d. Neuromodulators"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Regulators secreted from endocrine cells that act locally","a":"a","why":"<b>A.</b> Paracrines come from <b>endocrine cells</b> and <b>act locally</b>, diffusing a short distance (somatostatin, histamine).<br>B. Endocrine cells acting on distant tissue through the blood describes a hormone (gastrin, cholecystokinin, secretin, glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide).<br>C. Signals from neurons describe neurocrines (acetylcholine, vasoactive intestinal peptide, gastrin-releasing peptide).<br>D. Neuromodulators are nerve signals, not paracrines.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":24,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"24"},{"id":"G066-75-1","s":"What type of gastrointestinal peptide am I? I act locally within the same tissue that secretes me. My site of action is a short distance from my site of action.","o":["A. Hormone","B. Paracrine","C. Neurocrine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Paracrine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Paracrine.</b> A paracrine <b>acts locally</b> in the tissue that releases it, diffusing a short distance (somatostatin, histamine).<br>A. Hormone: enters the blood and travels to a distant target (gastrin, cholecystokinin, secretin, glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide).<br>C. Neurocrine: released from a neuron by an action potential (acetylcholine, vasoactive intestinal peptide, gastrin-releasing peptide).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":75,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":75,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 31"},{"id":"D040-78-1","s":"Match! / Endocrine / Paracrine / Neurocrine / Act locally, diffuse short distances / Synthesized in neurons of the GI tract, released by an action potential / Pass through portal blood and liver, and back to target","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Endocrine (gastrin, CCK, secretin, GIP) = pass through portal blood and liver, and back to target; Paracrine (somatostatin, histamine) = act locally, diffuse short distances; Neurocrine (ACh, norepinephrine, VIP, GRP) = synthesized in neurons of the GI tract, released by an action potential","a":null,"why":"<b>Hormones</b> (gastrin, cholecystokinin, secretin, glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) enter the <b>portal blood</b>, pass the liver and reach targets through the circulation. <b>Paracrines</b> (somatostatin, histamine) <b>act locally</b>, diffusing a short distance. <b>Neurocrines</b> (acetylcholine, norepinephrine, vasoactive intestinal peptide, gastrin-releasing peptide) are made in gut neurons and <b>released by an action potential</b>.<br>Common mix-up: hormones and paracrines both come from endocrine cells; only the route to the target differs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L51A slide 6 (GI Neurocrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":15,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":78,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C1 extra 32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C1","u":4,"label":"Saliva, pancreas and liver secretions aid digestion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-3.jpg","cap":"Digestive enzymes by source: saliva amylase and lipase; pancreas amylase, lipase, trypsin.","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G044-25.jpg","cap":"Biliary secretion: bile from the liver turns fat into microdroplets.","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"D048-8-1","s":"Electrolytes, enzymes, and mucus secretions from which organs aid in digestion? (Multiple answers)","o":["A. Liver","B. Kidney","C. Pancreas","D. Esophagus","E. Salivary glands"],"g":["A","C","E"],"k":["A","C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver; Pancreas; Salivary glands","a":"A,C,E","why":"<b>A, C and E.</b> The <b>salivary glands</b> (amylase, lipase, mucus), <b>pancreas</b> (enzymes and bicarbonate) and <b>liver</b> (bile salts for fat) add secretions that help digestion.<br>B. Kidney: not part of the digestive tract; it makes urine.<br>D. Esophagus: a transport tube; its mucus only lubricates and adds no digestive enzymes or electrolytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA weekly","n":"GI physiology weekly TA presentation","sl":8,"f":"D048"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C1 extra 33"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":5,"label":"Hormone-cell pairs: G, I, S, K, D, ECL","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP is produced by K cells of the duodenum and jejunum","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: gastrin from G cells, CCK from I cells, secretin from S cells","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Gland table: ECL cells make histamine, D cells somatostatin, G cells gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-65-1","s":"_______ cells secrete GIP and ______ cells secrete CCK","o":["A. S; I","B. K; I","C. S; K","D. K; G","E. I; K"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"K; I","a":"B","why":"<b>B. K; I.</b> <b>K cells</b> of the duodenum and jejunum make glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP); <b>I cells</b> of the duodenum and jejunum make cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>A. S cells make secretin, not GIP.<br>C. S cells make secretin and K cells make GIP, so both blanks are wrong.<br>D. G cells make gastrin, not CCK.<br>E. Reversed: I cells make CCK and K cells make GIP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":27,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":65,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"},{"id":"F1212-81-1","s":"Which type of cells secrete GIP?","o":["A. K cells","B. S cells","C. I cells","D. D cells","E. G cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"K cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A. K cells</b> of the duodenum and jejunum secrete glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP).<br>B. S cells: secretin.<br>C. I cells: cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>D. D cells: somatostatin.<br>E. G cells: gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":81,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-65-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 1"},{"id":"D036-61-1","s":"What type of cells secrete CCK?","o":["A. S cells","B. K cells","C. I cells","D. D cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"I cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. I cells</b> of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin (CCK) when fatty acids, monoglycerides, small peptides and amino acids arrive. CCK then contracts the gallbladder and releases pancreatic enzymes.<br>A. S cells: secretin.<br>B. K cells: glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP).<br>D. D cells: somatostatin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":61,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-65-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 2"},{"id":"D040-80-1","s":"Match again! / Gastrin / Cholecystokinin / Secretin / GIP / Somatostatin / Histamine / K cells / I cells / D cells / ECL cells / S cells / G cells","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gastrin = G cells; Cholecystokinin = I cells; Secretin = S cells; GIP = K cells; Somatostatin = D cells; Histamine = ECL cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastrin → G cells</b> (antrum); <b>cholecystokinin (CCK) → I cells</b> (duodenum, jejunum); <b>secretin → S cells</b> (duodenum); <b>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) → K cells</b> (duodenum, jejunum); <b>somatostatin → D cells</b>; <b>histamine → enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells</b> (stomach body).<br>Common mix-up: K cells make GIP and I cells make CCK; S cells make secretin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":17,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":80,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-65-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":5,"label":"Gastrin is made in antrum G cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-5.jpg","cap":"Antrum G cells make gastrin; body has parietal and chief cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/D060-83.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: antral G cells send gastrin into the circulation","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 83"}],"q":[{"id":"D037-39-1","s":"Biopsies are taken from the antral and duodenal mucosa of a 65 year old women. Which of the following hormones can be found in the tissue homogenates from both locations?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","B. Glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide (GLIP)","C. Gastrin","D. Motilin","E. Secretin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastrin.</b> Gastrin is made by <b>G cells of the gastric antrum</b>, and a few G cells also sit in the duodenum, so it is the only choice found in both biopsies.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): made by I cells of the duodenum and jejunum, not the stomach.<br>B. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): made by K cells of the duodenum and jejunum.<br>D. Motilin: made in the upper duodenum, not the antrum.<br>E. Secretin: made by S cells of the duodenum, not the antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA weekly","n":"Physio GI weeks 1 & 2","sl":39,"f":"D037"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":5,"label":"Which gut peptides are hormones","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-4.jpg","cap":"The four GI hormones: gastrin, CCK, secretin, GIP; via portal blood","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G040-7.jpg","cap":"Diagram: hormones travel portal then systemic blood; paracrines diffuse locally","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/D061-39.jpg","cap":"Hormone = into blood; paracrine = local (somatostatin, histamine); neurocrine list","where":"2023 TA review, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-56-1","s":"Which of the following is a hormone of the GI tract?","o":["A. Norepinephrine","B. Somatostatin","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastrin</b> is one of the four gut hormones: <b>gastrin, cholecystokinin (CCK), secretin and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>. Hormones travel in the blood.<br>A. Norepinephrine: a neurocrine, released from nerve endings by an action potential.<br>B. Somatostatin: a paracrine from D cells that acts locally to inhibit.<br>C. Histamine: a paracrine from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells; it is not even a peptide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L51A slide 6 (GI Neurocrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":56,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-83-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 4"},{"id":"F1212-83-1","s":"All of the following is a GI Peptide Hormone EXCEPT?","o":["A. Secretin","B. Gastrin","C. CCK","D. Somatostatin","E. Gastric inhibitory peptide/ Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Somatostatin</b> is a <b>paracrine</b>, not a hormone: D cells release it and it acts locally to inhibit. The four gut hormones are <b>gastrin, cholecystokinin (CCK), secretin and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>; they travel in the blood.<br>A. Secretin: a hormone from S cells.<br>B. Gastrin: a hormone from G cells.<br>C. CCK: a hormone from I cells.<br>E. GIP: a hormone from K cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":83,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"},{"id":"D061-37-1","s":"4. ________ is an example of a _________ because it is released from endocrine cells of the GI tract into systemic circulation.","o":["A. CCK, paracrine","B. Somatostatin, paracrine","C. ACh, hormone","D. Secretin, hormone","E. Gastrin, paracrine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin, hormone","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Secretin, hormone.</b> A peptide released from gut endocrine cells <b>into the circulation</b> is a <b>hormone</b>; secretin, from S cells, is one.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a hormone, not a paracrine.<br>B. Somatostatin is a paracrine, but paracrines act locally and do not travel in the blood, so it does not fit the sentence.<br>C. Acetylcholine (ACh) is a neurocrine released from nerves.<br>E. Gastrin is a hormone, not a paracrine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":37,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-83-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 5"},{"id":"D060-81-1","s":"Which statement about Gastrin is wrong?","o":["A. Gastrin leads to increased acid secretion","B. Gastrin leads to gastric mucosa hypertrophy","C. Gastrin is secreted by G cells and is a paracrine peptide","D. Zollinger-Ellinson Syndrome is caused by too much Gastrin production"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin is secreted by G cells and is a paracrine peptide","a":"C","why":"<b>C is the false statement.</b> Gastrin comes from G cells, but it is a <b>hormone, not a paracrine</b>: it enters the portal blood, passes the liver and reaches the parietal cell through the circulation.<br>A. True: gastrin raises acid (hydrogen ion) secretion from parietal cells.<br>B. True: gastrin makes the gastric lining grow (trophic effect).<br>D. True: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor (gastrinoma).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":81,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":66,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-83-1","kind":"harder","n":"C2 extra 6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":5,"label":"CCK-A receptor is CCK-selective","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell diagram: gastrin acts through the CCK-B receptor","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-86-1","s":"Which CCK receptors are specific ONLY to CCK?","o":["A. CCKA","B. CCKB"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"CCKA","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Cholecystokinin A (CCK-A) receptors are <b>selective for cholecystokinin</b>; pancreatic acinar cells use them.<br>B. Cholecystokinin B (CCK-B) receptors bind <b>cholecystokinin and gastrin equally</b>; gastrin acts on parietal cells through CCK-B, because the two hormones share the same C-terminal end.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":86,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":5,"label":"Minimum active fragments: gastrin 4, CCK 7","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-16.jpg","cap":"Gastrin summary: the C-terminal tetrapeptide is needed for activity","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/D012-17.jpg","cap":"CCK summary: the C-terminal heptapeptide is needed for activity","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-83-1","s":"What is the minimum fragment needed for biological activity of gastrin?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"the C-terminal tetrapeptide","a":null,"why":"<b>The C-terminal tetrapeptide</b>, the last four amino acids, is the smallest piece of gastrin with biologic activity, though it has only about one-sixth of whole gastrin's activity. Cholecystokinin (CCK) shares these end amino acids, which is why CCK has some gastrin activity.<br>Common mix-up: CCK's minimum active fragment is the C-terminal heptapeptide (seven amino acids).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":83,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D067-22-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 8"},{"id":"D067-22-1","s":"What is the minimum fragment of gastrin that is needed for its biological activity?","o":["A. The N-terminal tetrapeptide","B. The C-terminal tetrapeptide","C. The C-terminal heptapeptide","D. The whole 17-amino-acid molecule"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The C-terminal tetrapeptide","a":"B","why":"<b>B. The C-terminal tetrapeptide.</b> The <b>last four amino acids</b> of gastrin are the smallest piece that still works, with about one-sixth of the whole hormone's activity.<br>A. N-terminal tetrapeptide: the active end is the C-terminus, not the N-terminus.<br>C. C-terminal heptapeptide (seven amino acids): the minimum fragment for CCK (cholecystokinin). This is the classic mix-up.<br>D. The whole 17-amino-acid molecule is fully active, but it is not the minimum. Secretin is the hormone that needs its whole chain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":22,"f":"D067"}],"tier":1,"n":"28"},{"id":"D040-87-1","s":"What is the minimum fragment needed for the biological activity of CCK?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"C-terminal heptapeptide (don’t confuse with gastrin!!!)","a":null,"why":"<b>The C-terminal heptapeptide</b>, the last seven amino acids, is the smallest fragment of cholecystokinin (CCK) with biologic activity.<br>Common mix-up: gastrin's minimum active fragment is the C-terminal tetrapeptide (four amino acids); secretin has no active fragment, because all twenty-seven amino acids are needed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":87,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D067-22-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 9"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":6,"label":"Gastrin's main stimulus: distention","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Gastrin stimuli: stomach distention, protein parts, vagal ACh and GRP","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Gland diagram: stretch, amino acids and vagus make G cells release gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/D012-16.jpg","cap":"Gastrin summary: stretch is the greatest stimulus; low pH, somatostatin inhibit","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-20-1","s":"Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?","o":["a. Fatty Acids","b. Bicarbonate","c. Distention","d. Somatostatin","e. Secretin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Distention","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Distention</b> of the stomach is the <b>major stimulus</b> for gastrin release from G cells.<br>A. Fatty acids: release cholecystokinin (CCK) and secretin, not gastrin.<br>B. Bicarbonate: does not stimulate gastrin.<br>D. Somatostatin: inhibits gastrin release.<br>E. Secretin: inhibits gastrin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 34 (Q9. Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?)","L51B slide 35 (Q9. Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":20,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"29"},{"id":"D036-103-1","s":"Which of the following stimulates gastrin release?","o":["A. Low pH","B. Distention of antrum","C. Secretin","D. CCK","E. Somatostatin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Distention of antrum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Distention of the antrum</b> triggers local and vagal reflexes that release gastrin from G cells.<br>A. Low pH inhibits gastrin; this is gastrin's negative feedback.<br>C. Secretin inhibits gastrin release and gastrin's effect on parietal cells.<br>D. Cholecystokinin (CCK) does not stimulate G cells; it acts on the gallbladder and pancreas.<br>E. Somatostatin from D cells inhibits gastrin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":103,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 10"},{"id":"D060-65-1","s":"You just ate a burrito bowl at chipotle, which of the following is a major stimulant to the release of gastrin?","o":["A. Fatty acids","B. Distention","C. GLP-1","D. Histamine","E. Bicarb"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Distention","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Distention.</b> The meal stretches the stomach, which is the <b>major stimulus</b> for gastrin release from G cells.<br>A. Fatty acids: release cholecystokinin (CCK) and secretin.<br>C. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1): an incretin; it does not release gastrin.<br>D. Histamine: acts on parietal cells; gastrin releases histamine, not the reverse.<br>E. Bicarbonate: does not stimulate gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 35 (Q9. Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":65,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 11"},{"id":"D036-59-1","s":"Identify which is true about gastrin","o":["A. The release of gastrin is stimulated by distention of the stomach","B. Gastrin release inhibits H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells","C. Gastrin is secreted by G cells of the duodenum","D. Gastrin is inhibited by alkaline pH"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The release of gastrin is stimulated by distention of the stomach","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Distention (stretch) of the stomach</b> releases gastrin. Small peptides, amino acids and vagal gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) do too.<br>B. Gastrin increases hydrogen ion (acid) secretion by parietal cells; it does not inhibit it.<br>C. G cells sit mainly in the <b>gastric antrum</b>; the antrum, not the duodenum, is the main source.<br>D. Acid (low pH) inhibits gastrin; alkaline pH removes that brake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":59,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 12"},{"id":"D061-40-1","s":"5. Gastrin secretion is stimulated by all of the following except:","o":["A. Fatty acids","B. Phe","C. Distention of the stomach","D. Vagal stimulation","E. GRP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fatty acids","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Fatty acids</b> do not stimulate gastrin; they release cholecystokinin (CCK) and secretin in the duodenum.<br>B. Phenylalanine: a true stimulus; protein products, especially phenylalanine and tryptophan, are the most potent chemical stimuli.<br>C. Distention of the stomach: true; it is the major stimulus.<br>D. Vagal stimulation: true; the vagus drives G cells.<br>E. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP): true; it is the vagal transmitter on G cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":40,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":6,"label":"Little gastrin (G17) is the meal form","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"D015-73-1","s":"THE FORM OF GASTRIN SECRETED IN RESPONSE TO A MEAL HAS HOW MANY AMINO ACIDS IN ITS STRAIGHT CHAIN?","o":["A. 17","B. 27","C. 33","D. 34","E. 42"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"17","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Seventeen.</b> 'Little gastrin' (G17), a seventeen-amino-acid straight chain, is the form <b>released in response to a meal</b>.<br>B. Twenty-seven: the length of secretin.<br>C. Thirty-three: the length of cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>D. Thirty-four: 'big gastrin' (G34), released at low levels between meals.<br>E. Forty-two: the length of glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":73,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"31"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":6,"label":"Vagus drives G cells via GRP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Vagal stimulation of gastrin release uses gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure annotated: vagal GRP stimulates G cells, so atropine barely helps","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"},{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: atropine blocks the acetylcholine receptor, not G cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-2-1","s":"What is the neurotransmitter that directly stimulates G-cells?","o":["A. Ach","B. Norepinephrine","C. Substance P","D. GRP","E. Gastrin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"GRP","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP).</b> Vagal nerve endings release <b>GRP onto G cells</b>, which then secrete gastrin. That is why atropine does not block vagal gastrin release.<br>A. Acetylcholine (ACh): the vagal transmitter for parietal and enterochromaffin-like cells, not G cells.<br>B. Norepinephrine: the sympathetic transmitter; it does not drive G cells.<br>C. Substance P: a neurocrine for smooth-muscle contraction.<br>E. Gastrin: the product of G cells, not a neurotransmitter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L51A slide 6 (GI Neurocrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"D066"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":2,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":2,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"},{"id":"D010-86-1","s":"Which neurotransmitter is released from the vagus nerve to stimulate gastrin release?","o":["A. Ach","B. Norepinephrine","C. Epinephrine","D. Histamine","E. GRP"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"GRP","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP).</b> The vagus uses <b>GRP on G cells</b> to release gastrin.<br>A. Acetylcholine (ACh): the vagus uses ACh on parietal cells and enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, not on G cells.<br>B. Norepinephrine: sympathetic, not vagal.<br>C. Epinephrine: an adrenal-medulla hormone, not a vagal transmitter.<br>D. Histamine: released by ECL cells; it acts on parietal cells, not G cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L49 slide 22 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":86,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":86,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 13"},{"id":"F1212-116-1","s":"Atropine blocks Ach action on muscarinic receptors. Which of the following is true regarding atropine:","o":["A. It does not blocks vagal stimulation on gastrin producing cells","B. It does not block vagal stimulation on parietal cells","C. It does not cause dry mouth","D. It causes excessive tear production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It does not blocks vagal stimulation on gastrin producing cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The vagus stimulates G cells with <b>gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP), not acetylcholine (ACh)</b>, so atropine, a muscarinic blocker, <b>cannot block</b> vagal gastrin release.<br>B. False: the vagus stimulates parietal cells with ACh on muscarinic M3 receptors, which atropine blocks.<br>C. False: blocking muscarinic receptors on salivary glands causes dry mouth.<br>D. False: atropine reduces tear secretion and causes dry eyes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":116,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":6,"label":"Gastrin grows the gastric mucosa","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions: acid secretion, gastric lining growth, gastric motility","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: excess gastrin thickens mucosa, inactivates lipase, causes steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-8-1","s":"Which of the following is most likely to increase the growth of the gastric mucosa?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Cholecystokinin","c. Somatostatin","d. Secretin","e. Incretins (GLP, GIP)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Gastrin</b> has a <b>trophic effect</b>: it makes the gastric mucosa grow. A gastrinoma thickens the lining; removing the antrum makes it shrink.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK): makes the pancreas and gallbladder grow, not the stomach.<br>C. Somatostatin: inhibits; it does not cause growth.<br>D. Secretin: makes the pancreas grow and blocks gastrin's growth effect on the stomach.<br>E. Incretins, glucagon-like peptide (GLP) and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raise insulin; GLP grows the pancreas, not the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":8,"f":"D019"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":15,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"},{"id":"D040-82-1","s":"What are the actions of gastrin? (select all that apply)","o":["A. Contraction of the gallbladder","B. Secretion of bicarbonate","C. Secretion of H+","D. Secretion of insulin","E. Growth of the gastric mucosa"],"g":["C","E"],"k":["C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of H+; Growth of the gastric mucosa","a":"C,E","why":"<b>C and E.</b> Gastrin <b>raises acid (hydrogen ion) secretion</b> from parietal cells and makes the <b>gastric mucosa grow</b>. It also increases gastric motility.<br>A. Gallbladder contraction: done by cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>B. Bicarbonate secretion: done by secretin, with CCK helping.<br>D. Insulin secretion: done by glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), the main incretin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":21,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":82,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-8-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 14"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":7,"label":"Gastrin: the only hormone with feedback inhibition","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin feedback inhibition: acid (H+) suppresses gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G043-23.jpg","cap":"Inhibition of acid: H+ feedback-inhibits gastrin; somatostatin inhibits gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-59-1","s":"Which of the following hormones is the only one to have feedback inhibition?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin","B. Secretin","C. Gastrin","D. GIP","E. Motilin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastrin</b> is the only gut hormone with <b>feedback inhibition</b>: the acid it produces <b>suppresses gastrin</b> release (low pH also releases somatostatin, which blocks G cells).<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): no feedback inhibition is taught for it.<br>B. Secretin: stimulated by acid; no feedback inhibition is taught for it.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): no feedback inhibition.<br>E. Motilin: suppressed by eating, not by its own product.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 19 (Q1. Which of the following is the only GI hormones to have feedback inhibition?)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":59,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":7,"label":"PPI raises gastrin: acid feedback lost","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin feedback inhibition: acid (H+) suppresses gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: PPI blocks the H+/K+ ATPase acid pump","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G043-14.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell carbonic anhydrase makes the H+ that the pump secretes","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-63-1","s":"A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer. She is placed on omeprazole (proton pump inhibitor). Two days later the gastrin level is found to be elevated. What best explains this?","o":["A. Failure to use antibiotics","B. Zolinger-Ellison syndrome","C. Helicobacter pylori","D. Loss of feedback inhibition","E. Lack of efficacy of omeprazole"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Loss of feedback inhibition","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Loss of feedback inhibition.</b> Acid normally <b>suppresses gastrin</b>. Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), removes acid, so the brake is lost and gastrin rises within days.<br>A. Antibiotics treat Helicobacter pylori; they do not change gastrin feedback.<br>B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: high gastrin with high acid, not low acid.<br>C. Helicobacter pylori: not needed to explain a rise that the PPI fully explains.<br>E. The gastrin rise shows the PPI is working.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 37 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":63,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"},{"id":"D019-52-1","s":"A 23 year old woman with epigastric pain takes omeprazole (proton pump inhibitor) over the counter.. After use of this medication she is found to have a markedly elevated gastrin. What does this mean?","o":["a. Failure of the medication","b. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (Gastrinoma)","c. Expected finding","d. Failure of feedback inhibition","e. Indicates presence of an ulcer"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Expected finding","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Expected finding.</b> Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), removes acid, and acid is the signal that <b>suppresses gastrin</b>. With less acid, gastrin rises. The feedback loop is intact; it simply has no acid to respond to.<br>A. The high gastrin shows the drug is working.<br>B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma): high gastrin with high acid.<br>D. Feedback 'failure' is the gastrinoma pattern, where gastrin stays high despite high acid.<br>E. A gastrin level does not indicate an ulcer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 36 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51B slide 37 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":52,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-63-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 15"},{"id":"D019-42-1","s":"You have a patient with a gastric ulcer who goes on a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), amoxicillin and clarithromycin. Two weeks later you measure the serum gastrin level which is elevated. What is the etiology?","o":["a. This is an expected finding","b. New duodenal ulcer","c. Cancer","d. Failure of PPI","e. Gastrinoma"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"This is an expected finding","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Expected finding.</b> Acid normally <b>feeds back to suppress gastrin</b>. A proton pump inhibitor (PPI) removes acid, so the brake is lost and gastrin rises. This is normal on a PPI.<br>B. A new duodenal ulcer would not explain a gastrin rise.<br>C. Cancer: not needed; the PPI explains the rise.<br>D. Failure of PPI: the PPI is working; less acid is why gastrin rose.<br>E. Gastrinoma: suspect it only when gastrin and acid are both high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 36 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51B slide 37 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":42,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-63-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 16"},{"id":"F1212-140-1","s":"What is a side effect of chronic PPI use?","o":["A. Hypotension","B. Gastrin Induced hyperplasia","C. Parietal cell atrophy","D. Diarrhea","E. Gynecomastia"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin Induced hyperplasia","a":"B","why":"<b>B and D.</b> A proton pump inhibitor (PPI) removes acid, so <b>gastrin rises</b> (lost acid feedback), and its growth effect causes <b>gastric mucosal (enterochromaffin-like cell) hyperplasia</b>. <b>Diarrhea</b> is also a common PPI side effect, partly from more Clostridioides difficile infection.<br>A. Hypotension: not a PPI effect.<br>C. Parietal cells do not atrophy; high gastrin makes the lining grow.<br>E. Gynecomastia: caused by cimetidine, a histamine H2 blocker, not by PPIs.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only B; chronic PPI use also commonly causes diarrhea (including Clostridioides difficile infection), so D is also correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 36 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":140,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-63-1","kind":"harder","n":"C2 extra 17"},{"id":"F1203-68-1","s":"A 58 year old woman comes to the office because of heartburn for two weeks. Th pain most often occurs when she lies down to sleep. She also frequently wakes up with burning in her throat and epigastric area. She is treated with omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). After starting this therapy, which changes will occur in this woman for gastric acid secretion, gastrin secretion, and parietal cell carbonic anhydrase (CA) activity? (table columns: Gastric Acid Secretion | Gastrin Secretion | Parietal cell CA activity)","o":["A. Decreased | Decreased | Increased","B. Decreased | Increased | Decreased","C. Decreased | Increased | Increased","D. Increased | Decreased | Increased","E. Increased | Increased | Decreased","F. Decreased | No change | No change"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased | Increased | Decreased","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Acid down, gastrin up, carbonic anhydrase down.</b> Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), blocks the hydrogen-potassium pump, so <b>acid falls</b>. Less acid removes gastrin's feedback brake, so <b>gastrin rises</b>. With the pump blocked, the parietal cell needs less hydrogen ion, so <b>carbonic anhydrase (CA) activity falls</b>.<br>A. Gastrin rises, not falls.<br>C. CA activity falls, not rises.<br>D. Acid secretion falls on a PPI.<br>E. Acid secretion falls on a PPI.<br>F. Gastrin and CA activity both change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Answer table A-F is an image on slide 68 (transcribed); not needed once transcribed.","tg":["L51B slide 36 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":68,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-63-1","kind":"harder","n":"C2 extra 18"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":7,"label":"Gastrin: the only hormone with neural stimulation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Gastrin stimuli include vagal stimulation through ACh and GRP","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/D007-106.jpg","cap":"Gastrin is the only gastric hormone with neural stimulation and feedback inhibition","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-16-1","s":"Which of the following is stimulated most by neural innervation?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Cholecystokinin","c. Somatostatin","d. Secretin","e. Incretins (GLP, GIP)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Gastrin</b> is the only gut hormone with <b>neural stimulation</b>: the vagus releases <b>gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)</b> onto G cells.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK): released by fat and protein products in the duodenum.<br>C. Somatostatin: released mainly by acid (low pH).<br>D. Secretin: released by acid in the duodenum.<br>E. Incretins, glucagon-like peptide (GLP) and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): released by nutrients in the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 20 (Q2. Which of the following is the only GI hormones to have neural stimulation?)","L51B slide 21 (Q2. Which of the following is the only GI hormones to have neural stimulation?)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":16,"f":"D019"},{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":54,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":7,"label":"Gastrin increases gastric motility","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions include gastric motility; acid feeds back to suppress gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D007-106.jpg","cap":"Gastrin is the only gastric hormone that increases gastric motility","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-65-1","s":"What statement is true with regards to the hormone, Gastrin?","o":["A. It is inhibited by stretch of the stomach","B. It is stimulated by low pH levels within the stomach","C. Gastrin increases gastric motility","D. Gastrin is found within the chief cells of the stomach","E. Gastrin decreases acid secretion of the parietal cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin increases gastric motility","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Gastrin <b>increases gastric motility</b>; it is the only gut hormone that does.<br>A. Stretch of the stomach stimulates gastrin; it does not inhibit it.<br>B. Low pH inhibits gastrin (negative feedback).<br>D. Gastrin comes from G cells of the antrum; chief cells secrete pepsinogen.<br>E. Gastrin increases acid secretion by parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":31,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":65,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":65,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":40,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 19"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":7,"label":"Gastrin grows the gastric mucosa","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions: acid secretion, gastric lining growth, gastric motility","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: excess gastrin thickens mucosa, inactivates lipase, causes steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-7-1","s":"A 51 yo male complains about an oily anal leakage and his family adds that he has especially foul-smelling feces. Upon examination the patient exhibits hypertrophy of the gastric mucosa. Genetic analysis shows a point mutation in a GI peptide that confers a 10-fold hyperactivity. Which peptide has the mutation?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. GIP","d. Histamine","e. Secretin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastrin.</b> Too much gastrin causes <b>gastric mucosal hypertrophy</b> (its trophic effect) and too much acid. Acid in the duodenum <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested: oily, foul stools (steatorrhea).<br>A. Excess cholecystokinin (CCK) would help fat digestion and does not grow the stomach.<br>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin and lowers acid.<br>D. Histamine: raises acid but has no trophic effect.<br>E. Secretin: lowers acid and blocks gastrin's growth effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":18,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":3,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":7,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":97,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":12,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":46,"f":"D019"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":20,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":8,"label":"CCK contracts gallbladder, opens Oddi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK: gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D040-126.jpg","cap":"Bile: fat releases CCK from I cells; gallbladder contracts, Oddi relaxes","where":"2023 TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-14-1","s":"Which of these is most likely to open the sphincter of Oddi?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Cholecystokinin","c. Somatostatin","d. Secretin","e. Incretins (GLP, GIP)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokinin","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> <b>relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b> while it contracts the gallbladder, so bile flows into the duodenum.<br>A. Gastrin: acts on the stomach (acid, growth, motility).<br>C. Somatostatin: inhibits gallbladder and pancreatic secretion.<br>D. Secretin: raises bicarbonate; it does not open the sphincter.<br>E. Incretins, glucagon-like peptide (GLP) and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raise insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":14,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":8,"label":"CCK releases pancreatic enzymes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions include pancreatic enzyme release: lipase, amylase, trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Pancreas: CCK acts at acini (enzymes); secretin acts at ducts (bicarbonate)","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-89-1","s":"Which statement properly describes a mechanism of CCK?","o":["A. CCK acts to contract the sphincter of Oddi at the gall bladder.","B. CCK stimulates release of pancreatic enzymes and juices from the acinar cells.","C. CCK is the one GI hormone not affected by somatostatin.","D. In addition to CCK, gastrin also binds with high affinity to the CCKA receptor ."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK stimulates release of pancreatic enzymes and juices from the acinar cells.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Cholecystokinin (CCK) acts on <b>pancreatic acinar cells</b> to release enzymes (lipase, amylase, proteases).<br>A. CCK <b>relaxes</b> the sphincter of Oddi; it contracts the gallbladder.<br>C. Somatostatin inhibits CCK, as it inhibits all the gut hormones.<br>D. Gastrin binds well to the cholecystokinin B (CCK-B) receptor; the cholecystokinin A (CCK-A) receptor is selective for CCK.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":89,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-134-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 20"},{"id":"D015-63-1","s":"WHICH GI HORMONE INCREASES PANCREATIC ENZYME SECRETION?","o":["A. GASTRIN","B. CCK","C. SECRETIN","D. GLUCOSE-DEPENDENT INSULINOTROPIC PEPTIDE (GIP)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> acts on pancreatic acinar cells to <b>release enzymes</b>: lipase, amylase and trypsin (as trypsinogen).<br>A. Gastrin: raises stomach acid.<br>C. Secretin: acts on duct cells to release bicarbonate and water, not enzymes.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin from the endocrine pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":63,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-134-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 21"},{"id":"D004-134-1","s":"Which of the following is induced by CCK;","o":["A. Inhibition of bicarbonate secretion rich pancreatic Juice","B. Increased rate of gastric emptying","C. Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic enzymes","D. Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Cholecystokinin (CCK) <b>potentiates secretin</b> on pancreatic duct cells, so more bicarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid is secreted.<br>A. CCK increases, not inhibits, bicarbonate-rich juice.<br>B. CCK slows gastric emptying.<br>C. Enzyme release is CCK's own direct action on acinar cells; secretin acts on duct cells for bicarbonate, so 'potentiate secretin to increase enzymes' is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":56,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":134,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":8,"label":"Fat releases CCK, which grows the pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-10.jpg","cap":"CCK stimuli: fat in duodenum (monoglycerides, fatty acids), protein parts","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions include growth of the pancreas and gallbladder","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-36-1","s":"When fed to laboratory animals, which of the following meals is most likely to increase the size of the pancreas?","o":["a. Fat","b. Protein","c. Carbohydrates","d. Combination of protein and carbohydrates","e. Acid (HCl)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Fat.</b> Fatty acids and monoglycerides are the <b>strongest stimulus for cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>, and CCK makes the <b>exocrine pancreas and gallbladder grow</b>. Fatty acids also release secretin, which grows the pancreas too.<br>B. Protein: releases some CCK, but fat is the main stimulus.<br>C. Carbohydrates: mainly release glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), which does not grow the exocrine pancreas.<br>D. Protein plus carbohydrate: a weaker CCK stimulus than fat.<br>E. Acid: releases secretin only; fat releases both CCK and secretin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":36,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":8,"label":"CCK is the main brake on gastric emptying","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions end with 'Inhibits Gastric Emptying'","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Duodenal fat, acid, stretch and osmolarity slow gastric emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/D012-17.jpg","cap":"CCK summary: stimulated by fat, slows gastric emptying, releases enzymes","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D037-41-1","s":"Which of the following hormones are released by the presence of protein and fat in the small intestine and has a major effect to decrease gastric emptying?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","B. Glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide (GLIP)","C. Gastrin","D. Motilin","E. Secretin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> Fatty acids, monoglycerides, small peptides and amino acids in the small intestine release CCK from I cells. CCK is the <b>strongest inhibitor of gastric emptying</b>, giving time for fat digestion.<br>B. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): released by all nutrients; its main job is insulin.<br>C. Gastrin: released in the stomach; it increases gastric motility.<br>D. Motilin: released during fasting; it drives the migrating myoelectric complex.<br>E. Secretin: released by acid; its main job is bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA weekly","n":"Physio GI weeks 1 & 2","sl":41,"f":"D037"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":13,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":5,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":92,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"},{"id":"D067-45-1","s":"What hormone slows gastric emptying in response to the presence of fatty acids in the stomach?","o":["A. VIP","B. Insulin","C. CCK","D. Secretin and GIP","E. Motilin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"C","why":"<b>C. CCK (cholecystokinin).</b> Fatty acids that reach the duodenum make I cells release CCK. CCK is the <b>main hormone that slows gastric emptying</b>, which gives time to digest the fat.<br>D. Secretin and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide): tempting, because both also slow the stomach. But they are weaker brakes; secretin answers mainly acid and GIP mainly sugar. Fat's own hormone is CCK, so D is not the best answer.<br>A. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): relaxes the orad stomach to receive food.<br>B. Insulin: no role in emptying.<br>E. Motilin: fasting contractions.","ex":"","fl":["CCK is the intended and classic answer (fat → CCK → slower gastric emptying; G041:30-31 'stimulated most by fat' = CCK). The 2026 deck also lists fatty acids as a stimulus for secretin (G040:12) and GIP (G040:14) and says both slow the stomach (G040:13, G040:15), so option D is partly defensible from this year's slides; pick CCK. The stem's 'in the stomach' should read 'in the duodenum'."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":45,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 22"},{"id":"D015-59-1","s":"WHICH GI HORMONE INHIBITS GASTRIC EMPTYING?","o":["A. GASTRIN","B. CCK","C. SECRETIN","D. GLUCOSE-DEPENDENT INSULINOTROPIC PEPTIDE (GIP)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> is the main hormone that <b>inhibits gastric emptying</b>; fat in the duodenum releases it.<br>A. Gastrin: the only gut hormone that increases gastric motility.<br>C. Secretin: the 2026 slide lists slower emptying among its actions, but the effect is weak; its main job is bicarbonate.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): slows gastric motility weakly; its main job is insulin.","ex":"","fl":["Ambiguous vs 2026 deck (key CCK is the classic/intended answer): G040:13 also lists 'Inhibits gastric emptying' under secretin and G040:15 'Inhibits gastric motility' under GIP; pick CCK as the main inhibitor of gastric emptying."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":59,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 23"},{"id":"G067-54-1","s":"GI hormones have physiological effects that can be elicited at normal concentrations as well as pharmacological effects that require higher than normal concentrations. Which of the following has the greatest effect on gastric emptying?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Secretin","C. CCK","D. GLIP","E. Motilin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> has the <b>strongest effect on gastric emptying</b>: it slows emptying at normal blood levels so fat has time to be digested.<br>A. Gastrin: increases gastric motility.<br>B. Secretin: slows emptying only weakly.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): slows motility weakly; its main job is insulin.<br>E. Motilin: drives fasting contractions (migrating myoelectric complex), not the fed-state brake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":54,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 24"},{"id":"G063-4-17","s":"A 30-year-old male seeks help because he lost weight and feels full after eating only a small amount of food. He is diagnosed with a delay in gastric emptying. Which of the following hormones has at physiologic levels the strongest effect in inhibiting gastric emptying?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Motilin","c. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","d. VIP","e. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> has the <b>strongest physiologic effect slowing gastric emptying</b>, which fits early fullness.<br>A. Gastrin: increases gastric motility.<br>B. Motilin: speeds fasting contractions; erythromycin helps gastroparesis by mimicking it.<br>D. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP): a neurocrine that relaxes smooth muscle, not the hormone that brakes emptying.<br>E. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): only a weak inhibitor of gastric motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":4,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 25"},{"id":"D007-169-1","s":"When dietary lipids first appear in the small intestine, which of the following substances is secreted that slows gastric emptying and aids in lipid digestion?","o":["A. Enterokinase","B. Trypsin","C. CCK","D. Pancreatic lipase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>, a hormone from I cells, is released when fat reaches the duodenum. It <b>slows gastric emptying</b>, contracts the gallbladder and releases pancreatic lipase.<br>A. Enterokinase: a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen; it does not slow emptying.<br>B. Trypsin: a protein-digesting enzyme; it does not slow emptying.<br>D. Pancreatic lipase: digests fat and is itself released by CCK; it does not slow emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":169,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":108,"f":"F1203"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":128,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":128,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 26"},{"id":"D036-166-1","s":"A patient with a history of gallbladder dysfunction experiences difficulties in the digestion and absorption of dietary lipids. Which hormone, secreted in response to the arrival of dietary lipids in the small intestine, plays a critical role in slowing gastric emptying to allow sufficient time for lipid digestion and absorption in the intestine?","o":["A. Insulin","B. Ghrelin","C. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","D. Gastrin","E. Secretin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> Lipids arriving in the duodenum release CCK from I cells, and CCK <b>slows gastric emptying</b> so fat has time to be digested and absorbed.<br>A. Insulin: a pancreatic hormone; it does not control gastric emptying.<br>B. Ghrelin: a stomach hormone that raises appetite before meals.<br>D. Gastrin: increases gastric motility.<br>E. Secretin: released by acid; it raises bicarbonate and slows emptying only weakly.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":166,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D037-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":8,"label":"CCK actions: true or not","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions: gallbladder, Oddi, pancreas growth, enzymes, bicarbonate and insulin, emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: CCK raises enzymes and bicarbonate, contracts gallbladder, slows emptying","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-66-1","s":"Which of the following hormones stimulates bile secretion and pancreatic enzyme secretion and slows gastric emptying?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Secretin","C. CCK","D. GIP"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> is the one hormone that does all three: it <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> (delivers bile), <b>releases pancreatic enzymes</b> and <b>slows gastric emptying</b>.<br>A. Gastrin: raises acid and increases gastric motility.<br>B. Secretin: raises pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate but not enzymes.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin and lowers acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":66,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":66,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":234,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 28"},{"id":"D040-89-1","s":"Which is NOT an action of CCK?","o":["A. Contraction of the gallbladder","B. Secretion of pancreatic enzymes","C. Inhibition of gastric H+ secretion","D. Secretion of bicarbonate from the pancreas","E. Growth of the exocrine pancreas and gallbladder","F. Inhibition of gastric emptying"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inhibition of gastric H+ secretion","a":"C","why":"<b>C is not a cholecystokinin (CCK) action.</b> CCK does not inhibit gastric acid; somatostatin, secretin and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) do.<br>A. True: CCK contracts the gallbladder.<br>B. True: CCK releases lipase, amylase and proteases.<br>D. True: CCK potentiates secretin's bicarbonate secretion.<br>E. True: CCK makes the exocrine pancreas and gallbladder grow.<br>F. True: CCK slows gastric emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":89,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"},{"id":"D036-63-1","s":"Which is not a function of CCK?","o":["A. CCK stimulates the secretion of H+ by parietal cells","B. CCK promotes fat digestion and absorption","C. CCK stimulates contraction of the gallbladder","D. CCK stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK stimulates the secretion of H+ by parietal cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A is not a cholecystokinin (CCK) action.</b> Acid (hydrogen ion) from parietal cells is stimulated by gastrin, histamine and acetylcholine, not CCK.<br>B. True: CCK promotes fat digestion and absorption.<br>C. True: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.<br>D. True: CCK releases pancreatic enzymes (lipase, amylase, trypsin).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":63,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 29"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":9,"label":"CCK actions: true or not","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions: gallbladder, Oddi, pancreas growth, enzymes, bicarbonate and insulin, emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: CCK raises enzymes and bicarbonate, contracts gallbladder, slows emptying","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-56-1","s":"Which of following is a true statement regarding Cholecystokinin?","o":["A. It is secreted from G cells","B. Decreases Gastric H+ secretion","C. It contracts the gallbladder","D. It stimulates gastric emptying","E. Increases insulin secretion"],"g":["C","E"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It contracts the gallbladder","a":"C","why":"<b>C and E.</b> Cholecystokinin (CCK) <b>contracts the gallbladder</b>. The 2026 lecture also lists <b>insulin (and bicarbonate) production</b> among CCK's actions, so E is true, though glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) raises insulin far more.<br>A. G cells make gastrin; CCK comes from I cells.<br>B. Gastric acid is lowered by somatostatin, secretin and GIP, not CCK.<br>D. CCK slows gastric emptying; it does not speed it.","ex":"","fl":["C is the intended answer, but the 2026 deck lists 'Bicarbonate & Insulin production' among CCK actions (G040:11), so E is also true by this year's slides; GIP is the main insulin-raising hormone (G041:25)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":56,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","n":"C2 extra 30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":9,"label":"CCK contracts gallbladder, opens Oddi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK: gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D040-126.jpg","cap":"Bile: fat releases CCK from I cells; gallbladder contracts, Oddi relaxes","where":"2023 TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-8-1","s":"A 40-year-old obese pregnant woman presents to an ED physician with excruciating right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, perspiration, and uneasiness for the last 2 hours. The patient states that she had a whole pizza with extra cheese ~ 3-hours ago. Radioimaging and ultrasound show gallstones in the common bile duct. Diagnosis of acute cholecystitis is made. Which of the following stimuli will exacerbate the pain?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)","d. Secretin","e. Somatostatin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> The fatty pizza releases CCK, which <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> against the stone in the common bile duct and worsens the pain.<br>B. Gastrin: acts on the stomach.<br>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin.<br>D. Secretin: raises biliary bicarbonate and water but does not contract the gallbladder.<br>E. Somatostatin: inhibits gallbladder contraction, so it would ease the pain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":25,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":3,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":8,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":101,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":22,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"},{"id":"D019-50-1","s":"While eating fat pork sandwiches on a beach in Costa Rica, Dr. D. gets right upper quadrant abdominal pain. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Secretin","c. Cholecystokin","d. Glucose insulinotropic peptide (GIP)","e. Somatostatin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokin","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> Fat in the duodenum releases CCK from I cells. CCK <b>contracts the gallbladder</b>, so gallstones cause right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal.<br>A. Gastrin: raises stomach acid; no gallbladder effect.<br>B. Secretin: raises biliary and pancreatic bicarbonate but does not contract the gallbladder.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin.<br>E. Somatostatin: inhibits gallbladder contraction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":50,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-8-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 31"},{"id":"D010-68-1","s":"A 40 year old female arrives at the doctor’s office with RUQ pain that seems to worsen after eating meals high in fat. Ultrasound reveals a significant amount of cholesterol gallstones. Secretion of what hormone is responsible for causing the patient’s pain?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Secretin","C. Motilin","D. GIP","E. CCK"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> A fatty meal releases CCK, which <b>contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b>. Squeezing against gallstones causes right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals.<br>A. Gastrin: acts on the stomach.<br>B. Secretin: raises bicarbonate; no gallbladder contraction.<br>C. Motilin: drives fasting contractions; it falls after eating.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":68,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":68,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":43,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-8-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":10,"label":"Secretin: acid in, bicarbonate out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-12.jpg","cap":"Secretin is released by acid and fatty acids in the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G040-13.jpg","cap":"Secretin actions: pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, gastrin inhibition","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G044-23.jpg","cap":"More acid means more secretin, so more bicarbonate-rich pancreatic flow","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-67-1","s":"Which best describes secretin in the digestive process?","o":["A. Secretin promotes the secretion of acid in the stomach.","B. Secretin downregulates the production of bicarbonate in the pancreas.","C. Secretin production is stimulated by acid","D. Secretin is produced by the I-cells of the stomach"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin production is stimulated by acid","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Acid (hydrogen ion) in the duodenum</b> releases secretin from S cells; fatty acids do too.<br>A. Secretin lowers stomach acid by inhibiting gastrin.<br>B. Secretin increases pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>D. Secretin comes from S cells of the duodenum; I cells of the duodenum and jejunum make cholecystokinin (CCK).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 12 (Secretin)","L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":67,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"},{"id":"D019-18-1","s":"What is the function of secretin?","o":["a. Increases H+ secretion","b. Decreases HCO3- secretion","c. Stimulation of insulin secretion","d. Increases HCO3- secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increases HCO3- secretion","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> Secretin <b>increases bicarbonate secretion</b> from pancreatic and biliary duct cells to neutralize acid in the duodenum.<br>A. Secretin lowers hydrogen ion (acid) secretion by inhibiting gastrin.<br>B. It increases, not decreases, bicarbonate.<br>C. The 2026 slide lists a small insulin rise, but insulin is mainly raised by glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP); it is not secretin's function.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":18,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"},{"id":"D036-65-1","s":"What is the main function of secretin?","o":["A. Secretin promotes fat digestion and absorption","B. Secretin stimulates gastric emptying","C. Reduce the amount of H+ in the lumen of the small intestine","D. Secretin decreases insulin level"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Reduce the amount of H+ in the lumen of the small intestine","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Secretin's job is to <b>reduce acid (hydrogen ion) in the small intestine</b>: it releases pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin. A neutral duodenum keeps pancreatic lipase active.<br>A. Fat digestion and absorption is the job of cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>B. Secretin slows gastric emptying; it does not stimulate it.<br>D. Secretin raises insulin slightly; it does not lower it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":65,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-67-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 33"},{"id":"D012-18-1","s":"Secretin will also inhibit the effects of gastrin. Why would secretin want to inhibit gastrin?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gastrin stimulates parietal cells to secrete H+; inhibiting gastrin lets secretin accomplish its task of neutralizing H+","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastrin stimulates parietal cells to secrete acid (hydrogen ion).</b> Secretin's goal is to <b>neutralize acid</b> in the duodenum with bicarbonate. Blocking gastrin's release and its effect on parietal cells cuts acid at its source, while bicarbonate neutralizes the acid that already arrived. Both actions push the same way.<br>Common mix-up: acid releases secretin; secretin never raises acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 2","sl":18,"f":"D012"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-67-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 34"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":11,"label":"GIP responds to glucose, amino and fatty acids","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-14.jpg","cap":"GIP stimuli: carbohydrates, amino acids and fatty acids ('Food!')","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: GIP released by fatty acids, amino acids and oral glucose","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-6-1","s":"A 10 yo girl consumes a cheeseburger, fries and a chocolate shake. The meal stimulates the release of several GI hormones. The presence of fat, carbohydrate OR protein in the duodenum stimulates the release of which hormone from the duodenal mucosa in this case?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. GIP","d. Motilin","e. Secretin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>, from K cells of the duodenum and jejunum, is the <b>only gut hormone released by all three nutrients</b>: carbohydrate, protein (amino acids) and fat.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): fat and protein only.<br>B. Gastrin: protein and stretch in the stomach, not the duodenum.<br>D. Motilin: released during fasting and inhibited by food.<br>E. Secretin: acid and fatty acids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":15,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":2,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":6,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":94,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":9,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":16,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"},{"id":"D036-67-1","s":"Which is the only GI hormone secreted in response to fats, proteins, AND carbohydrates?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. GIP","C. Secretin","D. CCK"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> is the only gut hormone released by <b>glucose, amino acids and fatty acids</b>.<br>A. Gastrin: protein products and stretch.<br>C. Secretin: acid and fatty acids.<br>D. Cholecystokinin (CCK): fatty acids and protein products, not carbohydrate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":67,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-6-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 35"},{"id":"D015-61-1","s":"WHICH GI HORMONE IS STIMULATED BY CARBOHYDRATES?","o":["A. GASTRIN","B. CCK","C. SECRETIN","D. GLUCOSE-DEPENDENT INSULINOTROPIC PEPTIDE (GIP)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUCOSE-DEPENDENT INSULINOTROPIC PEPTIDE (GIP)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> is the gut hormone released by <b>carbohydrate</b> (oral glucose), as well as by amino acids and fatty acids.<br>A. Gastrin: released by stretch, peptides and amino acids.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK): released by fat and protein products.<br>C. Secretin: released by acid and fatty acids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":61,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-6-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 36"},{"id":"D019-12-1","s":"Which of these is stimulated most by carbohydrates?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Cholecystokinin","c. Somatostatin","d. Secretin","e. Incretins (GLP, GIP)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Incretins (GLP, GIP)","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Incretins</b>: glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP, from K cells) and glucagon-like peptide (GLP, from L cells). <b>Glucose in the gut</b> releases them, and they raise insulin.<br>A. Gastrin: released by stretch, peptides and amino acids.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK): released by fat and protein products.<br>C. Somatostatin: released by acid (low pH).<br>D. Secretin: released by acid and fatty acids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":12,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-6-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 37"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":11,"label":"Oral glucose releases GIP, more insulin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-14.jpg","cap":"Graph: oral glucose raises insulin more than IV glucose; GIP and GLP-1 incretins","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP: K-cell incretin that increases insulin release from the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G040-15.jpg","cap":"GIP actions: increases insulin release, inhibits gastric motility and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-17-1","s":"Which of the following has a more rapid effect in the secretion of insulin?","o":["A. IV glucose","B. Oral glucose"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oral glucose","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Oral glucose.</b> Glucose in the gut releases the <b>incretin glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>, which adds to glucose's direct effect on beta cells, so <b>more insulin comes sooner</b>.<br>A. Intravenous (IV) glucose raises blood glucose directly but bypasses the gut, so no GIP is released and insulin rises less.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":17,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":62,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":62,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":230,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 38"},{"id":"D040-91-1","s":"Two patients receive the same amount of glucose. Patient A is given the glucose orally, and patient B is given the glucose intravenously. Which patient will have a more rapid decrease in their postprandial glucose level?","o":["A. Patient A","B. Patient B"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Patient A","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Patient A (oral glucose).</b> Glucose in the gut releases the <b>incretins</b> glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1). They boost insulin, so blood glucose falls faster.<br>B. Patient B's intravenous (IV) glucose skips the gut, so no incretin is released and insulin rises less.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":91,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 39"},{"id":"D040-92-1","s":"WHY??? Is there greater/less secretion of insulin? (oral vs IV glucose)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"greater insulin (insulin brings the blood glucose down!)","a":null,"why":"<b>Greater insulin with oral glucose.</b> Oral glucose stimulates beta cells directly <b>and</b> releases the incretin glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) from the gut. Intravenous (IV) glucose gives only the direct effect. More insulin brings blood glucose down faster.<br>Common mix-up: the same blood glucose does not mean the same insulin; the gut adds the extra signal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":92,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 40"},{"id":"D004-122-1","s":"In an experiment, one group of subjects is given 50 grams of glucose intravenously and another is given 50 grams of glucose orally. Which factor can explain why the oral glucose load is cleared from the blood at a faster rate compared to the IV glucose load?","o":["A. CCK-induced insulin release","B. CCK-induced vasoactive intestinal peptide release","C. GIP-induced glucagon release","D. GIP-induced insulin release","E. VIP-induced GIP release"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP-induced insulin release","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Oral glucose releases <b>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> from K cells; GIP <b>adds extra insulin</b>, so oral glucose is cleared faster. Intravenous (IV) glucose acts only directly on beta cells.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) raises insulin only slightly, and glucose barely releases CCK.<br>B. CCK does not release vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP).<br>C. GIP raises insulin, not glucagon.<br>E. VIP does not release GIP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":122,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":34,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":46,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":2,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":14,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":60,"f":"G067"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":18,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"},{"id":"D040-93-1","s":"What hormone is this due to? (greater insulin after oral glucose)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), an incretin! (also, GLP-1)","a":null,"why":"<b>Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>, from K cells of the duodenum and jejunum, is the main <b>incretin</b>: a gut hormone that raises insulin when nutrients are eaten. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), from L cells, is the second incretin.<br>Common mix-up: cholecystokinin (CCK) raises insulin only slightly; GIP raises it the most.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":93,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 41"},{"id":"D015-77-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: ADMINISTRATION OF ORAL GLUCOSE HAS A MORE RAPID AFFECT THAN IV GLUCOSE ON GIP","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"FACT","a":"A","why":"<b>Fact.</b> K cells release glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) when <b>glucose is in the gut lumen</b>. Oral glucose raises GIP quickly; intravenous (IV) glucose bypasses the gut and barely raises it.<br>Common mix-up: IV glucose still raises insulin directly, but without the GIP boost.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":77,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 42"},{"id":"D060-61-1","s":"Which of the following increases insulin levels the most?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin","B. GIP","C. Gastrin","D. Secretin","E. Motilin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> is the main <b>incretin</b>: it raises insulin from pancreatic beta cells far more than any other gut hormone.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): the lecture lists only a small insulin effect.<br>C. Gastrin: no meaningful insulin effect.<br>D. Secretin: a small insulin effect only.<br>E. Motilin: drives fasting gut contractions; no insulin effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 25 (Q4. Which of the following increases insulin levels the most?)","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":61,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-122-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 43"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":11,"label":"GLP-1: L-cell incretin that raises insulin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G041-8.jpg","cap":"GLP is made by L cells of the small intestine; half-life 1-2 minutes","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G041-6.jpg","cap":"GLP is not like glucagon: inhibits glucagon, raises insulin, lowers glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-81-1","s":"WHICH STRUCTURE SYNTHESIZES AND SECRETES GIP-1?","o":["A. STOMACH","B. PANCREAS","C. SMALL INTESTINE","D. LARGE INTESTINE"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"SMALL INTESTINE (L cells; \"GIP-1\" in the stem means GLP-1)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Small intestine.</b> 'GIP-1' here means <b>glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)</b>, made by <b>L cells of the small intestine</b>.<br>A. Stomach: makes gastrin and ghrelin, not GLP-1.<br>B. Pancreas: its alpha cells make glucagon from the same precursor, but not GLP-1.<br>D. Large intestine: has some L cells, but the lecture names the small intestine as the source.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 8 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":81,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-72-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 44"},{"id":"D036-72-1","s":"What is the function of glucagon like peptide?","o":["A. Increases the sensitivity of pancreatic cells to proteins","B. Potentiates the effects of glucagon","C. Increases appetite","D. Stimulates insulin secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulates insulin secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), from L cells, is an <b>incretin</b> that <b>stimulates insulin secretion</b>. Despite its name, it is not like glucagon.<br>A. It makes beta cells more sensitive to glucose, not to proteins.<br>B. It inhibits glucagon.<br>C. It decreases appetite.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 6 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":72,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":11,"label":"DPP4 inhibitors spare the incretins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP slows gastric motility, raises insulin, and is broken down by DPP4","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G041-4.jpg","cap":"Inhibiting DPP4 treats diabetes: sitagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G041-6.jpg","cap":"GLP inhibits glucagon, lowers glucose, raises insulin, grows the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-85-1","s":"Decreased Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 activity would result in:","o":["A. Increased gastric motility","B. Increased acid production","C. Shrinks pancreas","D. Increased insulin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased insulin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Increased insulin.</b> Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP4) breaks down the incretins glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1). Less DPP4 means <b>more incretin, so more insulin</b>; this is how sitagliptin treats diabetes.<br>A. Incretins slow gastric motility; they do not increase it.<br>B. Incretins decrease acid.<br>C. GLP-1 makes the pancreas grow; it does not shrink it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 4 (GIP functions)","L51B slide 6 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":85,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"},{"id":"D010-71-1","s":"A man with Type II Diabetes is started on Sitagliptin, a drug that inhibits the enzyme DPP4. A specific incretin produced in the K-cells of the duodenum/jejunum is therefore not broken down. Which statement is true with regards to this incretin?","o":["A. It inhibits gastric motility","B. It is stimulated by only carbohydrate intake","C. It decreases insulin release from the pancreas","D. It stimulates production of gastric acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It inhibits gastric motility","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The K-cell incretin is <b>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>. Sitagliptin blocks dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP4), the enzyme that breaks GIP down. GIP <b>slows gastric motility</b>, raises insulin and lowers acid.<br>B. GIP responds to carbohydrate, amino acids and fatty acids, not carbohydrate only.<br>C. GIP increases insulin release.<br>D. GIP inhibits gastric acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51B slide 4 (GIP functions)","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":71,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":71,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":46,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-85-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 45"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":12,"label":"Motilin: fasting hormone, stopped by eating","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"MMC only between meals, starts 90-120 minutes after eating, driven by motilin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/D033-16.jpg","cap":"Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting states","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-10-1","s":"Which of these is INHIBITED by eating?","o":["a. CCK","b. Secretin","c. Gastrin","d. GLP/GIP","e. Motilin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Motilin</b> is released from the upper duodenum during <b>fasting</b> and drives the migrating myoelectric complex (MMC); <b>eating stops it</b>.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): released by fat and protein.<br>B. Secretin: released by acid and fatty acids.<br>C. Gastrin: released by stretch and protein.<br>D. Glucagon-like peptide (GLP) and glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): released by nutrients.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 28 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)","L51B slide 29 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":10,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"51"},{"id":"G064-80-1","s":"A 30 year old woman eats a large, well-balanced meal for dinner. Five minutes after eating the meal, many hormones are stimulated by the food intake except for this hormone. Which hormone is inhibited soon after eating this meal?","o":["A. CCK","B. Motilin","C. Secretin","D. Gastrin","E. GIP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Motilin.</b> Motilin is a <b>fasting</b> hormone that drives the migrating myoelectric complex (MMC), which sweeps leftover food out between meals; <b>a meal inhibits it</b>.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): rises with fat and protein.<br>C. Secretin: rises when acid reaches the duodenum.<br>D. Gastrin: rises with stretch and protein.<br>E. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): rises with all nutrients.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 28 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)","L51B slide 29 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)","L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":35,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":80,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":55,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 46"},{"id":"D015-79-1","s":"WHICH HORMONE IS SECRETED FROM THE UPPER DUODENUM DURING FASTING STATES?","o":["A. ENTEROGLUCAGON","B. GLUCAGON-LIKE PEPTIDE 1 (GIP-1)","C. PANCREATIC POLYPEPTIDE","D. MOTILIN"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"MOTILIN","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Motilin</b> is secreted from the <b>upper duodenum during fasting</b> and starts the migrating myoelectric complexes about every ninety minutes.<br>A. Enteroglucagon: released when blood glucose falls; acts on the liver.<br>B. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1): released from L cells by nutrients after a meal.<br>C. Pancreatic polypeptide: released by the pancreas after a meal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 29 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)","L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":79,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":12,"label":"Ghrelin raises appetite; leptin, insulin, GLP-1 lower it","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-21.jpg","cap":"Leptin decreases appetite, ghrelin increases it, GLP-1 inhibits appetite","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G048-11.jpg","cap":"Ghrelin from stomach rises with fasting, raises appetite; leptin lowers it","where":"2025 discussion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-80-1","s":"Which substance stimulates appetite?","o":["A. GLP-1","B. Leptin","C. Ghrelin","D. Insulin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ghrelin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ghrelin</b>, secreted by the stomach just before meals, <b>stimulates appetite</b> by exciting orexigenic (appetite-raising) neurons in the hypothalamus.<br>A. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1): decreases appetite.<br>B. Leptin: from fat cells; decreases appetite long-term.<br>D. Insulin: decreases appetite short-term.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":80,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"52"},{"id":"G066-88-1","s":"Of the list of satiety hormones below, indicate if they increase appetite or decrease appetite. Leptin; Insulin; GLP-1; Ghrelin; Peptide YY","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Leptin = decreases appetite; Insulin = decreases appetite (short term); GLP-1 = decreases appetite; Ghrelin = increases appetite; Peptide YY = decreases appetite","a":null,"why":"<b>Only ghrelin increases appetite.</b> The stomach releases ghrelin before meals (the stomach 'GHRowls'). <b>Leptin</b> (from fat, long-term), <b>insulin</b> (short-term), <b>glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)</b> from L cells, and <b>peptide YY (PYY)</b> all <b>decrease appetite</b>. They excite anorexigenic pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) neurons and inhibit orexigenic neuropeptide Y neurons in the hypothalamus.<br>Common mix-up: the source slide reversed leptin, insulin, GLP-1 and ghrelin.","ex":"","fl":["Key is wrong for 4 of 5: leptin, insulin and GLP-1 decrease appetite (satiety signals acting on anorexigenic POMC neurons) and ghrelin increases appetite (the slide's own 'stomach GHRowls' mnemonic, and the TA's own slide 87 'decreases appetite short term → insulin', contradict the key). Only 'Peptide YY → decreases appetite' is right (Costanzo, Hunger and satiety).","The source keyed leptin, insulin and GLP-1 as raising appetite and ghrelin as lowering it; in fact leptin, insulin, GLP-1 and peptide YY lower appetite and ghrelin raises it."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 6 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":88,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":88,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-80-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":12,"label":"Hypothalamic appetite neurons and centers","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G066-89-2","s":"What neurons in the hypothalamus, when stimulated, are responsible for decreasing appetite and increasing appetite, respectively?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Anorexigenic neurons → release POMC → decrease appetite; Orexigenic neurons → release neuropeptide Y → increase appetite","a":null,"why":"<b>Anorexigenic neurons</b> of the arcuate nucleus release <b>pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC)</b> and <b>decrease appetite</b>. <b>Orexigenic neurons</b> release <b>neuropeptide Y (NPY)</b> and <b>increase appetite</b>. Leptin, insulin and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) excite the anorexigenic neurons; ghrelin excites the orexigenic ones.<br>Common mix-up: 'orexigenic' means appetite-making (orexis means appetite); 'an-' means against.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":89,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":89,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 49"},{"id":"D015-85-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: THE SATIETY CENTER IS LOCATED IN THE LATERAL HYPOTHALAMIC AREA (LHA)","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CAP","a":"B","why":"<b>Cap (false).</b> The <b>lateral hypothalamic area (LHA)</b> is the <b>feeding (hunger) center</b>. The <b>satiety center</b> is in the ventromedial hypothalamus (ventromedial nucleus); Costanzo labels it the ventral posterior nucleus. Arcuate-nucleus neurons feed signals into both centers.<br>Common mix-up: damage to the LHA causes loss of appetite; damage to the satiety center causes overeating.","ex":"","fl":["Key (CAP) is correct, but the source explanation is wrong: the satiety center is the ventromedial nucleus (VMN) of the hypothalamus, not a 'ventral posterior nucleus'; the LHA is the feeding (hunger) center."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":85,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 50"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":12,"label":"Candidate hormones: motilin, PP, enteroglucagon, GLP-1","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D033-16.jpg","cap":"Candidate hormones: motilin, pancreatic polypeptide, enteroglucagon, peptide YY, GLP-1","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"Migrating myoelectric complex occurs between meals and is driven by motilin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-74-1","s":"Which of the following candidate hormone acts on the liver to increase glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis?","o":["A. Enteroglucagon","B. Motilin","C. Peptide YY","D. Pancreatic polypeptide"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enteroglucagon","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enteroglucagon</b> is released from intestinal cells when <b>blood glucose falls</b> and tells the <b>liver</b> to increase glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.<br>B. Motilin: starts the fasting migrating myoelectric complexes.<br>C. Peptide YY: from ileum and colon; inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion and appetite.<br>D. Pancreatic polypeptide: inhibits pancreatic bicarbonate and enzyme secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":74,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"53"},{"id":"D040-95-1","s":"More matching! Match candidate hormones with their function: / Motilin / Pancreatic polypeptide / Enteroglucagon / Glucagon-like peptide-1 / Inhibits pancreatic secretion of HCO3- and enzymes / Stimulates insulin secretion in response to oral glucose load / Released in response to decrease in blood glucose concentration, directs liver to increased glycogenolysis/gluconeogenesis / Initiates interdigestive myoelectric complexes","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Motilin = initiates interdigestive myoelectric complexes; Pancreatic polypeptide = inhibits pancreatic secretion of HCO3- and enzymes; Enteroglucagon = released in response to decrease in blood glucose, directs liver to increase glycogenolysis/gluconeogenesis; GLP-1 = stimulates insulin secretion in response to oral glucose load","a":null,"why":"<b>Motilin</b>: starts the interdigestive (fasting) myoelectric complexes.<br><b>Pancreatic polypeptide</b>: inhibits pancreatic bicarbonate and enzyme secretion.<br><b>Enteroglucagon</b>: released when blood glucose falls; makes the liver break down glycogen and make new glucose.<br><b>Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)</b>: an incretin from L cells that raises insulin after oral glucose.<br>Common mix-up: GLP-1 lowers blood glucose; enteroglucagon raises it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":95,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-74-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 51"},{"id":"G066-83-1","s":"Match the following candidate hormones with their corresponding actions. 1. Motilin 2. Pancreatic polypeptide 3. Enteroglucagon 4. Glucagon-like peptide -1 (GLP-1)","o":["A. Directs liver to increase glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","B. Initiate interdigestive myoelectric complexes that occur at every 90 minute intervals","C. Inhibits glucagon secretion and appetite and decreases gastric emptying","D. Inhibits pancreatic secretion of HCO3- and enzymes"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Motilin → B (interdigestive myoelectric complexes every 90 min); Pancreatic polypeptide → D (inhibits pancreatic HCO3- and enzyme secretion); Enteroglucagon → A (hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis); GLP-1 → C (inhibits glucagon secretion and appetite, decreases gastric emptying)","a":"1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C","why":"<b>Motilin → B</b>: released in fasting; starts the interdigestive myoelectric complexes about every ninety minutes.<br><b>Pancreatic polypeptide → D</b>: inhibits pancreatic bicarbonate and enzyme secretion.<br><b>Enteroglucagon → A</b>: released when blood glucose falls; tells the liver to break down glycogen and make new glucose.<br><b>Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) → C</b>: inhibits glucagon and appetite and slows gastric emptying.<br>Common mix-up: GLP-1 opposes glucagon; enteroglucagon acts like it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)","L51B slide 6 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)","L51B slide 7 (Glucagon-Like Peptide)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":83,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":83,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-74-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 52"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":13,"label":"Low pH releases somatostatin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-107.jpg","cap":"Somatostatin from D cells is stimulated by acidic (low) gastric pH","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 107"},{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Diagram: somatostatin from D cells brakes gastrin, histamine and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-78-1","s":"When is somatostatin released?","o":["A. In response to increase in pH","B. In response to decrease in pH","C. In response to increased osmolality","D. In response to decreased osmolality"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"In response to decrease in pH","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> D cells release somatostatin when <b>luminal pH falls</b> (more acid). Somatostatin then blocks gastrin and histamine and lowers acid. This is the negative-feedback brake on acid.<br>A. A rise in pH (less acid) removes the stimulus, so somatostatin falls and gastrin rises.<br>C. Increased osmolality: not a somatostatin trigger; in the duodenum it slows gastric emptying instead.<br>D. Decreased osmolality: not a somatostatin trigger either.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":78,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"54"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":13,"label":"Somatostatin: the main inhibitory paracrine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Diagram: D-cell somatostatin inhibits gastrin, ECL histamine and parietal acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D007-107.jpg","cap":"D cells secrete somatostatin; it inhibits everything, including acid and motility","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 107"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-77-1","s":"A man is recently diagnosed with a Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome, and his treatment of choice is Octreotide. Octreotide is an analog of an important hormone that is formed in the D cells of the pancreatic islets. What is true with regards to this hormone?","o":["A. It works through holocrine secretion.","B. It is inhibited by acid production.","C. It inhibits mostly everything including gastric acid secretion, gallbladder secretion, pancreatic secretion, insulin release, and glucagon release.","D. It promotes the production of insulin release"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It inhibits mostly everything including gastric acid secretion, gallbladder secretion, pancreatic secretion, insulin release, and glucagon release.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Octreotide copies <b>somatostatin</b>, made by D (delta) cells of the gut and pancreatic islets. Somatostatin <b>inhibits nearly everything</b>: gastric acid, gastrin, gallbladder and pancreatic secretion, insulin and glucagon.<br>A. It works by paracrine (and endocrine) release, not holocrine secretion, which destroys the whole cell.<br>B. Acid does not inhibit it; low pH stimulates its release.<br>D. It lowers insulin release; it does not raise it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)","L54 slide 13 (Diagrams MISleading!)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":29,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":77,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":77,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":52,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"55"},{"id":"D036-76-1","s":"Which of the following is the main inhibitory secretion of the GI tract?","o":["A. Somatostatin","B. Histamine","C. VIP","D. Acetylcholine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Somatostatin</b> is the main inhibitory gut signal. D cells release it, and it <b>inhibits the other gut hormones</b> and <b>gastric acid</b>.<br>B. Histamine: from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells; it increases acid.<br>C. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP): a neurocrine that relaxes smooth muscle and increases intestinal and pancreatic secretion.<br>D. Acetylcholine (ACh): increases motility and salivary, gastric and pancreatic secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":76,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-77-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 53"},{"id":"D015-83-1","s":"WHICH PARACRINE INHIBITS SECRETION OF OTHER GI HORMONES?","o":["A. SOMATOSTATIN","B. HISTAMINE","C. SUBSTANCE P","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"SOMATOSTATIN","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Somatostatin</b>, a paracrine from D cells, <b>inhibits the release of the other gut hormones</b> and gastric acid.<br>B. Histamine: a paracrine that stimulates acid from parietal cells.<br>C. Substance P: a neurocrine that contracts gut smooth muscle; it is not an inhibitory paracrine.<br>D. All of the above: wrong, because histamine and substance P are stimulators.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":83,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-77-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 54"},{"id":"G066-86-1","s":"What is the role of the paracrine hormone somatostatin in the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["A. Stimulates H+ secretion from gastric parietal cells","B. Decreases appetite","C. Inhibits secretion of the other GI hormones and inhibits gastric H+ secretion","D. Stimulates secretion of other GI hormones and inhibits gastric H+ secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inhibits secretion of the other GI hormones and inhibits gastric H+ secretion","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Somatostatin, a paracrine from D cells, <b>inhibits the release of the other gut hormones</b> and <b>inhibits gastric acid (hydrogen ion) secretion</b>.<br>A. Stimulating acid from parietal cells is histamine's job.<br>B. Decreasing appetite: done by leptin, insulin and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), not somatostatin.<br>D. Somatostatin inhibits the other hormones; it does not stimulate them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 16 (Paracrines)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":86,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":86,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-77-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 55"},{"id":"D060-67-1","s":"A new drug was created to treat Gastritis. Which of the following hormones would be the most similar to the drug?","o":["A. Somatostatin","B. Histamine","C. CCK","D. Motilin","E. GLP-1"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Somatostatin</b> is the gut's <b>inhibitory paracrine</b> from D cells. It blocks gastrin and histamine release and directly lowers acid, which is what a gastritis drug should do.<br>B. Histamine: raises acid through parietal-cell histamine H2 receptors; H2 blockers treat gastritis.<br>C. Cholecystokinin (CCK): contracts the gallbladder and releases pancreatic enzymes.<br>D. Motilin: drives fasting gut contractions.<br>E. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1): mainly raises insulin; its acid-lowering effect is minor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 5 (GI Paracrines)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":67,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-77-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C2 extra 56"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":13,"label":"Histamine raises acid via H2 receptors","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: histamine on the H2 receptor raises cAMP and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Gland table: ECL cells release histamine, which stimulates acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"G065-68-1","s":"Which of the following is correct about histamine?","o":["A. Stimulates release of acid from gastric parietal cells","B. Stimulates G cells to secrete gastrin","C. Inhibits the release of secretin to inhibit pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","D. Works at the H1 receptor on gastric parietal cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulates release of acid from gastric parietal cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Histamine, from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, is a paracrine that <b>stimulates acid from parietal cells</b> through histamine H2 receptors, raising cyclic adenosine monophosphate.<br>B. Gastrin stimulates ECL cells to release histamine; histamine does not stimulate G cells.<br>C. Histamine has no effect on secretin or pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>D. The parietal-cell receptor is H2, which H2 blockers (cimetidine, famotidine) block; H1 receptors mediate allergy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 17 (Paracrines)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":68,"f":"G065"}],"tier":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":13,"label":"Actions of gastrin, CCK, secretin, GIP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions: gallbladder, sphincter of Oddi, pancreatic enzymes, slows emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G040-13.jpg","cap":"Secretin actions: pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, gastrin inhibition","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: cell, stimuli and actions of the four GI hormones","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-71-1","s":"5. Which of the following is true?","o":["A. CCK = pancreatic enzyme secretion, contraction of the Gallbladder, and relax the Sphincter of Oddi","B. Secretin = secretion of bicarb and bile; decrease acid secretion","C. GIP = insulin secretion","D. A and C","E. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All of the above.</b><br>A. True: cholecystokinin (CCK) releases pancreatic enzymes, contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.<br>B. True: secretin releases pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin, so acid falls.<br>C. True: glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) raises insulin; this is why oral glucose is cleared faster than intravenous glucose.<br>D. A and C: incomplete, because B is also true.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":71,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 57"},{"id":"G066-77-1","s":"Match the following gastrointestinal hormones with their corresponding actions. 1. Gastrin 2. CCK 3. Secretin 4. GIP","o":["A. promote H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells and stimulate growth of the gastric mucosa","B. Stimulate insulin secretion by pancreatic B-cells in response to glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids","C. Promote fat digestion and absorption","D. Promotes secretion of pancreatic and biliary HCO3-, which neutralizes H+ in lumen of small intestine → essential for fat digestion"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gastrin → A (H+ secretion, gastric mucosal growth); CCK → C (fat digestion and absorption); Secretin → D (pancreatic and biliary HCO3-); GIP → B (insulin secretion)","a":"1-A, 2-C, 3-D, 4-B","why":"<b>Gastrin → A</b>: raises acid (hydrogen ion) from parietal cells and grows the gastric mucosa.<br><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK) → C</b>: promotes fat digestion and absorption (gallbladder contraction, pancreatic enzymes, slower gastric emptying).<br><b>Secretin → D</b>: releases pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate to neutralize acid in the duodenum.<br><b>Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) → B</b>: raises insulin from pancreatic beta cells.<br>Common mix-up: CCK answers fat; secretin answers acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":77,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":77,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C2 extra 58"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":13,"label":"Acid, sugar, fat, stretch: secretin, GIP, CCK, gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-12.jpg","cap":"Secretin is released by acid in the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G040-14.jpg","cap":"GIP is released by carbohydrates, amino acids and fatty acids","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: stimuli for gastrin, CCK, secretin and GIP side by side","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-4-1","s":"Acid, sugar, fat and distension are the most potent stimuli, respectively, for the release of which hormones?","o":["a. Gastrin, CCK, Motilin, Secretin","b. Gastrin, GIP, CCK, Secretin","c. Histamine, GIP, Motilin, CCK","d. Secretin, GIP, CCK, Gastrin","e. Secretin, CCK, GIP, Gastrin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin, GIP, CCK, Gastrin","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Acid → secretin</b> (S cells); <b>sugar → glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> (K cells); <b>fat → cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> (I cells); <b>distention → gastrin</b> (G cells).<br>A. Puts gastrin first, but acid inhibits gastrin.<br>B. Same error: gastrin for acid, and secretin for distention.<br>C. Histamine is not a hormone, and motilin does not respond to fat.<br>E. Swaps GIP and CCK: sugar releases GIP and fat releases CCK.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 27 (Q5. Which of the following is stimulated most by acid?)","L51B slide 31 (Q7. Which of the following is stimulated most by fat?)","L51B slide 35 (Q9. Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":4,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":1,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":4,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":86,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":4,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":14,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"57"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":14,"label":"Skeletal: pharynx, upper esophagus, external anal sphincter","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-3.jpg","cap":"Skeletal muscle exceptions: pharynx, upper third of esophagus, anal sphincter","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D004-141.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: skeletal muscle in the upper part, smooth muscle below","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 141"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-26-1","s":"Which of the following areas of the GI tract has voluntary control?","o":["A. Small intestine","B. Upper ⅓ of the esophagus","C. Lower ⅔ of the esophagus","D. Internal anal sphincter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Upper ⅓ of the esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Upper third of the esophagus.</b> Voluntary control needs <b>skeletal muscle</b>. The GI (gastrointestinal) tract has skeletal muscle in only three places: the <b>pharynx, upper third of the esophagus and external anal sphincter</b>.<br>A. Small intestine: smooth muscle, involuntary.<br>C. Lower two-thirds of the esophagus: smooth muscle, involuntary.<br>D. Internal anal sphincter: smooth muscle; the external sphincter is the voluntary one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 3 (Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":26,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":45,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":45,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":213,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"58"},{"id":"D036-39-1","s":"Which of the following is composed of skeletal muscle?","o":["A. Circular muscle","B. Longitudinal muscle","C. External anal sphincter","D. Epiglottis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"External anal sphincter","a":"C","why":"<b>C. External anal sphincter.</b> GI (gastrointestinal) muscle is smooth except at three skeletal sites: the <b>pharynx, upper third of the esophagus and external anal sphincter</b>. These are under voluntary control.<br>A. Circular muscle: smooth muscle layer that narrows the tube.<br>B. Longitudinal muscle: smooth muscle layer that shortens the tube.<br>D. Epiglottis: a flap of elastic cartilage, not muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 3 (Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":39,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-26-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":14,"label":"Gut muscle is unitary smooth muscle (gap junctions)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-3.jpg","cap":"All GI muscle is smooth muscle except three skeletal sites","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D004-109.jpg","cap":"Slow-wave slide listing syncytium, unitary smooth muscle and gap junctions","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 109"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-63-1","s":"The majority of the contractile tissue in the GI system can best be described as","o":["A. Striated Muscle","B. Unitary Smooth Muscle","C. Skeletal Muscle","D. Multi-unit Smooth Muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Unitary Smooth Muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Unitary smooth muscle.</b> Almost all GI (gastrointestinal) muscle is smooth muscle whose cells are linked by <b>gap junctions</b>, so they fire together as one unit.<br>A. Striated muscle: only the pharynx, upper third of the esophagus and external anal sphincter.<br>C. Skeletal muscle: the same three sites only.<br>D. Multi-unit smooth muscle: cells work separately with few gap junctions (for example, the iris); not the gut wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 3 (Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":63,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-70-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 2"},{"id":"D060-70-1","s":"What allows cell-to-cell spread of smooth muscle in the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["A. Tight junctions","B. Gap Junctions","C. Sodium Channels","D. Desmosomes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gap Junctions","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gap junctions.</b> GI (gastrointestinal) smooth muscle is <b>unitary</b>. <b>Gap junctions</b> let current pass straight from cell to cell, so a slow wave spreads and a whole segment contracts together.<br>A. Tight junctions: seal the epithelium; they do not carry current.<br>C. Sodium channels: carry current across one membrane, not between cells.<br>D. Desmosomes: hold cells together mechanically; no electrical link.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":70,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"59"},{"id":"D067-38-1","s":"What feature of smooth muscle allows for rapid cell to cell spread of action potentials in the GI tract?","o":["A. Interstitial cells of Cajal","B. Sphincters","C. Slow waves","D. Haustra","E. Gap junctions"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gap junctions","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Gap junctions.</b> Gut smooth muscle is unitary. <b>Gap junctions</b> are low-resistance channels between neighboring cells, so an action potential passes straight from cell to cell and a whole segment contracts together.<br>A. Interstitial cells of Cajal: the pacemakers where slow waves start.<br>B. Sphincters: rings of circular muscle that separate two regions.<br>C. Slow waves: the rhythm that sets when action potentials can fire, not the path between cells.<br>D. Haustra: the pouches of the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":38,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-70-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":14,"label":"Circular mixes, longitudinal propels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-4.jpg","cap":"Circular narrows and mixes; longitudinal shortens, drives peristalsis and mass movement","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G039-31.jpg","cap":"Peristalsis propels the bolus; segmentation by circular muscle mixes in place","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G039-5.jpg","cap":"GI wall layers with inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle labelled","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-24-1","s":"Which of the following is the function of the longitudinal muscle of the GI tract?","o":["A. Mixing of luminal substances","B. Regulating blood flow","C. Absorption and secretions","D. Peristaltic movement"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peristaltic movement","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Peristaltic movement.</b> The <b>longitudinal</b> layer <b>shortens the tube</b> and drives <b>peristalsis and mass movements</b>.<br>A. Mixing: done by the circular layer, which narrows the tube (segmentation).<br>B. Blood flow: set by vessels and their nerves, not by the gut muscle layers.<br>C. Absorption and secretion: jobs of the mucosa (epithelium), not the muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":24,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":42,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":42,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":210,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"60"},{"id":"D015-96-1","s":"SEGMENTATION CONTRACTIONS ARE DONE BY WHICH TYPE(S) OF MUSCLES?","o":["A. CIRCULAR MUSCLES","B. LONGITUDINAL MUSCLES","C. OBLIQUE MUSCLES","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"CIRCULAR MUSCLES","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Circular muscles.</b> <b>Segmentation</b> is a <b>mixing</b> movement. Rings of <b>circular muscle</b> squeeze and split the chyme back and forth with no forward push.<br>B. Longitudinal muscles: shorten the tube and drive peristalsis (propulsion).<br>C. Oblique muscles: an extra layer found only in the stomach, for churning.<br>D. All of the above: only circular muscle segments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":96,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 4"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":14,"label":"Phasic in antrum; tonic in sphincters","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-21.jpg","cap":"Thick-walled antrum churns food with strong rhythmic contractions","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Gastric mixing contractions follow the 3-per-minute basic electrical rhythm","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-41-1","s":"Where do phasic contractions occur?","o":["A. Lower esophageal sphincter","B. Orad stomach","C. Gastric antrum","D. Ileocecal sphincter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric antrum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastric antrum.</b> <b>Phasic</b> contractions (contract, then relax) occur where food is mixed and moved: the esophagus, gastric antrum and small intestine. <b>Tonic</b> contractions (steady tone) keep sphincters and storage areas closed.<br>A. LES (lower esophageal sphincter): tonic.<br>B. Orad stomach: tonic.<br>D. Ileocecal sphincter: tonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":41,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"61"},{"id":"D040-65-1","s":"2) Where does phasic contraction take place in the GI system?","o":["A. Lower Esophagus","B. Upper region of stomach","C. Ileocecal","D. Gastric Antrum"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric Antrum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastric antrum (and A. lower esophagus).</b> <b>Phasic</b> contractions (contract, then relax) occur in the <b>esophagus, gastric antrum and small intestine</b>. The smooth-muscle lower esophagus contracts phasically in each peristaltic wave; only its <b>sphincter</b>, the LES (lower esophageal sphincter), is tonic.<br>B. Upper (orad) stomach: tonic, for storage.<br>C. Ileocecal sphincter: tonic.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only the gastric antrum; the lower esophagus (its smooth-muscle body) also makes phasic peristaltic contractions, and only the lower esophageal sphincter is tonic, so A and D are both correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 21 (Antral wall is thicker than the body)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":65,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":14,"label":"Pyloric sphincter: stomach → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-22.jpg","cap":"Labelled stomach: pyloric sphincter between pyloric antrum and duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G039-20.jpg","cap":"Stomach regions from fundus down to antrum, pylorus and duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-92-1","s":"The pyloric sphincter separates the ___________ from the _______________.","o":["A. Esophagus; stomach","B. Ileum; cecum","C. Stomach; duodenum","D. Pharynx; upper esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach; duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach and duodenum.</b> The <b>pyloric sphincter</b> sits at the end of the antrum and controls emptying into the duodenum.<br>A. Esophagus and stomach: the LES (lower esophageal sphincter).<br>B. Ileum and cecum: the ileocecal sphincter.<br>D. Pharynx and esophagus: the UES (upper esophageal sphincter).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 18 (Q 2)","L50 slide 20 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":92,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":91,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":15,"label":"Slow waves are not action potentials","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-6.jpg","cap":"Slow waves: oscillating depolarization and repolarization; action potentials at the top","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/D048-28.jpg","cap":"Slow waves with action-potential bursts on their peaks, then tension","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-67-1","s":"3) Which of the following best described slow waves?","o":["A. Oscillating action potentials","B. Slow, prolonged depolarization and repolarization","C. Oscillating depolarization and repolarization","D. Slow, spontaneous action potentials"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Oscillating depolarization and repolarization","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Oscillating depolarization and repolarization.</b> Slow waves are rhythmic rises and falls of the smooth-muscle membrane potential. They are <b>not action potentials</b>; an action potential fires only when a wave's peak reaches threshold.<br>A. Oscillating action potentials: slow waves are not action potentials.<br>B. Slow, prolonged depolarization: they repeat in cycles; they are not one long shift.<br>D. Spontaneous action potentials: again, slow waves are not action potentials.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":67,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"62"},{"id":"D036-43-1","s":"Which of the following is false regarding slow waves?","o":["A. Slow waves are the same as action potentials","B. Slow waves are oscillating depolarization and repolarization of membrane potential of smooth muscle cells","C. The frequency of slow waves varies along the GI tract","D. The frequency of slow waves is highest in the duodenum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Slow waves are the same as action potentials","a":"A","why":"<b>A is false.</b> Slow waves are <b>not action potentials</b>. They are oscillating depolarization and repolarization of the smooth-muscle membrane. Action potentials fire <b>on top</b> of a slow wave only when it reaches threshold.<br>B. True: that is the definition.<br>C. True: stomach three per minute, ileum eight, duodenum twelve.<br>D. True: the duodenum is fastest at twelve per minute.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":43,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-67-1","kind":"harder","n":"C3 extra 7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":15,"label":"Interstitial cells of Cajal = gut pacemakers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-6.jpg","cap":"Slow waves come from pacemaker Cajal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/D004-110.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic nervous system diagram: ICC (Cajal cells) at the myenteric plexus","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 110"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-28-1","s":"Which of the following cells have synonymous function to the SA node of the heart?","o":["A. ECL cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Interstitial cells of Cajal","D. Alpha cells in Pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC).</b> Like the <b>SA (sinoatrial) node</b> of the heart, they are <b>pacemakers</b>. They fire rhythmic slow waves that set the rhythm of GI (gastrointestinal) smooth muscle.<br>A. ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells: release histamine to drive acid secretion.<br>B. Parietal cells: secrete acid and intrinsic factor.<br>D. Pancreatic alpha cells: secrete glucagon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":28,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":47,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":47,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":215,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"63"},{"id":"D040-69-1","s":"4) Which cells are considered the “pacemaker” of the GI tract?","o":["A. Parietal Cells","B. Interstitial cells of Cajal","C. Chief Cells","D. SA Node","E. Enterocytes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC).</b> They are the <b>pacemakers of the gut</b>. They generate the slow waves that set the maximum rate of contraction.<br>A. Parietal cells: make acid (hydrochloric acid) and intrinsic factor.<br>C. Chief cells: make pepsinogen.<br>D. SA (sinoatrial) node: the pacemaker of the heart, not the gut.<br>E. Enterocytes: absorptive cells lining the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":69,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 8"},{"id":"D015-89-1","s":"WHERE DO SLOW WAVES ORIGINATE FROM?","o":["A. PYLORIC SPHINCTER","B. PANCREAS","C. INTERSTITIAL CELLS OF CAJAL","D. SUBMUCOSAL PLEXUS"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"INTERSTITIAL CELLS OF CAJAL","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC).</b> Slow waves start in the ICC, the <b>pacemaker cells</b> of the gut, which sit mainly in the <b>myenteric plexus</b> layer.<br>A. Pyloric sphincter: a muscle ring between stomach and duodenum, not a pacemaker.<br>B. Pancreas: a gland that makes enzymes and hormones.<br>D. Submucosal plexus: controls secretion and local blood flow; the pacing ICC are in the myenteric layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":89,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 9"},{"id":"G066-97-1","s":"Where do you find the interstitial cells of Cajal in the GI tract and what is their function?","o":["A. Myenteric plexus; Secretion of gastrin","B. Submucosal plexus; Feeding center","C. Myenteric plexus; pacemakers","D. Submucosal plexus; decrease appetite"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric plexus; pacemakers","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Myenteric plexus; pacemakers.</b> The ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) lie mainly in the <b>myenteric plexus</b>, between the circular and longitudinal muscle. They act as the <b>pacemakers</b> that generate slow waves.<br>A. Gastrin: secreted by G cells in the gastric antrum.<br>B. Feeding center: in the hypothalamus; and ICC are not in the submucosal plexus.<br>D. Appetite: controlled by the brain and hormones, not by ICC.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":97,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":96,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 10"},{"id":"D060-74-1","s":"What controls the frequency of contractions in the GI tract?","o":["A. Parietal Cells","B. Myenteric Plexus","C. Chief Cells","D. Inward Na+ current"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric Plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Myenteric plexus.</b> The pacemaker ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) sit in the <b>myenteric plexus</b> region. Their <b>slow waves</b> set how often action potentials, and so contractions, can occur.<br>A. Parietal cells: secrete acid and intrinsic factor.<br>C. Chief cells: secrete pepsinogen.<br>D. Inward sodium current: the slow-wave upstroke is <b>calcium entering</b> the cell; potassium leaving repolarizes it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":74,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":15,"label":"Calcium in depolarizes, potassium out repolarizes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-6.jpg","cap":"Calcium in depolarizes, potassium out repolarizes the slow wave","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G048-13.jpg","cap":"Basic electrical rhythm: depolarization by calcium, repolarization by potassium","where":"2025 discussion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-52-1","s":"What causes the depolarization phase of the slow wave?","o":["A. Outward current of Na+","B. Inward current of Ca2+","C. Outward current of Ca2+","D. Inward current of K+","E. Outward current of K+"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inward current of Ca2+","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inward calcium current.</b> In a slow wave, <b>calcium flows in to depolarize</b> the membrane and <b>potassium flows out to repolarize</b> it.<br>A. Outward sodium current: positive charge leaving would hyperpolarize, not depolarize.<br>C. Outward calcium current: calcium enters; positive charge leaving would hyperpolarize.<br>D. Inward potassium current: potassium flows out, not in.<br>E. Outward potassium current: this is the repolarizing downstroke.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":52,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"64"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":15,"label":"Duodenum has the fastest slow waves (12/min)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-6.jpg","cap":"Slow-wave rates: stomach 3, ileum 8, duodenum 12 per minute","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G048-13.jpg","cap":"Basic electrical rhythm figure with regional frequencies; duodenum 12 per minute","where":"2025 discussion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-28-1","s":"Which part of the bowel contract most frequently?","o":["a. Stomach","b. Duodenum","c. Jejunum","d. Ileum","e. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> A contraction can only happen at the peak of a slow wave, so the fastest slow waves give the most frequent contractions. The <b>duodenum is fastest at twelve per minute</b>; the ileum runs eight and the stomach three.<br>A. Stomach: three per minute, the slowest.<br>C. Jejunum: slower than the duodenum; the rate falls along the small bowel.<br>D. Ileum: about eight per minute.<br>E. Colon: about eight per minute.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":28,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"65"},{"id":"D024-34-1","s":"Which portion of the GI tract has the highest rate of slow wave frequency?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Slow-wave frequency is set by the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) and differs by region: <b>duodenum twelve per minute</b> (highest), <b>ileum eight</b>, <b>stomach three</b> (lowest).<br>A. Stomach: three per minute, the lowest.<br>C. Jejunum: lower than the duodenum; the rate falls going down the small bowel.<br>D. Ileum: about eight per minute.<br>E. Colon: about eight per minute.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":34,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":54,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 12"},{"id":"D060-72-1","s":"Which portion of the GI tract has the highest rate of slow waves?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Stomach","C. Ascending Colon","D. Descending Colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Duodenum.</b> It has the <b>fastest slow waves, twelve per minute</b>. The <b>stomach is slowest at three per minute</b>, and the ileum runs about eight.<br>B. Stomach: three per minute, the slowest.<br>C. Ascending colon: slower than the duodenum.<br>D. Descending colon: slower than the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":72,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 13"},{"id":"D015-87-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING PRODUCES A SLOW WAVE OF 12 PER MINUTE?","o":["A. STOMACH","B. DUODENUM","C. COLON","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"DUODENUM","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Slow waves run at <b>twelve per minute in the duodenum</b>, about eight per minute in the ileum and <b>three per minute in the stomach</b>.<br>A. Stomach: three per minute, the slowest.<br>C. Colon: slower than the duodenum.<br>D. All of the above: only the duodenum reaches twelve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":87,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 14"},{"id":"D010-56-1","s":"Which statement is true with regards to the pacemaker “Cajal” cells?","o":["A. Action potentials are harder to occur at the top of the depolarization wave.","B. Calcium is responsible for the repolarization phase.","C. The fastest frequency of these pacemaker cells reside within the duodenum.","D. Potassium is responsible for the depolarization phase."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The fastest frequency of these pacemaker cells reside within the duodenum.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fastest in the duodenum.</b> The ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) pace the duodenum at <b>twelve slow waves per minute</b> and the stomach at only three.<br>A. False: action potentials are <b>easier</b> at the top of a slow wave, because the membrane is closest to threshold there.<br>B. False: <b>calcium entering depolarizes</b>; it does not repolarize.<br>D. False: <b>potassium leaving repolarizes</b>; it does not depolarize.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":56,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":56,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":31,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 15"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":16,"label":"Slow wave → action potential → contraction","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-6.jpg","cap":"At the top of a slow wave an action potential can occur","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/D048-28.jpg","cap":"Action-potential bursts on slow-wave peaks are followed by muscle tension","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-46-1","s":"You are explaining how slow waves work to a fellow medical student. You make it really simple and say: first (1) happens, then (2) and then (3).","o":["A. (1) action potential (2) contraction (3) slow wave","B. (1) slow wave (2) action potential (3) contraction","C. (1) slow wave (2) contraction (3) action potential","D. (1) action potential (2) slow wave (3) contraction","E. (1) contraction (2) action potential (3) slow wave"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"(1) slow wave (2) action potential (3) contraction","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Slow wave, then action potential, then contraction.</b> The slow wave comes first. If its peak reaches <b>threshold</b>, <b>action potentials</b> fire on top of it. They let calcium in, and the muscle <b>contracts</b>.<br>A. The slow wave cannot come last; it is the trigger.<br>C. Contraction follows the action potentials, not the reverse.<br>D. Action potentials ride on a slow wave; they do not start it.<br>E. Contraction is the final step, not the first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":46,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 16"},{"id":"D066-34-1","s":"You are studying gastrointestinal electrophysiology in a physiology lab. Intracellular recordings from the circular muscle layer of the distal stomach display rhythmic, spontaneous membrane potential depolarizations occurring at approximately 3 cycles per minute. However, mechanical tension transducers register no actual muscular contraction during these baseline oscillations. Which of the following statements best explains why mechanical contraction is absent during these baseline electrical events?","o":["A. Action potentials only occur when slow wave depolarizations exceed the electrical threshold and trigger voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","B. Smooth muscle cells are multi-unit and require individual autonomic nerve stimulation to contract","C. Interstitial cells of Cajal inhibit calcium influx during phase 0 of the slow wave cycle","D. Tonic contractions in the distal stomach replace phasic contractions during electrical baseline periods","E. Slow waves driven by inward Na+ currents that do not engage actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Action potentials only occur when slow wave depolarizations exceed the electrical threshold and trigger voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Action potentials fire only when a slow wave crosses threshold.</b> Slow waves are swings of membrane potential, not action potentials. Only when a wave's peak reaches <b>threshold</b> do voltage-gated calcium (Ca2+) channels open, spikes fire and the muscle contracts. Below threshold there is rhythm (three per minute in the stomach) without contraction.<br>B. Gut muscle is unitary, joined by gap junctions.<br>C. Interstitial cells of Cajal generate the slow waves; they do not block calcium.<br>D. The distal stomach contracts phasically, not tonically.<br>E. The upstroke is calcium entry, not sodium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":34,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"66"},{"id":"F1212-93-1","s":"Which of the following is correct about slow waves?","o":["A. Slow waves are derived from slowly paced action potentials","B. Slow wave frequency ranges normally from 15-30 waves per minute","C. Slow waves with bursts of action potentials on the plateau phase result in tension.","D. Slows waves in duodenum have the slowest rate","E. Slows waves in the stomach have the fastest rate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Slow waves with bursts of action potentials on the plateau phase result in tension.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Bursts of action potentials on the plateau cause tension.</b> When a slow wave's plateau reaches threshold, a burst of <b>action potentials</b> fires and the muscle <b>contracts</b>, so tension rises.<br>A. False: slow waves are not action potentials.<br>B. False: the normal range is three to twelve per minute.<br>D. False: the duodenum is the fastest (twelve per minute).<br>E. False: the stomach is the slowest (three per minute).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":93,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 17"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":16,"label":"Nerves change force, not slow-wave frequency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Vagal stimulation increases force, not frequency; gastric rhythm 3 per minute","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G048-13.jpg","cap":"Slow-wave frequency not affected by nerves, but contraction strength is","where":"2025 discussion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-25-1","s":"The existence of rhythmic depolarizations of the membrane (slow waves) in smooth muscle cells of the stomach and intestine requires which of the following?","o":["A. Intact parasympathetic innervation","B. Intact sympathetic innervation","C. Innervation by the enteric nervous system","D. None of the above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"None of the above","a":"D","why":"<b>D. None of the above.</b> Slow waves are <b>intrinsic</b>. The ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) make them on their own, and <b>gap junctions</b> spread them. They continue after the nerves are cut or blocked. Nerves only change the <b>force</b> of contraction.<br>A. Parasympathetic: increases force; not needed for slow waves.<br>B. Sympathetic: decreases force; not needed for slow waves.<br>C. Enteric nervous system: ICC are not neurons, and slow waves persist when enteric nerves are blocked.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":25,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":22,"f":"D003"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 18"},{"id":"D022-9-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the basic electrical rhythm (slow waves) in the GI tract?","o":["a. It is faster (i.e., higher frequency) in the stomach than the duodenum","b. It is increased in frequency by ACh","c. It is inhibited by GIP in the ileum","d. It is inhibited in the small intestines by CCK","e. None of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"None of the above","a":"e","why":"<b>E. None of the above.</b> The <b>basic electrical rhythm</b> (slow waves) is set by the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal). Nerves and hormones change the <b>force</b> of contraction, <b>not the slow-wave frequency</b>.<br>A. False: the stomach (three per minute) is slower than the duodenum (twelve per minute).<br>B. False: ACh (acetylcholine) increases force, not frequency.<br>C. False: GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) does not inhibit the rhythm.<br>D. False: CCK (cholecystokinin) does not inhibit the rhythm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":29,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":9,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":23,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":105,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":20,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"67"},{"id":"D022-10-1","s":"What will happen to slow waves frequency and smooth muscle contractions in duodenum if norepinephrine is added to the vicinity of interstitial cells of Cajal.","o":["a. Slow wave frequency will increase, and smooth muscle contractions will be same","b. Slow wave frequency will decrease, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased as well","c. Both are not affected by norepinephrine release","d. Slow wave frequency will not change, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Slow wave frequency will not change, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Slow-wave frequency unchanged; contractions decreased.</b> The slow-wave rate is fixed by the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal). <b>NE (norepinephrine)</b> hyperpolarizes the smooth muscle, so fewer action potentials ride on each wave and <b>contractions weaken</b>.<br>A. Nerves and transmitters do not raise slow-wave frequency.<br>B. Frequency does not fall; only the contractions do.<br>C. Contractions are affected: sympathetic input slows GI (gastrointestinal) motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":31,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":10,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":107,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"68"},{"id":"G061-8-1","s":"Phasic Contractions in the stomach depends upon:","o":["A. Gap junctions between smooth muscle cells.","B. Coordination of the phases of the basic electrical rhythms in different parts of the stomach by the vagus.","C. Entry of calcium ions into smooth muscle cells from the extracellular fluid.","D. All of the above.","E. Only a and c are correct."],"g":["E"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above.","a":"D","why":"<b>E. Only a and c.</b> Stomach contractions need <b>gap junctions</b> to spread each slow wave from cell to cell, and <b>calcium entry</b> from outside the cell to depolarize and contract. The rhythm comes from the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal), <b>not the vagus</b>.<br>A. True, but calcium entry is also needed.<br>B. False: the vagus changes contraction force, not the timing of the slow waves.<br>C. True, but gap junctions are also needed.<br>D. Wrong because b is false (the old key chose D).","ex":"","fl":["Statement b is false: slow-wave (BER) frequency and phase coordination are myogenic (interstitial cells of Cajal + gap-junction coupling) and do not depend on the vagus; the vagus changes contraction force, not BER frequency (G039:27). The same review's Q6 (slide 26) keys that slow waves need no extrinsic or enteric innervation. So only a and c are correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":8,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":8,"f":"D003"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 19"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":17,"label":"Pharyngeal phase: epiglottis covers larynx","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-9.jpg","cap":"Swallowing is a medullary reflex; epiglottis moves down to cover the larynx","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-46-1","s":"During which phase of swallowing is breathing inhibited?","o":["A. Oral phase","B. Pharyngeal phase","C. Esophageal phase","D. Duodenal phase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pharyngeal phase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pharyngeal phase.</b> As the bolus passes the pharynx, the <b>epiglottis moves down to cover the larynx</b> and breathing briefly stops, so food does not enter the airway. This phase is a medullary reflex.<br>A. Oral phase: voluntary; the tongue pushes the bolus back while breathing continues.<br>C. Esophageal phase: the bolus is already past the airway.<br>D. Duodenal phase: not a phase of swallowing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 9 (Chewing and Swallowing)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":46,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"69"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":17,"label":"Primary peristalsis vagal; secondary wave enteric","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-10.jpg","cap":"Primary peristalsis follows the swallow; secondary wave is automatic, enteric","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D033-28.jpg","cap":"Primary wave, then secondary wave starting at the point of distention","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-22-1","s":"After a complete vagus nerve removal, which of the following functions will be fully abolished in the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["a. Gastrin release","b. Histamine release","c. Slow waves","d. Primary peristalsis","e. Swallowing"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Primary peristalsis","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Primary peristalsis.</b> The primary wave follows a swallow and is run by the medullary swallowing center <b>through the vagus</b>, so cutting the vagus abolishes it. The <b>secondary wave</b> is run by the enteric nervous system and can still occur.<br>A. Gastrin: G cells also respond to amino acids and peptides in the stomach.<br>B. Histamine: ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells still respond to gastrin.<br>C. Slow waves: made by ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal), with no nerves needed.<br>E. Swallowing: it starts voluntarily (tongue, hypoglossal nerve), so it is impaired, not fully lost.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":26,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":22,"f":"D022"}],"tier":1,"n":"70"},{"id":"G063-8-36","s":"Secondary esophageal peristalsis","o":["a. Is preceded by an oral-pharyngeal phase of swallowing","b. Involves activation of medullary swallowing centers","c. Is accompanied by lower esophageal sphincter relaxation","d. Occurs in both the skeletal and smooth muscle portions of the esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Is accompanied by lower esophageal sphincter relaxation","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Accompanied by LES relaxation.</b> A secondary wave starts where leftover food <b>stretches the esophagus</b> and is run by the <b>enteric nervous system</b>. When it reaches the LES (lower esophageal sphincter), the sphincter relaxes to let the food into the stomach.<br>A. No swallow comes first; distention triggers it.<br>B. It does not need the medullary swallowing center; that center drives the primary wave.<br>D. The enteric system drives smooth muscle; the skeletal upper third needs motor commands from the brainstem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"71"},{"id":"G049-1-1","s":"CASE 43 Difficulty in Swallowing: Achalasia. Jim Booker, a 49-year-old insurance broker, is having digestive problems. He has difficulty swallowing both solid foods and liquids, and occasionally he regurgitates. The problem is most noticeable when he is under stress, especially when he eats too fast. Jim says that it feels like food is “stuck” in his esophagus and will not go down. Without intending to, he lost 10 pounds in the past 2 months. His wife was especially worried about the weight loss and insisted that Jim see a physician. After doing a complete physical examination, the physician ordered several tests, including a barium swallow and esophageal manometry. After the testing, he was diagnosed with achalasia, a disease characterized by impaired peristalsis in the distal two-thirds of the esophagus and failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax during swallowing. The physician recommended that Jim be treated with a procedure that physically dilates the LES. QUESTION: What is the role of esophageal peristalsis in normal swallowing?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Primary peristalsis (swallowing reflex, medullary center) propels the bolus caudally by sequential contractions behind it; if it fails, a secondary peristaltic wave triggered by distention and mediated by the enteric nervous system clears the esophagus.","a":null,"why":"<b>Peristalsis carries the bolus from the pharynx to the stomach.</b> The <b>primary wave</b> starts with the swallow and is run by the medullary swallowing center through the vagus. Each segment contracts behind the bolus and pushes it down. Food left behind stretches the wall and starts a <b>secondary wave</b>, run by the <b>enteric nervous system</b>, that clears the esophagus. The LES (lower esophageal sphincter) relaxes to let the bolus into the stomach.<br>Common mix-up: a secondary wave needs no swallow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L50 slide 9 (Chewing and Swallowing)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 2 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G049"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 2 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G049"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 2 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G049"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-22-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 20"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":17,"label":"Swallow sequence: UES, primary, LES, secondary","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-10.jpg","cap":"Esophageal motility: primary wave, VIP opens LES, then secondary wave","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D023-33.jpg","cap":"Numbered swallow steps: UES opens, primary wave, LES opens, secondary wave","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-48-1","s":"What is the correct sequence of esophageal motility?","o":["A. Upper esophageal sphincter opens → lower esophageal sphincter opens → primary peristaltic contraction → secondary peristaltic contraction","B. Upper esophageal sphincter opens → primary peristaltic contraction → lower esophageal sphincter opens → secondary peristaltic contraction","C. Primary peristaltic contraction → secondary peristaltic contraction → upper esophageal sphincter opens → lower esophageal sphincter opens","D. Primary peristaltic contraction → upper esophageal sphincter opens → secondary peristaltic contraction → lower esophageal sphincter opens"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Upper esophageal sphincter opens → primary peristaltic contraction → lower esophageal sphincter opens → secondary peristaltic contraction","a":"B","why":"<b>B. UES opens, primary contraction, LES opens, secondary contraction.</b> The UES (upper esophageal sphincter) relaxes to let the bolus in, then closes. The <b>primary wave</b> pushes the bolus down. As it nears the stomach, <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) from the vagus opens the LES</b> (lower esophageal sphincter). A <b>secondary wave</b> follows only if food is left.<br>A. The LES opens as the bolus arrives, after the primary wave has carried it down.<br>C. The swallow begins with the UES opening, not with peristalsis.<br>D. Peristalsis cannot start before the UES lets the bolus in.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":48,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"72"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":17,"label":"VIP (and NO) from vagus open the LES","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-10.jpg","cap":"VIP opens the lower esophageal sphincter; receptive relaxation in the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G038-25.jpg","cap":"VIP relaxes sphincter smooth muscle","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D033-28.jpg","cap":"Vagal peptidergic fibers release VIP to relax the LES as the bolus arrives","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-49-1","s":"8. As a bolus approaches the lower esophageal sphincter, which of the following occurs next?","o":["A. Opening of the lower esophageal sphincter by VIP release from the vagus nerve","B. The primary peristaltic contraction","C. Opening of the lower esophageal sphincter by ACh release from the vagus nerve","D. The secondary peristaltic contraction","E. The gastroesophageal reflux"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Opening of the lower esophageal sphincter by VIP release from the vagus nerve","a":"A","why":"<b>A. VIP from the vagus opens the LES.</b> As the primary wave brings the bolus near the stomach, vagal fibers release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> and the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) relaxes.<br>B. Primary contraction: already under way; it brought the bolus there.<br>C. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts the LES, so it cannot open it.<br>D. Secondary contraction: only clears leftover food afterward.<br>E. Reflux: abnormal backflow, not a normal step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":49,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"73"},{"id":"G066-94-1","s":"What neurotransmitter is released when the lower esophageal sphincter and orad stomach are simultaneously relaxed?","o":["A. CCK","B. VIP","C. GIP","D. Secretin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP","a":"B","why":"<b>B. VIP.</b> Vagal peptidergic fibers release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b>. It opens the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) and, at the same time, relaxes the orad stomach (<b>receptive relaxation</b>).<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): from I cells; it contracts the gallbladder and slows gastric emptying.<br>C. GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide): raises insulin release.<br>D. Secretin: from S cells; it raises pancreatic bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":94,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":93,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 21"},{"id":"D040-56-1","s":"Which neurotransmitter stimulates the opening of the lower esophageal sphincter?","o":["A. Epinephrine","B. Acetylcholine","C. Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide","D. Nitric oxide","E. Neuropeptide Y"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide","a":"C","why":"<b>C. VIP and D. nitric oxide.</b> Vagal inhibitory nerves release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> and <b>NO (nitric oxide)</b>. Both relax the smooth muscle of the LES (lower esophageal sphincter), so it opens. The 2026 slide names VIP; NO is its partner, and both are lost in achalasia.<br>A. Epinephrine: sympathetic-type transmitter; it tightens sphincters.<br>B. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts the LES and keeps it closed.<br>E. Neuropeptide Y: not the LES-opening transmitter.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only VIP; nitric oxide also opens the LES (both are vagal inhibitory transmitters whose loss causes achalasia), so C and D are both correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":56,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 22"},{"id":"D007-77-1","s":"Which of the following produces relaxation in the lower esophageal sphincter?","o":["A. VIP","B. ACh","C. NO","D. NpY","E. GRP"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP","a":"A","why":"<b>A. VIP and C. NO.</b> The vagus releases <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> and <b>NO (nitric oxide)</b> onto the LES (lower esophageal sphincter). Both relax its smooth muscle so the bolus can pass. The 2026 slide names VIP; loss of the VIP and NO neurons causes achalasia.<br>B. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts the LES.<br>D. NPY (neuropeptide Y): not the LES relaxant.<br>E. GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide): releases gastrin from G cells.","ex":"","fl":["Course answer VIP matches 2026 G039:10/G038:25, but NO (option C) is also a physiologic LES relaxant (achalasia = loss of NO/VIP inhibitory neurons); for this course pick VIP."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":77,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 23"},{"id":"D008-49-1","s":"Which of the following neurotransmitters play an important role in food passing along from the esophagus to the stomach?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Serotonin","C. GABA","D. VIP"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP","a":"D","why":"<b>D. VIP.</b> When the bolus reaches the lower esophagus, vagal fibers release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b>. The LES (lower esophageal sphincter) opens and the orad stomach relaxes (<b>receptive relaxation</b>), so food passes into the stomach.<br>A. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts smooth muscle and tightens the LES.<br>B. Serotonin: starts the intestinal peristaltic reflex.<br>C. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid): a brain inhibitory transmitter, not the LES relaxant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":49,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":49,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":217,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 24"},{"id":"D060-76-1","s":"A patient presents with a history of dysphagia. Which of the following hormones is released by the vagus nerve decreasing lower esophageal sphincter tone?","o":["A. ACh","B. Histamine","C. Secretin","D. VIP"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP","a":"D","why":"<b>D. VIP.</b> Vagal fibers release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b>, which relaxes sphincter smooth muscle and <b>lowers LES (lower esophageal sphincter) tone</b> so the bolus can enter the stomach.<br>A. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts GI (gastrointestinal) smooth muscle and tightens the LES.<br>B. Histamine: from ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells; it drives acid secretion.<br>C. Secretin: a hormone from duodenal S cells; it raises pancreatic bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L49 slide 25 (VIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":76,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 25"},{"id":"G063-8-37","s":"A 55-year-old man presents with weight loss, cough, and diffuse chest pain. A chest x-ray reveals normal heart and lungs, but the radiologist detects a “bird’s beak” narrowing of the terminal esophagus, which is also seen with a barium swallow. Follow-up history indicates that the patient also has dysphagia and regurgitation. Manometry shows increased lower esophageal sphincter (LES) pressure with no relaxation upon swallowing. A diagnosis of achalasia cardia is made. Which of the following is the putative neurotransmitter responsible for relaxation of gastrointestinal smooth muscle?","o":["a. Dopamine","b. Nitric Oxide","c. Somatostatin","d. Acetylcholine","e. PGE2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Nitric Oxide","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Nitric oxide.</b> Inhibitory enteric neurons release <b>NO (nitric oxide)</b>, together with VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide), to relax GI (gastrointestinal) smooth muscle, including the LES (lower esophageal sphincter). In achalasia these neurons are lost, so the LES stays tight.<br>A. Dopamine: acts mainly on the brain's vomiting pathway; not the LES relaxant.<br>C. Somatostatin: inhibits hormone release (gastrin, insulin).<br>D. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts GI smooth muscle.<br>E. PGE2 (prostaglandin E2): protects the mucosa; not the sphincter relaxant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"74"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":17,"label":"Vagus nerve supplies the LES","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-10.jpg","cap":"VIP opens the lower esophageal sphincter during swallowing","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D033-28.jpg","cap":"LES opening is mediated by peptidergic vagus nerve fibers releasing VIP","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-57-1","s":"What nerve innervates the lower esophageal sphincter?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Vagus nerve","a":null,"why":"<b>Vagus nerve.</b> The vagus (cranial nerve ten) carries the parasympathetic supply to the upper GI (gastrointestinal) tract, including the LES (lower esophageal sphincter). During a swallow its fibers release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> to open the LES.<br>Common mix-up: vagal ACh (acetylcholine) tightens the LES; vagal VIP opens it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L49 slide 19 (Parasympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":57,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":18,"label":"GERD = LES too loose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-12.jpg","cap":"GERD: sphincter too loose, acid rises causing heartburn; PPIs, pH monitor","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G039-13.jpg","cap":"Nissen fundoplication wraps the stomach around the esophagus to tighten it","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-11-1","s":"A 62 yo man who weighs 250 lbs was referred to a gastroenterologist by his primary care doctor after complaining of choking in his sleep, recurrent burning chest pain, and chest discomfort for the last 3 months. His pain was worse after have a large meal, alcohol, spicy food, and coffee and is suppressed by acid inhibitors. His EKG and spirometry findings appear normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","o":["a. Decrease in LES tone","b. Failure of the LES to relax","c. Increase in UES tone","d. Inhibition of gastric emptying","e. Inhibition of primary esophageal peristalsis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease in LES tone","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Decrease in LES tone.</b> In GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease) the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) is <b>too loose</b>. Acid rises into the esophagus and causes burning chest pain, worse after big meals, alcohol and coffee, and relieved by acid blockers.<br>B. LES failing to relax: achalasia, with dysphagia.<br>C. Higher UES (upper esophageal sphincter) tone: would not cause reflux.<br>D. Slow gastric emptying: can add to reflux, but the main defect is the loose LES.<br>E. Loss of primary peristalsis: causes dysphagia, not acid burning.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 12 (Gastroesophageal Reflux)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":36,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":11,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":112,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":26,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"75"},{"id":"G063-6-25","s":"A patient with a hiatus hernia while taking history also reports substernal burning pain that is most pronounced after ingestion of coffee, chocolate, french fries, and alcohol. Which of the following is the most likely cause of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) in this patient?","o":["a. Delayed gastric emptying","b. Decreased esophageal motility","c. Hiatal hernia","d. Decreased lower esophageal sphincter tone","e. Decreased upper esophageal sphincter tone"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased lower esophageal sphincter tone","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Decreased LES tone.</b> Coffee, chocolate, fatty food and alcohol all <b>lower LES (lower esophageal sphincter) tone</b>. A loose LES is the direct cause of GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>A. Delayed gastric emptying: can worsen reflux, but it is not the main defect.<br>B. Decreased esophageal motility: slows acid clearance, but it is not the main defect.<br>C. Hiatal hernia: a risk factor, but the food triggers point to LES tone.<br>E. The upper sphincter does not guard against acid reflux.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 12 (Gastroesophageal Reflux)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":6,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-11-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 27"},{"id":"D019-48-1","s":"A 55 yr old male biochemistry professor asks his colleague a physiology professor why he has developed acid reflux that only occurs on his way to the university. The physiology professor asks which Starbucks the biochemistry professor stops at on his way to work. Why?","o":["a. Caffeine increases parietal cell acid production","b. Caffeine increases reverse peristalsis in the stomach","c. Caffeine increases abdominal pressure forcing stomach contents into the esophagus","d. Caffeine constricts the upper esophageal sphincter","e. Caffeine relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Caffeine relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Caffeine relaxes the LES.</b> Caffeine <b>lowers LES (lower esophageal sphincter) tone</b>. A loose sphincter lets stomach acid flow back up: reflux.<br>A. Caffeine can raise acid a little, but acid cannot reflux past a tight LES.<br>B. Caffeine does not cause reverse peristalsis.<br>C. Abdominal pressure rises with obesity or pregnancy, not caffeine.<br>D. The upper sphincter is not the barrier against acid reflux.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 12 (Gastroesophageal Reflux)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":48,"f":"D019"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":25,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"76"},{"id":"D063-28-1","s":"GERD- Causes of Decreased LES tone?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"CCK, progesterone (pregnancy), nitrates, calcium channel blockers, high-fat meals, chocolate, smoking/nicotine, caffeine, alcohol","a":null,"why":"<b>Things that lower LES tone:</b> <b>hormones</b> (CCK, cholecystokinin; progesterone in pregnancy), <b>drugs</b> (nitrates, calcium channel blockers), <b>foods</b> (fatty meals, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol) and <b>smoking</b>. A loose LES (lower esophageal sphincter) lets acid reflux: GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>Common mix-up: achalasia is the opposite, an LES that is too tight.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 12 (Gastroesophageal Reflux)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":28,"f":"D063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-11-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 28"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":18,"label":"Achalasia = LES too tight, fails to relax","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-14.jpg","cap":"Achalasia: tight sphincter; barium shows dilated esophagus with narrow tip","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G039-15.jpg","cap":"Dilated esophagus above a tight LES, opened by balloon dilation","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/D004-115.jpg","cap":"Normal manometry: sequential esophageal waves; LES pressure drops after swallow","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 115"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-30-1","s":"Which of these is a sphincter that is too tight?","o":["a. Achalasia","b. Reflux disease","c. Esphageal spasm","d. Use of alcohol","e. Eating chocolate"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Achalasia","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Achalasia.</b> The LES (lower esophageal sphincter) is <b>too tight</b> and fails to relax on swallowing, so food sticks (dysphagia).<br>B. Reflux disease: the LES is too loose.<br>C. Esophageal spasm: sudden contractions of the esophageal body, not a tight sphincter.<br>D. Alcohol: loosens the LES.<br>E. Chocolate: loosens the LES.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":30,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-132-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 29"},{"id":"D015-91-1","s":"IMPAIRED PERISTALSIS OF THE LOWER 2/3 OF THE ESOPHAGUS RESULTS IN WHICH DISORDER?","o":["A. GERD","B. ACHALASIA","C. PEPTIC ULCER","D. VOMITING"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ACHALASIA","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Achalasia.</b> Loss of inhibitory neurons (VIP, vasoactive intestinal peptide; NO, nitric oxide) impairs <b>peristalsis in the lower two-thirds</b> of the esophagus, and the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) <b>fails to relax</b>. Food sticks: dysphagia.<br>A. GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease): the LES is too loose.<br>C. Peptic ulcer: acid damage to the stomach or duodenum.<br>D. Vomiting: a medullary reflex, not a motility disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":91,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-132-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 30"},{"id":"D024-38-1","s":"A TouroCOM student visiting Bangladesh decided to try paratha for the first time. About an hour later, she still felt the food in her upper abdominal area. She went to the hospital where they used a pressure manometry to diagnose her condition. What was she suffering from?","o":["A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","B. GERD","C. Diabetic gastroparesis","D. Achalasia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Achalasia","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Achalasia.</b> Food that still feels stuck after a meal points to a <b>tight LES</b> (lower esophageal sphincter) that will not relax. Achalasia is diagnosed with <b>pressure manometry</b>: LES pressure is high and does not drop on swallowing.<br>A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: a gastrin tumor causing acid ulcers.<br>B. GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease): loose LES with heartburn; tested with a twenty-four-hour pH monitor.<br>C. Diabetic gastroparesis: needs long-standing diabetes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":38,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":80,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-132-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 31"},{"id":"D004-132-1","s":"A 35-year old man presents with dysphagia (difficulty in swallowing) for solid foods and liquids. Imaging studies exhibit dilation of the proximal esophagus, and a tapering of the distal esophagus. In this scenario, you expect all of the following conditions except;","o":["A. Increased parasympathetic stimulation at LES","B. Persitaltic movement in esophagus is uncoordinated","C. Low-pressure increase in esophagus during swallowing","D. Gastric emptying is slowed"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased parasympathetic stimulation at LES","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Increased parasympathetic stimulation is NOT expected.</b> Proximal dilation with a narrow 'bird's beak' tip is <b>achalasia</b>. Inhibitory neurons (VIP, vasoactive intestinal peptide; NO, nitric oxide) are lost, so the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) stays tight. Its nerve input is reduced, not increased.<br>B. Expected: peristalsis is lost or uncoordinated.<br>C. Expected: swallows give weak, simultaneous, low-pressure waves.<br>D. Expected: food held above the LES reaches and clears the stomach late.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":73,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":132,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":47,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"77"},{"id":"G049-1-4","s":"CASE 43 Difficulty in Swallowing: Achalasia. Jim Booker, a 49-year-old insurance broker, is having digestive problems. He has difficulty swallowing both solid foods and liquids, and occasionally he regurgitates. The problem is most noticeable when he is under stress, especially when he eats too fast. Jim says that it feels like food is “stuck” in his esophagus and will not go down. Without intending to, he lost 10 pounds in the past 2 months. His wife was especially worried about the weight loss and insisted that Jim see a physician. After doing a complete physical examination, the physician ordered several tests, including a barium swallow and esophageal manometry. After the testing, he was diagnosed with achalasia, a disease characterized by impaired peristalsis in the distal two-thirds of the esophagus and failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax during swallowing. The physician recommended that Jim be treated with a procedure that physically dilates the LES. QUESTION: On barium swallow testing, the radiologist noted dilation of the esophagus. How can you explain this finding?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The high resting tone of the non-relaxing LES holds food in the esophagus, which dilates proximally.","a":null,"why":"<b>The LES does not relax, so food pools above it and stretches the esophagus.</b> In achalasia the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) has <b>high resting tone</b> and <b>fails to relax</b> on swallowing, and the lower esophagus has no real peristalsis. Food and liquid back up, and over time the esophagus dilates above a narrow 'bird's beak' tip.<br>Common mix-up: the narrowing is a sphincter that will not relax, not a scar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 2 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G049"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-132-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 32"},{"id":"G049-1-5","s":"CASE 43 Difficulty in Swallowing: Achalasia. Jim Booker, a 49-year-old insurance broker, is having digestive problems. He has difficulty swallowing both solid foods and liquids, and occasionally he regurgitates. The problem is most noticeable when he is under stress, especially when he eats too fast. Jim says that it feels like food is “stuck” in his esophagus and will not go down. Without intending to, he lost 10 pounds in the past 2 months. His wife was especially worried about the weight loss and insisted that Jim see a physician. After doing a complete physical examination, the physician ordered several tests, including a barium swallow and esophageal manometry. After the testing, he was diagnosed with achalasia, a disease characterized by impaired peristalsis in the distal two-thirds of the esophagus and failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax during swallowing. The physician recommended that Jim be treated with a procedure that physically dilates the LES. QUESTION: Results of Jim’s esophageal manometry (pressure measurements) are shown in Figure 5–1 and compared to results in normal persons. What major differences are seen in achalasia as compared to normal, and what is the explanation for these differences?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Normal: sequential caudal peristaltic pressure waves and LES pressure falling to zero during the swallow. Achalasia: simultaneous (non-peristaltic) contractions of all segments and an LES pressure that is high at rest and never falls during the swallow (loss of inhibitory innervation).","a":null,"why":"<b>Normal:</b> after a swallow, pressure waves pass <b>one after another</b> down the upper, middle and lower esophagus, and LES (lower esophageal sphincter) pressure <b>falls to zero</b> until the wave arrives. <b>Achalasia:</b> the middle and lower esophagus give weak waves <b>at the same time</b> (no peristalsis), and the LES stays <b>high and never relaxes</b>. Cause: loss of inhibitory VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide) neurons.<br>Common mix-up: the upper (skeletal) esophagus still contracts normally.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G049-2.jpg","in":"Figure 5–1 (page 2): esophageal pressure tracings after a swallow, normal vs achalasia — normal shows sequential upper→middle→lower esophageal waves and LES pressure dropping to 0 during the swallow; achalasia shows simultaneous low waves in middle/lower esophagus and LES pressure staying high.","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 2 problem set (with answers)","sl":2,"f":"G049"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-132-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 33"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":19,"label":"Stomach has an extra oblique muscle layer","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-20.jpg","cap":"Stomach has a unique extra third (oblique) muscle layer","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G039-22.jpg","cap":"Stomach muscularis labelled: longitudinal, circular and oblique layers","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-41-1","s":"Which of the following organs has 3 muscle layers?","o":["A. Large Intestine","B. Small Intestine","C. Stomach","D. Esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach.</b> It has <b>three muscle layers: oblique, circular and longitudinal</b>. The extra oblique layer gives more churning, and the wall thickens from the fundus down to the antrum.<br>A. Large intestine: two layers; the longitudinal one forms three bands (taeniae coli).<br>B. Small intestine: two layers, circular and longitudinal.<br>D. Esophagus: two layers (skeletal above, smooth below).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 20 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 23 (Oblique creates more “churning”)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":41,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":38,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":38,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"78"},{"id":"D019-26-1","s":"Which of the following has a third smooth muscle layer?","o":["a. Stomach","b. Duodenum","c. Jejunum","d. Large intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Stomach.</b> The stomach has a unique <b>extra third layer, the oblique muscle</b>, inside the circular layer. It adds churning power.<br>B. Duodenum: only circular and longitudinal layers.<br>C. Jejunum: only circular and longitudinal layers.<br>D. Large intestine: a circular layer plus longitudinal muscle gathered into three bands (taeniae coli); no oblique layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 20 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":26,"f":"D019"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":19,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-41-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 34"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":19,"label":"Receptive relaxation = orad stomach relaxes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-26.jpg","cap":"Receptive relaxation: stomach holds 50 to 1,500 ml; vagal reflex","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D048-31.jpg","cap":"Orad stomach relaxes to receive the bolus; vagovagal reflex using VIP","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-48-1","s":"Which of the following structures undergoes receptive relaxation when a bolus of food is swallowed?","o":["A. Orad stomach","B. Palatopharyngeal folds","C. Pharynx","D. Thoracic esophagus","E. Upper esophageal sphincter"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Orad stomach","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Orad stomach.</b> When a bolus is swallowed, a vagal reflex relaxes the <b>orad (upper) stomach</b>, the fundus and upper body, so it takes in food with little rise in pressure.<br>B. Palatopharyngeal folds: help close off the nasopharynx; they do not store food.<br>C. Pharynx: contracts to push the bolus down.<br>D. Thoracic esophagus: contracts in peristalsis.<br>E. Upper esophageal sphincter: opens briefly, then closes; this is not a storage relaxation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":48,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"79"},{"id":"G063-8-33","s":"Which of the following processes applies to the proximal stomach?","o":["a. Receptive relaxation","b. Peristalsis","c. Retropulsion","d. Segmentation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Receptive relaxation","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Receptive relaxation.</b> The <b>proximal (orad) stomach</b>, the fundus and upper body, relaxes as food arrives. It holds a large volume with little rise in pressure.<br>B. Peristalsis: strong waves of the distal stomach, body to antrum.<br>C. Retropulsion: the antrum throws chyme back against the closed pylorus.<br>D. Segmentation: a mixing pattern of the small intestine and colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-48-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 35"},{"id":"D040-71-1","s":"Which of the following is an example of receptive relaxation?","o":["A. Decreased GI contractions is response to rest","B. Decreased GI contractions in response to fight-or-flight","C. Lower esophageal sphincter and orad of stomach relaxing at same time","D. Lower esophageal sphincter and antrum of stomach relaxing at same time"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lower esophageal sphincter and orad of stomach relaxing at same time","a":"C","why":"<b>C. LES and orad stomach relaxing at the same time.</b> As a bolus arrives, vagal <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> opens the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) and relaxes the <b>orad stomach</b> together. Pressure there falls, so food moves in easily.<br>A. Rest: parasympathetic tone at rest increases GI (gastrointestinal) contraction.<br>B. Fight-or-flight: sympathetic slowing of motility, not receptive relaxation.<br>D. The antrum does not relax; it contracts to grind and mix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":71,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-48-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 36"},{"id":"D061-52-1","s":"9. Receptive relaxation of the stomach ____________.","o":["A. Reduces pressure and volume of the stomach","B. Relaxes the body of the stomach","C. Increases volume of the stomach","D. Is increased in gastroparesis","E. Is a vasovagal reflex"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increases volume of the stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increases the volume of the stomach.</b> The orad stomach relaxes, so its volume can grow from about <b>fifty to fifteen hundred milliliters</b> with little rise in pressure. It is a <b>vagovagal reflex</b>: sensory and motor fibers both run in the vagus.<br>A. It lowers pressure but raises volume.<br>B. It relaxes the orad region (fundus and upper body); the lower body and antrum keep contracting.<br>D. It is reduced in gastroparesis, where distension is not sensed.<br>E. 'Vasovagal' is the fainting reflex; the 2026 slide's 'vasovagal' means vagovagal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":52,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-48-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 37"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":19,"label":"Vagotomy abolishes receptive relaxation (vagovagal)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-26.jpg","cap":"Receptive relaxation is a vagal reflex letting the stomach hold 1,500 ml","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D048-31.jpg","cap":"Vagal VIP drives receptive relaxation; vagotomy eliminates this reflex","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G038-23.jpg","cap":"Vagus nerve: 75% afferent (stretch, acid sensing), 25% efferent","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-45-1","s":"Detection of distention of the stomach followed by smooth muscle wall relaxation of the orad stomach is an example of which kind of reflex?","o":["A. Peristaltic reflex","B. Vagovagal reflex","C. Gastrocolic reflex","D. Rectosphincteric reflex"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagovagal reflex","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vagovagal reflex.</b> Stretch is sensed by vagal afferents, and the response travels back down vagal efferents that release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b>, so the orad stomach relaxes. Both limbs run in the vagus. The 2026 slide's 'vasovagal' is a slip for vagovagal.<br>A. Peristaltic reflex: serotonin-triggered propulsion in the intestine.<br>C. Gastrocolic reflex: stomach stretch increases colon motility.<br>D. Rectosphincteric reflex: rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":45,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"80"},{"id":"D066-32-1","s":"A man presents to the clinic with persistent heartburn and difficulty swallowing solid food. A high-resolution esophageal manometry study is ordered. During swallowing, normal primary esophageal peristalsis is noted, followed by distension-induced secondary peristaltic waves. Upon arrival of the food bolus at the stomach, the proximal stomach relaxes to accommodate the incoming volume without a significant increase in intragastric pressure. Which of the following mediators and reflex pathways is primarily responsible for this accommodation response in the gastric reservoir?","o":["A. VIP and NO via intrinsic submucosal plexus neurons","B. VIP and NO via a vasovagal reflex","C. Motilin secreted by Mo cells acting on enteric smooth muscle receptors","D. Gastrin via endocrine activation of parietal cells","E. Norepinephrine via sympathetic postganglionic signaling"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP and NO via a vasovagal reflex","a":"B","why":"<b>B. VIP and NO through a vagovagal reflex</b> (the option writes it 'vasovagal'). This is receptive relaxation. Stretch travels up vagal afferents. Vagal efferents then drive enteric inhibitory neurons that release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide)</b>. The orad stomach relaxes, so volume rises with little pressure.<br>A. Right mediators, wrong route: the reflex needs the vagus (vagotomy abolishes it), and the submucosal plexus runs secretion.<br>C. Motilin: fasting contractions.<br>D. Gastrin on parietal cells: acid secretion.<br>E. Norepinephrine: sympathetic; not part of this reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 36 (Q3 Which of the following is mediated by the vagus nerve?)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":32,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-45-1","kind":"harder","n":"C3 extra 38"},{"id":"D019-38-1","s":"What is the physiologic response to cutting the tenth cranial nerve?","o":["a. Decreased gastric pH","b. Increased cholecystokinin release","c. Decreased dilation of stomach on eating","d. Diarrhea","e. Loss of swallowing"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased dilation of stomach on eating","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Less dilation of the stomach on eating.</b> Cutting the vagus (cranial nerve ten) removes the <b>vagovagal receptive relaxation</b>, so the orad stomach cannot relax and expand as food arrives.<br>A. Acid secretion falls after vagotomy, so pH rises, not falls.<br>B. CCK (cholecystokinin) is released by fat in the duodenum, not by the vagus.<br>D. Diarrhea: a possible later surgical complication, not the direct physiologic loss.<br>E. Swallowing starts voluntarily, and a gastric vagotomy is cut far below the throat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":38,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"81"},{"id":"F1203-74-1","s":"A patient undergoes a vagotomy, a procedure during which the Vagus Nerve is severed. Which of the following would be the result of this operation?","o":["A. Loss of migratory myoelectric complexes","B. Inability to vomit","C. Loss of gastric emptying due to fatty acid release","D. Inability of the stomach to engage in receptive relaxation","E. Loss of a cranial nerve that is 3/4th efferent and 1/4th afferent in nature"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inability of the stomach to engage in receptive relaxation","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Loss of receptive relaxation.</b> Receptive relaxation is a <b>vagovagal reflex</b>: its sensory and motor limbs both run in the vagus. Cut the vagus, and the orad stomach can no longer relax to take in a meal.<br>A. MMC (migrating myoelectric complex): driven by motilin, not the vagus.<br>B. Vomiting: run by the medulla, with many inputs besides the vagus.<br>C. Fat slows emptying through CCK (cholecystokinin) and enteric reflexes.<br>E. The vagus is about <b>seventy-five percent afferent</b> and twenty-five percent efferent, not the reverse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 35 (Q3 Which of the following is mediated by the vagus nerve?)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":74,"f":"F1203"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":75,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":77,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-45-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 39"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":19,"label":"Stomach stretch releases gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-26.jpg","cap":"Receptive relaxation; stomach needs a sense of stretch to release gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Gastrin stimuli: stomach stretch, protein fragments, vagal ACh and GRP","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-36-1","s":"Which statement best correlates with receptive relaxation of the stomach?","o":["A. The sympathetic nervous system is initiated by distention of the stomach after eating.","B. Stretch of the stomach inhibits gastrin release.","C. Vagotomy would increase receptive relaxation of the stomach.","D. Stretch of the stomach would lead to a decrease in the pH of the stomach."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stretch of the stomach would lead to a decrease in the pH of the stomach.","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stretch lowers stomach pH.</b> Stretch of the stomach is needed to <b>release gastrin</b>. Gastrin drives parietal cells to secrete acid, so the pH falls.<br>A. The reflex is <b>vagovagal</b>, not sympathetic.<br>B. Stretch increases gastrin release.<br>C. Vagotomy abolishes receptive relaxation; it does not increase it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":36,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":59,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":59,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":34,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-15-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 40"},{"id":"D022-15-1","s":"As you eat Thanksgiving dinner, which of the following is most likely occurring?","o":["a. Chemoreceptors are sensing the stretch of the stomach and signaling the brain through the efferent vagus nerve resulting in increased gastrin levels","b. Filling of the stomach results in release of NE into the myenteric plexus increasing muscle contraction","c. PSNS stimulation gradually increases the frequency of contraction of the stomach","d. The muscularis mucosa is primarily responsible for the motility","e. VIP, ACh and gastrin are released"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP, ACh and gastrin are released","a":"e","why":"<b>E. VIP, ACh and gastrin are released.</b> Vagal <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> relaxes the stomach to receive the meal. Vagal <b>ACh (acetylcholine)</b> increases contraction force and acid. Stretch and protein release <b>gastrin</b>.<br>A. Stretch is sensed by mechanoreceptors, not chemoreceptors, and travels up afferent, not efferent, vagal fibers.<br>B. NE (norepinephrine) inhibits motility.<br>C. Parasympathetic input raises force, not frequency.<br>D. The muscularis externa moves food; the muscularis mucosae only moves the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":48,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":15,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":124,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"82"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":20,"label":"Antral contractions against pylorus = retropulsion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-21.jpg","cap":"Antral wall is thicker than the body; plexuses control the churning","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Gastric mixing: only 1 mm particles, 2-3 ml leave per contraction","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/D023-37.jpg","cap":"Retropulsion: antral contractions push chyme backward to slow emptying","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"D063-31-2","s":"Retropulsion?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Antral contractions that push chyme backward into the stomach, preventing too-rapid gastric emptying","a":null,"why":"<b>Retropulsion:</b> strong antral contractions hit a <b>closed pylorus</b>, so most chyme is thrown <b>back into the stomach</b>. This grinds and mixes food. Only small particles (about one millimeter, two to three milliliters per contraction) pass into the duodenum, which prevents too-fast emptying.<br>Common mix-up: retropulsion happens in the antrum; receptive relaxation happens in the orad stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":31,"f":"D063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-128-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 41"},{"id":"D004-128-1","s":"A 50-year-old man was experiencing problems with his pyloric sphincter and needed a surgery. Which of the following will be affected the most until he heals from the surgery;","o":["A. Receptive relaxation","B. Segmentation","C. Haustrations","D. Retropulsion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Retropulsion","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Retropulsion.</b> It depends on antral contractions against a <b>closed pylorus</b>, which throw chyme back into the stomach. A damaged pyloric sphincter disturbs it most.<br>A. Receptive relaxation: in the orad stomach, far from the pylorus.<br>B. Segmentation: mixing in the small intestine.<br>C. Haustrations: mixing in the colon's pouches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 21 (Antral wall is thicker than the body)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":52,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":128,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":41,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"83"},{"id":"G061-29-1","s":"A 46-year-old man is diagnosed with a malignant tumor in the antrum. Which of the following processes is most likely to be affected?","o":["A. Receptive relaxation","B. Retropulsion","C. Segmentation","D. Duodenal peristalsis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Retropulsion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Retropulsion.</b> The <b>antrum</b> has the thickest muscle. Its strong contractions against a closed pylorus push chyme back to grind it, and a tumor there disrupts this.<br>A. Receptive relaxation: happens in the orad stomach (fundus).<br>C. Segmentation: a mixing pattern of the small intestine.<br>D. Duodenal peristalsis: happens beyond the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 21 (Antral wall is thicker than the body)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":29,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":26,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"84"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":20,"label":"Nerves change force, not slow-wave frequency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Vagal stimulation increases force, not frequency; gastric rhythm 3 per minute","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G048-13.jpg","cap":"Slow-wave frequency not affected by nerves, but contraction strength is","where":"2025 discussion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-25-1","s":"There is vagal stimulation of the stomach. Which of these is likely occurring as a result of this stimulation?","o":["A. Slow waves in the stomach are 7-8 waves per minute","B. Slow waves in the stomach are 1-2 waves per minute","C. Slow waves in the stomach are 3-5 waves per minute"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Slow waves in the stomach are 3-5 waves per minute","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Three to five per minute (unchanged).</b> The stomach's basic electrical rhythm is about <b>three per minute</b>. <b>Vagal stimulation increases the force, not the frequency</b>, of contraction, so the slow-wave rate stays the same.<br>A. Seven to eight per minute: near the ileum's rate; the vagus cannot speed slow waves.<br>B. One to two per minute: the vagus cannot slow them either.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":25,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2023 TA weekly","n":"GI physiology weekly TA presentation","sl":33,"f":"D048"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 42"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Isotonic calorie-free liquid empties fastest","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Gastric emptying: fat and hypertonic slow; isotonic, no-calorie fluid fastest","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-130-1","s":"Which of the following order is the best order and right sequence of food contents that will be emptying from stomach;","o":["A. Coke, brownie and Pizza","B. Pizza, brownie and coke","C. Brownie, coke and pizza","D. None of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Coke, brownie and Pizza","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Coke, brownie, pizza.</b> <b>Liquids empty before solids</b>, and <b>fat empties slowest</b>. Coke leaves first, the mostly carbohydrate brownie is next, and the fatty, protein-rich pizza is last.<br>B. Reverse order: puts the fattiest solid first.<br>C. Puts a solid before the liquid.<br>D. None of the above: A follows the rules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":54,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":130,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":43,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"85"},{"id":"D010-62-1","s":"All of these inhibit gastric emptying except…","o":["A. High fat meal","B. Isotonic water ingestion","C. CCK release","D. A meal rich in acidic foods"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Isotonic water ingestion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Isotonic water.</b> An <b>isotonic liquid with no calories</b> empties fastest, because nothing in it triggers the duodenal brakes.<br>A. High-fat meal: fat is the strongest brake.<br>C. CCK (cholecystokinin): released by fat; it slows emptying.<br>D. Acidic meal: acid (hydrogen ions) in the duodenum slows emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":62,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":62,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":37,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"86"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Fat (via CCK) and acid slow emptying","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Duodenal factors slowing emptying: stretch, acid, fat, osmolarity","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"Actions of CCK include inhibiting gastric emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-50-1","s":"Which of the following inhibits gastric emptying?","o":["A. Liquids","B. Isotonic contents","C. Fats","D. Stomach contraction"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fats","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fats.</b> Fatty acids in the duodenum release <b>CCK (cholecystokinin)</b>, which <b>slows gastric emptying</b>. This gives time to digest and absorb the fat.<br>A. Liquids: empty faster than solids.<br>B. Isotonic contents: empty fastest.<br>D. Stomach contraction: drives emptying; it does not stop it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":50,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 43"},{"id":"D040-73-1","s":"8) Which of the following slows or inhibits gastric emptying in the duodenum? (Multiple Answers)","o":["A. H+","B. Carbohydrates","C. Proteins","D. Fats"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["A","D"],"rv":false,"at":"H+; Fats","a":"A,D","why":"<b>A. H+ and D. fats.</b> <b>Acid (H+, hydrogen ions)</b> in the duodenum triggers enteric reflexes that slow emptying until pancreatic bicarbonate neutralizes it. <b>Fat</b> releases <b>CCK (cholecystokinin)</b>, which slows emptying so fat can be digested.<br>B. Carbohydrates: weak effect; they empty quickly.<br>C. Proteins: empty faster than fat; not a main duodenal brake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":73,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 44"},{"id":"D063-31-1","s":"Factors Regulating gastric emptying? Cck effect?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Fat (via CCK) and H+ in the duodenum slow emptying; liquids empty before solids; fats empty slowest, carbohydrates and proteins first; isotonic before hypotonic; acid slows emptying","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastric emptying takes thirty minutes to three hours.</b> <b>Liquids</b> empty before solids. <b>Isotonic, calorie-free</b> fluid is fastest; <b>fat</b> and <b>hypertonic</b> contents are slowest. The duodenal brakes are <b>stretch, acid, fat and high osmolarity</b>. Fat releases <b>CCK (cholecystokinin)</b>, which slows emptying so fat can be digested and absorbed.<br>Common mix-up: CCK slows emptying; it does not speed it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":31,"f":"D063"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 45"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Starch barely affects gastric emptying","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Emptying is slowed by duodenal stretch, acid, fat and osmolarity","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-38-1","s":"Which of the following characteristics of chyme has the smallest effect on gastric emptying?","o":["A. Fat","B. Protein","C. Acidity","D. Osmolarity","E. Starch"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Starch","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Starch.</b> Duodenal sensors slow emptying when they detect <b>fat, acid, high osmolarity</b> and protein breakdown products. Starch is a large, osmotically weak molecule that triggers none of these.<br>A. Fat: the strongest brake, via CCK (cholecystokinin).<br>B. Protein: its digestion products slow emptying.<br>C. Acidity: acid in the duodenum slows emptying.<br>D. Osmolarity: hypertonic chyme slows emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":38,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":36,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"87"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Gastrin increases gastric motility","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions include gastric motility; acid feeds back to suppress gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D007-106.jpg","cap":"Gastrin is the only gastric hormone that increases gastric motility","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"D063-19-1","s":"Hormones increasing or slowing motility?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Increase intestinal motility: gastrin, CCK, motilin, serotonin; gastrin increases gastric motility. Inhibit: secretin and glucagon (small intestine); CCK, secretin and GIP inhibit gastric emptying","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastrin is the only gut hormone that increases gastric motility.</b> <b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> is the strongest brake on gastric emptying; <b>secretin</b> and <b>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> slow it weakly. In the small intestine, gastrin, CCK, motilin and serotonin speed motility; secretin and glucagon slow it. Motilin also drives the fasting migrating myoelectric complex.<br>Common mix-up: CCK speeds intestinal motility but slows gastric emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 23 (Q3. Which of the following is the only GI hormones to increase gastric motility?)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":19,"f":"D063"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 46"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Hyperosmolar duodenum slows gastric emptying","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Hypertonic contents slow emptying; duodenal osmolarity is a brake","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G039-29.jpg","cap":"Diabetic gastroparesis: autonomic neuropathy, distension not sensed","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-4-16","s":"A long standing diabetic develops gastroparesis and is diagnosed for gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). Which of the following is likely to occur in regards to gastric emptying?","o":["a. Hyperosmolality of duodenal contents initiates a decrease in gastric emptying","b. Meals containing fat empty faster than carbohydrate-rich food","c. Solids empty more rapidly than liquids","d. Acidification of the antrum increases gastric emptying","e. Vagal stimulation decreases receptive relaxation in the upper portion of the stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hyperosmolality of duodenal contents initiates a decrease in gastric emptying","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Hyperosmolar duodenal contents slow gastric emptying.</b> High <b>osmolarity</b> in the duodenum is a brake on emptying, along with stretch, acid and fat.<br>B. Fat empties slower than carbohydrate.<br>C. Liquids empty faster than solids.<br>D. Acid does not speed emptying; acid reaching the duodenum slows it.<br>E. The vagus causes receptive relaxation; it does not decrease it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)","L50 slide 29 (Diabetic Gastroparesis)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":4,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"88"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":21,"label":"Vagotomy abolishes receptive relaxation (vagovagal)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-26.jpg","cap":"Receptive relaxation is a vagal reflex letting the stomach hold 1,500 ml","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D048-31.jpg","cap":"Vagal VIP drives receptive relaxation; vagotomy eliminates this reflex","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G038-23.jpg","cap":"Vagus nerve: 75% afferent (stretch, acid sensing), 25% efferent","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-5-22","s":"Before the introduction of H2 blockers and Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPI’s), vagotomy was a common procedure among the surgical fraternity to treat peptic ulcer disease. Which of the following is an unwanted consequence of this procedure?","o":["a. Stimulation of VIP release at the nerve ending","b. Increased intragastric pressure","c. Speeding of gastric emptying","d. Excessive stimulation of stomach peristalsis."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased intragastric pressure","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Increased intragastric pressure.</b> Vagotomy abolishes <b>receptive relaxation</b>, a vagovagal reflex. The orad stomach can no longer relax as food enters, so the pressure inside the stomach rises with each meal.<br>A. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) comes from vagal fibers; cutting them reduces it.<br>C. Solids empty more slowly after vagotomy, because antral grinding weakens; surgeons added a pyloroplasty for this.<br>D. Vagal input increases peristalsis; losing it weakens peristalsis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L53 slide 21 (What we did BEFORE PPIs!!)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":5,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"89"},{"id":"G063-7-30","s":"Which of the following statements about gastric emptying is correct?","o":["a. Solids empty more rapidly than liquids","b. Vagotomy accelerates the emptying of solids","c. Indigestible food empties during the digestive period","d. Vagotomy decreases accommodation of the proximal stomach"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagotomy decreases accommodation of the proximal stomach","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Vagotomy decreases accommodation of the proximal stomach.</b> Accommodation (<b>receptive relaxation</b>) is a vagovagal reflex, so cutting the vagus impairs it.<br>A. Liquids empty faster than solids.<br>B. Vagotomy slows the emptying of solids, because antral grinding weakens.<br>C. Indigestible food leaves between meals, swept out by the MMC (migrating myoelectric complex).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":7,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"G063-5-22","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":22,"label":"Sympathetic slows, parasympathetic speeds motility","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-30.jpg","cap":"Small intestine: parasympathetic stimulates, sympathetic inhibits","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G038-26.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic norepinephrine slows GI motility and constricts sphincters","where":"2026 lecture: L49 GI Structure & Innervation, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G065-53-1","s":"There is a common theme seen in the GI tract when it comes to innervation. Which system decreases contraction of the GI tract and decreased peristalsis?","o":["A. Sympathetic","B. Parasympathetic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sympathetic.</b> <b>Norepinephrine</b> from sympathetic nerves <b>slows GI (gastrointestinal) motility</b> and tightens sphincters (fight or flight). The 2026 rule for the whole gut: parasympathetic stimulates, sympathetic inhibits.<br>B. Parasympathetic: ACh (acetylcholine) from the vagus increases contraction and peristalsis (rest and digest).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":53,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":221,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":22,"label":"Segmentation mixes; peristalsis propels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-30.jpg","cap":"Segmentation (circular muscle) mixes; peristalsis moves and propels","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G039-31.jpg","cap":"Peristalsis pushes the bolus one way; segmentation chops and mixes it in place","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/D066-39.jpg","cap":"Segmentation splits chyme both ways, then it remerges; peristalsis contracts behind, relaxes ahead","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"D066-37-1","s":"Which contraction of the small intestine mixes the chyme and exposes it to pancreatic enzymes and secretions, without propelling it forward?","o":["A. Segmentation contractions","B. Peristaltic contractions","C. Migrating myoelectric complexes","D. Mass movements","E. Retropulsion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Segmentation contractions","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Segmentation contractions.</b> A ring of circular muscle squeezes the chyme and sends it both up and down. The ring relaxes and the pieces merge again. This <b>mixes</b> chyme with enzymes and brings it to the mucosa, with no net forward movement.<br>B. Peristaltic contractions: propel chyme toward the colon.<br>C. Migrating myoelectric complexes: fasting sweeps, about every 90 minutes.<br>D. Mass movements: colon, one to three times a day.<br>E. Retropulsion: the antrum throwing food back against a closed pylorus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":37,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"90"},{"id":"D067-42-1","s":"What is the main difference between peristaltic contractions and segmentation contractions?","o":["A. One happens in the colon and the other in the small intestine","B. One is to mix the chyme and the other propels it","C. Peristaltic contractions split the chyme while segmentation contractions happen behind the bolus","D. Reverse segmentation contractions are involved in the vomiting reflex while peristaltic contractions aren’t"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"One is to mix the chyme and the other propels it","a":"B","why":"<b>B. One mixes the chyme and the other propels it.</b> <b>Segmentation</b> splits the chyme and lets it merge again, so it mixes in place. <b>Peristalsis</b> contracts behind the bolus and relaxes ahead of it, so it propels.<br>A. Both occur in the small intestine.<br>C. Reversed: segmentation splits the chyme; peristalsis contracts behind the bolus.<br>D. Reversed: vomiting uses reverse peristalsis, not reverse segmentation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":42,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-37-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 49"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":22,"label":"Peristalsis: contract behind bolus, relax ahead","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-31.jpg","cap":"Peristalsis: contraction behind the bolus, relaxation ahead pushes it forward","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/D023-36.jpg","cap":"Behind bolus segment narrows (ACh, substance P); ahead widens (VIP, NO)","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-78-1","s":"Describe the state of muscles in peristaltic contraction behind the bolus.","o":["A. Circular muscles are inhibited, longitudinal muscles are activated","B. Circular muscles are inhibited, longitudinal muscles are inhibited","C. Circular muscles are activated, longitudinal muscles are inhibited","D. Circular muscles are activated, longitudinal muscles are activated"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Circular muscles are activated, longitudinal muscles are inhibited","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Behind the bolus: circular contracts, longitudinal relaxes.</b> The segment behind <b>narrows and lengthens</b> and squeezes the bolus forward. Ahead of the bolus the opposite happens: circular relaxes and longitudinal contracts, so that segment <b>widens and shortens</b> to receive it.<br>A. Circular inhibited, longitudinal active: this is the segment ahead of the bolus.<br>B. Both inhibited: nothing would push.<br>D. Both active: the layers are wired reciprocally, so they do not contract together.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)","L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":78,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-55-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 50"},{"id":"D066-37-2","s":"Which statement describes peristaltic contractions of the small intestine, but not segmentation contractions?","o":["A. They use contraction of circular smooth muscle only","B. They involve reciprocal innervation of circular and longitudinal muscles","C. They send the chyme in both the orad and caudad directions","D. They mix the chyme without net forward movement","E. They occur only during fasting, about every 90 minutes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"They involve reciprocal innervation of circular and longitudinal muscles","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Reciprocal action of circular and longitudinal muscle.</b> Peristalsis uses <b>both layers in opposite states</b>. Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts and longitudinal muscle relaxes. Ahead of it, circular relaxes and longitudinal contracts. The bolus moves one way.<br>A. Circular muscle only: segmentation.<br>C. Chyme sent both orad (toward the mouth) and caudad (toward the anus): segmentation.<br>D. Mixing without net forward movement: segmentation.<br>E. Only in fasting, about every 90 minutes: migrating myoelectric complexes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L50 slide 4 (Smooth Muscle Layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":37,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D061-55-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 51"},{"id":"D061-55-1","s":"10. To propel a bolus forward in the small intestine some segments must contact while others must relax. Which of the following is true of peristalsis?","o":["A. ACh is released behind the bolus to widen the segment and propel it forward","B. NO is released ahead of the bolus to narrow the segment and propel it forward","C. VIP is released behind the bolus to narrow the segment and propel it forward","D. Substance P is released behind the bolus to narrow the segment and propel it forward","E. Neuropeptide Y is released ahead of the bolus to widen the segment and propel it forward"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Substance P is released behind the bolus to narrow the segment and propel it forward","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Substance P behind the bolus narrows the segment.</b> Behind the bolus, excitatory neurons release <b>ACh (acetylcholine) and substance P</b>, so circular muscle contracts and pushes the bolus on. Ahead, inhibitory neurons release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide)</b>, so the segment relaxes and widens.<br>A. ACh: narrows the segment behind; it does not widen it.<br>B. NO: widens the segment ahead; it does not narrow it.<br>C. VIP: acts ahead of the bolus and relaxes.<br>E. Neuropeptide Y: the transmitters that widen the segment ahead are VIP and NO.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":55,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"91"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":22,"label":"Serotonin starts the peristaltic reflex","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-34.jpg","cap":"Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin, which initiates the peristaltic reflex","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G039-31.jpg","cap":"Peristalsis: contraction behind the bolus pushes it along","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-55-1","s":"Which of the following neurotransmitters help initiate the peristaltic reflex in the small intestine?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Serotonin","C. GABA","D. VIP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Serotonin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Serotonin.</b> Chyme stimulates <b>enterochromaffin cells</b> in the mucosa to release <b>serotonin</b>. Serotonin activates sensory neurons of the enteric nervous system, which start the <b>peristaltic reflex</b>.<br>A. ACh (acetylcholine): contracts muscle behind the bolus once the reflex is under way.<br>C. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid): not the trigger of this reflex.<br>D. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): relaxes muscle ahead of the bolus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":55,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":55,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":223,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"92"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":22,"label":"Small-bowel contractions triggered by distention","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-31.jpg","cap":"Bolus stretching the wall: contraction behind, relaxation ahead","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"Migrating myoelectric complex occurs only between meals","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-3-13","s":"A 27-year-old female osteopathic medical student with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) has an alteration in intestinal motility resulting in constipation and diarrhea. Her condition has worsened in the past month as the date she has scheduled for her USMLE/COMLEX examination approaches. Which of the following statements about small intestinal motility is correct?","o":["a. Contractile frequency is constant from duodenum to terminal ileum","b. Peristalsis is the only contractile activity that occurs during feeding","c. Migrating motor complexes occur during the digestive period","d. Vagotomy abolishes contractile activity during the digestive period","e. Contractile activity is initiated in response to bowel wall distention"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Contractile activity is initiated in response to bowel wall distention","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Contractions start in response to wall distention.</b> Stretch of the bowel wall triggers the <b>peristaltic reflex</b>: contraction behind the bolus and relaxation ahead of it.<br>A. False: the slow-wave rate falls from twelve per minute in the duodenum to eight in the ileum.<br>B. False: segmentation also mixes chyme during feeding.<br>C. False: MMCs (migrating myoelectric complexes) occur between meals.<br>D. False: the enteric nervous system keeps contractions going without the vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 30 (Small Intestine)","L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":3,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 52"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":23,"label":"MMC runs only between meals","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"MMC only between meals, starting 90-120 minutes after eating; motilin-based","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/D007-74.jpg","cap":"MMC between meals; eating shuts it down; controlled by motilin","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 74"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-93-1","s":"AT WHAT INTERVAL DO MIGRATING MYOELECTRIC COMPLEXES (MMCS) OCCUR?","o":["A. 30 MIN","B. 60 MIN","C. 90 MIN","D. 150 MIN"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"90 MIN","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ninety minutes.</b> During fasting, an <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b> sweeps from the stomach to the ileum about <b>every ninety minutes</b>. It starts ninety to one hundred twenty minutes after a meal and is driven by <b>motilin</b>.<br>A. Thirty minutes: too short.<br>B. Sixty minutes: too short.<br>D. One hundred fifty minutes: too long.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":93,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 53"},{"id":"D019-32-1","s":"Which of these is exclusively between meals based on motilin?","o":["a. Stomach motility","b. Tightening of pyloric sphincter","c. Paying the bill","d. Migrating myoelectric complex","e. Opening of iliocecal valve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Migrating myoelectric complex","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Migrating myoelectric complex.</b> The <b>MMC</b> occurs <b>only between meals</b>, starting ninety to one hundred twenty minutes after eating. It is driven by the hormone <b>motilin</b> and sweeps leftover contents down the gut.<br>A. Stomach motility: also occurs during meals.<br>B. Tightening of the pylorus: not motilin-based.<br>C. Paying the bill: a joke option.<br>E. Opening of the ileocecal valve: follows meals, not motilin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":32,"f":"D019"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":17,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"93"},{"id":"D067-40-1","s":"Name the contraction that occurs during fasting and help clears stomach residue","o":["A. Phasic contractions","B. Slow waves","C. Tonic contractions","D. Migrating myoelectric complex","E. Gastrocolic reflex"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Migrating myoelectric complex","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Migrating myoelectric complex.</b> During fasting, a band of strong contractions sweeps from the stomach down the small intestine about every 90 minutes. It clears leftover food and bacteria. The hormone <b>motilin</b> drives it, and eating stops it.<br>A. Phasic contractions: the ordinary contract-then-relax pattern, fed or fasting.<br>B. Slow waves: the electrical rhythm, not a contraction.<br>C. Tonic contractions: steady tone, as in sphincters.<br>E. Gastrocolic reflex: a full stomach makes the colon move.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":40,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 54"},{"id":"D040-60-1","s":"Migrating myoelectric complexes are responsible for?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Periodic gastric/intestinal contractions during fasting. Every 90 minutes to clear the stomach and small intestine from previous meals.","a":null,"why":"<b>Sweeping the stomach and small intestine clean between meals.</b> During fasting, bursts of strong contractions travel down the gut about every <b>ninety minutes</b>. They push leftover food and bacteria toward the colon. The MMC (migrating myoelectric complex) is driven by <b>motilin</b>, and eating stops it.<br>Common mix-up: it is a fasting pattern, not part of digesting a meal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":60,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 55"},{"id":"D010-80-1","s":"A 30 year old woman eats a large, well-balanced meal for dinner. Five minutes after eating the meal, many hormones are stimulated by the food intake except for this hormone. Which hormone is inhibited soon after eating this meal?","o":["A. CCK","B. Motilin","C. Secretin","D. Gastrin","E. GIP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Motilin.</b> Motilin is released during <b>fasting</b> and drives the <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b> between meals. <b>Eating stops it.</b><br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): released by fat and protein in the duodenum.<br>C. Secretin: released by acid in the duodenum.<br>D. Gastrin: released by stretch and protein in the stomach.<br>E. GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide): released by glucose, fat and protein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 29 (Q6. Which of the following is inhibited by food?)","L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":80,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 56"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":23,"label":"Motilin drives the migrating myoelectric complex","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"Migrating myoelectric complex is based on the hormone motilin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/D007-74.jpg","cap":"MMC mainly in small bowel, controlled by the motilin hormone","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 74"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-12-1","s":"Migrating myoelectric complexes (MMCs) occur about every 90 min between meals and are thought to be mediated by;","o":["a. Duodenal motility","b. Gastric emptying","c. Mass movements","d. Gastrin","e. Motilin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Motilin.</b> MMCs (migrating myoelectric complexes) are <b>based on motilin</b>, which is released between meals.<br>A. Duodenal motility: part of what the MMC does, not its trigger.<br>B. Gastric emptying: the MMC clears the stomach; emptying is not its cause.<br>C. Mass movements: colon events after meals.<br>D. Gastrin: released after meals.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":40,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":12,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":116,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":24,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"94"},{"id":"D040-59-1","s":"What hormone mediates the migrating myoelectric complex?","o":["A. CCK","B. Acetylcholine","C. Epinephrine","D. Motilin","E. Gastrin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Motilin.</b> Motilin, released from the upper small intestine during fasting, drives the <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b>.<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): slows gastric emptying and contracts the gallbladder.<br>B. ACh (acetylcholine): a neurotransmitter, not the hormone behind the MMC.<br>C. Epinephrine: slows motility.<br>E. Gastrin: stimulates acid secretion and is released after meals.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":59,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 57"},{"id":"D007-82-1","s":"The migrating myoelectric complex is modulated by the hormone","o":["A. VIP","B. CCK","C. Gastrin","D. Motilin","E. ACh"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Motilin.</b> The <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b> runs between meals and is <b>based on the hormone motilin</b>; eating stops it.<br>A. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): relaxes smooth muscle.<br>B. CCK (cholecystokinin): released after fatty meals.<br>C. Gastrin: released after meals; it raises acid.<br>E. ACh (acetylcholine): a neurotransmitter, not a hormone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":82,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 58"},{"id":"D015-94-1","s":"WHAT HORMONE MEDIATES THIS COMPLEX? (the migrating myoelectric complex)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"MOTILIN","a":null,"why":"<b>Motilin.</b> It is released from the upper small intestine during <b>fasting</b> and drives the <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b>, which sweeps the gut about every ninety minutes. Eating stops it.<br>Common mix-up: erythromycin acts like motilin, which is why it helps diabetic gastroparesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":94,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 59"},{"id":"D024-30-1","s":"Which of the following hormones make sure that the stomach is cleared of all the residual chyme?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. CCK","C. Motilin","D. Insulin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Motilin.</b> Between meals, motilin drives the <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b>, which sweeps residual chyme out of the stomach and small intestine.<br>A. Gastrin: raises acid secretion after a meal.<br>B. CCK (cholecystokinin): slows gastric emptying.<br>D. Insulin: a pancreatic hormone for glucose uptake, not motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":30,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":51,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":51,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":219,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 60"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":23,"label":"No MMC → small-bowel bacterial overgrowth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"MMC sweeps from top to bottom of the GI tract between meals","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-56-1","s":"Migrating motility complexes (MMC) occur about every 90 mins between meals and are thought to be stimulated by the GI hormone motilin. An absence of MMC’s cause an increase in which of the following?","o":["A. Duodenal motility","B. Gastric emptying","C. Intestinal bacteria","D. Mass movements","E. Swallowing"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intestinal bacteria","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Intestinal bacteria.</b> The <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b> sweeps food residue and bacteria from the stomach and small intestine into the colon. Without it, bacteria build up: <b>small intestinal bacterial overgrowth</b>.<br>A. Duodenal motility: falls without MMCs.<br>B. Gastric emptying of leftover material: falls without MMCs.<br>D. Mass movements: colon events after meals, unrelated.<br>E. Swallowing: not affected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":56,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"95"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":24,"label":"Vomiting center is in the medulla","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-33.jpg","cap":"Vomiting originates in the brain: medulla and fourth ventricle","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/D012-36.jpg","cap":"Medullary vomiting center receives input from the chemoreceptor trigger zone","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-99-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: THE VOMITING CENTER IS LOCATED IN THE MEDULLA","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"FACT","a":"A","why":"<b>Fact.</b> The <b>vomiting center is in the medulla</b>. It receives input from the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the floor of the <b>fourth ventricle</b>, the GI (gastrointestinal) tract, the throat and the vestibular system. Vomiting is <b>not</b> caused by gastric contraction; it needs the stomach and pylorus relaxed.<br>Common mix-up: the trigger zone (fourth ventricle) is separate from the medullary center that runs the act.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 33 (Vomiting)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":99,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 61"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":24,"label":"Vomiting: sphincters relax, abdomen and diaphragm contract","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-33.jpg","cap":"Vomiting: from medulla; stomach, pylorus relaxed; forceful inhalation; abdominal contraction","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/D012-36.jpg","cap":"Vomiting steps: reverse peristalsis, forced inspiration, LES relaxation, glottis closure","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-85-1","s":"A Juventus soccer player wanted to fuel up right before a big match against Inter Milan. He found a half-eaten plate of ribollita that had been sitting in the locker room for well over 6 weeks since their last match. He consumed the meal, and right when the match was starting, he got the urge to throw up. Which of the following is correct concerning the vomiting response?","o":["A. Forced expiration to decrease abdominal pressure","B. Opening of the glottis","C. Forward peristaltic contractions from stomach to intestine","D. Movement of the larynx upward and forward, and relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Movement of the larynx upward and forward, and relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter","a":"D","why":"<b>D. The larynx moves up and forward, and the LES relaxes.</b> In vomiting the larynx rises, the glottis closes, the stomach and LES (lower esophageal sphincter) relax, and a <b>forceful inhalation</b> plus <b>abdominal muscle contraction</b> raises abdominal pressure.<br>A. It is forced inspiration, and it raises abdominal pressure.<br>B. The glottis closes to protect the airway.<br>C. Contractions run backward (reverse peristalsis), not forward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 33 (Vomiting)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":85,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-52-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 62"},{"id":"D066-40-1","s":"All of the following are components of the vomiting reflex EXCEPT?","o":["A. Slow waves","B. Forced expiration to increase abdominal pressure","C. Reverse peristaltic contractions","D. Relaxation of stomach"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Forced expiration to increase abdominal pressure","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Forced expiration.</b> Vomiting uses a forced <b>inspiration</b>, plus contraction of the abdominal muscles, to raise abdominal pressure. Expiration is not a step.<br>A. Slow waves: tempting, since they are the gut's background electrical rhythm. But the reflex starts with a change in that rhythm before reverse peristalsis, so slow waves take part. Option B is the one that names a step backward, so B is the best answer.<br>C. Reverse peristalsis: a true step; it moves intestinal contents back into the stomach.<br>D. Relaxation of the stomach (and pylorus): a true step.","ex":"","fl":["B is the intended answer and is right: the reflex uses forced INSPIRATION (G039:33 'Forceful inhalation'). But option A (slow waves) is not a step of the vomiting reflex either: Costanzo's sequence is reverse peristalsis, relaxation of stomach and pylorus, forced inspiration, relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter, closure of the glottis, expulsion. The TA counted it as a component only because the pasted lecture slide prints 'Slow waves' in red as its last line. Pick B."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 33 (Vomiting)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":40,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-52-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 63"},{"id":"G067-52-1","s":"Vomiting is a complex process that requires coordination of numerous components by the vomiting center located in the medulla. Which of the following occurs during the vomiting act?","o":["A. Lower esophageal sphincter: Contract; Upper esophageal sphincter: Contract; Abdominal muscles: Contract; Diaphragm: Contract","B. Lower esophageal sphincter: Contract; Upper esophageal sphincter: Contract; Abdominal muscles: Relax; Diaphragm: Relax","C. Lower esophageal sphincter: Relax; Upper esophageal sphincter: Contract; Abdominal muscles: Contract; Diaphragm: Relax","D. Lower esophageal sphincter: Relax; Upper esophageal sphincter: Relax; Abdominal muscles: Contract; Diaphragm: Contract"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lower esophageal sphincter: Relax; Upper esophageal sphincter: Relax; Abdominal muscles: Contract; Diaphragm: Contract","a":"D","why":"<b>D. LES relaxed, UES relaxed, abdominal muscles and diaphragm contract.</b> The <b>medulla</b> runs vomiting. The stomach and LES (lower esophageal sphincter) relax, a forceful breath is taken against a closed glottis, the UES (upper esophageal sphincter) opens, and the <b>abdominal muscles and diaphragm contract</b> to squeeze the stomach.<br>A. Contracted sphincters would block the vomitus.<br>B. A relaxed abdomen and diaphragm give no squeeze.<br>C. A closed UES with a relaxed diaphragm cannot expel anything.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 33 (Vomiting)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":52,"f":"G067"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"96"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":24,"label":"Retching: UES closed, LES open","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-36.jpg","cap":"In retching the UES stays closed while the LES is open","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G039-33.jpg","cap":"Vomiting needs the stomach and pylorus relaxed, with forceful inhalation","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-101-1","s":"FILL IN THE BLANK: DURING RETCHING THE UPPER ESOPHAGEAL SPHINCTER IS OPEN/CLOSED WHILE THE LOWER ESOPHAGEAL SPHINCTER IS OPEN/CLOSED","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"UPPER ESOPHAGEAL SPHINCTER CLOSED; LOWER ESOPHAGEAL SPHINCTER OPEN","a":null,"why":"<b>UES closed; LES open.</b> In retching, the abdominal muscles and diaphragm squeeze the stomach. The LES (lower esophageal sphincter) is open, so contents move up into the esophagus. The UES (upper esophageal sphincter) stays <b>closed</b>, so nothing leaves the mouth, and the contents fall back into the stomach.<br>Common mix-up: in true vomiting the UES also opens.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 33 (Vomiting)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":101,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C3 extra 64"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":25,"label":"Colon: frequent haustrations, rare mass movements","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Colon: segmentation (mixing) and peristaltic contractions, plus mass movements","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-16-1","s":"Which is the most frequent type of movement in descending colon?","o":["a. Mass movements","b. Retropulsion","c. Haustrations","d. Peristalsis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Haustrations","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Haustrations.</b> These slow <b>segmenting</b> (mixing) contractions of the colon's pouches occur about every <b>thirty minutes</b>, so they are the most frequent colon movement.<br>A. Mass movements: strong but rare, about one to four times a day.<br>B. Retropulsion: a movement of the stomach's antrum.<br>D. Peristalsis: weak and infrequent in the colon compared with haustral mixing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":50,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":16,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":126,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":39,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"97"},{"id":"D015-105-1","s":"HOW OFTEN DO HAUSTRATIONS OCCUR?","o":["A. EVERY 20 MIN","B. EVERY 30 MIN","C. EVERY 1 HOUR","D. EVERY DAY"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"EVERY 30 MIN","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Every thirty minutes.</b> <b>Haustrations</b> are slow segmenting contractions of the colon's pouches (haustra). They occur about every thirty minutes and each lasts about a minute, mixing the contents for water absorption.<br>A. Twenty minutes: too often.<br>C. One hour: too rare.<br>D. Every day: closer to mass movements, which occur only a few times a day.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":105,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-16-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 65"},{"id":"D015-103-1","s":"MASS MOVEMENTS OCCUR EVERY HOW MANY DAYS?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1-4 TIMES PER DAY","a":null,"why":"<b>One to four times per day</b>, not every few days. <b>Mass movements</b> are strong waves that push stool over long stretches of the transverse and descending colon into the rectum. They are most common after meals because of the <b>gastrocolic reflex</b>.<br>Common mix-up: haustrations are frequent (about every thirty minutes); mass movements are rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":103,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-16-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 66"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":25,"label":"Gastrocolic reflex: eat, then urge to defecate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Large bowel: mass movements; gastrocolic reflex, eat and poop","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/D012-38.jpg","cap":"Gastrocolic reflex: stomach distension increases colon motility and mass movements","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-13-1","s":"A 54-year-old woman eats a healthy meal. Approximately 20 min later the woman feels the urge to defecate. Which of the following reflexes results in the urge to defecate when the stomach is stretched?","o":["a. Duodenocolic reflex","b. Gastrocolic reflex","c. Intestino-intestinal reflex","d. Rectosphincteric"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic reflex","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastrocolic reflex.</b> Food stretching the stomach raises colon motility and the frequency of <b>mass movements</b>. These fill the rectum and cause the urge to defecate.<br>A. Duodenocolic reflex: starts in the duodenum, not the stomach.<br>C. Intestinointestinal reflex: overdistention of one segment relaxes the rest.<br>D. Rectosphincteric reflex: rectal filling relaxes the internal sphincter, after stool arrives.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":44,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":13,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":120,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"98"},{"id":"D040-75-1","s":"9) Which of the following stimulates the gastrocolic reflex?","o":["A. Distention of the stomach by gas","B. Increased contents in the colon","C. Decreased contents in the stomach","D. Distention of the stomach by food","E. Distention of the colon by gastric juices"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Distention of the stomach by food","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Distention of the stomach by food.</b> The stretch of a meal starts the gastrocolic reflex, so colon motility and <b>mass movements</b> increase.<br>A. Gas: the reflex also has a hormonal limb (CCK, cholecystokinin, and gastrin) that needs food.<br>B. More contents in the colon: the stimulus is in the stomach, not the colon.<br>C. An emptier stomach: the opposite of the stimulus.<br>E. The reflex is sensed in the stomach, not the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":75,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-13-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 67"},{"id":"D036-52-1","s":"What is the gastrocolic reflex?","o":["A. Serotonin mediated activation of neurons that cause caudad propulsion of chyme in the small and large intestines","B. Distention of stomach by food increases the motility of the colon and increases the frequency of mass movements in the large intestine","C. As the rectum fills with feces, the smooth muscle wall of the rectum contracts and the internal sphincter relaxes","D. Distension of the stomach followed by smooth muscle wall relaxation of the orad stomach"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Distention of stomach by food increases the motility of the colon and increases the frequency of mass movements in the large intestine","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> In the gastrocolic reflex, <b>distention of the stomach by food</b> increases <b>colon motility</b> and the frequency of <b>mass movements</b>.<br>A. Serotonin-triggered propulsion: the peristaltic reflex.<br>C. Rectal contraction with internal sphincter relaxation: the rectosphincteric reflex.<br>D. Orad stomach relaxation after distention: receptive relaxation, a vagovagal reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":52,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-13-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 68"},{"id":"G061-14-1","s":"Which of the following may trigger an urge to defecate after a meal?","o":["A. Rectosphincteric reflex","B. Gastroileal reflex","C. Intestinointestinal reflex","D. Peristaltic rushes","E. Gastrocolic reflex"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic reflex","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Gastrocolic reflex.</b> <b>Stretch of the stomach</b> by a meal increases colon motility and <b>mass movements</b>. These push stool into the rectum and cause the urge ('eat and poop').<br>A. Rectosphincteric reflex: rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter; it follows the arrival of stool.<br>B. Gastroileal reflex: a meal opens the ileocecal valve; it moves chyme into the colon, not stool out.<br>C. Intestinointestinal reflex: overdistention of one gut segment relaxes the rest.<br>D. Peristaltic rushes: intense small-bowel waves during irritation, not the normal urge after meals.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":14,"f":"G061"},{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":4,"f":"G063"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":14,"f":"D003"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-13-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C3 extra 69"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":25,"label":"Gastrocolic efferent limb: CCK and gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-38.jpg","cap":"Afferent limb parasympathetic; efferent limb hormones CCK and gastrin","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Gastrocolic reflex: eat and poop","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-14-1","s":"Which of the following mediates the efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex?","o":["A. Parasympathetic stimulation","B. CCK and Gastrin","C. Motilin and CCK","D. Sympathetic stimulation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK and Gastrin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. CCK and gastrin.</b> In the gastrocolic reflex, stomach stretch is sensed through <b>parasympathetic afferents</b> (the afferent limb). The <b>efferent limb</b> that raises colon motility is carried by the hormones <b>CCK (cholecystokinin) and gastrin</b>.<br>A. Parasympathetic: forms the afferent limb.<br>C. Motilin: drives the MMC (migrating myoelectric complex) between meals.<br>D. Sympathetic: decreases motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":14,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":57,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":57,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":225,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"99"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":25,"label":"Holding stool: rectosphincteric reflex vs external sphincter","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Mass movements and the gastrocolic reflex fill the rectum after meals","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G039-3.jpg","cap":"Anal sphincter (external) is skeletal muscle, under voluntary control","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-46-1","s":"A healthy 21 year old woman eats a big meal and then takes a 3 hour ride on a bus that does not have a bathroom. Twenty minutes after eating the woman feels a strong urge to defecate, but manages to hold it. Which of the following have occured in this woman? (rectosphincteric reflex)","o":["A. Relaxation of internal anal sphincter: No; Contraction of external anal sphincter: No; Contraction of rectum: No","B. Relaxation of internal anal sphincter: No; Contraction of external anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of rectum: Yes","C. Relaxation of internal anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of external anal sphincter: No; Contraction of rectum: Yes","D. Relaxation of internal anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of external anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of rectum: Yes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Relaxation of internal anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of external anal sphincter: Yes; Contraction of rectum: Yes","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Yes, yes, yes.</b> Mass movements fill the rectum. In the <b>rectosphincteric reflex</b> the <b>rectum contracts</b> and the <b>internal anal sphincter</b> (smooth, involuntary) <b>relaxes</b>, causing the urge. She holds it by contracting the <b>external anal sphincter</b>, which is skeletal and voluntary.<br>A. With no reflex there would be no urge.<br>B. The internal sphincter does relax with rectal filling.<br>C. Without external sphincter contraction she could not hold it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)","L50 slide 3 (Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":46,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"100"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":26,"label":"Submandibular glands make ~70% of saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-9.jpg","cap":"Gland drawing with output shares: submandibular 65–70%, parotid 25%, sublingual 5%.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G042-5.jpg","cap":"Two submandibular glands make 70% of saliva; parotids 25%; sublingual 5%.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D023-42-1","s":"Which salivary glands produce the most saliva?","o":["A. Parotid glands","B. Sublingual glands","C. Submandibular glands"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular glands","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The two <b>submandibular glands</b>, on the floor of the mouth, make about <b>seventy percent</b> of saliva, a mixed serous and mucous secretion.<br>A. Parotid glands: the largest glands but only about twenty-five percent; purely serous.<br>B. Sublingual glands: about five percent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 5 (Structure of the Salivary Glands)","L52 slide 9 (65-70%)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":42,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":63,"f":"D061"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":81,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":81,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":249,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":124,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"101"},{"id":"F1203-85-1","s":"Which salivary glands are responsible for the majority of saliva produced?","o":["A. Parotid Glands","B. Sublingual Glands and Parotid Glands","C. Submandibular Glands","D. Sublingual Glands"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular Glands","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The two <b>submandibular glands</b>, on the floor of the mouth, make about <b>seventy percent</b> of saliva.<br>A. Parotid glands: in front of the ears; about twenty-five percent.<br>B. Sublingual plus parotid: about thirty percent together, still less than the submandibular.<br>D. Sublingual glands: about five percent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 5 (Structure of the Salivary Glands)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":85,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D023-42-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":26,"label":"Parotid: purely serous, watery saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-8.jpg","cap":"Parotid gland labelled 'Very Serous!!' beside submandibular and sublingual glands.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G042-13.jpg","cap":"Serous cells, mucous cells and a mixed acinus draining into ductal cells.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-87-1","s":"Which of the following is the major salivary gland that produces a serous, watery secretion?","o":["A. Parotid gland","B. Submandibular gland","C. Sublingual gland","D. Accessory salivary glands","E. None of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The <b>parotid gland</b> is <b>purely serous</b>: its saliva is thin and watery (lecture: 'Very serous!!').<br>B. Submandibular: mixed serous and mucous.<br>C. Sublingual: mixed, mostly mucous.<br>D. Accessory (minor) glands: mostly mucous.<br>E. None of the above: the parotid fits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 5 (Structure of the Salivary Glands)","L52 slide 8 (Very Serous!!)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":87,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"102"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":26,"label":"Acinus makes saliva; ducts modify it","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-10.jpg","cap":"Acinus makes the saliva; intercalated and striated ducts modify it.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G042-12.jpg","cap":"Ductal cells remove sodium and chloride, add bicarbonate and potassium.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-85-1","s":"Which cells are responsible for the modification of primary saliva?","o":["A. Acinar cells","B. Myoepithelial cells","C. Endothelial cells","D. Ductal cells","E. Chief cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ductal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The <b>acinus makes</b> the primary saliva; <b>ductal cells modify</b> it, removing sodium and chloride and adding potassium and bicarbonate.<br>A. Acinar cells: make the initial, plasma-like saliva.<br>B. Myoepithelial cells: contract to eject saliva.<br>C. Endothelial cells: line blood vessels.<br>E. Chief cells: gastric cells that make pepsinogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 10 (Acinus MAKES the saliva)","L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":85,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 2"},{"id":"D015-108-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING MODIFIES THE INITIAL SALIVA BY ALTERING CONCENTRATIONS OF ELECTROLYTES?","o":["A. ACINAR CELLS","B. DUCTAL CELLS","C. MYOEPITHELIAL CELLS","D. All THE ABOVE"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"DUCTAL CELLS","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Ductal cells</b> change the electrolytes: they <b>remove sodium and chloride</b> and <b>add potassium and bicarbonate</b>.<br>A. Acinar cells: make the initial saliva, with the same electrolytes as plasma.<br>C. Myoepithelial cells: contract to eject saliva.<br>D. All the above: only ductal cells modify it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 10 (Acinus MAKES the saliva)","L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":108,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":26,"label":"Myoepithelial cells contract to eject saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-11.jpg","cap":"Acinus micrograph labelling myoepithelial cells, which contract to eject saliva.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-100-1","s":"Which of the following is true in regards to the to the salivary glands?","o":["A. There are three glands which contain serous and mucous cells","B. Myoepithelial cells line the acini and intercalated duct, receiving neural input","C. Centroacinar cells in the acini secrete bicarbonate","D. The salivary glands responds to parasympathetic nervous system innervation only"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Myoepithelial cells line the acini and intercalated duct, receiving neural input","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Myoepithelial cells</b> wrap the <b>acini and intercalated ducts</b>; nerve input makes them contract and eject saliva.<br>A. Only two glands are mixed; the parotid is purely serous.<br>C. Centroacinar cells that secrete bicarbonate are in the pancreas, not salivary glands.<br>D. Salivary glands get both parasympathetic and sympathetic input, and both raise secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 11 (Structure of Gland)","L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":100,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"103"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":27,"label":"Saliva is hypotonic: water-tight ducts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-14.jpg","cap":"Saliva starts isotonic and enters the mouth hypotonic.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G042-16.jpg","cap":"Ducts impermeable to water; salt reabsorbed, water left behind.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-71-1","s":"Which of the following is characteristic of saliva?","o":["A. Hypotonicity relative to plasma","B. A lower HCO3- concentration than plasma","C. The presence of proteases","D. Secretion rate that is increased by vagotomy","E. Modification by the salivary ductal cells involves reabsorption of K+ and HCO3-"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypotonicity relative to plasma","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Final saliva is <b>hypotonic</b> to plasma: water-tight ducts remove more salt than they add.<br>B. Bicarbonate is higher than plasma, not lower.<br>C. Saliva has amylase and lipase but no protein-digesting enzyme.<br>D. Salivary glands get parasympathetic input through cranial nerves seven and nine; losing parasympathetic input lowers, never raises, saliva.<br>E. Ducts secrete, not reabsorb, potassium and bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)","L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":71,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":71,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":239,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"104"},{"id":"D015-110-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: COMPARED TO PLASMA, SALIVA IS HYPOTONIC","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"FACT","a":"A","why":"<b>Fact.</b> Saliva starts <b>isotonic</b> but leaves <b>hypotonic</b>: ducts absorb more sodium chloride than they secrete potassium bicarbonate, and they are <b>impermeable to water</b>, so water stays behind.<br>Common mix-up: the starting acinar saliva is isotonic; only the final saliva is hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":110,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 4"},{"id":"G067-67-1","s":"How does the hypotonic nature of saliva arise as it flows through the ductal cells?","o":["A. The ductal cells actively absorb water, diluting the solute concentrations and making saliva hypotonic.","B. The ductal cells are impermeable to water, and since more NaCl is absorbed than KHCO3 is secreted, the final saliva becomes hypotonic.","C. The ductal cells secrete water while absorbing NaCl and KHCO3, making the saliva hypotonic.","D. Saliva becomes hypotonic due to the passive diffusion of Na+ and Cl− from the ductal cells into the bloodstream."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The ductal cells are impermeable to water, and since more NaCl is absorbed than KHCO3 is secreted, the final saliva becomes hypotonic.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Ducts are <b>impermeable to water</b>, and they <b>absorb more sodium chloride than they secrete potassium bicarbonate</b>. Solute leaves, water stays, so saliva turns hypotonic.<br>A. Ducts do not absorb water; taking water out would concentrate saliva anyway.<br>C. Ducts do not secrete water.<br>D. Sodium is actively pumped, not passively leaked, and the water-tight duct is the key point.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)","L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":67,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"105"},{"id":"D066-20-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes the composition of final saliva and why?","o":["A. Hypotonic because acinar cells are impermeable to H20","B. Hypertonic because acinar cells are permeable to H20","C. Hypertonic because ductal cells are permeable to H20","D. Hypotonic because ductal cells are impermeable to H20"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypotonic because ductal cells are impermeable to H20","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hypotonic, because ductal cells are impermeable to water.</b> The acinus makes an isotonic fluid. The ducts remove more sodium chloride than the potassium bicarbonate they add, and <b>water cannot follow</b> the salt out. The saliva left behind is dilute.<br>A. Hypotonic is right, but the cause lies in the ducts; acinar cells make the isotonic first fluid.<br>B. Final saliva is never hypertonic.<br>C. Final saliva is not hypertonic, and the ducts do not let water through.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)","L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)","L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":20,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":27,"label":"Ducts remove NaCl, add potassium and bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-12.jpg","cap":"Ductal cells remove sodium and chloride, put bicarbonate and potassium into saliva.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G042-22.jpg","cap":"Saliva vs serum: sodium 15 vs 140, chloride 15 vs 110 mEq/L.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D007-88.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figures 8.12–8.13: duct ion transport and saliva versus plasma ion levels.","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 88"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-112-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING STATEMENT IS TRUE. COMPARED TO PLASMA, SALIVA:","o":["A. HAS A LOWER CONCENTRATION OF POTASSIUM & BICARBONATE","B. HAS A LOWER CONCENTRATION OF SODIUM & CHLORIDE","C. HAS A HIGHER CONCENTRATION OF SODIUM & CHLORIDE","D. HAS A LOWER WATER CONCENTRATION"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HAS A LOWER CONCENTRATION OF SODIUM & CHLORIDE","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Ducts <b>remove sodium and chloride</b> and add potassium and bicarbonate, so saliva has <b>lower sodium and chloride</b> than plasma.<br>A. Potassium and bicarbonate are higher, not lower.<br>C. Sodium and chloride are lower, not higher.<br>D. Saliva is hypotonic (fewer solutes), so it holds more water per volume, not less.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 22 (Serum Sodium 140 meq/L)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":112,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 6"},{"id":"D036-89-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes saliva composition compared to plasma?","o":["A. Higher Na+, lower K+","B. Lower Na+, higher K+","C. Higher bicarbonate, lower chloride","D. Lower bicarbonate, higher chloride","E. Isotonic"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Higher bicarbonate, lower chloride","a":"C","why":"<b>B and C are both true.</b> Ducts <b>remove sodium and chloride</b> and <b>add potassium and bicarbonate</b>, so saliva has <b>lower sodium and chloride</b> and <b>higher potassium and bicarbonate</b> than plasma.<br>A. Reversed: sodium is lower, potassium higher.<br>D. Reversed: bicarbonate is higher, chloride lower.<br>E. Saliva is hypotonic, not isotonic.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only C, but B is equally true: saliva has lower sodium and higher potassium than plasma (ducts remove sodium chloride and add potassium bicarbonate, G042:12)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 22 (Serum Sodium 140 meq/L)","L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":89,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 7"},{"id":"G067-65-1","s":"Which of the following best explains the net change in solute concentrations in saliva as it passes through the ductal cells?","o":["A. Net absorption of Na+ and Cl− and secretion of K+ and HCO3− results in an increase in the concentration of Na+ and Cl− in the saliva.","B. Net absorption of Na+ and Cl− and secretion of K+ and HCO3− results in a decrease in the concentration of Na+ and Cl− and an increase in the concentration of K+ and HCO3− in the saliva.","C. Net absorption of Na+ and Cl− and secretion of K+ and HCO3− results in no change in the solute concentrations of Na+, Cl−, K+, and HCO3− in the saliva.","D. There is no transport of solutes across the ductal cells, and therefore, the composition of saliva remains the same as plasma."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Net absorption of Na+ and Cl− and secretion of K+ and HCO3− results in a decrease in the concentration of Na+ and Cl− and an increase in the concentration of K+ and HCO3− in the saliva.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Ducts absorb sodium and chloride and secrete potassium and bicarbonate, so <b>sodium and chloride fall</b> and <b>potassium and bicarbonate rise</b> in saliva.<br>A. Absorbing sodium and chloride lowers, not raises, their concentration.<br>C. Net transport does change the concentrations.<br>D. Ducts do transport ions, and saliva ends up unlike plasma.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":65,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 8"},{"id":"D067-48-1","s":"What ions are absorbed during the production of saliva? Select all that apply","o":["A. K+","B. Cl-","C. HCO3-","D. Na+","E. H2O"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cl-; Na+","a":"B,D","why":"<b>B. Cl- and D. Na+.</b> The acinus makes a plasma-like fluid. The duct cells then <b>reabsorb sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-)</b> and secrete potassium and bicarbonate.<br>A. K+ (potassium): secreted into saliva, so it ends above its plasma level.<br>C. HCO3- (bicarbonate): secreted into saliva.<br>E. H2O: water is not an ion, and the ducts are nearly impermeable to it. Water stays behind while salt leaves, which is why saliva ends hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":48,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 9"},{"id":"D040-102-1","s":"Which of the following is true about how saliva is formed?","o":["A. Acinar cells secrete saliva that is initially hypertonic and is modified by ductal cells to become hypotonic","B. Amylase, Lipase, Mucin, IgA, and Kallikrein are secreted by ductal cells","C. The sympathetic nervous system halts all saliva production","D. Ductal cells modify saliva by exchange channels and active transport that make a net absorption of Na+ and Cl- and net secretion of K+ and HCO3-"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ductal cells modify saliva by exchange channels and active transport that make a net absorption of Na+ and Cl- and net secretion of K+ and HCO3-","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Ductal cells use exchangers and the sodium-potassium pump to give <b>net absorption of sodium and chloride</b> and <b>net secretion of potassium and bicarbonate</b>.<br>A. Initial saliva is isotonic, not hypertonic; it ends hypotonic.<br>B. Amylase, lipase, mucin and kallikrein are made by acinar cells; ducts only change the ions.<br>C. Sympathetic input lowers volume but still stimulates saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":102,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 10"},{"id":"D022-17-1","s":"In the process of making saliva, what is the function of ductal cells in salivary glands:","o":["a. These cells contract to eject saliva in the mouth","b. These cells produce a saliva which has highest relative concentration of potassium as compared to plasma","c. These cells produce initial saliva with relative osmolarity as compared to plasma","d. They add proteolytic enzymes to saliva"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"These cells produce a saliva which has highest relative concentration of potassium as compared to plasma","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Ductal cells</b> secrete potassium, so final saliva has <b>potassium far above plasma</b>; potassium is the ion most concentrated relative to plasma.<br>A. Contracting to eject saliva: myoepithelial cells.<br>C. Making the initial plasma-like (isotonic) saliva: acinar cells.<br>D. Saliva has no protein-digesting enzymes; acinar cells add amylase and lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 11 (Structure of Gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":5,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":1,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":17,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":152,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":5,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":5,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":25,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"106"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":27,"label":"Duct basolateral membrane: sodium-potassium pump","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-114.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.12 duct cell: ATP-driven sodium-potassium pump and chloride channel on blood side.","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-93-1","s":"Which transporter is found on the basolateral membrane of salivary gland ductal cells?","o":["A. Na+-Glucose cotransporter","B. Na+-K+ ATPase","C. Na+-HCO3- cotransporter","D. Cl--HCO3- exchanger","E. K+ channel"],"g":["B"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cl--HCO3- exchanger","a":"D","why":"<b>B.</b> The basolateral (blood-side) membrane of duct cells has the <b>sodium-potassium pump (Na+-K+ ATPase, an adenosine triphosphate-driven pump)</b>, which moves sodium into blood, plus chloride channels (Costanzo Figure 8.12).<br>A. Sodium-glucose cotransporter: in intestine and kidney, not salivary ducts.<br>C. Sodium-bicarbonate cotransporter: not part of the salivary duct model taught.<br>D. Chloride-bicarbonate exchanger: on the luminal side, where it puts bicarbonate into saliva.<br>E. Potassium enters saliva by luminal hydrogen-potassium exchange; no basolateral potassium channel.","ex":"","fl":["The Cl–HCO3- exchanger is on the LUMINAL membrane of salivary ductal cells (it secretes HCO3- into saliva); the basolateral membrane carries the Na+-K+ ATPase (plus Cl- channels) (Costanzo Fig. 8.12; G042:12 'remove salt, put bicarbonate into saliva'). Correct answer: B."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":93,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":28,"label":"Slow flow: more duct time, more NaCl removed","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-16.jpg","cap":"Sodium chloride reabsorption needs time: less flow = less sodium and chloride.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G042-17.jpg","cap":"Flow-rate graph: sodium, chloride, bicarbonate rise with flow; potassium falls.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G066-106.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figures 8.12–8.13 with note on contact time and the bicarbonate exception.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-50-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding saliva at LOW flow rate?","o":["A. Extra bicarbonate is secreted into saliva","B. Lower IgA concentrations are available","C. More likely to be hypertonic","D. Sodium and chloride reabsorbed more","E. Less potassium is secreted"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sodium and chloride reabsorbed more","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> At <b>low flow</b>, saliva stays longer in the ducts, so <b>more sodium and chloride are reabsorbed</b>.<br>A. Bicarbonate is lower at low flow; its secretion is stimulated at high flow.<br>B. Slow saliva is less diluted, so IgA (immunoglobulin A) is not lower.<br>C. Low flow makes saliva more hypotonic, not hypertonic.<br>E. More potassium, not less, is secreted at low flow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)","L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":50,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":48,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"107"},{"id":"D064-10-1","s":"Flow rate (ml/min) is dictating Tonicity? Hypotonic vs Isotonic - Contact time?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Lower flow = more ductal contact time = more net NaCl reabsorption (ducts impermeable to water) = more hypotonic saliva; at high flow saliva is closer to isotonic. Exception: HCO3- is higher at high flow (stimulated secretion).","a":null,"why":"<b>Lower flow = more contact time</b> in the water-tight ducts, so more sodium chloride is removed and saliva is <b>more hypotonic</b>. At <b>high flow</b> saliva stays closer to isotonic (plasma-like).<br>Exception to the contact-time rule: <b>bicarbonate rises with flow</b>, because stimulation (such as parasympathetic input) selectively boosts bicarbonate secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)","L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":10,"f":"D064"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 12"},{"id":"D015-117-1","s":"FILL IN THE BLANK: AT LOW/HIGH FLOW RATES FINAL SALIVA COMPOSITION MOST RESEMBLES PLASMA","o":["A. LOW","B. HIGH"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HIGH","a":"B","why":"<b>High.</b> At <b>high flow</b>, saliva spends little time in the ducts, so there is <b>less time to modify</b> it; it stays closest to plasma (the initial isotonic saliva).<br>Common mix-up: low flow gives the most change: lowest sodium and chloride, highest potassium, most hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":117,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 13"},{"id":"D067-51-1","s":"A higher flow rate means saliva composition will be most dissimilar to plasma concentration","o":["A. TRUE","B. FALSE"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"FALSE","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> At a <b>high flow rate</b> saliva passes through the ducts quickly, so the ductal cells have little time to remove sodium and chloride or to add potassium. The saliva stays <b>most like plasma</b>.<br>Common mix-up: low flow gives the most change (lowest sodium and chloride, highest potassium, most hypotonic). Bicarbonate is the one exception: it is highest at high flow, because stimulation switches on its secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":51,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 14"},{"id":"G067-69-1","s":"How does the salivary flow rate influence the ionic composition of saliva?","o":["A. At high flow rates, the final saliva has higher concentrations of Na+ and Cl− and lower concentrations of K+.","B. At low flow rates, the final saliva has higher concentrations of Na+ and Cl− and lower concentrations of K+.","C. At high flow rates, the final saliva is most similar to plasma and the initial saliva produced by acinar cells, while at low flow rates, the final saliva has lower Na+ and Cl− concentrations and higher K+ concentrations.","D. Flow rate does not influence the ionic composition of saliva; it remains constant."],"g":["A","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"At high flow rates, the final saliva is most similar to plasma and the initial saliva produced by acinar cells, while at low flow rates, the final saliva has lower Na+ and Cl− concentrations and higher K+ concentrations.","a":"C","why":"<b>A and C are both true.</b> At <b>high flow</b> ducts have little time to work, so saliva stays close to <b>plasma and the initial acinar saliva</b>: more sodium and chloride, less potassium. At <b>low flow</b> more sodium and chloride are removed and more potassium added.<br>B. Reversed: low flow gives lower sodium and chloride and higher potassium.<br>D. Flow rate clearly changes the ion pattern.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only C, but A is also true: at high flow saliva has more sodium and chloride and less potassium than at low flow (G042:17)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":69,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 15"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":28,"label":"Bicarbonate falls at low flow, rises at high","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-17.jpg","cap":"Low flow: sodium, chloride, bicarbonate low; high flow: all high; potassium falls.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G042-16.jpg","cap":"Potassium and bicarbonate pushed into saliva: more flow = more bicarbonate.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G066-106.jpg","cap":"Graph plus note: bicarbonate secretion is stimulated at high flow.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-75-1","s":"Flow rate changes the composition of saliva. How would a low flow rate affect the composition of saliva?","o":["A. Sodium low, chloride low, bicarbonate high, hypotonic saliva","B. Sodium low, chloride low, bicarbonate low, hypotonic saliva","C. Sodium high, chloride high, bicarbonate low, hypertonic saliva","D. Sodium high, chloride high, bicarbonate high, hypertonic saliva"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sodium low, chloride low, bicarbonate low, hypotonic saliva","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> At <b>low flow</b>: <b>sodium low, chloride low, bicarbonate low</b>, and saliva is <b>hypotonic</b>. Ducts have time to remove salt and stay water-tight.<br>A. Bicarbonate is low, not high, at low flow; it rises with flow.<br>C. Sodium and chloride are low at low flow, and saliva is never hypertonic.<br>D. Sodium, chloride and bicarbonate are all low at low flow, and saliva is never hypertonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":75,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":75,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":243,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":91,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":127,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":83,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-104-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 16"},{"id":"D036-91-1","s":"How does low salivary flow rate affect HCO3- concentration?","o":["A. It is increased","B. It is decreased","C. It is unchanged","D. It equals plasma HCO3-","E. It approaches zero"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It is decreased","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Bicarbonate secretion is <b>stimulated along with saliva output</b>, so <b>low flow = low bicarbonate</b> (lecture: 'More flow = more bicarbonate').<br>A. Increased: the opposite; bicarbonate rises at high flow.<br>C. Unchanged: bicarbonate clearly changes with flow.<br>D. Equal to plasma: no; it is lowest at low flow and climbs above plasma as flow rises.<br>E. Approaches zero: too strong; it is low, not absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)","L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":91,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-104-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 17"},{"id":"G066-104-1","s":"Salivary composition is affected by flow rate, however, the bicarbonate concentration is high during high flow rate. What is the reason for this?","o":["A. Not enough time to reabsorb bicarbonate","B. Bicarbonate is always high in saliva","C. Flow rate does not affect bicarbonate levels","D. Bicarbonate production is high when saliva production is high"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bicarbonate production is high when saliva production is high (selective stimulation)","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Bicarbonate breaks the contact-time rule: stimulating saliva (for example by parasympathetic nerves) <b>selectively stimulates bicarbonate secretion</b>, so <b>more flow = more bicarbonate</b>.<br>A. Ducts secrete bicarbonate; they do not reabsorb it.<br>B. Bicarbonate is low at low flow, so it is not always high.<br>C. Flow rate clearly changes bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":104,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":103,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"108"},{"id":"D067-52-1","s":"At a high salivary flow rate the ductal cells have less contact time to modify the saliva, so its composition stays closer to plasma. Which ion does NOT follow this contact-time explanation?","o":["A. Na+","B. HCO3-","C. Cl-","D. K+"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HCO3-","a":"B","why":"<b>B. HCO3- (bicarbonate).</b> Contact time predicts that a secreted ion is highest at slow flow. Bicarbonate does the opposite: it is <b>highest at high flow</b>, well above plasma, because the nerves that raise flow also switch on ductal bicarbonate secretion.<br>A. Na+ (sodium): less time to reabsorb it at high flow, so it rises toward plasma. Fits.<br>C. Cl- (chloride): same as sodium. Fits.<br>D. K+ (potassium): less time to secrete it, so it falls toward plasma. Fits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 16 (Ducts are Impermeable to water)","L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":52,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-104-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 18"},{"id":"D023-41-1","s":"Which represents fast Salivary flow?","o":["A. High Sodium, High Bicarb, High Chloride, Low Potassium","B. High Sodium, High Chloride, Low Bicarb, Low Potassium","C. Low Sodium, Low Bicarb, Low Chloride, High Potassium","D. High Sodium, High Bicarbonate, Low Chloride, Low Potassium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"High Sodium, High Bicarb, High Chloride, Low Potassium","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Fast</b> salivary flow: <b>high sodium, high chloride, high bicarbonate, low potassium</b>. Ducts have little time to modify saliva, and bicarbonate secretion is stimulated.<br>B. High sodium and chloride with low bicarbonate: slow pancreatic juice.<br>C. Low sodium, chloride and bicarbonate with high potassium: slow salivary flow.<br>D. High bicarbonate with low chloride: fast pancreatic juice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 17 (Flow Rate Changes Composition)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":41,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":60,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"109"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Sympathetic also increases saliva; parasympathetic dominant","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Only gland where parasympathetic and sympathetic both increase secretion; parasympathetic main control.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G042-25.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic control: norepinephrine, beta receptors, cAMP; lower volume.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: both parasympathetic and sympathetic arrows feed into saliva.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-102-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding the autonomic innervation of salivary glands?","o":["A. Stimulated by sympathetics only","B. Stimulated by parasympathetics only","C. Stimulated by both, but sympathetic is dominant","D. Stimulated by both, but parasympathetic is dominant"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulated by both, but parasympathetic is dominant","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Salivary glands are <b>stimulated by both</b> systems, but the <b>parasympathetic is dominant</b> (high volume, blocked by atropine).<br>A. Not sympathetic only.<br>B. Not parasympathetic only; sympathetic input also raises secretion.<br>C. The sympathetic system gives a lower volume, so it is not dominant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":102,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":101,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"110"},{"id":"D015-119-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: SALIVARY SECRETION IS INCREASED BY PARASYMPATHETIC STIMULATION AND DECREASED BY SYMPATHETIC STIMULATION","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CAP","a":"B","why":"<b>Cap (false).</b> Salivary secretion is <b>increased by both</b> parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation; the <b>parasympathetic is dominant</b> and gives high volume. Sympathetic input gives a smaller, thicker volume.<br>Common mix-up: elsewhere in the gut the two systems oppose each other, but salivary glands are the exception.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":119,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-102-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 19"},{"id":"D008-73-1","s":"What is the effect of sympathetic stimulation of the parotid salivary gland?","o":["A. Decreased bicarbonate in the saliva","B. Decreased water impermeability","C. Decreased saliva production","D. Increased saliva production","E. Isotonic saliva production"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased saliva production","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Salivary glands are the only glands where <b>sympathetic and parasympathetic input both increase secretion</b>. Sympathetic input gives a smaller, thicker volume.<br>A. Bicarbonate is not decreased; stimulated saliva has more bicarbonate.<br>B. Ducts stay water-impermeable; nerves do not change this.<br>C. Saliva rises, not falls.<br>E. Final saliva stays hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":73,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":73,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":241,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":27,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-102-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 20"},{"id":"D015-66-1","s":"WHICH NEUROTRANSMITTER(S) INCREASE SALIVARY SECRETION?","o":["A. ACETYLCHOLINE (ACH)","B. NOREPINEPHRINE (NE)","C. SUBSTANCE P","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"ALL OF THE ABOVE","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Acetylcholine (ACh)</b>, <b>norepinephrine (NE)</b> and <b>substance P</b> all raise salivary secretion (Costanzo Table 8.1). Salivary glands are stimulated by both autonomic branches.<br>A, B, C. Each is true, but alone each is incomplete.<br>Common mix-up: norepinephrine dries other gut secretions but still raises saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":66,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-102-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 21"},{"id":"D036-98-1","s":"Which of the following combinations promotes high salivary flow rate?","o":["A. Low parasympathetic, low sympathetic","B. High parasympathetic, low sympathetic","C. Low parasympathetic, high sympathetic","D. High parasympathetic, high sympathetic","E. Basal parasympathetic, basal sympathetic"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"High parasympathetic, high sympathetic","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Both systems <b>stimulate</b> salivary glands, so <b>high parasympathetic plus high sympathetic</b> input gives the most flow.<br>A. Low input to both gives little saliva.<br>B. High parasympathetic alone gives a lot, but adding sympathetic gives more.<br>C. High sympathetic alone gives only a small, thick volume.<br>E. Basal input gives resting flow only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":98,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-102-1","kind":"harder","n":"C4 extra 22"},{"id":"D007-99-1","s":"Two anesthesiologists from neighboring countries Gabon and Cameroon were creating a pilot study to look at the factors that decrease salivary secretion. All of the following decrease salivary secretion except:","o":["A. Dehydration","B. Sympathetic stimulation","C. Atropine","D. Sleep"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic stimulation","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Sympathetic stimulation increases</b> saliva, so it is the exception. Norepinephrine on beta receptors gives a smaller, thicker volume.<br>A. Dehydration: inhibits parasympathetic drive, so saliva falls.<br>C. Atropine: blocks muscarinic receptors, so saliva falls.<br>D. Sleep: inhibits parasympathetic drive, so saliva falls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":99,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-102-1","kind":"harder","n":"C4 extra 23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Parasympathetic VII/IX, acetylcholine, muscarinic drive saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic main control: facial and ninth cranial nerves, muscarinic receptors, IP3 raises calcium.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: cranial nerves seven, nine → acetylcholine → muscarinic receptor → calcium.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-107-1","s":"Which neural pathway is primarily responsible for the stimulation of salivary secretion in response to the anticipation or presence of food?","o":["A. Sympathetic nervous system","B. Parasympathetic nervous system","C. Somatic nervous system","D. Enteric nervous system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parasympathetic nervous system","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Seeing, smelling or tasting food drives saliva through the <b>parasympathetic</b> system, the <b>main control</b> of salivary glands.<br>A. Sympathetic: adds a small, thick volume; it is not the food response.<br>C. Somatic nerves control skeletal muscle, not glands.<br>D. Enteric nervous system: lives in the gut wall; salivary glands are under autonomic nerves only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":107,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":106,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 24"},{"id":"D022-18-1","s":"Which of the following is correct for autonomic nervous system regulation of salivary secretory rate?","o":["a. Salivary secretory rate is decreased in response to parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation.","b. Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerves VII and IX.","c. Salivary secretory rate is significantly increased in response to sympathetic stimulation as compared to parasympathetic stimulation.","d. Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerve X."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerves VII and IX.","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> Saliva rises with <b>parasympathetic</b> input carried by <b>cranial nerves seven (facial) and nine (glossopharyngeal)</b>; this is the main control.<br>A. Both systems increase, not decrease, saliva.<br>C. Sympathetic input gives a smaller, thicker volume; parasympathetic is dominant.<br>D. Cranial nerve ten (vagus) supplies the gut tube, not the salivary glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":10,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":18,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":154,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":7,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":7,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"111"},{"id":"D066-43-1","s":"The parasympathetic input to the salivary glands is carried by which nerves?","o":["A. Facial (CN VII) and Vagus (CN X)","B. Facial (CN VII) and Trigeminal (CN V)","C. Facial (CN VII) and Hypoglossal (CN XII)","D. Facial (CN VII) and Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Facial (CN VII) and Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Facial (CN VII) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX).</b> CN means cranial nerve. Parasympathetic fibers reach the submandibular and sublingual glands in the <b>facial nerve</b> and the parotid gland in the <b>glossopharyngeal nerve</b>. They are the main control of saliva.<br>A. Vagus (CN X): supplies the gut from the esophagus to the ascending colon, not the salivary glands.<br>B. Trigeminal (CN V): sensation of the face and the chewing muscles.<br>C. Hypoglossal (CN XII): motor nerve of the tongue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":43,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 25"},{"id":"D040-106-1","s":"After the exam he goes with his friends to get a reward, a giant slice of cake. He begins to salivate at the sight of the cake. Which is true about the pathway of salivary production in this case?","o":["A. ACh is released which stimulates muscarinic cells that lead to the IP3/Ca++ pathway","B. NE is released which stimulates muscarinic cells that lead to the IP3/Ca++ pathway","C. It activates the sympathetic pathway","D. It has no effect on salivation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ACh is released which stimulates muscarinic cells that lead to the IP3/Ca++ pathway","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Salivating at the sight of food is <b>parasympathetic</b>: <b>acetylcholine (ACh)</b> acts on <b>muscarinic receptors</b>, and <b>inositol trisphosphate (IP3)</b> raises calcium.<br>B. Norepinephrine (NE) acts on beta receptors and raises cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate), not on muscarinic receptors.<br>C. The sympathetic pathway is not the food-anticipation response.<br>D. The sight of food clearly increases salivation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":106,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 26"},{"id":"D066-57-1","s":"After a long day of volunteering at a sports medicine event, Lead Head TA Maram begins eating an Italian dish with french fries. She experiences increased salivary secretion, believed to be a parasympathetic response. Which of the following best describes the mechanism responsible for this increase in salivary secretion?","o":["A. Activation of beta-adrenergic receptors increases cAMP, producing abundant watery saliva","B. Activation of M3 receptors increases intracellular Ca2+, promoting watery saliva secretion","C. Activation of alpha-1 adrenergic receptors decreases intracellular Ca2+, increasing saliva production","D. Activation of M3 receptors decreases intracellular cAMP, promoting protein-rich secretion","E. Activation of H2 receptors increases intracellular cAMP, stimulating saliva production"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of M3 receptors increases intracellular Ca2+, promoting watery saliva secretion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. M3 receptors raise intracellular Ca2+ and give watery saliva.</b> Parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine onto <b>muscarinic (M3) receptors</b>. These work through IP3 (inositol trisphosphate), which raises cell calcium (Ca2+) and produces a large volume of watery saliva.<br>A. Beta receptors and cAMP (cyclic AMP): the sympathetic route; small volume, thick saliva.<br>C. Alpha-1 receptors are sympathetic, and they would raise calcium, not lower it.<br>D. M3 signals through calcium; it does not lower cAMP.<br>E. H2 receptors: histamine receptors of gastric parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":57,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"112"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Citric acid: top salivary stimulant","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-26.jpg","cap":"Citric acid: number one stimulant of salivary secretion.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-77-1","s":"What is the number one stimulant of salivary secretion?","o":["A. Aldosterone","B. Citric Acid","C. Acetylcholine","D. Prostaglandin","E. Beta-adrenergic stimulation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Citric Acid","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The lecture calls <b>citric acid</b> the <b>number-one stimulant</b> of salivary secretion; sour taste triggers a strong salivary reflex.<br>A. Aldosterone: reabsorbs sodium and volume from saliva.<br>C. Acetylcholine: the main nerve transmitter, but citric acid is named as the top stimulant.<br>D. Prostaglandin: not a salivary stimulant in this course.<br>E. Beta-adrenergic stimulation: sympathetic; gives only a small volume.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 26 (Salivary Effects)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":77,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":77,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":130,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":245,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Saliva stimuli vs inhibitors","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: food, smell, nausea stimulate; dehydration, fear, sleep inhibit.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"},{"src":"../sl/G042-26.jpg","cap":"Citric acid: number one stimulant of salivary secretion.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-95-1","s":"All of the following stimulate saliva secretion EXCEPT:","o":["A. Parasympathetic activation","B. Sympathetic activation","C. Hypertonic solution","D. Acidic solution","E. Mastication"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic solution","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A <b>hypertonic solution</b> is not a salivary stimulus. Dehydration, which makes body fluids hypertonic, actually inhibits saliva. The other choices all drive secretion.<br>A. Parasympathetic activation: the main stimulus.<br>B. Sympathetic activation: also stimulates (smaller volume); salivary glands are the exception.<br>D. Acidic solution: citric acid is the number-one stimulant.<br>E. Mastication (chewing): reflexly stimulates saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 26 (Salivary Effects)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":95,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D067-54-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 28"},{"id":"D067-54-1","s":"Which factor does NOT stimulate saliva secretion?","o":["A. Food","B. Fear","C. Nausea","D. Smell","E. Conditioning"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fear","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fear.</b> Saliva is under nerve control only, and the parasympathetic nerves are the main drive. <b>Fear, sleep and dehydration inhibit</b> that drive, so the mouth goes dry when you are nervous.<br>A. Food: taste and chewing stimulate.<br>C. Nausea: stimulates (the mouth waters before vomiting).<br>D. Smell: stimulates.<br>E. Conditioning: stimulates, as in Pavlov's dogs.<br>Common mix-up: sympathetic nerves do add a little thick saliva, but fear still lowers the total because it removes the large parasympathetic flow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)","L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":54,"f":"D067"}],"tier":1,"n":"113"},{"id":"D066-59-1","s":"SGA Treasurer Mike Ntamere notices increased saliva production when chewing gum, but remembers all the club reimbursements waiting for him. As this memory is triggered, he seems to develop a dry mouth. Which of the following explains these changes?","o":["A. Chewing stimulates salivary reflexes, while fear can decrease watery salivary secretion","B. Chewing inhibits parasympathetic stimulation, fear increases watery salivary secretion","C. Chewing activates gastric parietal cells, while fear inhibits gastrin release","D. Chewing decreases ACh release, while fear increases PSNS stimulation","E. Both chewing and fear primarily increase watery salivary secretion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chewing stimulates salivary reflexes, while fear can decrease watery salivary secretion","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chewing stimulates salivary reflexes; fear lowers watery secretion.</b> Chewing, taste and smell drive the <b>parasympathetic</b> nerves, the main control, which give a large watery flow. <b>Fear</b> turns that parasympathetic drive down. The sympathetic nerves add only a little thick saliva, so the mouth feels dry.<br>B. Chewing stimulates parasympathetic output; fear does not raise watery flow.<br>C. Parietal cells and gastrin concern stomach acid, not saliva.<br>D. Chewing raises ACh (acetylcholine) release, and fear lowers parasympathetic activity.<br>E. Only chewing increases watery secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)","L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":59,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D067-54-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 29"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Muscarinic blockade (atropine) dries the mouth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic acetylcholine is main control; high volume blocked by atropine.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: atropine blocks the muscarinic receptor on acinar and duct cells.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-187-1","s":"Which of the following decreases salivary secretion?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Atropine","C. Bradykinin","D. Citric acid","E. Facial nerve (7th cranial) stimulation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Atropine","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Atropine blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors</b>, cutting off the main (parasympathetic) drive, so saliva drops.<br>A. Acetylcholine: the main stimulus of saliva.<br>C. Bradykinin: dilates the gland's blood vessels, which supports secretion.<br>D. Citric acid: the number-one stimulant of saliva.<br>E. Facial nerve (cranial nerve seven) stimulation: parasympathetic, so it raises saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":50,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":187,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":40,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":37,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"114"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Sympathetic saliva: T1–T3, norepinephrine, beta, cAMP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-25.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic: T1–T3 roots, superior cervical ganglion, norepinephrine, beta → cAMP, lower volume.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: sympathetic T1–T3 → norepinephrine → beta receptor → cAMP.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-104-1","s":"A student is studying for an exam. He is very nervous, drinking copious amounts of coffee and is extremely stressed. Which of the following is the effect this will have on the salivary glands?","o":["A. It will stimulate Parasympathetic nervous system to increase saliva production","B. It will stimulate the release of NE from T1-T3 on beta receptors to stimulate IP3/Ca++","C. It will stimulate the release of NE from T1-T3 on beta receptors to stimulate cAMP","D. It will stimulate the release of ACh that stimulates muscarinic cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It will stimulate the release of NE from T1-T3 on beta receptors to stimulate cAMP","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Stress is <b>sympathetic</b>: fibers from spinal levels <b>T1 to T3</b> (first to third thoracic) relay in the superior cervical ganglion, and postganglionic fibers release <b>norepinephrine (NE)</b> onto <b>beta receptors</b>, raising <b>cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate)</b>. Volume is low, so the mouth feels dry.<br>A. Stress does not activate the parasympathetic system.<br>B. Beta receptors work through cAMP; IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) and calcium are the muscarinic pathway.<br>D. Acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors is the parasympathetic pathway.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 25 (Sympathetic Control of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":104,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"115"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":29,"label":"Aldosterone reabsorbs sodium from saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-26.jpg","cap":"Aldosterone reabsorbs sodium and volume from saliva.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-97-1","s":"What is the effect of aldosterone on saliva?","o":["A. It helps with secretion of volume into the oral cavity","B. It reabsorbs Na+ from saliva","C. It allows kallikrein to be a more effective enzyme","D. It increases bicarbonate secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It reabsorbs Na+ from saliva","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Aldosterone</b> acts on salivary ducts as it does on the kidney: it <b>reabsorbs sodium</b> (and volume) from saliva.<br>A. It removes volume from saliva; it does not add it.<br>C. Kallikrein makes bradykinin to dilate gland vessels; aldosterone does not change it.<br>D. Aldosterone raises potassium secretion, not bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 26 (Salivary Effects)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":97,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"116"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":30,"label":"Saliva digests starch and fat, not protein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-30.jpg","cap":"Special content: amylase, lingual lipase; no protein enzyme.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G042-27.jpg","cap":"Functions of saliva: lubricates food, cleans teeth, alkaline bicarbonate neutralizes acid.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-83-1","s":"Which of the following enzymes is secreted by salivary glands and begins the digestion of starches?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Trypsin","C. α-Amylase","D. Lipase","E. Nuclease"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"α-Amylase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Salivary <b>alpha-amylase</b> starts <b>starch digestion</b> in the mouth; stomach acid later inactivates it.<br>A. Pepsin: from the stomach's chief cells (as pepsinogen).<br>B. Trypsin: from the pancreas.<br>D. Lipase: digests fat, not starch.<br>E. Nuclease: pancreatic; digests nucleic acids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":83,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"117"},{"id":"F1212-120-1","s":"Which of the following is the correct association of a saliva enzyme to its function?","o":["A. Pepsinogen for protein","B. IgM as antimicrobial","C. R-protein (Haptocorrin) for Vitamin B6","D. Salivary amylase degrades carbs"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Salivary amylase degrades carbs","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Salivary amylase</b> breaks down <b>carbohydrates</b> (starch) in the mouth; stomach acid later stops it.<br>A. Pepsinogen: gastric; saliva has no protein-digesting enzyme.<br>B. The salivary antibody is IgA (immunoglobulin A), not IgM (immunoglobulin M).<br>C. R-protein (haptocorrin) binds vitamin B12, not vitamin B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)","L52 slide 31 (Immune Protective Effects of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":120,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-83-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C4 extra 30"},{"id":"D040-98-1","s":"Which of the following is not a function of the saliva?","o":["A. Breakdown of starch","B. Breakdown of lipid","C. Breakdown of polypeptides","D. Lubrication of food with mucus to pass down food","E. Dilution and buffering of ingested foods."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Breakdown of polypeptides","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Saliva has <b>no protein-digesting enzyme</b>; its only enzymes are alpha-amylase and lingual lipase. Protein digestion starts in the stomach with pepsin.<br>A. Starch breakdown: alpha-amylase.<br>B. Lipid breakdown: lingual lipase.<br>D. Lubrication: mucus coats food for swallowing.<br>E. Dilution and buffering: water and bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 27 (Function of Saliva)","L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":98,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"118"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":30,"label":"What acinar cells secrete (IgA, kallikrein, not bradykinin)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-23.jpg","cap":"Acini make kallikrein; kallikrein splits plasma kininogen into bradykinin.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 23"},{"src":"../sl/G042-31.jpg","cap":"Immune protection: immunoglobulin A, lysozyme, lactoferrin, lactoperoxidase.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G042-30.jpg","cap":"Saliva's enzymes: amylase and lingual lipase; no protein enzyme.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-115-1","s":"ACINAR CELLS SECRETE ALL THE FOLLOWING EXCEPT:","o":["A. ALPHA- AMYLASE","B. LINGUAL LIPASE","C. KALLIKREIN","D. IGE","E. BRADYKININ","F. MUCIN GLYCOPROTEIN"],"g":["D","E"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"IGE","a":"D","why":"<b>D and E.</b> Saliva carries <b>IgA (immunoglobulin A)</b>, not <b>IgE (immunoglobulin E)</b>. <b>Bradykinin</b> is not secreted either: acini make <b>kallikrein</b>, which splits the plasma protein kininogen into bradykinin to widen the gland's vessels.<br>A. Alpha-amylase: acinar enzyme for starch.<br>B. Lingual lipase: the fat-digesting enzyme of saliva.<br>C. Kallikrein: made by the acini.<br>F. Mucin glycoprotein: made by mucous acinar cells.","ex":"","fl":["Wording ambiguity (key IgE is the intended answer): acinar cells secrete kallikrein, which then generates bradykinin from kininogen, so bradykinin itself is not an acinar secretion either; saliva carries IgA, not IgE.","Source keyed only IgE; bradykinin is also not an acinar secretion: acini secrete kallikrein, which cuts the plasma protein kininogen into bradykinin (G042:23)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)","L52 slide 31 (Immune Protective Effects of Saliva)","L52 slide 23 (Blood Flow)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":115,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 31"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":30,"label":"Saliva's immune defenses: IgA, lysozyme, lactoferrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-31.jpg","cap":"Immune protection: immunoglobulin A, lysozyme, lactoferrin, lactoperoxidase.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G042-32.jpg","cap":"Lysozyme lyses bacterial muramic acid; lactoferrin binds iron before bacteria can.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-95-1","s":"Which of the following is not one of the immune protective secretions of saliva?","o":["A. Lipase","B. Lactoferrin","C. Lysozyme","D. Lactoperoxidase","E. Immunoglobulin A"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lipase","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Lipase</b> digests fat; it is the one choice that is not an immune protein. Saliva's defenses are IgA (immunoglobulin A), lysozyme, lactoferrin and lactoperoxidase.<br>B. Lactoferrin: binds iron so bacteria cannot use it.<br>C. Lysozyme: breaks bacterial walls (muramic acid).<br>D. Lactoperoxidase: destroys bacteria and viruses.<br>E. Immunoglobulin A: stops bacteria from attacking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 31 (Immune Protective Effects of Saliva)","L52 slide 32 (Lysozyme (muramidase) and Lactoferrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":95,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"119"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":31,"label":"Sjögren syndrome: replace saliva to save teeth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-34.jpg","cap":"Sjögren syndrome: antibodies attack salivary and lacrimal glands; replace tears and saliva.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G042-27.jpg","cap":"Saliva cleans teeth and neutralizes bacterial acid: no saliva = no teeth.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-79-1","s":"A 34-year-old woman is complaining of persistent dry eyes and a dry mouth for the last 4 months. The physician orders a blood test, which comes back positive for antibodies attacking the salivary glands and the lacrimal glands. What should the physician order for the patient to prevent the development of dental caries?","o":["A. Oral antibiotics","B. Diet modification advice","C. Artificial saliva","D. Supplementary salivary amylase","E. Artificial tears"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Artificial saliva","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> This is <b>Sjögren syndrome</b>: antibodies destroy salivary and lacrimal glands. Without saliva's cleaning and bicarbonate buffering, teeth decay, so give <b>artificial saliva</b>.<br>A. Antibiotics do not replace saliva's flushing and buffering.<br>B. Diet advice alone cannot replace saliva.<br>D. Amylase digests starch; it does not protect teeth.<br>E. Artificial tears treat dry eyes, not dental caries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 34 (Sjogren’s Syndrome)","L52 slide 27 (Function of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":79,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":79,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":247,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":81,"f":"F1203"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":93,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":132,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"120"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":31,"label":"Muscarinic blockade (atropine) dries the mouth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic acetylcholine is main control; high volume blocked by atropine.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: atropine blocks the muscarinic receptor on acinar and duct cells.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-63-1","s":"A 35-year-old man presents to the clinic with complaints of dry mouth (xerostomia) for several weeks. Upon further history, the patient reports using over-the-counter medication to treat seasonal allergies. His symptoms improved after discontinuing the medication. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism by which this medication caused his symptoms?","o":["A. Inhibition of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","B. Stimulation of β-adrenergic receptors","C. Increased cAMP in acinar cells","D. Stimulation of norepinephrine release"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Inhibition of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Many over-the-counter allergy drugs (first-generation antihistamines) also <b>block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors</b>. That removes the main parasympathetic drive to the glands, causing <b>dry mouth (xerostomia)</b>.<br>B. Beta-adrenergic stimulation raises saliva (small volume); it does not dry the mouth.<br>C. More cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) in acinar cells also stimulates secretion.<br>D. Norepinephrine still stimulates some saliva; drug-induced dry mouth is classically muscarinic blockade.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":63,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"121"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":31,"label":"Saliva vs pancreas: enzymes shared, control differs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Salivary glands: only gland where both autonomic branches increase secretion.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G044-20.jpg","cap":"Pancreas neural supply: parasympathetic secretes, sympathetic dries.","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/D024-60.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figures 8.12 and 8.21: hypotonic saliva versus isotonic pancreatic juice.","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-58-1","s":"Select all that apply to the exocrine pancreas and the salivary glands","o":["A. Both secrete enzymes that help to digest food","B. Both have parasympathetic and sympathetic excitatory stimulation","C. Both release an isotonic secretion","D. All of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Both secrete enzymes that help to digest food","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Both <b>salivary glands and pancreas secrete digestive enzymes</b> (both make amylase and lipase).<br>B. Only salivary glands are stimulated by both systems; in the pancreas, parasympathetic secretes and sympathetic dries.<br>C. Pancreatic juice is isotonic, but saliva is hypotonic.<br>D. All of the above: B and C are false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)","L52 slide 14 (Formation of Saliva)","L54 slide 20 (Neural Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":58,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C4 extra 32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Parietal cells: HCl and intrinsic factor","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Cell table: parietal cells secrete gastric acid and intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G040-18.jpg","cap":"Gastric gland drawing: parietal cell secretes hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-101-1","s":"Which of the following cells secrete hydrochloric acid?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Mucous neck cells","D. G cells","E. D cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells</b> secrete <b>hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and intrinsic factor. They sit in the oxyntic glands of the stomach body.<br>A. Chief cells: secrete pepsinogen.<br>C. Mucous neck cells: secrete mucus and bicarbonate.<br>D. G cells: secrete gastrin in the antrum. Gastrin stimulates acid but is not acid.<br>E. D cells: secrete somatostatin, which inhibits acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":101,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-61-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 1"},{"id":"F1203-124-1","s":"Which cell secretes the glycoprotein intrinsic factor?","o":["A. Gastric chief cell","B. Pancreatic exocrine gland","C. M cell","D. Gastric parietal cell"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric parietal cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastric parietal cell.</b> Parietal cells in the stomach body make both <b>hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Intrinsic factor is the glycoprotein needed to absorb vitamin B12 in the ileum. The 2026 lecture calls it the one essential gastric secretion.<br>A. Chief cell: makes pepsinogen.<br>B. Pancreatic exocrine gland: makes digestive enzymes and bicarbonate.<br>C. M cell: samples antigens over Peyer patches in the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":124,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-61-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 2"},{"id":"D066-61-1","s":"Physiology TA Tino decides to embark on a vegan bodybuilding journey. A few months in, he develops numbness in his extremities. Laboratory testing reveals a vitamin B12 deficiency. In addition to his vegan diet, is autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells. Which of the following gastric secretions would be directly reduced?","o":["A. Pepsinogen and Gastrin","B. HCl and Intrinsic Factor","C. Gastrin and Mucus","D. Pepsinogen and Intrinsic Factor","E. Mucus and HCl"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HCl and Intrinsic Factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. HCl and intrinsic factor.</b> <b>Parietal cells</b> of the gastric body make both HCl (hydrochloric acid) and intrinsic factor. Without intrinsic factor, vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the ileum, which adds to his dietary lack and explains the numbness.<br>A. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells and gastrin from G cells.<br>C. Gastrin comes from G cells and mucus from mucous cells.<br>D. Intrinsic factor is right, but pepsinogen is a chief-cell product.<br>E. HCl is right, but mucus is a mucous-cell product.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":61,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"122"},{"id":"D035-140-2","s":"Vit B12 questions… / What cell releases intrinsic factor?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Parietal Cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Parietal cells</b> release <b>intrinsic factor</b>, along with hydrochloric acid (HCl). Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 so it can be absorbed in the ileum. Without it, B12 is not absorbed and pernicious anemia develops.<br>Common mix-up: chief cells sit in the same glands but make pepsinogen, not intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":140,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-61-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Antral G cells secrete gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-5.jpg","cap":"Body: parietal and chief cells; antrum: G cells make gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G051-5.jpg","cap":"Stomach map with G cells in the antrum sending gastrin to circulation","where":"2025 discussion, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-71-1","s":"WHERE IN THE STOMACH IS GASTRIN SECRETED?","o":["A. CARDIA","B. FUNDUS","C. BODY","D. ANTRUM"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"ANTRUM","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Antrum.</b> <b>G cells</b> in the antrum (the lower stomach next to the pylorus) secrete <b>gastrin</b> into the blood.<br>A. Cardia: mostly mucous glands next to the esophagus.<br>B. Fundus: oxyntic glands with parietal and chief cells, like the body.<br>C. Body: parietal cells (hydrochloric acid, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen), not G cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":71,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 4"},{"id":"D067-57-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches a gastric secretion with its site of synthesis?","o":["A. HCl — Chief cells of the antrum","B. Pepsinogen — Parietal cells of the fundus","C. Gastrin — G cells of the antrum","D. Intrinsic factor — Mucous cells of the pylorus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin — G cells of the antrum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastrin: G cells of the antrum.</b> G cells sit in the glands of the antrum and release <b>gastrin</b> into the blood.<br>A. HCl (hydrochloric acid): made by parietal cells of the body; chief cells make pepsinogen.<br>B. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells; parietal cells make HCl and intrinsic factor.<br>D. Intrinsic factor: made by parietal cells of the body; mucous cells make mucus and bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":57,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"D cells secrete somatostatin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Cell table: D cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G051-5.jpg","cap":"Stomach map with D cells and somatostatin in the antrum","where":"2025 discussion, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-126-1","s":"WHICH CELL TYPE SECRETES SOMATOSTATIN AND WHERE ARE THEY LOCATED IN THE STOMACH?","o":["A. PARIETAL CELL: BODY","B. CHIEF CELLS: BODY","C. G CELLS: ANTRUM","D. MUCOUS CELLS: ANTRUM","E. D CELLS: ANTRUM"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"D CELLS: ANTRUM","a":"E","why":"<b>E. D cells: antrum.</b> <b>D cells</b> secrete <b>somatostatin</b>, which inhibits gastrin release, histamine release and parietal-cell acid. Antral D cells sense acid and brake the G cells next to them.<br>A. Parietal cells (body): make hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor.<br>B. Chief cells (body): make pepsinogen.<br>C. G cells (antrum): make gastrin.<br>D. Mucous cells: make mucus and bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":126,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"123"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Enterochromaffin cells make serotonin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D023-53.jpg","cap":"EC (Kulchitsky) cells secrete serotonin; map of other gut secretory cells","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 53"},{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"2026 cell table: ECL cells make histamine, chief pepsinogen, D cells somatostatin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-78-1","s":"7. Which of the following best describes enterochromaffin cells?","o":["A. Submucosal glands located in the duodenum.  Their main function is the production of a mucus-rich alkaline secretion with bicarbonate.","B. Cells that secrete enzymes for protein digestion, including pepsinogen.","C. Cells that produce mucus that cover the inside of the stomach, guarding it from gastric acid.  These cells line the gastric mucosa, located within the proximal portion of the gastric pits.","D. Cells that can be found within the stomach, intestine and the pancreatic islets that secrete somatostatin","E. Cells that synthesize and secrete Serotonin for modulation of gastrointestinal neurons"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cells that synthesize and secrete Serotonin for modulation of gastrointestinal neurons","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Enterochromaffin (EC) cells</b> are gut endocrine cells that make <b>serotonin</b>, which acts on enteric neurons (motility and secretion). Don't confuse them with enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, which make histamine for acid.<br>A. Describes Brunner glands: duodenal submucosal glands that make alkaline mucus.<br>B. Describes chief cells (pepsinogen).<br>C. Describes surface mucous cells.<br>D. Describes D cells (somatostatin).","ex":"","fl":["Option A's description (duodenal submucosal mucus/HCO3 glands) fits Brunner glands, not 'pancreatic glands' as the source explanation says; key E (serotonin) is correct."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":78,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Enterochromaffin cells secrete serotonin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D023-53.jpg","cap":"Enterochromaffin cells: enteroendocrine cells secreting serotonin to modulate gut neurons","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-66-1","s":"Which of the following best describes enterochromaffin cells?","o":["A. Submucosal glands located in the duodenum. Their main function is the production of a mucus-rich alkaline secretion with bicarbonate.","B. Cells that secrete enzymes for protein digestion, including pepsinogen.","C. Cells that produce mucus that cover the inside of the stomach, guarding it from gastric acid. These cells line the gastric mucosa, located within the proximal portion of the gastric pits.","D. Cells that can be found within the stomach, intestine and the pancreatic islets that secrete somatostatin","E. Cells that synthesize and secrete Serotonin for modulation of gastrointestinal neurons."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cells that synthesize and secrete Serotonin for modulation of gastrointestinal neurons.","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Enterochromaffin (EC) cells</b> are enteroendocrine cells of the gut lining that make <b>serotonin</b>, which signals gut neurons to adjust motility and secretion.<br>A. Brunner glands: duodenal submucosal glands making alkaline mucus.<br>B. Chief cells: pepsinogen.<br>C. Surface mucous cells: protective mucus.<br>D. D cells: somatostatin.<br>Do not confuse them with enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, which make histamine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":66,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Hormone-cell pairs: G, I, S, K, D, ECL","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP is produced by K cells of the duodenum and jejunum","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: gastrin from G cells, CCK from I cells, secretin from S cells","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Gland table: ECL cells make histamine, D cells somatostatin, G cells gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D002-11-1","s":"Which cells type is properly matched with its secretion?","o":["A. Mucous cells – Trypsinogen","B. Chief cell - Intrinsic factor","C. Parietal cell – Gastrin","D. G-cell – Pepsinogen","E. S-cells- Secretin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"S-cells- Secretin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. S cells → secretin</b> (S cells of the duodenum).<br>A. Mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate; trypsinogen comes from pancreatic acinar cells.<br>B. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells.<br>C. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor; gastrin comes from G cells.<br>D. G cells secrete gastrin; pepsinogen comes from chief cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 12 (Secretin)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":14,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":2,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":11,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":11,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":27,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"124"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":32,"label":"Cell–secretion pairs: S cells make secretin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Gastric gland table: each cell type with the substance it secretes","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D061-80.jpg","cap":"Stomach–duodenum map: S cells release secretin, G cells gastrin, chief cells pepsinogen","where":"2023 TA review, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-19-1","s":"Which cells type is properly matched with its secretion?","o":["A. Mucous cells – Trypsinogen","B. Chief cell - Intrinsic factor","C. Parietal cell – Gastrin","D. G-cell – Pepsinogen","E. S-cells- Secretin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"S-cells- Secretin","a":"e","why":"<b>E. S cells – secretin</b> is the only correct pair. <b>S cells</b> in the duodenum release <b>secretin</b> when acid enters the duodenum.<br>A. Mucous cells make mucus and bicarbonate; trypsinogen comes from pancreatic acinar cells.<br>B. Chief cells make pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells.<br>C. Parietal cells make hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor; gastrin comes from G cells.<br>D. G cells make gastrin; pepsinogen comes from chief cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L51A slide 12 (Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":19,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":158,"f":"D004"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 8"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":33,"label":"Carbonic anhydrase makes H+ and bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-14.jpg","cap":"Carbonic anhydrase equation: CO2 plus water gives H+ plus bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G043-17.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: carbonic anhydrase inside, pump on lumen side, bicarbonate to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-77-1","s":"What enzyme catalyzes the formation of H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ inside parietal cells?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Histaminase","C. Carbonic anhydrase","D. Trypsin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbonic anhydrase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Carbonic anhydrase</b> joins carbon dioxide and water into carbonic acid, which splits into <b>H+ (acid) and HCO3- (bicarbonate)</b>. The pump sends H+ into the lumen; bicarbonate goes to the blood.<br>A. Pepsin: protein-digesting enzyme in the lumen.<br>B. Histaminase: breaks down histamine.<br>D. Trypsin: pancreatic protease that works in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":77,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"125"},{"id":"D067-60-1","s":"Which of the following steps is essential for HCl formation in parietal cells?","o":["A. Secretion of H+ through the Na+/K+ ATPase","B. Conversion of H2CO3 to H+ and HCO3– via carbonic anhydrase","C. Diffusion of K+ into the lumen through Cl– channels","D. Exchange of HCO3– for Na+ at the luminal surface","E. Passive secretion of H+ via facilitated diffusion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Conversion of H2CO3 to H+ and HCO3– via carbonic anhydrase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Carbonic anhydrase supplies the H+.</b> Inside the parietal cell, <b>carbonic anhydrase</b> joins CO2 and water into carbonic acid (H2CO3), which splits into H+ and HCO3- (bicarbonate). The H+ is pumped into the lumen; the bicarbonate leaves to the blood.<br>A. H+ is secreted by the H+/K+ ATPase, not the Na+/K+ ATPase.<br>C. Chloride, not potassium, moves through chloride channels.<br>D. Bicarbonate is exchanged for chloride on the blood side, not for sodium at the lumen.<br>E. H+ secretion is primary active transport, not passive.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)","L53 slide 15 (“AE”=Anion Exchanger)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":60,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-77-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 9"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":33,"label":"Pump apical; Cl⁻/HCO3⁻ exchanger basolateral","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-15.jpg","cap":"Anion exchanger sends bicarbonate to blood; ATP-split pump faces lumen","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/G043-17.jpg","cap":"Proton pump on lumen side; chloride–bicarbonate exchanger toward capillary","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-84-1","s":"The Gastric parietal cell contains H+/K+ ATPase and Cl- channels on its basolateral membrane side and Na+/K+ ATPase and Cl-/HCO3- cotransporter on its apical membrane side.","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> The sides are swapped. The <b>apical</b> (lumen) side has the <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (hydrogen–potassium pump) and chloride channels, which make hydrochloric acid. The <b>basolateral</b> (blood) side has the <b>Na+/K+ ATPase</b> (sodium–potassium pump) and the <b>chloride–bicarbonate exchanger</b>, which sends bicarbonate to the blood (alkaline tide).<br>Common mix-up: the bicarbonate carrier is an exchanger (chloride in, bicarbonate out), not a cotransporter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 15 (“AE”=Anion Exchanger)","L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)","L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":84,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":84,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":252,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 10"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":33,"label":"Alkaline tide: bicarbonate into gastric venous blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-14.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: acid to lumen, bicarbonate to blood = alkaline tide","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G043-9.jpg","cap":"Mucosa diagram: bicarbonate made and sent to blood, labeled alkaline tide","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G044-22.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic duct cell: bicarbonate to lumen, H+ out to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-111-1","s":"In combination of both the apical and basolateral membranes of a gastric parietal cell, which if the following is a net result regarding the function of this cell?","o":["A. Net secretion of HCl","B. Net secretion of HCO3-","C. Net absorption of HCO3-","D. Net absorption of HCl","E. A and C","F. B and D"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"A and C (net secretion of HCl and net absorption of HCO3-)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. A and C.</b> The apical H+/K+ ATPase (hydrogen–potassium pump) sends acid into the lumen: <b>net hydrochloric acid (HCl) secretion</b>. The basolateral chloride–bicarbonate exchanger moves bicarbonate into the blood: <b>net bicarbonate (HCO3-) absorption</b>, the alkaline tide.<br>B. Bicarbonate goes to the blood, not the lumen.<br>D. Hydrochloric acid is secreted, not absorbed.<br>F. Both halves are reversed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)","L53 slide 15 (“AE”=Anion Exchanger)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":111,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":110,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-23-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 11"},{"id":"D022-23-1","s":"Which blood sample would likely have the highest pH?","o":["a. Cerebral artery","b. Gastric vein","c. Pancreatic veins","d. Portal vein","e. Renal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric vein","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastric vein.</b> Parietal cells send acid (H+) into the lumen and <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-) into the gastric venous blood</b>, the <b>alkaline tide</b>.<br>A. Cerebral artery: ordinary arterial pH, about seven point four.<br>C. Pancreatic veins: duct cells put bicarbonate in the duct and H+ in the blood, so this blood is acidic.<br>D. Portal vein: mixes alkaline gastric blood with acidic pancreatic blood and gut blood, so it is less alkaline.<br>E. Renal artery: ordinary arterial pH.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":32,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":23,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":171,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":24,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":22,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"126"},{"id":"D014-31-1","s":"Which blood sample would have the highest pH?","o":["A. a. Pancreatic veins","B. b. Gastric vein","C. c. Cerebral artery","D. d. Renal artery","E. e. Hemorrhoids"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"b. Gastric vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastric vein.</b> After a meal, parietal cells pump acid (H+) into the lumen and send <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-) into the blood</b>. This <b>alkaline tide</b> makes gastric venous blood the most alkaline.<br>A. Pancreatic veins: duct cells secrete bicarbonate into the duct and send H+ into the blood, so this blood is acidic.<br>C. Cerebral artery: ordinary arterial pH, about seven point four.<br>D. Renal artery: ordinary arterial pH, about seven point four.<br>E. Hemorrhoids (rectal veins): ordinary venous blood, slightly more acidic than arterial.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)","L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":31,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-23-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 12"},{"id":"D015-149-1","s":"FILL IN THE BLANK: GASTRIC VEINS HAVE THE HIGHEST/LOWEST PH AND PANCREATIC VEINS HAVE THE HIGHEST/LOWEST PH","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"GASTRIC VEINS: HIGHEST PH; PANCREATIC VEINS: LOWEST PH","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastric veins: highest pH. Pancreatic veins: lowest pH.</b> Parietal cells send acid (H+) to the lumen and bicarbonate (HCO3-) to the blood, the <b>alkaline tide</b>. Pancreatic duct cells do the reverse: bicarbonate into the duct, H+ into the blood.<br>Common mix-up: pancreatic duct juice is alkaline, but pancreatic vein blood is acidic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)","L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":149,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-23-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 13"},{"id":"D064-27-1","s":"Pancreatic Exocrine Secretion: Centroacinar and Duct cells – secrete HCO3- and H2O. Acidification of Pancreatic blood?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"As ductal cells secrete HCO3- into the lumen they send H+ into the blood, so pancreatic venous blood is acidic; this neutralizes the gastric alkaline tide","a":null,"why":"<b>Pancreatic venous blood is acidified.</b> When duct cells secrete bicarbonate (HCO3-) into the duct, carbonic anhydrase also makes acid (H+), and that H+ leaves into the blood. This acid tide offsets the stomach's alkaline tide.<br>Common mix-up: pancreatic juice is alkaline; the blood leaving the pancreas is acidic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":27,"f":"D064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-23-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 14"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":34,"label":"Acid stimulants: ACh, gastrin, histamine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell with M3, H2, CCKB receptors; stimulators and inhibitors listed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure annotated: ACh, gastrin, histamine stimulate; somatostatin, prostaglandins inhibit","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-115-1","s":"What 3 substances stimulate H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells?","o":["A. Ach, Histamine, Gastrin","B. Ach, Histamine, Somatostatin","C. Gastrin, Histamine, Somatostatin","D. Ach, Prostaglandins, Gastrin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ach, Histamine, Gastrin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Acetylcholine (ACh), histamine, gastrin.</b> ACh comes from the vagus (M3, muscarinic receptor). Histamine comes from enterochromaffin-like cells (H2 receptor). Gastrin comes from antral G cells (CCK-B, cholecystokinin B receptor).<br>B. Somatostatin inhibits acid.<br>C. Somatostatin inhibits acid.<br>D. Prostaglandins inhibit acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":115,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":114,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"127"},{"id":"G052-7-1","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: What is the mechanism of gastric H+ secretion, and what factors regulate it?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Apical H+-K+ ATPase secretes H+; stimulated by ACh (M3), gastrin (CCKB) and histamine (H2); inhibited by somatostatin and prostaglandins.","a":null,"why":"<b>Mechanism:</b> carbonic anhydrase turns carbon dioxide plus water into acid (H+) plus bicarbonate. The apical <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (proton pump) swaps H+ into the lumen for potassium; bicarbonate leaves to the blood in exchange for chloride. <b>Stimulators:</b> acetylcholine (M3, muscarinic), gastrin (CCK-B, cholecystokinin B), histamine (H2). <b>Inhibitors:</b> somatostatin, prostaglandins.<br>Common mix-up: gastrin is the only hormone among the three stimulators.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-115-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 15"},{"id":"D060-88-1","s":"Which of the following is the main stimulus for the release of gastric juice from parietal cells?","o":["A. Presence of chyme in the duodenum","B. Distension of the stomach","C. Low pH in the stomach","D. Acetylcholine release from the vagus nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Acetylcholine release from the vagus nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Acetylcholine (ACh) from the vagus</b> acts directly on M3 (muscarinic type three) receptors of parietal cells. The vagus drives the whole cephalic phase and much of the gastric phase.<br>A. Chyme in the duodenum: starts the intestinal phase, which mostly inhibits acid (secretin, GIP = glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide).<br>B. Distension: a trigger, but it works through vagal ACh and gastrin, not directly on parietal cells.<br>C. Low gastric pH: inhibits acid by blocking gastrin release (negative feedback).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":88,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-115-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 16"},{"id":"G067-73-1","s":"Which hormone directly stimulates parietal cells to secrete HCl?","o":["A. Secretin","B. Gastrin","C. Somatostatin","D. Cholecystokinin (CCK)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastrin</b> is the <b>hormone</b> that binds CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) receptors on parietal cells to make acid. Acetylcholine (a nerve signal) and histamine (a local paracrine) also stimulate, but they are not hormones.<br>A. Secretin: inhibits gastrin and acid; it drives pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>C. Somatostatin: inhibits acid, gastrin and histamine.<br>D. Cholecystokinin (CCK): contracts the gallbladder, releases pancreatic enzymes and slows gastric emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":73,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-115-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 17"},{"id":"D008-68-1","s":"Which of the following is correct about histamine?","o":["A. Stimulates release of acid from gastric parietal cells","B. Stimulates G cells to secrete gastrin","C. Inhibits the release of secretin to inhibit pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","D. Works at the H1 receptor on gastric parietal cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulates release of acid from gastric parietal cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Histamine</b>, released from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, binds <b>H2 (histamine type two) receptors</b> on parietal cells and <b>stimulates acid</b>.<br>B. Backwards: gastrin stimulates ECL cells to release histamine; histamine does not drive G cells.<br>C. Histamine has no role in secretin; acid in the duodenum releases secretin.<br>D. Parietal cells use H2, not H1, receptors. That is why H2 blockers like cimetidine cut acid; H1 receptors mediate allergy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)","L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":68,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":236,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-115-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 18"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":34,"label":"Parietal receptors: Gq, Gs and Gi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-41.jpg","cap":"Histamine via Gs and cAMP; PGE2 via Gi; ACh and gastrin via calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: histamine–H2–cAMP; gastrin and ACh raise calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Annotated figure: M3 and CCKB use PLC–IP3–calcium; H2 uses cAMP–PKA","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-63-1","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to his primary care physician with burning epigastric chest pain. He states that his pain is worse at night and after he eats a large meal. Along with prescribing medication, the physician also decides to perform manipulation to the occipitoatlantal and suboccipital musculature. Such manipulation will balance vagal stimulation on the gastric parietal cells by normalizing","o":["A. Activation of Gi subunit protein","B. Activation of Gs subunit protein","C. Activation of phospholipase C","D. Activation of protein kinase A","E. Vasoconstriction and sphincter tone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of phospholipase C","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Activation of phospholipase C.</b> Vagal acetylcholine binds M3 (muscarinic type three) receptors on parietal cells. They use Gq, which activates <b>phospholipase C</b>, making IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) that releases calcium.<br>A. Gi (inhibitory G protein): used by somatostatin and prostaglandins.<br>B. Gs (stimulatory G protein): used by histamine.<br>D. Protein kinase A: downstream of cyclic AMP from histamine.<br>E. Vasoconstriction and sphincter tone: sympathetic effects, not vagal acid control.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":33,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":63,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"128"},{"id":"D024-41-1","s":"Which correctly matches stimulant of gastric parietal cells and the G protein associated.","o":["A. ACh; Gq","B. Gastrin; Gs","C. Histamine; Gq","D. Prostaglandins; Gs"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ACh; Gq","a":"A","why":"<b>A. ACh; Gq.</b> Acetylcholine (ACh) binds M3 (muscarinic type three) receptors coupled to <b>Gq</b>, which raises IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) and calcium.<br>B. Gastrin also uses Gq (CCK-B, cholecystokinin B receptor), not Gs.<br>C. Histamine uses Gs (stimulatory G protein) and cyclic AMP, not Gq.<br>D. Prostaglandins inhibit acid through Gi (inhibitory G protein); they are not stimulants.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":41,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"129"},{"id":"D007-60-1","s":"Histamine stimulates the increase of gastric acid secretion by","o":["A. Inhibiting the Gs receptor, decreasing the cAMP in the cell","B. Stimulating the Gi receptor, increasing cAMP in the cell","C. Simulating the Gq receptor, increasing Ca2+ in the cell","D. Stimulating the Gs receptor, increasing cAMP in the cell","E. Inhibiting the Gq receptor, decreasing the Ca2+ in the cell"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulating the Gs receptor, increasing cAMP in the cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Histamine binds <b>H2 (histamine type two) receptors</b> coupled to the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>. Gs raises <b>cyclic AMP (cAMP)</b>, which turns on the proton pump.<br>A. Gs is stimulated, not inhibited, and cAMP rises.<br>B. Gi (inhibitory G protein) lowers cAMP; somatostatin and prostaglandins use it.<br>C. Gq raising calcium is the acetylcholine and gastrin pathway.<br>E. Histamine does not act through Gq at all.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":60,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"130"},{"id":"D061-74-1","s":"6. For the Parietal cell, match the receptor to the mechanism that increases/decreases acid: Gs, Gi, Gq / ___ M3 receptor, Vagus ACh: increase acid / ___ CCKB receptor, Gastrin: increase acid / ___ H2 receptor, Histamine: increase acid / ___ Somatostatin: decrease acid / ___ Prostaglandins: decrease acid","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"M3 (ACh) = Gq; CCKB (gastrin) = Gq; H2 (histamine) = Gs; somatostatin = Gi; prostaglandins = Gi","a":null,"why":"<b>M3 (acetylcholine) = Gq. CCK-B (gastrin) = Gq. H2 (histamine) = Gs. Somatostatin = Gi. Prostaglandins = Gi.</b> Gq raises calcium through phospholipase C. Gs (stimulatory) raises cyclic AMP. Gi (inhibitory) lowers cyclic AMP. M3 = muscarinic type three; H2 = histamine type two.<br>Common mix-up: gastrin's CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) receptor is Gq like acetylcholine's, not Gs like histamine's.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":74,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-63-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 19"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":34,"label":"Gastrin is a hormone released into blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-7.jpg","cap":"Hormone path: endocrine cell to portal then systemic circulation to target","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G040-4.jpg","cap":"GI hormones pass through portal blood and liver back to target","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-96-1","s":"4. All of the following statements are true, EXCEPT?","o":["A. Gastrin is released into the stomach and directly stimulates HCl release from parietal cell and indirectly stimulates HCl by stimulating histamine release","B. The gastric phase provides the most HCl secretion into the stomach and is stimulated by the distention of the stomach","C. Histamine is released from ECL cell and binds to H2 receptors on the parietal cell","D. HCl release into the stomach is stimulated by Ach via vagus, gastrin via G cells and histamine via ECL cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin is released into the stomach and directly stimulates HCl release from parietal cell and indirectly stimulates HCl by stimulating histamine release","a":"A","why":"<b>A</b> is the false statement. Gastrin is a <b>hormone</b>: G cells release it <b>into the blood</b> (portal circulation, then back to the stomach), not into the stomach lumen. The rest of A is true.<br>B. True: the gastric phase gives about sixty percent of acid, driven by distension.<br>C. True: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cell histamine acts on H2 (histamine type two) receptors.<br>D. True: acetylcholine (vagus), gastrin (G cells) and histamine (ECL cells) all stimulate acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":96,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 20"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":34,"label":"Which gut peptides are hormones","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-4.jpg","cap":"The four GI hormones: gastrin, CCK, secretin, GIP; via portal blood","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G040-7.jpg","cap":"Diagram: hormones travel portal then systemic blood; paracrines diffuse locally","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/D061-39.jpg","cap":"Hormone = into blood; paracrine = local (somatostatin, histamine); neurocrine list","where":"2023 TA review, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G064-96-1","s":"4. All of the following statements are true, EXCEPT?","o":["A. Gastrin is released into the stomach and directly stimulates HCl release from parietal cell and indirectly stimulates HCl by stimulating histamine release","B. The gastric phase provides the most HCl secretion into the stomach and is stimulated by the distention of the stomach","C. Histamine is released from ECL cell and binds to H2 receptors on the parietal cell","D. HCl release into the stomach is stimulated by Ach via vagus, gastrin via G cells and histamine via ECL cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin is released into the stomach and directly stimulates HCl release from parietal cell and indirectly stimulates HCl by stimulating histamine release","a":"A","why":"<b>A is the false statement.</b> Gastrin is released <b>into the blood</b>, not into the stomach lumen; it is a hormone that reaches the parietal cell through the circulation. The rest of A is true: it acts directly and also through histamine.<br>B. True: the gastric phase gives about sixty percent of acid secretion and is driven by stomach distention.<br>C. True: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells release histamine onto the parietal cell's histamine H2 receptors.<br>D. True: acetylcholine (ACh) from the vagus, gastrin and histamine all stimulate acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 4 (GI Peptides: Hormones)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":96,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 21"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":35,"label":"Somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"D cell somatostatin blocks gastrin, ECL cell and parietal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-41.jpg","cap":"PGE2 inhibits acid through Gi, the inhibitory G protein","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-23.jpg","cap":"Inhibition: H+ feedback, prostaglandins stop acid, somatostatin inhibits gastrin and histamine","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-75-1","s":"Which of the following inhibits gastric acid secretion?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Prostaglandins","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Prostaglandins","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Prostaglandins</b> (prostaglandin E2) <b>inhibit acid</b> through the inhibitory G protein (Gi), which lowers cyclic AMP in parietal cells. They also stop histamine release, and separately boost protective mucus and bicarbonate.<br>A. Acetylcholine: stimulates acid (M3, muscarinic receptor).<br>C. Histamine: stimulates acid (H2, histamine type two receptor).<br>D. Gastrin: stimulates acid (CCK-B, cholecystokinin B receptor).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":75,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"131"},{"id":"G063-9-38","s":"An accomplished gastroenterology scientist from a Denver medical school is trying out a newly synthesized experimental compound in his lab for the treatment of acid peptic disorder. The new agent is likely to mimic the effect of which of the following in inhibiting gastric acid secretion?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Somatostatin","c. Histamine","d. Pepsin","e. Acetylcholine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Somatostatin.</b> An acid-lowering drug would copy somatostatin, which <b>inhibits</b> parietal cells (through Gi, the inhibitory G protein) and blocks gastrin and histamine release. Octreotide is such a copy.<br>A. Gastrin: stimulates acid.<br>C. Histamine: stimulates acid.<br>D. Pepsin: a protein-digesting enzyme, not an acid regulator.<br>E. Acetylcholine: stimulates acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":9,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"132"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":35,"label":"Acid stimulants: ACh, gastrin, histamine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell with M3, H2, CCKB receptors; stimulators and inhibitors listed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure annotated: ACh, gastrin, histamine stimulate; somatostatin, prostaglandins inhibit","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-117-1","s":"Which of the following does NOT lead to H+ secretion? (Select all that apply)","o":["A. Histamine","B. Gastrin","C. Somatostatin","D. Acetylcholine","E. Prostaglandins"],"g":["C","E"],"k":["C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin | Prostaglandins","a":"C,E","why":"<b>C. Somatostatin and E. Prostaglandins</b> do not cause acid secretion. Both <b>inhibit</b> it through the inhibitory G protein (Gi) on parietal cells.<br>A. Histamine: stimulates through H2 (histamine type two) receptors.<br>B. Gastrin: stimulates through CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) receptors.<br>D. Acetylcholine: stimulates through M3 (muscarinic type three) receptors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":117,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 22"},{"id":"D007-113-1","s":"All of the following stimulate parietal cells to secrete HCl except:","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Histamine","C. Acetylcholine","D. Somatostatin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Somatostatin</b> is the exception. D cells release it to <b>inhibit</b> acid, directly on parietal cells and by blocking gastrin and histamine release.<br>A. Gastrin: hormone from G cells; stimulates through CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) receptors.<br>B. Histamine: from enterochromaffin-like cells; stimulates through H2 (histamine type two) receptors.<br>C. Acetylcholine: from the vagus; stimulates through M3 (muscarinic type three) receptors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":113,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":135,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":35,"label":"Acid inhibitors act via Gi, lowering cAMP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-41.jpg","cap":"PGE2 acts through Gi against cAMP; histamine raises cAMP through Gs","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/D061-76.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit parietal cells via Gi","where":"2023 TA review, slide 76"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-91-1","s":"2. Through which mechanism is HCl secretion inhibited?","o":["A. Increase of cAMP","B. Decrease of cAMP","C. Increase of intracellular Ca and DAG","D. Decrease of intracellular Ca and DAG"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease of cAMP","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The acid inhibitors <b>somatostatin</b> (from D cells) and <b>prostaglandin E2</b> act on the parietal cell through the <b>inhibitory G protein (Gi)</b>. Gi blocks adenylyl cyclase, so <b>cyclic AMP (cAMP) falls</b> and the proton pump slows.<br>A. Rising cAMP is how histamine (H2 receptor, stimulatory Gs) <b>stimulates</b> acid.<br>C. Rising calcium (through inositol trisphosphate and diacylglycerol) is how acetylcholine and gastrin stimulate acid.<br>D. The inhibitors act on the cAMP arm, not by lowering calcium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":91,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":91,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"133"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":35,"label":"Parietal receptors: Gq, Gs and Gi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-41.jpg","cap":"Histamine via Gs and cAMP; PGE2 via Gi; ACh and gastrin via calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: histamine–H2–cAMP; gastrin and ACh raise calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Annotated figure: M3 and CCKB use PLC–IP3–calcium; H2 uses cAMP–PKA","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-86-1","s":"What Effect does Somatostatin have on H+ secretion by parietal gastric cells?","o":["A. Somatostatin directly stimulates H+ secretion by binding to parietal receptors that are coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gi protein.","B. Somatostatin indirectly stimulates H+ secretion by binding to parietal receptors that are coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gi protein.","C. Somatostatin directly inhibits H+ secretion by binding to parietal receptors that are coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gi protein.","D. Somatostatin has no effect on HCl secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin directly inhibits H+ secretion by binding to parietal receptors that are coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gi protein.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Somatostatin</b> binds parietal-cell receptors linked to the <b>inhibitory G protein (Gi)</b>. Gi blocks adenylyl cyclase, so cyclic AMP falls and acid drops. Somatostatin also blocks gastrin and histamine release.<br>A. Wrong direction: Gi inhibits; it does not stimulate.<br>B. Somatostatin never stimulates acid, directly or indirectly.<br>D. It clearly lowers acid; it is the main local (paracrine) brake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":86,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":86,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":254,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 24"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":36,"label":"H2 blockers partial; histamine potentiates ACh, gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-18.jpg","cap":"H2 blockers block 65% of acid; histamine potentiates ACh and gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Gastrin also drives ECL histamine; three receptors on the parietal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-110-1","s":"Q1. Which of the following is true about the mechanisms of GI acid production?","o":["A. Gastrin and ACh potentiate the action of Histamine on acid secretion.","B. The vagus nerve uses ACh to mediate G-cells located in the antrum to release Gastrin.","C. Since Atropine is anticholinergic, it can can fully prevent the parietal cells to secrete acid.","D. The K cells release Gastrin which stimulate ECL (Enterochromaffin cells) to release histamine, enhancing parietal HCl secretion.","E. Both choices A and B."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin and ACh potentiate the action of Histamine on acid secretion.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The acid stimulants <b>potentiate</b> each other: together they make more acid than the sum of each alone, because histamine (cyclic AMP) and gastrin or acetylcholine (ACh; calcium) use different signals. The 2026 slide says histamine potentiates ACh and gastrin.<br>B. The vagus stimulates G cells with gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP), not ACh.<br>C. Atropine can't fully stop acid; gastrin and histamine still act.<br>D. G cells, not K cells, make gastrin. K cells make GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide).<br>E. B is false, so 'A and B' is false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":110,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"134"},{"id":"D067-63-1","s":"Which combination of mediators demonstrates potentiation of acid secretion by acting through distinct signaling pathways?","o":["A. ACh and gastrin","B. Histamine and gastrin","C. ACh and histamine","D. Gastrin, ACh, and histamine together","E. Somatostatin, gastrin, and histamine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin, ACh, and histamine together","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastrin, ACh and histamine together.</b> Histamine works through cAMP (cyclic AMP). ACh (acetylcholine) and gastrin work through IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) and calcium. Different pathways reinforce each other, so the combined response exceeds the sum: <b>potentiation</b>. All three together show it most fully.<br>B and C. Tempting: each pair does potentiate (histamine with gastrin, histamine with ACh). But each names only part of the set that D contains, so D is the best answer.<br>A. ACh and gastrin share one pathway.<br>E. Somatostatin inhibits acid.","ex":"","fl":["D (all three together) is the intended, most complete answer. Strictly, histamine (H2, cAMP) paired with either ACh or gastrin (both IP3/Ca2+) already shows potentiation (G043:18 'Histamine potentiates acetylcholine and gastrin'), so pairs B and C are true examples too; only pair A shares one pathway."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":63,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-110-1","kind":"harder","n":"C5 extra 25"},{"id":"F1212-137-1","s":"What is the mechanism for Histamine potentiation?","o":["A. Histamine acts via Gs coupled G protein","B. H2 blockers are very effective because gastrin and Ach potentiates ECL cells to produce histamine","C. The alkaline tide potentiates the acid production reaction forward","D. Histamine binding to the H2 receptor potentiates the release of gastrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"H2 blockers are very effective because gastrin and Ach potentiates ECL cells to produce histamine","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Gastrin and acetylcholine (ACh) also act by making enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells release <b>histamine</b>. So an H2 (histamine type two) blocker also blunts part of their effect, which is why it still cuts about sixty-five percent of acid.<br>A. True (H2 uses Gs, the stimulatory G protein), but it doesn't explain why histamine boosts the other stimulants.<br>C. The alkaline tide is bicarbonate in the blood; it does not push acid production.<br>D. Histamine does not release gastrin; gastrin releases histamine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":137,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-110-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 26"},{"id":"G061-16-1","s":"Nobel Prize winner Sir James Black invented the first H2 receptor blocker, cimetidine in 1973. He had to quit his original job because his boss told him histamine is one of only three stimulants of acid secretion and that an H2 blocker will be less effective enough as a drug. What is the reason that H2 blockers are less effective as compared to PPIs?","o":["A. Histamine stimulates somatostatin","B. Cimetidine stimulates prostaglandins","C. Histamine inhibits inositol trisphosphate as well","D. Effects of acetylcholine and gastrin on gastric acid secretions","E. Acetylcholine is an insignificant stimulant of acid secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Effects of acetylcholine and gastrin on gastric acid secretions","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Acid has three stimulants. H2 (histamine type two) blockers remove only histamine, so <b>acetylcholine and gastrin still drive acid</b>; they block about sixty-five percent. Proton pump inhibitors block the final pump all three use.<br>A. Histamine does not stimulate somatostatin; somatostatin inhibits histamine.<br>B. Cimetidine has no effect on prostaglandins.<br>C. Histamine works through cyclic AMP; it does not inhibit IP3 (inositol trisphosphate).<br>E. Acetylcholine from the vagus is a major stimulant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":16,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":16,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"135"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":36,"label":"Atropine spares the vagal GRP–gastrin route","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: atropine blocks the acetylcholine receptor only","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/D061-83.jpg","cap":"Atropine X on ACh to parietal cells; GRP still drives G-cell gastrin","where":"2023 TA review, slide 83"},{"src":"../sl/G043-24.jpg","cap":"Stretch makes vagal ACh; ACh makes pepsinogen and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-118-1","s":"How does atropine affect HCl secretion?","o":["A. Partially stimulates HCl secretion","B. Completely blocks HCl secretion","C. Partially stimulates HCl secretion","D. Partially blocks HCl secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Partially blocks HCl secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Partially blocks.</b> Atropine blocks <b>muscarinic (M3) receptors</b>, so vagal acetylcholine can't act on parietal cells. But the vagus stimulates G cells with <b>gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)</b>, not acetylcholine, so gastrin and histamine keep driving acid.<br>A. Partially stimulates: atropine is a blocker; it never stimulates acid.<br>B. Not complete: the GRP–gastrin and histamine routes remain.<br>C. Same as A: atropine never stimulates acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":118,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":117,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"136"},{"id":"D066-55-1","s":"TA Colton Peck decides to embark on a journey to experience first hand what the different medications we learn about in medical school actually do to his patients. He believes that doing so would make him a more empathetic doctor. He starts with Atropine, and experiences a decrease in gastric acid secretions. Which of the following best explains how atropine affects gastric acid secretion?","o":["A. Blocks H2 receptors, decreasing cAMP production","B. Blocks M3 receptors, reducing intracellular Ca2+ signaling and potentiation of histamine-mediated acid secretion","C. Inhibits gastrin release by blocking CCK-B receptors","D. Directly inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase","E. Stimulates somatostatin release, inhibiting gastric acid secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Blocks M3 receptors, reducing intracellular Ca2+ signaling and potentiation of histamine-mediated acid secretion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Blocks M3 receptors.</b> Atropine blocks the <b>muscarinic (M3) receptor</b> on the parietal cell, where vagal ACh (acetylcholine) raises cell calcium (Ca2+). Acid falls by ACh's own share plus the boost ACh gave to histamine (potentiation). Acid is cut, not stopped.<br>A. Blocking H2 (histamine) receptors and lowering cAMP (cyclic AMP): cimetidine.<br>C. Atropine does not block CCK-B (gastrin) receptors, and vagal gastrin release uses GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide), which atropine spares.<br>D. Blocking the H+/K+ ATPase (the acid pump): omeprazole.<br>E. Atropine does not act through somatostatin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)","L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":55,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"137"},{"id":"D007-119-1","s":"Atropine blocks ACh action on muscarinic receptors. Which of the following is true regarding atropine:","o":["A. It does not block vagal stimulation on gastrin producing cells","B. It does not block vagal stimulation on parietal cells","C. It does not cause dry mouth","D. It causes excessive tear production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It does not block vagal stimulation on gastrin producing cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Vagal fibers to G cells release <b>gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)</b>, not acetylcholine. So atropine, a muscarinic blocker, <b>does not block vagal stimulation of gastrin</b>.<br>B. False: vagal acetylcholine acts on parietal-cell M3 (muscarinic) receptors, which atropine blocks.<br>C. False: atropine causes dry mouth by blocking salivary muscarinic receptors.<br>D. False: it reduces tears; anticholinergic drugs dry secretions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":119,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":81,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-118-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":37,"label":"PPIs block the pump; H2 blockers block histamine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"PPI blocks the H+/K+ ATPase; histamine acts at the H2 receptor","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G043-18.jpg","cap":"H2 blockers listed: ranitidine, cimetidine, famotidine; block 65% of acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-129-1","s":"WHAT MEDICATION IS USED FOR ULCER TREATMENT TO REDUCE H+ BY DIRECTLY INHIBITING H+-K+ ATPASE?","o":["A. OMEPRAZOLE","B. CIMETIDINE","C. ATROPINE","D. METOPROLOL"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"OMEPRAZOLE","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Omeprazole</b> is a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). It directly and irreversibly blocks the <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (hydrogen–potassium pump), the last step for all acid.<br>B. Cimetidine: blocks H2 (histamine type two) receptors.<br>C. Atropine: blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors.<br>D. Metoprolol: a beta-one blocker for blood pressure and the heart; no acid effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 19 (Proton Pump Inhibitors)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":129,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 28"},{"id":"D060-90-1","s":"A 45-year-old male with a history of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is prescribed a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). Which of the following is the primary mode of action of PPIs in treating GERD?","o":["A. Inhibiting the H2 receptors on parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion.","B. Inhibiting the proton pump (H+/K+ ATPase) on parietal cells, decreasing gastric acid production.","C. Neutralizing gastric acid in the stomach to increase pH.","D. Increasing the production of mucus and bicarbonate to protect the gastric lining from acid."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inhibiting the proton pump (H+/K+ ATPase) on parietal cells, decreasing gastric acid production.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) block the <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (hydrogen–potassium pump) on parietal cells, the final step for acid from every stimulus.<br>A. Blocking H2 (histamine type two) receptors is how H2 blockers like cimetidine work.<br>C. Neutralizing acid already made is what antacids do.<br>D. More mucus and bicarbonate is what prostaglandins such as misoprostol do.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 19 (Proton Pump Inhibitors)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":90,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"138"},{"id":"D066-63-1","s":"Excited about the GI Mod, Physiology TA Riya asks you to explain the normal function of a apical membrane transporter that is blocked by PPIs to reduce gastric acid secretion. What is the normal function of the transporter, and what is the most likely drug in question?","o":["A. Exchange intracellular H+ for luminal K+, allowing H+ secretion into the stomach; Omeprazole","B. Exchange intracellular HCO3- for luminal Cl-, producing the alkaline tide; Omeprazole","C. Transports H+ from the gastric lumen into parietal cells; omeprazole","D. Exchanges intracellular Na+ for luminal H+ to maintain gastric acidity; omeprazole","E. Transports HCO3- directly into the gastric lumen to neutralize acid; omeprazole"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Exchange intracellular H+ for luminal K+, allowing H+ secretion into the stomach; Omeprazole","a":"A","why":"<b>A. It pumps cell H+ out in exchange for luminal K+; omeprazole.</b> The <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (proton pump) on the apical membrane of the parietal cell uses ATP (adenosine triphosphate) to push hydrogen ions (H+) into the lumen and bring potassium (K+) in. PPIs (proton pump inhibitors) such as omeprazole block it.<br>B. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger sits on the blood side and is not the PPI target.<br>C. Backward: H+ goes out to the lumen.<br>D. No apical Na+/H+ exchanger makes gastric acid.<br>E. Bicarbonate (HCO3-) goes to the blood, not the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 19 (Proton Pump Inhibitors)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":63,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"139"},{"id":"D060-92-1","s":"A 45-year-old male with a history of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is prescribed a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). What is the name of the drug that was given?","o":["A. Acetaminophen","B. Cimetidine","C. Calcium Carbonate","D. Lansoprazole"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lansoprazole","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lansoprazole.</b> Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) names end in <b>-prazole</b>: omeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole.<br>A. Acetaminophen: pain and fever reliever; no acid effect.<br>B. Cimetidine: H2 (histamine type two) blocker.<br>C. Calcium carbonate: an antacid that neutralizes acid already made.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 19 (Proton Pump Inhibitors)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":92,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 29"},{"id":"D036-105-1","s":"Which of the following drugs inhibits acid secretion by blocking histamine receptors?","o":["A. Omeprazole","B. Misoprostol","C. Cimetidine","D. Octreotide","E. Metoclopramide"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cimetidine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cimetidine</b> is an <b>H2 (histamine type two) receptor blocker</b>, like ranitidine, famotidine and nizatidine. It blocks about sixty-five percent of acid.<br>A. Omeprazole: proton pump inhibitor; blocks the H+/K+ ATPase.<br>B. Misoprostol: prostaglandin E1 analog; protects the lining and lowers acid.<br>D. Octreotide: somatostatin analog.<br>E. Metoclopramide: dopamine D2 blocker; speeds gastric emptying and eases nausea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":105,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"140"},{"id":"D035-134-4","s":"Answer the following questions about Zollinger- Ellison Syndrome / What receptors are blocked by the medications?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Cimetidine → H2 receptor; Omeprazole → H-K ATPase","a":null,"why":"<b>Cimetidine blocks the H2 (histamine type two) receptor. Omeprazole blocks the H+/K+ ATPase</b> (proton pump), an enzyme rather than a receptor. For Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma), the 2026 lecture uses lifelong proton pump inhibitors when there are metastases.<br>Common mix-up: H2 blockers stop only about sixty-five percent of acid, so pump blockers are stronger.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":134,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 30"},{"id":"D024-43-1","s":"Which of the following would be the result of treating a patient with omeprazole?","o":["A. Omeprazole inhibits the H2 receptors, leading to a complete reduction of acid secretion.","B. Treatment with Atropine acts on a separate mechanism of acid production, compared to omeprazole.","C. Treatment with omeprazole can result in a reduction of lipid degradation in the stomach.","D. Treatment with cimetidine will reduce acid secretion at the same magnitude as treatment with omeprazole.","E. None of the above are correct statements."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"None of the above are correct statements.","a":"E","why":"<b>E. None are correct</b>: each statement has an error.<br>A. Omeprazole blocks the H+/K+ ATPase (proton pump), not H2 (histamine type two) receptors.<br>B. There is no separate acid mechanism: acetylcholine-driven acid leaves through the same pump omeprazole blocks. Atropine only blocks one input upstream.<br>C. Stomach fat digestion is minor, and gastric lipase still works at the higher pH a proton pump inhibitor causes.<br>D. Cimetidine blocks only about sixty-five percent of acid, so it is weaker than omeprazole.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":43,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-90-1","kind":"harder","n":"C5 extra 31"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":37,"label":"Chronic PPI → high gastrin → hyperplasia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"PPI lowers acid, lifting feedback on G cells; gastrin-induced parietal hyperplasia","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions: acid and gastric lining growth; acid suppresses gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-115-1","s":"What is a side effect of chronic PPI use?","o":["A. Hypotension","B. Gastrin-induced hyperplasia of parietal cells","C. Parietal cell atrophy","D. Diarrhea","E. Gynecomastia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin-induced hyperplasia of parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A proton pump inhibitor (PPI) lowers acid. Acid normally shuts off gastrin, so gastrin <b>rises</b> (lost feedback). Gastrin is <b>trophic</b>: chronically high levels cause <b>gastrin-induced hyperplasia</b> of parietal (and enterochromaffin-like) cells.<br>A. Hypotension is not a PPI effect.<br>C. Backwards: the cells grow, not shrink.<br>D. Diarrhea is a nonspecific drug side effect, not the gastrin-driven change asked here.<br>E. Gynecomastia goes with cimetidine (a histamine type 2 blocker), not PPIs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 37 (Q10. A patient presents with epigastric pain. Upper endoscopy shows a duodenal u)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":115,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"141"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":37,"label":"PPI lowers acid, so gastrin rises","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"PPI: less acid removes feedback on G cells, so gastrin rises","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G043-17.jpg","cap":"PPI blocks the pump; carbonic anhydrase makes H+ and bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-85-1","s":"9. A 58 year old cis-female patient comes to the office because of heartburn for two weeks. The pain most often occurs when she lies down to sleep. She also frequently wakes up with burning in her throat and epigastric area. She is treated with omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). After starting this therapy, which changes will occur for gastric acid secretion, gastrin secretion, and parietal cell carbonic anhydrase (CA) activity?","o":["A. Decreased / Decreased / Increased","B. Decreased / Increased / Decreased","C. Decreased / Increased / Increased","D. Increased / Decreased / Increased","E. Increased / Increased / Decreased","F. Decreased / No change / No change"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased / Increased / Decreased","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Decreased / Increased / Decreased.</b> Omeprazole blocks the proton pump, so <b>acid falls</b>. Acid normally brakes gastrin, so <b>gastrin rises</b>. With H+ no longer leaving, carbonic anhydrase (which makes H+ and bicarbonate) has <b>less work</b>.<br>A. Gastrin rises, not falls.<br>C. Carbonic anhydrase activity falls, not rises.<br>D. Acid falls, not rises.<br>E. Acid falls, not rises.<br>F. Gastrin and carbonic anhydrase activity do change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Option table (columns: gastric acid secretion / gastrin secretion / parietal cell CA activity) is a picture on slide 85; transcribed into the options","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":85,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Cephalic phase (30%) is entirely vagal","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-20.jpg","cap":"Cephalic phase 30%: smell, taste, conditioned reflexes","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G043-21.jpg","cap":"Vagotomy cut of anterior and posterior vagus, used before PPIs","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-116-1","s":"Q4. A hungry student was not focused during class because he was anticipating lunch time and all he could think about was food. During this time, what is occurring?","o":["A. The vagus nerve is promoting acid secretion and gastrin release.","B. His stomach is reacting and causes distension, triggering gastrin release from G-cells.","C. Protein digestion is occurring and the amino acids are stimulating CCK.","D. Nothing occurs until food enters the stomach."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The vagus nerve is promoting acid secretion and gastrin release.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Thinking about food starts the <b>cephalic phase</b> (about thirty percent of acid). The vagus releases acetylcholine onto parietal cells (direct) and gastrin-releasing peptide onto G cells (gastrin, indirect).<br>B. Distension needs food in the stomach: gastric phase.<br>C. Amino acids releasing CCK (cholecystokinin) is the intestinal phase.<br>D. Acid secretion starts before food arrives.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":116,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-58-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 33"},{"id":"D067-65-1","s":"During which phase of gastric secretion does vagal stimulation before food enters the stomach play the primary role?","o":["A. Cephalic phase","B. Gastric phase","C. Intestinal phase","D. Enterogastric phase","E. Fasting phase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cephalic phase","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cephalic phase.</b> The sight, smell, taste and thought of food act through the <b>vagus</b> before food reaches the stomach. This gives about 30% of the acid response, and vagotomy abolishes it.<br>B. Gastric phase: food in the stomach; distension and peptides; about 60%.<br>C. Intestinal phase: chyme in the duodenum; about 10%.<br>D. 'Enterogastric' names a reflex from the duodenum that slows the stomach, not a phase.<br>E. Fasting: only a low basal secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":65,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-58-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 34"},{"id":"G067-58-1","s":"The cephalic phase of gastric secretions accounts for about 30% of the acid response to a meal. Which of the following can totally eliminate the cephalic phase of gastric secretion?","o":["A. Antacids","B. Anti-gastrin antibodies","C. Atropine","D. Histamine H2 blocker","E. Vagotomy"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagotomy","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vagotomy.</b> The cephalic phase is <b>entirely vagal</b>: the vagus releases acetylcholine onto parietal cells and gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto G cells. Cutting the vagus removes both.<br>A. Antacids: neutralize acid; they don't stop secretion.<br>B. Anti-gastrin antibodies: block only the gastrin route.<br>C. Atropine: blocks only acetylcholine; the GRP–gastrin route remains.<br>D. H2 (histamine type two) blocker: blocks only histamine's part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":58,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"142"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Vagus releases GRP onto G cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Gastrin stimuli: stretch, amino acids, vagal gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/D004-100.jpg","cap":"Stomach map: GRP from vagus on G cells; gastrin to circulation","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 100"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-134-1","s":"FILL IN THE BLANK: THE VAGUS NERVE DIRECTLY/INDIRECTLY PRODUCES HCL THROUGH THE SIMULATION OF G CELLS BY GASTRIN","o":["A. DIRECTLY","B. INDIRECTLY"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"INDIRECTLY","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Indirectly.</b> The vagus releases <b>gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)</b> onto antral G cells. They release gastrin into the blood, and gastrin then stimulates parietal cells. The direct route is acetylcholine acting on parietal cells.<br>A. Directly: that describes vagal acetylcholine on parietal M3 (muscarinic) receptors, not the G-cell route.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":134,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 35"},{"id":"D009-20-1","s":"Cephalic: accounts for 30% of total HCL secretions in response to meals. Stimulated by Smelling, tasting, chewing, swallowing and conditioned reflexes. Two main mechanisms, Direct via vagus nerve stimulation, releasing Ach to act on parietal cells. And the indirect via gastrin stimulation of parietal cells. How is gastrin stimulated in the Indirect mechanism?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The vagus nerve releases GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide) onto G cells, which release gastrin to act on parietal cells","a":null,"why":"<b>The vagus releases gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto G cells.</b> G cells release gastrin into the blood, and gastrin stimulates parietal cells through CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) receptors. This is the indirect vagal route of the cephalic phase.<br>Common mix-up: the G-cell transmitter is GRP, not acetylcholine, so atropine does not block this route.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 3","sl":20,"f":"D009"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 36"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Gastric phase (60%): distension and protein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-20.jpg","cap":"Phases: cephalic 30% smell/taste, gastric 60% distention/amino acids, intestinal 10%","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Stretch, amino acids and vagus make gastrin, acid and pepsinogen","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-132-1","s":"WHAT PERCENTAGE OF TOTAL HCL SECRETION DOES THE GASTRIC PHASE ACCOUNT FOR?","o":["A. 30%","B. 60%","C. 10%","D. 50%"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"60%","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sixty percent.</b> The <b>gastric phase</b> gives most of the acid. Its triggers are distension, amino acids and peptides, vagal reflexes, gastrin, alcohol and caffeine.<br>A. Thirty percent: cephalic phase (smell, taste, conditioned reflexes).<br>C. Ten percent: intestinal phase.<br>D. Fifty percent: not a phase value.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":132,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-67-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 37"},{"id":"D066-67-1","s":"A 24-year-old medical student has been fasting since the previous evening. While walking past a restaurant, she smells freshly prepared food and begins anticipating her meal. Shortly afterward, she eats a protein-rich lunch, resulting in gastric distension and increased acid secretion. Which of the following best describes the mechanisms responsible for gastric acid secretion before and after eating?","o":["A. The cephalic phase accounts for approximately 60% of gastric acid secretion and is primarily stimulated by gastric distension.","B. The gastric phase accounts for approximately 30% of gastric acid secretion and is primarily mediated by secretin release.","C. The cephalic phase 30% is vagally mediated, while the gastric phase 60% responds to distension and protein digestion products.","D. The intestinal phase accounts for approximately 60% of gastric acid secretion and is primarily stimulated by acidic chyme entering the duodenum.","E. Gastric acid secretion is inhibited during the gastric phase due to increased gastrin and histamine release."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The cephalic phase 30% is vagally mediated, while the gastric phase 60% responds to distension and protein digestion products.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cephalic 30%, vagal; gastric 60%, distension and protein products.</b> Smell and anticipation act through the <b>vagus</b> before food arrives: the cephalic phase, about 30% of the acid. Food in the stomach then acts by <b>distension and peptides</b>, through gastrin and vagal reflexes: the gastric phase, about 60%.<br>A. The cephalic phase is 30% and involves no distension.<br>B. The gastric phase is 60%; secretin inhibits acid.<br>D. The intestinal phase is only about 10%.<br>E. Gastrin and histamine stimulate acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":67,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"143"},{"id":"D008-88-1","s":"The Gastric phase accounts for 60% of total postprandial HCl secretion. It’s stimulated by smelling, tasting and conditioned reflexes.","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> Sixty percent is right for the <b>gastric phase</b>, but its triggers are <b>stomach distension and protein products (amino acids, peptides)</b>, acting through vagal reflexes and gastrin. Smelling, tasting and conditioned reflexes trigger the <b>cephalic phase</b> (thirty percent).<br>Common mix-up: both phases use the vagus, but only the cephalic phase starts before food reaches the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":88,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":88,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":256,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-67-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 38"},{"id":"D024-45-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches stimuli with mechanisms for HCl secretion?","o":["A. The sound of bacon sizzling in the frying pan, leading to vagal stimulation of parietal cells.","B. Distension of the stomach antrum, increasing gastrin stimulation of parietal cells.","C. Carbohydrates in the stomach stimulates gastrin secretion stimulating parietal cells.","D. All of the above are correct."],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Distension of the stomach antrum, increasing gastrin stimulation of parietal cells.","a":"B","why":"<b>A and B</b> are both correct. A food sound is a <b>conditioned (cephalic) stimulus</b>: the vagus releases acetylcholine onto parietal cells. <b>Antral distension</b> (gastric phase) releases gastrin, which stimulates parietal cells.<br>C. Carbohydrates do not release gastrin; amino acids and small peptides do.<br>D. All of the above: C is false.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only B, but a food sound is a cephalic (conditioned) stimulus that triggers vagal acetylcholine on parietal cells, so A is also correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)","L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":45,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-67-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 39"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Stretch makes gastrin, ACh, acid, prostaglandins, mucus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Gastric pit: stretch makes gastrin, acid, pepsinogen and prostaglandins; mucus protects","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G043-24.jpg","cap":"Distention makes gastrin, vagal ACh, acid, pepsinogen, prostaglandins and mucus","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D063-36-1","s":"As you eat Thanksgiving dinner, which of the following is most likely occurring?","o":["A. Chemoreceptors are sensing the stretch of the stomach and signaling the brain through the efferent vagus nerve resulting in increased gastrin levels","B. Filling of the stomach results in release of NE into the myenteric plexus increasing muscle contraction","C. PSNS stimulation gradually increases the frequency of contraction of the stomach","D. The muscularis mucosa is primarily responsible for the motility","E. VIP, ACh and gastrin are released"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP, ACh and gastrin are released","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Eating stretches the stomach. Vagovagal reflexes release <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> for receptive relaxation and <b>acetylcholine</b>; stretch and protein release <b>gastrin</b>.<br>A. Stretch is sensed by mechanoreceptors and carried by vagal afferents, not chemoreceptors and efferents.<br>B. Norepinephrine inhibits gut contraction.<br>C. The vagus raises contraction force; the rate is set by slow waves from ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal).<br>D. The muscularis externa, not the muscularis mucosae, drives motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":36,"f":"D063"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 40"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Acid stimulants: ACh, gastrin, histamine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell with M3, H2, CCKB receptors; stimulators and inhibitors listed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure annotated: ACh, gastrin, histamine stimulate; somatostatin, prostaglandins inhibit","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-28-1","s":"Which of the following agents is expected to enhance HCl secretion by acting on parietal cells during a period of stomach distention (allowing enough time for physiological responses to distention to occur)?","o":["a. Acetylcholine","b. Gastrin","c. Histamine","d. A, B and C are correct","e. B and C are correct (but not A)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"A, B and C are correct","a":"d","why":"<b>D. A, B and C.</b> Stretch triggers vagal reflexes that release <b>acetylcholine (ACh)</b> onto parietal cells. It raises <b>gastrin</b> from antral G cells, and gastrin makes enterochromaffin-like cells release <b>histamine</b>. All three bind parietal-cell receptors: M3 (muscarinic), CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) and H2 (histamine type two).<br>A, B, C alone: each is true but incomplete.<br>E. Leaves out ACh, which distension releases through vagovagal reflexes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":48,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":3,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":28,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":185,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":38,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":35,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":33,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"144"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Protein meal: gastrin plus acid buffering","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Amino acids, stretch and vagus make gastrin and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G043-23.jpg","cap":"Inhibition of HCl: H+ (acid) feedback-inhibits gastrin release","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-5-1","s":"Protein-rich meals cause the largest secretion of HCl because: (Assume the meals being compared cause equal stomach distension.)","o":["A. Of the buffering capacity of protein.","B. Protein stimulates cells in the antrum of the stomach to release gastrin.","C. Digested protein stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) which speeds stomach emptying.","D. All of the above.","E. Only a and b are correct."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Only a and b are correct.","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Only A and B.</b> Protein products (amino acids, peptides) <b>stimulate antral G cells</b> to release gastrin. Protein also <b>buffers acid</b>, so stomach pH stays higher and acid's feedback brake on gastrin is lifted.<br>A. True but incomplete.<br>B. True but incomplete.<br>C. False: cholecystokinin (CCK) slows gastric emptying.<br>D. All of the above: C is false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":5,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":5,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"145"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":38,"label":"Somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"D cell somatostatin blocks gastrin, ECL cell and parietal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-41.jpg","cap":"PGE2 inhibits acid through Gi, the inhibitory G protein","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-23.jpg","cap":"Inhibition: H+ feedback, prostaglandins stop acid, somatostatin inhibits gastrin and histamine","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-7-31","s":"Which of the following is the principal paracrine secretion involved in the inhibitory feedback regulation of gastric acid secretions?","o":["a. Gastrin","b. Somatostatin","c. Histamine","d. Enterogastrone"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Somatostatin</b> is the main <b>paracrine</b> (local) brake. Acid in the antrum makes D cells release somatostatin, which blocks gastrin and histamine release and acts on parietal cells.<br>A. Gastrin: a hormone that stimulates acid.<br>C. Histamine: a paracrine that stimulates acid.<br>D. Enterogastrone: a general name for intestinal hormones, such as GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide), that inhibit the stomach; hormones, not paracrines.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)","L53 slide 11 (Histamine makes acid)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":7,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"146"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Acid converts pepsinogen to pepsin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-9.jpg","cap":"Acid activates pepsinogen in the lumen above the mucus layer","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G043-27.jpg","cap":"Pepsinogen converted by acid to pepsin; inactivated above pH 5","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-29-1","s":"How is pepsinogen activated?","o":["A. a. Increased pH","B. b. Proteolytic cleavage by trypsin","C. c. Proteolytic cleavage by enterokinase","D. d. Increased concentration of intrinsic factor","E. e. Increased concentration of protons"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"e. Increased concentration of protons","a":"E","why":"<b>E. More protons (acid).</b> Low pH converts pepsinogen into active <b>pepsin</b>, and pepsin then activates more pepsinogen. Pepsin is inactivated above pH five.<br>A. A higher pH inactivates pepsin.<br>B. Trypsin activates pancreatic proenzymes in the duodenum, not pepsinogen.<br>C. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen into trypsin.<br>D. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12; it has no role in pepsinogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":29,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"147"},{"id":"F1203-70-1","s":"A 45 year-old Male presents to the Emergency Department complaining of right upper quadrant pain. He also reports a history of foul-smelling diarrhea. He is subsequently diagnosed with a somatostatinoma and is informed that he may experience gastritis due to a decrease in gastrin secretion. The enzyme that is most affected by this decrease in gastrin secretion is:","o":["A. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","B. Pepsin","C. Secretin","D. Trypsin","E. VIP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pepsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pepsin.</b> A somatostatinoma floods the body with somatostatin, which <b>lowers gastrin</b>, so <b>acid falls</b>. Acid converts pepsinogen (from chief cells) into active <b>pepsin</b>, so pepsin drops.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): a hormone, not an enzyme.<br>C. Secretin: a hormone, not an enzyme.<br>D. Trypsin: activated by enterokinase in the duodenum, not by acid.<br>E. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): a neurotransmitter, not an enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":70,"f":"F1203"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":73,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D014-29-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 41"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Pepsin: not essential, inactivated in duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-28.jpg","cap":"Pepsin starts protein digestion, not essential, neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G043-27.jpg","cap":"Pepsin inactivated above pH 5; mucus surface pH near 7","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-120-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices regarding pepsin is correct?","o":["A. Pepsin is essential for protein digestion","B. Pepsinogen is secreted by parietal cells","C. Pepsin is most active at an alkaline pH","D. Pepsinogen is activated to pepsin by trypsin","E. Pepsin is inactivated once it reaches the duodenum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pepsin is inactivated once it reaches the duodenum","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Pepsin is <b>neutralized by bicarbonate in the duodenum</b>; it is inactivated above pH five. Trypsin and the other pancreatic proteases take over.<br>A. Pepsin is not essential: pancreatic proteases digest protein without it.<br>B. Chief cells, not parietal cells, secrete pepsinogen.<br>C. Pepsin works best at acid pH, about one to three.<br>D. Acid, not trypsin, activates pepsinogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 28 (Starts process of Protein digestion)","L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":120,"f":"D008"}],"tier":1,"n":"148"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Less gastrin, less acid, less pepsin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-9.jpg","cap":"Acid in the gastric lumen activates pepsinogen to pepsin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin's first action: acid (H+) secretion from parietal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-58-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with right upper quadrant pain. He also states a history of foul-smelling diarrhea. He is subsequently diagnosed with somatostatinomas and informed that he may experience gastritis due to decrease gastrin secretion. The enzyme that is most affected by decrease gastrin secretion is","o":["A. CCK","B. Pepsin","C. Secretin","D. Trypsin","E. VIP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pepsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pepsin.</b> Gastrin drives acid secretion, and <b>acid converts pepsinogen into active pepsin</b>. A somatostatinoma lowers gastrin, so less acid forms and less pepsin is activated.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): a hormone, not an enzyme.<br>C. Secretin: a hormone, not an enzyme.<br>D. Trypsin: activated in the duodenum by enterokinase, not by stomach acid.<br>E. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP): a neurocrine, not an enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":19,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":58,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 42"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Atropine spares the vagal GRP–gastrin route","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: atropine blocks the acetylcholine receptor only","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/D061-83.jpg","cap":"Atropine X on ACh to parietal cells; GRP still drives G-cell gastrin","where":"2023 TA review, slide 83"},{"src":"../sl/G043-24.jpg","cap":"Stretch makes vagal ACh; ACh makes pepsinogen and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-99-1","s":"5. Which substance does atropine block its release?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Mucous","C. Pepsin","D. Histamine"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Histamine","a":"D","why":"<b>C. Pepsin and D. Histamine.</b> Atropine blocks muscarinic acetylcholine (ACh) receptors. The 2026 lecture says <b>ACh makes pepsinogen</b>, so less pepsin forms. ACh also drives enterochromaffin-like cells to release <b>histamine</b>.<br>A. Gastrin: the vagus stimulates G cells with gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP), which atropine does not block.<br>B. Mucus: the 2026 lecture ties mucus to prostaglandins made by stretch, not to acetylcholine.","ex":"","fl":["Course key is histamine (vagal ACh drives ECL histamine release). But the 2026 deck also says 'Ach makes pepsinogen' (G043:24), so atropine would also block pepsinogen/pepsin release (option C); only gastrin (GRP-mediated) is clearly not blocked.","Source keyed only histamine, but the 2026 lecture says 'ACh makes pepsinogen,' so atropine also lowers pepsinogen and therefore pepsin."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 16 (dcvasdcasdcasdcsadcasdcascdsdccc)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":99,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":99,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 43"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Intrinsic factor → B12 absorbed in ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor: the essential gastric secretion, needed for B12 absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"B12 path: freed by pepsin, R-protein, then intrinsic factor, terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D046-8.jpg","cap":"Parietal cells in the body secrete HCl and intrinsic factor","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-71-1","s":"What is the primary function of intrinsic factor?","o":["A. Neutralize stomach acid","B. Activate pepsinogen to pepsin","C. Facilitate absorption of vitamin B12 in the ileum","D. Protect the gastric mucosa"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Facilitate absorption of vitamin B12 in the ileum","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Intrinsic factor</b>, a glycoprotein from parietal cells, binds vitamin B12. The complex is absorbed by receptors in the <b>terminal ileum</b>. Without it, B12 is not absorbed (pernicious anemia).<br>A. Acid is neutralized by bicarbonate from surface mucous cells and the pancreas.<br>B. Hydrochloric acid activates pepsinogen to pepsin.<br>D. Mucus and bicarbonate protect the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":71,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"149"},{"id":"D036-107-1","s":"Which of the following is needed for the absorption of vitamin B12?","o":["A. Gastrin","B. Secretin","C. Pepsinogen","D. Intrinsic factor","E. Acid"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Intrinsic factor</b> from parietal cells binds vitamin B12 so it can be absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>. It is the only gastric secretion we cannot live without.<br>A. Gastrin stimulates acid; B12 does not need it.<br>B. Secretin stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>C. Pepsin helps free B12 from food, but it is not essential.<br>E. Acid helps release B12 from food, but it is not essential.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":107,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 44"},{"id":"F1212-156-1","s":"Which of the following is essential for Vitamin B12 absorption?","o":["A. Intrinsic Factor","B. Pepsin","C. Gastrin","D. Bicarbonate","E. Hydrochloric Acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic Factor","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Intrinsic factor</b>, secreted by <b>parietal cells</b>, binds vitamin B12 so it is absorbed in the terminal ileum. Without it, B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia) follows.<br>B. Pepsin frees B12 from food but is not essential.<br>C. Gastrin stimulates acid.<br>D. Bicarbonate neutralizes acid.<br>E. Hydrochloric acid helps release B12 but is not essential.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":156,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 45"},{"id":"G066-109-1","s":"In the process of vitamin B12 absorption, what is the crucial factor secreted by the stomach lining that binds to the vitamin and allows its absorption in the small intestine?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Gastrin","C. Intrinsic factor","D. Mucin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Parietal cells of the stomach body (oxyntic glands) secrete <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which binds vitamin B12 and lets it be absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>A. Pepsin digests protein (and frees B12 from food) but does not carry it.<br>B. Gastrin stimulates acid.<br>D. Mucin forms the protective mucus layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":109,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":108,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 46"},{"id":"G066-139-1","s":"A patient presents with fatigue, weakness, and pallor. Further investigation reveals a deficiency in Vitamin B12. Which of the following is essential for the absorption of Vitamin B12?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Gastrin","D. Pancreatic lipase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Fatigue and pallor from vitamin B12 deficiency: B12 absorption needs <b>intrinsic factor</b> from parietal cells, and the B12–intrinsic factor complex is absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>A. Pepsin is not essential.<br>C. Gastrin stimulates acid.<br>D. Pancreatic lipase digests fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":139,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":138,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-71-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Intrinsic factor: only essential gastric secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-49.jpg","cap":"Pepsin, acid, mucus not essential; intrinsic factor is, for B12","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G047-4.jpg","cap":"Gastrectomy: no intrinsic factor, so B12 must be given by injection","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-136-1","s":"Which is the only essential stomach secretion, which cell secretes it, and what is the name of the gastrointestinal disorder that occurs when there is a deficiency in its secretion?","o":["A. Gastrin, released by G-cells in the antrum, deficiency may lead to malabsorption","B. Mucous, released by Mucous cells in the Antrum, deficiency may lead to ulcers","C. Intrinsic factor, released by parietal cells in the body of the stomach, deficiency leads to pernicious anemia","D. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin), released by parietal cells in in the body of the stomach, deficiency leads to pernicious anemia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor, released by parietal cells in the body of the stomach, deficiency leads to pernicious anemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Intrinsic factor</b> is the only <b>essential</b> stomach secretion. <b>Parietal cells</b> in the body make it; without it, vitamin B12 is not absorbed and <b>pernicious anemia</b> follows.<br>A. Gastrin (G cells, antrum) is not essential.<br>B. Mucus is not essential: without acid it is not needed.<br>D. Vitamin B12 comes from food, not from parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":136,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":136,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":304,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"150"},{"id":"D010-94-1","s":"3. Which of the following is the only essential secretion from the stomach?","o":["A. Intrinsic factor","B. Vitamine B12","C. Gastrin","D. Mucous"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Without a stomach we still digest food: pepsin, and so acid and mucus, are <b>not essential</b>. The one thing we cannot replace by mouth is <b>intrinsic factor</b>, from parietal cells, needed for vitamin B12 absorption.<br>B. Vitamin B12 is eaten in food; the stomach does not secrete it.<br>C. Gastrin is not essential.<br>D. Mucus is needed only to protect against acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":94,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":94,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-136-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":39,"label":"Parietal cell loss → pernicious anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-14.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: acid to lumen, bicarbonate to blood (alkaline tide)","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D046-8.jpg","cap":"Parietal cells in the stomach body secrete HCl and intrinsic factor","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G047-4.jpg","cap":"Gastrectomy: no parietal cells for intrinsic factor; B12 must be injected","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-137-1","s":"ABSENCE OF INTRINSIC FACTOR SECRETION CAUSES WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING?","o":["A. GASTRIC ULCER","B. ZOLLINGER-ELLISON SYNDROME","C. CYSTIC FIBROSIS","D. PERNICIOUS ANEMIA"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"PERNICIOUS ANEMIA","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> No intrinsic factor means vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the ileum: <b>pernicious anemia</b> (a megaloblastic anemia), usually from autoimmune destruction of parietal cells.<br>A. Gastric ulcer comes from loss of the mucous barrier (Helicobacter pylori, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs).<br>B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor.<br>C. Cystic fibrosis is a chloride-channel gene defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":137,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G063-5-19","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 49"},{"id":"D067-69-1","s":"A patient presents with macrocytic anemia, glossitis, and loss of proprioception. Which of the following best explains these findings?","o":["A. Lack of gastrin secretion","B. Destruction of G cells in the antrum","C. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells and loss of intrinsic factor","D. Deficiency of pancreatic proteases","E. Zinc deficiency leading to mucosal atrophy"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells and loss of intrinsic factor","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells with loss of intrinsic factor.</b> Without <b>intrinsic factor</b>, vitamin B12 is not absorbed in the ileum. B12 deficiency gives large red cells (macrocytic anemia), a sore smooth tongue, and damage to the posterior columns of the spinal cord (lost position sense).<br>A. Low gastrin lowers acid; it does not block B12 uptake.<br>B. G cells make gastrin, not intrinsic factor.<br>D. Protease lack would show mainly as fatty stools; this triad is classic pernicious anemia.<br>E. Zinc deficiency does not cause B12 deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":69,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G063-5-19","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 50"},{"id":"G063-5-19","s":"A 60-year-old man with atrophic gastritis and has been found to have a significantly decreased number of parietal cells. A deficiency in which of the following is most likely to be observed in this patient?","o":["a. Neutralization of chyme by bicarbonate","b. Pepsinogen secretion","c. Secretion of gastrin","d. Absorption of vitamin B12","e. Mucus coating of the gastric lining"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of vitamin B12","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> Parietal cells secrete acid and <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Losing them in atrophic gastritis removes intrinsic factor, so <b>vitamin B12 is not absorbed</b> in the ileum.<br>A. Bicarbonate that neutralizes chyme comes from the pancreas and duodenum.<br>B. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells.<br>C. Gastrin (G cells, antrum) actually <b>rises</b>, because low acid removes its brake.<br>E. Mucus comes from mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":5,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"151"},{"id":"D066-69-1","s":"A 58-year-old woman with a history of autoimmune gastritis presents with progressive fatigue, numbness in her feet, and difficulty maintaining balance. Laboratory studies reveal macrocytic anemia and decreased serum vitamin B12 levels. Further testing demonstrates antibodies against gastric parietal cells. Which of the following best explains the mechanism responsible for her condition?","o":["A. Destruction of chief cells prevents pepsinogen secretion, resulting in impaired vitamin B12 absorption in the duodenum.","B. Loss of parietal cells decreases intrinsic factor secretion, preventing vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum.","C. Decreased gastrin secretion prevents the conversion of vitamin B12 into its active form.","D. Loss of gastric hydrochloric acid directly prevents vitamin B12 absorption in the jejunum, independently of intrinsic factor.","E. Destruction of mucous cells prevents bile salt formation, impairing vitamin B12 absorption."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Loss of parietal cells decreases intrinsic factor secretion, preventing vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lost parietal cells, less intrinsic factor, no B12 absorption in the terminal ileum.</b> Parietal cells make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which must bind vitamin B12 for uptake in the terminal ileum. Autoimmune loss of these cells gives pernicious anemia: large red cells and nerve damage.<br>A. Chief cells make pepsinogen, which B12 absorption does not need.<br>C. Gastrin does not activate B12.<br>D. Acid only helps free B12 from food; absorption is in the ileum and needs intrinsic factor.<br>E. Bile salts come from the liver, not mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)","L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":69,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"152"},{"id":"D040-118-1","s":"Q5. A patient was found to have an autoimmune destruction to cells whose role includes creating an alkaline tide on the basolateral side. What additional findings would also be present?","o":["A. Increase in amylase and lipase.","B. The body loses its ability to secrete pepsinogen.","C. The destruction is found in the antrum of the stomach.","D. Low hemoglobin."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Low hemoglobin.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The <b>alkaline tide</b> (bicarbonate sent into the blood) comes from <b>parietal cells</b> as they secrete acid. Parietal cells also make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so their autoimmune loss blocks vitamin B12 absorption: <b>pernicious anemia</b>, low hemoglobin.<br>A. Amylase and lipase come from the pancreas.<br>B. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells.<br>C. Parietal cells live in the body and fundus, not the antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":118,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G063-5-19","kind":"harder","n":"C5 extra 51"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":40,"label":"Mucus–bicarbonate barrier protects the lining","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-27.jpg","cap":"Bicarbonate trapped in mucus keeps the cell surface near pH 7","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Gastric pit: stretch makes prostaglandins; mucus protects","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-124-1","s":"WHAT IS THE ROLE OF MUCUS IN THE GASTRIC SYSTEM?","o":["A. TRAPS ALLERGENS AND DUST","B. SECRETES BICARBONATE AND PEPSINOGEN","C. INITIATES PROTEIN DIGESTION","D. PROTECTS GASTRIC MUCOSA FROM HCL & LUBRICATES GASTRIC CONTENTS"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"PROTECTS GASTRIC MUCOSA FROM HCL & LUBRICATES GASTRIC CONTENTS","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Mucus</b> forms a gel that <b>protects the gastric lining from hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin</b> and lubricates the contents. Bicarbonate trapped in the gel keeps the cell surface near pH seven while the lumen is near pH two.<br>A. Trapping dust and allergens: that is airway mucus.<br>B. Mucus is a product; it secretes nothing. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells.<br>C. Protein digestion starts with pepsin, made from pepsinogen by acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 7 (Stretch +/)","L53 slide 25 (Stretch)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":124,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 52"},{"id":"D064-21-1","s":"What protects stomach lining?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The mucosal barrier (mucus + HCO3-), maintained by prostaglandins; mucosal blood flow also protects by bringing O2/HCO3- and removing H+","a":null,"why":"<b>The mucus–bicarbonate barrier.</b> Mucous cells make a gel, and surface cells secrete bicarbonate (HCO3-) into it, so the cell surface stays near pH seven. <b>Prostaglandins</b> keep up the mucus and bicarbonate. <b>Mucosal blood flow</b> brings oxygen and bicarbonate and carries acid away.<br>Common mix-up: NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) cause ulcers by stopping prostaglandins, which removes this barrier.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":21,"f":"D064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 53"},{"id":"G052-7-2","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: Normally, why is not the gastric mucosa eroded and digested by the H+ and pepsin that are present in the gastric lumen?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A mucus gel barrier from mucous neck cells with trapped HCO3- secreted by epithelial cells neutralizes H+ and inactivates pepsin before they reach the epithelium.","a":null,"why":"<b>A mucus gel barrier with trapped bicarbonate.</b> Mucous neck cells secrete mucus. Surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate (HCO3-) into it. Any acid (H+, hydrogen ion) that gets in is neutralized, and pepsin is inactivated above pH five before it reaches the cells.<br>Common mix-up: pepsin alone does not cause ulcers. The barrier must be lost first, for example by H. pylori or NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)","L53 slide 25 (Stretch)","L53 slide 7 (Stretch +/)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 54"},{"id":"G066-122-1","s":"Which of the following components contributes to the maintenance of the protective barrier between gastric epithelial cells and the gastric lumen?","o":["A. Mucous neck glands","B. Gastric epithelial cells","C. Prostaglandin E1","D. Pepsin"],"g":["A","B","C"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucous neck glands","a":"A","why":"<b>A, B and C</b> all help keep the <b>mucus–bicarbonate barrier</b>. Mucous neck cells make the mucus gel. Gastric surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate (HCO3-) that is trapped in the gel. E-type prostaglandins boost mucus and bicarbonate: the 2026 lecture says prostaglandins make mucous, and misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 copy, protects the stomach.<br>D. Pepsin: attacks the lining once the barrier is lost; it does not protect it.","ex":"","fl":["Key A is acceptable (mucus is the barrier), but the item is ambiguous: surface gastric epithelial cells (B) secrete the HCO3- and mucus of the barrier, and prostaglandins (C) maintain it (2026 G043:25 'stretch makes prostaglandins that make the mucous'; misoprostol is a PGE1 analog). The explanation's 'PGE1 keeps ductus open' is not a valid reason to exclude C.","Source keyed only A (mucous neck glands), but surface epithelial cells secrete the bicarbonate trapped in the mucus and E-type prostaglandins maintain the mucus, so B and C also contribute."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 25 (Stretch)","L53 slide 7 (Stretch +/)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":122,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":121,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 55"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":40,"label":"Stretch makes gastrin, ACh, acid, prostaglandins, mucus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-7.jpg","cap":"Gastric pit: stretch makes gastrin, acid, pepsinogen and prostaglandins; mucus protects","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G043-24.jpg","cap":"Distention makes gastrin, vagal ACh, acid, pepsinogen, prostaglandins and mucus","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-99-1","s":"Which of the following results from distension of the stomach?","o":["A. Gastrin release","B. Acid release","C. Acetylcholine release","D. Prostaglandin release","E. Mucous Release","F. All of the above"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"F","why":"<b>F. All of the above.</b> In the 2026 lecture, <b>stretch makes gastrin</b> (gastrin makes acid), <b>stretch makes vagal acetylcholine</b> (which makes acid and pepsinogen), and <b>stretch makes prostaglandins</b>, which make the mucus that protects from acid and pepsin.<br>A–E alone: each is true, but none is the whole answer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 24 (Distention (Stretch) makes…)","L53 slide 7 (Stretch +/)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":117,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 56"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":40,"label":"NSAIDs remove prostaglandin-driven mucus barrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-43.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs remove the mucus layer, then acid cooks the lining","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G043-42.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs block COX-1 prostaglandins that protect the gastric mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/D036-113.jpg","cap":"Protective factors (mucus, bicarbonate, prostaglandins, blood flow) versus NSAIDs","where":"2024 TA review, slide 113"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-112-1","s":"Q2. A patient with a past medical history of rheumatoid arthritis was found to have a NSAID-induced peptic ulcer and was prescribed Misoprostol, a PGE1 analog. How would this benefit the patient?","o":["A. It irreversibly inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase in stomach parietal cells.","B. It antagonizes histamine’s stimulatory action on H+ secretion by activating Gs and increasing cAMP.","C. Its vasodilatory effect allows for increased mucosal blood flow and promotes mucus/bicarb production.","D. All of the above."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Its vasodilatory effect allows for increased mucosal blood flow and promotes mucus/bicarb production.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog, replaces the <b>prostaglandins</b> that nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) remove. Prostaglandins <b>raise mucosal blood flow</b> and drive <b>mucus and bicarbonate</b>, rebuilding the barrier.<br>A. Irreversible block of the H+/K+ ATPase is how proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole) work.<br>B. Prostaglandins do oppose histamine, but through the <b>inhibitory G protein (Gi)</b>, which <b>lowers</b> cyclic AMP (cAMP); it does not use Gs to raise it.<br>D. A and B are false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)","L53 slide 25 (Stretch)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":112,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"153"},{"id":"D022-27-1","s":"An electrophysiologist develops low back pain exacerbated by leaning over microscopes and sudden stretching movements to reach all of the electronic dials and switches. She regularly takes medications for the back pain and develops abdominal pain. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Decreased mucus production","b. Excess acid secretion","c. Increased acetylcholine","d. Increased prostaglandins","e. Increased ammonia production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased mucus production","a":"a","why":"<b>A.</b> Her back-pain drug is a <b>nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)</b>. NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase (COX), so the stomach loses <b>prostaglandin E2</b>, which drives <b>mucus and bicarbonate</b>. Without that barrier, acid 'cooks' the lining.<br>B. Excess acid is not the main problem; normal acid harms once the mucus is gone.<br>C. NSAIDs do not change acetylcholine.<br>D. Backwards: prostaglandins fall.<br>E. Ammonia is Helicobacter pylori's survival trick, not an NSAID effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 43 (NSAIDs remove this)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":46,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":27,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":169,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":22,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":20,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":35,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"154"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":40,"label":"NSAIDs, including aspirin, cause gastric ulcers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-42.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs block COX-1, losing prostaglandins that protect gastric mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G043-33.jpg","cap":"Ulcer causes listed: H. pylori, NSAIDs, trauma, burns, ventilators","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-121-1","s":"Which of the following in excess most likely causes gastric ulcers?","o":["A. Aspirin","B. Acetaminophen","C. Aspirin","D. Erythromycin","E. Penicillin"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Aspirin","a":"A","why":"<b>A and C</b> (both read aspirin, a source typo). Aspirin is a <b>nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)</b>. It blocks cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1), so the stomach loses the <b>prostaglandins</b> that maintain mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow, and acid erodes the lining.<br>B. Acetaminophen barely blocks COX in the stomach, so it does not cause ulcers.<br>D. Erythromycin is an antibiotic (it can cause nausea), not an ulcer cause.<br>E. Penicillin does not cause ulcers.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only A, but options A and C both read 'Aspirin', so both are correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 43 (NSAIDs remove this)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":121,"f":"D007"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 57"},{"id":"F1212-150-1","s":"Which of the following most commonly causes gastric ulcers?","o":["A. NSAIDs","B. Acetaminophen","C. Aspirin","D. Erythromycin","E. Penicillin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"NSAIDs","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)</b> block cyclooxygenase (COX), removing the <b>prostaglandins</b> that maintain the mucus–bicarbonate barrier. With Helicobacter pylori, they are a leading cause of gastric ulcers.<br>B. Acetaminophen is not an NSAID and spares the stomach.<br>C. Aspirin is one NSAID; the whole class (A) is the better answer.<br>D. Erythromycin is an antibiotic, not an ulcer cause.<br>E. Penicillin does not cause ulcers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":150,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 58"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"Ulcer needs acid plus lost mucus barrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-43.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs remove the mucus layer, then acid cooks the lining","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G043-33.jpg","cap":"Peptic ulcer causes: H. pylori, NSAIDs, trauma; barrier loss needed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-111-1","s":"Which one does NOT contribute to peptic ulcer disease?","o":["A. Excessive H+","B. Excessive HCO3-","C. Pylori","D. NSAIDS","E. Loss of mucus barrier"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Excessive HCO3-","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Excess bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b> protects the lining by neutralizing acid in the mucus layer. Ulcers need acid plus loss of the mucus barrier.<br>A. Excess acid (H+): overwhelms the barrier; no acid, no ulcer.<br>C. H. pylori: destroys the mucus barrier and damages the epithelium.<br>D. NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs): stop protective prostaglandin E2.<br>E. Loss of the mucus barrier: lets acid and pepsin reach the cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":111,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"155"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"H. pylori causes gastritis and ulcers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"H. pylori colonizes mucus, survives by urease, then damages the mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G043-33.jpg","cap":"Ulcer causes: H. pylori, NSAIDs; pepsin alone does not ulcerate","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-145-1","s":"A 56 year old male comes in to your office complaining of heartburn and a dull pain in his stomach. He lost 10 pounds due to not wanting to eat because of the pain. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Omeprazole","C. Helicobacter pylori","D. Campylobacter jejuni","E. Pepsinogen"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Helicobacter pylori","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Burning, dull stomach pain that makes him <b>avoid eating</b> suggests a <b>gastric ulcer</b>, and the commonest cause is <b>Helicobacter pylori</b>. It destroys the mucous barrier so acid and pepsin can digest the lining.<br>A. Pepsin does not cause ulcers by itself; the mucous barrier must be lost first.<br>B. Omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that treats ulcers.<br>D. Campylobacter jejuni causes bloody diarrhea, not ulcers.<br>E. Pepsinogen is pepsin's inactive precursor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 59"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"Urease makes NH3, which neutralizes acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-37.jpg","cap":"Urease splits urea; NH3 grabs acid, forming safe NH4+","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"Panel two: urease turns urea into CO2 and NH3, neutralizing acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D023-56-1","s":"What does H Pylori produce to neutralize acid?","o":["A. Urease","B. Urea","C. Ammonia (NH3)","D. Ammonium (NH4+)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ammonia (NH3)","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) neutralizes acid with <b>ammonia (NH3)</b>. Its enzyme urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and ammonia grabs acid (H+) to form harmless ammonium (NH4+).<br>A. Urease is the enzyme that makes the ammonia; it does not neutralize acid itself.<br>B. Urea is the starting material it splits.<br>D. Ammonium is the product left after ammonia has already bound the acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 37 (Acid Attack!)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":56,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":84,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":87,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-189-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 60"},{"id":"D004-189-1","s":"This organism is found in a stomach biopsy. What is the mechanisms that allows it to exist in the acidic environment of the stomach?","o":["A. Ammonia neutralizing H+","B. Increasing prostaglandins","C. Increasing urea levels","D. Inhibition of proton pumps","E. Inhibition of urease"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ammonia neutralizing H+","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The curved, flagellated rod is <b>Helicobacter pylori</b>. Its enzyme <b>urease</b> splits urea into <b>ammonia (NH3)</b>, which binds acid (H+) and makes a protective alkaline cloud.<br>B. Prostaglandins protect the stomach lining; the bacterium does not use them.<br>C. It uses up urea; it does not raise urea levels.<br>D. Proton pump inhibitors are drugs used to treat it, not its survival trick.<br>E. It needs urease working, not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/D004-189.jpg","in":"Illustrations of a curved, multi-flagellated gram-negative rod (Helicobacter pylori) beside the options; the organism must be recognized as H. pylori.","tg":["L53 slide 37 (Acid Attack!)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":52,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":189,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":42,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":39,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":39,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"156"},{"id":"G066-124-1","s":"Which of the following is the main mechanism utilized by Helicobacter pylori to enhance its survival in the gastric environment?","o":["A. Attachment to gastric epithelial cells","B. Release of cytotoxins to break down protective mucous","C. Conversion of urea to NH3 using urease","D. Colonization of the gastric mucus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Conversion of urea to NH3 using urease","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Survival in acid depends on <b>urease</b>, which converts urea to <b>ammonia (NH3)</b>. Ammonia neutralizes the acid around Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori), so it can live in the stomach.<br>A. Attachment to epithelial cells happens only after urease has made the area safe.<br>B. Cytotoxins and mucus breakdown cause the damage (ulcer), not the survival.<br>D. Living in the mucus is the result of surviving the acid, not the mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 37 (Acid Attack!)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":124,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":123,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-189-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 61"},{"id":"G052-7-5","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: H. pylori contains the enzyme urease, which permits the bacterium to colonize gastric mucus. What is the permissive role of urease?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Urease converts urea to NH3, which alkalinizes the local environment so the bacterium survives and binds the gastric epithelium (NH4+ also damages the epithelium).","a":null,"why":"Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) makes <b>urease</b>. Urease <b>splits urea</b> into carbon dioxide and <b>ammonia (NH3)</b>. Ammonia grabs acid (H+) to form ammonium (NH4+), so a <b>small alkaline cloud</b> surrounds the bacterium. It survives the acid, stays in the mucus, and binds the epithelium instead of being shed. Ammonium also injures epithelial cells.<br>Common mix-up: urease does not make urea; it breaks urea down.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 37 (Acid Attack!)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-189-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 62"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"H. pylori lowers somatostatin → duodenal ulcer","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"D-cell somatostatin brakes gastrin and parietal cells; losing it raises acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-39.jpg","cap":"Ulcer sites: gastric ulcers in stomach, duodenal ulcer past the pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-131-1","s":"How does Helicobacter pylori infection contribute to the formation of duodenal ulcers?","o":["A. H. pylori directly damages the duodenal mucosa through cytotoxins","B. H. pylori induces excessive pepsin secretion in the duodenum","C. Inhibition of somatostatin secretion leads to increased H+ secretion","D. H. pylori enhances duodenal HCO3- secretion, causing alkalosis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inhibition of somatostatin secretion leads to increased H+ secretion","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) in the antrum <b>suppresses somatostatin</b> from D cells. Somatostatin normally brakes gastrin from G cells, so gastrin rises and parietal cells <b>secrete more acid</b>. The extra acid reaches the duodenum and erodes it.<br>A. The lecture notes it can injure duodenal cells, but the textbook duodenal-ulcer mechanism is the extra acid.<br>B. Pepsin comes from gastric chief cells, not the duodenum.<br>D. Backwards: H. pylori <b>lowers</b> duodenal bicarbonate secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":131,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":130,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"157"},{"id":"D009-23-1","s":"Duodenal Ulcer: More common than Gastric ulcers. H+ secretion higher than normal. Can also be caused by H. Pylori indirectly, How?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"H. pylori inhibits somatostatin (the H+ secretion inhibitor), so acid secretion rises; it can also spread to the duodenum and inhibit HCO3- secretion","a":null,"why":"Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) acts <b>indirectly</b>, in two ways. First, in the antrum it <b>inhibits somatostatin</b> from D cells. Somatostatin normally brakes gastrin from G cells, so gastrin and parietal-cell <b>acid rise</b>, and a bigger acid load reaches the duodenum. Second, it spreads to the duodenum and <b>cuts duodenal bicarbonate</b> secretion, so the acid is not neutralized.<br>Common mix-up: net acid is <b>high</b> in duodenal ulcer but <b>low</b> in gastric ulcer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 39 (Duodenal vs Gastric ulcers)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 3","sl":23,"f":"D009"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-131-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 63"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"H. pylori duodenal ulcer: less somatostatin, more gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-35.jpg","cap":"H. pylori damages both gastric and duodenal epithelial cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"D cell somatostatin inhibits gastrin, ECL cells and parietal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"G052-7-4","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: H. pylori colonizes the gastric mucus. How does this lead to duodenal ulcer?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gastric (antral) infection inhibits somatostatin from D cells → more gastrin → more H+ delivered to the duodenum; spread to the duodenum inhibits duodenal HCO3- secretion → inadequate neutralization → duodenal ulcer.","a":null,"why":"<b>Antral H. pylori lowers somatostatin</b> from D cells, so <b>gastrin and acid rise</b> and more acid reaches the duodenum. The infection also spreads to the duodenum and <b>cuts duodenal bicarbonate</b>. Too much acid and too little bicarbonate let acid and pepsin erode the duodenal lining.<br>Common mix-up: in gastric ulcers H. pylori damages the mucus and cells directly; duodenal ulcers come from the acid load.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 64"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"C13-urea breath test detects urease","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-38.jpg","cap":"Breath test: drink C13-urea; urease releases labelled CO2, exhaled","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"Urease reaction: urea plus water gives CO2 and NH3","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-126-1","s":"Which diagnostic test do you use to check for H.pylori?","o":["A. Barium swallow","B. Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy","C. Helicobacter pylori antibody test","D. 13C-urea breath test"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"13C-urea breath test","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The <b>carbon-13 (C13) urea breath test</b> detects the <b>urease</b> of live Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori): urease splits labelled urea into labelled carbon dioxide, which is exhaled. It is non-invasive.<br>A. Barium swallow shows outlines (ulcers), not bacteria.<br>B. Endoscopy alone shows ulcers; it detects H. pylori only if a biopsy is taken.<br>C. Antibody (serology) tests stay positive after cure, so they cannot show active infection.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 38 (C13-Urea Breath Test)","L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":126,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":125,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"158"},{"id":"G066-127-2","s":"13C-urea breath test: HOW DOES THIS TEST WORK?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pt drinks solutions containing 13-C urea → converted to 13- CO2 and NH3 in the stomach → 13- CO2 is absorbed into blood then expired by the lungs","a":null,"why":"The patient drinks urea labelled with <b>carbon-13</b> (C13). Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) <b>urease</b> in the stomach splits it into ammonia and <b>labelled carbon dioxide (CO2)</b>. The labelled CO2 enters the blood and is <b>breathed out</b>, where the test measures it.<br>Common mix-up: the labelled CO2 comes only from bacterial urease. No H. pylori means no urease and no labelled CO2.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 38 (C13-Urea Breath Test)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":127,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":126,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-126-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 65"},{"id":"G052-7-6","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: What is the 13C-urea breath test, and why is it positive in H. pylori infection?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The patient drinks 13C-urea; H. pylori urease splits it into 13CO2 (exhaled and measured) and NH3, so the test is positive only if urease-containing bacteria are present.","a":null,"why":"The patient drinks urea labelled with <b>carbon-13</b> (C13). If Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) is present, its <b>urease</b> splits the urea into ammonia and <b>labelled carbon dioxide (CO2)</b>. The labelled CO2 is absorbed into the blood and <b>exhaled</b>, where it is measured.<br>Common mix-up: human cells have no urease. So no H. pylori means no urease and no labelled CO2 in the breath; a positive test means live bacteria.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 38 (C13-Urea Breath Test)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-126-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 66"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"H. pylori: PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-35.jpg","cap":"Treat H. pylori with PPI, clarithromycin and amoxicillin; tests listed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-148-1","s":"You suspect the patient has a gastric ulcer. To confirm your diagnosis, you perform a Urea C13 breath test and it comes back positive. How would you treat them?","o":["A. Cimetidine, Azithromycin, Amoxicillin","B. Omeprazole, Clindamycin, Ampicillin","C. Ranitidine, Clarithromycin, Amoxicillin","D. Cimetidine, Erythromycin, Ampicillin","E. Omeprazole, Clarithromycin, Amoxicillin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Omeprazole, Clarithromycin, Amoxicillin","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) is treated with a <b>proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</b> plus <b>clarithromycin</b> and <b>amoxicillin</b>. The PPI (omeprazole) lowers acid so the ulcer heals; the two antibiotics kill the bacterium.<br>A. Cimetidine is a histamine type 2 (H2) blocker, not a PPI; azithromycin is not in the regimen.<br>B. Omeprazole is right, but clindamycin and ampicillin are not.<br>C. Ranitidine is an H2 blocker, not a PPI.<br>D. Wrong acid drug and wrong antibiotics.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 38 (C13-Urea Breath Test)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":148,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"159"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":41,"label":"PPI blocks acid pump, ulcer heals","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-16.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: proton pump inhibitor blocks the H+/K+ ATPase acid pump","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G043-35.jpg","cap":"Helicobacter pylori: treat with PPI plus clarithromycin and amoxicillin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"G052-7-7","s":"CASE 46 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Helicobacter pylori Infection. Dolly Spector is a 59-year-old real estate agent who had frequent bouts of “acid indigestion.” She described burning and a dull ache in her stomach, which improved when she ate food or took over-the-counter antacid medication. A client mentioned that she had an ulcer that started with the same symptoms, which prompted Dolly to see her physician. On physical examination, Dolly had epigastric tenderness. A serologic test and a 13C-urea breath test were both positive, consistent with H. pylori infection. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of a duodenal ulcer. Dolly was treated with an antibiotic (to eradicate H. pylori) and omeprazole. QUESTION: What is the basis for Dolly’s treatment with omeprazole?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Omeprazole inhibits the H+-K+ ATPase, reducing H+ delivered to the duodenum and its damaging effect while the infection is eradicated.","a":null,"why":"<b>Omeprazole</b> is a <b>proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</b>. It blocks the <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b> (the acid pump) of parietal cells, the last step of acid secretion. Less acid reaches the duodenum, so the duodenal ulcer heals while the antibiotic kills Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori).<br>Common mix-up: the PPI does not kill the bacterium. Clarithromycin and amoxicillin do that; the PPI only removes the acid that keeps damaging the ulcer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 13 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 67"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"Chronic antral gastritis: think H. pylori","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-35.jpg","cap":"H. pylori: destroys mucus, damages epithelium; biopsy, stool antigen, urea breath test","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"H. pylori burrows into mucus, neutralizes acid with urease, damages mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-21-1","s":"A 45-year-old man has had vague abdominal pain and nausea for the past 3 years. This pain is unrelieved by antacid medications. On physical examination there are no abnormal findings. Upper GI endoscopy reveals antral mucosal erythema, but no ulcerations or masses. Biopsies are taken, and microscopically there is a chronic non-specific gastritis. Which of the following conditions is most likely to be present in this man?","o":["a. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","b. Pernicious anemia","c. Helicobacter pylori infection","d. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Helicobacter pylori infection","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Helicobacter pylori.</b> It colonizes the <b>antrum</b> and causes <b>chronic gastritis</b> with redness, even without an ulcer. Diagnose by biopsy, stool antigen or carbon-13 urea breath test.<br>A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: gastrinoma with high acid and large, multiple ulcers.<br>B. Pernicious anemia: autoimmune gastritis of the body and fundus with vitamin B12 deficiency, not the antrum.<br>D. H. pylori fits, so 'none' is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":58,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":23,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":21,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":167,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":57,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":20,"f":"D002"}],"tier":1,"n":"160"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"H. pylori causes gastritis and ulcers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"H. pylori colonizes mucus, survives by urease, then damages the mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G043-33.jpg","cap":"Ulcer causes: H. pylori, NSAIDs; pepsin alone does not ulcerate","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D003-55-1","s":"16. A 45-year-old man has had vague abdominal pain and nausea for the past 3 years. This pain is unrelieved by antacid medications. On physical examination there are no abnormal findings. Upper GI endoscopy reveals antral mucosal erythema, but no ulcerations or masses. Biopsies are taken, and microscopically there is a chronic non-specific gastritis. Which of the following conditions is most likely to be present in this man?","o":["A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","B. Pernicious anemia","C. Helicobacter pylori infection","D. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Helicobacter pylori infection","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Years of vague pain, <b>antral redness</b> and <b>chronic non-specific gastritis</b> on biopsy, with no ulcer or mass, is the classic picture of <b>Helicobacter pylori</b> infection. The bacterium lives in the antrum and inflames the mucosa.<br>A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (a gastrin-secreting tumor) gives large, multiple ulcers and very high acid.<br>B. Pernicious anemia is autoimmune gastritis of the body and fundus, not the antrum, with vitamin B12 deficiency.<br>D. C fits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":55,"f":"D003"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 68"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"H. pylori lowers somatostatin → duodenal ulcer","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"D-cell somatostatin brakes gastrin and parietal cells; losing it raises acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-39.jpg","cap":"Ulcer sites: gastric ulcers in stomach, duodenal ulcer past the pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-114-1","s":"Q3. A patient complains of epigastric pain that improves with meals but worsens at night on an empty stomach. Laboratory results came back positive for H. pylori and the physician concludes that the patient most likely has an ulcer. What could be contributing to her pain?","o":["A. She most likely has a gastric ulcer where there is both a net increase in H+ and gastrin secretion.","B. The bacteria’s enzyme allows for urea formation which creates an alkaline environment that allows it to survive in the gut and cause damage.","C. The bacteria prevents somatostatin secretion from D cells, causing increased delivery of H+ to the duodenum where it causes duodenal ulcers.","D. All of the above."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The bacteria prevents somatostatin secretion from D cells, causing increased delivery of H+ to the duodenum where it causes duodenal ulcers.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Pain that <b>improves with meals</b> and worsens on an empty stomach points to a <b>duodenal ulcer</b>. Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) blocks somatostatin from D cells, so gastrin from G cells and acid rise, and the extra acid erodes the duodenum.<br>A. Gastric ulcers have <b>low</b> net acid (it leaks into the damaged lining), with high gastrin.<br>B. Urease <b>splits</b> urea into ammonia; it does not make urea.<br>D. A and B are false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 39 (Duodenal vs Gastric ulcers)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":114,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"161"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"H. pylori gastric ulcer: low acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"H. pylori breaks the mucus, then inflames and kills mucosal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/D023-55.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: gastric ulcer has low acid but high gastrin","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-109-1","s":"How does Helicobacter pylori infection cause hypochlorhydria in gastric ulcer disease?","o":["A. By stimulating D cell somatostatin secretion","B. By inhibiting gastrin secretion","C. By stimulating prostaglandin secretion","D. By directly inhibiting parietal cell function","E. By stimulating histamine secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"By stimulating D cell somatostatin secretion","a":"A","why":"<b>D.</b> In gastric ulcer, Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) <b>inflames and damages the acid-making lining</b>, so parietal cells secrete less acid, and some acid <b>leaks back</b> into the damaged mucosa. Net acid falls.<br>A. Backwards: H. pylori <b>lowers</b> somatostatin from D cells, which raises gastrin and acid (the duodenal-ulcer path).<br>B. Gastrin <b>rises</b> in gastric ulcer, because low acid removes its brake.<br>C. Prostaglandins protect the lining; H. pylori does not act by raising them.<br>E. More histamine would raise acid.","ex":"","fl":["H. pylori DECREASES D-cell somatostatin (raising gastrin – the duodenal-ulcer/hyperchlorhydria pathway), so A is backwards. Low acid in gastric-ulcer/corpus disease comes from mucosal and parietal-cell damage (H. pylori 'destroys mucous barrier, damages gastric epithelial cells', G043:35) with H+ back-leak; of the options, D is the defensible answer."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":109,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 69"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"High gastrin enlarges parietal cell mass","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Somatostatin from D cells normally brakes gastrin; H. pylori removes it","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"High gastrin causes gastrin-induced hyperplasia of parietal and ECL cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/D023-55.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: duodenal ulcer has increased parietal cell mass from gastrin","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-101-1","s":"6. Which cell becomes hypertrophic in an H.pylori infection? (H.pylori colonizes the gastric mucosa and alkalinizes the environment to survive)","o":["A. ECL cells","B. Chief cells","C. G cells","D. Parietal cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"G cells","a":"C","why":"<b>D.</b> Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) in the antrum lowers somatostatin from D cells, so G cells release <b>more gastrin</b>. Gastrin is <b>trophic</b> (growth-promoting), so the <b>parietal cell mass increases</b> (Costanzo, duodenal ulcer) and acid output rises.<br>A. Enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells also grow with gastrin, mainly with very high, long-lasting gastrin (proton pump inhibitors, atrophic gastritis).<br>B. Chief cells make pepsinogen; gastrin does not enlarge them.<br>C. G cells release more gastrin, but they are the source, not the cells that grow.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed G cells, but H. pylori raises gastrin by lowering somatostatin, and gastrin's growth effect enlarges the parietal cell mass."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":101,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":101,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 70"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":42,"label":"Acid and gastrin: ulcers versus ZES","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-47.jpg","cap":"High acid AND high gastrin = Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/D023-55.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.4: acid and gastrin in gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, ZES","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-90-1","s":"Which Gastric disorder is characterized by a decrease in H+ secretion, and an increase in Gastrin levels","o":["A. Gastric Ulcer","B. Duodenal Ulcer","C. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","D. None of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric Ulcer","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> In <b>gastric ulcer</b>, the damaged mucosal barrier lets acid (H+) <b>leak back</b> into the lining, so net acid secretion is <b>low</b>. Low acid removes the brake on gastrin, so <b>gastrin rises</b>. Helicobacter pylori is a major cause.<br>B. Duodenal ulcer: acid is high and gastrin is high.<br>C. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (a gastrin-secreting tumor): acid and gastrin are both very high.<br>D. A fits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 39 (Duodenal vs Gastric ulcers)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":90,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":90,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":258,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"162"},{"id":"D036-114-1","s":"Which two gastric disorders are characterized by an increase in H+ concentration and gastrin secretion?","o":["A. Gastric Ulcer","B. Duodenal Ulcer","C. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","D. Gastric and peptic ulcers","E. None of the above"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["B","C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal Ulcer | Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","a":"B,C","why":"<b>B and C.</b> <b>Duodenal ulcer</b> has high acid (H+) and high gastrin, especially after meals. <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> (a gastrin-secreting tumor) has very high acid and very high gastrin, because the tumor ignores acid feedback.<br>A. Gastric ulcer has <b>low</b> net acid (it leaks into the damaged lining), with high gastrin.<br>D. 'Peptic' covers both gastric and duodenal ulcers, and gastric ulcer has low acid.<br>E. B and C fit.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 47 (ZES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":114,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 71"},{"id":"D067-67-1","s":"Which of the following correctly compares gastric and duodenal ulcers?","o":["A. Gastric ulcers have increased acid secretion and low gastrin.","B. Duodenal ulcers are associated with increased gastrin levels and high acid output.","C. Gastric ulcers occur with hypersecretion of mucus.","D. Duodenal ulcers result from decreased parietal cell mass.","E. Gastric ulcers are caused by decreased prostaglandin synthesis."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal ulcers are associated with increased gastrin levels and high acid output.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenal ulcers go with high gastrin and high acid output.</b> In duodenal ulcer, acid (H+) secretion is high and gastrin rises more than normal after meals. In gastric ulcer, net acid is low and gastrin is high.<br>E. Tempting: NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) do cause gastric ulcers by cutting prostaglandins. But that is one cause, not the rule (Helicobacter pylori is the main one), and it compares nothing. B is the best answer.<br>A. Reversed: gastric ulcers have low acid and high gastrin.<br>C. The mucus barrier is lost, not increased.<br>D. Parietal cell mass is increased.","ex":"","fl":["B is the intended comparison and is correct (duodenal ulcer: high acid, gastrin response raised; gastric ulcer: low acid, high gastrin, damaged mucous barrier). Option E is true for one cause: NSAIDs stop PGE2 and cause gastric ulcers (G043:41, G043:33), but it is not the comparison asked for and H. pylori is the other main cause; pick B."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 39 (Duodenal vs Gastric ulcers)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)","L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":67,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 72"},{"id":"G066-137-1","s":"Describe the H+ secretion and Gastrin Levels in the following conditions: Gastric ulcer; Duodenal ulcer; Zollinger- Ellison Syndrome","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gastric ulcer: H+ secretion ↓, gastrin ↑; Duodenal ulcer: H+ secretion ↑, gastrin ↑; Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome: H+ secretion ↑↑, gastrin ↑↑","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastric ulcer:</b> acid (H+) <b>low</b>, because acid leaks back into the damaged lining; gastrin <b>high</b>, because low acid removes its brake.<br><b>Duodenal ulcer:</b> acid <b>high</b>; gastrin <b>high</b>, mainly after meals; parietal cell mass is increased.<br><b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> (a gastrin-secreting tumor): acid <b>very high</b> and gastrin <b>very high</b>, because the tumor ignores acid feedback.<br>Common mix-up: all three have high gastrin; the acid level is what separates them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 39 (Duodenal vs Gastric ulcers)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 23 (Inhibition of HCl)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":137,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":136,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 73"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"ZES = gastrin-secreting tumor (gastrinoma)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison tumor in pancreas causing duodenal ulcers from hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES is a gastrinoma: high gastrin despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-60-1","s":"Zollinger Ellison syndrome is a tumor of which of the following hormones?","o":["A. Secretin","B. CCK","C. Gastrin","D. GIP"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES) is a <b>gastrinoma</b>: a tumor, usually in the pancreas or duodenum, that secretes <b>gastrin</b> (normally made by G cells of the antrum).<br>A. Secretin, from duodenal S cells, drives pancreatic bicarbonate; it is not the ZES hormone.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK), from I cells, drives pancreatic enzymes and gallbladder contraction.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), from K cells, stimulates insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":60,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":228,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion summary (L49-51)","sl":7,"f":"G048"}],"tier":1,"n":"163"},{"id":"D015-139-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING IS CAUSED BY A GASTRIN SECRETING TUMOR?","o":["A. GASTRIC ULCER","B. ZOLLINGER-ELLISON SYNDROME","C. CYSTIC FIBROSIS","D. CELIAC DISEASE"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ZOLLINGER-ELLISON SYNDROME","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A <b>gastrin-secreting tumor</b> (gastrinoma) causes <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b>: very high gastrin and acid, with large, multiple, distal ulcers and steatorrhea.<br>A. Gastric ulcer comes from a damaged mucous barrier (Helicobacter pylori, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), with low acid.<br>C. Cystic fibrosis is a defect in the CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) chloride channel.<br>D. Celiac disease is gluten-triggered villous damage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":23,"f":"D067"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":139,"f":"D015"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":84,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-60-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 74"},{"id":"G066-133-1","s":"A patient presents with recurrent peptic ulcers, diarrhea, and steatorrhea. Further investigations reveal a pancreatic islet cell adenoma secreting high levels of gastrin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Crohn's disease","B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","C. Celiac disease","D. Irritable bowel syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A pancreatic islet tumor that secretes <b>gastrin</b> (gastrinoma) is <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b>. Huge gastrin drives huge acid: recurrent ulcers, and acid in the duodenum inactivates lipase, causing diarrhea and steatorrhea (fatty stool).<br>A. Crohn disease is patchy, full-thickness bowel inflammation.<br>C. Celiac disease is gluten-triggered villous damage.<br>D. Irritable bowel syndrome has no tumor and no ulcers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":133,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":132,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-60-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 75"},{"id":"G063-6-26","s":"A 42-year-old male is referred to a gastroenterologist for evaluation of refractory peptic ulcer disease. Subsequent endoscopic and laboratory data are suggestive of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. The increased basal acid output and maximal acid output of the patient is best explained by an increase in the plasma concentration of which of the following?","o":["a. Gastrin-releasing peptide","b. Gastrin","c. Somatostatin","d. Secretin","e. Histamine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a <b>gastrin-secreting tumor</b>. High plasma <b>gastrin</b> drives parietal-cell acid and enlarges the parietal cell mass, so both basal and maximal acid output rise.<br>A. Gastrin-releasing peptide is a vagal transmitter acting on G cells.<br>C. Somatostatin inhibits acid.<br>D. Secretin inhibits gastrin and acid, and raises bicarbonate.<br>E. Histamine raises acid locally, but it is not the circulating hormone this tumor makes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":6,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"164"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrinoma","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison tumor in the pancreas with duodenal ulcers from hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: gastrinoma, acid feedback fails, high gastrin despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: gastrinoma, gastric hypersecretion, refractory peptic ulcers","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"G065-60-1","s":"Zollinger Ellison syndrome is a tumor of which of the following hormones?","o":["A. Secretin","B. CCK","C. Gastrin","D. GIP"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastrin.</b> Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES) is a <b>gastrinoma</b>, usually in the pancreas or duodenum. It causes high gastrin despite high acid, with large, multiple, distal ulcers.<br>A. Secretin: not the tumor hormone; intravenous secretin is used to diagnose gastrinoma.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK): not the tumor hormone in ZES.<br>D. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): not the tumor hormone in ZES.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 38 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":60,"f":"G065"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 76"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"Acid hypersecretion means high plasma gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison: gastrinoma, high gastrin despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Gastrinoma in pancreas causing duodenal ulcers from hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-3-14","s":"A 53-year-old and over amibitious businessman presents to a medical practitioner for intermittent midepigastric pain that is relieved by antacids or eating. Gastric analysis reveals that basal and maximal acid output exceed normal values. The gastric acid hypersecretion can be explained by an increase in the plasma concentration of which of the following?","o":["a. Somatostatin","b. Histamine","c. Gastrin","d. Secretin","e. Enterogastrone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Gastrin.</b> High basal and maximal acid output points to excess <b>gastrin</b>, which stimulates parietal cells and makes them multiply. A gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) shows high gastrin despite high acid.<br>A. Somatostatin: lowers acid.<br>B. Histamine: acts locally (paracrine); its plasma level does not drive acid.<br>D. Secretin: inhibits gastrin and acid.<br>E. Enterogastrone: intestinal hormones that inhibit the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":3,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"165"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"ZES ulcer: excess acid overwhelms bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Gastrinoma in pancreas; duodenal ulcers due to hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: acid overwhelms the pancreas's bicarbonate production","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells, which secrete the acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-64-1","s":"A 41 year-old man comes to the office due to abdominal pain and diarrhea that began two weeks ago. He is worried because his father had a pancreatic tumor. Endoscopy shows multiple duodenal ulcers. Serum gastrin levels are elevated. Stimulation of which of the following cells led to this man’s ulcers?","o":["A. Brunners Glands","B. Chief Cells","C. Enterochromaffin Cells","D. Mucous Neck Cells","E. Parietal Cells"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal Cells","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> High gastrin with multiple duodenal ulcers means a <b>gastrinoma</b>. Gastrin stimulates <b>parietal cells</b>, which secrete the hydrochloric acid that erodes the duodenum.<br>A. Brunner glands are duodenal submucosal glands that make protective <b>alkaline mucus</b>.<br>B. Chief cells make pepsinogen.<br>C. Enterochromaffin cells make serotonin; gastrin also stimulates enterochromaffin-like (histamine) cells, but the acid comes from parietal cells.<br>D. Mucous neck cells make protective mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 77"},{"id":"G066-129-1","s":"What is the primary mechanism by which an ulcer forms in the duodenum?","o":["A. Overproduction of pepsin by the duodenal mucosa, leading to erosion","B. Duodenal release of excess bicarbonate, causing alkalosis","C. Increased H+ secretion overwhelming HCO3- buffering capacity","D. Pancreatic juice deficiency in bicarbonate secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased H+ secretion overwhelming HCO3- buffering capacity","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A duodenal ulcer forms when the stomach delivers <b>more acid (H+)</b> than duodenal and pancreatic <b>bicarbonate can neutralize</b>. The leftover acid, with pepsin, erodes the lining. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome the acid load is extreme.<br>A. Pepsin comes from gastric chief cells, not the duodenum, and does not cause ulcers by itself.<br>B. Bicarbonate protects; it does not cause ulcers.<br>D. The problem is too much acid, not a primary lack of pancreatic bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 33 (“Peptic” Ulcer Formation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":129,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":128,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 78"},{"id":"D010-136-1","s":"Busy day at the office as your next patient presents with a recent diagnosis of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. What is the cause of this patient’s condition?","o":["A. Pancreas fails to secrete adequate amounts of HCO3- and fails to neutralize H+ from stomach","B. Pancreatic insufficiency","C. Gastric parietal cells secrete excess amounts of H+"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric parietal cells secrete excess amounts of H+","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, a tumor makes huge amounts of <b>gastrin</b>, which drives <b>parietal cells to secrete excess acid (H+)</b>. The acid overwhelms normal pancreatic bicarbonate and causes ulcers.<br>A. Pancreatic bicarbonate output is normal; there is simply too much acid.<br>B. Pancreatic insufficiency (cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis) is a different disease, with low enzymes and bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":136,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":136,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":116,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 79"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"ZES steatorrhea: acid inactivates pancreatic lipase","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"ZES: diarrhea (steatorrhea) because lipase is inactivated by acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"Without bicarbonate, lipase is inactivated at acid pH below five","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"Gastrinoma: excess acid inactivates pancreatic lipase, causing steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-32-1","s":"Which of the following is the pathophysiology for the presentation of steatorrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome?","o":["A. Failure of CCK secretion reduces fat absorption in the small intestine","B. The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","C. The alkaline environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","D. The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic protease reducing fat digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, excess gastrin drives so much acid that the duodenum turns <b>acidic</b>. <b>Pancreatic lipase is inactivated</b> at low pH (below about five), so fat is not digested: steatorrhea.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) secretion is not the problem.<br>C. Backwards: the duodenum becomes acidic, not alkaline.<br>D. Proteases digest protein, not fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":32,"f":"D024"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":64,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":232,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"166"},{"id":"D066-18-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes Zollinger Ellison’s?","o":["A. It is an insulinoma","B. It increases the pH of the intestinal lumen","C. It inactivates lipase which causes steatorrhea","D. Omeprazole is not a treatment option"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It inactivates lipase which causes steatorrhea","a":"C","why":"<b>C. It inactivates lipase, which causes steatorrhea.</b> ZES (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) is a gastrin-secreting tumor. The extra gastrin drives so much acid that the duodenum stays acidic. <b>Acid inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested and leaves in the stool.<br>A. ZES is a gastrinoma; an insulinoma secretes insulin.<br>B. The acid lowers intestinal pH; it does not raise it.<br>D. Omeprazole, a PPI (proton pump inhibitor), is the standard drug: it blocks the acid pump whatever the gastrin level.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 47 (ZES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":18,"f":"D066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 80"},{"id":"D010-74-1","s":"A man comes into the office with abdominal pain, heartburn, and diarrhea that seems to contain a lot of fat per the patient. Testing reveals a tumor that is over-producing gastrin along with many ulcers in the duodenum. Why does the patient have fatty diarrhea with regards to this suspected syndrome?","o":["A. Inhibition of secretin","B. Inhibition of CCK","C. Inactivation of lipase","D. Inhibition of parietal cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inactivation of lipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A gastrin-making tumor (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) floods the duodenum with acid. <b>Acid inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested and the stool is fatty.<br>A. Secretin is not inhibited; acid actually stimulates its release.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK) release is not the defect.<br>D. Parietal cells are overstimulated, not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":74,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":49,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 81"},{"id":"D010-103-1","s":"7. You notice that you have been experiencing foul-smelling stool that is pale and floats. You go to your PCP and you are diagnosed with a gastrin releasing tumor in your pancreas. What do you think is happening?","o":["A. You’ve been eating too much fatty foods and need to cut down","B. You have Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and your pancreatic lipases are being inactivated by the increase Hcl secretion","C. Your gastric mucosa is being colonized by a bacteria that is disrupting your fat absorption","D. You have an overactivation of your vagus nerve which is stimulating your G cells to release gastrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"You have Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and your pancreatic lipases are being inactivated by the increase Hcl secretion","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A gastrin-releasing tumor means <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b>. The extra hydrochloric acid enters the duodenum and <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat passes undigested: pale, floating, foul stool (steatorrhea).<br>A. Diet does not explain a gastrin tumor.<br>C. Helicobacter pylori does not block fat absorption.<br>D. The gastrin comes from the tumor, not from vagal stimulation of G cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":103,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":103,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 82"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":43,"label":"ZES diarrhea: acid inactivates lipase","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"ZES clues: big distal ulcers, steatorrhea because lipase is inactivated by acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"Without bicarbonate, lipase is inactivated at acid pH below 5","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: acid inactivates pancreatic lipase, causing steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"G065-64-1","s":"Which of the following is the pathophysiology for the presentation of steatorrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome?","o":["A. Failure of CCK secretion reduces fat absorption in the small intestine","B. The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","C. The alkaline environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","D. The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic protease reducing fat digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The acidic environment due to excess Gastrin denatures pancreatic lipase reducing fat digestion","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Excess gastrin causes excess acid. The <b>acidic duodenum inactivates (denatures) pancreatic lipase</b>, which works best at pH six to eight, so fat is not digested: steatorrhea.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) secretion does not fail in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.<br>C. Gastrin makes the duodenum acidic, not alkaline.<br>D. Proteases digest protein, not fat; the enzyme that matters is lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 40 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":64,"f":"G065"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-40-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 83"},{"id":"G064-74-1","s":"A man comes into the office with abdominal pain, heartburn, and diarrhea that seems to contain a lot of fat per the patient. Testing reveals a tumor that is over-producing gastrin along with many ulcers in the duodenum. Why does the patient have fatty diarrhea with regards to this suspected syndrome?","o":["A. Inhibition of secretin","B. Inhibition of CCK","C. Inactivation of lipase","D. Inhibition of parietal cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inactivation of lipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Inactivation of lipase.</b> The gastrinoma drives constant acid secretion; <b>acid in the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested and fatty diarrhea follows.<br>A. Secretin is not inhibited; acid actually releases more of it.<br>B. Cholecystokinin (CCK) is not inhibited.<br>D. Parietal cells are overstimulated, not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 40 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":74,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-40-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 84"},{"id":"D019-40-1","s":"You have a patient with large ulcers of the distal duodenum. There is diarrhea as well. When treated with omeprazole (proton pump inhibitor) the symptoms are controlled and the ulcers resolve. When treatment stops, the diarrhea recurs. What is the etiology of the recurrence of diarrhea?","o":["a. Steatorrhea/Fat malabsorption","b. Acid increases stomach motility","c. Increased motilin","d. Decreased cholecystokinin (CCK)","e. Gastrin directly increases jejunal/ileal motility"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea/Fat malabsorption","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Steatorrhea (fat malabsorption).</b> These are Zollinger-Ellison syndrome ulcers. Off omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor, the gastrinoma floods the duodenum with acid. <b>Acid inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested and fatty diarrhea returns.<br>B. Acid does not increase stomach motility.<br>C. Motilin is not raised in this syndrome.<br>D. Cholecystokinin (CCK) is not decreased in this syndrome.<br>E. Gastrin does not drive jejunal or ileal motility enough to cause this diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 40 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":40,"f":"D019"}],"tier":1,"n":"167"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"Suspect ZES: large, multiple, distal ulcers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Suspect ZES: ulcers over one centimeter, multiple, near ligament of Treitz","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Gastrinoma in pancreas with duodenal ulcers from hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-123-1","s":"A 34 year old male comes in complaining of multiple episodes of foul smelling, floating diarrhea. He states he has a burning pain in his upper abdomen. You perform an EGD and find multiple ulcers greater than 1 cm near the ligament of treitz. What is your most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Helicobacter pylori","B. Chronic NSAID use","C. GERD","D. Zollinger Ellison Syndrome","E. Chronic PPI use"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Zollinger Ellison Syndrome","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Suspect <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> when ulcers are <b>larger than one centimeter, multiple</b>, and <b>distal, near the ligament of Treitz</b>, especially with fatty, floating diarrhea (acid inactivates lipase).<br>A. Helicobacter pylori ulcers are usually single, in the stomach or first part of the duodenum.<br>B. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug ulcers are mostly gastric, without steatorrhea.<br>C. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) affects the esophagus.<br>E. Chronic proton pump inhibitor use heals ulcers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":123,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 85"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"ZES vignette → excess circulating gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Suspect ZES: large multiple distal ulcers, steatorrhea from acid-inactivated lipase","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: pancreatic or duodenal gastrinoma, gastric hypersecretion, peptic ulcers","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-43-1","s":"6. A 50-year old man comes to see you complaining of foul-smelling diarrhea and says that he experiences severe epigastric pain 3-4 hours after eating. On endoscopic exam you notice hyperplasia of the gastric mucosa. What is the cause?","o":["A. Celiac Disease","B. Gastrin-secreting tumor","C. Achalasia","D. Impaired gastrocolic reflex","E. Stomach cancer"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin-secreting tumor","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Pain hours after eating (duodenal ulcer), <b>foul-smelling fatty diarrhea</b> and <b>gastric mucosal hyperplasia</b> point to a <b>gastrin-secreting tumor</b> (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Gastrin grows the lining and drives acid, which inactivates lipase.<br>A. Celiac disease causes malabsorption but not ulcers or a thick gastric lining.<br>C. Achalasia is failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax.<br>D. The gastrocolic reflex does not cause ulcers.<br>E. Stomach cancer does not cause this pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L51B slide 40 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":43,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 86"},{"id":"D035-80-1","s":"42 year-old female presents to your office with complaints of abdominal pain, nausea, loss of appetite, diarrhea, and belching for the past 8 months. Patient states that the pain is worse at nights and is sometimes relieved with eating food or taking antacids that contain HCO3- . The endoscopy revealed an ulcer in the duodenum. The stool sample was positive for blood and fat. CT showed a 2 cm mass at the head of the pancreas. Which of the following is the cause of the patients symptoms?","o":["A. Decreased secretion of CCK from the pancreas","B. Increased secretion of Secretin","C. Increased secretion of gastrin into the circulation","D. Increased insulin secretion from the B-pancreatic cells of the pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased secretion of gastrin into the circulation","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Duodenal ulcer, <b>fat and blood in the stool</b>, relief with food or antacids, and a <b>pancreatic head mass</b> mean a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome): <b>too much gastrin in the circulation</b>.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) comes from duodenal I cells, not the pancreas, and its loss would not cause ulcers.<br>B. More secretin would raise bicarbonate and lower gastrin, easing ulcers.<br>D. Excess insulin (insulinoma) causes low blood sugar, not ulcers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":80,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 87"},{"id":"F1212-69-1","s":"A 34-year-old Caucasian male comes into your office complaining of dull epigastric pain of several weeks' duration, which is worse after meals. His past medical history is significant for recurrent kidney stones and progressively worsening vision. Family history reveals that his grandfather had the same set of symptoms at a similar age. Physical exam reveals paravertebral ropiness from T6-9 and a tender nodule in the left 5th intercostal space near the sternum. The most likely cause of this patient's epigastric pain is","o":["A. Antibodies directed towards parietal cells","B. Gastrin overstimulation leading to acid release","C. Helicobacter pylori infection","D. Inhibition of prostaglandin E2 synthesis","E. Trauma-induced vasoconstriction leading to poor gastric blood supply"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin overstimulation leading to acid release","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Kidney stones (overactive parathyroids), failing vision (pituitary tumor) and a family history suggest <b>multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1)</b>, which includes <b>gastrinomas</b>. Excess <b>gastrin overstimulates acid</b>, causing ulcer pain.<br>A. Anti-parietal-cell antibodies (pernicious anemia) cause <b>low</b> acid.<br>C. Helicobacter pylori does not explain the stones, vision or family history.<br>D. Blocking prostaglandin E2 is the nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) mechanism.<br>E. Nothing suggests trauma or stress ulcer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":69,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 88"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrinoma","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-45.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison tumor in the pancreas with duodenal ulcers from hyperacidity","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: gastrinoma, acid feedback fails, high gastrin despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: gastrinoma, gastric hypersecretion, refractory peptic ulcers","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-80-1","s":"42 year-old female presents to your office with complaints of abdominal pain, nausea, loss of appetite, diarrhea, and belching for the past 8 months. Patient states that the pain is worse at nights and is sometimes relieved with eating food or taking antacids that contain HCO3- . The endoscopy revealed an ulcer in the duodenum. The stool sample was positive for blood and fat. CT showed a 2 cm mass at the head of the pancreas. Which of the following is the cause of the patients symptoms?","o":["A. Decreased secretion of CCK from the pancreas","B. Increased secretion of Secretin","C. Increased secretion of gastrin into the circulation","D. Increased insulin secretion from the B-pancreatic cells of the pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased secretion of gastrin into the circulation","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> This is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: a <b>gastrin-secreting tumor (gastrinoma)</b> in the pancreas releases gastrin <b>into the circulation</b>. The result is high acid, duodenal ulcers and fatty stools.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) comes from intestinal I cells, not the pancreas, and less CCK would not cause ulcers.<br>B. More secretin would raise bicarbonate and inhibit gastrin, the opposite.<br>D. Extra insulin (insulinoma) causes low blood glucose, not ulcers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L51B slide 38 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":80,"f":"G066"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 89"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"ZES features: ulcers, steatorrhea, thick mucosa","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Suspect ZES: large distal ulcers; steatorrhea because acid inactivates lipase","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"Gastrin-induced hyperplasia: high gastrin grows parietal and ECL cells","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: gastrinoma, gastric hypersecretion, peptic ulcers; treatment listed","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-120-1","s":"Q6. A 50 year old male presents with abdominal pain and fatty stools. He was diagnosed with a gastrin secreting tumor. What would you expect to see in this patient?","o":["A. Inactivated lipase.","B. Hypertrophy of gastric mucosa.","C. Gastrin levels will rise after secretin administration.","D. All of the above."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> All three fit a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Huge acid in the duodenum <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b> (fatty stools). High gastrin makes the <b>gastric mucosa grow</b>. In the <b>secretin stimulation test</b>, secretin paradoxically <b>raises</b> gastrin from the tumor; normal G cells do not respond this way.<br>A, B and C are each true, so 'all of the above' is the best choice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L56 slide 42 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":120,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 90"},{"id":"D035-134-1","s":"Answer the following questions about Zollinger- Ellison Syndrome / What happens to the parietal cells","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Increased Parietal cell mass due to high gastrin induced secretion of H+","a":null,"why":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma), the very high gastrin is <b>trophic</b> (growth-promoting) to the stomach lining, so the <b>parietal cell mass increases</b>. More parietal cells, each maximally stimulated, means <b>very high acid secretion</b>. On endoscopy this shows as thick gastric folds (mucosal hypertrophy).<br>Common mix-up: gastrin makes parietal cells grow; it is G cells that make gastrin, and in this disease the tumor makes it instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":134,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 91"},{"id":"G048-7-2","s":"Gastrin – Pathophys: Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome. Signs and symptoms?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Steatorrhea (secondary to pancreatic lipase inactivation), duodenal ulcers, mucosal hypertrophy","a":null,"why":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES) is a <b>gastrin-secreting tumor</b>. Huge gastrin drives huge acid, so it causes <b>large, multiple, distal duodenal ulcers</b> (pain), <b>diarrhea with steatorrhea</b> (fatty stool, because acid <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>), and <b>gastric mucosal hypertrophy</b> (gastrin makes the lining grow).<br>Common mix-up: the pancreas works normally; its lipase is simply destroyed by the acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion summary (L49-51)","sl":7,"f":"G048"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 92"},{"id":"G066-135-1","s":"Answer the following questions about Zollinger- Ellison Syndrome: What happens to the parietal cells? Why is there steatorrhea? What is the treatment protocol? What receptors are blocked by the medications?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Parietal cells: Increased Parietal cell mass due to high gastrin induced secretion of H+. Steatorrhea: Low duodenal pH inactivates the pancreatic lipases. Treatment: Cimetidine, Omeprazole and Surgical removal of tumor. Receptors blocked: Cimetidine→ H2 receptor; Omeprazole→ H-K ATPase","a":null,"why":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma), high gastrin <b>enlarges the parietal cell mass</b> and drives excess acid. Acid entering the duodenum <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested (steatorrhea). Treatment: <b>remove the tumor</b>; if it has spread, a <b>lifelong proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</b>. Omeprazole blocks the <b>H+/K+ ATPase</b>; cimetidine blocks the <b>histamine type 2 (H2) receptor</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the proton pump is an enzyme, not a receptor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":135,"f":"G066"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 93"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"Secretin test: gastrin rises in gastrinoma","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: gastrinoma ignores normal brakes; gastrin stays high despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"D033-20-1","s":"A 48 year old man presents to ER complaining of severe abdominal pain & diarrhea. Patient has had recurrent peptic ulcer disease and a family hx of duodenal ulcer. Physician suspects gastrinoma & administers IV secretin test. Which of the following supports evidence of gastrinoma after injecting secretin?","o":["A. Delayed gastric emptying","B. Stimulation of pancreatic HCO3- secretion","C. Increase or no change in serum gastrin","D. Release of gastrin from D cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase or no change in serum gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In a gastrinoma, intravenous (IV) secretin makes the tumor <b>release more gastrin</b>, so serum gastrin <b>rises</b>. In healthy people secretin lowers gastrin or leaves it unchanged, so the 'increase' in this option is the diagnostic part.<br>A. Secretin slows gastric emptying in everyone; not diagnostic.<br>B. Secretin raises pancreatic bicarbonate in everyone; not diagnostic.<br>D. D cells make somatostatin, not gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 39 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )","L53 slide 47 (ZES)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":20,"f":"D033"}],"tier":1,"n":"168"},{"id":"D023-29-1","s":"A 48-year-old man presents in the ER complaining of severe abdominal pain and diarrhea. The patient has had recurrent peptic ulcer disease, and has a strong family history of duodenal ulcer. After a careful physical assessment of the patient, the physician suspects gastrinoma and decides to administer an intravenous secretin test. Which of the following supports the existence of gastrinoma after injecting secretin?","o":["A. Delayed gastric emptying","B. Stimulation of pancreatic HCO3 - secretion","C. Unchanged (basal high) serum gastrin","D. Release of gastrin from G cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Unchanged (basal high) serum gastrin","a":"C","why":"<b>D.</b> A gastrinoma is a tumor of gastrin-making G-type cells. Intravenous (IV) secretin <b>stimulates these tumor cells to release gastrin</b>, so serum gastrin <b>rises</b>; this rise is the diagnostic result.<br>A. Secretin slows gastric emptying in everyone; not diagnostic.<br>B. Secretin raises pancreatic bicarbonate in everyone; not diagnostic.<br>C. Unchanged gastrin is a normal response: healthy people show no change or a fall.","ex":"","fl":["C is the only defensible option, but strictly gastrin RISES further after IV secretin in gastrinoma (the slide's own highlighted Costanzo excerpt; G041:39 'Rise in gastrin after IV secretin'); 'unchanged' is the normal response.","In gastrinoma, IV secretin paradoxically RAISES serum gastrin (secretin stimulates gastrinoma cells, whereas it lowers/does not change gastrin from normal G cells). The 2026 lecture keys 'Rise in gastrin after IV secretin' (G041:39) and Costanzo Case 45 (G052) says the same; 'unchanged (basal high) serum gastrin' (keyed c) is the NON-diagnostic result. Option d (release of gastrin — the tumor is a G-cell tumor) is the only choice describing the rise.","Key C is wrong: in gastrinoma, IV secretin causes a paradoxical RISE in serum gastrin (2026 lecture G041:39 'Rise in gastrin after IV secretin'; Costanzo). D is the closest option (the gastrin actually comes from the gastrinoma cells)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 39 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )","L53 slide 47 (ZES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":29,"f":"D023"},{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":5,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D033-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 94"},{"id":"G052-1-5","s":"CASE 45 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Zollinger–Ellison Syndrome. Abe Rosenfeld, who is 47 years old, owns a house painting business with his brothers. The brothers pride themselves on maintaining high standards and satisfying their customers. For several months, Abe had a number of symptoms, including indigestion, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. One day, he remarked to his brothers that his diarrhea looked “oily.” The abdominal pain was relieved temporarily by eating and by taking over-the-counter antacids. Finally, he saw his physician, who referred him to a gastroenterologist. Abe underwent fiberoptic endoscopy, which showed an ulcer in the duodenal bulb. To determine the cause of the ulcer, additional tests were performed, including a serum gastrin level, analysis of gastric contents, a pentagastrin stimulation test, and a secretin stimulation test (Table 5–1). Table 5–1: Abe’s Laboratory Values and Results of Laboratory Tests Serum gastrin level 800 pg/mL (normal, 0–130 pg/mL) Basal gastric H+ secretion 100 mEq/hr (normal, 10 mEq/h) Pentagastrin stimulation test No increase in H+ secretion Secretin stimulation test Serum gastrin increased to 1,100 pg/mL A computed tomography scan showed a 3-cm mass on the head of the pancreas. The mass was thought to be a gastrinoma (gastrin-secreting tumor). While awaiting surgery to remove the mass, Abe was treated with a drug called omeprazole. Abe underwent laparoscopic surgery, during which the tumor was localized and removed. Abe’s ulcer subsequently healed, and his symptoms disappeared. QUESTION: In the secretin stimulation test, Abe’s serum gastrin level increased from his basal level of 800 pg/ mL (already very elevated!) to 1,100 pg/mL. In healthy persons, the secretin stimulation test causes no change, or a decrease, in the serum gastrin level. Propose a mechanism to explain Abe’s response to secretin.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Secretin directly stimulates gastrin release from gastrinoma cells (but not from normal antral G cells, where gastrin falls or is unchanged) — the basis of the secretin stimulation test.","a":null,"why":"<b>Secretin directly stimulates the gastrinoma's tumor cells</b> to release gastrin, so serum gastrin <b>rises</b> (here from eight hundred to eleven hundred picograms per milliliter). In healthy people, secretin lowers gastrin or leaves it unchanged, because it inhibits normal G cells. A rise after intravenous (IV) secretin is the diagnostic test for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.<br>Common mix-up: high gastrin alone also occurs on proton pump inhibitors; the secretin rise separates the two.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 38 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )","L51B slide 39 (Q11. A patient presents with ulcers that recur despite eradication of H. pylori )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"D033-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 95"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"Pentagastrin can't raise already-maximal acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Gastrin drives parietal-cell acid through cholecystokinin-B (CCK-B) receptors","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: gastrinoma keeps gastrin high despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G052-1-4","s":"CASE 45 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Zollinger–Ellison Syndrome. Abe Rosenfeld, who is 47 years old, owns a house painting business with his brothers. The brothers pride themselves on maintaining high standards and satisfying their customers. For several months, Abe had a number of symptoms, including indigestion, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. One day, he remarked to his brothers that his diarrhea looked “oily.” The abdominal pain was relieved temporarily by eating and by taking over-the-counter antacids. Finally, he saw his physician, who referred him to a gastroenterologist. Abe underwent fiberoptic endoscopy, which showed an ulcer in the duodenal bulb. To determine the cause of the ulcer, additional tests were performed, including a serum gastrin level, analysis of gastric contents, a pentagastrin stimulation test, and a secretin stimulation test (Table 5–1). Table 5–1: Abe’s Laboratory Values and Results of Laboratory Tests Serum gastrin level 800 pg/mL (normal, 0–130 pg/mL) Basal gastric H+ secretion 100 mEq/hr (normal, 10 mEq/h) Pentagastrin stimulation test No increase in H+ secretion Secretin stimulation test Serum gastrin increased to 1,100 pg/mL A computed tomography scan showed a 3-cm mass on the head of the pancreas. The mass was thought to be a gastrinoma (gastrin-secreting tumor). While awaiting surgery to remove the mass, Abe was treated with a drug called omeprazole. Abe underwent laparoscopic surgery, during which the tumor was localized and removed. Abe’s ulcer subsequently healed, and his symptoms disappeared. QUESTION: In Abe, pentagastrin, a gastrin analogue, did not stimulate gastric H+ secretion. How is this finding consistent with the diagnosis of Zollinger–Ellison syndrome? How would a healthy person respond to the pentagastrin stimulation test?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"H+ secretion was already maximally stimulated by the tumor's gastrin, so extra gastrin analogue could not raise it; a healthy person roughly triples H+ secretion with pentagastrin.","a":null,"why":"Pentagastrin is a <b>gastrin look-alike</b>. In a healthy person it stimulates parietal cells, and acid secretion rises to about <b>three times</b> basal. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, the tumor's gastrin has already pushed parietal cells to their <b>maximum</b>, so the small extra dose adds <b>nothing</b>.<br>Common mix-up: no response does not mean the parietal cells are broken; they are already working flat out.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 96"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":44,"label":"ZES treatment: surgery, or lifelong PPI","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-47.jpg","cap":"ZES: local tumor removed surgically; metastases need lifelong PPI","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"PPI blocks the pump; acid falls but gastrin rises","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G043-18.jpg","cap":"H2 blockers (cimetidine, famotidine) block about 65% of acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-34-1","s":"You have a person with large, multiple duodenal ulcers that recur after treatment with a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). Off the PPI, the gastric acid output is elevated, and the gastrin level is elevated. Endoscopic ultrasound shows multiple lesions in the pancreas, duodenum and liver. The patient is placed on a PPI. What do you expect to occur?","o":["a. Increase in acid output","b. Resolution of the pancreas and liver lesions","c. Resolution of the ulcers","d. Decrease in gastrin level","e. Increase in secretin level"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Resolution of the ulcers","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> Lesions in pancreas, duodenum and liver mean a <b>metastatic gastrinoma</b> (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). It cannot be removed, so treatment is a <b>lifelong proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</b>. The PPI blocks the acid pump, so the <b>ulcers heal</b>.<br>A. Acid output falls on a PPI.<br>B. A PPI does not shrink the tumor.<br>D. Gastrin stays high; the tumor ignores feedback.<br>E. Less acid reaching the duodenum lowers secretin, not raises it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":34,"f":"D019"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":21,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"169"},{"id":"G048-7-3","s":"Gastrin – Pathophys: Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome. Treatments?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"H2 receptor blocker (cimetidine), PPI (omeprazole), tumor removal, resection","a":null,"why":"Treat Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma) by <b>removing the tumor</b> when it is local. If it has spread (metastases), give a <b>lifelong proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</b> such as omeprazole, which blocks the H+/K+ ATPase. Histamine type 2 (H2) blockers such as cimetidine also lower acid, but less well.<br>Common mix-up: acid drugs heal the ulcers but do not shrink the tumor, and gastrin stays high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion summary (L49-51)","sl":7,"f":"G048"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":134,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D019-34-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 97"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":45,"label":"Food buffers acid, easing ulcer pain","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G052-1-8","s":"CASE 45 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Zollinger–Ellison Syndrome. Abe Rosenfeld, who is 47 years old, owns a house painting business with his brothers. The brothers pride themselves on maintaining high standards and satisfying their customers. For several months, Abe had a number of symptoms, including indigestion, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. One day, he remarked to his brothers that his diarrhea looked “oily.” The abdominal pain was relieved temporarily by eating and by taking over-the-counter antacids. Finally, he saw his physician, who referred him to a gastroenterologist. Abe underwent fiberoptic endoscopy, which showed an ulcer in the duodenal bulb. To determine the cause of the ulcer, additional tests were performed, including a serum gastrin level, analysis of gastric contents, a pentagastrin stimulation test, and a secretin stimulation test (Table 5–1). Table 5–1: Abe’s Laboratory Values and Results of Laboratory Tests Serum gastrin level 800 pg/mL (normal, 0–130 pg/mL) Basal gastric H+ secretion 100 mEq/hr (normal, 10 mEq/h) Pentagastrin stimulation test No increase in H+ secretion Secretin stimulation test Serum gastrin increased to 1,100 pg/mL A computed tomography scan showed a 3-cm mass on the head of the pancreas. The mass was thought to be a gastrinoma (gastrin-secreting tumor). While awaiting surgery to remove the mass, Abe was treated with a drug called omeprazole. Abe underwent laparoscopic surgery, during which the tumor was localized and removed. Abe’s ulcer subsequently healed, and his symptoms disappeared. QUESTION: Abe felt better when he ate. Why?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Food buffers H+, so less free acid reaches the duodenum.","a":null,"why":"Food is a <b>buffer</b>: its proteins bind acid (H+). Eating 'mops up' some of the excess acid made under the tumor's gastrin, so <b>less free acid reaches the duodenal ulcer</b> and the pain eases for a while.<br>Common mix-up: relief with eating points to a <b>duodenal</b> ulcer; pain that worsens with eating points to a <b>gastric</b> ulcer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 98"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":45,"label":"ZES diarrhea: acid inactivates lipase","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"ZES clues: big distal ulcers, steatorrhea because lipase is inactivated by acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"Without bicarbonate, lipase is inactivated at acid pH below 5","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: acid inactivates pancreatic lipase, causing steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D019-44-1","s":"A 35 yr old male triathlete complains about an oily anal leakage and his wife adds that he has especially foul-smelling feces. Upon examination the patient hypertrophy of the gastric mucosa is found. What is the mechanism of the oily anal leakage?","o":["a. Decreased levels of cholecystokinin","b. Increased levels of cholecystokinin","c. Increased levels of somatostatin","d. Decreased pH in the intestinal lumen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased pH in the intestinal lumen","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Decreased pH in the intestinal lumen.</b> Gastric mucosal hypertrophy points to <b>excess gastrin</b>. The extra acid lowers duodenal pH and <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat passes undigested: oily, foul stools.<br>A. Low cholecystokinin (CCK) can impair fat digestion, but it does not explain the thick gastric lining.<br>B. More CCK would improve fat digestion.<br>C. More somatostatin would lower acid and gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 41 (Q12. Which of the following explains the etiology of diarrhea in Zollinger-Ellis)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":44,"f":"D019"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 99"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":45,"label":"ZES: gastrin escapes acid feedback","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: acid feedback fails, so gastrin stays high despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"Normal loop: acid inhibits G cells; acid blockers lower acid, raising gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-20-1","s":"You and your study partner are discussing the relationship between acid secretion and gastrin levels at 2am on a Sunday morning. Your partner is sleep deprived and says several things that are incorrect. However, in a moment of clarity he says one thing that is correct and insightful. Which of the following is the correct statement?","o":["a. H2 blockers are the preferred treatment for metastatic gastrinoma","b. H2 blockers routinely depress gastrin levels","c. Increased acid production and elevated gastrin levels indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","d. PPIs have no effect on gastrin levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased acid production and elevated gastrin levels indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> Normally acid shuts off gastrin. <b>High acid AND high gastrin together</b> means the gastrin source ignores that feedback: <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> (gastrinoma).<br>A. Metastatic gastrinoma gets a lifelong proton pump inhibitor (PPI), not histamine type 2 (H2) blockers.<br>B. H2 blockers lower acid, which <b>raises</b> gastrin.<br>D. PPIs also lower acid, so they <b>raise</b> gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":19,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":20,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":163,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":16,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"170"},{"id":"G052-1-2","s":"CASE 45 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Zollinger–Ellison Syndrome. Abe Rosenfeld, who is 47 years old, owns a house painting business with his brothers. The brothers pride themselves on maintaining high standards and satisfying their customers. For several months, Abe had a number of symptoms, including indigestion, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. One day, he remarked to his brothers that his diarrhea looked “oily.” The abdominal pain was relieved temporarily by eating and by taking over-the-counter antacids. Finally, he saw his physician, who referred him to a gastroenterologist. Abe underwent fiberoptic endoscopy, which showed an ulcer in the duodenal bulb. To determine the cause of the ulcer, additional tests were performed, including a serum gastrin level, analysis of gastric contents, a pentagastrin stimulation test, and a secretin stimulation test (Table 5–1). Table 5–1: Abe’s Laboratory Values and Results of Laboratory Tests Serum gastrin level 800 pg/mL (normal, 0–130 pg/mL) Basal gastric H+ secretion 100 mEq/hr (normal, 10 mEq/h) Pentagastrin stimulation test No increase in H+ secretion Secretin stimulation test Serum gastrin increased to 1,100 pg/mL A computed tomography scan showed a 3-cm mass on the head of the pancreas. The mass was thought to be a gastrinoma (gastrin-secreting tumor). While awaiting surgery to remove the mass, Abe was treated with a drug called omeprazole. Abe underwent laparoscopic surgery, during which the tumor was localized and removed. Abe’s ulcer subsequently healed, and his symptoms disappeared. QUESTION: The gastroenterologist diagnosed Abe with Zollinger–Ellison syndrome, or gastrinoma (a gastrin-secreting tumor). Abe had two important laboratory findings that were consistent with this diagnosis: (i) an elevated serum gastrin level and (ii) an elevated basal level of gastric H+ secretion. How does Zollinger–Ellison syndrome increase gastric H+ secretion?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The gastrinoma secretes gastrin that is not feedback-inhibited by low gastric pH, so gastrin keeps stimulating parietal-cell H+ secretion (and mucosal hypertrophy) even when the stomach is very acidic.","a":null,"why":"Normally, acid (H+) in the antrum <b>shuts off gastrin</b> from G cells (negative feedback). A gastrinoma secretes gastrin that <b>ignores this feedback</b>, so gastrin stays very high even when the stomach is very acidic. Gastrin keeps driving <b>parietal-cell acid secretion</b> and enlarges the <b>parietal cell mass</b>, so Abe's basal acid was about ten times normal.<br>Common mix-up: high gastrin with high acid means a tumor; high gastrin with low acid means the normal feedback is working.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C5 extra 100"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":45,"label":"Acid and gastrin: ulcers versus ZES","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-47.jpg","cap":"High acid AND high gastrin = Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/D023-55.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Table 8.4: acid and gastrin in gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, ZES","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-92-1","s":"Which gastric disorder causes steatorrhea, and is characterized by high amounts of H+ and gastrin secretion.","o":["A. Duodenal ulcer","B. Gastric Ulcer","C. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","D. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> (a gastrin-secreting tumor) gives <b>very high gastrin and very high acid (H+)</b> together. The acid floods the duodenum and <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested: <b>steatorrhea</b>.<br>A. Duodenal ulcer: acid and gastrin are high but not extreme, and there is no steatorrhea.<br>B. Gastric ulcer: acid is <b>low</b> (it leaks into the damaged lining).<br>D. C fits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":92,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":92,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":260,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C5 extra 101"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":46,"label":"Sphincter of Oddi passes bile and pancreatic juice","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-7.jpg","cap":"Common bile duct and pancreatic duct open together into the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-137-1","s":"On ultrasound, a patient is found to have a dilated common bile duct and it is deduced that she has an obstruction of the Sphincter of Oddi. Which of the following will have issues meeting the small intestinal lumen?","o":["A. Amylase","B. Trypsinogen","C. Bile","D. Pepsinogen","E. A,B,C"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"A,B,C","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Amylase, trypsinogen and bile</b>. The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct empty together through the <b>sphincter of Oddi</b>, so blocking it stops <b>both bile and pancreatic juice</b>.<br>A, B, C. Each alone is incomplete; all three pass this sphincter.<br>D. Pepsinogen comes from gastric chief cells and enters through the pylorus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":137,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"171"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":46,"label":"Exocrine pancreas: enzymes plus bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-13.jpg","cap":"Exocrine tissue 85–90% (bicarbonate-rich fluid) versus 2% islet cells","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G044-21.jpg","cap":"Acini make amylase, lipase and inactive proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-134-1","s":"Which of the following is an exocrine function of the pancreas? (More than one can be correct)","o":["A. Regulation of blood sugar","B. Secretion of HCO3- for neutralization","C. Storage of bile","D. Absorption of nutrients","E. Synthesis of digestive enzymes"],"g":["B","E"],"k":["B","E"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of HCO3- for neutralization | Synthesis of digestive enzymes","a":"B,E","why":"<b>B and E</b>. The exocrine pancreas sends juice into the duodenum: <b>acinar cells make digestive enzymes</b> and <b>duct cells secrete bicarbonate (HCO3⁻)</b> to neutralize stomach acid.<br>A. Blood sugar control: endocrine (islet insulin and glucagon released into blood).<br>C. Bile storage: the gallbladder.<br>D. Nutrient absorption: the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 21 (Formation of Secretions)","L54 slide 13 (Diagrams MISleading!)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":134,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 1"},{"id":"D008-95-1","s":"Which of the following is an exocrine function of the pancreas?","o":["A. Insulin production and secretion","B. Secreting H+ for digestion","C. Secreting HCO3- for neutralization","D. Absorption of nutrients from digested chyme in the stomach"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secreting HCO3- for neutralization","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secreting bicarbonate (HCO3⁻) to neutralize acid</b>. The exocrine pancreas sends juice to the duodenum: <b>enzymes from acinar cells</b> and a <b>bicarbonate-rich fluid from duct cells</b>. Exocrine tissue is 85 to 90 percent of the gland.<br>A. Insulin: endocrine, from islet beta cells into the blood.<br>B. Acid (H⁺) secretion: parietal cells of the stomach.<br>D. Nutrient absorption: the small intestine does this, not the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 13 (Diagrams MISleading!)","L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":95,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":95,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":263,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":46,"label":"Beta cells make insulin (type 1 diabetes)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-13.jpg","cap":"Islet cells: beta insulin 60–75%, alpha glucagon, delta somatostatin","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G044-8.jpg","cap":"Islet drawing labels alpha, beta, delta and PP cells among acini","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-108-1","s":"5 year old boy is brought in by his mother because she noticed that he looked increasingly tired lately. She states that he’s been bumping into things during the day, while also wetting the bed at night because he’s been so thirsty. What is the mechanism of his diagnosis?","o":["A. Alpha cell defect","B. Beta cell defect","C. Delta cell defect","D. Gamma cell defect","E. Zeta cell defect"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Beta cell defect","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Beta cell defect</b>. A young child with thirst, bed-wetting (polyuria), tiredness and blurry vision has <b>type 1 diabetes</b>: autoimmune loss of <b>beta cells</b>, the insulin makers (60 to 75 percent of islet cells).<br>A. Alpha cells make glucagon; losing them would lower, not raise, blood sugar.<br>C. Delta cells make somatostatin; they do not cause this picture.<br>D. Gamma (PP) cells make pancreatic polypeptide; no high-sugar picture.<br>E. There are no zeta cells in the islets.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 13 (Diagrams MISleading!)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":108,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":108,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":46,"label":"Acinar cells make enzymes; ducts modify fluid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-14.jpg","cap":"Acinar cells with zymogen granules, centroacinar cells and intercalated duct labelled","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D009-29.jpg","cap":"TA slide: centroacinar cells secrete fluid; ductal cells modify it","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-144-1","s":"WHICH TYPE OF CELLS SECRETE THE ENZYMATIC COMPONENT OF PANCREATIC SECRETION?","o":["A. ACINAR CELLS","B. CENTROACINAR CELLS","C. DUCTAL CELLS","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ACINAR CELLS","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Acinar cells</b> make the enzymes, store them in <b>zymogen granules</b>, and release them when CCK (cholecystokinin) binds.<br>B. Centroacinar cells: start the watery, bicarbonate-rich part of the juice.<br>C. Ductal cells: modify that watery part by swapping chloride for bicarbonate.<br>D. Only acinar cells make enzymes, so ‘all of the above’ is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 21 (Formation of Secretions)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":144,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-81-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 4"},{"id":"D067-72-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the formation of pancreatic secretions?","o":["A. Enzymes are secreted from ductal cells and bicarbonate from acinar cells","B. Enzymes are secreted by acinar cells, and bicarbonate by ductal cells","C. Both enzymes and bicarbonate are secreted by the same cell type","D. Pancreatic secretion is independent of hormonal control","E. Bicarbonate is secreted by the gallbladder epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Enzymes are secreted by acinar cells, and bicarbonate by ductal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Enzymes are secreted by acinar cells, and bicarbonate by ductal cells.</b> <b>Acinar cells</b> make and release the digestive enzymes. <b>Ductal cells</b> add the watery, bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes stomach acid.<br>A. Reversed: ducts do not make enzymes, and acini do not make the bicarbonate.<br>C. Same cell type: two different cells do the two jobs.<br>D. No hormonal control: CCK (cholecystokinin) drives the acini and secretin drives the ducts.<br>E. Gallbladder: it stores and concentrates bile; it does not make pancreatic bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 21 (Formation of Secretions)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":72,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-81-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 5"},{"id":"D008-97-1","s":"Which of the following cell is associated with modification of the pancreatic secretion?","o":["A. Acinar cells","B. Alpha cells","C. Ductal cells","D. Centroacinar cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ductal cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ductal cells</b> modify the juice: under secretin they <b>swap chloride for bicarbonate</b>, making the fluid bicarbonate-rich.<br>A. Acinar cells: secrete the enzymes, not the modifying step.<br>B. Alpha cells: islet cells that make glucagon (endocrine).<br>D. Centroacinar cells: secrete the first watery fluid; the ducts then modify it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":97,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":97,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":265,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-81-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 6"},{"id":"D066-81-1","s":"A patient has impaired pancreatic ductal cell function and cannot adequately neutralize acidic chyme entering the duodenum. What is the normal role of these cells?","o":["A. Secrete pepsinogen","B. Secrete bicarbonate rich fluid","C. Secrete active trypsin","D. Secrete HCl","E. Secrete intrinsic factor"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Secrete bicarbonate rich fluid","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid.</b> Pancreatic <b>ductal cells</b> secrete a watery fluid rich in bicarbonate when secretin arrives. It neutralizes the acidic chyme entering the duodenum, so the pancreatic enzymes can work.<br>A. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells of the stomach.<br>C. Active trypsin: acinar cells secrete inactive trypsinogen; it becomes trypsin only in the duodenum.<br>D. Hydrochloric acid (HCl): made by parietal cells of the stomach.<br>E. Intrinsic factor: also made by parietal cells; needed to absorb vitamin B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L54 slide 13 (Diagrams MISleading!)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":81,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"172"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":46,"label":"Sympathetic inhibits pancreatic secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-20.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic secretes, sympathetic dries; vagus and splanchnic nerves drawn","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/D040-123.jpg","cap":"TA slide: vagus stimulates, sympathetic (celiac ganglion) inhibits pancreatic secretion","where":"2023 TA review, slide 123"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-142-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: SYMPATHETIC STIMULATION INCREASES PANCREATIC SECRETION","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CAP","a":"B","why":"<b>Cap (false)</b>. <b>Sympathetic stimulation inhibits</b> pancreatic secretion; parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation increases it. The lecture rule: ‘parasympathetic secretes, sympathetic dries’. Nerves matter much less than hormones here.<br>Common mix-up: in salivary glands both divisions increase secretion, but the pancreas follows the usual ‘rest and digest’ pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 20 (Neural Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":142,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":47,"label":"Pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive zymogens","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-21.jpg","cap":"Acini make active amylase and lipase but inactive proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G044-9.jpg","cap":"Trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase: activated only in duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G045-42.jpg","cap":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen; trypsin then activates the other proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-196-1","s":"Which of the following is produced with a negative regulatory domain?","o":["A. Chymotrypsinogen","B. Lingual amylase","C. Lingual lipase","D. Pancreatic amylase","E. Pancreatic lipase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chymotrypsinogen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chymotrypsinogen</b>. The ‘-ogen’ ending marks a <b>zymogen</b>: an inactive protease whose extra regulatory piece blocks it until <b>trypsin</b> cuts it off in the duodenum. That keeps the protein-made pancreas from digesting itself.<br>B. Lingual amylase: a salivary starch enzyme, secreted active.<br>C. Lingual lipase: secreted active in saliva.<br>D. Pancreatic amylase: secreted active; the pancreas is not made of starch.<br>E. Pancreatic lipase: secreted active; the pancreas is not made of fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":59,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":196,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":49,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"173"},{"id":"D036-128-1","s":"Which of the following is released in active form from the pancreas?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Carboxypeptidase","C. Elastase","D. Amylase","E. Trypsinogen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Amylase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Amylase</b>. Pancreatic <b>amylase and lipase are secreted active</b> (the pancreas is not made of starch or fat); <b>proteases are secreted inactive</b> and switched on in the duodenum.<br>A. Trypsin: leaves as trypsinogen; enterokinase activates it in the duodenum.<br>B. Carboxypeptidase: leaves as procarboxypeptidase.<br>C. Elastase: leaves as proelastase.<br>E. Trypsinogen is released, but as the inactive form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 21 (Formation of Secretions)","L54 slide 10 (Amylase and Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":128,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"174"},{"id":"D015-186-1","s":"ALL OF THE FOLLOWING ARE INACTIVE PRECURSORS OF PANCREATIC PROTEASES EXCEPT:","o":["A. PEPSINOGEN","B. TRYPSINOGEN","C. CHYMOTRYPSINOGEN","D. PROELASTASE","E. PROCARBOXYPEPTIDASE A & B"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"PEPSINOGEN","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Pepsinogen</b> is the exception: it is a <b>stomach</b> zymogen made by <b>chief cells</b> and activated by acid, not a pancreatic one.<br>B. Trypsinogen: pancreatic; enterokinase in the duodenal lining turns it into trypsin.<br>C. Chymotrypsinogen: pancreatic; trypsin activates it.<br>D. Proelastase: pancreatic; trypsin activates it.<br>E. Procarboxypeptidase A and B: pancreatic; trypsin activates them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)","L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":186,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-196-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 8"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":47,"label":"Pancreatitis = premature trypsinogen activation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-45.jpg","cap":"Autodigestion: acute pancreatitis is a premature explosion of trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G044-9.jpg","cap":"Proteases made inactive because the pancreas is made of protein","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D007-127.jpg","cap":"TA slide: zymogen granules; early activation makes the pancreas autodigest","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 127"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-135-1","s":"A 60 year old male with a 40 year old history of alcoholism comes in with pancreatitis. What is the physiological mechanism that causes pancreatitis?","o":["A. Amylase damaging cells","B. Lipases autodigesting organ","C. Premature activation of trypsinogen","D. Helicobacter pylori","E. Bleeding"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Premature activation of trypsinogen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Premature activation of trypsinogen</b>. Alcohol makes trypsin switch on inside the pancreas; trypsin activates the other proteases and the gland <b>digests itself</b> (the lecture calls it an ‘explosion of trypsin’).<br>A. Amylase digests starch; the pancreas is not starch, so amylase is made active safely.<br>B. Lipase digests fat and is also made active; it rises in blood as a marker, not the trigger.<br>D. H. pylori (Helicobacter pylori) causes gastritis and ulcers, not pancreatitis.<br>E. Bleeding is at most a complication, not the mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 12 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":135,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"175"},{"id":"D036-139-1","s":"What is the physiologic cause of pancreatitis?","o":["A. Activation of trypsinogen in the pancreas","B. Change in bile production","C. High carbohydrate diet","D. Overproduction of gastric acid leading to pancreatic inflammation","E. Impaired blood supply to the pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of trypsinogen in the pancreas","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Activation of trypsinogen in the pancreas</b>. Proteases are made inactive because the pancreas is made of protein. If trypsinogen turns into trypsin too early, trypsin activates the others and the gland <b>autodigests</b>.<br>B. Bile production changes are not the mechanism; even gallstone pancreatitis works through early trypsin.<br>C. A high-carbohydrate diet plays no role.<br>D. Gastric acid does not reach or inflame the pancreas.<br>E. Poor blood supply is not the basic mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 12 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)","L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":139,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-135-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 9"},{"id":"D010-106-1","s":"A 31 year old man presents with pain that radiates from his abdomen to his back. He has no history of trauma, but did mention he consumed an entire bottle of vodka last weekend while watching football. Amylase is increased. What can be expected to be occurring in the affected organ?","o":["A. A decrease in active protease concentration","B. Increase in trypsin","C. Decrease in lipase","D. Increase in bile conjugation","E. Increase in tyrosine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase in trypsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Increase in trypsin</b>. Acute pancreatitis after a binge is <b>premature activation of trypsinogen to trypsin inside the pancreas</b>; trypsin then switches on the other proteases, which digest the gland. High amylase points to the pancreas.<br>A. Active protease levels go up, not down.<br>C. Lipase leaks into the blood and rises; it does not fall.<br>D. Bile conjugation happens in the liver and is unchanged.<br>E. Tyrosine has no role here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 11 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)","L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":106,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":106,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-135-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 10"},{"id":"F1203-87-1","s":"A 39 year old Male with a history of alcohol abuse presents to the Emergency Department complaining of vomiting for the past 2 hours with severe, mid-epigastric abdominal pain that radiates to his mid-back. When you perform a Red Reflex on him, you note (+) TART changes over the region of T5 - T9. You note from a previous chart that during the patient’s last visit 2 months ago, a mass was noted on CT Abdomen/Pelvis at the head of the pancreas. Based on the patient’s history, what is the physiology behind his current presentation?","o":["A. Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)","B. Perforated peptic ulcer in the fundus of the stomach","C. Exacerbation of gastroesophageal reflux (GERD)","D. Premature activation of trypsinogen into trypsin within the pancreatic parenchyma"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Premature activation of trypsinogen into trypsin within the pancreatic parenchyma","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Premature activation of trypsinogen into trypsin within the pancreas</b>. Epigastric pain boring to the back with vomiting, in a drinker with a mass blocking pancreatic outflow, is acute pancreatitis: early trypsin activates the other zymogens, which <b>digest the pancreas itself</b>.<br>A. AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm): a vascular problem with no link to alcohol or pancreatic outflow.<br>B. Perforated ulcer: sudden rigid abdomen with free air; nothing ties it to the pancreatic mass.<br>C. GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease): burning chest pain, not back pain with vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 11 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)","L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":87,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-135-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":47,"label":"Acute pancreatitis → lipase rises in blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-21.jpg","cap":"Acinus drawing: acini make amylase and lipase, which leak when injured","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/D007-127.jpg","cap":"TA slide: acinar enzymes, and pancreatitis as early protease autodigestion","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 127"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-133-1","s":"A patient is diagnosed with acute pancreatitis. Which of the following helped the physician in the diagnosis:","o":["A. Elevated plasma CCK","B. Elevated plasma secretin","C. Elevated plasma lipase","D. Elevated plasma gastrin","E. Elevated plasma Ca2+"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated plasma lipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Elevated plasma lipase</b>. In acute pancreatitis, injured acinar cells leak their enzymes, so <b>amylase and lipase rise in the blood</b>; lipase is the more pancreas-specific marker.<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): a duodenal I-cell hormone, not a marker of pancreatic injury.<br>B. Secretin: a duodenal S-cell hormone, not a marker.<br>D. Gastrin: from antral G cells; high in gastrinoma, not pancreatitis.<br>E. Calcium tends to fall in pancreatitis (fat necrosis binds it); high calcium is a cause, not a sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 11 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":133,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":109,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"176"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":48,"label":"Pancreatic duct-cell transporters","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-22.jpg","cap":"Apical chloride–bicarbonate exchanger; basolateral sodium–hydrogen exchanger and sodium–potassium pump","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D007-128.jpg","cap":"TA slide: annotated duct cell; more acid, more secretin, more bicarbonate","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 128"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-143-1","s":"What exchangers are found on the apical and basolateral membranes of the pancreatic cells?","o":["A. Na+/Ca2+ exchanger; K/Cl co-transporter; H+/K+ ATPase","B. Cl-/HCO3- exchanger; Na/K ATPase; Na/H Exchanger","C. K+/Na+ co-transporter; Cl-/HCO3- exchanger; H+/Ca2+ ATPase","D. Na/H exchanger; HCO3-/Cl- co-transporter; K+/Mg2+ ATPase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cl-/HCO3- exchanger; Na/K ATPase; Na/H Exchanger","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Chloride–bicarbonate exchanger; sodium–potassium ATPase; sodium–hydrogen exchanger</b>. In duct cells, carbonic anhydrase makes bicarbonate; the <b>apical</b> chloride–bicarbonate exchanger secretes it into the duct, and the <b>basolateral</b> sodium–hydrogen exchanger and sodium–potassium pump get rid of the acid.<br>A. Hydrogen–potassium ATPase is the parietal-cell acid pump.<br>C. Duct cells have no hydrogen–calcium ATPase.<br>D. Chloride and bicarbonate are exchanged, not cotransported; no potassium–magnesium ATPase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":143,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":142,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"177"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":48,"label":"Secretin → ductal cAMP → bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-22.jpg","cap":"Duct cell: carbonic anhydrase, apical chloride–bicarbonate exchanger and chloride channel","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D009-32.jpg","cap":"Acid → S cells → secretin → ductal cAMP → sodium bicarbonate fluid","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Acid stimulates secretin; secretin acts at ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-82-1","s":"A 48-year-old man with a history of chronic duodenal ulcers presents with worsening abdominal pain and diarrhea. Laboratory testing reveals hypergastrinemia and elevated basal acid output, leading to a suspected diagnosis of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. During your explanation of the pathophysiology, the patient asks how the pancreas normally protects the duodenum from excessive gastric acidity. Which of the following best explains the physiological mechanism by which pancreatic secretions neutralize gastric chyme under conditions of high acid load in the duodenum?","o":["A. Direct stimulation of pancreatic acinar cells by gastrin to increase bicarbonate secretion","B. Enhanced chloride-bicarbonate exchange at the luminal membrane of ductal cells via stimulation by cholecystokinin (CCK)","C. Secretin-mediated activation of cAMP in pancreatic ductal cells, increasing CFTR activity and promoting bicarbonate secretion into the ductal lumen","D. Stimulation of the vagus nerve leading to H+/HCO3⁻ antiport activation at the basolateral membrane of pancreatic ductal cells","E. Increased gastric acid neutralization by paracrine signaling from somatostatin, suppressing bicarbonate reabsorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin-mediated activation of cAMP in pancreatic ductal cells, increasing CFTR activity and promoting bicarbonate secretion into the ductal lumen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secretin raises cAMP in ductal cells, increasing CFTR activity and bicarbonate secretion</b>. Acid in the duodenum releases secretin from <b>S cells</b>: more acid, more secretin, more bicarbonate to neutralize it. (cAMP = cyclic adenosine monophosphate; CFTR = cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator.)<br>A. Gastrin stimulates gastric acid, not pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>B. CCK (cholecystokinin) mainly drives acinar enzymes; it only potentiates secretin.<br>D. The vagus only potentiates; the basolateral exchanger is sodium–hydrogen.<br>E. Somatostatin inhibits secretion; it neutralizes nothing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":82,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"178"},{"id":"D015-146-1","s":"WHICH HORMONE IS THE MAJOR STIMULANT OF BICARBONATE SECRETION OF PANCREATIC DUCTAL CELLS?","o":["A. GASTRIN","B. SOMATOSTATIN","C. SECRETIN","D. PEPTIDE YY"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"SECRETIN","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secretin</b>, released from duodenal <b>S cells</b> when acid arrives, is the major stimulus for <b>bicarbonate secretion by pancreatic duct cells</b>.<br>A. Gastrin: from antral G cells; stimulates gastric acid.<br>B. Somatostatin: from D cells; inhibits secretion.<br>D. Peptide YY: from ileal and colonic L cells; slows the gut, does not drive bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":146,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-82-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 12"},{"id":"G067-80-1","s":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the increase in bicarbonate concentration in pancreatic secretions during high flow rates, and its role in neutralizing gastric chyme in the duodenum?","o":["A. Direct stimulation of acinar cells by gastrin to secrete bicarbonate","B. Enhanced H+/K+ exchange at the apical membrane of ductal cells","C. Activation of secretin-mediated CFTR channels leading to HCO3⁻ secretion by pancreatic ductal cells","D. Increased activity of CCK stimulating SGLT-1 for bicarbonate reabsorption","E. Enhanced activity of vagus nerve promoting H+/HCO3⁻ antiport at the basolateral membrane"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of secretin-mediated CFTR channels leading to HCO3⁻ secretion by pancreatic ductal cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secretin opens CFTR channels in ductal cells</b>. Secretin raises cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate), which opens <b>CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) chloride channels; chloride cycles back through the chloride–bicarbonate exchanger, so <b>more secretin means more flow and more bicarbonate</b>.<br>A. Gastrin drives parietal-cell acid; acinar cells make enzymes.<br>B. Hydrogen–potassium exchange is the parietal-cell acid pump.<br>D. CCK (cholecystokinin) drives enzymes; SGLT-1 (sodium–glucose cotransporter 1) absorbs glucose.<br>E. The basolateral exchanger is sodium–hydrogen; the vagus only potentiates.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)","L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":80,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-82-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 13"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":48,"label":"Higher flow → more bicarbonate, less chloride","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-23.jpg","cap":"More secretin = more flow = more bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 23"},{"src":"../sl/D009-30.jpg","cap":"Flow-rate graph: bicarbonate rises, chloride falls, sodium and potassium flat","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-127-1","s":"Your patient is injected with secretin. The pancreatic secretions they produce will be what in this table? (rows: Bicarb / Cl- / Na+ / K+)","o":["A. Bicarb Hi, Cl- Hi, Na+ Hi, K+ Lo","B. Bicarb Hi, Cl- Lo, Na+ Hi, K+ Lo","C. Bicarb Lo, Cl- Lo, Na+ Hi, K+ Lo","D. Bicarb Lo, Cl- Hi, Na+ Hi, K+ Lo"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bicarb Hi, Cl- Lo, Na+ Hi, K+ Lo","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bicarbonate high, chloride low, sodium high, potassium low</b>. Secretin raises duct-cell flow, and at high flow the juice is <b>rich in bicarbonate and poor in chloride</b>. Sodium and potassium stay at plasma levels (sodium high, potassium low) at every flow.<br>A. Bicarbonate and chloride cannot both be high; they trade places.<br>C. Both low is impossible; the juice stays isotonic.<br>D. This is the low-flow pattern, without secretin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":127,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"179"},{"id":"D066-83-1","s":"Physiology TA Jennifer administers secretin to a patient, causing pancreatic flow to increase. Which change in pancreatic juice is expected?","o":["A. Increased Cl- and Decreased HCO3-","B. Decreased Na+ and Increased K+","C. Increased HCO3- and Decreased Cl-","D. Increased HCO3- and Increased Cl-","E. Decreased HCO3- and unchanged Cl-"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased HCO3- and Decreased Cl-","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased bicarbonate (HCO3⁻) and decreased chloride (Cl⁻).</b> Secretin makes the duct cells pour out more fluid, and that extra fluid is rich in bicarbonate. The juice stays isotonic, so chloride falls as bicarbonate rises. Sodium (Na⁺) and potassium (K⁺) stay at plasma levels at every flow.<br>A. More chloride, less bicarbonate: the low-flow pattern, without secretin.<br>B. Sodium and potassium do not change with flow.<br>D. Both up: the two anions always move in opposite directions.<br>E. Bicarbonate rises, not falls, and chloride does change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":83,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"180"},{"id":"D036-136-1","s":"When the parasympathetic nervous system is activated, how does it impact the pancreatic flow rate?","o":["A. Reduced flow rate; HCO3- concentration is highest and Cl- concentration is lowest","B. Increased flow rate; HCO3- concentration is highest and Cl- concentration is lowest","C. Increased flow rate; HCO3- concentration is lowest and Cl- concentration is highest","D. Reduced flow rate; HCO3- concentration is lowest and Cl- concentration is highest","E. Reduced flow rate; HCO3- concentration and Cl- concentration are both lowered"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased flow rate; HCO3- concentration is highest and Cl- concentration is lowest","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Flow rises; bicarbonate (HCO3⁻) highest, chloride (Cl⁻) lowest</b>. The parasympathetic system <b>increases</b> pancreatic secretion. The juice stays isotonic: sodium and potassium are constant, but at <b>high flow bicarbonate rises and chloride falls</b>; low flow is the reverse.<br>A. Reduced flow is the sympathetic effect, and low flow means low bicarbonate.<br>C. Right flow, but the ions are flipped.<br>D. This is the low-flow, sympathetic pattern.<br>E. Bicarbonate and chloride move in opposite directions, never both down.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)","L54 slide 20 (Neural Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":136,"f":"D036"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":99,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":99,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":267,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-127-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 14"},{"id":"D067-75-1","s":"As pancreatic flow rate increases, which of the following changes occurs in pancreatic juice composition?","o":["A. Cl⁻ concentration increases","B. HCO₃⁻ concentration increases","C. Na⁺ concentration decreases","D. K⁺ concentration increases","E. HCO₃⁻ concentration decreases"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HCO₃⁻ concentration increases","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bicarbonate (HCO3⁻) concentration increases.</b> Higher flow means more secretin-driven duct secretion, and that fluid is rich in bicarbonate. So as flow rises, bicarbonate rises and chloride (Cl⁻) falls as its mirror image.<br>A. Chloride increases: it falls at high flow; chloride is high only at low flow.<br>C. Sodium (Na⁺) decreases: sodium stays at its plasma level at every flow.<br>D. Potassium (K⁺) increases: potassium also stays at its plasma level.<br>E. Bicarbonate decreases: that is the low-flow pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":75,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-127-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 15"},{"id":"G066-146-1","s":"Pancreatic juice composition is affected by flow rate. During low flow rates, there is a higher concentration of Cl-. Why is this the case?","o":["A. At low (basal rates), pancreatic cells secrete an isotonic solution composed mainly of Na, Cl-, and water.","B. Cl- is always high in the saliva, regardless of flow rate","C. Flow rate does not affect Cl- levels","D. Not enough HCO3- is released during low flow rates, making it seem like there is more Cl-"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"At low (basal rates), pancreatic cells secrete an isotonic solution composed mainly of Na, Cl-, and water.","a":"A","why":"<b>A</b>. At low (basal) flow, pancreatic juice is an isotonic fluid of mainly <b>sodium and chloride</b>. As secretin raises flow, duct cells swap chloride for bicarbonate, so <b>bicarbonate rises and chloride falls</b>; sodium and potassium stay constant.<br>B. That is saliva, a different gland, and saliva chloride actually falls at low flow.<br>C. Flow rate clearly changes chloride.<br>D. Chloride really is higher, not just apparently; the two anions trade places to keep the juice isotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 23 (Higher Flow=Higher HCO3)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":146,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":145,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"181"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"Intestinal phase = 80–90% of pancreatic secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Cephalic and gastric phases minor; intestinal phase 80–90% via CCK, secretin","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D009-31.jpg","cap":"TA slide: three phases; cephalic and gastric mainly enzymatic","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-125-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes the phases of pancreatic secretion during digestion?","o":["A. The gastric phase accounts for 80% of pancreatic secretion","B. Enzymatic and aqueous secretion are equal in all three phases","C. The vagus nerve stimulates the intestinal phase","D. CCK and Secretin regulate the intestinal phase","E. Acinar cells secrete Na+ HCO3- and H20 during the intestinal phase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK and Secretin regulate the intestinal phase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. CCK (cholecystokinin) and secretin regulate the intestinal phase</b>, which gives 80 to 90 percent of pancreatic secretion.<br>A. The intestinal phase, not the gastric phase, is the 80 percent.<br>B. Not equal: cephalic and gastric phases are mostly enzymes; the intestinal phase adds the bicarbonate fluid.<br>C. The vagus drives the minor cephalic and gastric phases; in the intestinal phase it only potentiates.<br>E. Sodium, bicarbonate and water come from ductal cells, not acinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":125,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"182"},{"id":"D024-61-1","s":"Which phase is the most integral for pancreatic secretions?","o":["A. Cephalic phase","B. Gastric phase","C. Intestinal phase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intestinal phase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Intestinal phase</b>: it gives <b>80 to 90 percent</b> of pancreatic secretion, driven by CCK (cholecystokinin) and secretin from the duodenum.<br>A. Cephalic phase: minor; smell and taste act through the vagus.<br>B. Gastric phase: minor; stomach stretch acts through vagal reflexes. (For gastric acid, by contrast, the gastric phase is the biggest.)","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":61,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-125-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"Cephalic phase: smell, taste, conditioning","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D009-31.jpg","cap":"Cephalic phase: smell, taste and conditioning, mediated by the vagus nerve","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Cephalic and gastric phases are minor: vagal acetylcholine potentiates hormones","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-149-1","s":"What initiates the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion?","o":["A. Distension of stomach","B. Proteins only","C. Amino acids only","D. Smell, taste, and conditioning"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Smell, taste, and conditioning","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Smell, taste and conditioning</b> start the cephalic phase, acting through the <b>vagus nerve</b> (acetylcholine). It is a minor, mostly enzyme phase.<br>A. Stomach distension starts the gastric phase.<br>B. Proteins alone do not start it; protein products in the duodenum act in the intestinal phase.<br>C. Amino acids in the duodenum release CCK (cholecystokinin) in the intestinal phase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L53 slide 20 (Gastric HCl Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":149,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":148,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 17"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"Phe, Met, Trp release CCK","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D012-17.jpg","cap":"CCK released by tryptophan, methionine, phenylalanine and fatty acids","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G040-10.jpg","cap":"CCK stimuli: fat in duodenum and protein parts (amino acids, peptides)","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-97-1","s":"Which of the amino acids are the most potent stimulators of CCK?","o":["A. Cysteine, Arginine, Valine","B. Phenylalanine, Methionine, and Tryptophan","C. Leucine, Isoleucine, Valine","D. Aspartate, Glutamate, Methionine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Phenylalanine, Methionine, and Tryptophan","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Phenylalanine, methionine and tryptophan</b> are the most potent amino acid stimuli for cholecystokinin (CCK) release from I cells (Costanzo). Fatty acids and monoglycerides also release CCK.<br>A. Cysteine, arginine, valine: not the main CCK stimuli.<br>C. Leucine, isoleucine, valine: branched-chain amino acids, not the main CCK stimuli.<br>D. Aspartate, glutamate, methionine: aspartate and glutamate are acidic amino acids; only methionine belongs on the list.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":97,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"183"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"CCK → acinar IP3/calcium → enzymes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Amino acids, peptides and fats stimulate CCK; CCK acts at acini","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D009-32.jpg","cap":"I cells → CCK → acinar IP3 and calcium → enzymes","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"Actions of CCK include pancreatic lipase, amylase and trypsin release","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-47-1","s":"Match the correct sequence of events.","o":["A. Acid release into the intestine; I cells release secretin, increase in aqueous secretion through cGMP","B. Acid release into the duodenum; S cells secrete CCK, increase in intracellular cAMP, release of HCO3","C. Peptides and fatty acids are released into the duodenum; I cells secrete CCK, increase in intracellular IP3/Ca, release of HCO3","D. Peptides and fatty acids release into the duodenum; I cells secrete CCK, increase in intracellular IP3/Ca, release of enzymes into the duodenum","E. Peptides and fatty acids release into the duodenum; S cells secrete CCK through increase in IP3/Ca"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peptides and fatty acids release into the duodenum; I cells secrete CCK, increase in intracellular IP3/Ca, release of enzymes into the duodenum","a":"D","why":"<b>D</b>. Peptides and fatty acids in the duodenum → <b>I cells</b> release CCK (cholecystokinin) → acinar cells raise <b>IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) and calcium</b> → <b>enzymes</b> are released.<br>A. Secretin comes from S cells and uses cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate), not cGMP.<br>B. S cells make secretin, not CCK.<br>C. CCK’s acinar output is enzymes; bicarbonate is secretin’s job.<br>E. CCK comes from I cells, not S cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":47,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"184"},{"id":"D066-85-1","s":"Physiology TA Mandela is talking to a patient who just consumed a meal rich in fat and protein. The patient asks him when fatty acids and amino acids reach the duodenum, which response is most likely?","o":["A. Secretin stimulates acinar enzyme release","B. Gastrin stimulates ductal bicarbonate release","C. CCK stimulates acinar digestive enzyme release","D. Somatostatin stimulates bicarbonate release","E. Histamine stimulates pancreatic enzyme release"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK stimulates acinar digestive enzyme release","a":"C","why":"<b>C. CCK stimulates acinar digestive enzyme release.</b> Fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum make I cells release <b>CCK</b> (cholecystokinin). CCK acts on the <b>acinar cells</b>, which release the enzymes.<br>A. Secretin: released by acid, and it acts on duct cells to secrete bicarbonate, not on acini.<br>B. Gastrin: stimulates stomach acid; it does not drive ductal bicarbonate.<br>D. Somatostatin: inhibits pancreatic secretion.<br>E. Histamine: stimulates acid from parietal cells in the stomach, not pancreatic enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":85,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"185"},{"id":"G066-151-1","s":"During the intestinal phase of pancreatic secretion, small peptides, fatty acids, and amino acids stimulate I cells to secrete CCK, which then acts on acinar cells in the pancreas and stimulate which second messenger pathway to allow for the ultimate release of enzymes?","o":["A. cAMP pathway","B. IP3 pathway","C. Na-K ATPase pathway","D. Receptor tyrosine kinase pathway"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"IP3 pathway","a":"B","why":"<b>B. IP3 pathway</b> (inositol trisphosphate, which raises calcium). CCK (cholecystokinin) from duodenal <b>I cells</b> binds acinar cells, raises IP3 and calcium, and releases <b>enzymes</b>.<br>A. cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) is secretin’s pathway in ductal cells.<br>C. The sodium–potassium ATPase is a pump, not a second-messenger pathway.<br>D. Receptor tyrosine kinases serve insulin and growth factors, not CCK.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":151,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":150,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-47-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 18"},{"id":"D061-110-1","s":"Which of the following substances is secreted by the pancreas in response to food and stomach acid entering the duodenum?","o":["A. Glucagon","B. CCK","C. Growth hormone","D. Pancreatic amylase"],"g":["D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"B","why":"<b>D. Pancreatic amylase</b>. Food (fat, amino acids) in the duodenum makes <b>duodenal I cells</b> release CCK (cholecystokinin); CCK then makes pancreatic acinar cells secrete enzymes such as amylase.<br>A. Glucagon: from pancreatic alpha cells, released for low blood sugar, not by food or acid in the duodenum.<br>B. CCK: made by the duodenum, not the pancreas.<br>C. Growth hormone: anterior pituitary.","ex":"","fl":["Wrong key: correct = D (pancreatic amylase). CCK is secreted by duodenal I cells, not by the pancreas; CCK then acts on pancreatic acini, which secrete amylase (G044:24, G040:11, G044:25 'CCK released from I cells')."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":110,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-47-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 19"},{"id":"D008-102-1","s":"Which of the following hormones are stimulants for enzymatic and HCO3- secretion in the pancreas respectively?","o":["A. Insulin; H+","B. Secretin; CCK","C. CCK; Secretin","D. Somatostatin; H+"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK; Secretin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. CCK; secretin</b>. <b>CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) acts on acinar cells to release <b>enzymes</b>; <b>secretin</b> acts on duct cells to release <b>bicarbonate (HCO3⁻)</b>. Acetylcholine potentiates both.<br>A. Insulin does not stimulate pancreatic juice; acid (H⁺) works only by releasing secretin.<br>B. Order reversed.<br>D. Somatostatin inhibits pancreatic secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":102,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":102,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":270,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-47-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 20"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"Secretin → ductal cAMP → bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-22.jpg","cap":"Duct cell: carbonic anhydrase, apical chloride–bicarbonate exchanger and chloride channel","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D009-32.jpg","cap":"Acid → S cells → secretin → ductal cAMP → sodium bicarbonate fluid","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Acid stimulates secretin; secretin acts at ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-154-1","s":"During the intestinal phase of pancreatic secretion, H+ stimulates S cells to release secretin, which acts on the ductal cells in the pancreas and stimulate which second messenger pathway to allow for the ultimate release of an aqueous secretion?","o":["A. Decrease cAMP","B. Increase IP3","C. Increase cAMP","D. Increase PLC"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase cAMP","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increase cAMP</b> (cyclic adenosine monophosphate). Acid releases secretin from duodenal <b>S cells</b>; secretin acts on <b>ductal cells</b> through cAMP to secrete a watery, bicarbonate-rich fluid.<br>A. cAMP rises, it does not fall.<br>B. IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) is the CCK (cholecystokinin) pathway in acinar cells.<br>D. PLC (phospholipase C) makes IP3; again the CCK and acetylcholine pathway, not secretin’s.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":154,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":153,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"186"},{"id":"D067-77-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches a regulator with its effect on pancreatic secretion?","o":["A. Secretin — stimulates enzyme secretion","B. CCK — inhibits acinar cell secretion","C. Secretin — stimulates bicarbonate secretion","D. ACh — inhibits bicarbonate secretion","E. Somatostatin — stimulates enzyme secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin — stimulates bicarbonate secretion","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secretin stimulates bicarbonate secretion.</b> Acid in the duodenum releases <b>secretin</b>, which acts on the duct cells to secrete bicarbonate and fluid.<br>A. Secretin and enzymes: enzyme secretion is the job of CCK (cholecystokinin), acting on the acini.<br>B. CCK inhibits acini: CCK stimulates them.<br>D. ACh (acetylcholine) inhibits bicarbonate: ACh from the vagus potentiates secretin, so bicarbonate goes up.<br>E. Somatostatin stimulates enzymes: somatostatin inhibits pancreatic secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)","L51A slide 13 (Actions of Secretin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":77,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-154-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 21"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":49,"label":"ACh potentiates CCK; ACh and CCK potentiate secretin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D009-32.jpg","cap":"ACh potentiates CCK on acini; ACh and CCK potentiate secretin on ducts","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Minor: vagus/acetylcholine potentiates CCK and secretin","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-152-2","s":"CCK acts on pancreatic acinar cells via the IP3 pathway. What is this potentiated by?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"ACh","a":null,"why":"<b>ACh (acetylcholine)</b>, released by the vagus. ACh stimulates acinar enzyme release itself and <b>potentiates CCK</b> (cholecystokinin), whose acinar pathway uses IP3 (inositol trisphosphate) and calcium.<br>Common mix-up: CCK’s acinar effect is potentiated by ACh, while secretin’s ductal effect is potentiated by <b>both ACh and CCK</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":152,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":151,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 22"},{"id":"G066-155-2","s":"Secretin acts on pancreatic ductal cells via increased cAMP. What is this potentiated by?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"ACh, CCK","a":null,"why":"<b>ACh (acetylcholine) and CCK (cholecystokinin)</b>. Secretin raises cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) in ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate; vagal ACh and CCK <b>amplify</b> that response.<br>Common mix-up: two potentiators for secretin (ACh and CCK), but only one for CCK’s acinar effect (ACh).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":155,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":154,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":50,"label":"Blocked pancreatic duct → no enzymes, steatorrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-21.jpg","cap":"Acini make the digestive enzymes: amylase, lipase, inactive proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G045-42.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic trypsinogen activated by enterokinase; trypsin activates the rest","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-92-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman presents with progressive weight loss, steatorrhea, and fatigue. Imaging confirms advanced pancreatic adenocarcinoma causing obstruction of the pancreatic duct. Her symptoms are explained by the inability of pancreatic secretions to reach the duodenum. Which of the following best describes the primary physiological role of pancreatic secretions in the duodenum and the consequences of their absence?","o":["A. Direct activation of enterokinase on the brush border, facilitating bile salt recycling and lipid absorption","B. Neutralization of gastric acid through secretin-induced HCO3⁻ secretion, optimizing enzyme activity and mucosal protection","C. Absorption of essential nutrients through active transport mediated by pancreatic-derived co-transporters","D. Stimulation of bile release via pancreatic proteases, enhancing micelle formation and cholesterol absorption","E. Delivery of inactive zymogens such as trypsinogen, which are subsequently activated to facilitate macronutrient digestion"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Delivery of inactive zymogens such as trypsinogen, which are subsequently activated to facilitate macronutrient digestion","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Delivery of zymogens that are later activated to digest food</b>. A blocked pancreatic duct means <b>no enzymes reach the duodenum</b>, so protein, fat and starch go undigested: weight loss and fatty stools (steatorrhea).<br>A. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen; pancreatic juice does not activate it.<br>B. Bicarbonate neutralizing acid is real but secondary; missing enzymes explain her symptoms.<br>C. The pancreas supplies no transporters; the gut wall absorbs.<br>D. CCK (cholecystokinin), not proteases, releases bile.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 21 (Formation of Secretions)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":92,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"187"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":50,"label":"Secretin stimulation test diagnoses chronic pancreatitis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-56.jpg","cap":"IV secretin test: normal massive bicarbonate; chronic pancreatitis decreased","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G045-55.jpg","cap":"Chronic pancreatitis: burnt-out pancreas, no enzymes, low bicarbonate to secretin","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-129-1","s":"A 58-year-old woman with a history of chronic alcohol use disorder comes to the office because of diarrhea, generalized weakness, and a 6.8-kg (15-lb) weight loss over the past 6 months. She reports intermittent dull upper abdominal pain that will last for days at a time and is not improved with antacids. After meals, she feels that her abdomen is distended. She characterizes her multiple daily bowel movements as greasy, foul-smelling, and oily. Secretin stimulation test is abnormal. What is probably causing the diarrhea?","o":["A. Celiac disease","B. Chronic gastritis","C. Chronic pancreatitis","D. Crohn’s disease"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chronic pancreatitis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Chronic pancreatitis.</b> Alcohol, greasy foul stools (steatorrhea), weight loss and an <b>abnormal secretin stimulation test</b> (low bicarbonate output) point to a burnt-out pancreas making no enzymes.<br>A. Celiac disease: gluten-driven villous atrophy; the pancreas and secretin test are normal.<br>B. Chronic gastritis: stomach inflammation; doesn't cause fat malabsorption.<br>D. Crohn disease: bowel inflammation (often terminal ileum); the secretin test is normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)","L55 slide 56 (IV Secretin Stimulation Test)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":129,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 24"},{"id":"D022-24-1","s":"A 43 yo woman presents with oily, floating stool. She has alcohol overuse issues. She has lost 25 lbs unintentionally. The tests for celiac disease (autoimmune disease resulting in inflammation and loss of villi and absorptive surface area) are equivocal. Which of the following is the most specific test of whether or not this is chronic pancreatitis?","o":["a. Biopsy of pancreas","b. Blood levels of Vitamin A, D, E and K","c. CCK level in the blood","d. Secretin stimulation test"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin stimulation test","a":"d","why":"<b>d. Secretin stimulation test.</b> IV (intravenous) secretin normally causes a <b>massive bicarbonate</b> release; in chronic pancreatitis bicarbonate output is <b>markedly decreased</b>. It tests pancreatic function directly.<br>a. Pancreas biopsy: risky, and not a test of function.<br>b. Vitamin A, D, E, K levels: low in any fat malabsorption, celiac disease included.<br>c. CCK (cholecystokinin) level: not a diagnostic test for chronic pancreatitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 56 (IV Secretin Stimulation Test)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":35,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":24,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":174,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":27,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":25,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":33,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"188"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":50,"label":"Cystic fibrosis: CFTR chloride defect, opposite of cholera","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-57.jpg","cap":"CF: clogged pancreatic ducts, enzymes not released, 'opposite of cholera'","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 57"},{"src":"../sl/D007-148.jpg","cap":"TA summary: CFTR defect lowers chloride secretion and clogs pancreatic ducts","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 148"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-131-1","s":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an inherited disorder of the exocrine glands affecting children and young people. Mucus in the exocrine glands becomes thick and sticky and eventually blocks the ducts of these glands (especially in the pancreas, lungs, and liver), forming cysts. A primary disruption in the transfer of which ion across cell membranes occurs in CF, leading to decreased secretion of fluid?","o":["A) Calcium","B) Chloride","C) Phosphate","D) Potassium","E) Sodium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Chloride","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Chloride.</b> CF (cystic fibrosis) is a defect in <b>CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), a cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate)-regulated <b>chloride channel</b>. Less chloride secretion means less water follows, so secretions thicken and block ducts.<br>A. Calcium: not the CF defect.<br>C. Phosphate: not involved.<br>D. Potassium: not the primary defect.<br>E. Sodium: follows chloride secondarily; it is not the primary defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)","L55 slide 58 (Cystic Fibrosis (CF)  vs Chronic Pancreatitis (CP))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":131,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-90-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 25"},{"id":"G067-90-1","s":"A 12-year-old patient with cystic fibrosis presents with malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, and failure to thrive. Despite pancreatic enzyme supplementation, the patient continues to show evidence of fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies. Which of the following best explains the underlying defect that contributes to impaired pancreatic exocrine function and subsequent malabsorption in patients with cystic fibrosis?","o":["A. Hypersecretion of acidic gastric juice overwhelms bicarbonate secretion, leading to denaturation of pancreatic enzymes in the duodenum","B. Overproduction of mucus in the intestinal lumen traps pancreatic enzymes and prevents interaction with dietary substrates","C. Defective CFTR-mediated chloride and bicarbonate secretion results in thickened pancreatic secretions that obstruct ductal outflow and impair enzyme delivery to the duodenum","D. Premature activation of zymogens, including trypsinogen, within the pancreatic ducts leads to autodigestion and subsequent enzyme depletion","E. Loss of bile salt synthesis secondary to hepatic fibrosis reduces micelle formation and leads to fat malabsorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Defective CFTR-mediated chloride and bicarbonate secretion results in thickened pancreatic secretions that obstruct ductal outflow and impair enzyme delivery to the duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Defective <b>CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator, a chloride channel) means little chloride, bicarbonate and water secretion. Thick secretions <b>plug the pancreatic ducts</b>: enzymes are made but can't reach the duodenum.<br>A. Gastric acid excess is not the CF defect.<br>B. Gut mucus trapping enzymes is not the main problem; the ducts are blocked.<br>D. Premature zymogen activation describes acute pancreatitis.<br>E. Lost bile salt synthesis is not the cause of CF malabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)","L55 slide 58 (Cystic Fibrosis (CF)  vs Chronic Pancreatitis (CP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":90,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"189"},{"id":"D004-228-1","s":"Is there a connection between cystic fibrosis and cholera?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes – both hinge on the CFTR Cl⁻ channel of intestinal/ductal epithelium. Cholera toxin → Gs stuck on → ↑cAMP → CFTR held open → massive Cl⁻ (then Na⁺, water) secretion = secretory diarrhea. In CF the CFTR channel is defective → too little Cl⁻/fluid secretion → thick secretions plug pancreatic ducts → fat & ADEK malabsorption: the 'opposite of cholera'.","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes: both hinge on CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), the epithelial <b>chloride channel</b>. Cholera toxin raises cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) and holds CFTR open, so chloride, sodium and water pour out: secretory diarrhea. In CF, CFTR is defective, so too little fluid is secreted and pancreatic ducts clog: the lecture's <b>'opposite of cholera'</b>.<br>Common mix-up: CF causes fatty stool from blocked enzymes, not watery secretory diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)","L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":22,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":228,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":28,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"190"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":51,"label":"Bile composition","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-27.jpg","cap":"Bile: salts 50%, phospholipids 40%, cholesterol 4%, bilirubin 2%","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G051-11.jpg","cap":"Bile parts: salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, bilirubin pigment, bicarbonate-rich fluid","where":"2025 discussion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-123-1","s":"Which is NOT a part of bile?","o":["A. Bilirubin","B. Cholesterol","C. Phospholipids","D. Amylase","E. Ions + Water"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Amylase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Amylase</b> is not in bile; salivary glands and the pancreas make it. Bile is about <b>50 percent bile salts, 40 percent phospholipids, 4 percent cholesterol, 2 percent bilirubin</b>, and the rest water and electrolytes.<br>A. Bilirubin: the bile pigment, about 2 percent.<br>B. Cholesterol: about 4 percent.<br>C. Phospholipids: about 40 percent.<br>E. Ions and water: the rest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 27 (Bile Salts:          50%)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":123,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"191"},{"id":"D007-137-1","s":"Bile contains all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["A. Bile salt","B. Phospholipids","C. Cholesterol","D. Bilirubin","E. Water","F. Electrolytes","G. Bile acids","H. Fatty acids"],"g":["H"],"k":["H"],"rv":false,"at":"Fatty acids","a":"H","why":"<b>H. Fatty acids</b> are not a bile component; they come from <b>digested dietary fat</b> in the gut lumen. Bile is bile salts (about 50 percent, with some bile acids), phospholipids (40), cholesterol (4), bilirubin (2), and water with electrolytes.<br>A to G. Bile salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, bilirubin, water, electrolytes and bile acids are all listed parts of bile.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 27 (Bile Salts:          50%)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":137,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":114,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-123-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":51,"label":"7α-hydroxylase: rate-limiting, feedback-inhibited","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D009-36.jpg","cap":"Pathway: cholesterol → 7α-hydroxylase → primary acids → bacteria → secondary","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G066-180.jpg","cap":"TA slide: rate-limiting 7α-hydroxylase; bile synthesis under negative feedback","where":"2024 TA review, slide 180"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-157-1","s":"WHAT IS THE RATE LIMITING ENZYME IN THE BIOSYNTHETIC PATHWAY OF THE ENTEROHEPATIC CIRCULATION?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase","a":null,"why":"<b>Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase</b> (7-alpha-hydroxylase) in the liver is the rate-limiting step from cholesterol to bile acids. Bile salts returning in the enterohepatic circulation <b>inhibit</b> it; when fewer return (ileal disease), it speeds up.<br>Common mix-up: HMG-CoA reductase (3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A reductase) limits cholesterol <b>synthesis</b>, not bile acid synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":157,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":51,"label":"Secondary bile acids: deoxycholic, lithocholic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Primary bile acids from cholesterol: cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D009-36.jpg","cap":"Gut bacteria turn cholic into deoxycholic, chenodeoxycholic into lithocholic acid","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-151-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING ARE SECONDARY BILE ACIDS? (MULTIPLE ANSWER CHOICES)","o":["A. CHOLIC ACID","B. CHENODEOXYCHOLIC ACID","C. DEOXYCHOLIC ACID","D. LITHOCHOLIC ACID"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C","D"],"rv":false,"at":"DEOXYCHOLIC ACID; LITHOCHOLIC ACID","a":"C,D","why":"<b>C and D</b>. <b>Deoxycholic and lithocholic acid</b> are secondary bile acids: gut <b>bacteria</b> remove a hydroxyl group from the primary acids.<br>A. Cholic acid: primary, made in the liver from cholesterol.<br>B. Chenodeoxycholic acid: primary, made in the liver from cholesterol.<br>Memory hook: deoxycholic comes from cholic; lithocholic comes from chenodeoxycholic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":151,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"192"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":51,"label":"Liver conjugates bile acids with glycine/taurine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-20.jpg","cap":"Cholic acid (bile acid) plus glycine makes glycocholic acid (bile salt)","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Bile acids from cholesterol, conjugated with glycine and taurine into salts","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D009-36.jpg","cap":"Full pathway: primary and secondary bile acids, then conjugation in liver","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-170-1","s":"3. Where does the conjugation of bile occur?","o":["A. Liver","B. Terminal ileum","C. Cecum","D. Gallbladder","E. Blood"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Liver</b>. Hepatocytes make bile acids from cholesterol and <b>conjugate them with glycine or taurine</b> to make bile salts.<br>B. Terminal ileum: reabsorbs bile salts.<br>C. Cecum: bacteria here deconjugate and make secondary bile acids; no conjugation.<br>D. Gallbladder: stores and concentrates bile.<br>E. Blood: portal blood carries returning bile salts; no conjugation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":170,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":169,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"193"},{"id":"D010-112-2","s":"Question 4 True/false: Bile acids are conjugated with cholesterol","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False</b>. Bile acids are <b>made from</b> cholesterol, then <b>conjugated with glycine or taurine</b> in the liver to make bile salts.<br>Common mix-up: cholesterol is the starting material, not the conjugating partner. The conjugating partners are the two amino acids glycine and taurine, which form the water-soluble side of the bile salt.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":112,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 28"},{"id":"D010-112-3","s":"Question 4 True/false: Cholesterol is used to make the water soluble form of Bile","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False</b>. Cholesterol is made into bile <b>acids</b> (cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid), which are poorly water-soluble. The water-soluble form, bile <b>salts</b>, comes only after the liver <b>conjugates</b> those acids with glycine or taurine.<br>Common mix-up: cholesterol is just the starting material; conjugation is the step that adds water solubility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":112,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 29"},{"id":"G052-11-1","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: What steps are involved in the biosynthesis of bile acids? What is a primary bile acid? What is a secondary bile acid? What are bile salts? What purpose is served by converting bile acids to bile salts?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Liver makes primary bile acids (cholic, chenodeoxycholic) from cholesterol (rate-limiting 7α-hydroxylase); intestinal bacteria dehydroxylate them to secondary bile acids (deoxycholic, lithocholic); conjugation with glycine or taurine makes bile salts, whose lower pK keeps them ionized and water-soluble at duodenal pH.","a":null,"why":"The liver makes <b>primary bile acids</b> (cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid) from cholesterol; the rate-limiting enzyme is <b>cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase</b>. Gut bacteria remove a hydroxyl group to make <b>secondary bile acids</b> (deoxycholic and lithocholic). The liver <b>conjugates</b> them with <b>glycine or taurine</b> into bile salts, whose low pKa (the pH where half is ionized) keeps them ionized and water-soluble in the duodenum.<br>Common mix-up: bacteria make secondary acids in the gut; conjugation happens in the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-170-1","kind":"harder","n":"C6 extra 30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":51,"label":"Bile salts: water-soluble and amphipathic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Bile salts water soluble; fat side touches fat, glycine/taurine side water","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Micelle drawing: water-loving heads outside, fat-loving tails inside","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D009-35.jpg","cap":"TA slide: conjugation lowers pKa, so bile salts ionize and dissolve","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-52-1","s":"True or false: bile salts are less soluble in the aqueous duodenum environment than bile acids","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False</b>. Bile <b>salts are more water-soluble</b> than bile acids. Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa (the pH at which half the molecules are ionized) from about seven to about one to four, so in the duodenum bile salts are <b>ionized and dissolve</b>, while bile acids stay un-ionized.<br>Common mix-up: conjugation exists to make bile salts more soluble, not less.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":52,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 31"},{"id":"D010-112-1","s":"Question 4 True/false: Bile salts would cross a lipid membrane","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False</b>. Bile salts are <b>ionized and water-soluble</b>, so they cannot diffuse through a lipid membrane. Micelle lipids diffuse into the cell, but the bile salts stay in the lumen until the terminal ileum takes them up on a <b>sodium–bile salt cotransporter</b>.<br>Common mix-up: un-ionized bile acids (made when bacteria deconjugate bile salts) can diffuse, which is why bacterial overgrowth loses bile acids too early.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":112,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":52,"label":"Bile salts: water-soluble and amphipathic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Bile salts water soluble; fat side touches fat, glycine/taurine side water","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Micelle drawing: water-loving heads outside, fat-loving tails inside","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D009-35.jpg","cap":"TA slide: conjugation lowers pKa, so bile salts ionize and dissolve","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-106-1","s":"Which of the following statements are correct regarding bile?","o":["A. Bile salts are insoluble in water because of their high pka","B. Bile salts are amphipathic in nature","C. Unoxidized bilirubin gives stool its dark color","D. All of the above"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts are amphipathic in nature","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile salts are amphipathic</b>: one side is fat-soluble and touches fat, the glycine or taurine side is water-soluble and touches water, so they coat fat droplets and form micelles.<br>A. False: conjugation gives bile salts a <b>low</b> pKa (the pH where half is ionized: about one to four), so they are ionized and water-soluble.<br>C. False: stool’s brown color comes from <b>stercobilin</b>, the oxidized product of urobilinogen.<br>D. Only B is true.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":106,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":106,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":274,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"194"},{"id":"D015-153-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: BILE SALTS ARE AMPHIPATHIC","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"FACT","a":"A","why":"<b>Fact</b>. Bile salts are <b>amphipathic</b>: a fat-soluble steroid side touches fat and a water-soluble glycine or taurine side touches water. That lets them emulsify fat and build micelles, and makes them more soluble than bile acids in the duodenum.<br>Common mix-up: amphipathic does not mean fat-soluble only; it means one side of each kind.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":153,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-106-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 33"},{"id":"D010-112-4","s":"Question 4 True/false: The fat soluble side of bile interacts with fat in the intestines","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"True","a":"A","why":"<b>True</b>. Bile salts are amphipathic: the <b>fat-soluble (steroid) side touches fat</b> and the water-soluble glycine or taurine side faces water. That is how they break a large fat blob into small micelles.<br>Common mix-up: the water-soluble side faces the watery lumen, not the fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":112,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-106-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 34"},{"id":"G064-112-1","s":"Question 4 True/false:","o":["A. Bile salts would cross a lipid membrane","B. Bile acids are conjugated with cholesterol","C. Cholesterol is used to make the water soluble form of Bile","D. The fat soluble side of bile interacts with fat in the intestines"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A False; B False; C False; D True (only the last statement is true)","a":null,"why":"Only <b>D is true</b>.<br>A. False: bile salts are ionized and water-soluble, so they do not cross lipid membranes; the ileum takes them up on a sodium–bile salt cotransporter.<br>B. False: bile acids are conjugated with <b>glycine or taurine</b>, not cholesterol.<br>C. False: cholesterol makes bile <b>acids</b>; conjugation makes the water-soluble <b>salts</b>.<br>D. True: the fat-soluble side touches fat and the water-soluble side touches water (amphipathic).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":112,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-106-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 35"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":52,"label":"Bile salts emulsify fat","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Bile salts break a large fat blob into small droplets","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Emulsification: large fat blob to small micelle","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-41-1","s":"Which of these is most DIRECTLY involve in fat digestion?","o":["A. a. Bile acids","B. b. Bile salts","C. c. Cholesterol","D. d. Phospholipids","E. e. Bilirubin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"b. Bile salts","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile salts</b> emulsify fat and carry its digestion products in micelles. Conjugation with glycine or taurine makes them water-soluble, so they work in the duodenum far better than bile acids.<br>A. Bile acids: unconjugated, mostly un-ionized in the duodenum, so poorly soluble.<br>C. Cholesterol: raw material for bile acids and itself a lipid to absorb.<br>D. Phospholipids: help form micelles, but bile salts do the main work.<br>E. Bilirubin: a waste pigment with no role in fat digestion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":41,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-191-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 36"},{"id":"D067-81-1","s":"Which of the following is a major component of bile and correctly matched to its function?","o":["A. Bilirubin — fat emulsification","B. Bile salts — fat digestion and absorption","C. Cholesterol — protein digestion","D. Phospholipids — bilirubin conjugation","E. Bicarbonate — bile pigment production"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts — fat digestion and absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile salts: fat digestion and absorption.</b> <b>Bile salts</b> are about half of bile's solids. They emulsify fat for lipase and form the micelles that carry the products to the cell.<br>A. Bilirubin: a waste pigment from hemoglobin; it colors bile and stool and does not emulsify fat.<br>C. Cholesterol: bile is its route of excretion; it digests no protein.<br>D. Phospholipids: they help form micelles; bilirubin is conjugated by a liver enzyme.<br>E. Bicarbonate: it buffers acid; bile pigment comes from heme breakdown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 27 (Bile Salts:          50%)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":81,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-191-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 37"},{"id":"D004-191-1","s":"Bile is produced by the liver continuously. (Which of the following is true?)","o":["A. bile functions to catalyze digestive processes in a manner similar to enzymes","B. bile salts emulsify large droplets of triglycerides","C. bile salts reduce the surface area of droplets of triglycerides","D. 95% of bile salts are lost in the feces"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"bile salts emulsify large droplets of triglycerides","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile salts emulsify large triglyceride droplets</b> into small ones, which <b>increases surface area</b> for pancreatic lipase.<br>A. Bile is a detergent, not a catalyst; it has no enzyme action.<br>C. Emulsification raises surface area; it does not reduce it.<br>D. About 95 percent of bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and recycled; only about 600 milligrams a day are lost in stool.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":54,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":191,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":44,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":41,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"195"},{"id":"D067-79-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the role of bile in digestion?","o":["A. It emulsifies fats to increase surface area for lipase action","B. It digests triglycerides directly","C. It neutralizes acid in the duodenum","D. It activates pancreatic lipase","E. It hydrolyzes peptide bonds"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It emulsifies fats to increase surface area for lipase action","a":"A","why":"<b>A. It emulsifies fats to increase surface area for lipase action.</b> Bile salts coat large fat drops and break them into small droplets. This digests nothing; it gives pancreatic lipase far more surface to work on.<br>B. Digests triglycerides directly: that is lipase; bile has no enzyme action.<br>C. Neutralizes duodenal acid: mainly the job of pancreatic bicarbonate; the bicarbonate in bile is a small helper, not bile's main role.<br>D. Activates lipase: lipase is secreted already active.<br>E. Hydrolyzes peptide bonds: that is the proteases, such as trypsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":79,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-191-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 38"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":53,"label":"CCK contracts gallbladder, relaxes sphincter of Oddi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-25.jpg","cap":"CCK from I cells contracts gallbladder and relaxes sphincter of Oddi","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"Actions of CCK: gallbladder contraction, Oddi relaxation, pancreatic enzyme release","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D009-34.jpg","cap":"Bile circuit diagram: CCK acts on gallbladder and sphincter of Oddi","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-160-1","s":"What hormone in the biliary tract is responsible for stimulating gallbladder contraction to release stored bile and also relaxing the sphincter of Oddi?","o":["A. Secretin","B. CCK","C. Gastrin","D. Somatostatin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"B","why":"<b>B. CCK</b> (cholecystokinin), from duodenal I cells, <b>contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b>, so bile flows into the duodenum.<br>A. Secretin: adds bicarbonate to pancreatic juice and bile; does not empty the gallbladder.<br>C. Gastrin: stimulates gastric acid.<br>D. Somatostatin: inhibits gallbladder contraction and most gut secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":160,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":159,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 39"},{"id":"D008-109-1","s":"Which of the following hormones has a major effect on the gallbladder?","o":["A. Insulin","B. CCK","C. Somatostatin","D. Secretin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"B","why":"<b>B. CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) is the main gallbladder hormone: it <b>contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b>, so bile spurts into the duodenum.<br>A. Insulin: islet beta-cell hormone for blood sugar.<br>C. Somatostatin: inhibits, rather than drives, gallbladder emptying.<br>D. Secretin: adds bicarbonate and water to bile in the ducts but does not empty the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":109,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":109,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":277,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 40"},{"id":"D036-121-1","s":"What is the effect of CCK?","o":["A. Concentrates bile","B. Turns cholesterol into colic acid","C. Gallbladder contraction","D. Sphincter of Oddi contraction","E. Pancreatic secretion"],"g":["C","E"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder contraction","a":"C","why":"<b>C and E</b>. CCK (cholecystokinin) <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> (and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi) and <b>stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion</b> from acinar cells.<br>A. The gallbladder concentrates bile on its own by absorbing water and salts; CCK does not do this.<br>B. Cholesterol becomes cholic acid in the liver via 7α-hydroxylase; not a CCK action.<br>D. CCK relaxes, not contracts, the sphincter of Oddi.","ex":"","fl":["E (pancreatic secretion) is also a true CCK action (pancreatic enzyme release, G040:11; CCK acts at acini, G044:24); C is the source's intended answer. (Option D appears as 'Sphincter of Oddi concentration' on question slide 121 – typo for 'contraction', corrected on slide 122.)"],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":121,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 41"},{"id":"G063-7-28","s":"A patient’s abdominal pain is relieved after eating. Which of the following is true regarding contraction of the gallbladder following a meal?","o":["a. It is inhibited by a fat rich meal","b. It is inhibited by the presence of amino acids in the duodenum","c. It is stimulated by pancreatic enzymes delivered to duodenal bulb","d. It occurs in response to cholecystokinin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It occurs in response to cholecystokinin","a":"d","why":"<b>D. It occurs in response to CCK</b> (cholecystokinin). Fat and amino acids in the duodenum release CCK from <b>I cells</b>; CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.<br>A. A fat-rich meal is the strongest stimulus, not an inhibitor.<br>B. Amino acids also release CCK, so they promote contraction.<br>C. Pancreatic enzymes do not trigger it; CCK triggers both enzyme release and gallbladder contraction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":7,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 42"},{"id":"D010-110-1","s":"Absence of CCK would cause which of these? (could be more than one)","o":["A. Increased gall bladder secretions","B. Steatorrhea","C. Increased sphincter of Oddi tone","D. Increased I-cell secretions","E. Decrease in microdroplet micelles"],"g":["B","C","E"],"k":["B","C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea; Increased sphincter of Oddi tone; Decrease in microdroplet micelles","a":"B,C,E","why":"<b>B, C and E</b>. Without CCK (cholecystokinin) the gallbladder does not contract and the <b>sphincter of Oddi stays tight</b>, so little bile (and less pancreatic lipase) reaches the gut. Without bile, fat cannot form <b>micelles</b>, so it ends up in the stool (<b>steatorrhea</b>).<br>A. Gallbladder output falls, not rises.<br>D. I cells are the source of CCK; nothing makes them secrete more.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":110,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":110,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 43"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":53,"label":"Fat, amino acids → I-cell CCK → bile","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-25.jpg","cap":"Fat touches duodenum → CCK from I cells → gallbladder contracts → microdroplets","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G040-10.jpg","cap":"CCK stimuli: monoglycerides, free fatty acids, amino acids and peptides","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G066-177.jpg","cap":"TA slide: liver makes bile, gallbladder stores; I cells in duodenum, jejunum","where":"2024 TA review, slide 177"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-175-1","s":"5. Fill in the blanks: Bile is made by _______ and is stored in _______. I cells are located in ______. CCK leads to contraction of ____ and relaxation of _____.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile is made by the liver and is stored in the gallbladder. I cells are located in the jejunum and duodenum. CCK leads to contraction of gallbladder and relaxation of sphincter of oddi.","a":null,"why":"Bile is made by the <b>liver</b> and stored in the <b>gallbladder</b>. I cells are in the <b>duodenum and jejunum</b>. CCK (cholecystokinin) <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> and <b>relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the gallbladder only stores and concentrates bile, it does not make it; and CCK relaxes, not contracts, the sphincter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L54 slide 28 (Stores bile)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":175,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":174,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 44"},{"id":"G066-173-1","s":"4. A 45 y/o female presents to the ER with right upper quadrant pain. While collecting a detailed history you learn her diet consists of a lot of cheese and bacon. You are worried she may have cholecystitis. You think back to your physiology class and remember there is a series of events that leads to the secretion of bile from the gallbladder ejecting bile. Which of the following is true about this series of events?","o":["A. CCK stimulated the relaxation of the gallbladder and contraction of the sphincter of oddi","B. Ejection of bile begins within 15 minutes after a meal","C. CCK is secreted by alpha cells of the pancreas","D. CCK is secreted by the I cells in response to amino acids","E. I cells are in the ileum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK is secreted by the I cells in response to amino acids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. CCK (cholecystokinin) is secreted by I cells in response to amino acids</b> (and fatty acids). CCK then contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.<br>A. Reversed: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter.<br>B. Bile ejection starts within about 30 minutes of a meal in the course sources, not 15.<br>C. Pancreatic alpha cells make glucagon, not CCK.<br>E. I cells are in the duodenum and jejunum, not the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":173,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":172,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 45"},{"id":"F1203-78-1","s":"Which of the following lists the correct order by which the ingestion of fatty food products elicits gastrointestinal stimulation and secretion(s)?","o":["A. CCK induces gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi - Fatty acids enter the duodenum - CCK released from I Cells - Bile is released into the duodenum and emulsifies fat droplets into micro droplets","B. Fatty acids enter the duodenum - CCK released from I Cells - CCK induces gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi - Bile is released into the duodenum and emulsifies fat droplets into micro droplets","C. Bile is released into the duodenum and emulsifies fat droplets into micro droplets - Fatty acids enter the duodenum - CCK released from I Cells - CCK induces gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fatty acids enter the duodenum - CCK released from I Cells - CCK induces gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi - Bile is released into the duodenum and emulsifies fat droplets into micro droplets","a":"B","why":"<b>B</b>. Fatty acids enter the duodenum → <b>I cells release CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) → CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi → bile enters and <b>emulsifies fat into microdroplets</b>.<br>A. Starts with CCK acting before anything has released it.<br>C. Starts with bile release, which needs CCK first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":78,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 46"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":53,"label":"Gallbladder stores and concentrates bile","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-28.jpg","cap":"The gallbladder stores bile and concentrates it by reabsorbing water and electrolytes","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D067-83-1","s":"Which statement best describes the role of the gallbladder?","o":["A. Synthesizes bile salts from cholesterol","B. Concentrates and stores bile","C. Neutralizes duodenal acid","D. Secretes digestive enzymes","E. Produces intrinsic factor"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Concentrates and stores bile","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Concentrates and stores bile.</b> The liver makes bile all the time. Between meals the <b>gallbladder</b> stores it and reabsorbs water and electrolytes, which concentrates it. CCK (cholecystokinin) then contracts the gallbladder after a meal.<br>A. Makes bile salts from cholesterol: the liver does this.<br>C. Neutralizes duodenal acid: bicarbonate from the pancreatic ducts does this.<br>D. Secretes digestive enzymes: pancreatic acinar cells do; bile has no enzymes.<br>E. Makes intrinsic factor: parietal cells of the stomach do.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 28 (Stores bile)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":83,"f":"D067"}],"tier":1,"n":"196"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":53,"label":"Secretin dilutes bile; CCK relaxes Oddi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-13.jpg","cap":"Secretin actions include biliary bicarbonate production and release","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G044-25.jpg","cap":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/D009-34.jpg","cap":"Secretin adds ions and water to bile; CCK empties gallbladder","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-50-1","s":"Select the true statement.","o":["A. Signaling from Ach and CCK help to potentiate the release of Na and HCO3","B. Secretin stimulates the dilution of bile","C. CCK stimulates the contraction of the gallbladder and sphincter of oddi","D. Bile salts, once digested are circulated back to the liver via the venous system","E. A and B","F. A and C","G. A, B, and D","H. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"A and C","a":"F","why":"<b>E. A and B</b>. A is true: acetylcholine (ACh) and CCK (cholecystokinin) <b>potentiate secretin’s</b> sodium-bicarbonate fluid. B is true: secretin makes bile-duct cells add <b>bicarbonate and water</b>, diluting bile.<br>C. False: CCK contracts the gallbladder but <b>relaxes</b> the sphincter of Oddi.<br>D. False: bile salts are not digested; the terminal ileum reabsorbs them intact into portal blood.<br>F, G, H: each includes the false C or D.","ex":"","fl":["Statement C is false: CCK contracts the gallbladder but RELAXES the sphincter of Oddi (G044:25, G040:11). With B and D false (per the source's own notes), only statement A is true, so the answer is A, not 'A and C'.","The source keyed F (A and C) and the page accepted A alone, but CCK relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (C is false) and secretin does dilute bile by adding bicarbonate and water (B is true), so the answer is E (A and B)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":50,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Sodium–bile salt cotransporter in ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum returns bile to the liver through the hepatic portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G066-180.jpg","cap":"TA slide: sodium–bile salt cotransporters move ileal bile salts into portal blood","where":"2024 TA review, slide 180"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-26-1","s":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile salts will involve which one of the following steps","o":["a. Bile salts are transported from duodenum into liver","b. Portal blood transports bile salts from bile duct to liver","c. Bile salts are transported from intestinal lumen to blood by Na+-bile salt cotransporters","d. All of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts are transported from intestinal lumen to blood by Na+-bile salt cotransporters","a":"c","why":"<b>C</b>. In the terminal ileum, bile salts move from the lumen into blood on a <b>sodium–bile salt cotransporter</b>; portal blood then carries them back to the liver.<br>A. They are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, not the duodenum.<br>B. Portal blood brings them from the ileum, not from the bile duct.<br>D. Only C is correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":43,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":3,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":26,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":182,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":35,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":31,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"197"},{"id":"G066-178-1","s":"6. Which kind of transporters are used in the ileum to transport bile salts from the intestinal lumen to the portal blood?","o":["A. Na+ / bile salt cotransporter","B. K+ / bile salt cotransporter","C. Ca 2+ / bile salt cotransporter","D. Cl- / bile salt cotransporter"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Na+ / bile salt cotransporter","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sodium–bile salt cotransporter</b>. In the terminal ileum, sodium moving into the cell down its gradient carries bile salts with it (secondary active transport); the bile salts then go into portal blood and back to the liver.<br>B. Potassium is not the driving ion.<br>C. Calcium is not the driving ion.<br>D. Chloride is not the driving ion; almost all intestinal cotransport rides on sodium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":178,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":177,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-26-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Bile salts reabsorbed at terminal ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Diagram: terminal ileum sends bile salts back to liver via portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G044-29.jpg","cap":"Bile salts re-absorbed at terminal ileum; 600 mg lost daily","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"TA slide: ileum is the site of B12 and bile salt absorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-164-1","s":"A 34 y/o presents to the ER complaining of abdominal pain. You look in his chart and see he has a history of Crohn’s disease. You remember that Crohn’s can impact the area of the GI tract where bile salts are reabsorbed. Where are bile salts reabsorbed?","o":["A. Proximal Ileum","B. Distal Jejunum","C. Descending Colon","D. Distal Jejunum","E. Cecum","F. Distal Ileum"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"Distal Ileum","a":"F","why":"<b>F. Distal ileum</b>. Bile salts are reabsorbed in the <b>terminal (distal) ileum</b>, the part Crohn disease most often damages.<br>A. Proximal ileum: too early; the transporter is in the terminal part.<br>B and D. Distal jejunum: absorbs fat, not bile salts.<br>C. Descending colon: absorbs water and sodium.<br>E. Cecum: holds bacteria; no bile-salt reabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":164,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":163,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"198"},{"id":"G066-158-1","s":"In what part of the intestines are bile salts reabsorbed back to the liver from?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ileum</b> (its terminal part). Bile salts stay in the lumen through the duodenum and jejunum to do their fat work, then the <b>terminal ileum</b> reabsorbs them into portal blood.<br>A. Duodenum: where bile enters; it absorbs iron and calcium.<br>B. Jejunum: absorbs most fat and sugar, but not bile salts.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: absorbs water; bile salts reaching the colon cause diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":158,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":157,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-164-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 49"},{"id":"D008-104-1","s":"What is one of the major functions of bile and what part of the GI tract is the bile reabsorbed?","o":["A. Solubilize and absorption of carbohydrates; duodenum","B. Digestion of proteins; terminal ileum","C. Solubilize and absorption of lipids; terminal ileum","D. Solubilize and absorption of lipids; Ascending colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Solubilize and absorption of lipids; terminal ileum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lipid solubilization and absorption; terminal ileum</b>. Bile salts emulsify fat and carry its products in micelles; after the fat is absorbed, the <b>terminal ileum</b> reabsorbs the bile salts.<br>A. Bile does not handle carbohydrates, and the duodenum is not the reabsorption site.<br>B. Proteases, not bile, digest protein.<br>D. Right function, wrong place: the colon does not reabsorb bile salts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":104,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":104,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":272,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-164-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 50"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Enterohepatic circulation, step by step","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-29.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder contracts → micelles → fat absorbed → bile salts reabsorbed at ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Bile salts: 24 g/day secreted, over 95% returned in portal blood","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/D009-34.jpg","cap":"Numbered circuit: liver, gallbladder, duodenum, ileum, portal circulation","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-166-1","s":"2. Put the following events of the enterohepatic circulation in the correct order. Bile touches fat; Lipase dissolves fat; Gallbladder contracts; Bile salts reabsorbed at terminal ileum; Fat absorbed; Fats turn into micelles","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1 Gallbladder contracts → 2 Bile touches fat → 3 Fats turn into micelles → 4 Lipase dissolves fat → 5 Fat absorbed → 6 Bile salts reabsorbed at terminal ileum","a":null,"why":"Order: <b>gallbladder contracts → bile touches fat → fats turn into micelles → lipase digests the fat → fat absorbed → bile salts reabsorbed at the terminal ileum</b>. Each step needs the one before, so a break anywhere (no CCK, meaning cholecystokinin; no lipase; no terminal ileum) causes fat malabsorption.<br>Common mix-up: bile salts are recycled last, in the terminal ileum, after the fat has been absorbed upstream.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":166,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":165,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 51"},{"id":"G052-11-2","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: Describe the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi; after acting in the duodenum/jejunum, bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum by Na+-bile salt cotransport into portal blood and extracted by the liver (~95% recycled; ~25% of the pool replaced daily).","a":null,"why":"Liver makes bile salts → gallbladder stores and concentrates bile → <b>CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi → bile salts form micelles for fat absorption in the duodenum and jejunum → the <b>terminal ileum</b> reabsorbs them on a sodium–bile salt cotransporter → <b>portal blood</b> returns them to the liver (about 95 percent recycled).<br>Common mix-up: bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, not where the fat itself is absorbed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 52"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"7α-hydroxylase: rate-limiting, feedback-inhibited","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D009-36.jpg","cap":"Pathway: cholesterol → 7α-hydroxylase → primary acids → bacteria → secondary","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G066-180.jpg","cap":"TA slide: rate-limiting 7α-hydroxylase; bile synthesis under negative feedback","where":"2024 TA review, slide 180"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-56-1","s":"What happens when bile salts are in high concentration in the liver?","o":["A. CCK is released","B. Na-bile salt cotransporters are downregulated in the terminal ileum","C. Cholesterol 7alpha-hydroxylase is inhibited","D. All of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholesterol 7alpha-hydroxylase is inhibited","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase is inhibited</b>. This liver enzyme is the rate-limiting step from cholesterol to bile acids, and returning bile salts <b>switch it off</b>. When bile salts are lost (ileal resection), synthesis rises.<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin) is released by fat and amino acids in the duodenum, not by liver bile salts.<br>B. Liver bile-salt levels control liver synthesis; they do not act on the ileal sodium–bile salt cotransporter.<br>D. Only C is correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L56 slide 45 (Q2. A patient with colon cancer has the entire colon including the terminal ileu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":56,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"199"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Ileal resection → bile salts lost in stool","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Removing the terminal ileum interferes with bile re-circulation","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"No fat absorption, no A, D, E, K; bile insufficiency lowers them","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"TA slide: ileal resection lowers bile salt and B12 absorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-114-1","s":"A 60 year old man needs to have his distal ileum removed. What side effect do you have to warn him about?","o":["A. Constipation","B. Diabetes","C. Increased bile in the stool","D. Decrease in CCK","E. Increase G-cell activity"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased bile in the stool","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased bile in the stool</b>. The <b>distal ileum</b> reabsorbs bile salts; without it they pass to the colon and out in the stool, causing diarrhea and later fat malabsorption.<br>A. Constipation: the opposite; bile salts in the colon cause diarrhea.<br>B. Diabetes: insulin comes from the pancreas.<br>D. CCK (cholecystokinin) comes from duodenal and jejunal I cells, which remain.<br>E. G cells sit in the stomach antrum and are unaffected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":114,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":114,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":131,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-73-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 53"},{"id":"D066-73-1","s":"A 48-year-old man with a history of Crohn disease undergoes surgical resection of the terminal ileum. Several months later, he presents with chronic diarrhea, greasy stools, and unintentional weight loss. Laboratory studies reveal deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins. Which of the following best explains the underlying mechanism responsible for his symptoms?","o":["A. Decreased bile acid synthesis by the liver due to loss of cholecystokinin stimulation.","B. Increased bile salt reabsorption in the jejunum, resulting in reduced bile delivery to the duodenum.","C. Increased bile salt recycling through the portal circulation, resulting in excessive micelle formation.","D. Decreased pancreatic lipase secretion due to impaired secretin release.","E. Impaired bile salt reabsorption in the terminal ileum, resulting in increased bile salt loss and decreased recycling."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired bile salt reabsorption in the terminal ileum, resulting in increased bile salt loss and decreased recycling.","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Impaired bile salt reabsorption in the terminal ileum.</b> The <b>terminal ileum</b> returns more than 95 percent of bile salts to the liver. Without it they are lost in stool, the pool shrinks, and too few micelles form. Fat and vitamins A, D, E and K are then not absorbed.<br>A. Bile acid synthesis rises, not falls, and CCK (cholecystokinin) does not control it.<br>B. The jejunum does not reabsorb bile salts.<br>C. Recycling falls, so fewer micelles form.<br>D. The pancreas is normal, and secretin drives bicarbonate, not lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Physio GI TA office hours 11/5/26","sl":73,"f":"D066"}],"tier":1,"n":"200"},{"id":"D040-133-1","s":"A patient with Crohn’s disease has to have a complete surgical resection of the terminal ileum. Which of the following would be most consistent with his condition post-op?","o":["A. Patient has decreased absorption of bile salts","B. Patient has decreased secretion of amylase","C. Digestive enzyme deficiency & chronic pancreatitis","D. Glucose malabsorption with recurrent hypoglycemia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Patient has decreased absorption of bile salts","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Decreased absorption of bile salts</b>. The <b>terminal ileum</b> reabsorbs bile salts, so without it they are lost in stool; the pool shrinks and fat absorption suffers.<br>B. Amylase comes from the pancreas, which is untouched.<br>C. Pancreatic enzymes and pancreatitis do not depend on the ileum.<br>D. Glucose is absorbed mostly in the duodenum and jejunum, so it stays normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":133,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D066-73-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 54"},{"id":"D007-135-1","s":"Crohn’s Disease is an inflammatory disease that classically targets the terminal ileum. In patients with Crohn’s, you would expect that they have a malabsorption of which of the following:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Proteins","C. Carbohydrates","D. Vitamin C","E. Iron"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin D</b>. Terminal-ileum disease loses bile salts, so <b>micelles</b> form poorly and fat plus the fat-soluble vitamins <b>A, D, E and K</b> are malabsorbed.<br>B. Proteins: digested and absorbed in the upper small intestine.<br>C. Carbohydrates: absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum.<br>D. Vitamin C: water-soluble; absorbed without bile.<br>E. Iron: absorbed in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":135,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"201"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Ileal resection → bile salt loss, steatorrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: removing terminal ileum breaks bile recirculation (drawn loop)","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/D015-161.jpg","cap":"Enterohepatic circulation: ileal bile salts return via portal blood to liver","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 161"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"Ileal resection: less B12 and bile salt absorption, fat malabsorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"G064-146-1","s":"322. A 57-year-old woman undergoes resection of the terminal ileum as part of treatment for her chronic inflammatory bowel disease. Removal of the terminal ileum will most likely result in which of the following?","o":["A. Decreased glucose absorption","B. Decreased water content of the feces","C. Increased bile acid concentration in the enterohepatic circulation","D. Increased excretion of fatty acids","E. Increased iron absorption"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased excretion of fatty acids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Increased excretion of fatty acids.</b> The terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts. Without it, bile salts are lost in stool, the liver cannot keep up, <b>micelles fail, and fat is lost in stool (steatorrhea)</b>.<br>A. Glucose is absorbed long before the terminal ileum.<br>B. Feces get wetter, not drier: bile salts and fat in the colon cause fluid secretion.<br>C. The bile acid pool in the enterohepatic circulation falls, not rises.<br>E. Iron is absorbed in the duodenum; it does not increase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 9 (Ileum)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":146,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"202"},{"id":"D067-85-1","s":"Which of the following occurs after ileal resection?","o":["A. Decreased bile salt synthesis","B. Increased bile salt reabsorption","C. Steatorrhea due to loss of bile salt recycling","D. Enhanced enterohepatic circulation","E. Increased micelle formation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea due to loss of bile salt recycling","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Steatorrhea due to loss of bile salt recycling.</b> The terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts. After its removal they are lost in stool, the pool shrinks, micelles fail, and fat stays in the stool (<b>steatorrhea</b>).<br>A. Decreased synthesis: the liver makes more bile acids, because fewer return to hold synthesis back; it still cannot keep up.<br>B. Increased reabsorption: the reabsorbing segment is gone.<br>D. Enhanced enterohepatic circulation: the circuit is broken.<br>E. Increased micelle formation: fewer bile salts mean fewer micelles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 45 (Q2. A patient with colon cancer has the entire colon including the terminal ileu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":85,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G064-146-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 55"},{"id":"G052-11-5","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: Paul has “bile acid diarrhea.” Why do bile acids cause diarrhea? (Big hint: They stimulate colonic Cl– secretion.) Why do not healthy persons have bile acid diarrhea?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile salts reaching the colon stimulate colonic Cl- secretion, with Na+ and water following (secretory diarrhea); normally they are reabsorbed in the ileum before reaching the colon.","a":null,"why":"<b>Bile salts in the colon stimulate chloride (Cl-) secretion</b>; sodium and water follow Cl- into the lumen, a <b>secretory diarrhea</b>. Healthy people don't get it because the <b>terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts</b> (enterohepatic circulation) before they reach the colon.<br>Common mix-up: cholestyramine binds bile salts and stops the diarrhea, but fat malabsorption (steatorrhea) remains because the bile salt pool is still too small.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 9 (Ileum)","L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G064-146-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 56"},{"id":"G063-7-27","s":"A 72-year-old woman presents with diarrhea, abdominal pain, and flushing - a classic case of “serotonin syndrome’’. The urinary excretion of the serotonin metabolite is elevated. A presence of tumor in the terminal ileum is confirmed on abdominal CT. Terminal ilectomy will most likely result in which of the following?","o":["a. A decrease in absorption of amino acids","b. An increase in the water content of the feces","c. An increase in the concentration of bile acid in the enterohepatic circulation","d. A decrease in the fat content of the feces","e. An increase in the absorption of iron"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"An increase in the water content of the feces","a":"b","why":"<b>B. An increase in the water content of the feces.</b> After terminal ileectomy, bile salts are not reabsorbed and spill into the colon, where they <b>stimulate chloride (Cl-) secretion</b>; sodium and water follow (bile acid diarrhea). Unabsorbed fat adds to it.<br>A. Amino acids are absorbed mostly in the jejunum.<br>C. The bile acid pool falls, not rises.<br>D. Fecal fat rises (steatorrhea), not falls.<br>E. Iron absorption (duodenum) does not rise.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 9 (Ileum)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":7,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"G064-146-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C6 extra 57"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Cholestyramine binds bile salts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Bile salts escaping ileal reabsorption reach the colon and leave in feces","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G052-11-6","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: Cholestyramine is a cationic resin that binds bile salts. Propose a mechanism that explains its effectiveness in treating Paul’s diarrhea.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Cholestyramine binds bile salts in the lumen so they cannot stimulate colonic Cl- secretion (it also depletes the bile salt pool, worsening fat absorption).","a":null,"why":"Cholestyramine <b>binds bile salts</b> in the gut lumen. Unabsorbed bile salts reaching the colon make it <b>secrete chloride and water</b> (secretory diarrhea); bound bile salts cannot, so the diarrhea improves.<br>Common mix-up: it does not fix fat malabsorption. Bound bile salts leave in stool, the bile-salt pool shrinks further, and the steatorrhea persists.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C6 extra 58"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":54,"label":"Fecal bile acid loss = cholesterol loss","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Bile acid synthesis 0.4 g/day matches fecal loss 0.4 g/day","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G044-29.jpg","cap":"About 600 mg of bile salts lost in stool per day","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-148-1","s":"342. A 56-year-old man presents with postprandial diarrhea persisting since an ileal resection. The gastric surgeon suspects bile acid malabsorption. Which of the following best describes bile acid function?","o":["A. They are essentially water insoluble.","B. The majority of bile acids are absorbed by passive diffusion.","C. Glycine conjugates are more soluble than taurine conjugates.","D. The amount lost in the stool each day represents the daily loss of cholesterol.","E. The bile acid–dependent fraction of bile is stimulated by the hormone secretin."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The amount lost in the stool each day represents the daily loss of cholesterol.","a":"D","why":"<b>D</b>. Bile acids are made from cholesterol, so the bile acid lost in stool each day (about 600 milligrams in the lecture) is a <b>main route of cholesterol loss</b>; the liver replaces it from cholesterol.<br>A. They are amphipathic and, once conjugated, water-soluble.<br>B. Most are taken up actively on the ileal <b>sodium–bile salt cotransporter</b>.<br>C. Taurine conjugates are more soluble (lower pKa, the pH where half is ionized) than glycine conjugates.<br>E. Secretin drives the bile-acid-<b>independent</b> part (duct bicarbonate and water).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":148,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":148,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"203"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":55,"label":"Cellular route: apical in, basolateral out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-7.jpg","cap":"Transcellular path through apical carriers and basolateral GLUT2; paracellular through junctions","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G045-4.jpg","cap":"Cellular route: cross apical membrane, extruded at basolateral membrane","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-142-1","s":"How are nutrients primarily absorbed in the small intestine?","o":["A. Movement through lateral intercellular spaces","B. Extrusion across the basolateral membrane","C. Cellular uptake through the basolateral membrane","D. Passage through tight junctions into the bloodstream","E. Digestive enzyme breakdown in the luminal membrane"],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cellular uptake through the basolateral membrane","a":"C","why":"<b>B. Extrusion across the basolateral membrane.</b> Nutrients are absorbed mainly by the cellular (transcellular) route: in across the <b>apical</b> (lumen-facing) membrane, through the cytoplasm, then <b>out across the basolateral membrane</b> into the blood. B names a true step of that route.<br>A. Lateral intercellular spaces: the paracellular route (between cells); minor for nutrients.<br>C. Cellular uptake through the basolateral membrane: the right route but the wrong membrane. Uptake happens at the apical membrane; the basolateral membrane is the exit.<br>D. Through tight junctions: also the paracellular route.<br>E. Enzyme breakdown at the luminal membrane: that is digestion (by brush-border enzymes), not absorption.","ex":"","fl":["Intended answer = the cellular (transcellular) route, but option C misnames the membrane: nutrients are taken up across the APICAL membrane and only extruded across the basolateral membrane (G045:4; the source's own explanation says so). B describes the extrusion step of the same route.","Source keyed C, but C puts nutrient uptake at the wrong membrane: nutrients enter across the apical (lumen) membrane and leave across the basolateral membrane. B, the exit step of that same cellular route, is the only true option."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 4 (Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":142,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":55,"label":"Folds of Kerckring (circular folds)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-8.jpg","cap":"Circular folds of Kerckring carry villi; villus lined by epithelial cells","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G045-9.jpg","cap":"Endoscopic view of the circular folds of the small intestine","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-169-1","s":"THE SURFACE OF THE SMALL INTESTINE ARE ARRANGED IN LONGITUDINAL FOLDS CALLED:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"FOLDS OF KERCKRING","a":null,"why":"<b>Folds of Kerckring</b> (plicae circulares): folds of mucosa and submucosa that roughly <b>triple the surface area</b>. <b>Villi</b> project from them, and <b>microvilli</b> (the brush border) cover the villus cells.<br>Common mix-up: these folds actually run <b>circularly</b> around the lumen, as the lecture picture labels them; 'longitudinal' in the stem is a slip. Longitudinal folds are the stomach's rugae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)","L55 slide 10 (Kerckring)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":169,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":55,"label":"Villi longest in duodenum, shortest in ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-12.jpg","cap":"Villi and microvilli decrease: duodenum > jejunum > ileum","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G045-11.jpg","cap":"Villus structure: capillary bed, lacteal, absorptive epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-79-1","s":"Where are the villi shortest?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Terminal Ileum","D. Colon","E. Anus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Terminal Ileum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Terminal ileum.</b> Villi are <b>longest in the duodenum</b> (most digestion and absorption) and <b>shortest in the terminal ileum</b>, which mainly absorbs bile salts and vitamin B12.<br>A. Duodenum: longest villi.<br>B. Jejunum: in between.<br>D. Colon: has crypts but no villi.<br>E. Anus: no villi at all.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 11 (Villi)","L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":79,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"204"},{"id":"D015-171-1","s":"FILL IN THE BLANK: VILLI ARE LONGEST/SHORTEST IN THE DUODENUM AND LONGEST/SHORTEST IN THE TERMINAL ILEUM","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"LONGEST IN THE DUODENUM; SHORTEST IN THE TERMINAL ILEUM","a":null,"why":"Villi are <b>longest in the duodenum</b>, where most digestion and absorption happen, and <b>shortest in the terminal ileum</b>. Villus height and number fall from duodenum to jejunum to ileum.<br>Common mix-up: goblet cells run the other way, fewest in the duodenum and most in the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)","L55 slide 11 (Villi)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":171,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-79-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":55,"label":"Brush border = microvilli, not villi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-13.jpg","cap":"Zoom series: villi, then epithelial cells, then microvilli forming the brush border","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G045-8.jpg","cap":"Small intestine: circular folds, villi, microvilli/brush border","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-123-1","s":"4) Which of the following is untrue?","o":["A. Galactose and glucose are both absorbed via the same transport protein","B. The body can only absorb monosaccharides from the gut lumen","C. Fats are broken down by salivary, gastric, and pancreatic secretions","D. The term villi is synonymous with brush border","E. Lactose intolerance is due to a deficiency of a disaccharidase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The term villi is synonymous with brush border","a":"D","why":"<b>D is untrue.</b> The <b>brush border = microvilli</b>, the tiny projections on each enterocyte's apical membrane. Villi are the larger finger-like mucosal projections that those cells cover.<br>A. True: SGLT-1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) carries both glucose and galactose.<br>B. True: only monosaccharides are absorbed.<br>C. True: lingual (salivary), gastric and pancreatic lipases all digest fat.<br>E. True: lactase is a brush-border disaccharidase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)","L55 slide 13 (Microvilli /Brush Border)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":123,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":123,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"205"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":55,"label":"Enterocytes replaced every three to six days","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Crypt regenerative stem cells constantly replace villus enterocytes","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G045-13.jpg","cap":"Villi, enterocytes and their microvilli (brush border) magnified","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-173-1","s":"THE EPITHELIAL CELLS OF THE SMALL INTESTINE HAVE THE HIGHEST TURNOVER RATE. HOW OFTEN ARE THEY REPLACED?","o":["A. EVERY 1-2 DAYS","B. EVERY 2-4 DAYS","C. EVERY 3-6 DAYS","D. EVERY 10 DAYS"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"EVERY 3-6 DAYS","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Every three to six days.</b> Small-intestine epithelial cells have one of the <b>highest turnover rates</b> in the body; crypt stem cells keep replacing them. That makes them very sensitive to <b>radiation and chemotherapy</b>.<br>A. Every one to two days: too fast.<br>B. Every two to four days: not the textbook range (Costanzo: three to six).<br>D. Every ten days: too slow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":173,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-193-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 4"},{"id":"D004-193-1","s":"Why are enterocytes (epithelial cells) in the duodenum replaced every 3-5 days?","o":["A. Acid damages them","B. Proteolytic enzymes damage them","C. There is physical or mechanical damage","D. A, B and C are correct","E. B and C are correct (but not A)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"A, B and C are correct","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Duodenal enterocytes face <b>acid</b> from the stomach, <b>proteolytic enzymes</b> (shed cells are even digested as dietary protein) and <b>mechanical</b> wear from chyme. So crypt stem cells replace them every few days.<br>A, B, C. Each is true, but each is only part of the reason.<br>E. Leaves out acid, yet acidic chyme enters the duodenum before bicarbonate neutralizes it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)","L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":56,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":193,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":46,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":43,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"206"},{"id":"D010-125-1","s":"6) A cancer patient is undergoing both chemotherapy and radiation treatment for their disease. As a consequence of their therapy, what will be the most likely effect?","o":["A. Loss of HCl secretion by parietal cells","B. Impaired duodenal digestion of macronutrients","C. Impaired autonomic stimulation of peristalsis (motility) of the gut","D. Loss of integrity of the longitudinal muscle layer of the gut","E. Perforation of the GI tract"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired duodenal digestion of macronutrients","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Chemotherapy and radiation kill <b>rapidly dividing cells</b>. Enterocytes are replaced every three to six days, so villi and their <b>brush-border enzymes</b> are lost, and digestion and absorption fail.<br>A. Parietal cells: long-lived, slowly renewing; acid secretion is not the main hit.<br>C. Autonomic nerves: neurons don't divide, so they are spared.<br>D. Longitudinal muscle: smooth muscle turns over slowly.<br>E. Perforation: not an expected effect of therapy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 13 (Microvilli /Brush Border)","L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":125,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":125,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"207"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":56,"label":"Only monosaccharides are absorbed","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"Only glucose, galactose and fructose have brush-border entry carriers","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/D040-146.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: all carbohydrates end as glucose, galactose or fructose","where":"2023 TA review, slide 146"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-140-1","s":"Carbohydrates can only be absorbed by the intestinal epithelial cells in which of the following forms?","o":["A. Starches","B. Oligosaccharides","C. Disaccharides","D. Monosaccharides"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Monosaccharides","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Monosaccharides</b> only: <b>glucose, galactose and fructose</b>. Everything bigger must be split first.<br>A. Starches: cut by salivary and pancreatic α-amylase.<br>B. Oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins and maltotriose are split by brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase and sucrase.<br>C. Disaccharides: split by brush-border lactase, sucrase, maltase and trehalase. Unsplit lactose stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":140,"f":"D040"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":155,"f":"D007"}],"tier":1,"n":"208"},{"id":"D015-177-1","s":"FACT OR CAP: ONLY DISACCHARIDES ARE ABSORBED BY INTESTINAL EPITHELIAL CELLS","o":["A. FACT","B. CAP"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CAP","a":"B","why":"<b>Cap (false).</b> Only <b>monosaccharides</b> (glucose, galactose, fructose) are absorbed. Disaccharides must first be split by brush-border enzymes (lactase, sucrase, maltase, trehalase).<br>Common mix-up: in lactase deficiency, unsplit lactose (a disaccharide) can't be absorbed, so it stays in the lumen and pulls in water, causing osmotic diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":177,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-140-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 5"},{"id":"F1203-91-1","s":"Which carbohydrate molecules can be both digested and absorbed by intestinal epithelial cells?","o":["A. Sucrose, Lactose","B. Maltose, Trehalose","C. Glucose, Fructose","D. None of the above"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose, Fructose","a":"C","why":"Only <b>monosaccharides</b> (glucose, galactose, fructose) are <b>absorbed</b>; bigger sugars must be <b>digested</b> to them first. So C is the intended answer: glucose and fructose are the absorbable ones. Read literally, no pair is both digested and absorbed, so D also counts.<br>A. Sucrose, lactose: disaccharides; split by sucrase and lactase, never absorbed intact.<br>B. Maltose, trehalose: disaccharides; split by maltase and trehalase first.","ex":"","fl":["Wording is loose: glucose and fructose are absorbed but need no digestion, while sucrose/lactose are digested but not absorbed. The point being tested (source explanation, G045:19) is that only monosaccharides are absorbed, so C is the intended answer.","Source keyed only C, but read literally glucose and fructose are absorbed without being digested and the disaccharides are digested but never absorbed, so D (none of the above) is equally right; the tested fact is that only monosaccharides are absorbed."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-140-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":56,"label":"Food disaccharides: sucrose, lactose, maltose, trehalose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-18.jpg","cap":"Structures of sucrose, trehalose, lactose and maltose","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/D015-179.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure labels disaccharides and their monosaccharide products","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-175-1","s":"WHICH ARE EXAMPLES OF DISACCHARIDES? (MULTIPLE ANSWER CHOICES)","o":["A. SUCROSE","B. LACTOSE","C. MALTOSE","D. TREHALOSE","E. GLUCOSE","F. FRUCTOSE"],"g":["A","B","C","D"],"k":["A","B","C","D"],"rv":false,"at":"SUCROSE; LACTOSE; MALTOSE; TREHALOSE","a":"A,B,C,D","why":"<b>Sucrose, lactose, maltose and trehalose</b> are all disaccharides (two sugars joined).<br>Sucrose = glucose + fructose; lactose = glucose + galactose; maltose = glucose + glucose; trehalose = glucose + glucose (found in mushrooms).<br>E. Glucose: a monosaccharide.<br>F. Fructose: a monosaccharide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 17 (Food Disaccharides)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":175,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":56,"label":"α-Amylase splits starch into small sugars","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-15.jpg","cap":"Brush-border enzymes split α-dextrin, maltotriose, maltose, lactose and sucrose","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/D040-146.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: α-amylase turns starch into α-dextrins, maltose, maltotriose","where":"2023 TA review, slide 146"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-144-1","s":"Starch is broken down to maltose by which of the following enzymes?","o":["A. ɑ-amylase","B. Maltose","C. Sucrase","D. Lactase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ɑ-amylase","a":"A","why":"<b>A. α-Amylase</b> breaks starch into <b>maltose</b>, maltotriose and α-limit dextrins. Salivary amylase starts, stomach acid stops it, and pancreatic amylase finishes in the duodenum.<br>B. Maltose: the product (glucose + glucose), a sugar, not an enzyme; maltase splits it.<br>C. Sucrase: splits sucrose into glucose + fructose (it also helps on maltose).<br>D. Lactase: splits lactose into glucose + galactose.","ex":"","fl":["Key (α-amylase) is correct; explanation slip: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose + fructose (maltase handles maltose/maltotriose)."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)","L55 slide 43 (One Amylase)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":144,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 8"},{"id":"G066-186-1","s":"Which of the following enzymes are responsible for the digestion of oligosaccharides into disaccharides?","o":["A. A-amylase","B. Maltase","C. Lactase","D. A-dextrinase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"A-amylase","a":"A","why":"<b>A. α-Amylase</b> (salivary, then pancreatic) cuts starch into <b>maltose, maltotriose and α-limit dextrins</b>. Every other choice is a brush-border enzyme that makes monosaccharides.<br>B. Maltase: splits maltose (and maltotriose) into glucose.<br>C. Lactase: splits lactose into glucose + galactose.<br>D. α-Dextrinase: splits α-limit dextrins (branched oligosaccharides, not disaccharides) into glucose by cutting the branch bonds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)","L55 slide 14 (Carbohydrate Digestion & Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":186,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":185,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 9"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":56,"label":"α-Amylase cuts α-1,4 bonds, not α-1,6","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-142.jpg","cap":"TA slide: amylase cleaves α-1,4 bonds; brush-border enzyme table","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 142"},{"src":"../sl/G045-15.jpg","cap":"Branched α-dextrin left by amylase is split by α-dextrinase","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-180-1","s":"PANCREATIC AMYLASE DIGESTS WHICH TYPE OF BONDS IN STARCH?","o":["A. 1,4-GLYCOSIDIC BONDS","B. 1,6-GLYCOSIDIC BONDS"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1,4-GLYCOSIDIC BONDS","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> α-Amylase cuts the straight-chain <b>α-1,4 glycosidic bonds</b> inside starch, leaving maltose, maltotriose and α-limit dextrins.<br>B. α-1,6 bonds: the branch points. Amylase can't cut them; the brush-border enzyme <b>α-dextrinase</b> (isomaltase) does, turning α-limit dextrins into glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 14 (Carbohydrate Digestion & Absorption)","L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":180,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"209"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":56,"label":"Maltose digestion yields two glucose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-15.jpg","cap":"Maltose is two glucoses; lactose has galactose; sucrose has fructose","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/G045-18.jpg","cap":"Structures: maltose glucose-glucose, sucrose glucose-fructose, lactose galactose-glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-65-1","s":"Digestion of which of the following carbohydrates would result in the greatest amount of product that can directly be phosphorylated upon entry of a skeletal muscle cell and directed into glycolysis? Assume equal starting amounts of reactant, and same enzyme kinetics.","o":["A. Maltose","B. Sucrose","C. Lactose","D. Triglyceride","E. Galactose"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Maltose","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Maltose</b> = glucose + glucose, so it gives <b>two glucose</b>. Glucose enters muscle, and <b>hexokinase</b> phosphorylates it to glucose-6-phosphate, which goes straight into glycolysis.<br>B. Sucrose: one glucose + one fructose; fructose is handled mainly by the liver, and muscle hexokinase binds it poorly.<br>C. Lactose: one glucose + one galactose; galactose must first be converted (galactokinase pathway).<br>D. Triglyceride: a fat, not a carbohydrate.<br>E. Galactose: a monosaccharide that needs conversion first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 17 (Food Disaccharides)","L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":65,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"210"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":57,"label":"Glucose & galactose: secondary active transport","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-19.jpg","cap":"Glucose, galactose: secondary active via SGLT-1; fructose: facilitated diffusion via GLUT-5","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G045-27.jpg","cap":"SGLT-1 co-transports glucose together with Na⁺","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-188-1","s":"The monosaccharides Glucose and Galactose are absorbed via which of the following modes of transport on the apical membrane of the epithelial cell in the small intestine?","o":["A. Facilitated diffusion","B. Primary Active transport","C. Secondary Active transport","D. Simple diffusion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secondary Active transport","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secondary active transport.</b> <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) moves glucose or galactose uphill, powered by sodium flowing downhill. The basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump) keeps that sodium gradient.<br>A. Facilitated diffusion: how fructose enters (GLUT-5, glucose transporter 5) and how all three sugars exit (GLUT-2).<br>B. Primary active: ATP is used only by the basolateral pump, not by SGLT-1.<br>D. Simple diffusion: sugars can't cross the lipid membrane without a carrier.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 27 (Glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":188,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":187,"f":"D035"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":112,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":116,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":112,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":116,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":280,"f":"F1257"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":284,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"211"},{"id":"D036-144-1","s":"Which monosaccharides utilize secondary active transport for absorption in the small intestine?","o":["A. Glucose and fructose","B. Galactose and fructose","C. Glucose and galactose","D. Glucose only","E. Fructose only"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose and galactose","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Glucose and galactose</b> share <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) and ride in with sodium: secondary active transport.<br>A, B. Fructose doesn't use SGLT-1; it enters by facilitated diffusion through GLUT-5 (glucose transporter 5).<br>D. Glucose only: leaves out galactose, which uses the same carrier.<br>E. Fructose only: the opposite; fructose is the passive one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":88,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":144,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-188-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 10"},{"id":"D010-120-1","s":"1) Describe the mechanism by which galactose is absorbed from the intestinal lumen into the gut enterocyte.","o":["A. Absorbed via secondary active transport against its electrochemical gradient","B. Absorbed via facilitated diffusion","C. Passage via osmosis","D. Absorbed directly through a sodium/potassium ATPase","E. Galactose is not absorbed by human enterocytes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorbed via secondary active transport against its electrochemical gradient","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Galactose, like glucose, enters by <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1): <b>secondary active transport</b> uphill, driven by the sodium gradient that the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump) keeps.<br>B. Facilitated diffusion: fructose's entry (GLUT-5, glucose transporter 5).<br>C. Osmosis: movement of water, not sugar.<br>D. The sodium-potassium ATPase moves only sodium and potassium; it builds the gradient but carries no galactose.<br>E. Galactose is absorbed normally.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 24 (Secondary Active Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":120,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":120,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-188-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 11"},{"id":"G067-86-1","s":"A 56-year-old patient presents with chronic diarrhea and unintentional weight loss. Endoscopic biopsy reveals villous atrophy consistent with celiac disease. As part of your explanation, you describe how nutrient absorption occurs in the intact regions of the small intestine. Specifically, you explain how glucose, a key carbohydrate, is absorbed into enterocytes despite adverse luminal conditions. Which of the following mechanisms most accurately describes how glucose uptake occurs at the luminal surface of the small intestine?","o":["A. Passive facilitated diffusion through GLUT-5, driven by a luminal-to-intracellular glucose concentration gradient","B. Secondary active transport via a Na⁺-H⁺ antiport mechanism dependent on the proton gradient established by gastric acid","C. Sodium-glucose co-transport mediated by SGLT-1, utilizing the electrochemical sodium gradient maintained by basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","D. Paracellular movement of glucose through tight junctions, driven by osmotic gradients between the lumen and the interstitial space","E. Primary active transport via ATP-dependent glucose transporters at the apical membrane of enterocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sodium-glucose co-transport mediated by SGLT-1, utilizing the electrochemical sodium gradient maintained by basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Glucose enters with sodium through <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1). The sodium gradient comes from the basolateral <b>sodium-potassium ATPase</b> (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump). Glucose then leaves by GLUT-2 (glucose transporter 2).<br>A. GLUT-5 is fructose's door, not glucose's.<br>B. A sodium-hydrogen antiporter carries no glucose, and stomach acid doesn't power intestinal uptake.<br>D. Paracellular glucose flow is negligible.<br>E. There is no apical ATP-driven glucose pump; ATP is used only by the basolateral pump.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 27 (Glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":86,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-188-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 12"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":57,"label":"Na⁺/K⁺ pump's sodium gradient powers sugar uptake","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-22.jpg","cap":"Na⁺-glucose symporter driven by low cell sodium from basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G045-25.jpg","cap":"Basolateral ATPase pumps sodium into blood, creating the sodium gradient","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-146-1","s":"What is the energy source for the absorption of glucose and galactose in the small intestine?","o":["A. ATP generated in the apical membrane","B. Facilitated diffusion by GLUT5 transporter","C. Energy derived from glucose breakdown","D. Na+ gradient across the apical membrane"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Na+ gradient across the apical membrane","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The energy is the <b>sodium gradient</b> across the apical membrane: low sodium inside the cell, kept by the basolateral <b>sodium-potassium ATPase</b> (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump). SGLT-1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) lets sodium run downhill and drags glucose or galactose uphill.<br>A. No ATP is made or used at the apical membrane; it is spent only at the basolateral pump.<br>B. GLUT-5 (glucose transporter 5) is fructose's passive door and needs no energy.<br>C. Glucose breakdown doesn't power glucose uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 25 (Secondary Active Transport)","L55 slide 26 (Then….. Glucose and galactose come down gradient)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":146,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-69-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 13"},{"id":"D024-69-1","s":"Which statement regarding membrane proteins for carbohydrate absorption in the small intestine is correct?","o":["A. SGLT1 and SGLT2 are both secondary active transporters for monosaccharides, however SGLT1 is an antiporter and SGLT2 is a symporter.","B. In the small intestine, fructose utilizes only facilitated diffusion to cross the apical membrane of the epithelial cells, relying on GLUT1.","C. GLUT2 is responsible for secondary active transport of glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane of small intestine epithelium.","D. Disruption of the sodium potassium pump in the basolateral membrane of the small intestine epithelial will disrupt carbohydrate absorption."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Disruption of the sodium potassium pump in the basolateral membrane of the small intestine epithelial will disrupt carbohydrate absorption.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The basolateral <b>sodium-potassium pump</b> keeps cell sodium low. That sodium gradient powers <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1), so glucose and galactose absorption stops if the pump fails.<br>A. SGLT-1 and SGLT-2 are both symporters (sodium and sugar move the same way); SGLT-2 is in the kidney.<br>B. Fructose enters by GLUT-5 (glucose transporter 5), not GLUT-1.<br>C. GLUT-2 is passive facilitated diffusion, not secondary active transport.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 25 (Secondary Active Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":69,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"212"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":58,"label":"Fructose: GLUT-5 in, GLUT-2 out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-28.jpg","cap":"Fructose 'just a door': GLUT-5 at lumen, GLUT-2 to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"Diagram: GLUT5 apical fructose entry; GLUT2 basolateral exit","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-96-1","s":"Galactose and glucose are absorbed by the same mechanism using the SGLT1 receptor alongside a molecule of Na+. Fructose is the only monosaccharide that follows a different method of absorption which is:","o":["A. Simple diffusion, GLUT-2","B. Simple diffusion, GLUT-5","C. Facilitated diffusion, GLUT-2","D. Facilitate diffusion, GLUT-5"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Facilitate diffusion, GLUT-5","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Facilitated diffusion via GLUT-5</b> (glucose transporter 5). Fructose moves down its gradient through a carrier, with no sodium, then exits to blood by GLUT-2.<br>A. Simple diffusion, GLUT-2: GLUT-2 is the blood-side exit, and carrier-mediated movement isn't 'simple'.<br>B. Simple diffusion, GLUT-5: right door, wrong mechanism; simple diffusion uses no protein.<br>C. Facilitated diffusion, GLUT-2: right mechanism, but GLUT-2 is basolateral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 28 (Fructose; Gets Just a door)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":96,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-190-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 14"},{"id":"D040-142-1","s":"Fructose is transported absorbed across the apical membrane of the intestinal epithelial cell via which transporter?","o":["A. SGLT-1","B. GLUT-5","C. GLUT-2","D. Na K+ ATPase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUT-5","a":"B","why":"<b>B. GLUT-5</b> (glucose transporter 5) carries fructose across the <b>apical</b> membrane by facilitated diffusion.<br>A. SGLT-1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1): apical, but for glucose and galactose with sodium.<br>C. GLUT-2: the <b>basolateral</b> exit for all three sugars (the source explanation swaps these membranes).<br>D. Sodium-potassium ATPase (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump): basolateral; moves ions, not sugar.","ex":"","fl":["Key (GLUT-5) is correct, but the explanation swaps the membranes: SGLT-1 is the APICAL Na+-glucose/galactose cotransporter and GLUT-2 is the BASOLATERAL exit for glucose, galactose and fructose (G045:19, G045:28)."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 28 (Fructose; Gets Just a door)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":142,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-190-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 15"},{"id":"D010-121-1","s":"2) What transport protein facilitates the passage of fructose from the gut lumen into the gut epithelium (intracellular)?","o":["A. GLUT2","B. GLUT4","C. GLUT1","D. FRUC2","E. GLUT5"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUT5","a":"E","why":"<b>E. GLUT-5</b> (glucose transporter 5) is fructose's apical door: <b>facilitated diffusion</b>, no sodium.<br>A. GLUT-2: the basolateral exit to blood for glucose, galactose and fructose.<br>B. GLUT-4: insulin-regulated, in muscle and fat.<br>C. GLUT-1: red cells and the blood-brain barrier.<br>D. FRUC2: not a real transporter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 28 (Fructose; Gets Just a door)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":121,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":121,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-190-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 16"},{"id":"G066-190-1","s":"Which of the following correctly pairs the transporters for Fructose on the Apical and Basolateral membrane respectively?","o":["A. GLUT5 and GLUT2","B. SGLT1 and GLUT2","C. GLUT1 and GLT2","D. GLUT4 and GLUT2"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUT5 and GLUT2","a":"A","why":"<b>A. GLUT-5, then GLUT-2.</b> Fructose enters across the apical membrane through <b>GLUT-5</b> (glucose transporter 5) and leaves across the basolateral membrane through <b>GLUT-2</b>, both by facilitated diffusion.<br>B. SGLT-1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) and GLUT-2: the pair for glucose and galactose.<br>C. GLUT-1: red cells and brain, not the gut lumen.<br>D. GLUT-4: the insulin-dependent transporter of muscle and fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 28 (Fructose; Gets Just a door)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":190,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":189,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"213"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":58,"label":"Match sugars to SGLT-1, GLUT-5, GLUT-2","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"SGLT1 for glucose/galactose, GLUT5 for fructose, GLUT2 the shared exit","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G066-192.jpg","cap":"Costanzo cell: apical SGLT1 and GLUT5; basolateral GLUT2 for each sugar","where":"2024 TA review, slide 192"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-180-1","s":"Match with absorption into enterocytes: A - glucose; B - galactose; C - fructose. Transporters: 1 - GLUT5; 2 - GLUT2; 3 - SGLT1","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A (glucose) -> 3 SGLT1; B (galactose) -> 3 SGLT1; C (fructose) -> 1 GLUT5 (GLUT2 not matched: it is the basolateral exit to blood for all three)","a":null,"why":"<b>Glucose and galactose → SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1), secondary active transport with sodium. <b>Fructose → GLUT-5</b> (glucose transporter 5), facilitated diffusion.<br>Common mix-up: <b>GLUT-2</b> is not an entry door. It sits on the basolateral (blood) side and is the exit for all three sugars.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Both slides carry only the title in text; the items and arrows are handwritten ink (slide 180 = items, slide 181 = arrows).","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 28 (Fructose; Gets Just a door)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":180,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 17"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":58,"label":"Glucose: SGLT-1 in, GLUT-2 out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"SGLT1 brings glucose/galactose in, GLUT5 fructose; GLUT2 exits to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G045-7.jpg","cap":"SGLT1 at lumen, GLUT2 at blood side, Na⁺/K⁺ pump basolateral","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-149-1","s":"Glucose is absorbed via _______ into cells and via _______ into blood?","o":["A. GLUT5; GLUT2","B. SGLT1; GLUT 4","C. SGLT1; GLUT 2","D. SGLT2; GLUT 4","E. SGLT2; GLUT 2"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"SGLT1; GLUT 2","a":"C","why":"<b>C. SGLT-1; GLUT-2.</b> Glucose enters with sodium through <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) and exits to blood through <b>GLUT-2</b> (glucose transporter 2).<br>A. GLUT-5; GLUT-2: fructose's pair.<br>B. GLUT-4: insulin-dependent, in muscle and fat.<br>D. SGLT-2: kidney proximal tubule; GLUT-4 is wrong too.<br>E. SGLT-2: kidney, not intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":149,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":178,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"214"},{"id":"D061-114-1","s":"Glucose is absorbed into the intestinal cells then into the bloodstream by which transporters?","o":["A. GLUT 2; SGLT 1","B. SGLT 1; GLUT 2","C. SGLT 1; GLUT 1","D. SGLT 2; GLUT 4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"SGLT 1; GLUT 2","a":"B","why":"<b>B. SGLT-1, then GLUT-2.</b> Glucose enters from the lumen with sodium through <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1), then leaves to blood through <b>GLUT-2</b> (glucose transporter 2).<br>A. Reversed order.<br>C. GLUT-1: red cells and brain, not the intestinal exit.<br>D. SGLT-2 is the kidney's proximal-tubule carrier; GLUT-4 is the insulin-dependent carrier of muscle and fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":114,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-149-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 18"},{"id":"D010-122-1","s":"3) What transport protein facilitates the passage of glucose from the intracellular compartment of gut enterocytes into the portal blood?","o":["A. GLUT1","B. GLUT3","C. GLUT2","D. GLUT9","E. GLUT5"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUT2","a":"C","why":"<b>C. GLUT-2</b> (glucose transporter 2) on the <b>basolateral</b> membrane lets glucose leave the enterocyte into portal blood by facilitated diffusion. It also exports galactose and fructose.<br>A. GLUT-1: red cells and the blood-brain barrier.<br>B. GLUT-3: neurons.<br>D. GLUT-9: carries uric acid, mainly in the kidney.<br>E. GLUT-5: apical fructose entry.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":122,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":122,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-149-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 19"},{"id":"G055-4-1","s":"GLUT2 and SGLT1 are both found in brush border cells. Why are both required for glucose absorption?","o":["a. GLUT2 transports fructose into the cell and SGLT1 transports glucose into the cell","b. GLUT2 drives glucose against the concentration from the lumen into the cell and SGLT1 transports glucose out of the cell with the concentration gradient","c. GLUT2 transports glucose with the concentration gradient from the lumen into the cell and SGLT1 drives glucose out of the cell against the concentration gradient","d. SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against the concentration gradient and GLUT2 transports glucose out of the cell into the blood with the concentration gradient"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against the concentration gradient and GLUT2 transports glucose out of the cell into the blood with the concentration gradient","a":"d","why":"<b>d.</b> <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) on the lumen side pulls glucose <b>into</b> the cell against its gradient, riding on sodium. <b>GLUT-2</b> (glucose transporter 2) on the blood side lets it <b>out</b>, down its gradient. (GLUT-2 is basolateral, not in the brush border.)<br>a. Fructose enters by GLUT-5; GLUT-2 is the exit for all three sugars.<br>b. Reversed: GLUT-2 is passive and on the blood side.<br>c. Reversed: SGLT-1 brings glucose in; it doesn't push it out.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 27 (Glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":4,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":4,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":201,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":153,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":4,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":36,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"215"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":59,"label":"Lactase splits lactose into glucose + galactose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-21.jpg","cap":"Lactase splits lactose into glucose plus galactose","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G045-30.jpg","cap":"Lactase deficiency: disaccharide not absorbed, osmotic diarrhea, flatus, cramping","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-95-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient presents with bloating and diarrhea after consuming dairy products. A stool test reveals increased lactose levels. Which enzyme is deficient in this patient, and where is it typically found?","o":["A. Amylase; salivary glands","B. Lactase; brush border of the small intestine","C. Sucrase; stomach","D. Maltase; pancreatic acini"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lactase; brush border of the small intestine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lactase, on the brush border</b> (microvilli) of the small intestine. It splits lactose into glucose + galactose; without it, lactose stays in the stool and pulls in water.<br>A. Amylase: from salivary glands and pancreas; digests starch.<br>C. Sucrase: splits sucrose and sits on the brush border, not in the stomach.<br>D. Maltase: a brush-border enzyme, not made by pancreatic acini; splits maltose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":95,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":90,"f":"D067"}],"tier":1,"n":"216"},{"id":"F1212-182-1","s":"A lack of _____ in the ______ is responsible for a very prevalent malabsorption disease commonly found in Asian people","o":["A. Pepsin; stomach","B. Intrinsic factor; stomach","C. Trehalase; brush border","D. Lactase; brush border","E. Sucrase; brush border"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lactase; brush border","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lactase; brush border.</b> Lactase deficiency (lactose intolerance) is the most common carbohydrate malabsorption and is very common in Asian populations.<br>A. Pepsin; stomach: not essential, so losing it causes no malabsorption disease.<br>B. Intrinsic factor; stomach: its loss causes vitamin B12 malabsorption (pernicious anemia).<br>C. Trehalase; brush border: deficiency is rare (trehalose is in mushrooms).<br>E. Sucrase; brush border: inherited sucrase deficiency is rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-95-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 20"},{"id":"D024-67-1","s":"A 16yo male patient presents to their PCP complaining of persistent bloating, flatulence, and diarrhea. The patient informs the PCP that symptoms did not improve after trying a strict gluten-free diet for one month, and also denies any blood in stool. Which of the following could explain the patient’s symptoms?","o":["A. Decreased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and fructose, resulting in secretory diarrhea.","B. Increased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and fructose, resulting in osmotic diarrhea.","C. Increased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and galactose, resulting in osmotic diarrhea.","D. Decreased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and fructose, resulting in secretory diarrhea.","E. Decreased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and galactose, resulting in osmotic diarrhea."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased actions of enzyme responsible for breaking down a disaccharide into glucose and galactose, resulting in osmotic diarrhea.","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Bloating, gas and diarrhea that don't improve off gluten (so not celiac disease) fit <b>lactase deficiency</b>. Lactase normally splits lactose into <b>glucose + galactose</b>; unsplit lactose holds water, giving <b>osmotic diarrhea</b>.<br>A, D. Glucose + fructose is sucrase's product, and the diarrhea isn't secretory.<br>B. Extra enzyme activity causes no disease; wrong enzyme too.<br>C. Extra lactase activity would improve digestion, not cause diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":67,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-95-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 21"},{"id":"F1203-94-1","s":"Patient comes into your office with diarrhea and flatulence after ingestion of dairy products and is found to have a lactase deficiency. Which of the following monosaccharides is the patient unable to form due to breakdown of lactose?","o":["A. Maltose","B. Fructose","C. Maltotriose","D. Galactose"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Galactose","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Galactose.</b> Lactase splits lactose into galactose + glucose. Without it, galactose is never freed from lactose.<br>A. Maltose: a disaccharide (glucose + glucose) made by amylase, not a monosaccharide.<br>B. Fructose: comes from sucrose (sucrase), not lactose.<br>C. Maltotriose: three glucoses; an amylase product.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 17 (Food Disaccharides)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":94,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-95-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 22"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":59,"label":"Lactose intolerance causes osmotic diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-32.jpg","cap":"Unabsorbed sugar: extra osmotic draw in bowel, bacterial overgrowth","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G045-30.jpg","cap":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea; stop milk products","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-246-1","s":"3. You and a few of your friends go out for ice cream to celebrate after the GI physio exam. You remember one of your friends has lactose deficiency and remind them that the ice cream place also has some sorbet. What kind of diarrhea is caused by lactase deficiency?","o":["A. Secretory diarrhea","B. Hematochezia","C. Osmotic diarrhea","D. Steatorrhea"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Osmotic diarrhea","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Osmotic diarrhea.</b> Unsplit lactose stays in the lumen and pulls water with it. Clues: it <b>stops with fasting</b> or when the sugar is stopped, and the stool osmotic gap is high.<br>A. Secretory: active chloride secretion (for example cholera); continues during fasting.<br>B. Hematochezia: bright red blood from the rectum; a sign, not a diarrhea type.<br>D. Steatorrhea: fatty stool from fat malabsorption (pancreatic insufficiency, bile salt loss).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":246,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":245,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"217"},{"id":"D040-186-1","s":"A 27-year-old female lawyer presents to your family medicine practice complaining of gas, bloating and diarrhea whenever she eats dairy. What type of diarrhea is this patient presenting with?","o":["A) Secretory","B) Bacterial","C) Osmotic","D) Fungal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Osmotic","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Osmotic.</b> Dairy triggers it because lactase deficiency leaves <b>lactose unsplit</b>; it stays in the lumen and draws water. Colon bacteria ferment it, causing gas and bloating.<br>A. Secretory: active chloride secretion, as in cholera; not triggered by each meal.<br>B. Bacterial: an infection, not a reaction to each dairy meal.<br>D. Fungal: not a cause of dairy-linked diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":186,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-246-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 23"},{"id":"D015-182-1","s":"OSMOTIC DIARRHEA IS CAUSED BY WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING DISORDERS?","o":["A. CHRONIC PANCREATITIS","B. CYSTIC FIBROSIS","C. LACTOSE INTOLERANCE","D. CYSTINURIA"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"LACTOSE INTOLERANCE","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lactose intolerance.</b> Unsplit lactose stays in the lumen and pulls water: <b>osmotic diarrhea</b>.<br>A. Chronic pancreatitis: enzymes not made, so fat malabsorption gives steatorrhea (fatty stool).<br>B. Cystic fibrosis: thick secretions block the pancreatic ducts, giving steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin loss.<br>D. Cystinuria: amino acid transporter defect; the gut defect causes no symptoms, the problem is kidney stones.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":182,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-246-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 24"},{"id":"D036-149-1","s":"What is the primary reason for osmotic diarrhea in individuals with lactose intolerance?","o":["A. Presence of brush border lactase","B. Absorption of lactose in the small intestine","C. Hydrolysis of lactose into glucose and galactose","D. Nonabsorption of lactose and water in the GI tract","E. Flatus and cramping symptoms"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Nonabsorption of lactose and water in the GI tract","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Without lactase, lactose is <b>not absorbed</b>. It stays in the gut and holds <b>water</b> in the lumen by osmosis: osmotic diarrhea.<br>A. Brush-border lactase is what's missing, not present.<br>B. Lactose is never absorbed intact.<br>C. Splitting lactose into glucose + galactose is exactly what fails.<br>E. Flatus and cramping are symptoms of bacterial fermentation, not the cause of the diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":149,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-246-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 25"},{"id":"G055-24-1","s":"A 25-year-old male is suffering from lactose intolerance; which of the following does not happen in lactose intolerance;","o":["A. Non-persistence of enzyme lactase at the jejunal brush border","B. Undigested lactose being absorbed by the ileal cells","C. Abdominal symptoms e.g. bloating, and pain","D. Gut Bacteria degrading lactose into osmotically active solutes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Undigested lactose being absorbed by the ileal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B does not happen.</b> Lactose is a disaccharide and only monosaccharides are absorbed, so <b>undigested lactose is never absorbed</b>. It stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea.<br>A. Happens: lactase 'non-persistence' (its normal fall after childhood) is the usual cause.<br>C. Happens: bloating and pain from gas and retained water.<br>D. Happens: colon bacteria ferment lactose into small, osmotically active molecules and gas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":29,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":24,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":220,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":44,"f":"D016"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":22,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"218"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":60,"label":"Protein digestion starts in stomach with pepsin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-54.jpg","cap":"Protein to peptides by pepsin and pancreatic proteases; carriers absorb amino acids","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 54"},{"src":"../sl/G045-52.jpg","cap":"L-amino acids absorbed by sodium secondary active transport","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-193-1","s":"During your rotation, an attending asks you where does the digestion of protein start, and through which enzyme. Your reply is:","o":["A. Mouth; amylase","B. Mouth; lipase","C. Stomach; pepsin","D. Stomach; intrinsic factor"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach; pepsin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach; pepsin.</b> Protein digestion <b>starts in the stomach</b> with pepsin (from chief cells, activated by acid), though pepsin is not essential.<br>A. Mouth; amylase: starts carbohydrate digestion.<br>B. Mouth; lipase: lingual lipase starts fat digestion.<br>D. Stomach; intrinsic factor: binds vitamin B12; it is not a protease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L55 slide 39 (Protein Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":193,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":192,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":60,"label":"Non-protease among digestive enzymes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-42.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic proteases: trypsin activates chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidase A and B","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/D015-165.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: digestive enzymes for carbohydrate, protein and lipid","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 165"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-163-1","s":"ALL OF THE FOLLOWING DIGESTIVE ENZYMES DEGRADE PROTEINS EXCEPT:","o":["A. TRYPSIN","B. CHYMOTRYPSIN","C. ELASTASE","D. AMYLASE","E. CARBOXYPEPTIDASE"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"AMYLASE","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Amylase</b> digests <b>carbohydrate</b> (starch), not protein. The other four are pancreatic proteases.<br>A. Trypsin: endopeptidase (cuts inside the chain); it activates the other proteases.<br>B. Chymotrypsin: endopeptidase, activated by trypsin.<br>C. Elastase: endopeptidase, activated by trypsin.<br>E. Carboxypeptidase: exopeptidase; removes one amino acid at a time from the carboxyl (C-terminal) end.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 3 (Digestion breaks it down!)","L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":163,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"219"},{"id":"D024-72-1","s":"All of the following enzymes are necessary for the digestion of proteins in the small intestine, except?","o":["A. Lipase","B. Carboxypeptidase A","C. Carboxypeptidase B","D. Trypsinogen","E. Elastase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lipase","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lipase</b> digests <b>fat</b> (triglyceride), so protein digestion doesn't need it. The rest are pancreatic proteases or a protease precursor.<br>B. Carboxypeptidase A: exopeptidase; clips amino acids off the carboxyl end.<br>C. Carboxypeptidase B: same, for basic end amino acids.<br>D. Trypsinogen: inactive precursor; enterokinase makes it trypsin, which activates all the others.<br>E. Elastase: endopeptidase, activated by trypsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":72,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D015-163-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":60,"label":"Acid converts pepsinogen to pepsin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-27.jpg","cap":"Pepsinogen converted by acid to pepsin; inactivated above pH 5","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/D040-151.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: pepsinogen becomes pepsin at low pH","where":"2023 TA review, slide 151"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-147-1","s":"Protein digestion begins with which of the following mechanisms?","o":["A. Chief cells secrete trypsinogen which is activated by the low pH of the stomach which transforms it into the active form, trypsin","B. Chief cells secrete trypsinogen which is activated by the high pH of the stomach which transforms it into the active form, trypsin","C. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen which is activated by the low pH of the stomach which transforms it into the active form, pepsin","D. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen which is activated by the high pH of the stomach which transforms it into the active form, pepsin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen which is activated by the low pH of the stomach which transforms it into the active form, pepsin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Chief cells</b> secrete <b>pepsinogen</b>, which <b>low pH</b> (stomach acid) converts to pepsin.<br>A. Trypsinogen comes from the pancreas, not chief cells, and is activated by enterokinase in the duodenum.<br>B. Wrong enzyme and wrong pH; the stomach is acidic.<br>D. Right enzyme, wrong pH: high pH (above five) inactivates pepsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":92,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":147,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 28"},{"id":"G066-195-1","s":"From the following options, which leads to the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin?","o":["A. Basic environment (pH > 5)","B. Intrinsic factor help","C. Acidic environment (pH: 1-3)","D. Parietal cell inhibition"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Acidic environment (pH: 1-3)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Acidic environment.</b> Stomach acid (pH one to three) converts inactive pepsinogen to pepsin.<br>A. Basic environment (pH above five): inactivates pepsin.<br>B. Intrinsic factor: binds vitamin B12; no role in pepsin activation.<br>D. Parietal cell inhibition: less acid means less pepsin activation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":195,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":194,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 29"},{"id":"D036-152-1","s":"What is the primary activator of pepsin in the stomach?","o":["A. Gastric chief cells","B. Gastric H+","C. Pancreatic proteases","D. Trypsin","E. Duodenal HCO3-"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric H+","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastric H⁺</b> (hydrogen ions, that is, acid). Chief cells secrete inactive pepsinogen; <b>low pH</b> converts it to pepsin, and pepsin then activates more pepsinogen.<br>A. Chief cells: they make pepsinogen; they don't activate it.<br>C. Pancreatic proteases: work in the duodenum.<br>D. Trypsin: activates pancreatic precursors, not pepsinogen.<br>E. Duodenal bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻): raises pH and inactivates pepsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":152,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":60,"label":"Pepsin inactive above pH 5 (duodenum)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-41.jpg","cap":"Pepsin: chief cells, works at pH 1-3, denatured above 5","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-28.jpg","cap":"Pepsin neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate; trypsin takes over","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-154-1","s":"Why is pepsin denatured in the duodenum of the small intestine?","o":["A. High pH","B. Presence of brush-border proteases","C. Activation by trypsin","D. Secretion by gastric chief cells","E. Low pH"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"High pH","a":"A","why":"<b>A. High pH.</b> Pepsin works only at <b>pH one to three</b> and is inactivated above pH five. Pancreatic <b>bicarbonate</b> raises duodenal pH and shuts it off; trypsin and the other pancreatic proteases take over.<br>B. Brush-border proteases: digest peptides, not pepsin.<br>C. Trypsin neither activates nor denatures pepsin.<br>D. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen; that's its source, not its fate.<br>E. Low pH activates pepsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 28 (Starts process of Protein digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":154,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 31"},{"id":"D015-184-1","s":"PEPSIN IS INACTIVATED ABOVE A PH OF:","o":["A. 2","B. 3","C. 4","D. 5"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"5","a":"D","why":"<b>D. pH five.</b> Pepsin works at <b>pH one to three</b> and is <b>inactivated above five</b>, so duodenal bicarbonate shuts it off.<br>A, B. Two and three: inside pepsin's working range.<br>C. Four: activity is already falling, but the lecture's inactivation cutoff is above five.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":184,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 32"},{"id":"G065-120-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices regarding pepsin is correct?","o":["A. Pepsin is essential for protein digestion","B. Pepsinogen is secreted by parietal cells","C. Pepsin is most active at an alkaline pH","D. Pepsinogen is activated to pepsin by trypsin","E. Pepsin is inactivated once it reaches the duodenum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pepsin is inactivated once it reaches the duodenum","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Pancreatic <b>bicarbonate</b> raises duodenal pH above five, which <b>inactivates pepsin</b>.<br>A. False: pepsin is not essential; pancreatic proteases digest protein without it.<br>B. False: pepsinogen comes from chief cells; parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor.<br>C. False: pepsin works at acidic pH, one to three.<br>D. False: acid activates pepsinogen; trypsin works in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 27 (Pepsinogen)","L53 slide 28 (Starts process of Protein digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":120,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":288,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 33"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":60,"label":"Pepsin not essential for protein digestion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-41.jpg","cap":"Pepsin: not essential for life","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G043-28.jpg","cap":"Trypsin and friends take over protein digestion in the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-98-1","s":"A patient has his stomach removed and is no longer able to secrete gastric H+, and cannot activate pepsinogen to pepsin. What would be his protein digestion and absorption compared to normal?","o":["A. Low","B. Normal","C. Elevated"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Normal","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Normal.</b> Pepsin is <b>not essential</b>: pancreatic proteases (trypsin and friends) plus brush-border and intracellular peptidases digest protein fully.<br>A. Low: true only if pancreatic proteases were lost (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis).<br>C. Elevated: nothing here increases protein absorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L53 slide 29 (NOT essential)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":98,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"220"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":61,"label":"Enterokinase (brush border) activates trypsinogen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-60.jpg","cap":"Enteropeptidase activates trypsin; trypsin activates the other proenzymes","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 60"},{"src":"../sl/D040-151.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: brush-border enterokinase turns trypsinogen into trypsin","where":"2023 TA review, slide 151"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-101-1","s":"Which of the following is required for the activation of pancreatic proteases in the small intestine?","o":["A. Enterokinase in the brush border","B. Pepsin in the stomach","C. Secretin released from duodenal cells","D. Bile salts secreted from the liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterokinase in the brush border","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enterokinase</b> (enteropeptidase) on the duodenal <b>brush border</b> turns trypsinogen into trypsin; trypsin then activates all the other pancreatic proteases.<br>B. Pepsin: works in the stomach and is inactivated in the duodenum.<br>C. Secretin: hormone from duodenal S cells that triggers pancreatic bicarbonate, not enzyme activation.<br>D. Bile salts: emulsify fat; they don't activate proteases.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":101,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"221"},{"id":"G063-8-34","s":"After secretion of trypsinogen into the duodenum, the enzyme is converted into its active form, trypsin, by which of the following?","o":["a. Enteropeptidase","b. Procarboxypeptidase","c. Pancreatic lipase","d. Chymotrypsin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enteropeptidase","a":"a","why":"<b>a. Enteropeptidase</b> (enterokinase), a duodenal <b>brush-border</b> enzyme, cuts trypsinogen to active trypsin.<br>b. Procarboxypeptidase: an inactive precursor that trypsin activates.<br>c. Pancreatic lipase: digests fat.<br>d. Chymotrypsin: activated by trypsin, not the activator of trypsinogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 34"},{"id":"D036-158-1","s":"In the small intestine, what is the enzyme responsible for the activation of trypsinogen to its active form, trypsin?","o":["A. Carboxypeptidase A","B. Chymotrypsinogen","C. Proelastase","D. Procarboxypeptidase B","E. Enterokinase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterokinase","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Enterokinase</b> (enteropeptidase), a <b>brush-border</b> enzyme of the duodenum, converts trypsinogen to trypsin.<br>A. Carboxypeptidase A: exopeptidase, activated by trypsin.<br>B. Chymotrypsinogen: inactive precursor waiting for trypsin.<br>C. Proelastase: inactive precursor waiting for trypsin.<br>D. Procarboxypeptidase B: inactive precursor waiting for trypsin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":158,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 35"},{"id":"D008-114-1","s":"Which of the following enzymes is responsible for activating trypsinogen and initiating the process of pancreatic protein digestion?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Carboxypeptidase","C. Enterokinase","D. Elastase","E. Lactase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterokinase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Enterokinase</b>, on the duodenal brush border, activates trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the other pancreatic proteases.<br>A. Trypsin: activates more trypsinogen, but only after enterokinase makes the first trypsin.<br>B. Carboxypeptidase: exopeptidase, activated by trypsin.<br>D. Elastase: endopeptidase, activated by trypsin.<br>E. Lactase: brush-border enzyme that splits lactose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":114,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":114,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":282,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 36"},{"id":"G067-88-1","s":"A 37-year-old man with a history of recurrent acute pancreatitis presents with epigastric pain and steatorrhea. Imaging reveals pancreatic calcifications suggestive of chronic pancreatitis. You suspect a defect in protein digestion and absorption. To educate the patient, you explain the enzymatic cascade critical for protein digestion in the small intestine. Which of the following best describes the physiological process responsible for the activation of the proteolytic enzyme trypsin from its precursor form, trypsinogen?","o":["A. Spontaneous autoactivation of trypsinogen occurs in the presence of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretions in the duodenum","B. Trypsinogen is directly activated by bile salts secreted from the gallbladder during lipid digestion","C. Trypsinogen is cleaved by enterokinase, an enzyme expressed on the brush border of duodenal enterocytes, initiating the proteolytic cascade","D. Pepsin, an acid-stable protease secreted in the stomach, cleaves trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","E. Chymotrypsin acts on trypsinogen to produce active trypsin, which subsequently activates other proteolytic enzymes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsinogen is cleaved by enterokinase, an enzyme expressed on the brush border of duodenal enterocytes, initiating the proteolytic cascade","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Enterokinase</b> (enteropeptidase) on the duodenal brush border cuts trypsinogen to trypsin, which starts the cascade.<br>A. Bicarbonate doesn't activate trypsinogen; premature self-activation is what causes pancreatitis.<br>B. Bile salts: emulsify fat; they don't cut trypsinogen.<br>D. Pepsin: inactivated by duodenal bicarbonate.<br>E. Chymotrypsin: is activated by trypsin, not the reverse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":88,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 37"},{"id":"F1203-104-1","s":"What is the first step that would be affected in protein digestion and absorption if the brush border enzymes are ineffective?","o":["A. Activation of trypsin","B. Absorption of dipeptides, tripeptides, amino acids","C. Amylase activity","D. None of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of trypsin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Enterokinase</b>, the trigger that turns trypsinogen into trypsin, is a <b>brush-border</b> enzyme. Without it no trypsin forms, so no other pancreatic protease is activated: the first step lost.<br>B. Peptide and amino acid uptake uses hydrogen- and sodium-coupled transporters and comes later.<br>C. Amylase is secreted already active; it needs no brush-border activation.<br>D. Wrong: trypsin activation is affected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":104,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 38"},{"id":"D010-126-1","s":"Baby, Riordan Finnegan, 3 days old appears to have a digestive problem. Based on studies, he seems unable to digest or absorb any forms of protein, be it di/tripeptides or amino acids from food. Which of the following is the MOST LIKELY scenario regarding his condition?","o":["A. He is exhibiting a deficiency in all of his brush border protease sub-types","B. He is exhibiting absence of pepsinogen/pepsin","C. He is unable to produce adequate HCl from his parietal cells, this preventing him from activating pepsin from pepsinogen","D. He is deficient in the enzyme enterokinase","E. He lacks parasympathetic innervation to the upper GI tract"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"He is deficient in the enzyme enterokinase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Enterokinase deficiency.</b> One missing brush-border enzyme means no trypsin, and without trypsin <b>none of the pancreatic proteases</b> are activated, so protein digestion fails. It shows in infancy as poor growth and low blood protein (edema).<br>A. Losing every brush-border peptidase at once is far less likely than one enzyme defect.<br>B, C. Pepsin is not essential, so losing it, or the acid that activates it, doesn't block protein digestion.<br>E. Parasympathetic loss affects motility and secretion, not protease activation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L55 slide 46 (Q4. Deficiency of which of these leads to deficiency of all of them?)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":126,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":126,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"222"},{"id":"D067-94-1","s":"A patient with severe protein malabsorption is found to have absent activity of enterokinase in the small intestine. Which pancreatic enzyme would be MOST directly reduced as a result?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Chymotrypsin","C. Elastase","D. Pepsin","E. Carboxypeptidase A"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Trypsin.</b> <b>Enterokinase</b>, a brush-border enzyme of the duodenum, turns trypsinogen into trypsin. Without it no trypsin forms. This is the first and most direct loss.<br>B. Chymotrypsin: also falls, but only second-hand, because trypsin is what activates chymotrypsinogen.<br>C. Elastase: the same; trypsin activates proelastase.<br>D. Pepsin: a stomach enzyme activated by acid; it is not pancreatic and does not need enterokinase.<br>E. Carboxypeptidase A: also activated by trypsin, so it is lost indirectly.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)","L55 slide 47 (Q4. Deficiency of which of these leads to deficiency of all of them?)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":94,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-101-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 39"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":61,"label":"Trypsin activates all other pancreatic proteases","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-42.jpg","cap":"Trypsin activates more of itself plus chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidases","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/D015-190.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: trypsin converts every pancreatic proenzyme to its active form","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 190"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-75-1","s":"The previous patient is also informed that he may develop pancreatic insufficiency with resultant protein digestion deficiency. Which enzyme released from the pancreas is the first to become activated?","o":["A. CCK","B. Pepsin","C. Secretin","D. Trypsin","E. Enterokinase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Trypsin.</b> Trypsinogen is the first pancreatic enzyme activated (by brush-border enterokinase); trypsin then activates all the other proteases.<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): hormone from I cells, not an enzyme.<br>B. Pepsin: gastric, from chief cells.<br>C. Secretin: hormone from S cells.<br>E. Enterokinase: the activator, but it comes from the duodenal brush border, not the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":75,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 40"},{"id":"D040-149-1","s":"Trypsin functions to convert inactive precursors of pancreatic proteases into active precursors which will perform which of the following functions?","o":["A. The breakdown of dietary proteins into amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides","B. The conversation of proelastase to elastase","C. The conversion of chymotrypsinogen to chymotrypsin","D. The conversion of procarboxypeptidase A to carboxypeptidase A"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The breakdown of dietary proteins into amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Once trypsin activates them, the pancreatic proteases <b>break dietary protein</b> into oligopeptides, amino acids, dipeptides and tripeptides.<br>B. Proelastase → elastase: trypsin's own job, not the activated enzymes' function.<br>C. Chymotrypsinogen → chymotrypsin: also trypsin's job.<br>D. Procarboxypeptidase A → carboxypeptidase A: also trypsin's job.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":149,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 41"},{"id":"G066-197-1","s":"A patient presents with malabsorption, and the defect is localized in only one of the pancreatic enzymes. What is this enzyme?","o":["A. Elastase","B. Trypsin","C. Carboxypeptidase A","D. Carboxypeptidase B"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Trypsin.</b> Trypsin activates <b>all the other</b> pancreatic proteases (chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidase A and B), so one trypsin defect looks like loss of all of them.<br>A. Elastase: losing it removes only elastase.<br>C. Carboxypeptidase A: losing it removes only that exopeptidase.<br>D. Carboxypeptidase B: same; the others still work.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L55 slide 46 (Q4. Deficiency of which of these leads to deficiency of all of them?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":197,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":196,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"223"},{"id":"F1203-72-1","s":"The patient from the previous question is also informed that he may develop pancreatic insufficiency with resultant protein digestion deficiency. The inactivity of which enzyme will contribute most to the protein indigestion?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","B. Pepsin","C. Secretin","D. Trypsin","E. VIP"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Trypsin</b>, the 'master' protease: it activates chymotrypsin, elastase and the carboxypeptidases, so its loss hurts protein digestion most.<br>A. CCK (cholecystokinin): hormone from duodenal I cells that triggers enzyme secretion; not an enzyme.<br>B. Pepsin: not essential.<br>C. Secretin: hormone from S cells that triggers bicarbonate.<br>E. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide): a neurotransmitter for secretion and relaxation, not a protease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":72,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 42"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":61,"label":"Acute pancreatitis = premature trypsin activation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-45.jpg","cap":"Acute pancreatitis is a premature explosion of trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G044-9.jpg","cap":"Proteases made in inactive form so the pancreas isn't digested","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-156-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient presents with severe abdominal pain, nausea, and elevated serum amylase levels. The patient has a history of alcohol abuse. Physical examination reveals tenderness on palpation of the epigastric region. Further investigation indicates damage to pancreatic tissue. What is the most likely mechanism contributing to the development of acute pancreatitis in this patient?","o":["A. Low production of trypsin","B. Inhibition of trypsin by pancreatic enzymes","C. Hyperactivation and premature activation of trypsin","D. Excessive secretion of gastric H+","E. Overproduction of pepsin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hyperactivation and premature activation of trypsin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Acute pancreatitis is a <b>premature 'explosion' of trypsin</b> inside the pancreas; trypsin activates the other enzymes and the gland digests itself. Alcohol, gallstones and trauma trigger it.<br>A. Low trypsin: fits chronic pancreatitis (a burnt-out gland).<br>B. Trypsin inhibitors protect the pancreas; inhibition prevents damage.<br>D. Excess gastric acid: causes ulcers.<br>E. Pepsin: a stomach enzyme; not involved.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)","L54 slide 12 (Q1. An alcoholic man comes after a binge with epigastric pain and tenderness. He)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":96,"f":"D067"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":156,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C7 extra 43"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":61,"label":"Exopeptidase (carboxypeptidase) vs endopeptidases","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-44.jpg","cap":"Exopeptidase clips C-terminal amino acids (carboxypeptidase); endopeptidases cut the middle","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/D040-151.jpg","cap":"Trypsin activates its own trypsinogen and every other proenzyme","where":"2023 TA review, slide 151"}],"q":[{"id":"D024-74-1","s":"Which of the following enzymes is activated by trypsin and is an endonuclease?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Trypsinogen","C. Procarboxypeptidase A","D. Carboxypeptidase A","E. Procarboxypeptidase B","F. Chymotrypsin"],"g":["A","F"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Trypsinogen","a":"B","why":"<b>F. Chymotrypsin</b> (and <b>A. Trypsin</b>). The stem means <b>endopeptidase</b>: an enzyme that cuts bonds inside a protein chain. Trypsin turns chymotrypsinogen into <b>chymotrypsin</b>, an endopeptidase. Trypsin also turns more trypsinogen into <b>trypsin</b> (autoactivation), and trypsin is an endopeptidase too. Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) starts the cascade by making the first trypsin.<br>B. Trypsinogen: it is activated by trypsin, but it is the inactive precursor (zymogen) and cuts nothing until it becomes trypsin.<br>C, E. Procarboxypeptidase A and B: activated by trypsin, but they become exopeptidases.<br>D. Carboxypeptidase A: an exopeptidase; it removes one amino acid at a time from the carboxyl end.","ex":"","fl":["Stem means 'endopeptidase' (not endonuclease). Keyed B works only via trypsin autoactivation (trypsinogen is activated chiefly by enterokinase; 'trypsin activates more of itself', G045:42). F (chymotrypsin) is also an endopeptidase produced by trypsin activation, so F is equally defensible.","Source keyed B (trypsinogen), but trypsinogen is the inactive precursor, not an endopeptidase ('endonuclease' in the stem should read endopeptidase). The active enzymes made by trypsin that cut inside proteins are chymotrypsin (F) and trypsin itself (A, by autoactivation)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 42 (Protein Digestion)","L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":74,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-238-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 44"},{"id":"D004-238-1","s":"Which of the following is an exopeptidase?","o":["A. Enterokinase (Enteropeptidase)","B. Trypsin","C. Elastase","D. Carboxypeptidase b","E. Chymotrypsin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Carboxypeptidase b","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Carboxypeptidase B</b> is an <b>exopeptidase</b>: it removes one amino acid at a time from the carboxyl (C-terminal) end. Carboxypeptidase A works the same way.<br>A. Enterokinase: activates trypsinogen with one specific cut; not a digestive exopeptidase.<br>B. Trypsin: endopeptidase (cuts inside the chain).<br>C. Elastase: endopeptidase.<br>E. Chymotrypsin: endopeptidase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":44,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":238,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":38,"f":"D065"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":188,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"224"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":62,"label":"Protein digestion starts in stomach with pepsin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-54.jpg","cap":"Protein to peptides by pepsin and pancreatic proteases; carriers absorb amino acids","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 54"},{"src":"../sl/G045-52.jpg","cap":"L-amino acids absorbed by sodium secondary active transport","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-108-1","s":"Which of the following substances is responsible for protein digestion in the stomach and how are the amino acids absorbed?","o":["A. Trypsin, Diffusion into the intestinal cell","B. Pepsin, K+ - amino acid cotransport","C. Trypsin, H+/K+ antiporter","D. Pepsin, Na+ - amino acid cotransport"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pepsin, Na+ - amino acid cotransport","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Pepsin</b> digests protein in the stomach. Free L-amino acids are absorbed by <b>sodium-amino acid cotransport</b> (secondary active transport; four carriers: neutral, acidic, basic, imino).<br>A. Trypsin works in the duodenum, and amino acids need carriers, not plain diffusion.<br>B. Right enzyme, wrong ion: potassium is not cotransported.<br>C. Trypsin isn't gastric; the hydrogen-potassium exchanger is the parietal cell's acid pump.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 41 (Pepsin)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":108,"f":"D061"}],"tier":1,"n":"225"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":62,"label":"Di/tripeptides absorbed with H⁺ cotransport","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-52.jpg","cap":"Di/tripeptides absorbed easier with H⁺; L-amino acids with sodium","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 52"},{"src":"../sl/D040-154.jpg","cap":"Costanzo cell: Na⁺-amino acid and H⁺-peptide cotransport; peptidase inside","where":"2023 TA review, slide 154"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-152-1","s":"Dipeptides and tripeptides are absorbed by the intestinal epithelial cells at the apical surface via which mechanism?","o":["A. Peptidases","B. Na K+ ATPase","C. H+ dependant cotransporter","D. Na+  dependant cotransporter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"H+ dependant cotransporter","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hydrogen ion (H⁺)-dependent cotransporter.</b> Di- and tripeptides cross the apical membrane with H⁺; peptidases inside the cell then split them.<br>A. Peptidases: enzymes that split peptides; they don't move them across membranes.<br>B. Sodium-potassium ATPase (adenosine triphosphate-splitting pump): basolateral; it builds the sodium gradient.<br>D. Sodium-dependent cotransporter: used by free L-amino acids, not peptides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)","L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":152,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"226"},{"id":"G066-199-1","s":"What ion gradient is necessary for effective protein absorption to take place?","o":["A. Ca gradient","B. Mg gradient","C. Cl gradient","D. H gradient"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"H gradient","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hydrogen ion (H⁺) gradient.</b> Di- and tripeptides, the main absorbed form of protein, enter by <b>H⁺ cotransport</b>. An apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger keeps H⁺ high in the lumen. (A sodium gradient drives free amino acids, but sodium isn't offered.)<br>A. Calcium: absorbed in the duodenum with vitamin D; not a protein driver.<br>B. Magnesium: no role in protein uptake.<br>C. Chloride: not used for peptide transport.","ex":"","fl":["Ambiguity: G045:52 also teaches L-amino acids absorbed by Na+ secondary active transport, so a Na+ gradient is equally necessary; Na+ is not among the options, so H+ (di/tripeptide cotransport) is the only listed answer."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":199,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":198,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-152-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 45"},{"id":"D010-124-1","s":"5) Which of the following most accurately describes the mechanism by which protein (di- and tripeptides) is absorbed from the intestinal lumen into the intracellular enterocyte.","o":["A. Passive diffusion down a concentration gradient","B. Secondary active transport","C. Primary active transport","D. Osmosis","E. Paracellular transport"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Secondary active transport","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Secondary active transport.</b> Di- and tripeptides ride in with <b>hydrogen ions (H⁺)</b> on an apical cotransporter. The H⁺ gradient comes from the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger, which depends on the sodium gradient.<br>A. Passive diffusion: peptides need a carrier and move uphill.<br>C. Primary active: the carrier splits no ATP (adenosine triphosphate).<br>D. Osmosis: movement of water only.<br>E. Paracellular: tight junctions block peptides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":124,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":124,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-152-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 46"},{"id":"D008-118-1","s":"Which of the following transport mechanisms best characterizes the absorption of dipeptides and tripeptides in the lumen of the intestine?","o":["A. Primary Active Transport","B. Secondary Active Transport","C. Diffusion","D. Cotransport","E. Dipeptides and tripeptides cannot be absorbed unless broken down into amino acids"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cotransport","a":"D","why":"<b>B and D.</b> Di- and tripeptides enter by <b>cotransport with hydrogen ions (H⁺)</b>. That is <b>secondary active transport</b>, because an apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger builds the H⁺ gradient. It is faster than free amino acid uptake.<br>A. Primary active: the carrier itself splits no ATP (adenosine triphosphate).<br>C. Diffusion: peptides need a carrier.<br>E. False: unlike sugars, protein can be absorbed as di- and tripeptides.","ex":"","fl":["H+-dipeptide cotransport (PepT1) IS secondary active transport (driven by the H+ gradient that the apical Na+/H+ exchanger builds); B and D both describe it. The 2026 slide G045:40 words it 'Co-Transport with hydrogen ions', supporting D; G064 slide 124 keys 'Secondary active transport' for the same fact. On an exam that offers only one of the two, pick it.","Key D (cotransport, with H+) matches G045:40/52. Option B 'Secondary Active Transport' is also technically true, since H+-peptide cotransport is driven by the H+ gradient that the apical Na+/H+ exchanger makes; for this course pick D."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":118,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":118,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":286,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-152-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 47"},{"id":"D007-151-1","s":"Which is faster absorption of protein?","o":["A. Free amino acids with Na+ cotransport","B. Free amino acids with H+ cotransport","C. Di/tri-peptides with Na+ cotransport","D. Di/tri-peptides with H+ cotransport"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Di/tri-peptides with H+ cotransport","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Di- and tripeptides with hydrogen ions (H⁺)</b> are absorbed faster than free amino acids. Peptidases inside the cell then split them into amino acids.<br>A. Free amino acids with sodium: real (secondary active transport) but slower.<br>B. Free amino acids with H⁺: not a pathway.<br>C. Di- and tripeptides with sodium: not a pathway; peptides ride with H⁺.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":151,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":184,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"227"},{"id":"D024-76-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding protein digestion or absorption?","o":["A. Amino acid absorption from the GI lumen uses only a sodium symporter, while the movement of amino acids via these epithelial cells into blood utilizes facilitated diffusion.","B. Cystic fibrosis can result in the deficiency of proteases, leading to impaired digestion and/or absorption of proteins.","C. Activation of pancreatic proteases begins with the conversion of trypsinogen to trypsin by a brush border enterokinase.","D. Some amino acids are absorbed from the GI lumen in a di- or tripeptide form, most of which are cleaved by cytosolic proteases once inside the epithelial cell before transport into blood."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Amino acid absorption from the GI lumen uses only a sodium symporter, while the movement of amino acids via these epithelial cells into blood utilizes facilitated diffusion.","a":"A","why":"<b>A is incorrect.</b> Free amino acids enter with sodium, but much protein enters as <b>dipeptides and tripeptides with hydrogen ions (H⁺)</b>, so absorption doesn't use 'only a sodium symporter'.<br>B. True: in cystic fibrosis, thick secretions block the ducts, so proteases never reach the gut.<br>C. True: brush-border <b>enterokinase</b> turns trypsinogen into trypsin, which activates the rest.<br>D. True: peptidases inside the cell split di- and tripeptides, then amino acids exit to blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 52 (Absorption of Proteins)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":76,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-152-1","kind":"harder","n":"C7 extra 48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":62,"label":"Intracellular peptidases finish di/tripeptides","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-54.jpg","cap":"Small peptides split by intracellular peptidases into amino acids","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 54"},{"src":"../sl/G045-40.jpg","cap":"Brush border peptidase digests di/tripeptides INTRAcellularly","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-102-1","s":"During protein absorption, most of the protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide form using a H+-cotransporter rather than in the amino acid form which uses a Na+-cotransporter. Which enzyme is used to hydrolyze dipeptides and tripeptides into amino acids inside the intestinal cell?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Elastase","C. Pepsin","D. Peptidase","E. Carboxypeptidase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peptidase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Peptidase</b> (cytoplasmic peptidases inside the enterocyte). The <b>last step</b> of protein digestion is <b>intracellular</b>: di- and tripeptides are split to amino acids, which then leave to blood.<br>A. Trypsin: pancreatic endopeptidase working in the lumen.<br>B. Elastase: pancreatic endopeptidase in the lumen.<br>C. Pepsin: stomach enzyme; inactive above pH five.<br>E. Carboxypeptidase: pancreatic exopeptidase in the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 40 (Protein Absorption: Unique Properties)","L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":102,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"228"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":63,"label":"Cystinuria: no basic amino acid carrier (COAL)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-59.jpg","cap":"Cystinuria: can't absorb basic amino acids cystine, ornithine, arginine, lysine","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 59"},{"src":"../sl/D007-148.jpg","cap":"Cystinuria: dibasic amino acid transport defect in gut and proximal tubule","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 148"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-191-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING DISORDERS ARE CHARACTERIZED BY THE ABSENCE OF THE DIBASIC AMINO ACID TRANSPORTER IN THE SMALL INTESTINE AND THE KIDNEY?","o":["A. CHRONIC PANCREATITIS","B. CYSTIC FIBROSIS","C. CYSTINURIA","D. CELIAC DISEASE"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CYSTINURIA","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cystinuria.</b> The carrier for <b>basic amino acids</b>, 'COAL' (cystine, ornithine, arginine, lysine), is missing in the small intestine and the kidney. Cystine stones form and dissolve with alkalinization.<br>A. Chronic pancreatitis: pancreatic enzymes not made.<br>B. Cystic fibrosis: pancreatic enzymes made but not released.<br>D. Celiac disease: gluten-triggered villous atrophy shrinks the absorptive surface.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 59 (Cystinuria)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":191,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-160-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C7 extra 49"},{"id":"D036-160-1","s":"A patient is diagnosed with cystinuria, a genetic disorder characterized by the absence of a transporter for dibasic amino acids (cystine, lysine, arginine, and ornithine) in both the small intestine and kidneys. What is the primary consequence of the intestinal defect in cystinuria?","o":["A. Increased reabsorption of amino acids in the kidneys","B. Decreased excretion of amino acids in the urine","C. Failure to absorb amino acids in the intestine","D. Enhanced absorption of cystine in the small intestine","E. Improved transport of amino acids across the intestinal epithelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure to absorb amino acids in the intestine","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The missing carrier for <b>basic (dibasic) amino acids</b>, cystine, ornithine, arginine and lysine ('COAL'), means the gut <b>fails to absorb them</b>. The gut defect isn't felt, since they still enter as di- and tripeptides; the kidney defect causes cystine stones.<br>A, B. Opposite: the kidney reabsorbs less, so urine excretion rises.<br>D, E. Opposite: absorption is reduced, not enhanced.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 59 (Cystinuria)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":160,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"229"},{"id":"D040-163-1","s":"A 65-year-old man with a longstanding history of alcohol use presents with epigastric abdominal pain that radiates to the back. After a few imaging and laboratory studies, he is diagnosed with chronic pancreatitis. Which of the following clinical features is least likely related to his chronic pancreatitis?","o":["A. Deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins","B. Steatorrhea","C. Pancreatic diabetes","D. Cystinuria","E. Unintentional weight loss"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystinuria","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Cystinuria</b> is an inherited <b>amino acid transporter defect</b> (gut and kidney) for cystine, ornithine, arginine and lysine. It has nothing to do with the pancreas.<br>A. Fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency: from lipase loss.<br>B. Steatorrhea: fat malabsorption from lipase loss.<br>C. Pancreatic diabetes: islet cells are destroyed along with the gland.<br>E. Weight loss: from malabsorption and pain with eating.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 59 (Cystinuria)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":163,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-160-1","kind":"harder","n":"C7 extra 50"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":63,"label":"CF: all pancreatic enzymes made, not released","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-58.jpg","cap":"CF enzymes made but not released; chronic pancreatitis enzymes not made","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 58"},{"src":"../sl/G045-57.jpg","cap":"Clogged ducts block release of all pancreatic enzymes","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 57"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-106-1","s":"Which disorder is represented by a deficiency in all pancreatic enzymes, including proteases?","o":["A. Cystic Fibrosis","B. Cystinuria","C. Lactase Deficiency","D. All of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic Fibrosis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cystic fibrosis.</b> Thick secretions block the pancreatic ducts, so <b>all pancreatic enzymes</b>, proteases included, are made but <b>never released</b> into the duodenum.<br>B. Cystinuria: one amino acid transporter is missing; enzymes are normal.<br>C. Lactase deficiency: one brush-border enzyme is missing.<br>D. All of the above: only CF affects every pancreatic enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)","L55 slide 59 (Cystinuria)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":106,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"230"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":64,"label":"Fat digestion starts in stomach, mostly intestine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-4.jpg","cap":"Stomach: lingual and gastric lipases digest only about ten percent of fat","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G046-5.jpg","cap":"Small intestine: bile salts emulsify; pancreatic lipase splits triglyceride to monoglyceride plus fatty acids","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-95-1","s":"Where does lipid digestion first takes place:","o":["A. Stomach","B. Liver","C. Mouth","D. Large Intestine","E. Small Intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Stomach.</b> Fat digestion begins in the <b>stomach</b>, where <b>lingual and gastric lipases</b> digest about ten percent of dietary fat.<br>B. Liver: makes bile salts but digests no fat itself.<br>C. Mouth: lingual lipase is secreted here, but food leaves too fast; the enzyme does its work in the stomach.<br>D. Large intestine: fat is already absorbed before chyme gets there.<br>E. Small intestine: does most fat digestion, but it is not the first site.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":95,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"231"},{"id":"D036-164-1","s":"In the digestion of lipids, what is the primary role of lingual and gastric lipases?","o":["A. Hydrolyzing triglycerides to monoglycerides and fatty acids","B. Activating pancreatic lipases","C. Absorbing lipids in the small intestine","D. Enhancing gastric emptying","E. Inhibiting the secretion of CCK"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydrolyzing triglycerides to monoglycerides and fatty acids","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Lingual and gastric lipases <b>start splitting triglycerides</b> into fatty acids in the stomach. They digest only about <b>ten percent</b> of fat; pancreatic lipase does the rest.<br>B. Activating pancreatic lipase: pancreatic lipase is secreted already active.<br>C. Absorbing lipids: enzymes digest; intestinal cells absorb.<br>D. Enhancing gastric emptying: fat in the duodenum slows emptying through cholecystokinin (CCK).<br>E. Inhibiting CCK secretion: fatty acids and monoglycerides stimulate CCK; they do not inhibit it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":164,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-95-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 1"},{"id":"G066-202-1","s":"Where does most lipid digestion occur?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Small intestine","C. Large intestine","D. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Small intestine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Small intestine.</b> Most fat is digested in the <b>small intestine</b> by <b>pancreatic lipase</b>, after bile salts emulsify the fat into small droplets.<br>A. Stomach: lingual and gastric lipases digest only about ten percent of fat.<br>C. Large intestine: fat is digested and absorbed before chyme gets there.<br>D. Colon: the same organ as the large intestine; no fat digestion there.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)","L56 slide 5 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":202,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":201,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-95-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":64,"label":"Bile salts emulsify fat and form micelles","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-11.jpg","cap":"Bile breaks clumped fat into small droplets so enzymes can work","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Bile salts split fat into droplets; micelle heads face water, tails inward","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-99-1","s":"Bile acids are essential for the digestion and absorption of lipids because they:","o":["A. Directly hydrolyze triglycerides into monoglycerides and fatty acids","B. Increase the surface area for pancreatic lipase activity by emulsifying lipids","C. Transport fatty acids through the intestinal epithelium via facilitated diffusion","D. Stimulate the release of secretin to enhance lipid breakdown"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase the surface area for pancreatic lipase activity by emulsifying lipids","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Bile acids <b>emulsify</b> fat: they break big fat blobs into tiny droplets, giving <b>pancreatic lipase more surface area</b>. Later they also form micelles that carry the digestion products to the cell.<br>A. Hydrolyze triglycerides: that is lipase's job; bile has no enzyme activity.<br>C. Facilitated transport of fatty acids: fatty acids leave the micelle and cross the membrane by simple diffusion; bile salts stay in the lumen.<br>D. Secretin release: acid in the duodenum releases secretin, which brings bicarbonate; it does not break down fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 5 (Small Intestine)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":99,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 3"},{"id":"D067-101-1","s":"Which of the following best describes how dietary fats are emulsified in the small intestine?","o":["A. Mechanical digestion in the stomach breaks triglycerides into micelles","B. Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides into glycerol before emulsification","C. Bile salts surround and disperse large lipid droplets into smaller droplets","D. Villi of the small intestine physically fragment fat particles","E. Enterocytes secrete colipase to emulsify fats prior to absorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts surround and disperse large lipid droplets into smaller droplets","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Bile salts surround and disperse large lipid droplets into smaller droplets.</b> Bile salts coat fat drops, and their charges keep the small droplets apart. This is <b>emulsification</b>; it gives lipase more surface.<br>A. Stomach churning mixes fat but makes no micelles; micelles form later from lipase's products.<br>B. Lipase works after emulsification and leaves a monoglyceride and two fatty acids, not glycerol.<br>D. Villi add absorptive surface; they do not break up fat.<br>E. Colipase comes from the pancreas (as procolipase, which trypsin activates) and anchors lipase; it does not emulsify.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 5 (Small Intestine)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":101,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 4"},{"id":"G052-11-3","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: What role do bile salts play in the absorption of dietary lipids?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile salts emulsify dietary fat (more surface area for lipases) and form micelles that carry the digestion products to the brush border, where the lipids diffuse into the cell.","a":null,"why":"Bile salts do two jobs. First, they <b>emulsify</b> fat into small droplets, giving pancreatic lipase more surface. Second, they <b>form micelles</b> that wrap monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol and lysolecithin and carry them to the brush border, where the lipids <b>diffuse</b> into the cell.<br>Common mix-up: bile salts are <b>not absorbed</b> with the fat; they stay in the lumen and are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. That is why Paul's ileal resection caused steatorrhea (fat in stool).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"},{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":65,"label":"Pancreatic lipase removes two fatty acids","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-6.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic lipase: secreted active, removes two of three fatty acids, leaves position-two monoglyceride","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G046-4.jpg","cap":"Lingual lipase removes all three fatty acids from a triglyceride","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-45-1","s":"Which of the following is the DIFFERENCE between pancreatic lipase and lingual lipase?","o":["A. a. Number of fatty acids removed","B. b. Need for trypsin","C. c. Activation by enterokinase","D. d. Lactase as cofactor","E. e. Secretion as active form"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"a. Number of fatty acids removed","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Number of fatty acids removed.</b> In the 2026 lecture, <b>lingual lipase removes all three</b> fatty acids from a triglyceride, while <b>pancreatic lipase removes two of three</b>, leaving a position-two monoglyceride.<br>B. Need for trypsin: neither needs trypsin; both are secreted active.<br>C. Activation by enterokinase: enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not lipases.<br>D. Lactase as cofactor: lactase is a brush-border sugar enzyme, not a lipase cofactor.<br>E. Secretion as active form: both are secreted active, so this is not a difference.","ex":"","fl":["Key (A) is correct, but the explanation is reversed: per the 2026 deck LINGUAL lipase removes all three fatty acids (G046:4) while PANCREATIC lipase removes two (positions 1 and 3), leaving a 2-monoglyceride (G046:6)."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)","L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":45,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-133-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 6"},{"id":"D067-105-1","s":"Which enzyme is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of triglycerides into monoglycerides and free fatty acids in the small intestine?","o":["A. Lingual lipase","B. Pancreatic lipase","C. Gastric lipase","D. Phospholipase A2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreatic lipase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pancreatic lipase.</b> Most triglyceride is digested in the small intestine by <b>pancreatic lipase</b>. It removes two of the three fatty acids and leaves a monoglyceride.<br>A. Lingual lipase: comes with saliva; it and gastric lipase together digest only about 10 percent of fat, in the stomach.<br>C. Gastric lipase: the same minor, stomach-based role.<br>D. Phospholipase A2: a pancreatic enzyme that cuts phospholipids, not triglycerides, leaving lysolecithin and a fatty acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 5 (Small Intestine)","L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":105,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-133-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 7"},{"id":"D008-133-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices regarding pancreatic lipase is correct?","o":["A. Pancreatic lipase is inactivated by excessive bicarbonate secretion","B. Pancreatic lipase is secreted in the jejunum","C. Pancreatic lipase is secreted as a zymogen","D. Pancreatic lipase digestion of triglycerides yields a position 2 monoglyceride","E. Fat digestion is more dependent on lingual lipase than pancreatic lipase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreatic lipase digestion of triglycerides yields a position 2 monoglyceride","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Pancreatic lipase removes <b>two of three fatty acids</b>, leaving a <b>position-two monoglyceride</b>.<br>A. Inactivated by bicarbonate: it is inactivated by <b>acid</b> (pH below five); bicarbonate protects it.<br>B. Secreted in the jejunum: the pancreas secretes it into the duodenum.<br>C. Secreted as a zymogen (inactive precursor): it is secreted active, unlike the proteases.<br>E. More dependent on lingual lipase: lingual and gastric lipases digest only about ten percent of fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":133,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":133,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":301,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"232"},{"id":"D067-107-1","s":"What are the main products formed after pancreatic lipase acts on triglycerides during fat digestion?","o":["A. Diglycerides and glycerol","B. Monoglycerides and free fatty acids","C. Fatty acids and cholesterol","D. Lysophospholipids and free fatty acids"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Monoglycerides and free fatty acids","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Monoglycerides and free fatty acids.</b> Pancreatic lipase cuts the two outer fatty acids off a triglyceride. Each triglyceride gives <b>two free fatty acids and one 2-monoglyceride</b> (the middle fatty acid stays on).<br>A. Diglycerides and glycerol: a diglyceride is only a halfway step, and lipase does not go all the way to glycerol.<br>C. Fatty acids and cholesterol: that is cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters.<br>D. Lysophospholipids and fatty acids: that is phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L54 slide 25 (Biliary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":107,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-133-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 8"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":65,"label":"Duodenal acid inactivates lipase (Zollinger-Ellison)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"No lipase, no lipid absorption; lipase is inactivated by acid below pH five","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: steatorrhea because acid inactivates lipase","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-97-1","s":"Which of the following is most favorable for adequate lipid digestion:","o":["A. Decrease lumen PH, activated Gastic enzymes, Bile Salts","B. Increase Lumen PH, activated Pancreatic enzymes, Bile Salts","C. No change in PH, inactivated Pancreatic enzymes, no bile salts","D. Decrease Lumen PH, activated Pancreatic enzymes, Bile Salts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase Lumen PH, activated Pancreatic enzymes, Bile Salts","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Fat digestion needs the duodenum <b>neutralized by bicarbonate (higher pH)</b>, <b>active pancreatic enzymes</b>, and <b>bile salts</b> to emulsify fat. Lipase is inactivated below about pH five.<br>A. Lower pH with gastric enzymes: acid inactivates pancreatic lipase, and gastric enzymes digest little fat.<br>C. Inactive enzymes, no bile salts: almost no fat would be digested.<br>D. Lower pH: acid destroys the pancreatic enzymes even if they start out active.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L56 slide 5 (Small Intestine)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":97,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"233"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":65,"label":"Colipase: trypsin-activated, pushes bile salts aside","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-8.jpg","cap":"Trypsin cuts procolipase into colipase plus enterostatin, the hunger-reducing peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G046-11.jpg","cap":"Colipase displaces bile salts so lipase can touch the fat","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-168-1","s":"What is the primary consequence of bile salts on pancreatic lipase activity, and how is this potential problem addressed during lipid digestion?","o":["A. Bile salts activate pancreatic lipase, facilitating lipid digestion","B. Bile salts inhibit pancreatic lipase, causing lipid malabsorption","C. Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase, reducing lipid digestion","D. Bile salts enhance pancreatic lipase secretion, promoting lipid absorption","E. Bile salts convert pancreatic lipase into its inactive form"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase, reducing lipid digestion","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Bile salts coat the fat droplet and <b>push pancreatic lipase off the lipid-water surface</b>, so lipase can't touch the fat. <b>Colipase</b> fixes this by displacing the bile salts and anchoring lipase.<br>A. Activate lipase: lipase is secreted active; bile salts only get in its way.<br>B. Inhibit lipase, causing malabsorption: colipase prevents the problem, so no malabsorption normally occurs.<br>D. Enhance lipase secretion: cholecystokinin (CCK) drives enzyme secretion, not bile salts.<br>E. Convert lipase to an inactive form: bile salts block access; they don't change the enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 11 (Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":168,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 9"},{"id":"D036-170-1","s":"How is colipase activated, and what role does it play in the process of lipid digestion?","o":["A. Activated by gastric acid, enhances lipid absorption in the stomach","B. Activated by pancreatic lipase, inhibits bile salt secretion","C. Activated by bile salts, facilitates lipid digestion in the small intestine","D. Activated by trypsin, displaces bile salts to promote lipid digestion","E. Activated by enterostatin, decreases hunger and fat intake"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Activated by trypsin, displaces bile salts to promote lipid digestion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Colipase is secreted as <b>procolipase</b> and activated by <b>trypsin</b> in the duodenum. It then <b>displaces bile salts</b> so pancreatic lipase can reach the fat.<br>A. Activated by gastric acid: acid inactivates pancreatic enzymes.<br>B. Activated by lipase, inhibits bile secretion: neither is true.<br>C. Activated by bile salts: bile salts are what colipase pushes aside.<br>E. Activated by enterostatin: enterostatin is the small piece cut off procolipase; it reduces hunger but does not activate colipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 8 (Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 11 (Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":170,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"234"},{"id":"D008-131-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices best describes the function of colipase?","o":["A. Removes 2 out of 3 fatty acids in a triglyceride molecule","B. Displaces bile salts from triglycerides","C. Pulls the fatty acid off of cholesterol","D. Splits phospholipids","E. Removes 1 out of 3 fatty acids in a triglyceride molecule"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Displaces bile salts from triglycerides","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Colipase displaces bile salts</b> from the surface of the fat droplet so pancreatic lipase can touch the triglycerides and do its job.<br>A. Removes two of three fatty acids: that is pancreatic lipase.<br>C. Pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol: that is cholesterol ester hydrolase.<br>D. Splits phospholipids: that is phospholipase A2, which makes lysolecithin.<br>E. Removes one of three fatty acids: no enzyme in the lecture does this; lingual lipase removes all three.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 11 (Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":131,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":131,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":299,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 10"},{"id":"G066-204-1","s":"Which pancreatic enzyme is secreted in inactive form, is activated by trypsin, and once activated displaces bile salts at the lipid-water interface and binds to pancreatic lipase, preventing its inactivation?","o":["A. Cholesterol ester lipase","B. Phospholipase A2","C. Colipase","D. Pancreatic lipase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Colipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Colipase.</b> Made as inactive <b>procolipase</b>, cut into colipase by <b>trypsin</b>; it then <b>displaces bile salts</b> and binds pancreatic lipase to the fat.<br>A. Cholesterol ester lipase (hydrolase): secreted active; it frees cholesterol from its fatty acid.<br>B. Phospholipase A2: also activated by trypsin, but it splits phospholipids into lysolecithin and a fatty acid.<br>D. Pancreatic lipase: secreted active; it is the enzyme colipase protects.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 8 (Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 11 (Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":204,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":203,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 11"},{"id":"D015-193-1","s":"WHAT ENZYME IS ACTIVATED BY TRYPSIN TO PREVENT THE INACTIVATION OF PANCREATIC LIPASE BY BILE SALTS?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"COLIPASE","a":null,"why":"<b>Colipase.</b> The pancreas secretes inactive <b>procolipase</b>, and <b>trypsin</b> activates it in the duodenum. Colipase then <b>pushes bile salts off the fat droplet</b> and anchors pancreatic lipase, so bile salts can't block lipase.<br>Common mix-up: phospholipase A2 is also activated by trypsin, but it digests phospholipids and does not protect lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 8 (Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 11 (Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":193,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-170-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 12"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":65,"label":"Which pancreatic lipid enzymes are secreted active","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-6.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted in active form","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G046-12.jpg","cap":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase pulls fatty acid off cholesterol; secreted active","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G046-13.jpg","cap":"Phospholipase A2 makes lysolecithin; secreted inactive and activated by trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-156-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes an enzyme of lipid digestion and absorption?","o":["A. Gastric lipase - secreted in the mouth along with saliva, and functions to hydrolyze triglycerides into glycerol and free fatty acids","B. Pancreatic lipase - secreted in an inactive form and hydrolyzes triglycerides into a monoglyceride and free fatty acids","C. Colipase - secreted in an active form and hydrolyzes phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids","D. Cholesterol ester hydrolase - secreted in an active form and hydrolyzes cholesterol ester to free cholesterol and fatty acids","E. Phospholipase A2 - secreted as a proenzyme and displaces bile salts at the lipid-water interface"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase - secreted in an active form and hydrolyzes cholesterol ester to free cholesterol and fatty acids","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> is secreted <b>active</b> and pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol ester, giving free cholesterol plus a fatty acid.<br>A. Gastric lipase: made by gastric chief cells in the stomach; lingual lipase is the one from the mouth.<br>B. Pancreatic lipase: secreted active, not inactive.<br>C. Colipase: has no enzyme action; splitting phospholipids is phospholipase A2's job.<br>E. Phospholipase A2: is secreted inactive, but displacing bile salts is colipase's job.","ex":"","fl":["Key (D) is correct; explanation slip: colipase is secreted as INACTIVE procolipase and activated by trypsin (G046:8)."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 12 (Pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol)","L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 13 (Phospholipase A2)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":156,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"235"},{"id":"D024-78-1","s":"Which of the following pancreatic enzymes is secreted in its active form?","o":["A. A. Pancreatic Lipase","B. B. Colipase","C. C. Phospholipase A2","D. D. Cholesterol ester hydrolase","E. E. A and B","F. F. B and C","G. G. A and C","H. H. A and D","I. I. A, C, and D"],"g":["H"],"k":["H"],"rv":false,"at":"A and D","a":"H","why":"<b>H. A and D.</b> <b>Pancreatic lipase</b> and <b>cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> are secreted <b>active</b>; colipase and phospholipase A2 are secreted inactive and need <b>trypsin</b>.<br>A. Lipase alone: true but incomplete.<br>B. Colipase: secreted as inactive procolipase.<br>C. Phospholipase A2: secreted inactive.<br>D. Cholesterol ester hydrolase alone: true but incomplete.<br>E. A and B: colipase is inactive.<br>F. B and C: both are inactive.<br>G. A and C: phospholipase A2 is inactive.<br>I. A, C and D: phospholipase A2 is inactive.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)","L56 slide 12 (Pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol)","L56 slide 13 (Phospholipase A2)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":78,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-156-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 13"},{"id":"D004-240-1","s":"Is PLA2 produced as a zymogen?","o":["A. Yes, and it is activated by enterokinase","B. Yes, and it is activated by trypsin","C. No","D. No, but it is not active until the environment pH is basic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes, and it is activated by trypsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Yes. <b>Phospholipase A2</b> is secreted <b>inactive</b> (a zymogen, meaning an inactive precursor), and <b>trypsin</b> activates it. It then splits phospholipids into lysolecithin and a fatty acid.<br>A. Enterokinase: activates trypsinogen; trypsin then activates phospholipase A2.<br>C. No: lipase and cholesterol ester hydrolase are secreted active, but phospholipase A2 is not.<br>D. Active only at basic pH: it needs cutting by trypsin, not just a pH change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 13 (Phospholipase A2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":46,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":240,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":40,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"236"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":66,"label":"Micelles: bile-salt shell around lipid products","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-19.jpg","cap":"Micelles of monoglyceride, cholesterol, fatty acids, lysolecithin coated in bile salts; enter by diffusion","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Micelle: water-loving heads outside, fat-loving tails inside around the lipid core","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D010-133.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: bile-salt micelle carries cholesterol, monoglyceride, lysophospholipid and fatty acid","where":"2021 Middletown TA review, slide 133"}],"q":[{"id":"D010-131-1","s":"What would be seen on the micelle exterior and interior respectively?","o":["A. Exterior - Cholesterol, Interior - Bile acids","B. Exterior - Triglycerides, Interior - Bile acids","C. Exterior - Bile acids, Interior - Monoglycerides","D. Exterior - Bile acids, Interior - Triglycerides"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Exterior - Bile acids, Interior - Monoglycerides","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A micelle is a <b>single layer of bile salts</b>: their water-loving sides face <b>outward</b>, and their fat-loving sides face a <b>core of monoglycerides</b>, fatty acids, cholesterol and lysolecithin.<br>A. Cholesterol outside, bile acids inside: reversed; cholesterol sits in the core.<br>B. Triglycerides outside: triglycerides are digested before micelles form, and bile acids form the shell.<br>D. Triglycerides inside: the core holds digestion products (monoglycerides), not intact triglycerides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 21 (Micelles are a MONO-layer)","L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":131,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":131,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":111,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":1,"n":"237"},{"id":"D024-80-1","s":"All of the following lipid metabolism intermediates are mixed in micelles in the intestinal lumen, except?","o":["A. Lysolecithin","B. Free fatty acids","C. Glycerol","D. Monoglycerides","E. cholesterol"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glycerol","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Glycerol.</b> Micelles hold the fat-soluble products: <b>monoglycerides, free fatty acids, cholesterol and lysolecithin</b>, wrapped in bile salts. Glycerol is <b>water-soluble</b>, so it needs no micelle.<br>A. Lysolecithin: made by phospholipase A2; carried in micelles.<br>B. Free fatty acids: carried in micelles.<br>D. Monoglycerides: pancreatic lipase leaves a position-two monoglyceride; carried in micelles.<br>E. Cholesterol: freed by cholesterol ester hydrolase; carried in micelles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":80,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"238"},{"id":"G066-207-1","s":"How are fats absorbed in the epithelial cells of the small intestine?","o":["A. Bile salts","B. Water","C. Micelles","D. Chylomicrons"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Micelles","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Micelles.</b> Bile-salt <b>micelles</b> ferry monoglycerides, fatty acids and cholesterol to the brush border, where the lipids <b>diffuse</b> into the cell.<br>A. Bile salts: they form the micelle but are <b>not absorbed</b> with the fat.<br>B. Water: lipids don't dissolve in water; that is why micelles are needed.<br>D. Chylomicrons: built inside the cell after absorption; they carry fat out to lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":207,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":206,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-131-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 14"},{"id":"D067-103-1","s":"A micelle formed in the intestinal lumen is best described as:","o":["A. A lipoprotein particle that transports triglycerides into lymphatic vessels","B. A spherical aggregate of bile salts that carries lipid digestion products to the brush border","C. A vesicle produced by enterocytes to transport fats into the bloodstream","D. A droplet of unmodified dietary fat suspended in gastric juice","E. A phospholipid bilayer sphere that enters enterocytes via endocytosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A spherical aggregate of bile salts that carries lipid digestion products to the brush border","a":"B","why":"<b>B. A spherical aggregate of bile salts that carries lipid digestion products to the brush border.</b> A <b>micelle</b> has bile salts on the outside and monoglycerides, fatty acids and cholesterol inside. It ferries them to the cell surface, where they diffuse in.<br>A. Lipoprotein into lymph: that is a chylomicron.<br>C. Vesicle made by enterocytes: chylomicrons are built in the cell and go to lymph, not straight to blood.<br>D. Unmodified fat in gastric juice: an undigested fat droplet.<br>E. Bilayer sphere taken in by endocytosis: a micelle is a single layer and stays in the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 21 (Micelles are a MONO-layer)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":103,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-131-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 15"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":66,"label":"Lingual and pancreatic lipases; products form micelles","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-19.jpg","cap":"Micelles of monoglyceride, cholesterol, fatty acids, lysolecithin coated in bile salts; enter by diffusion","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/D015-165.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: lingual lipase from saliva; lipase-colipase, phospholipase A2, cholesterol ester hydrolase from pancreas","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 165"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-186-1","s":"Lipases, from _______, turn lipids into _______ with the help of _________","o":["A. Pancreas only; micelles; unicorns","B. Pancreas and salivary; micelles; bile acids","C. Salivary only; micelles; Dr. Binstock","D. Pancreas only; chylomicrons; love","E. Pancreas and salivary; chylomicrons; bile acids","F. Salivary only; chylomicrons; magic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas and salivary; micelles; bile acids","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Fat-digesting lipases come from the <b>salivary glands (lingual lipase)</b> and the <b>pancreas (pancreatic lipase)</b>. Their products (fatty acids, monoglycerides) gather into <b>micelles</b> coated with <b>bile acids (bile salts)</b>.<br>A. Pancreas only: leaves out lingual lipase.<br>C. Salivary only: leaves out pancreatic lipase, which does most of the work.<br>D. Pancreas only; chylomicrons: chylomicrons are built inside intestinal cells, not by lipases.<br>E. Chylomicrons: same error; lipase products form micelles in the lumen.<br>F. Salivary only; chylomicrons: wrong on both counts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 4 (Lipid Digestion)","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L52 slide 30 (Special Content of Saliva)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":186,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":67,"label":"Chylomicron size and coat composition","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-27.jpg","cap":"Chylomicron: about one thousand angstroms; mostly triglyceride, apoprotein B-48 on surface","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G046-28.jpg","cap":"Chylomicron covering is eighty percent phospholipid and twenty percent apoprotein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-161-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes chylomicrons?","o":["A. Have an average diameter of ~50 Å","B. The core is composed phospholipids and apoproteins","C. The outside is composed of triglycerides and cholesterol","D. Abetalipoproteinemia is a disease of excessive chylomicron absorption","E. Phospholipids cover 80% of the outside and the remaining 20% is covered with apolipoproteins"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Phospholipids cover 80% of the outside and the remaining 20% is covered with apolipoproteins","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> The chylomicron <b>covering is eighty percent phospholipid and twenty percent apoprotein</b>.<br>A. Diameter about fifty angstroms: it is about <b>one thousand angstroms</b>.<br>B. Core of phospholipids and apoproteins: those form the coat; the core is triglyceride (eighty-five to ninety-two percent) and cholesterol.<br>C. Outside of triglycerides and cholesterol: that is the core.<br>D. Excess chylomicron absorption: abetalipoproteinemia is a <b>failure to make apoprotein B</b>, so chylomicrons can't form and fat isn't absorbed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 28 (This is the CONTENTS of the chylomicron)","L56 slide 27 (Chylomicron)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":161,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"239"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":67,"label":"No apoprotein B, no chylomicrons","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-29.jpg","cap":"No apoprotein B, no absorption; apoprotein B is made in intestinal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G046-26.jpg","cap":"Chylomicron surface studded with apoproteins, including several apoprotein B molecules","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D007-162.jpg","cap":"TA summary: apoprotein B-48 from gut cells; abetalipoproteinemia blocks dietary lipid absorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 162"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-172-1","s":"In the synthesis of chylomicrons, which component is synthesized by intestinal epithelial cells and is essential for their absorption?","o":["A. Triglycerides","B. Cholesterol","C. Phospholipids","D. Apoproteins","E. Lipoproteins"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Apoproteins","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Apoproteins.</b> <b>Apoprotein B is made in the intestinal epithelial cells</b> and coats the chylomicron. No apoprotein B, no chylomicron export, no fat absorption.<br>A. Triglycerides: rebuilt in the cell, but they are the cargo, not what makes export possible.<br>B. Cholesterol: cargo inside the chylomicron.<br>C. Phospholipids: part of the coat, but the essential piece is apoprotein B.<br>E. Lipoproteins: the chylomicron itself is a lipoprotein; it is the product, not a component.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":172,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"240"},{"id":"G055-13-1","s":"Abetalipoproteinemia is a rare inherited disorder in which;","o":["a. There is a normal absorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins from food","b. The intestinal epithelial cells are unable to package chylomicrons","c. There is overexpression of apoproteins, leading to the absorption of chylomicrons","d. Dietary lipids are enclosed by protein Apo B"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The intestinal epithelial cells are unable to package chylomicrons","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> In <b>abetalipoproteinemia</b>, intestinal cells can't make <b>apoprotein B</b>, so they <b>can't package chylomicrons</b>; fat stays trapped in the cells.<br>A. Normal fat absorption: absorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E and K falls.<br>C. Overexpression of apoproteins: the problem is a lack of apoprotein B.<br>D. Lipids enclosed by apoprotein B: that describes normal chylomicron packaging, the very step that fails.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":16,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":13,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":211,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":13,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"241"},{"id":"D067-111-1","s":"Which of the following conditions is characterized by a genetic defect in apolipoprotein B synthesis, leading to impaired chylomicron formation and severe fat malabsorption?","o":["A. Pancreatic insufficiency","B. Bacterial overgrowth syndrome","C. Abetalipoproteinemia","D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Abetalipoproteinemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Abetalipoproteinemia.</b> In this inherited disease the intestinal cell cannot make <b>apoprotein B</b> (apoB) lipoproteins. Without apoB it cannot package chylomicrons, so absorbed fat stays trapped in the cell and fat is lost in stool.<br>A. Pancreatic insufficiency: too little lipase, so fat is not digested; apoB is normal.<br>B. Bacterial overgrowth: bacteria deconjugate bile salts, so micelles fail.<br>D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: a gastrin tumor; its excess acid inactivates lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":111,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-172-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 17"},{"id":"D036-179-1","s":"What is the consequence of a failure to synthesize Apoprotein B in abetalipoproteinemia, specifically concerning the absorption of lipids in the small intestine?","o":["A. Increased absorption of lipids into the blood","B. Formation of chylomicrons with enhanced transport out of intestinal cells","C. No impact on the absorption of lipids","D. Decreased absorption of lipids into the blood","E. Reduced lipid buildup within intestinal cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased absorption of lipids into the blood","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Without <b>apoprotein B</b>, chylomicrons can't form or leave the intestinal cell, so <b>less fat reaches the lymph and blood</b>.<br>A. Increased absorption: the opposite.<br>B. Better chylomicron export: chylomicrons can't form.<br>C. No impact: fat absorption fails.<br>E. Less lipid buildup in the cells: fat <b>builds up</b> inside the intestinal cells because it can't be exported.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":179,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-172-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 18"},{"id":"D061-106-1","s":"A patient presents with a history of abetalipoproteinemia. What would you expect to occur in the patient’s condition?","o":["A. Decreased cholesterol synthesis","B. Increased triglyceride production","C. Unstable LDL receptor","D. Decreased chylomicron formation"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased chylomicron formation","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Abetalipoproteinemia means <b>no apoprotein B</b>, so intestinal cells <b>can't form chylomicrons</b> and dietary fat isn't absorbed.<br>A. Less cholesterol synthesis: the defect is in packaging fat, not making cholesterol.<br>B. More triglyceride production: blood triglycerides are actually very low.<br>C. Unstable LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor: that is familial hypercholesterolemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":106,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-172-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 19"},{"id":"D007-171-1","s":"A patient presents with abetalipoproteinemia. What is the origin of this patients disorder?","o":["A. Synthesize cholesterol","B. Absorb dietary lipids","C. Transport dietary glucose","D. LDL receptor defect"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorb dietary lipids","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Absorb dietary lipids.</b> No <b>apoprotein B</b> means no chylomicrons, so the gut <b>can't absorb dietary fat</b> into lymph and blood.<br>A. Synthesize cholesterol: not the defect.<br>C. Transport glucose: glucose uses sodium-glucose cotransport and is unaffected.<br>D. LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor defect: that is familial hypercholesterolemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":171,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":134,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":134,"f":"G064"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":114,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-172-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 20"},{"id":"D015-195-1","s":"WHICH DISORDER CAUSES THE INABILITY TO ABSORB CHYLOMICRONS AND THEREFORE DIETARY LIPIDS?","o":["A. TROPICAL SPRUE","B. ABETALIPOPROTEINEMIA","C. CYSTIC FIBROSIS","D. OSTEOMALACIA"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ABETALIPOPROTEINEMIA","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Abetalipoproteinemia.</b> Failure to make <b>apoprotein B</b> means no chylomicrons, so dietary lipids can't leave the intestinal cell.<br>A. Tropical sprue: an infection that destroys villi and cuts absorptive surface.<br>C. Cystic fibrosis: clogged pancreatic ducts block lipase release, so fat isn't digested.<br>D. Osteomalacia: soft bones from low vitamin D; a result of fat malabsorption, not a cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":195,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-172-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 21"},{"id":"D024-82-1","s":"A patient presents with a deficiency in vitamins A, D, E, and K. Upon clinical work-up, the patient does not have any signs of structural damage to the GI tract. Which of the following best explains why the deficiency?","o":["A. The patient has a deficiency in the chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidase A, but all other pancreatic enzymes are functioning properly.","B. The patient fails to synthesize B-lipoprotein","C. The patient is unable to absorb chylomicrons directly from intestinal epithelium to blood, causing a decrease in fat-soluble vitamin absorption.","D. The patient is experiencing an infection with a bacteria producing a toxin binding to alpha subunit of a GPCR leading to increased cAMP and decreasing water soluble vitamin absorption."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The patient fails to synthesize B-lipoprotein","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> \"B-lipoprotein\" means <b>apoprotein B</b>. Without it (<b>abetalipoproteinemia</b>), intestinal cells can't make chylomicrons, so fat and vitamins A, D, E and K aren't absorbed, even though the gut looks normal.<br>A. Missing chymotrypsin, elastase and carboxypeptidase A: these digest protein, not fat.<br>C. Chylomicrons go directly to blood: they never do; they enter lymph.<br>D. Toxin raising cyclic AMP (adenosine monophosphate), like cholera: causes secretory diarrhea, not loss of fat-soluble vitamins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":82,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"242"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":67,"label":"Chylomicrons exit via lacteals and thoracic duct","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-31.jpg","cap":"Chylomicron formed in the intestinal cell exits into the central lacteal","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G046-24.jpg","cap":"Cisterna chyli and thoracic duct carry chylomicron-rich lymph into the neck veins","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-8-35","s":"Dietary fat, after being processed, is extruded from the mucosal cells of the gastrointestinal tract into the lymphatic ducts in the form of:","o":["a. Monoglycerides","b. Diglycerides","c. Triglycerides","d. Chylomicrons"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Chylomicrons","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Chylomicrons.</b> Inside the cell, fats are re-esterified and packed into <b>chylomicrons</b>, which are too big for blood capillaries and enter <b>lacteals</b> (lymphatic ducts).<br>A. Monoglycerides: enter the cell from micelles, then get rebuilt into triglycerides.<br>B. Diglycerides: not the export form.<br>C. Triglycerides: they leave packed inside chylomicrons, not on their own.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)","L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":8,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-167-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 22"},{"id":"D007-167-1","s":"During surgical intervention for a rare benign aortic growth the thoracic duct is accidentally blocked. What effect will this have on the processes of digestion and absorption ?","o":["A. Decrease in bile recirculation","B. Decrease in lymph circulation","C. Decreased lipid absorption into the bloodstream","D. No effect"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased lipid absorption into the bloodstream","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Chylomicrons enter <b>lacteals</b> (lymph) and reach the blood only at the <b>thoracic duct</b>. Block the duct and dietary fat can't reach the bloodstream.<br>A. Less bile recirculation: bile salts return to the liver by the portal vein, not the thoracic duct.<br>B. Less lymph circulation: lymph does back up, but the question asks about digestion and absorption; the key effect is on fat absorption.<br>D. No effect: fat absorption depends on this route.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)","L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":167,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":195,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"243"},{"id":"D067-109-1","s":"Chylomicrons enter systemic circulation primarily through which of the following pathways?","o":["A. Direct absorption into blood capillaries in the intestinal villi","B. Direct secretion into the bile ducts","C. Passage through the hepatic portal vein to the liver","D. Entry into the lacteals and transport via the lymphatic system"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Entry into the lacteals and transport via the lymphatic system","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Entry into the lacteals and transport via the lymphatic system.</b> Chylomicrons are too large to enter blood capillaries. They enter the <b>lacteal</b> (the lymph vessel in each villus), travel in lymph, and reach the blood at the <b>thoracic duct</b>.<br>A. Villus blood capillaries: these take sugars and amino acids; chylomicrons do not fit.<br>B. Bile ducts: they carry bile from the liver to the gut.<br>C. Portal vein: it carries water-soluble nutrients to the liver; chylomicrons bypass the liver at first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)","L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":109,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-167-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":67,"label":"Absorption order: micelle, cell, chylomicron, lymph","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-19.jpg","cap":"Micelles coated in bile salts carry lipids to the brush border; entry by diffusion","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G046-31.jpg","cap":"Inside the cell, monoglycerides and fatty acids become chylomicrons that enter the central lacteal","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/D010-133.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: micelle, diffusion, re-esterification, chylomicron, exocytosis to lymph (thoracic duct)","where":"2021 Middletown TA review, slide 133"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-158-1","s":"Which of the following is the correct order for lipid absorption in the small intestine?","o":["A. Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries → Re-esterification → Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell → Micelle formation","B. Micelle formation → Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Re-esterification → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries → Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell","C. Micelle formation → Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell → Re-esterification → Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries","D. Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell → Micelle formation → Re-esterification → Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries","E. Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell → Re-esterification → Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries → Micelle formation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Micelle formation → Lipid diffusion into small intestine epithelial cell → Re-esterification → Lipids packaged into chylomicrons → Chylomicron exocytosis into lymphatic capillaries","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Micelle formation</b> in the lumen → lipids <b>diffuse</b> into the intestinal cell → <b>re-esterification</b> → packing into <b>chylomicrons</b> → <b>exocytosis</b> into lymphatic capillaries (lacteals).<br>A. Starts with chylomicrons: that is the last step, not the first.<br>B. Chylomicrons before entering the cell: they form inside the cell.<br>D. Diffusion before micelles: micelles must first carry lipids to the membrane.<br>E. Micelles last: micelle formation is the first step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":158,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"244"},{"id":"F1212-188-1","s":"Choose the correct order of lipid absorption: 1) Chylomicrons, 2) Micelles, 3) Lymph vessels, 4) Free fatty acids/monoglycerides","o":["A. 1, 2, 3, 4","B. 4, 3, 2, 1","C. 3, 2, 1, 4","D. 2, 4, 1, 3"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"2, 4, 1, 3","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 2, 4, 1, 3.</b> <b>Micelles</b> carry the products to the brush border and release <b>free fatty acids and monoglycerides</b>, which diffuse into the cell. These are rebuilt into <b>chylomicrons</b>, which enter <b>lymph vessels</b>.<br>A. 1, 2, 3, 4: chylomicrons cannot come first; they are made inside the cell.<br>B. 4, 3, 2, 1: lymph cannot come before micelles.<br>C. 3, 2, 1, 4: lymph is the last step, not the first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":188,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-158-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 24"},{"id":"G066-209-1","s":"Which of the following is the correct order of lipid digestion?","o":["A. Fats → intestinal epithelial cells → re-esterify → micelles → exocytosis → chylomicrons → blood","B. Fats → micelles → chylomicrons → re-esterify —> intestinal epithelial cells → exocytosis → blood","C. Micelles → intestinal epithelial cells → re-esterify → chylomicrons → exocytosis → blood","D. Micelles → re-esterify → chylomicrons → intestinal epithelial cells → chylomicrons → blood"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Micelles → intestinal epithelial cells → re-esterify → chylomicrons → exocytosis → blood","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Micelles</b> deliver lipids to the <b>intestinal cell</b> → lipids are <b>re-esterified</b> → packed into <b>chylomicrons</b> → leave by <b>exocytosis</b> into lacteals (lymph), reaching the blood at the thoracic duct.<br>A. Re-esterify before micelles: micelles come first, in the lumen.<br>B. Chylomicrons before entering the cell: chylomicrons form inside the cell.<br>D. Chylomicrons before the cell, and listed twice: wrong place and repeated.","ex":"","fl":["Minor wording issue: chylomicrons enter lacteals/lymph (thoracic duct), not 'blood' directly (G046:23); C is still the only correct order."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":209,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":208,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-158-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 25"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":68,"label":"Steatorrhea means fat in the stool","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome lists steatorrhea: acid-inactivated lipase leaves fat in stool","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G066-214.jpg","cap":"Unabsorbed fat excreted in feces is called steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 214"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-70-1","s":"Steatorrhea is defined as","o":["A. Undigested food in the stool","B. Fats excreted in the stool","C. Watery diarrhea","D. Bloody diarrhea"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fats excreted in the stool","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Steatorrhea</b> means <b>excess fat in the stool</b>: oily, greasy, pale, floating and foul-smelling. It happens when fat isn't digested or absorbed, for example when acid inactivates lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.<br>A. Undigested food in stool: too broad; steatorrhea is specifically fat.<br>C. Watery diarrhea: secretory or osmotic diarrhea, like cholera.<br>D. Bloody diarrhea: points to infection or inflammation, not fat malabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 42 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":70,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":68,"label":"No lipase, no fat absorption: pancreatic insufficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"No lipase, no lipid absorption; lipase is inactivated by acid below pH five","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G045-55.jpg","cap":"Chronic pancreatitis: burnt-out pancreas, no enzymes, lipase levels drop, less bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-35-1","s":"A 67-year-old male with a history of alcohol abuse presents to the ED with severe epigastric pain, hypotension, abdominal distension, and diarrhea with steatorrhea. A diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis is made. The steatorrhea can be accounted for by a decrease in the intraluminal concentration of which of the following pancreatic enzymes?","o":["A. Trypsin","B. Amylase","C. Chymotrypsin","D. Colipase","E. Lipase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lipase","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lipase.</b> In chronic pancreatitis the pancreas <b>can't secrete lipase</b>, so triglycerides aren't digested and fat stays in the stool. No lipase, no lipid absorption.<br>A. Trypsin: digests protein.<br>B. Amylase: digests starch.<br>C. Chymotrypsin: digests protein.<br>D. Colipase: a helper protein with no enzyme action of its own; lipase does the actual fat digestion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":35,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":33,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"245"},{"id":"G067-101-1","s":"A 45-year-old man with a history of chronic alcohol use presents with weight loss, abdominal pain, and frequent oily, foul-smelling stools. Laboratory studies reveal low serum albumin and fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies. Imaging shows pancreatic calcifications. Which of the following best explains the patient's symptoms related to lipid digestion?","o":["A. Decreased bile salt synthesis","B. Impaired secretion of pancreatic lipase","C. Increased intestinal motility","D. Loss of brush border enzymes","E. Impaired gastric acid secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired secretion of pancreatic lipase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Chronic alcohol use plus pancreatic calcifications means <b>chronic pancreatitis</b>: the burnt-out pancreas <b>can't secrete lipase</b>. No lipase, no fat digestion, so oily stools and low vitamins A, D, E and K.<br>A. Less bile salt synthesis: the liver is not the problem here.<br>C. Faster intestinal motility: doesn't explain fat malabsorption with a calcified pancreas.<br>D. Loss of brush-border enzymes: those digest sugars and peptides, not fat.<br>E. Less gastric acid: doesn't cause steatorrhea; excess acid does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":101,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 27"},{"id":"G066-212-1","s":"50 y/o male presents to your office with complaints of having foul smelling stools with mucus . His stool sample shows fat in his stools. He also complains of chronic abdominal pain, nause, vomiting, and loose stools. Patient is a chronic alcoholic. Which of the following is a potential cause of his abnormal lipid digestion?","o":["A. Chronic alcoholism","B. Cancer","C. Too much intestinal lipid absorption","D. Deficiency of bile salts"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chronic alcoholism","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chronic alcoholism.</b> Alcohol causes <b>chronic pancreatitis</b>, and the burnt-out pancreas can't secrete enough lipase, so fat stays in the stool.<br>B. Cancer: nothing in the history points to a tumor.<br>C. Too much lipid absorption: that would leave less fat in the stool, not more.<br>D. Bile salt deficiency: can cause fatty stools, but nothing suggests liver disease or ileal resection.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":212,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":211,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 28"},{"id":"G063-6-24","s":"A 67-year-old male with a history of alcohol abuse presents to the ED with severe epigastric pain, hypotension, abdominal distension, and diarrhea with steatorrhea. Serum amylase and lipase are found to be abnormally elevated. A diagnosis of pancreatitis is made. The steatorrhea can be accounted for by a decrease in the intraluminal concentration of which of the following pancreatic enzymes?","o":["a. Trypsin","b. Amylase","c. Chymotrypsin","d. Colipase","e. Lipase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lipase","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Lipase.</b> Steatorrhea depends on lipase inside the <b>intestinal lumen</b>. The damaged pancreas delivers <b>less lipase to the duodenum</b>, even though the lipase leaking into the <b>blood</b> is high.<br>A. Trypsin: digests protein.<br>B. Amylase: digests starch.<br>C. Chymotrypsin: digests protein.<br>D. Colipase: only helps lipase reach the fat; lipase itself digests triglycerides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L56 slide 6 (Pancreatic Lipase & Co-Lipase)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":6,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-35-1","kind":"harder","n":"C8 extra 29"},{"id":"D061-98-1","s":"A 65-year old patient presents to the office with terrible epigastric pain that worsens with lying supine and improves with forward leaning. Imaging reveals several calcifications and a large pseudocyst on the pancreas. Serum lipase and amylase levels come back within normal range. He is diagnosed with chronic pancreatitis. What is the most likely outcome complication of the patient’s condition?","o":["A. Decreased bile synthesis","B. Lactose intolerance","C. Fat malabsorption","D. IV Secretin Stimulation Test showing elevated levels of HCO3- production"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat malabsorption","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fat malabsorption.</b> Chronic pancreatitis destroys the pancreatic acini, so <b>lipase output drops</b> and undigested fat appears as steatorrhea.<br>A. Less bile synthesis: bile is made by the liver, which is fine.<br>B. Lactose intolerance: lactase is a brush-border enzyme on intestinal cells, not a pancreatic one.<br>D. High bicarbonate on secretin stimulation: chronic pancreatitis shows <b>decreased</b> bicarbonate output.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":98,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 30"},{"id":"G063-5-21","s":"A 52-year-old man presents in the ER with nausea, vomiting and excruciating upper abdominal pain radiating to the back. The patient has a history of alcohol consumption over the last 20 years. Abdominal examination reveals tenderness in the epigastric region. Ultrasound shows inflammation of the exocrine pancreas. Pancreatic lipase and amylase are elevated on a lab profile. A diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis is made. Which of the following is likely to be observed in the patient’s blood?","o":["a. High level of Cl-","b. Low level of bicarbonate","c. High level of CCK","d. Low level of triglycerides"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Low level of triglycerides","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Low triglycerides.</b> Chronic pancreatitis means <b>no lipase</b>, so dietary fat isn't digested or absorbed, and fewer chylomicron triglycerides reach the blood.<br>A. High chloride: no link to pancreatic enzyme loss.<br>B. Low bicarbonate: the pancreas sends less bicarbonate into the duodenum; blood bicarbonate is not lowered.<br>C. High cholecystokinin (CCK): CCK is released by fatty acids, monoglycerides and amino acids in the duodenum; failed fat digestion does not raise it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":5,"f":"G063"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 31"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":68,"label":"Duodenal acid inactivates lipase (Zollinger-Ellison)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-32.jpg","cap":"No lipase, no lipid absorption; lipase is inactivated by acid below pH five","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G043-46.jpg","cap":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: steatorrhea because acid inactivates lipase","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-23-1","s":"A 35 yr old male triathlete complains about an oily anal leakage and his wife adds that he has especially foul-smelling feces. Upon examination the patient hypertrophy of the gastric mucosa is found. What is the mechanism of the oily anal leakage?","o":["A. a. Decreased levels of cholecystokinin","B. b. Increased levels of cholecystokinin","C. c. Increased levels of somatostatin","D. d. Decreased pH in the intestinal lumen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"d. Decreased pH in the intestinal lumen","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> A thick gastric lining plus oily stool suggests a <b>gastrinoma</b> (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Excess acid floods the duodenum, <b>lowers pH</b>, and <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b> (below pH five), so fat leaks out.<br>A. Less cholecystokinin (CCK): nothing in the stem lowers CCK; gastrin explains the thick gastric lining.<br>B. More CCK: would increase lipase and bile release, helping fat digestion.<br>C. More somatostatin: would suppress gastrin and acid, not thicken the gastric lining.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 42 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":23,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 32"},{"id":"D067-115-1","s":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, fat malabsorption primarily results from:","o":["A. Excess bile salt loss in the stool","B. Decreased pancreatic lipase activity due to low intestinal pH","C. Autoimmune destruction of enterocytes","D. Impaired chylomicron formation in enterocytes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased pancreatic lipase activity due to low intestinal pH","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Decreased pancreatic lipase activity due to low intestinal pH.</b> The gastrin tumor drives a huge acid output. Acid floods the duodenum, the pH falls below 5, and <b>lipase is inactivated</b>, so fat is not digested.<br>A. Bile salt loss in stool: that is ileal resection or ileal disease.<br>C. Autoimmune destruction of enterocytes: that describes celiac disease, where the villi are flattened.<br>D. Impaired chylomicron formation: that is abetalipoproteinemia (no apoprotein B).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)","L56 slide 43 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":115,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 33"},{"id":"D035-134-2","s":"Answer the following questions about Zollinger- Ellison Syndrome / Why is there steatorrhea?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Low duodenal pH inactivates the pancreatic lipases","a":null,"why":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, a gastrin-secreting tumor drives huge acid output that overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate. The <b>low duodenal pH inactivates pancreatic lipase</b> (below about pH five), so fat isn't digested and passes into the stool.<br>Common mix-up: lipase is still made; acid destroys it. Acid also makes bile acids absorb too early, which adds to the problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 42 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":134,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 34"},{"id":"G052-1-7","s":"CASE 45 Peptic Ulcer Disease: Zollinger–Ellison Syndrome. Abe Rosenfeld, who is 47 years old, owns a house painting business with his brothers. The brothers pride themselves on maintaining high standards and satisfying their customers. For several months, Abe had a number of symptoms, including indigestion, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. One day, he remarked to his brothers that his diarrhea looked “oily.” The abdominal pain was relieved temporarily by eating and by taking over-the-counter antacids. Finally, he saw his physician, who referred him to a gastroenterologist. Abe underwent fiberoptic endoscopy, which showed an ulcer in the duodenal bulb. To determine the cause of the ulcer, additional tests were performed, including a serum gastrin level, analysis of gastric contents, a pentagastrin stimulation test, and a secretin stimulation test (Table 5–1). Table 5–1: Abe’s Laboratory Values and Results of Laboratory Tests Serum gastrin level 800 pg/mL (normal, 0–130 pg/mL) Basal gastric H+ secretion 100 mEq/hr (normal, 10 mEq/h) Pentagastrin stimulation test No increase in H+ secretion Secretin stimulation test Serum gastrin increased to 1,100 pg/mL A computed tomography scan showed a 3-cm mass on the head of the pancreas. The mass was thought to be a gastrinoma (gastrin-secreting tumor). While awaiting surgery to remove the mass, Abe was treated with a drug called omeprazole. Abe underwent laparoscopic surgery, during which the tumor was localized and removed. Abe’s ulcer subsequently healed, and his symptoms disappeared. QUESTION: The oily appearance of Abe’s stools was caused by fat in the stool (steatorrhea). Why did Abe have steatorrhea?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Acidic duodenal contents inactivate pancreatic lipase, make bile acids non-ionized so they are absorbed too early (no micelles), and damage the mucosa, so dietary fat is not digested/absorbed.","a":null,"why":"His gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) pours so much acid into the duodenum that bicarbonate can't neutralize it. First, <b>acid inactivates pancreatic lipase</b> (below about pH five). Second, bile acids become <b>non-ionized</b> and are absorbed too early, so <b>micelles fail</b>. Third, acid <b>damages the mucosa</b>, cutting absorptive surface. Undigested fat ends up in the stool (steatorrhea).<br>Common mix-up: lipase is still made normally; acid destroys it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":1,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C8 extra 35"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":69,"label":"Ileal loss breaks bile recycling; micelles fail","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Removing the terminal ileum interferes with bile recirculation; no bile, minimal fat absorbed","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Enterohepatic circulation: over ninety-five percent of bile salts return by the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/D015-161.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure: bile salts absorbed in the ileum return to liver via portal blood","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 161"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-84-1","s":"A patient with ileal resection is experiencing fat malabsorption and steatorrhea. Which of the following processes has been most directly disrupted, leading to impaired fat digestion and absorption?","o":["A. Reduced synthesis of primary bile acids in the liver due to lack of feedback inhibition by bile salts","B. Inability to reabsorb bile salts via secondary active transport in the terminal ileum","C. Impaired secretion of pancreatic lipase in response to hormonal deficiency","D. Decreased bicarbonate secretion leading to reduced micelle formation","E. Altered microbiota reducing deconjugation of bile salts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inability to reabsorb bile salts via secondary active transport in the terminal ileum","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts by <b>sodium-dependent secondary active transport</b>. Lose it, and bile salts drain into the stool, the pool shrinks, and <b>micelles can't form</b>.<br>A. Reduced bile acid synthesis: synthesis actually <b>increases</b> because fewer bile acids return to hold it back, but not enough to keep up.<br>C. Impaired lipase secretion: the pancreas is normal.<br>D. Less bicarbonate: bicarbonate isn't affected; micelles fail from lack of bile salts.<br>E. Less bacterial deconjugation: bacteria are not the problem here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":84,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-165-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 36"},{"id":"D015-159-1","s":"A PATIENT WHO UNDERWENT AN ILEAL RESECTION WOULD HAVE WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING?","o":["A. DIMINISHED BILE SALT POOL","B. LARGE AMOUNT OF BILE SALT EXCRETION","C. IMPAIRED ABSORPTION OF DIETARY LIPIDS","D. STEATORRHEA/MALABSORPTION OF FATS","E. ALL THE ABOVE"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"ALL THE ABOVE","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All the above.</b> Ileal resection breaks bile recycling, so <b>more bile salts are excreted</b>, the <b>bile salt pool shrinks</b>, <b>dietary lipid absorption is impaired</b>, and <b>steatorrhea</b> follows.<br>A. Diminished bile salt pool: true, but so are B to D.<br>B. Large bile salt excretion: true, but incomplete.<br>C. Impaired lipid absorption: true, but incomplete.<br>D. Steatorrhea: true, but incomplete.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":159,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-165-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 37"},{"id":"G066-181-1","s":"7. A 60 y/o transgender patient presents with steatorrhea. You look in their chart and see that they had an ileal resection 15 years ago. How does their ileal resection relate to their current symptoms?","o":["A. Proteins cannot be properly absorbed","B. Their ileal resection caused them to have lactose intolerance","C. Bile salts recirculation was interrupted","D. Water-soluble vitamins cannot be properly absorbed"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts recirculation was interrupted","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts</b> for the enterohepatic circulation. After ileal resection, bile salts are lost in stool, the liver can't keep up, micelles fail, and <b>fat appears in the stool</b>.<br>A. Protein malabsorption: proteins are absorbed in the upper small intestine.<br>B. Lactose intolerance: lactase is a brush-border enzyme, unrelated to the ileum.<br>D. Water-soluble vitamins: only vitamin B12 is lost after ileal resection, and that causes anemia, not steatorrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":181,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":180,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-165-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 38"},{"id":"D040-165-1","s":"A 59-year-old male patient with severe Crohn disease has been unresponsive to medical therapy and undergoes an ileal resection. After the surgery, he will have steatorrhea because:","o":["A. Micelles do not form in the intestinal lumen","B. The pancreas does not secrete lipase","C. The liver bile acid pool increases","D. Chylomicrons do not form in the intestinal lumen","E. Dietary triglycerides cannot be digested"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Micelles do not form in the intestinal lumen","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Without the ileum, bile acids aren't recycled, the liver's bile acid pool drops, and <b>micelles don't form</b> to carry digested fat to the cell.<br>B. Pancreas doesn't secrete lipase: pancreatic secretion is normal.<br>C. Bile acid pool increases: it <b>decreases</b>.<br>D. Chylomicrons don't form in the lumen: chylomicrons form inside intestinal cells, never in the lumen.<br>E. Triglycerides can't be digested: lipase still works; the failure is micelle-dependent absorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":165,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"246"},{"id":"D067-113-1","s":"Which of the following best explains why patients with ileal resection have fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies?","o":["A. Decreased bile salt reabsorption leading to impaired micelle formation","B. Reduced secretion of pancreatic enzymes","C. Increased gastric acid production damaging the intestinal mucosa","D. Impaired intrinsic factor production causing poor vitamin absorption"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased bile salt reabsorption leading to impaired micelle formation","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Decreased bile salt reabsorption leading to impaired micelle formation.</b> The ileum recycles bile salts. Without it the pool shrinks and micelles do not form. Vitamins A, D, E and K need micelles to reach the cell, so they are lost with the fat.<br>B. Fewer pancreatic enzymes: the pancreas is not affected.<br>C. More gastric acid damaging the mucosa: that is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.<br>D. Less intrinsic factor: intrinsic factor comes from the stomach and serves vitamin B12, a water-soluble vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":113,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-165-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 39"},{"id":"G063-7-29","s":"A 26-year-old male presents to the ER with a 48-hour bout of diarrhea with steatorrhea. Which of the following best accounts for the appearance of excess fat in the stool?","o":["a. Decreased bile salt pool size","b. Delayed gastric emptying","c. Decreased gastric acid secretion","d. Decreased secretion of intrinsic factor"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased bile salt pool size","a":"a","why":"<b>A.</b> Fat in the stool means lipids weren't absorbed. A <b>small bile salt pool</b> means too few micelles to carry digested fat to the intestinal cells.<br>B. Delayed gastric emptying: gives more time for digestion; it doesn't cause steatorrhea.<br>C. Less gastric acid: if anything it helps pancreatic lipase; excess acid causes steatorrhea.<br>D. Less intrinsic factor: lowers vitamin B12 absorption, not fat absorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":7,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"247"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":69,"label":"Terminal ileum loss causes steatorrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"No bile, minimal fat absorbed; terminal ileum returns bile salts to the liver","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Enterohepatic circulation: over ninety-five percent of bile salts return by the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-25-1","s":"A 39 yo woman is diagnosed with intestinal cancer and has a significant portion of her distal ileum removed. She is given regular B12 injections to avoid pernicious anemia. Which of the following conditions is she also very likely to experience?","o":["a. Duodenal ulcer","b. Malabsorption of protein","c. Pancreatic insufficiency","d. Secretory diarrhea","e. Steatorrhea"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Steatorrhea.</b> The <b>terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts</b>. Remove it and bile salts are lost in stool, the liver can't keep up, the bile salt pool shrinks, <b>micelles fail</b>, and fat stays in the stool.<br>A. Duodenal ulcer: caused by acid or Helicobacter pylori, not ileal loss.<br>B. Protein malabsorption: protein is absorbed higher up.<br>C. Pancreatic insufficiency: the pancreas is untouched.<br>D. Secretory diarrhea: bile acids reaching the colon can draw some water, but the classic result is steatorrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":67,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":41,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":2,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":25,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":180,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":47,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":33,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":30,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":58,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":45,"f":"D003"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":29,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"248"},{"id":"D008-127-1","s":"A 40 year old man suffers a serious automobile accident in which his small bowel ruptures. The man subsequently has to have his ileum resected. Which of the following conditions is the man most likely going to be at risk for moving forward?","o":["A. Pancreatitis","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Pelagra","D. Kwashiorkor","E. Steatorrhea"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Steatorrhea.</b> Bile salts are reabsorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b> and recycled to the liver. Without the ileum, bile salts are lost, micelles fail, and fat stays in the stool. He will also need vitamin B12 injections.<br>A. Pancreatitis: unrelated to ileal loss.<br>B. Hypoglycemia: glucose is absorbed in the upper small intestine.<br>C. Pellagra: niacin deficiency; niacin is absorbed higher up.<br>D. Kwashiorkor: protein malnutrition from poor intake, not ileal loss.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":127,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":127,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":295,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-25-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 40"},{"id":"D010-146-1","s":"322. A 57-year-old woman undergoes resection of the terminal ileum as part of treatment for her chronic inflammatory bowel disease. Removal of the terminal ileum will most likely result in which of the following?","o":["A. Decreased glucose absorption","B. Decreased water content of the feces","C. Increased bile acid concentration in the enterohepatic circulation","D. Increased excretion of fatty acids","E. Increased iron absorption"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased excretion of fatty acids","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Without the terminal ileum, bile salts aren't reabsorbed, the pool shrinks, and micelles fail, so <b>more fatty acids are lost in the feces</b>.<br>A. Less glucose absorption: glucose is absorbed before the terminal ileum.<br>B. Drier feces: bile acids and fat reaching the colon pull water in, so feces get <b>wetter</b>.<br>C. More bile acid in the enterohepatic circulation: it <b>decreases</b>, because bile acids are lost in stool.<br>E. More iron absorption: iron is absorbed in the duodenum and is unchanged.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":146,"f":"D010"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-25-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 41"},{"id":"D007-165-1","s":"20 year old female presents with a 2 year history of abdominal pain and aggressive terminal ileitis requiring 2 surgical procedures to resect the affected bowel. Based on this history you would anticipate which of the following scenarios?","o":["A. Macrocytic anemia due to folic acid deficiency","B. Lactose deficiency due to lactose malabsorption","C. Celiac Sprue due to an autoimmune reaction","D. Fat malabsorption due to bile acid deficiency","E. Multiple hematomas due to Vitamin K malabsorption"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat malabsorption due to bile acid deficiency","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Repeated <b>terminal ileum</b> resections remove the site that reabsorbs bile salts, so the bile acid pool runs low and <b>fat is malabsorbed</b>.<br>A. Folate-deficiency macrocytic anemia: folate is absorbed in the jejunum; ileal loss causes vitamin B12 deficiency instead.<br>B. Lactose malabsorption: lactase sits on the brush border of the upper small intestine.<br>C. Celiac sprue: an autoimmune reaction to gluten, not caused by surgery.<br>E. Hematomas from vitamin K loss: a later, downstream effect; D is the direct result.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":165,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":191,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"D022-25-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 42"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":69,"label":"Bacteria deconjugate bile salts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-35.jpg","cap":"Bacteria deconjugate bile salts, so bile acids are reabsorbed too early","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G066-215.jpg","cap":"TA summary: bacterial overgrowth deconjugates bile salts, reducing their effectiveness","where":"2024 TA review, slide 215"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-168-1","s":"Which of the following reasons can reduce the effectiveness of bile salts?","o":["A. Increased pH in the intestinal lumen","B. Bacterial overgrowth","C. Decreased number of intestinal cells for absorption","D. Failure to synthesize apoproteins","E. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bacterial overgrowth","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bacterial overgrowth.</b> The liver conjugates bile acids into bile salts; bacteria <b>deconjugate</b> them, and the bile acids are then <b>absorbed too early</b>, before micelles finish their job.<br>A. Increased pH: higher pH keeps bile salts ionized and working; low pH harms them.<br>C. Fewer intestinal cells (tropical sprue): reduces absorptive surface, not bile salt function.<br>D. No apoproteins (abetalipoproteinemia): blocks chylomicron export.<br>E. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: its acid mainly inactivates lipase; the harm to bile salts is secondary, so B is best.","ex":"","fl":["Ambiguity: Zollinger-Ellison (option E) also reduces bile-salt effectiveness in Costanzo (low duodenal pH makes bile acids non-ionized so they are absorbed too early), the same mechanism the source gives for its 'decreased pH' note; key B (bacterial overgrowth, G046:35) is the intended single best answer."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 35 (Bacterial Overgrowth)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":168,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"249"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":70,"label":"Celiac disease: stop gluten","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-37.jpg","cap":"Celiac: gluten antibodies flatten villi; biopsy most accurate; stop gluten","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G046-39.jpg","cap":"Normal tall villi versus flattened villi in celiac disease","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-223-1","s":"A 23-year-old man complains of a low volume but chronic, foul-smelling oily diarrhea for the past year. On physical examination there is no abdominal pain or mass and bowel sounds are present. His stool is negative for occult blood. Imaging and microscopic examination show absence of villi, increased surface intraepithelial lymphocytes, and hyperplastic appearing crypts. Which of the following therapies is most likely to be useful for this man?","o":["A. Antibiotics","B. Gluten-free diet","C. Selective vagotomy","D. Corticosteroids","E. Segmental duodenal resection"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gluten-free diet","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gluten-free diet.</b> Flat villi, extra intraepithelial lymphocytes and deep crypts mean <b>celiac disease</b>: antibodies against gluten (wheat, rye, barley) strip the villi. <b>Stopping gluten</b> heals it in weeks.<br>A. Antibiotics: treat tropical sprue, an infection that needs a travel history.<br>C. Selective vagotomy: an old ulcer operation.<br>D. Corticosteroids: not the standard treatment; removing gluten is.<br>E. Duodenal resection: celiac affects the whole small bowel; surgery doesn't help.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 37 (Celiac Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":223,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":55,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":27,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":26,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":53,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"250"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":70,"label":"Tropical sprue: villi lost, less surface","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-36.jpg","cap":"Tropical sprue and celiac destroy villi: no villi, no absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G046-40.jpg","cap":"Tropical sprue: small-bowel infection, found on biopsy, fat malabsorption, antibiotics","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-103-1","s":"A 38-year-old woman who recently returned from a prolonged stay in Southeast Asia presents with chronic foul-smelling diarrhea, weight loss, and fatigue. Laboratory studies show macrocytic anemia, low serum albumin, and deficiencies in vitamins A, D, E, and K. A small bowel biopsy reveals villous atrophy with chronic inflammatory infiltrates indicating tropical sprue. Which of the following best explains her fat malabsorption?","o":["A. Impaired bile acid synthesis","B. Decreased pancreatic enzyme secretion","C. Reduced intestinal absorptive surface area","D. Defective chylomicron formation","E. Increased intestinal motility"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Reduced intestinal absorptive surface area","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Tropical sprue is an infection that <b>destroys villi</b>: no villi, no absorption. The <b>absorptive surface area shrinks</b>, so fat and vitamins A, D, E and K can't get in.<br>A. Impaired bile acid synthesis: the liver is normal.<br>B. Less pancreatic enzyme secretion: the pancreas is normal.<br>D. Defective chylomicron formation: that is abetalipoproteinemia.<br>E. Faster motility: not the mechanism of fat malabsorption in sprue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 40 (Tropical Sprue)","L56 slide 36 (Celiac disease/Tropical Sprue)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":103,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"251"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":70,"label":"Sudan stain detects fecal fat","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"D060-103-1","s":"A 50-year-old male presents with steatorrhea and weight loss. A biopsy of the small intestine reveals villous atrophy and decreased absorption. What additional test would confirm a diagnosis of fat malabsorption?","o":["A. D-xylose absorption test","B. Fecal elastase test","C. Sudan stain of stool","D. Hydrogen breath test"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sudan stain of stool","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Sudan stain of stool.</b> Sudan dye stains fat, so it shows <b>excess fecal fat</b> and confirms fat malabsorption (steatorrhea).<br>A. D-xylose test: checks mucosal absorption and separates gut-wall disease from pancreatic disease, but doesn't measure fat.<br>B. Fecal elastase: tests for pancreatic insufficiency.<br>D. Hydrogen breath test: detects carbohydrate malabsorption, such as lactase deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 50 (Q5. Which of the following is most specific diagnostic test for tropical sprue?)","L56 slide 37 (Celiac Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":103,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"252"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":70,"label":"No fat absorption, no fat-soluble vitamins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"No fat absorption, no vitamin A, D, E, K absorption; no lipase, none either","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D drives calbindin-mediated calcium absorption in intestinal cells","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic proteases must remove R-protein before vitamin B12 can bind intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-35-1","s":"Tropical sprue infection is often associated with low serum calcium. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Tropical sprue infectious agents require calcium as a cofactor, reducing calcium available for dietary intake","b. Tropical sprue infectious agents produce a calcium ATPase toxin preventing dietary calcium absorption","c. Tropical sprue impairs absorption of lipids and lipid soluble vitamins","d. Tropical sprue is associated with increased incidence of bowel lymphoma that decreases serum calcium levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tropical sprue impairs absorption of lipids and lipid soluble vitamins","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> Tropical sprue flattens villi, so <b>fat and fat-soluble vitamins</b> are poorly absorbed. Low <b>vitamin D</b> means less calbindin and <b>less calcium absorption</b>, so serum calcium falls.<br>A. Organisms use up calcium: no such mechanism.<br>B. Calcium ATPase (calcium pump) toxin: no such toxin.<br>D. Bowel lymphoma: linked to celiac disease, and it doesn't lower calcium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 40 (Tropical Sprue)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":38,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":35,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":232,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"253"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":70,"label":"Causes of steatorrhea: which do, which don't","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"No bile, minimal fat absorbed; terminal ileum returns bile salts to the liver","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G066-214.jpg","cap":"TA summary: pancreatic insufficiency and duodenal acidity cause steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 214"},{"src":"../sl/G066-215.jpg","cap":"TA summary: bile salt deficiency, bacterial overgrowth, sprue, no apoprotein B","where":"2024 TA review, slide 215"}],"q":[{"id":"D008-129-1","s":"Which of the following conditions is least likely to be associated with steatorrhea?","o":["A. Cystic fibrosis","B. Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome (Gastrinoma)","C. Small Intestine Bacterial Overgrowth","D. Obesity","E. Abetalipoproteinemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Obesity","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Obesity.</b> Obesity doesn't impair fat digestion or absorption; each other choice breaks a step.<br>A. Cystic fibrosis: thick mucus clogs pancreatic ducts, so lipase can't reach the gut.<br>B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma): excess acid inactivates pancreatic lipase.<br>C. Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth: bacteria deconjugate bile salts, which are then absorbed too early, so micelles fail.<br>E. Abetalipoproteinemia: no apoprotein B, so no chylomicrons.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L56 slide 35 (Bacterial Overgrowth)","L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":129,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":129,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":297,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"254"},{"id":"D015-75-1","s":"ALL OF THE FOLLOWING DISORDERS CAUSE STEATORRHEA EXCEPT:","o":["A. ZOLLINGER-ELLISON SYNDROME","B. CELIAC DISEASE","C. RENAL TUBULAR ACIDOSIS (RTA)","D. CYSTIC FIBROSIS"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"RENAL TUBULAR ACIDOSIS (RTA)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Renal tubular acidosis (RTA).</b> A kidney acid-handling defect; it doesn't touch fat digestion or absorption.<br>A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: excess acid inactivates pancreatic lipase.<br>B. Celiac disease: gluten antibodies flatten villi, so less absorption.<br>D. Cystic fibrosis: clogged pancreatic ducts keep lipase from reaching the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 42 (Q1 A patient comes with oily, greasy, floating, foul smelling diarrheal stool. T)","L56 slide 37 (Celiac Disease)","L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":75,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-129-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 43"},{"id":"D024-54-1","s":"A patient comes in complaining about steatorrhea ( fatty stools), what could be a cause of this?","o":["A. A. Resection of the duodenum","B. B. Resection of the ileum","C. C. Reduced pancreatic secretions","D. D. Reduced Intrinsic factor release","E. E. Increase in CCK","F. F. All of the above","G. G. A, C, D","H. H. B and C","I. I. B, C, and D"],"g":["H"],"k":["H"],"rv":false,"at":"B and C","a":"H","why":"<b>H. B and C.</b> <b>Ileal resection</b> loses bile salts (no recycling, no micelles), and <b>reduced pancreatic secretion</b> means too little lipase. Both cause steatorrhea.<br>A. Duodenal resection: not a classic cause; the jejunum still absorbs fat.<br>D. Less intrinsic factor: lowers vitamin B12 absorption, not fat.<br>E. More cholecystokinin (CCK): increases lipase and bile release, helping fat digestion.<br>F. All of the above: A, D and E are wrong.<br>G. A, C and D: A and D are wrong.<br>I. B, C and D: D is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":54,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-129-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 44"},{"id":"D065-15-1","s":"Fat malabsorption disorders?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile salt problems, duodenal acidity (e.g., ZES), bacterial overgrowth, pancreatic insufficiency, tropical sprue, failure to synthesize apoproteins (abetalipoproteinemia)","a":null,"why":"Fat malabsorption happens when any step fails: <b>pancreatic insufficiency</b> (no lipase), <b>acid in the duodenum</b> (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome inactivates lipase), <b>bile salt problems</b> (ileal resection, bacterial overgrowth), <b>loss of villi</b> (celiac disease, tropical sprue), and <b>no apoprotein B</b> (abetalipoproteinemia).<br>Common mix-up: gallbladder removal does NOT cause fat malabsorption, because bile still flows and recirculates.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 32 (Pancreas Effect on Lipid)","L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 35 (Bacterial Overgrowth)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":15,"f":"D065"}],"tier":2,"like":"D008-129-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 45"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":71,"label":"Vitamins A, D, E, K fat-soluble; B water-soluble","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"Water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport; B12 needs intrinsic factor and terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-242-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT considered a fat-soluble vitamin?","o":["A. vitamin B12","B. vitamin K","C. vitamin E","D. vitamin A","E. vitamin D"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"vitamin B12","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin B12.</b> It is <b>water-soluble</b> and is absorbed in the terminal ileum bound to intrinsic factor. The <b>fat-soluble</b> vitamins are <b>A, D, E and K</b>.<br>B. Vitamin K: fat-soluble.<br>C. Vitamin E: fat-soluble.<br>D. Vitamin A: fat-soluble.<br>E. Vitamin D: fat-soluble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":48,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":242,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":42,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"255"},{"id":"D007-173-1","s":"Which of the following listed is a water soluble vitamin?","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Vitamin E","C. Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin B.</b> The B vitamins (and vitamin C) are <b>water-soluble</b> and are absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport. Vitamins <b>A, D, E and K</b> are fat-soluble and need micelles.<br>A. Vitamin D: fat-soluble.<br>B. Vitamin E: fat-soluble.<br>C. Vitamin K: fat-soluble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":173,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":139,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":139,"f":"G064"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-242-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 46"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":71,"label":"Fat-soluble vitamins ride micelles, then chylomicrons","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-19.jpg","cap":"Micelles coated in bile salts carry lipids to the brush border; entry by diffusion","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G046-31.jpg","cap":"Inside the cell, monoglycerides and fatty acids become chylomicrons that enter the central lacteal","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/D015-168.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: fat-soluble vitamins join micelles and diffuse into the cell, like other lipids","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 168"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-217-1","s":"Describe the pathway of absorption of fat soluble vitamins.","o":["A. In the intestinal lumen, fat- soluble vitamins incorporated into chylomicrons → transported to the apical membrane of the intestinal cells → Diffuse across the membrane into the cells → Incorporated into micelles → Extruded into lymph → Circulation","B. In the intestinal lumen, fat- soluble vitamins incorporated into micelles → transported to the basal membrane of the intestinal cells → Diffuse across the membrane into the cells → Incorporated into chylomicrons → Extruded into lymph → Circulation","C. In the intestinal lumen, fat- soluble vitamins incorporated into chylomicrons → transported to the basal membrane of the intestinal cells → Diffuse across the membrane into the cells → Incorporated into micelles → Extruded into lymph → Circulation","D. In the intestinal lumen, fat- soluble vitamins incorporated into micelles → transported to the apical membrane of the intestinal cells → Diffuse across the membrane into the cells → Incorporated into chylomicrons → Extruded into lymph → Circulation"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"In the intestinal lumen, fat- soluble vitamins incorporated into micelles → transported to the apical membrane of the intestinal cells → Diffuse across the membrane into the cells → Incorporated into chylomicrons → Extruded into lymph → Circulation","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Fat-soluble vitamins join <b>micelles</b> in the lumen → reach the <b>apical</b> (lumen-facing) membrane → <b>diffuse</b> into the cell → are packed into <b>chylomicrons</b> → are extruded into <b>lymph</b> → reach the circulation.<br>A. Chylomicrons in the lumen: chylomicrons form only inside the cell; micelles are in the lumen.<br>B. Basal membrane: micelles meet the apical membrane, which faces the lumen.<br>C. Chylomicrons in the lumen and the basal membrane: wrong on both counts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":217,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":216,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"256"},{"id":"D036-177-1","s":"Describe the process by which fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed in the small intestine, from the intestinal lumen to their delivery into the general circulation.","o":["A. Fat-soluble vitamins are directly absorbed into blood capillaries in the intestinal villi.","B. Fat-soluble vitamins enter intestinal cells through exocytosis and then diffuse into the bloodstream.","C. Fat-soluble vitamins are transported across the apical membrane and incorporated into micelles in the intestinal lumen.","D. Fat-soluble vitamins undergo endocytosis by intestinal cells, followed by release into the bloodstream.","E. Fat-soluble vitamins are absorbed through tight junctions between intestinal cells directly into lymphatics."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat-soluble vitamins are transported across the apical membrane and incorporated into micelles in the intestinal lumen.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Vitamins A, D, E and K travel like other fats: they join <b>micelles</b> in the lumen, <b>diffuse</b> across the apical membrane, are packed into <b>chylomicrons</b>, and leave into <b>lymph</b>. C is the only choice with micelles (its wording reverses the order).<br>A. Directly into blood capillaries: chylomicrons are too big; they enter lacteals.<br>B. Enter cells by exocytosis: exocytosis is how chylomicrons leave the cell.<br>D. Endocytosis then blood: they enter by diffusion and leave by lymph.<br>E. Through tight junctions: they cross the cell, not between cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":177,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-217-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":71,"label":"Fat-soluble vitamins need bile salts and micelles","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E, K are fat-soluble; bile insufficiency decreases their absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Bile salts recycle from the terminal ileum; no bile, minimal fat absorbed","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/D015-168.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: fat-soluble vitamins need bile-salt micelles; bile salts reabsorbed in the ileum","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 168"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-44-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins is absorbed poorly by patients lacking bile salts?","o":["A. Vitamin B12","B. Vitamin C","C. Vitamin D","D. Folic acid","E. Niacin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin D.</b> Without bile salts there are no <b>micelles</b>, so the <b>fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K</b> are poorly absorbed.<br>A. Vitamin B12: water-soluble; needs intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum.<br>B. Vitamin C: water-soluble; sodium-dependent cotransport.<br>D. Folic acid: a water-soluble B vitamin.<br>E. Niacin: a water-soluble B vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":44,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":42,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"257"},{"id":"D067-117-1","s":"Which vitamin deficiency is most likely to occur in a patient with fat malabsorption due to bile salt deficiency?","o":["A. Vitamin B12","B. Vitamin C","C. Vitamin K","D. Folate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin K.</b> Vitamins <b>A, D, E and K</b> are fat-soluble. They need bile-salt micelles to be absorbed, so a lack of bile salts lowers all four. Low vitamin K means fewer clotting factors and easy bleeding.<br>A. Vitamin B12: water-soluble; needs intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum.<br>B. Vitamin C: water-soluble; absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport.<br>D. Folate: a water-soluble B vitamin; no micelles needed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":117,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-44-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 48"},{"id":"D008-125-1","s":"Micelle formation is necessary for the intestinal absorption of:","o":["A. Glycerol","B. Methylcobalamin","C. Phenylalanine","D. Vitamin A","E. Bile Acids"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin A.</b> Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) ride in <b>micelles</b> to the brush border, just like the other fat digestion products.<br>A. Glycerol: water-soluble; needs no micelle.<br>B. Methylcobalamin (vitamin B12): binds intrinsic factor and is absorbed in the terminal ileum.<br>C. Phenylalanine: an amino acid; absorbed by sodium-amino acid cotransport.<br>E. Bile acids: they build micelles but are reabsorbed separately by a sodium-bile salt cotransporter in the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":125,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":125,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":293,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-44-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 49"},{"id":"F1212-111-1","s":"Crohn’s Disease is an inflammatory disease that classically targets the terminal ileum. In patients with Crohn’s, you would expect that they have a malabsorption of which of the following:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Proteins","C. Carbohydrates","D. Vitamin C","E. Iron"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin D.</b> Crohn disease of the <b>terminal ileum</b> stops bile salt reabsorption; the bile salt pool shrinks, micelles fail, and <b>fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)</b> are malabsorbed. Vitamin B12 is lost too.<br>B. Proteins: absorbed in the upper small intestine.<br>C. Carbohydrates: absorbed in the upper small intestine.<br>D. Vitamin C: water-soluble, absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport; it doesn't need bile.<br>E. Iron: absorbed in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":111,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-44-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 50"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":71,"label":"No fat absorption, no fat-soluble vitamins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"No fat absorption, no vitamin A, D, E, K absorption; no lipase, none either","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D drives calbindin-mediated calcium absorption in intestinal cells","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic proteases must remove R-protein before vitamin B12 can bind intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-175-1","s":"A 52-year-old patient presents with a history of recurrent abdominal pain and malabsorption symptoms. The patient has been diagnosed with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, a rare disorder characterized by gastrin-secreting tumors in the pancreas. Further investigation reveals Crohn's disease and a history of ileal resection. Additionally, the patient exhibits alterations in gastric pH due to chronic medication use. Considering the patient's complex medical history, what is the likely impact of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, Crohn's disease, ileal resection, and alterations in pH on the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, specifically A, D, E, and K?","o":["A. The conditions promote efficient absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.","B. These disorders have minimal impact on fat-soluble vitamin absorption.","C. The patient is likely to experience increased synthesis of fat-soluble vitamins.","D. The collective disorders contribute to reduced fat-soluble vitamin absorption.","E. The altered conditions lead to the accumulation of fat-soluble vitamins in the bloodstream."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The collective disorders contribute to reduced fat-soluble vitamin absorption.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Vitamins A, D, E and K are <b>fat-soluble</b>: <b>no fat absorption, no vitamin A, D, E, K absorption</b>. Zollinger-Ellison acid inactivates lipase, and Crohn disease plus ileal resection lose bile salts, so each lowers their absorption.<br>A. Efficient absorption: the opposite.<br>B. Minimal impact: each disorder impairs fat absorption.<br>C. Increased synthesis: the body doesn't make more of these vitamins to compensate.<br>E. Accumulation in the blood: levels fall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":175,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D014-51-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 51"},{"id":"D061-96-1","s":"An 8-year old patient is brought into the clinic for concerns of steatorrhea and abdominal cramps. The patient tells you that he has had recurrent pneumonia infections within the past year. The patient admits to having fast food the night before for dinner. You run a chloride sweat test and it comes back positive. You suspect cystic fibrosis. Which of the following processes would most likely be affected?","o":["A. Bone formation","B. Collagen synthesis","C. Nucleic acid synthesis","D. Red blood cell synthesis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bone formation","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Bone formation.</b> Cystic fibrosis clogs pancreatic ducts, so lipase can't reach the gut and fat is malabsorbed. <b>Vitamin D</b> is lost with the fat, calcium absorption falls, and <b>bone formation</b> suffers.<br>B. Collagen synthesis: needs vitamin C, which is water-soluble.<br>C. Nucleic acid synthesis: needs folate and vitamin B12.<br>D. Red blood cell synthesis: mainly needs iron, folate and vitamin B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":96,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D014-51-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C8 extra 52"},{"id":"D014-51-1","s":"A patient comes with chronic pancreatitis from recurrent episodes of alcoholic pancreatitis. His pancreas on abdominal CT is calcified. Which of the following is most likely to be found?","o":["A. a. Normal calcium level","B. b. Normal hematocrit","C. c. Normal bleeding studies","D. d. Normal vision","E. e. Normal B12 levels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"b. Normal hematocrit","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Normal hematocrit.</b> Without lipase, vitamins A, D, E and K are poorly absorbed; without proteases, vitamin B12 stays stuck to R-protein. Hematocrit has no direct link, and B12 stores last years before anemia appears.<br>A. Calcium: low vitamin D lowers calcium absorption.<br>C. Bleeding studies: low vitamin K prolongs clotting times.<br>D. Vision: low vitamin A causes night blindness.<br>E. B12 levels: pancreatic proteases must remove R-protein in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)","L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":51,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"258"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":72,"label":"Intestinal fluid: diet supplies 2 of 9 liters","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-6.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: nine liters in daily; food and water supply two","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/D004-219.jpg","cap":"Bar chart: diet about two liters among nine liters entering intestine","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 219"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-203-1","s":"WHAT IS THE TOTAL VOLUME SUM OF LIQUID IN THE DIET?","o":["A. 1.5 L","B. 3 L","C. 2 L","D. 1 L"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"2 L","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Two liters.</b> About <b>nine liters</b> of fluid enter the intestine each day: <b>food and water, two</b>; saliva, one to two; gastric juice, two; pancreatic juice, two to three; plus bile and intestinal secretions. Almost all is reabsorbed.<br>A. One and a half liters: too low for the diet.<br>B. Three liters: too high; that is about the pancreatic volume.<br>D. One liter: closer to saliva than to the diet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 6 (Intestinal Fluid & Electrolytes)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":203,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"259"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":72,"label":"Water follows solutes; needs no carrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-18.jpg","cap":"Intestine absorbs fluid isosmotically with electrolytes by two routes","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G045-4.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cellular and paracellular absorption routes","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D061-112-1","s":"Which of the following substances DOES NOT require assistance for absorption into the enterocyte?","o":["A. Water","B. Glucose","C. Protein","D. Lipids","E. Vitamin C"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Water","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Water.</b> Water needs no carrier of its own; it <b>follows absorbed solutes by osmosis</b>, both between and through cells.<br>B. Glucose: needs a sodium-glucose cotransporter.<br>C. Protein: must be digested to amino acids and small peptides, then carried by sodium- or hydrogen-coupled transporters.<br>D. Lipids: need bile-salt micelles to reach the cell surface.<br>E. Vitamin C: needs sodium-dependent cotransport.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":112,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":72,"label":"Leaky small-intestine vs tight colon junctions","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-18.jpg","cap":"Colon uses cellular route (tight junctions); small intestine leaky paracellular route","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G045-4.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cellular versus paracellular (between tight junctions) absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-225-1","s":"What is the primary difference in the permeability of tight junctions between the small intestine and the colon?","o":["A. Tight junctions in the small intestine allow paracellular movement, while those in the colon do not.","B. Both the small intestine and the colon have leaky tight junctions.","C. Tight junctions in the colon allow paracellular movement, while those in the small intestine do not.","D. Both the small intestine and the colon have tight, impermeable junctions."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Tight junctions in the small intestine allow paracellular movement, while those in the colon do not.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Small-intestine tight junctions are <b>leaky</b>, so water and ions can pass <b>between cells (the paracellular route)</b> and absorbed fluid stays isosmotic. Colon tight junctions are <b>tight</b>, so the colon moves ions through cells and can dry the stool.<br>B. The colon's junctions are not leaky.<br>C. Reversed.<br>D. The small intestine's junctions are leaky, not tight.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 4 (Absorption)","L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":225,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":224,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"260"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":72,"label":"Duodenum Ca2+/iron, jejunum Na+, ileum B12/bile","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: calcium, iron, magnesium absorbed in the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G047-8.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: jejunum is the major site of sodium absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G047-9.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: ileum absorbs B12 and bile","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-186-1","s":"Reabsorption location: _______ in duodenum, ________ in jejunum, ________ in ileum","o":["A. B12 & Bile; divalent cations (like Ca2+); Na+","B. Na+; divalent cations (like Ca2+); B12 & Bile","C. Divalent cations (like Ca2+); B12 & Bile; Na+","D. Divalent cations (like Ca2+); Na+; B12 & Bile"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Divalent cations (like Ca2+); Na+; B12 & Bile","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Duodenum</b>: divalent cations (calcium, iron, magnesium). <b>Jejunum</b>: the major site of <b>sodium (Na+)</b> absorption. <b>Ileum</b>: <b>vitamin B12 and bile salts</b>.<br>A. Puts B12 and bile in the duodenum; they are ileal.<br>B. Puts Na+ in the duodenum and divalent cations in the jejunum; reversed.<br>C. Puts B12 and bile in the jejunum and Na+ in the ileum; the last two are swapped.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)","L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":186,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":206,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"261"},{"id":"G063-6-23","s":"A 23-year-old woman complains of abdominal cramps and bloating that are relieved by defecation. Subsequent clinical evaluation reveals an increased maximal acid output, decreased serum calcium and iron concentrations, and microcytic anemia. Inflammation in which area of the GI tract best explains these findings?","o":["a. Colon","b. Jejunum","c. Duodenum","d. Ileum","e. Stomach"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Duodenum.</b> <b>Calcium and iron are absorbed mainly in the duodenum</b>, so duodenal inflammation lowers both, and low iron gives a microcytic (small red cell) anemia. High acid output fits duodenal disease from excess acid.<br>A. Colon: absorbs water and salt, no nutrients.<br>B. Jejunum: main sodium and nutrient site, but not the main calcium and iron site.<br>D. Ileum: loss would give B12 (large red cell) anemia and bile salt loss.<br>E. Stomach: absorbs no calcium or iron.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":6,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"262"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":72,"label":"Fat-soluble vitamins jejunum; bile salts ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-161.jpg","cap":"Lipids enter cells from micelles; bile salts left for terminal ileum","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 161"},{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: vitamins A, D, E, K absorbed only with fat","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"D023-57-1","s":"Absorption: fill in the blanks. Fat soluble vitamins (__________) are absorbed at the __________, while bile salts are absorbed at the __________.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A, D, E, K; jejunum; ileum","a":null,"why":"<b>A, D, E and K</b> are fat-soluble and are absorbed with other fats from micelles in the <b>upper small intestine (duodenum and jejunum)</b>. <b>Bile salts</b> are left behind in the lumen and reabsorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>, then return to the liver.<br>Common mix-up: the ileum takes back bile salts and B12, not the fat-soluble vitamins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 9 (Ileum)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":57,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":89,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":73,"label":"Na+/K+ ATPase powers intestinal absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-8.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: jejunal absorption is secondary active transport","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G066-229.jpg","cap":"Jejunal cell: basolateral ATP-driven Na+/K+ pump beside sodium cotransporters","where":"2024 TA review, slide 229"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-176-1","s":"What is the transporter that provides the drive for the absorption of electrolytes in the small intestine?","o":["A. Na-AA co-transporter","B. Na-K ATPase","C. Na-H antiport","D. HCO3-Cl antiport","E. Passive Na reabsorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Na-K ATPase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase).</b> On the blood side it pumps sodium (Na+) out, keeping cell Na+ low. That <b>Na+ gradient powers every apical cotransporter</b> (sugars, amino acids, Na+/H+ exchange): this is <b>secondary active transport</b>.<br>A. Na+-amino acid cotransporter: uses the gradient; does not create it.<br>C. Na+/H+ antiporter: also runs on the gradient.<br>D. Bicarbonate-chloride exchanger: an ileal exchanger, not the driving force.<br>E. Passive Na+ reabsorption: needs a gradient that the pump built.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L55 slide 25 (Secondary Active Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":176,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":73,"label":"Jejunum: Na+ with sugar/amino acids, Na+/H+ exchange","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-8.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: jejunum, major sodium site, like proximal tubule, net bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/D007-178.jpg","cap":"Jejunal cell: apical Na+ cotransporters and Na+/H+ exchanger; basolateral pump","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 178"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-227-1","s":"What are the major mechanisms involved in overall net sodium (Na+) absorption in the Jejunum?","o":["A. Na-Cl cotransporters on the apical membrane, and H+/K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane","B. Na-Mg2+ cotransporters and Na/K ATPase on the apical membrane","C. Na-Monosaccharide cotransporters, Na-amino acid cotransporters, and Na/H exchangers on the apical membrane, with Na/K ATPase on the basolateral membrane","D. Na/K ATPase on both apical and basolateral membranes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Na-Monosaccharide cotransporters, Na-amino acid cotransporters, and Na/H exchangers on the apical membrane, with Na/K ATPase on the basolateral membrane","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Jejunal sodium (Na+) enters across the <b>apical</b> membrane with <b>monosaccharides and amino acids</b> and by <b>Na+/H+ exchange</b>; the <b>basolateral sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase)</b> pushes it into blood. The jejunum is the main Na+ site and works like the proximal tubule.<br>A. Na-Cl cotransport is a kidney distal-tubule carrier, and the hydrogen-potassium pump is the stomach's acid pump.<br>B. There is no Na-magnesium cotransporter, and the Na+/K+ pump is never apical here.<br>D. The Na+/K+ pump sits only on the basolateral membrane.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":227,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":226,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"263"},{"id":"D008-138-1","s":"The Jejunum Epithelial cell contains 2 cotransporters (Na+/monosaccharides), (Na+/Amino acids) & via a Na+/H+ exchanger on its apical surface and Na+/K+ ATPase along with a bicarbonate, sugar, amino acid channel on basolateral surface.","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"True","a":"A","why":"<b>True.</b> The jejunal cell has <b>apical</b> sodium-sugar and sodium-amino acid cotransporters and a sodium-hydrogen (Na+/H+) exchanger. Its <b>basolateral</b> side has the sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase) plus exits for sugar, amino acids and bicarbonate. Net result: <b>sodium bicarbonate absorption</b>, like the kidney's proximal tubule.<br>Common mix-up: the chloride-bicarbonate exchanger belongs to the ileum, not the jejunum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":138,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":138,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":306,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-227-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 4"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":73,"label":"Free fatty acids: gut absorbs, kidney never filters","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-161.jpg","cap":"Lipid products diffuse from micelles into enterocytes; packaged into chylomicrons","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 161"},{"src":"../sl/G047-8.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: jejunum works like the kidney's proximal tubule","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-43-1","s":"Which of the following is absorbed in the small bowel that is NOT absorbed by the proximal tubule?","o":["A. a. Galactose","B. b. Free fatty acids","C. c. Amino acids","D. d. Fructose","E. e. Sodium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"b. Free fatty acids","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Free fatty acids.</b> The gut absorbs them by <b>diffusion from micelles</b>. The kidney never sees them: plasma free fatty acids ride on <b>albumin</b>, which is too big to filter, so the proximal tubule has nothing to reabsorb.<br>A. Galactose: taken up by sodium-coupled transport in both gut and proximal tubule.<br>C. Amino acids: reabsorbed in both.<br>D. Fructose: absorbed in the gut and reabsorbed by the proximal tubule.<br>E. Sodium: absorbed in both; the jejunum is 'like the proximal tubule'.","ex":"","fl":["Key (free fatty acids) is right, but the explanation is off: plasma free fatty acids are carried bound to ALBUMIN (so they are not filtered), not 'bound to triglycerides in LDL/HDL'."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L56 slide 46 (Q3. Which of the following is the most important mechanism for absorption of lip)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":43,"f":"D014"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":73,"label":"Ileum: Cl-/HCO3- exchange → net NaCl absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-9.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: ileum has no net bicarbonate absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D013-19.jpg","cap":"Jejunum versus ileum cells; ileum adds apical Cl-/HCO3- exchanger","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"Ileum secretes HCO3- in exchange for Cl-: net NaCl absorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-106-1","s":"What best differentiates the electrolyte absorption from the jejunum and ileum?","o":["A. Na+ reabsorption","B. Amino acid absorption","C. Chloride absorption","D. Proton excretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chloride absorption","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Chloride absorption.</b> Only the <b>ileum</b> has the apical <b>chloride-bicarbonate (Cl-/HCO3-) exchanger</b>, so it absorbs sodium chloride; the <b>jejunum</b> absorbs sodium bicarbonate instead.<br>A. Sodium (Na+) reabsorption: both segments absorb Na+; the jejunum is the main site.<br>B. Amino acid absorption: both use Na+-amino acid cotransport.<br>D. Proton excretion: both secrete hydrogen ion through Na+/H+ exchange.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":106,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"264"},{"id":"G066-230-1","s":"In which segment of the gastrointestinal tract do epithelial cells generate H+ and HCO3-, with H+ being secreted into the lumen via Na/H exchanger and HCO3- being secreted into the lumen via Cl/HCO3 exchanger, resulting in net absorption of NaCl into the cell and no absorption of HCO3?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ileum.</b> The ileum has the jejunal transporters <b>plus an apical chloride-bicarbonate (Cl-/HCO3-) exchanger</b>. Hydrogen ion (H+) leaves by sodium-hydrogen exchange and HCO3- leaves by Cl-/HCO3- exchange, so the result is <b>net sodium chloride absorption and no net HCO3- absorption</b>.<br>A. Jejunum: secretes H+ but sends HCO3- into blood, so it has net sodium bicarbonate absorption.<br>B. Duodenum: main site for calcium and iron.<br>D. Colon: aldosterone-driven sodium absorption and potassium secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":230,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":229,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D060-106-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":74,"label":"Colon: aldosterone → Na+ absorbed, K+ secreted","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-10.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: colon Na+ reabsorption controlled by aldosterone; K+ excreted","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D015-208.jpg","cap":"Colon cell figure: aldosterone adds Na+ channels, raising K+ secretion","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 208"},{"src":"../sl/D007-180.jpg","cap":"Colon works like collecting duct: aldosterone drives Na+ in, K+ out","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 180"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-192-1","s":"What effect does aldosterone have on the Colon channels?","o":["A. Aldosterone inhibits the synthesis of Na+ channels on the apical surface of the colon, thereby decreasing Na+ absorption and K+ secretion","B. Aldosterone increases the synthesis of Na+ channels on the apical surface of the colon, thereby increasing Na+ absorption and K+ secretion","C. Aldosterone has no effect on Na+ channels","D. Aldosterone increases K+ channels leading to increased K+ secretion primarily and Na+ absorption secondarily"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Aldosterone increases the synthesis of Na+ channels on the apical surface of the colon, thereby increasing Na+ absorption and K+ secretion","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Aldosterone <b>increases synthesis of apical sodium (Na+) channels</b> in colon cells, so Na+ absorption rises and, with it, <b>potassium (K+) secretion</b>.<br>A. Aldosterone stimulates the channels; it does not inhibit them.<br>C. It clearly acts on the colon (2026 slide: colon Na+ reabsorption is controlled by aldosterone).<br>D. The main effect is on Na+ channels; extra K+ secretion follows from more Na+ entry, not the reverse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":192,"f":"D036"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":140,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":140,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":308,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":1,"n":"265"},{"id":"G066-232-1","s":"Which of the following mechanisms contributes to increased sodium (Na+) absorption and potassium (K+) secretion in the colon?","o":["A. Inhibition of Na/K ATPase action","B. Decrease in the number of Na+ channels on the apical membrane","C. Reduction in aldosterone levels","D. Induction of aldosterone leading to increased Na+ channels and Na/K ATPase activity"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Induction of aldosterone leading to increased Na+ channels and Na/K ATPase activity","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Aldosterone</b> adds apical sodium (Na+) channels and raises sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase) activity on the blood side. More Na+ is absorbed, more potassium (K+) is pumped into the cell, and more K+ leaks out into the lumen.<br>A. Blocking the Na+/K+ pump would lower both.<br>B. Fewer Na+ channels would lower Na+ absorption.<br>C. Less aldosterone would lower both.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":232,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":231,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-192-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 7"},{"id":"D013-20-1","s":"Colon: Contains Na+ and K+ channels on the luminal side, and Na+/K+ Atpase and K+ channel on the basolateral side. Overall Na+ absorption into the blood and K+ secretion, which is further stimulated by aldosterone, How?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Aldosterone adds more apical Na+ channels, so more Na+ enters and more K+ is secreted (via the Na+/K+ ATPase)","a":null,"why":"<b>Aldosterone increases the number of apical sodium (Na+) channels.</b> More Na+ enters, the basolateral <b>sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase)</b> moves it to blood and brings more <b>potassium (K+)</b> into the cell, and that K+ leaves through apical K+ channels into the lumen.<br>Common mix-up: the colon works like the kidney's collecting duct; the jejunum works like the proximal tubule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 4","sl":20,"f":"D013"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-192-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 8"},{"id":"D060-108-1","s":"Which of the following sites is most similar to the action of aldosterone on the principal cells of the kidney?","o":["A. Ileum","B. Stomach","C. Jejunum","D. Colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> Aldosterone makes colon cells insert more <b>apical sodium (Na+) channels</b>, so more Na+ is absorbed and more <b>potassium (K+) is secreted</b>, just as in kidney principal cells.<br>A. Ileum: absorbs B12 and bile salts and swaps chloride for bicarbonate; not aldosterone-controlled.<br>B. Stomach: secretes acid; no aldosterone effect.<br>C. Jejunum: absorbs Na+ with sugars and amino acids, like the proximal tubule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":108,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"266"},{"id":"D040-190-1","s":"As a throwback to renal physio, the renal principal cells of the late-DCT and CD secrete K and reabsorb Na in response to aldosterone. Which part of the GI tract exhibits the same mechanism?","o":["A) The colon","B) The jejunum","C) The ileum","D) The mouth","E) The duodenum","F) The stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The colon","a":"A","why":"<b>A. The colon.</b> Like kidney principal cells, colon cells have <b>apical sodium (Na+) channels added by aldosterone</b> and <b>potassium (K+) channels</b>; aldosterone raises Na+ absorption and K+ secretion.<br>B. Jejunum: absorbs Na+ with sugars and amino acids, like the proximal tubule.<br>C. Ileum: adds chloride-bicarbonate exchange; not aldosterone-driven.<br>D. The mouth: its lining does not do this (aldosterone acts on salivary gland ducts, which are glands, not the mouth).<br>E. Duodenum: absorbs calcium and iron.<br>F. Stomach: secretes acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":190,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-192-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 9"},{"id":"G067-108-1","s":"A 55-year-old male patient presents with increased blood pressure, muscle cramps, for 3 months. Lab work reveals elevated aldosterone and low renin. Ultrasound showed benign tumor on the left adrenal gland. Which of the following would explain the effects of his condition on the transport of electrolytes through his colon:","o":["A. Increased sodium and water reabsorption, leading to hyperkalemia","B. Decreased sodium reabsorption, leading to hypokalemia","C. Increased sodium reabsorption , leading to hypokalemia","D. Increased chloride absorption, leading to hyperkalemia","E. Decreased potassium secretion, leading to hyperkalemia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased sodium reabsorption, leading to hypokalemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased sodium reabsorption, leading to hypokalemia.</b> An adrenal tumor making aldosterone (high aldosterone, low renin) adds colonic <b>sodium (Na+) channels</b>, so Na+ absorption rises and more <b>potassium (K+) is secreted</b> into stool, adding to the low blood K+ (muscle cramps).<br>A. Na+ and water absorption do rise, but K+ falls, not rises.<br>B. Aldosterone increases, not decreases, Na+ absorption.<br>D. Chloride absorption is not the main effect, and K+ falls.<br>E. K+ secretion rises, not falls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":108,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"267"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":74,"label":"VIP/acetylcholine → cAMP → crypt Cl- channels open","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: VIP increases intestinal secretion","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D047-21.jpg","cap":"Hormones activate adenylyl cyclase; cAMP opens apical Cl- channels in crypts","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-235-1","s":"What is the mechanism by which chloride (Cl-) channels are activated in the epithelial cells of a certain organ, leading to Cl- secretion?","o":["A. Binding of histamine to apical membrane receptors","B. Binding of AcH or VIP to basolateral membrane receptors","C. Activation of Na/K ATPase","D. Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Binding of AcH or VIP to basolateral membrane receptors","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Binding of ACh or VIP to basolateral membrane receptors.</b> Acetylcholine (ACh) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) bind receptors on the crypt cell's <b>blood side</b>, activate adenylyl cyclase, raise <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b>, and open apical chloride channels.<br>A. Histamine is not the crypt trigger; it drives stomach acid through histamine type 2 receptors on parietal cells.<br>C. The sodium-potassium pump keeps gradients but does not open the channels.<br>D. Adenylyl cyclase is activated, not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":235,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":234,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"268"},{"id":"D013-21-1","s":"Intestinal secretion: Apical side of the epithelial cell contains Cl- channels, which are normally closed but get activated by neurotransmitters VIP, Ach. How?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"VIP and ACh bind basolateral receptors, activate adenylyl cyclase, raise cAMP, which opens the apical Cl- channel; Na+ and water follow Cl- into the lumen","a":null,"why":"<b>Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) and acetylcholine (ACh) bind basolateral receptors</b> linked to the stimulatory G protein, so adenylyl cyclase makes <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b>, which opens the apical chloride channels. Chloride enters the lumen; sodium and water follow.<br>Common mix-up: cholera toxin uses this same pathway, but switches it on permanently.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 4","sl":21,"f":"D013"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-235-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 10"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":74,"label":"Crypt cells: basolateral Na-K-2Cl, like loop of Henle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: Na/K/2Cl transporter like the loop of Henle","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D007-181.jpg","cap":"Crypt cell: basolateral Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter loads Cl-; apical Cl- channel","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 181"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-16-1","s":"Where in the intestine would you find Cl- entering into the cells by way of secondary active transport?","o":["A. Apical membrane of epithelial cells of jejunum","B. Apical membrane of pancreatic acinar cells","C. Apical membrane of epithelial cells of colon","D. Basolateral membrane of epithelial cells of crypts"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Basolateral membrane of epithelial cells of crypts","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Basolateral membrane of crypt epithelial cells.</b> Crypt cells load chloride (Cl-) from blood through the <b>sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter</b> on the basolateral side. It is secondary active: the sodium gradient made by the sodium-potassium pump drives Cl- in. Cl- then exits to the lumen through apical Cl- channels.<br>A. Jejunal apical membrane: absorbs sodium with sugars and amino acids; no Cl- cotransporter.<br>B. Pancreatic acinar apical membrane: Cl- leaves there; it does not enter by cotransport.<br>C. Colon apical membrane: sodium and potassium channels, not Cl- cotransport.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":19,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":16,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":214,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":42,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"269"},{"id":"D036-188-1","s":"Which part of the gastrointestinal tract exhibits a similar mechanism of fluid and electrolyte secretion as the epithelial cells lining intestinal crypts, and what is the major site for Cl- and Na+ secretion in this location?","o":["A. Stomach; major site for Cl- secretion in parietal cells.","B. Thick Ascending Limb of the Loop of Henle; major site for Na+ and H2O secretion.","C. Small intestine; major site for Cl- and Na+ secretion in the villi.","D. Colon; major site for Cl- and Na+ secretion in surface epithelial cells.","E. Duodenum; major site for Cl- secretion in the villi."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thick Ascending Limb of the Loop of Henle; major site for Na+ and H2O secretion.","a":"B","why":"<b>B</b> is the intended answer: crypt cells use a basolateral <b>sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter</b>, 'like the loop of Henle' (2026 slide). True fact: the <b>thick ascending limb reabsorbs</b> sodium and chloride and is water-tight; it does not secrete them. Only the cotransporter is shared.<br>A. Parietal cells secrete acid with a hydrogen-potassium pump and a chloride-bicarbonate exchanger, not this setup.<br>C. Intestinal secretion happens in the crypts, not the villi (villi absorb).<br>D. Colon surface cells absorb; colonic secretion is also in crypts.<br>E. Duodenal villi absorb; they are not the secretory site.","ex":"","fl":["Flawed item: the stem asks for a 'part of the gastrointestinal tract' but the keyed answer is a nephron segment. Intended fact = crypt cells use a basolateral Na-K-2Cl cotransporter 'like the Loop of Henle' (G047:11); the TAL actually REABSORBS (does not secrete) Na+, and is water-impermeable."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":188,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-16-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":75,"label":"Diarrhea: baroreceptors and RAAS defend blood pressure","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-12.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: diarrhea can certainly lower blood pressure","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G066-244.jpg","cap":"Fluid loss lowers pressure; baroreceptor reflex and RAAS restore it","where":"2024 TA review, slide 244"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-240-1","s":"A 29 y/o male presents to the ER after an extensive bout of gastroenteritis. He has been having diarrhea for the past 5 days and has not been able to maintain adequate hydration. Which mechanisms have helped your patient maintain their blood pressure while they have been experiencing this significant volume loss? (select all that apply)","o":["A. Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone-System (RAAS)","B. Gastrocolic reflex","C. Baroreceptor mechanism","D. Cushing’s reflex"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["A","C"],"rv":false,"at":"Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone-System (RAAS); Baroreceptor mechanism","a":"A, C","why":"<b>A and C.</b> Diarrhea loses extracellular fluid, so blood volume and <b>arterial pressure fall</b> (2026 slide: diarrhea 'certainly can lower blood pressure'). The fast <b>baroreceptor reflex</b> raises heart rate and vessel tone, and the slower <b>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</b> restores volume.<br>B. Gastrocolic reflex: colon movement after a meal; it does not support blood pressure.<br>D. Cushing reflex: high blood pressure with slow heart rate from raised pressure inside the skull; not volume loss.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":240,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":239,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 12"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":75,"label":"Diarrhea → normal-gap hyperchloremic acidosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-12.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: diarrhea gives normal anion gap, high chloride, low bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D013-22.jpg","cap":"Diarrheal fluid rich in K+ and HCO3-: hyperchloremic normal-gap acidosis","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-192-1","s":"If a patient was experiencing severe diarrhea, which of the following electrolyte abnormalities would most probably be present?","o":["A) Hyperkalemia","B) Hypochloremia","C) Hyperphosphatemia","D) Metabolic acidosis","E) Metabolic alkalosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Metabolic acidosis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Metabolic acidosis.</b> Diarrheal fluid is rich in <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b>; losing it lowers blood HCO3-, and chloride (Cl-) rises to keep charges balanced: a <b>normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis</b>.<br>A. Hyperkalemia: wrong direction; the colon loses potassium, so it falls.<br>B. Hypochloremia: chloride goes up, not down.<br>C. Hyperphosphatemia: not a feature of diarrhea.<br>E. Metabolic alkalosis: comes from vomiting (acid loss), the opposite.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)","L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":192,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-37-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 13"},{"id":"G067-106-1","s":"35 y/o female presents to the emergency room with diarrhea after attending a Cruise vacation for the past week. Bonus Question: What would her acid-base disorder","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Hyperchloremic Metabolic Acidosis with nml anion Gap","a":null,"why":"<b>Normal anion gap, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.</b> Diarrhea loses <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b>-rich fluid. As HCO3- falls, <b>chloride (Cl-) rises</b> to replace it, so the anion gap (sodium minus chloride and bicarbonate) stays normal. 2026 slide: 'Cl- up, HCO3- down = normal gap.'<br>Common mix-up: vomiting loses acid and causes metabolic alkalosis; diarrhea causes acidosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)","L57 slide 10 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":106,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-37-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 14"},{"id":"G055-37-1","s":"Blood chemistry results from a 17-year-old female with a history of laxative abuse show acidosis and Elevated chloride levels. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Diarrhea similar in magnitude and mechanism to cholera","b. Increased H+ cotransport with peptides","c. Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium","d. Increased compensatory acid production to replace gastric fluid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium.</b> Laxative diarrhea washes out intestinal fluid that is high in <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-) and potassium (K+)</b>. Losing HCO3- causes acidosis; chloride rises in its place (normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis), and K+ falls.<br>A. Not a cholera-like process: the acidosis comes from HCO3- loss, not from toxin-driven chloride secretion.<br>B. Hydrogen ion-peptide cotransport absorbs peptides; it does not cause acidosis.<br>D. Losing gastric fluid (vomiting) causes alkalosis, not acidosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":40,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":37,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":234,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":34,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":38,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"270"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":75,"label":"Diarrhea loses HCO3- and K+ → acidosis, hypokalemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-10.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: colon excretes HCO3- and K+; diarrhea = acidosis + low K+","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D007-180.jpg","cap":"Flow-dependent colonic K+ secretion: hypokalemic, normal-gap metabolic acidosis","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 180"},{"src":"../sl/G066-244.jpg","cap":"Diarrheal fluid high in HCO3- and K+: acidosis and hypokalemia","where":"2024 TA review, slide 244"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-105-1","s":"35 y/o female presents to the emergency room with diarrhea after attending a Cruise vacation for the past week. Which of the following would describe her electrolyte imbalance:","o":["A. Hyponatremia","B. Hypokalemia","C. Hypocalcemia","D. Hypomagenisa"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypokalemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypokalemia.</b> Diarrhea speeds fluid through the colon, which raises <b>flow-dependent potassium (K+) secretion</b>; the K+-rich stool lowers blood K+.<br>A. Hyponatremia: diarrheal fluid is about isotonic, so blood sodium usually stays near normal.<br>C. Hypocalcemia: not caused by diarrhea itself.<br>D. Hypomagnesemia: appears only with long-term losses; low K+ is the classic finding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":105,"f":"G067"}],"tier":1,"n":"271"},{"id":"D015-206-1","s":"DIARRHEA CAUSES WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING?","o":["A. HYPOCALCEMIA","B. HYPOKALEMIA","C. HYPONATREMIA","D. METABOLIC ALKALOSIS"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HYPOKALEMIA","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypokalemia.</b> Fast fluid flow through the colon raises <b>potassium (K+) secretion</b>, and diarrheal fluid is rich in K+, so blood K+ falls. 2026 slide: '<b>Diarrhea = acidosis + low K+</b>.'<br>A. Hypocalcemia: not a typical result of diarrhea.<br>C. Hyponatremia: diarrheal fluid is roughly isotonic, so sodium is not the key change.<br>D. Metabolic alkalosis: diarrhea causes acidosis (bicarbonate loss); alkalosis comes from vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":206,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-105-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 15"},{"id":"D010-144-1","s":"315. A 37-year-old man presents with dehydration and hypokalemic metabolic acidosis. This acid–base and electrolyte disorder can occur with excess fluid loss from which of the following organs?","o":["A. Colon","B. Duodenum","C. Liver","D. Pancreas","E. Stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Colon.</b> Intestinal and pancreatic fluids carry <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b>, so losing them causes metabolic acidosis. Only the <b>colon secretes potassium (K+)</b>, more so at high flow, so colonic fluid loss adds <b>hypokalemia</b>.<br>B. Duodenum: its loss would not specifically cause low K+.<br>C. Liver: bile loss gives acidosis, but not the colon's K+ loss.<br>D. Pancreas: HCO3--rich juice gives acidosis but little K+ loss.<br>E. Stomach: losing acid (vomiting) gives hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":144,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":144,"f":"G064"},{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":4,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"272"},{"id":"G066-242-1","s":"2. Which substances are present in high concentration in diarrheal fluid and significantly contribute to the acid-base disturbance caused by diarrhea?","o":["A. K+, Na+","B. HCO3-, Na+","C. HCO3-, K+","D. K+, Cl-","E. Na+, Cl-"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"HCO3-, K+","a":"C","why":"<b>C. HCO3-, K+.</b> Diarrheal fluid is high in <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b> (from pancreatic, biliary and intestinal secretions) and <b>potassium (K+)</b> (from colonic secretion). Losing HCO3- causes metabolic acidosis; losing K+ causes hypokalemia.<br>A. K+, Na+: sodium loss lowers volume but does not drive the acid-base change.<br>B. HCO3-, Na+: sodium is not the acid-base culprit.<br>D. K+, Cl-: chloride rises in blood; losing it is not the cause.<br>E. Na+, Cl-: losing salt does not change acid-base status.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":242,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":241,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G067-105-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":76,"label":"Cholera toxin activates Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-211.jpg","cap":"Costanzo crypt cell: toxin acts on Gs alpha, cAMP opens Cl- channel","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 211"},{"src":"../sl/G066-253.jpg","cap":"Steps: Gs alpha ADP-ribosylated, GTP stuck, adenylyl cyclase on, cAMP up","where":"2024 TA review, slide 253"},{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cholera increases chloride channels into the lumen","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-209-1","s":"AN EXAMPLE OF SECRETORY DIARRHEA IS CHLORA. WHICH IS CAUSED BY EXCESSIVE SECRETION OF FLUID. WHICH SUBUNIT IS GTP PERMANENTLY BOUND TO CAUSING PERMANENT ACTIVATION OF ADENYLYL CYCLASE?","o":["A. αs","B. β","C. γ"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"αs","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Alpha-s.</b> Cholera toxin adds an adenosine diphosphate-ribose (ADP-ribosylation) to the <b>alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>. This blocks its GTP-splitting (GTPase) action, so <b>guanosine triphosphate (GTP) stays bound</b> and adenylyl cyclase stays on.<br>B. Beta: part of the beta-gamma pair; the toxin does not modify it.<br>C. Gamma: also not modified; GTP binds only the alpha subunit.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":209,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 17"},{"id":"D008-122-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices best describes the mechanism of the secretory diarrhea which is often associated by Vibrio cholerae (cholera toxin)?","o":["A. Inactivation of Gi protein coupled to adenylyl cyclase","B. Activation of the Gs protein coupled to adenylyl cyclase","C. Increased potassium secretion into the lumen of the intestine","D. Increased bicarbonate secretion into the lumen of the intestine","E. Activation of the Gq protein coupled to phospholipase C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of the Gs protein coupled to adenylyl cyclase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Activation of the Gs protein coupled to adenylyl cyclase.</b> Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates (adds adenosine diphosphate-ribose to) the alpha subunit of the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>, locking it on. Cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) rises and opens apical chloride channels; sodium and water follow.<br>A. Pertussis toxin, not cholera, inactivates the inhibitory G protein (Gi).<br>C. Potassium secretion is not the cholera mechanism.<br>D. Bicarbonate secretion is not the main mechanism; chloride secretion is.<br>E. The Gq protein activates phospholipase C (calcium signaling), not the cholera pathway.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":122,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":122,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":290,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 18"},{"id":"D061-102-1","s":"A 27-year patient complains of watery and cloudy diarrhea since a recent trip to South America. She states she drank water from the tap the day before she left. Stool cultures come back positive for Vibrio cholerae. What is the mechanism of action of the toxin?","o":["A. Gs stimulation","B. Gi stimulation","C. Gs inhibition","D. Decreased levels of amino acid absorption"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gs stimulation","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gs stimulation.</b> Cholera toxin keeps the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b> switched on, so adenylyl cyclase makes more cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP), chloride is secreted, and secretory diarrhea follows.<br>B. Stimulating the inhibitory G protein (Gi) would lower cAMP. Pertussis toxin is the one that targets Gi, by blocking it.<br>C. Gs inhibition would lower cAMP and stop secretion.<br>D. Amino acid absorption is unaffected; the problem is excess secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":102,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 19"},{"id":"G066-251-1","s":"5. Cholera A toxin catalyzes ADP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit which inhibits the GTPase activity. What is one of the downstream effects of this reaction?","o":["A. Increased cGMP","B. Decreased Cl- secretion","C. Increased cAMP","D. Decreased cAMP","E. Decreased Na+ secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased cAMP","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased cAMP.</b> With the stimulatory G protein (Gs) alpha subunit unable to split guanosine triphosphate (GTP), adenylyl cyclase stays on and <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) rises</b>, holding apical chloride (Cl-) channels open.<br>A. Cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) is raised by Escherichia coli heat-stable toxin, not cholera.<br>B. Cl- secretion increases, not decreases.<br>D. cAMP increases, not decreases.<br>E. Sodium secretion increases (it follows Cl-), not decreases.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":251,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":250,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 20"},{"id":"G055-28-1","s":"A patient develops abdominal cramps and watery diarrhea by excessive secretion of fluid caused by Vibrio Cholerae. The causative agent is most likely to induce","o":["a. ATP formation","b. Adenylyl cyclase inhibition","c. Adenylyl cyclase activation","d. Closure of chloride channels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Adenylyl cyclase activation","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Adenylyl cyclase activation.</b> Cholera toxin locks the stimulatory G protein (Gs) on, so <b>adenylyl cyclase keeps making cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b>. cAMP opens crypt chloride channels, and chloride, sodium and water pour out.<br>A. ATP formation: adenosine triphosphate is used up to make cAMP, not formed.<br>B. Adenylyl cyclase is activated, not inhibited.<br>D. Chloride channels open, not close.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":31,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":28,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":225,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":25,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"273"},{"id":"G066-237-1","s":"In the context of cholera infection, what is the primary mechanism underlying the life-threatening diarrhea observed in affected individuals?","o":["A. Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase","B. Overstimulation of adenylyl cyclase","C. Inhibition of fluid secretion by crypt cells","D. Overstimulation of the absorptive capacity of villi"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Overstimulation of adenylyl cyclase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Overstimulation of adenylyl cyclase.</b> Cholera toxin keeps the stimulatory G protein (Gs) active, so <b>adenylyl cyclase runs nonstop</b>, cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) stays high, and crypt chloride channels stay open. Crypt secretion then <b>overwhelms the villi's absorptive capacity</b>.<br>A. Adenylyl cyclase is stimulated, not inhibited.<br>C. Crypt secretion is increased, not inhibited.<br>D. Villus absorption is overwhelmed, not overstimulated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":237,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":236,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 21"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":76,"label":"Cholera chain: ADP-ribosylated Gs → cAMP → Cl-","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G066-253.jpg","cap":"A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha; GTP stays bound; cAMP rises; Cl- secreted","where":"2024 TA review, slide 253"},{"src":"../sl/G066-254.jpg","cap":"Crypt cell figure: toxin on Gs alpha, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, Cl- channel","where":"2024 TA review, slide 254"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-249-1","s":"4. You travel abroad on a medical mission trip but don’t get all of your vaccines prior to your trip. When you get back, you start having diarrhea. Your stool is very watery and has white specks in it. You go to the ER and are diagnosed with Cholera. What kind of diarrhea does Cholera cause and what reaction does the Cholera toxin cause?","o":["A. Secretory diarrhea; catalyzes ATP ribosylation of the Gi alpha subunit","B. Osmotic diarrhea; catalyzes ADP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit","C. Steatorrhea; catalyzes ATP ribosylation of the Gi alpha subunit","D. Osmotic diarrhea; catalyzes ATP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit","E. Secretory diarrhea; catalyzes ADP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretory diarrhea; catalyzes ADP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Secretory diarrhea; ADP ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit.</b> Cholera toxin adds adenosine diphosphate (ADP)-ribose to the alpha subunit of the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>, locking adenylyl cyclase on. Cyclic adenosine monophosphate rises, chloride channels open, and fluid is <b>secreted</b>.<br>A. Right diarrhea type, but the donor is ADP, not adenosine triphosphate (ATP), and the target is Gs, not the inhibitory Gi.<br>B. Cholera diarrhea is secretory, not osmotic.<br>C. Steatorrhea is fatty stool from fat malabsorption, and Gi is wrong.<br>D. Wrong type (osmotic) and wrong donor (ATP).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":249,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":248,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 22"},{"id":"D008-142-1","s":"How does Cholera lead to secretory Diarrhea?","o":["A. Cholera catalyzes ADP ribosylation of alpha subunit of the Gs protein, causing GTP to permanently bind to alpha subunit, prolonging cAMP activation, which in turn prolings Cl- channel opening","B. Cholera prevents GDP from becoming GTP hence cAMP can’t get synthesized and Cl- channels remained closed","C. Cholera catalyzes ADP ribosylation of alpha subunit of the Gi protein, causing GTP to permanently bind to alpha subunit, prolonging cAMP activation, which in turn prolings Cl- channel opening","D. Cholera doesn’t cause secretory diarrhea"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholera catalyzes ADP ribosylation of alpha subunit of the Gs protein, causing GTP to permanently bind to alpha subunit, prolonging cAMP activation, which in turn prolings Cl- channel opening","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The toxin ADP-ribosylates (adds adenosine diphosphate-ribose to) the alpha subunit of the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>. Guanosine triphosphate (GTP) stays bound, adenylyl cyclase stays on, <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b> stays high, and chloride channels stay open.<br>B. Wrong direction: cholera raises cAMP and opens the channels.<br>C. The target is Gs, not the inhibitory G protein (Gi); Gi is pertussis toxin's target.<br>D. Cholera is the classic secretory diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review exam 1 2022","sl":142,"f":"D008"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"GI physiology exam 1 TA review Spring 2022","sl":142,"f":"G065"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"(post) Physiology 2 exam 1 TA review","sl":310,"f":"F1257"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 23"},{"id":"G067-112-1","s":"A 25-year-old patient presents with profuse watery diarrhea after recently traveling to an area with poor sanitation. Stool analysis confirms the presence of Vibrio cholerae. Bonus Question: How does Cholera toxin increase Cl- secretion?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Binds to As subunit of G protein, causing permanent bound of GTP leadings to increase cAMP - causing Cl- channels to remain open","a":null,"why":"<b>The toxin's A subunit ADP-ribosylates (adds adenosine diphosphate-ribose to) the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs).</b> That blocks its GTP-splitting (GTPase) action, so guanosine triphosphate stays bound and <b>adenylyl cyclase stays on</b>. <b>Cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b> rises and keeps apical chloride channels open; sodium and water follow.<br>Common mix-up: the toxin acts on Gs (stimulatory), not Gi; pertussis toxin is the Gi one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":112,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 24"},{"id":"D040-180-1","s":"How does cholera cause secretory diarrhea in the intestines?","o":["A. Inhibits protein synthesis by cleaving host cell rRNA","B. Activates Gs → ↓ adenylyl cyclase → ↓ cAMP → close Cl- channels for retention→ retain Na and H2O","C. Disrupts the tight cell junctions and recruits neutrophils","D. Activates Gs → ↑ adenylyl cyclase → ↑ cAMP → open Cl- channels for secretion → secrete Na and H2O"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Activates Gs → ↑ adenylyl cyclase → ↑ cAMP → open Cl- channels for secretion → secrete Na and H2O","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Cholera toxin activates the <b>stimulatory G protein (Gs)</b>, which raises <b>adenylyl cyclase activity</b> and <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b>. Apical chloride channels open; chloride is secreted, and sodium and water follow.<br>A. Cleaving ribosomal RNA to stop protein synthesis is Shiga toxin's action.<br>B. Gs activation raises, not lowers, cAMP, and the channels open, not close.<br>C. Breaking tight junctions and recruiting neutrophils describes invasive infection, not cholera.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":180,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 25"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":76,"label":"Cholera opens crypt Cl- channels → Cl- secretion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cholera increases the chloride channels into the lumen","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D015-211.jpg","cap":"Crypt cell: cholera toxin, Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, apical Cl- exit","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 211"}],"q":[{"id":"G061-32-1","s":"Which is the mechanism of how cholera causes diarrhea?","o":["A. Inhibition of vasoactive intestinal peptide","B. Stimulation of potassium channels in colon","C. Inhibition of sodium/potassium ATPase in crypts","D. Stimulation of chloride channels in small intestine","E. Inhibition of Na+/K+/2 Cl- cotransporter in small intestine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stimulation of chloride channels in small intestine","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stimulation of chloride channels in small intestine.</b> Cholera toxin raises cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) in small-intestine crypt cells, which <b>holds apical chloride (Cl-) channels open</b>. Cl-, then sodium and water, flood the lumen (2026 slide: 'cholera increases the chloride channels into the lumen').<br>A. Vasoactive intestinal peptide is not inhibited; it raises cAMP just as the toxin does.<br>B. Colonic potassium channels are not the cholera target.<br>C. The sodium-potassium pump keeps working; it is not inhibited.<br>E. The Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter keeps loading Cl- into the cell; it is not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":32,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":29,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"274"},{"id":"D060-110-1","s":"Which of the following describes effects of cholera toxin in the intestinal crypts?","o":["A. Increase in cGMP","B. Increase in Cl- secretion","C. Decrease in cAMP","D. Increase in K+ secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase in Cl- secretion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Increase in chloride (Cl-) secretion.</b> Cholera toxin turns on adenylyl cyclase in crypt cells; <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) rises</b> and opens apical Cl- channels. Cl- pours into the lumen and sodium and water follow: secretory diarrhea.<br>A. Cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) rises with Escherichia coli heat-stable toxin, not cholera.<br>C. cAMP increases, not decreases.<br>D. Potassium secretion is a colon process; the cholera step is Cl- secretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":110,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 26"},{"id":"G067-110-1","s":"A 25-year-old patient presents with profuse watery diarrhea after recently traveling to an area with poor sanitation. Stool analysis confirms the presence of Vibrio cholerae. Which of the following best describes the content of the lumen for this patient:","o":["A. Decrease Osmolarity, Decrease Cl-, Decrease Na+","B. Increased Osmolarity, Decrease Cl-, Decrease Na+","C. Decreased Osmolarity, Decrease K+, Increase Cl-","D. Increased Osmolarity, Increase Cl-, Increased Na+"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased Osmolarity, Increase Cl-, Increased Na+","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Increased osmolarity, increased Cl-, increased Na+.</b> Cholera toxin opens crypt chloride (Cl-) channels: <b>Cl- is secreted, sodium (Na+) follows, and water follows the salt</b>. D is the only choice in which both Cl- and Na+ rise in the lumen.<br>A. Cl- and Na+ go up, not down.<br>B. Cl- and Na+ go up, not down.<br>C. Cl- rises, but potassium is not the key ion and the lumen gains, not loses, solute.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":110,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G061-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":76,"label":"Cholera → secretory (not osmotic) diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-181.jpg","cap":"Crypt secretion; cholera keeps adenylate cyclase on, giving rice-water stools","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 181"},{"src":"../sl/G066-248.jpg","cap":"Contrast: osmotic diarrhea comes from unabsorbed solute such as lactose","where":"2024 TA review, slide 248"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-247-1","s":"The binding of cholera toxin irreversibly activates adenylate cyclase in the plasma membranes of crypt cells of the small intestine. This is most likely to cause:","o":["A. jaundice","B. secretory diarrhea","C. gallstones","D. osmotic diuresis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"secretory diarrhea","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Secretory diarrhea.</b> Permanently active adenylyl cyclase in crypt cells keeps cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) high, which opens chloride channels; <b>chloride, sodium and water are secreted</b> into the lumen.<br>A. Jaundice: yellow skin from excess bilirubin; unrelated.<br>C. Gallstones: form in the gallbladder; unrelated.<br>D. Osmotic diuresis: extra urine from filtered solute (glucose, mannitol) in the kidney, not the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":53,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":247,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":47,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"275"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":76,"label":"Cholera vs cystic fibrosis: both the CFTR channel","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-57.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cystic fibrosis clogs pancreatic ducts, 'opposite of cholera'","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 57"},{"src":"../sl/D004-216.jpg","cap":"Crypt Cl- channel (CFTR) opened by cAMP; cholera, massive secretory diarrhea","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 216"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-19-1","s":"Is there a connection between cystic fibrosis and cholera?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes – both involve the CFTR Cl- channel of intestinal crypt/duct cells: cholera toxin activates Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP, holding CFTR open → massive Cl- (and Na+/water) secretion = secretory diarrhea; in cystic fibrosis CFTR is defective → too little Cl-/fluid secretion → thick secretions clog pancreatic ducts (the 2026 slide calls CF the 'opposite of cholera').","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes: both hinge on the CFTR chloride channel</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), which cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) opens. <b>Cholera</b> keeps cAMP high, so the channel stays open: massive chloride and water secretion, secretory diarrhea. In <b>cystic fibrosis</b> the channel is defective: too little fluid, so thick secretions clog pancreatic ducts. 2026 slide: CF is the '<b>opposite of cholera</b>'.<br>Common mix-up: cystic fibrosis causes fat and vitamin A, D, E, K malabsorption, not watery diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)","L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":19,"f":"G055"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 28"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":77,"label":"Lactose intolerance → osmotic diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-30.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: lactase deficiency, unabsorbed lactose causes osmotic diarrhea","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G066-248.jpg","cap":"Osmotic diarrhea: undigested lactose stays in lumen and holds water","where":"2024 TA review, slide 248"},{"src":"../sl/G066-253.jpg","cap":"Contrast: secretory diarrhea, cholera toxin raises cAMP, Cl- secreted","where":"2024 TA review, slide 253"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-190-1","s":"Distinguish between Osmotic Diarrhea and Secretory Diarrhea, focusing on their causes and underlying mechanisms.","o":["A. Osmotic Diarrhea results from excessive fluid secretion by crypt cells, while Secretory Diarrhea is caused by non-absorbable solutes in the lumen.","B. Osmotic Diarrhea is characterized by overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria, leading to excessive fluid secretion, whereas Secretory Diarrhea is caused by lactose deficiency.","C. Osmotic Diarrhea is due to the presence of non-absorbable solutes in the lumen, while Secretory Diarrhea results from overactive Cl- channels induced by factors like the cholera toxin.","D. Osmotic Diarrhea is treated with oral rehydration solutions containing glucose, Na+, Cl-, and HCO3-, while Secretory Diarrhea is managed by limiting lactose intake.","E. The major cause of Osmotic Diarrhea is the catalysis of ADP ribosylation by cholera toxin, leading to increased cAMP levels."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Osmotic Diarrhea is due to the presence of non-absorbable solutes in the lumen, while Secretory Diarrhea results from overactive Cl- channels induced by factors like the cholera toxin.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Osmotic diarrhea</b> = a <b>non-absorbable solute</b> (such as undigested lactose) holds water in the lumen. <b>Secretory diarrhea</b> = crypt cells secrete too much fluid because <b>chloride (Cl-) channels are held open</b>, as with cholera toxin.<br>A. Swaps the two definitions.<br>B. Bacterial overgrowth does not define osmotic diarrhea, and lactase deficiency causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea.<br>D. Reversed: oral rehydration treats secretory diarrhea; limiting lactose treats osmotic diarrhea.<br>E. Cholera toxin raises cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) and causes secretory, not osmotic, diarrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)","L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":190,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"276"},{"id":"D060-112-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents the office with a history of bloating, diarrhea and cramping. She states she recently changed to a “keto diet” where she has been eating a lot of cheese and dairy products. Which of the following describes the type of diarrhea she is experiencing?","o":["A. Secretory","B. Laxative abuse","C. Osmotic","D. Bloody"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Osmotic","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Osmotic.</b> Lots of dairy plus bloating and cramps means <b>lactase deficiency</b>: undigested lactose stays in the gut lumen and <b>pulls water in by osmosis</b>.<br>A. Secretory: crypt cells pump out chloride (Cl-), as with cholera toxin; a food sugar does not do this.<br>B. Laxative abuse: a cause of diarrhea, not a type, and nothing here suggests it.<br>D. Bloody: comes from mucosal injury or invasive infection, not from lactose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 2 (57: Objectives)","L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":112,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-190-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 29"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":77,"label":"Cholera → secretory (not osmotic) diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-181.jpg","cap":"Crypt secretion; cholera keeps adenylate cyclase on, giving rice-water stools","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 181"},{"src":"../sl/G066-248.jpg","cap":"Contrast: osmotic diarrhea comes from unabsorbed solute such as lactose","where":"2024 TA review, slide 248"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-188-1","s":"A patient complains of watery diarrhea and culture found it to be Vibrio cholerae. The cholera toxin moves across the intestinal epithelial cell and catalyzes the ADP ribosylation of the alpha subunit of the Gs protein that is coupled to adenylyl cyclase. cAMP levels remain high resulting in secretion of Cl-, Na+ and H2O. Which type of diarrhea is this presentation?","o":["A. Osmotic Diarrhea","B. Secretory Diarrhea"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretory Diarrhea","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Secretory diarrhea.</b> Cholera toxin keeps <b>cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b> high, so crypt cells actively <b>secrete chloride, sodium and water</b>. The fluid is pumped out, not held in by an unabsorbed solute.<br>A. Osmotic diarrhea: caused by a non-absorbable solute in the lumen, such as lactose in lactase deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":188,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":129,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":77,"label":"Lactose intolerance causes osmotic diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-32.jpg","cap":"Unabsorbed sugar: extra osmotic draw in bowel, bacterial overgrowth","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G045-30.jpg","cap":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea; stop milk products","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-47-1","s":"A patient comes with recurrent episodes of diarrhea. Stool culture is normal and there was no response to antibiotic. Calculate stool osmolarity: 2 X (Na + K) = 150 mOsm Measured stool osmolarity: 300 What is the cause?","o":["A. a. Excess glucose in drinks","B. b. Fatty food","C. c. Use of proton pump inhibitors","D. d. Lactose intolerance","E. e. Excess fructose ingestion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"d. Lactose intolerance","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lactose intolerance.</b> Stool osmotic gap = measured stool osmolarity minus two times (sodium + potassium): three hundred minus one hundred fifty leaves a gap of one hundred fifty. A big gap means an unabsorbed solute holds water: <b>osmotic diarrhea</b>, classically from lactose.<br>A. Excess glucose: SGLT-1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) has huge capacity, so glucose is absorbed.<br>B. Fatty food: normal fat is fully digested; fat malabsorption gives steatorrhea.<br>C. Proton pump inhibitors: lower stomach acid; not an osmotic cause.<br>E. Excess fructose: can cause osmotic diarrhea in large amounts, but lactose intolerance is the common, classic cause.","ex":"","fl":["Ambiguity: the osmotic gap (300 - 150 = 150 mOsm) fits any osmotic diarrhea, and excess fructose (absorbed only by slow GLUT-5 diffusion, G045:28-29) also causes one; lactose intolerance is the intended classic answer."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":47,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"277"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":77,"label":"Osmotic diarrhea stops with fasting","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G066-248.jpg","cap":"Osmotic diarrhea: an unabsorbed solute such as lactose holds water in the lumen","where":"2024 TA review, slide 248"},{"src":"../sl/D067-129.jpg","cap":"Fasting removes the unabsorbed solute, so osmotic diarrhea stops; secretory diarrhea continues","where":"2025 Harlem TA review, slide 129"}],"q":[{"id":"D067-128-1","s":"Which of the following features is characteristic of osmotic diarrhea?","o":["A. Diarrhea continues during fasting","B. Stool osmotic gap is low (<50 mOsm/kg)","C. Caused by excess active secretion of electrolytes","D. Diarrhea stops with fasting"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Diarrhea stops with fasting","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Diarrhea stops with fasting.</b> Osmotic diarrhea comes from an unabsorbed solute, such as lactose, that holds water in the lumen. The solute comes from food, so fasting removes it and the diarrhea stops.<br>A. Continues during fasting: that is secretory diarrhea, where the gut keeps secreting chloride and water.<br>B. Low stool osmotic gap (below 50): also secretory, because that stool is salt water; an unmeasured solute makes the gap large (above 125) in osmotic diarrhea.<br>C. Excess active secretion of electrolytes: the definition of secretory diarrhea, as in cholera.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 2 (57: Objectives)","L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":128,"f":"D067"}],"tier":1,"n":"278"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":77,"label":"E. coli toxin blocks electroneutral NaCl absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-19.jpg","cap":"Jejunum and ileum cells: Na+/H+ and Cl-/HCO3- exchangers absorb NaCl","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/D047-21.jpg","cap":"Toxin-raised cAMP opens crypt Cl- channels; secretion overwhelms absorption","where":"2023 TA weekly, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"G063-3-12","s":"A 27-year-old female comes to the ER because of a 2-day bout of profuse watery diarrhea. Physical examination reveals dry mucous membrane with decreased skin turgor. The patient is diagnosed with acute secretory diarrhea and dehydration. Stool and blood cultures are positive for E. Coli. Which of the following cellular mechanism is inhibited by the enterotoxin?","o":["a. Sodium-glucose- cotransport","b. Electroneutral Na+-Cl- transport","c. Electrogenic sodium diffusion","d. Sodium-hydrogen antiport","e. Sodium-bile salt cotransport"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Electroneutral Na+-Cl- transport","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Electroneutral Na+-Cl- transport.</b> Escherichia coli enterotoxins raise cyclic nucleotides (cyclic AMP or cyclic GMP) in intestinal cells. These <b>block the paired sodium-hydrogen and chloride-bicarbonate exchangers</b> that absorb sodium chloride without moving net charge, and they open chloride channels.<br>A. Sodium-glucose cotransport keeps working; that is why <b>oral rehydration solution</b> (salt plus glucose) works.<br>C. Electrogenic sodium channels are the colon's aldosterone pathway, not the toxin's target.<br>D. The sodium-hydrogen exchanger is only half of the blocked pair; B names the whole process.<br>E. Ileal sodium-bile salt cotransport is not affected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)","L57 slide 8 (Jejunal Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"? Q bank","n":"Q bank GI physiology (ignore acid-base)","sl":3,"f":"G063"}],"tier":1,"n":"279"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":78,"label":"Water-soluble vitamins: Na+-dependent cotransport","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: water-soluble vitamins use Na+-dependent cotransport; B12 differs","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G066-222.jpg","cap":"Water-soluble vitamin list, sodium cotransport, and the B12 exception","where":"2024 TA review, slide 222"},{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: vitamins A, D, E, K are fat-soluble","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-119-1","s":"Which of the following listed is a water soluble vitamin?","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Vitamin E","C. Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin B.</b> The B vitamins and vitamin C are <b>water-soluble</b>. The fat-soluble vitamins are <b>A, D, E and K</b>, absorbed with fat from micelles.<br>A. Vitamin D: fat-soluble.<br>B. Vitamin E: fat-soluble.<br>C. Vitamin K: fat-soluble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":119,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D015-166-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 31"},{"id":"D040-172-1","s":"Categorize the Vitamins (fat or water soluble) and explain how each are absorbed. / biotin / K / B12 / B6 / Folic acid / E / A / B1 / Pantothenic acid / B2 / D / Nicotinic acid / C","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Fat soluble: A, D, E, K (incorporated into micelles, diffuse across the apical membrane, packaged into chylomicrons). Water soluble: biotin (B7), B12, B6, folic acid (B9), B1, pantothenic acid (B5), B2, nicotinic acid (B3), C (Na+-dependent cotransport; B12 instead binds R proteins, then intrinsic factor, absorbed in the ileum).","a":null,"why":"<b>Fat-soluble = A, D, E, K</b>: carried in <b>micelles</b>, they diffuse into the cell and leave in <b>chylomicrons</b>, so they need bile and lipase. <b>Water-soluble = all B vitamins</b> (B1, B2, nicotinic acid, pantothenic acid, B6, biotin, folic acid, B12) <b>and C</b>: absorbed by <b>sodium-dependent cotransport</b>. <b>B12 is the exception</b>: R-protein, then intrinsic factor, then the terminal ileum.<br>Common mix-up: B12 is water-soluble even though its absorption route is special.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":172,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"D015-166-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 32"},{"id":"D015-166-1","s":"WHICH MECHANISM FACILITATES ABSORPTION OF WATER-SOLUBLE VITAMINS IN THE SMALL INTESTINE?","o":["A. NA+-GLUCOSE COTRANSPORT","B. NA+-AMINO ACID COTRANSPORT","C. DIFFUSION INTO INTESTINAL CELLS","D. NA+-DEPENDENT COTRANSPORT"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"NA+-DEPENDENT COTRANSPORT","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sodium (Na+)-dependent cotransport.</b> Most water-soluble vitamins (B vitamins, vitamin C) enter intestinal cells by <b>Na+-dependent cotransport</b>, as the 2026 slide states. B12 is the exception (intrinsic factor, terminal ileum).<br>A. Na+-glucose cotransport: the specific carrier for glucose and galactose.<br>B. Na+-amino acid cotransport: the carrier for amino acids.<br>C. Diffusion into cells: how fat-soluble vitamins and lipids enter from micelles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":166,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"280"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":78,"label":"B12 path: pepsin, R-protein, intrinsic factor, ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: pepsin, R-protein, proteases, intrinsic factor, terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D007-176.jpg","cap":"Numbered B12 absorption steps with gastrectomy clinical pearl","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 176"}],"q":[{"id":"D007-184-1","s":"Order the B12 pathway: 1) R-protein added, 2) digestion from food source, 3) absorption into system, 4) intrinsic factor added","o":["A. 1,2,3,4","B. 4,3,2,1","C. 1,3,2,4","D. 2,4,1,3","E. 2,1,4,3"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"2,1,4,3","a":"E","why":"<b>E. 2, 1, 4, 3.</b> (2) <b>Pepsin frees B12 from food</b> in the stomach, then (1) B12 binds <b>R-protein</b> from saliva. Pancreatic proteases strip R-protein in the duodenum, so (4) B12 binds <b>intrinsic factor</b>, and (3) the complex is <b>absorbed in the terminal ileum</b>.<br>A. 1,2,3,4: R-protein cannot bind before pepsin frees B12 from food.<br>B. 4,3,2,1: fully reversed; absorption is the last step.<br>C. 1,3,2,4: puts absorption before release from food.<br>D. 2,4,1,3: intrinsic factor binds only after R-protein is removed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":184,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":204,"f":"F1212"}],"tier":1,"n":"281"},{"id":"D067-120-1","s":"Which protein protects vitamin B12 from degradation in the acidic environment of the stomach before it binds intrinsic factor in the small intestine?","o":["A. Transcobalamin II","B. R-protein (haptocorrin)","C. Intrinsic factor","D. Gastrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"R-protein (haptocorrin)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. R-protein (haptocorrin).</b> In the stomach, B12 freed from food binds <b>R-protein</b>, which comes from saliva. R-protein shields B12 from acid. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases digest the R-protein, and B12 moves onto intrinsic factor.<br>A. Transcobalamin II: carries B12 in the blood after absorption.<br>C. Intrinsic factor: binds B12 later, in the duodenum, and takes it to the terminal ileum.<br>D. Gastrin: a hormone that raises stomach acid; it binds no vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":120,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-184-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 33"},{"id":"D036-184-1","s":"What is the key role of Intrinsic Factor in the absorption of vitamin B12, and what is the sequence of events leading to its absorption in the ileum?","o":["A. Intrinsic Factor prevents the release of vitamin B12 from foods and promotes its absorption in the stomach.","B. Vitamin B12 binds to R proteins in the ileum, forming a complex resistant to pancreatic proteases.","C. Pancreatic proteases in the duodenum directly facilitate the absorption of vitamin B12.","D. Intrinsic Factor facilitates the transfer of vitamin B12 from R proteins, forming a complex resistant to pancreatic proteases, for absorption in the ileum.","E. Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the stomach, and Intrinsic Factor ensures its transport to the ileum."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic Factor facilitates the transfer of vitamin B12 from R proteins, forming a complex resistant to pancreatic proteases, for absorption in the ileum.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Pepsin frees B12 in the stomach; B12 binds <b>R-proteins</b> from saliva; pancreatic proteases digest R-protein in the duodenum, and B12 moves to <b>intrinsic factor</b>. That complex <b>resists proteases</b> and is absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>A. Intrinsic factor does not block release, and B12 is not absorbed in the stomach.<br>B. B12 binds R-proteins in the stomach, and that complex is not protease-resistant (proteases digest it).<br>C. Proteases only strip R-protein; they do not absorb B12.<br>E. B12 is never absorbed in the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":184,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"D007-184-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 34"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":78,"label":"B12 absorbed in terminal ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: five B12 steps ending with absorption at terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D004-210.jpg","cap":"Parietal-cell intrinsic factor carries B12 to the distal ileum","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 210"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"Ileum absorbs B12 and bile salts; resection lowers both","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"D023-52-1","s":"Which of the following is essential for Vitamin B12 absorption?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Gastrin","C. Bicarbonate","D. Intrinsic Factor","E. Hydrochloric acid","F. Functional Duodenum","G. Functional Jejunum","H. Functional terminal Ileum"],"g":["D","H"],"k":["D","H"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic Factor | Functional terminal Ileum","a":"D,H","why":"<b>D and H.</b> B12 absorption needs <b>intrinsic factor</b> (from parietal cells) and a working <b>terminal ileum</b>, where the intrinsic factor-B12 complex is absorbed. The 2026 lecture says it plainly: pepsin and acid are not essential; intrinsic factor is.<br>A. Pepsin: helps free B12 from food, but is not essential.<br>B. Gastrin: stimulates acid; no direct role.<br>C. Bicarbonate: neutralizes acid; not needed for B12.<br>E. Hydrochloric acid: not essential (2026 lecture).<br>F. Duodenum: R-protein is removed here by pancreatic enzymes, but nothing is absorbed here.<br>G. Jejunum: does not absorb B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":52,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":69,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-142-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 35"},{"id":"D010-142-1","s":"313. An 18-year-old man with pernicious anemia lacks intrinsic factor, which is necessary for the absorption of cyanocobalamin. Vitamin B12 is absorbed primarily in which portion of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ileum.</b> The intrinsic factor-vitamin B12 complex travels down the small intestine and is taken up by specific receptors in the <b>terminal ileum</b>. Pernicious anemia removes intrinsic factor, so B12 cannot be absorbed.<br>A. Stomach: makes intrinsic factor but absorbs no B12 (no food is absorbed there).<br>B. Duodenum: pancreatic proteases free B12 from R-protein here, but it is not absorbed here.<br>C. Jejunum: absorbs most nutrients, but not B12.<br>E. Colon: absorbs water and sodium; no nutrients.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"MT TA review 2021","sl":142,"f":"D010"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2021","sl":142,"f":"G064"}],"tier":1,"n":"282"},{"id":"D035-140-1","s":"Vit B12 questions… / Where does Vitamin B12 absorption occur?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ileum","a":null,"why":"<b>The terminal ileum.</b> Vitamin B12 rides on <b>intrinsic factor</b> (made by gastric parietal cells), and the intrinsic factor-B12 complex is taken up only by receptors in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>Common mix-up: most other vitamins, iron and calcium are absorbed higher up (duodenum, jejunum). The ileum is special for <b>B12 and bile salts</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":140,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-142-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 36"},{"id":"G066-141-1","s":"Vit B12 questions: Where does Vitamin B12 absorption occur? What cell releases intrinsic factor? What condition occurs from an absence of Intrinsic factor/ Vit B12?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Where: Ileum. Cell releasing intrinsic factor: Parietal Cells. Condition: Pernicious Anemia","a":null,"why":"<b>Terminal ileum; parietal cells; pernicious anemia.</b> <b>Parietal cells</b> of the stomach body make intrinsic factor. The intrinsic factor-B12 complex is absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>. Without intrinsic factor (autoimmune loss of parietal cells, or gastrectomy) B12 is not absorbed, giving <b>pernicious anemia</b>, a megaloblastic (large red cell) anemia.<br>Common mix-up: chief cells make pepsinogen, not intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":141,"f":"G066"}],"tier":2,"like":"D010-142-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 37"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":79,"label":"B12 deficiency → pernicious (megaloblastic) anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D004-210.jpg","cap":"Autoimmune loss of parietal-cell intrinsic factor blocks ileal B12 uptake","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 210"},{"src":"../sl/D007-176.jpg","cap":"Without intrinsic factor, B12 is lost: megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 176"}],"q":[{"id":"F1203-121-1","s":"Which of the following vitamin deficiencies gives you megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia?","o":["A. Vitamin B2","B. Vitamin C","C. Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B12"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin B12.</b> B12 is needed for DNA synthesis in dividing marrow cells; without it red cells grow large and immature, a <b>megaloblastic anemia</b>. When the cause is <b>loss of intrinsic factor</b>, it is called <b>pernicious anemia</b>.<br>A. Vitamin B2 (riboflavin): lack causes cracked lips and sore tongue, not megaloblastic anemia.<br>B. Vitamin C: lack causes scurvy (bleeding gums, poor wound healing).<br>C. Vitamin K: lack causes bleeding (low clotting factors).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":121,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-245-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 38"},{"id":"D035-140-3","s":"Vit B12 questions… / What condition occurs from an absence of Intrinsic factor/ Vit B12?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pernicious Anemia","a":null,"why":"<b>Pernicious anemia.</b> No intrinsic factor (autoimmune attack on parietal cells, or gastrectomy) means B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, so red cells cannot mature: a <b>megaloblastic anemia</b> (large red cells), often with nerve signs such as numbness.<br>Common mix-up: oral B12 does not fix it, because the problem is absorption; B12 must be <b>injected</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":140,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-245-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 39"},{"id":"D004-245-1","s":"What is Vitamin B12 essential for?","o":["A. WBC formation","B. Vitamin B6 absorption","C. The maturation of RBCs","D. Folic acid absorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The maturation of RBCs","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The maturation of red blood cells.</b> B12 is a cofactor for DNA synthesis, so marrow cells cannot divide normally without it; red cells stay <b>large and immature (megaloblastic anemia)</b>. B12 also keeps nerve myelin healthy.<br>A. White blood cell formation: can be disturbed too, but the tested role is red cell maturation.<br>B. Vitamin B6 absorption: B12 does not control it.<br>D. Folic acid absorption: B12 does not control it; B12 lack traps folate inside cells instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 60 (Review of Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":51,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":245,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"283"},{"id":"D067-122-1","s":"Vitamin B12 deficiency due to pernicious anemia primarily results from:","o":["A. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells leading to intrinsic factor deficiency","B. Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency","C. Lack of gastric acid secretion","D. Ileal resection"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells leading to intrinsic factor deficiency","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells leading to intrinsic factor deficiency.</b> <b>Pernicious anemia</b> is an autoimmune attack on the stomach's parietal cells. They make intrinsic factor, and without it vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum.<br>B. Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency: can lower B12 absorption (R-protein is not removed), but it is not pernicious anemia.<br>C. Lack of gastric acid: goes along with parietal-cell loss, but the missing intrinsic factor is what causes the deficiency.<br>D. Ileal resection: removes the absorption site; a different cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)","L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":122,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D004-245-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 40"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":79,"label":"Gastrectomy → no intrinsic factor → B12 loss","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-4.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: gastrectomy leaves no parietal cells; B12 must be injected","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/D007-176.jpg","cap":"Gastrectomy pearl: no intrinsic factor, oral B12 fails, inject B12","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 176"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-197-1","s":"The stomach of a 60-year-old Japanese male had to be totally removed due to cancer. Which of the following disorders is he likely to develop?","o":["A. Steatorrhea","B. Acute pancreatitis","C. Pernicious anemia","D. Excess fecal carbohydrate loss","E. Occasional episodes of paralysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pernicious anemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pernicious anemia.</b> Total gastrectomy removes all <b>parietal cells</b>, so there is <b>no intrinsic factor</b>; B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum and must be <b>injected</b>.<br>A. Steatorrhea: fat digestion relies on pancreatic lipase and bile, which remain.<br>B. Acute pancreatitis: not caused by removing the stomach.<br>D. Excess fecal carbohydrate loss: carbohydrates are digested and absorbed in the small intestine, which remains.<br>E. Occasional episodes of paralysis: not a result of gastrectomy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":197,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":10,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":10,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":208,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":10,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":40,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"284"},{"id":"D036-182-1","s":"Elaborate on the impact of gastrectomy and pernicious anemia on the development of vitamin B12 deficiency and the subsequent consequences.","o":["A. Gastrectomy leads to increased intrinsic factor production, causing vitamin B12 deficiency.","B. Pernicious anemia is a result of excessive absorption of vitamin B12 after gastrectomy.","C. Loss of parietal cells in gastrectomy results in enhanced absorption of vitamin B12.","D. Gastrectomy causes a decrease in intrinsic factor, leading to impaired vitamin B12 absorption and potential development of pernicious anemia.","E. Pernicious anemia is a consequence of gastrectomy, independent of vitamin B12 levels."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrectomy causes a decrease in intrinsic factor, leading to impaired vitamin B12 absorption and potential development of pernicious anemia.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Gastrectomy removes parietal cells, so <b>intrinsic factor falls</b>; B12 cannot be absorbed in the ileum, causing B12 deficiency and <b>pernicious anemia</b>.<br>A. Gastrectomy lowers, not raises, intrinsic factor.<br>B. Pernicious anemia comes from too little B12, not too much.<br>C. Losing parietal cells blocks, not enhances, B12 absorption.<br>E. Pernicious anemia is caused by low B12, so it is not independent of B12 levels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":182,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 41"},{"id":"D015-122-1","s":"PATIENT PRESENTS FOR GASTRIC BYPASS SURGERY. FOLLOWING HER PROCEDURE, SHE IS ADVISED TO TAKE ADDITIONAL VITAMIN B12 SUPPLEMENTATION DAILY. WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING IS DIRECTLY RESPONSIBLE FOR THIS MICRONUTRIENT DEFICIENCY?","o":["A. DECREASED PRODUCTION OF MUCOUS CELLS","B. REDUCTION OF STOMACH SIZE","C. DECREASED LEVELS OF INTRINSIC FACTOR","D. DECREASED HCL LEVELS"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"DECREASED LEVELS OF INTRINSIC FACTOR","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Decreased levels of intrinsic factor.</b> Removing or bypassing stomach removes <b>parietal cells</b>, the source of intrinsic factor, which B12 needs to be absorbed in the terminal ileum.<br>A. Fewer mucous cells: mucus protects the lining; it does not carry B12.<br>B. Smaller stomach size: size itself does not matter; losing parietal cells does.<br>D. Decreased hydrochloric acid: acid is not essential for B12 absorption (2026 lecture); intrinsic factor is.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)","L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L53 slide 49 (Intrinsic factor)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":122,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 42"},{"id":"D040-188-1","s":"A patient presents with a history of severe progressive stomach cancer. Left with no alternatives, you recommend a total gastrectomy. The stomach is removed and a few weeks later the patient is back complaining of fatigue and a swollen red tongue. Your diagnosis is pernicious anemia, what is the mechanism of this patient’s presentation?","o":["A) Lack of absorption of vitamin B12 in the stomach","B) Inability to digest food due to the absence of the stomach","C) It is a common side effect of surgery","D) Lack of intrinsic factor secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lack of intrinsic factor secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lack of intrinsic factor secretion.</b> Gastrectomy removes the <b>parietal cells</b> that make intrinsic factor; without it, B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, giving pernicious anemia (fatigue, smooth red tongue).<br>A. B12 is never absorbed in the stomach; it is absorbed in the ileum.<br>B. Food is still digested by pancreatic and brush-border enzymes; that is not what causes this anemia.<br>C. It is not a random side effect; it has a clear mechanism (no intrinsic factor).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":188,"f":"D040"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 43"},{"id":"D061-104-1","s":"A 44-year-old patient presents to the clinic for a follow-up following his gastric bypass surgery a few months ago. He had a portion of his stomach removed to help with his weight loss journey. He reports some mild paresthesias in his hands and feet but otherwise feels fine. Fasting serum glucose returns in normal range. Why is the patient experiencing his reported side effects?","o":["A. Removal of parietal cells from the fundus","B. Gastroesophageal reflux","C. Removal of the duodenum","D. Vagotomy"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Removal of parietal cells from the fundus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Removal of parietal cells from the fundus.</b> Parietal cells live in the fundus and body; removing part of the stomach cuts <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so B12 is poorly absorbed. Low B12 damages nerves, causing <b>tingling of hands and feet (paresthesias)</b>. Normal glucose rules out diabetic nerve damage.<br>B. Gastroesophageal reflux: causes heartburn, not nerve symptoms.<br>C. Removal of the duodenum: this surgery removed stomach, and the duodenum does not absorb B12 anyway.<br>D. Vagotomy: cutting the vagus nerve lowers acid and slows emptying; it does not cause paresthesias.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":104,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1212-197-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 44"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":79,"label":"B12 absorbed in terminal ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: five B12 steps ending with absorption at terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D004-210.jpg","cap":"Parietal-cell intrinsic factor carries B12 to the distal ileum","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 210"},{"src":"../sl/D007-179.jpg","cap":"Ileum absorbs B12 and bile salts; resection lowers both","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 179"}],"q":[{"id":"G067-50-1","s":"Which of the following is a likely consequence of ileal resection?","o":["A. Achalasia","B. Atrophic gastritis","C. Constipation","D. Peptic ulcer","E. Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vitamin B12 deficiency.</b> The <b>terminal ileum</b> is the only site that absorbs the intrinsic factor-B12 complex, so after resection B12 must be <b>injected</b>. (Bile salt recycling is lost too.)<br>A. Achalasia: the lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax; unrelated to the ileum.<br>B. Atrophic gastritis: chronic or autoimmune stomach damage, not caused by bowel surgery.<br>C. Constipation: ileal resection causes diarrhea, because bile salts reach the colon.<br>D. Peptic ulcer: acid or Helicobacter pylori injury of the stomach and duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":50,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 45"},{"id":"G052-11-7","s":"CASE 48 Bile Acid Deficiency: Ileal Resection. Paul Bostian is a 39-year-old high school guidance counselor who was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease (an inflammatory bowel disease) when he was a teenager. For 20 years, he was treated medically with antidiarrheal agents and strong anti-inflammatory drugs, including glucocorticoids. During that time, Paul had two spontaneous remissions. However, after these remissions, his disease always returned “with a vengeance.” Last year, he had a small bowel obstruction that could not be relieved with nonsurgical approaches, and he underwent emergency surgery that removed 80% of his ileum. Since the surgery, Paul has had diarrhea. His stools are oily, pale, and foul smelling. He takes the drug cholestyramine to control his diarrhea. However, he continues to have steatorrhea. Paul also receives monthly injections of vitamin B12. QUESTION: Why did Paul need monthly injections of vitamin B12? What conditions can lead to vitamin B12 deficiency?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The intrinsic factor–B12 complex is absorbed only in the terminal ileum, which was removed; B12 deficiency also follows loss of intrinsic factor (gastrectomy, parietal-cell atrophy) or pancreatic insufficiency (R-protein not cleaved).","a":null,"why":"<b>Only the terminal ileum absorbs the intrinsic factor-B12 complex</b>, and Paul lost most of his ileum, so oral B12 is useless and he needs injections. Other causes follow the steps: <b>no intrinsic factor</b> (gastrectomy, autoimmune parietal-cell loss = pernicious anemia) or <b>pancreatic insufficiency</b> (proteases cannot strip R-protein, so B12 never moves onto intrinsic factor).<br>Common mix-up: his oily stools come from the same resection (bile salts lost), not from B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 3 (Water Soluble Vitamins)","L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)","L57 slide 9 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 problem set","n":"supplemental session 3 problem set (with answers)","sl":11,"f":"G052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 46"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":80,"label":"Active vitamin D = 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D drives calcium absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxy (liver) to 1,25-dihydroxy (kidney)","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/D007-177.jpg","cap":"Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 177"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-174-1","s":"What is the name of the active form of the vitamin that aids in the absorption of calcium","o":["A. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","B. 1α-hydroxylase","C. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","D. Cholecalciferol","E. Calbindin D-28K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> is the active vitamin D; it raises intestinal calcium absorption by inducing calbindin.<br>A. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol: inactive form made in the liver.<br>B. 1-alpha-hydroxylase: the kidney enzyme that makes the active form, not the vitamin itself.<br>D. Cholecalciferol: inactive vitamin D3 from skin or diet.<br>E. Calbindin D-28K: the calcium-binding protein that active vitamin D induces, not a vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":174,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"285"},{"id":"F1203-126-1","s":"Which of the following represents the active form of vitamin D?","o":["A. Cholecalciferol","B. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","C. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","D. None"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","a":"B","why":"<b>B. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> is active vitamin D. Path: <b>cholecalciferol</b> (vitamin D3 from skin or diet, inactive), then the liver makes <b>25-hydroxycholecalciferol</b> (inactive, main circulating form), then kidney <b>1-alpha-hydroxylase</b> makes 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol.<br>A. Cholecalciferol: the inactive starting form.<br>C. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol: inactive circulating form made in the liver.<br>D. None: wrong, because B is the active form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":126,"f":"F1203"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":199,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-174-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 47"},{"id":"D067-124-1","s":"Which is the active form of vitamin D responsible for calcium homeostasis?","o":["A. Vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol)","B. 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol)","C. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol)","D. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol).</b> This is the active hormone. The liver adds the first hydroxyl group and the kidney adds the second (with 1-alpha-hydroxylase). It raises calcium absorption in the gut by inducing calbindin.<br>A. Vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol): an inactive form from plants and supplements.<br>B. 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol): the liver's product; the main form in blood, but still inactive.<br>D. Cholecalciferol (vitamin D3): the inactive form made in skin or eaten.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":124,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-174-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 48"},{"id":"D013-17-1","s":"Calcium is absorbed in small intestine, with the help of active vitamin D by inducing the synthesis of calbindin D-28K. Vitamin D Starts out as inactive cholecalciferol, to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25- dihydroxycholecalciferol, (by what enzyme?)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1α-hydroxylase","a":null,"why":"<b>1-alpha-hydroxylase</b> in the <b>kidney</b> (proximal tubule) adds the second hydroxyl group, turning 25-hydroxycholecalciferol into active <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b>. Parathyroid hormone turns this enzyme up.<br>Common mix-up: the first step (25-hydroxylation) happens in the liver; the activating step happens in the kidney.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review wk 4","sl":17,"f":"D013"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-174-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 49"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":80,"label":"Active vitamin D → calbindin → Ca2+ absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: duodenal divalent cations, 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D, calbindin, apoferritin","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D activated in liver and kidney, then induces intestinal calbindin","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/D007-177.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: active vitamin D builds calbindin; iron rides apoferritin then transferrin","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 177"}],"q":[{"id":"D036-186-1","s":"What is the role of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol in promoting calcium absorption in the small intestine, and what consequence may arise in children in case of vitamin D deficiency or failure to convert vitamin D to its active form?","o":["A. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol inhibits calcium absorption and leads to hypercalcemia in children.","B. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the synthesis of calbindin D-28K, enhancing calcium absorption in the small intestine.","C. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol has no impact on calcium absorption, and children remain unaffected in cases of vitamin D deficiency.","D. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol decreases the synthesis of calbindin D-28K, impairing calcium absorption in the small intestine."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the synthesis of calbindin D-28K, enhancing calcium absorption in the small intestine.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (active vitamin D) <b>induces calbindin D-28K</b>, which raises calcium absorption in the small intestine. In children, lack of vitamin D (or failure to activate it) causes <b>rickets</b>: soft, poorly mineralized bones.<br>A. Active vitamin D increases calcium absorption; its lack causes low, not high, calcium.<br>C. It has a major effect, and its lack harms children's bones.<br>D. It increases, not decreases, calbindin synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":186,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"286"},{"id":"D015-197-1","s":"WHAT PROTEIN PROMOTES CALCIUM ABSORPTION?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"CALBINDIN D-28K","a":null,"why":"<b>Calbindin D-28K.</b> Active vitamin D (<b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b>) switches on the gene for calbindin, a <b>calcium-binding protein</b> inside intestinal cells that carries calcium (Ca2+) across the cell, mainly in the duodenum.<br>Common mix-up: calbindin is the carrier protein; 1-alpha-hydroxylase is the kidney enzyme that makes active vitamin D.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":197,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-186-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 50"},{"id":"D060-97-1","s":"Which nutrient is absorbed primarily in the duodenum and jejunum and requires the presence of specific binding proteins for its transport in enterocytes?","o":["A. Vitamin B12","B. Iron","C. Calcium","D. Lipids"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Calcium","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Calcium</b> is the intended answer: absorbed mainly in the <b>duodenum and jejunum</b>, it needs <b>calbindin</b>, a binding protein made under active vitamin D. <b>B. Iron</b> also fits: it is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and crosses the cell bound to <b>apoferritin</b> (2026 slide: apoferritin to transferrin).<br>A. Vitamin B12: absorbed in the terminal ileum, not the duodenum.<br>D. Lipids: diffuse into the cell from micelles; no carrier protein is taught.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed only C; iron is also absorbed mainly in the duodenum and moves through the intestinal cell bound to a protein (apoferritin, per the 2026 slide), so B is equally right."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":97,"f":"D060"}],"tier":2,"like":"D036-186-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 51"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":80,"label":"Liver disease → less vitamin D → hypocalcemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Liver performs 25-hydroxylation; calcium absorption depends on vitamin D and calbindin","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: calcium absorption needs 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D and calbindin","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-7-1","s":"A 10-year-old boy is taken to a Quick Care clinic with yellow eye and skin. After a careful evaluation including liver function tests a diagnosis of hepatitis A is made. Which of the following is also likely to be observed in his profile?","o":["a. Hyperkalemia","b. Hypocalcemia","c. Decreased absorption of protein digestion products","d. None of the above"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypocalcemia","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Hypocalcemia.</b> The liver performs vitamin D's first activation step (<b>25-hydroxylation</b>) and makes the bile needed to absorb this fat-soluble vitamin. Inflamed liver, less active vitamin D, less <b>calbindin</b>, less calcium absorbed: low blood calcium.<br>A. Hyperkalemia: hepatitis has no link to high potassium.<br>C. Protein absorption: needs pancreatic and brush-border enzymes, not the liver or bile.<br>D. None of the above: the liver-vitamin D link makes B correct.","ex":"","fl":["Source itself marks the key with '??'. Intended logic: liver 25-hydroxylates vitamin D and cholestasis cuts fat-soluble vitamin absorption → less 1,25-(OH)2D → less Ca2+ absorption (G047:5). Acute hepatitis A rarely causes clinical hypocalcemia, so D is arguable; keep B as the course's intended answer.","Keep B as the intended answer (liver needed for 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D and bile for its absorption, so Ca2+ absorption falls), but the source itself marks it '??': acute hepatitis A rarely causes measurable hypocalcemia, so 'None of the above' is arguable."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":7,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":7,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":205,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":7,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"287"},{"id":"D015-201-1","s":"WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING OCCURS WITH HEPATITIS A?","o":["A. HYPOKALEMIA","B. HYPOCALCEMIA","C. UNABLE TO CONVERT VITAMIN D","D. ALL THE ABOVE"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"ALL THE ABOVE","a":"D","why":"<b>B and C.</b> The liver performs vitamin D's first activation step (<b>25-hydroxylation</b>), so an inflamed liver leaves the body <b>less able to convert vitamin D</b>. Less active vitamin D means less calbindin, less calcium absorbed, and <b>hypocalcemia</b>.<br>A. Hypokalemia: hepatitis A has no link to low potassium (low potassium is a diarrhea fact).<br>D. All the above: wrong because A is not a feature.","ex":"","fl":["Source keyed 'All the above', but hepatitis A has no link to low potassium; only the vitamin D step (C) and the hypocalcemia it causes (B) are right."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":201,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-7-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 52"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":80,"label":"Iron: apoferritin in cell, transferrin in blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: ferrous iron absorbed; apoferritin to transferrin","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G066-223.jpg","cap":"Iron path: Fe2+ enters, binds apoferritin, crosses basolateral membrane, transferrin","where":"2024 TA review, slide 223"},{"src":"../sl/D007-177.jpg","cap":"Fe2+ binds apoferritin to cross into blood, then binds transferrin","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 177"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-194-1","s":"As a throwback to Biochemistry, which protein does iron bind to as it is transported from the intestinal epithelial cell to the blood through the basolateral membrane?","o":["A) Apoferritin","B) Transferrin","C) Lactoferrin","D) Hepcidin","E) Hemopexin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Apoferritin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Apoferritin.</b> Iron enters intestinal cells as <b>ferrous iron (Fe2+)</b> or heme. Inside, free Fe2+ binds <b>apoferritin</b> and is moved across the blood-side (basolateral) membrane; in blood it binds <b>transferrin</b> (2026 slide: apoferritin to transferrin). Biochemistry names the exit channel ferroportin.<br>B. Transferrin: carries iron in the blood, after it leaves the cell.<br>C. Lactoferrin: iron-binding protein in milk and neutrophils; not this step.<br>D. Hepcidin: liver hormone that blocks iron export; not a carrier.<br>E. Hemopexin: plasma protein that binds free heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":194,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"288"},{"id":"D067-126-1","s":"Which protein transports iron in the bloodstream after it is absorbed and exported from enterocytes?","o":["A. Ferritin","B. Hemosiderin","C. Transferrin","D. Hepcidin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Transferrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Transferrin.</b> Inside the intestinal cell, iron binds apoferritin. Once it leaves the cell, <b>transferrin</b> carries it in the blood to the liver and bone marrow.<br>A. Ferritin: the storage form of iron inside cells (iron bound to apoferritin), not the blood carrier.<br>B. Hemosiderin: another storage form inside cells, seen when iron is in excess.<br>D. Hepcidin: a liver hormone that blocks iron export from cells; it controls absorption but carries no iron.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":126,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"like":"D040-194-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 53"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":80,"label":"Gastrectomy → pernicious and iron-deficiency anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G066-222.jpg","cap":"Gastrectomy equals loss of intrinsic factor, so pernicious anemia","where":"2024 TA review, slide 222"},{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: we eat ferric iron but absorb ferrous iron","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-220-1","s":"What might a patient be diagnosed with after a gastrectomy? (may be more than 1 correct answer)","o":["A. Aplastic anemia","B. Pernicious anemia","C. Hemolytic anemia","D. Iron deficiency anemia"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pernicious anemia; Iron deficiency anemia","a":"B, D","why":"<b>B and D.</b> Gastrectomy removes <b>parietal cells</b>: no intrinsic factor, no B12 absorption, so <b>pernicious anemia</b>. It also removes stomach acid, which helps turn dietary <b>ferric iron (Fe3+)</b> into <b>ferrous iron (Fe2+)</b>, the form absorbed in the duodenum, so <b>iron-deficiency anemia</b> follows.<br>A. Aplastic anemia: bone-marrow failure (drugs, radiation, viruses), not gastrectomy.<br>C. Hemolytic anemia: red cells destroyed in the blood; gastrectomy does not cause it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)","L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":220,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":219,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"289"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":81,"label":"Heme breakdown order: biliverdin to stercobilin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-24.jpg","cap":"Red cells to biliverdin, bilirubin, conjugated bilirubin, urobilinogen, stercobilin","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D040-182-1","s":"What is the order of degradation of hemoglobin?","o":["A. Biliverdin → bilirubin → conjugated bilirubin → urobilinogen → stercobilin","B. Bilirubin → urobilinogen → biliverdin → conjugated bilirubin → stercobilin","C. Stercobilin → urobilinogen → conjugated bilirubin → bilirubin → biliverdin","D. conjugated bilirubin → bilirubin → stercobilin → biliverdin → stercobilin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Biliverdin → bilirubin → conjugated bilirubin → urobilinogen → stercobilin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Heme becomes <b>biliverdin</b> (green), then <b>bilirubin</b> (yellow, unconjugated). The liver conjugates it with uridine diphosphate (UDP)-glucuronyl transferase. Gut bacteria make <b>urobilinogen</b>, and the colon makes <b>stercobilin</b> (brown, in feces).<br>B. Biliverdin comes before bilirubin, not after urobilinogen.<br>C. Fully reversed.<br>D. Starts with conjugated bilirubin and repeats stercobilin; wrong order.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":182,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"290"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":81,"label":"Conjugated bilirubin water-soluble; unconjugated is not","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-182.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin rides albumin to liver; conjugation makes it water-soluble","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-114-1","s":"Which of the following is accurate in regards to bilirubin?","o":["A. Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble","B. Indirect bilirubin is water soluble","C. Unconjugated bilirubin is water soluble","D. Bilirubin is conjugated in the lungs"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble.</b> The liver attaches glucuronic acid, making <b>conjugated (direct) bilirubin</b> water-soluble so it can enter bile, or urine if it backs up.<br>B. Indirect bilirubin is the unconjugated form: not water-soluble.<br>C. Unconjugated bilirubin: fat-soluble, carried on albumin.<br>D. Conjugation happens in the liver, not the lungs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":114,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"291"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":81,"label":"UDP-glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-24.jpg","cap":"Liver UDP-glucuronyl transferase turns bilirubin into conjugated bilirubin","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D007-182.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin from old red cells is conjugated in liver to become water-soluble","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-256-1","s":"Which one of the following enzyme is responsible for the conjugation of bilirubin?","o":["A. Alanine transaminase","B. Alkaline phosphatase","C. Cytochrome P450","D. UDP-glucuronyl transferase","E. Lactate dehydrogenase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"UDP-glucuronyl transferase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. UDP-glucuronyl transferase.</b> This liver enzyme (UDP = uridine diphosphate) attaches <b>glucuronic acid</b> to bilirubin, making water-soluble conjugated bilirubin that can enter bile.<br>A. Alanine transaminase: moves amino groups; a blood marker of liver-cell injury.<br>B. Alkaline phosphatase: a marker of bile-duct blockage and bone turnover.<br>C. Cytochrome P450: phase I drug oxidation, not bilirubin conjugation.<br>E. Lactate dehydrogenase: converts pyruvate and lactate; a marker of cell damage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":256,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":255,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 54"},{"id":"D067-130-1","s":"Which enzyme is responsible for conjugating bilirubin in the liver?","o":["A. Heme oxygenase","B. Biliverdin reductase","C. UDP-glucuronyltransferase","D. Cytochrome P450"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"UDP-glucuronyltransferase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. UDP-glucuronyltransferase.</b> In the liver cell this enzyme (UDP = uridine diphosphate) attaches <b>glucuronic acid</b> to bilirubin. Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble, so it can leave in bile.<br>A. Heme oxygenase: an earlier step; it opens the heme ring to make biliverdin.<br>B. Biliverdin reductase: the next step; it turns biliverdin into unconjugated bilirubin.<br>D. Cytochrome P450: phase I drug metabolism in the liver, not bilirubin conjugation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)","L54 slide 3 (Bile acids & bile salt formation & function)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physio/Pharm Exam 7 GI TA review 2025 (Harlem)","sl":130,"f":"D067"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 55"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":81,"label":"Stercobilin colors stool brown","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-24.jpg","cap":"Colon turns urobilinogen into urobilin and stercobilin, excreted in feces","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D015-214.jpg","cap":"Bile duct obstruction stops bilirubin reaching gut, so no stool pigment","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 214"}],"q":[{"id":"D015-155-1","s":"WHAT COMPOUNDS GIVE STOOL ITS DARK COLOR?","o":["A. UROBILIN AND STERCOBILIN","B. UROBILIN AND BILIRUBIN GLUCURONIDE","C. BILIRUBIN GLUCURONIDE & UROBILINOGEN","D. STERCOBILIN & UROBILINOGEN"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"UROBILIN AND STERCOBILIN","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Urobilin and stercobilin.</b> Gut bacteria turn conjugated bilirubin into <b>urobilinogen</b>; in the colon it becomes <b>urobilin and stercobilin</b>, which leave in feces and color stool brown (stercobilin is the main brown pigment).<br>B. Bilirubin glucuronide (conjugated bilirubin) is changed by gut bacteria before stool forms.<br>C. Bilirubin glucuronide and urobilinogen are earlier steps; urobilinogen is colorless.<br>D. Urobilinogen is colorless; only the stercobilin half is right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":155,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 56"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"Jaundice = bilirubin in skin and sclera","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-13.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: liver converts hemoglobin breakdown products to bilirubin","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/D007-182.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin production and excretion pathway through liver, bile and gut","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-258-1","s":"A 56 year old male patient comes in stating that he has been feel tired recently. When asked, he states he has been drinking alchol since he was 24 years old. On physical examination you notice yellowing of the skin and sclera of the eyes, and his lab report shows elevated liver enzyme levels?","o":["A. Lactase Deficiency","B. Duodenal ulcers","C. Jaundice","D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","E. Peptic ulcers"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jaundice","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jaundice.</b> Yellow skin and sclera = <b>bilirubin deposited in tissues</b>. Here, alcohol-damaged liver cells (high liver enzymes) cannot process bilirubin normally.<br>A. Lactase deficiency: bloating and osmotic diarrhea after dairy.<br>B. Duodenal ulcers: burning pain eased by food; no jaundice.<br>D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: gastrin tumor causing excess acid and ulcers.<br>E. Peptic ulcers: acid injury to stomach or duodenum; no jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":258,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":257,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"C9 extra 57"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"Conjugated bilirubin water-soluble; unconjugated is not","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-182.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin rides albumin to liver; conjugation makes it water-soluble","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-49-1","s":"A patient is brought from the airport with fever, weakness and the sudden onset anemia. Malaria is detected in her red blood cells. The hematocrit drops from 40% to 30%. What do you expect to find in the urine?","o":["A. a. Cholic acid","B. b. Conjugated bilirubin","C. c. Unconjugated bilirubin","D. d. Lithocholic acid","E. e. None of these"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"e. None of these","a":"E","why":"<b>E. None of these.</b> Malaria hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, which is bound to albumin and <b>not filtered</b>, so it does not reach urine. The liver conjugates the extra load and sends it into bile, not blood. (Urine urobilinogen does rise, but it is not listed.)<br>A. Cholic acid: a bile acid; not in urine.<br>B. Conjugated bilirubin: appears in urine only when bile outflow is blocked or liver cells are damaged.<br>C. Unconjugated bilirubin: albumin-bound and insoluble, so it does not enter urine.<br>D. Lithocholic acid: a secondary bile acid; not in urine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":49,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"292"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"Hemolysis → pigment (bilirubin) gallstones","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-214.jpg","cap":"Hemolysis floods the pathway with bilirubin that the liver sends into bile","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 214"}],"q":[{"id":"D014-37-1","s":"A patient has chronic hemolytic anemia from sickle cell disease. They develop right upper quadrant abdominal pain. What is the mechanism?","o":["A. a. Ulcer disease","B. b. Inflammatory bowel","C. c. Cholesterol Gallstones","D. d. Pigmented/Bilirubin stones"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"d. Pigmented/Bilirubin stones","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pigmented (bilirubin) stones.</b> Chronic hemolysis (sickle cell disease) breaks down lots of hemoglobin, so the liver pours <b>extra bilirubin into bile</b>; it precipitates with calcium as <b>pigment gallstones</b>, causing right upper quadrant pain.<br>A. Ulcer disease: acid injury; not linked to hemolysis.<br>B. Inflammatory bowel disease: immune bowel disease; not linked.<br>C. Cholesterol gallstones: from bile with too much cholesterol (obesity, female sex), not from hemolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":37,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"293"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"UDP-glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-24.jpg","cap":"Liver UDP-glucuronyl transferase turns bilirubin into conjugated bilirubin","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D007-182.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin from old red cells is conjugated in liver to become water-soluble","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-116-1","s":"A newborn baby exhibits signs on jaundice. Which of the following enzymes is decreased in this newborn and what type of bilirubin has accumulated?","o":["A. UDP-glucuronyl transferase, conjugated bilirubin","B. Heme oxygenase, heme","C. Biliverdin reductase, biliverdin","D. UDP-glucuronyl transferase, unconjugated bilirubin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"UDP-glucuronyl transferase, unconjugated bilirubin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. UDP-glucuronyl transferase, unconjugated bilirubin.</b> Newborns make too little <b>uridine diphosphate (UDP)-glucuronyl transferase</b> in the first days, so they cannot conjugate fast enough and <b>unconjugated bilirubin</b> builds up (physiological jaundice).<br>A. Too little conjugating enzyme causes unconjugated, not conjugated, buildup.<br>B. Heme oxygenase turns heme into biliverdin; it is not deficient.<br>C. Biliverdin reductase turns biliverdin into bilirubin; it is not deficient.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":116,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"294"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"Bile-duct obstruction → conjugated bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-214.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin pathway: hemolysis gives unconjugated, duct obstruction gives conjugated jaundice","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 214"},{"src":"../sl/D004-231.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin pathway beside bile flow; red X marks the blocked duct","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 231"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-32-1","s":"Jaundice caused by conjugated bilirubin is likely to be triggered by;","o":["a. Hemolytic Jaundice","b. Common bile duct obstruction","c. Physiological jaundice","d. All of the above"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Common bile duct obstruction","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Common bile duct obstruction.</b> Bilirubin is conjugated (joined to glucuronic acid) in the liver; if the duct is blocked, the <b>conjugated</b> form backs up into blood.<br>A. Hemolytic jaundice: excess bilirubin production overwhelms conjugation, so unconjugated rises.<br>C. Physiological (newborn) jaundice: immature uridine diphosphate (UDP)-glucuronyl transferase, so unconjugated rises.<br>D. All of the above: only B gives conjugated jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":35,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":32,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":229,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":60,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":41,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":29,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":39,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"295"},{"id":"F1203-131-1","s":"Jaundice that is the result of obstruction of bile ducts would result in increased production of which type of bilirubin?","o":["A. Unconjugated bilirubin","B. Conjugated bilirubin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Conjugated bilirubin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Conjugated bilirubin.</b> The liver still conjugates bilirubin, but a blocked bile duct stops it reaching the gut, so <b>conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood</b>. Being water-soluble, it spills into urine (dark urine), and stools turn pale (clay-colored).<br>A. Unconjugated bilirubin: rises when production outpaces conjugation, as in hemolysis or newborn jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)","L54 slide 3 (Bile acids & bile salt formation & function)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Middletown TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":131,"f":"F1203"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 58"},{"id":"D015-212-1","s":"OBSTRUCTION OF THE BILE DUCTS CAUSES WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING?","o":["A. PHYSIOLOGICAL JAUNDICE","B. HEMOLYTIC JAUNDICE","C. CONJUGATED JAUNDICE","D. ALL OF THE ABOVE"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"CONJUGATED JAUNDICE","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Conjugated jaundice.</b> Bile-duct obstruction traps bilirubin that the liver has already <b>conjugated</b>, and it backs up into blood.<br>A. Physiological jaundice: newborns make too little of the conjugating enzyme, uridine diphosphate (UDP)-glucuronyl transferase, so unconjugated bilirubin rises.<br>B. Hemolytic jaundice: too many red cells broken down; unconjugated bilirubin rises.<br>D. All of the above: A and B are unconjugated causes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":212,"f":"D015"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 59"},{"id":"D060-86-1","s":"A 43-year old female patient presents to the clinic with moderate upper right quadrant pain. She states that the pain radiates to the tip of her right shoulder. Murphy’s sign is positive on examination and her skin has a slight yellow tint. Her BMI is 35 and she has two children. She is diagnosed with cholelithasis. Which of the following would you find in the patient’s lab work?","o":["A. Increased levels of unconjugated bilirubin","B. Increased levels of conjugated bilirubin","C. Increased levels of bile salt recycling","D. Decreased levels of protein absorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased levels of conjugated bilirubin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Increased levels of conjugated bilirubin.</b> A gallstone blocking bile flow (yellow tint) traps <b>conjugated bilirubin</b>, which backs up into blood.<br>A. Unconjugated bilirubin rises with hemolysis, not obstruction.<br>C. Bile salt recycling falls, because less bile reaches the intestine.<br>D. Protein absorption doesn't need bile; it uses pancreatic and brush-border enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)","L54 slide 3 (Bile acids & bile salt formation & function)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":86,"f":"D060"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"GI TA review exam 1 Spring 2023","sl":100,"f":"D061"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 60"},{"id":"G067-99-1","s":"Patient presents with yellowing of the skin and “clay-colored stool” which of the following would be elevated in the patient’s blood:","o":["A. Stercobilin","B. Unconjugated Bilirubin","C. Conjugated Bilirubin","D. Billirubin-Albumin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Conjugated Bilirubin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Conjugated bilirubin.</b> Clay-colored stool means bilirubin is <b>not reaching the gut</b>: the duct is blocked. The liver keeps conjugating, and conjugated bilirubin <b>backs up into blood</b>.<br>A. Stercobilin: the brown stool pigment made in the colon; it is low here (pale stool), and it is not a blood pigment.<br>B. Unconjugated bilirubin: rises in hemolysis or newborn jaundice.<br>D. Bilirubin-albumin: the unconjugated form carried in blood; not the main rise in obstruction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":99,"f":"G067"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-32-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 61"},{"id":"D024-84-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding bilirubin metabolism?","o":["A. Jaundice from red blood cell lysis results from a build up in conjugated bilirubin, causing dark urine and light stool.","B. Obstructive jaundice, there is an increased build up of conjugated bilirubin, and a decrease in stercobilin.","C. Jaundice is always pathological requiring immediate clinical intervention.","D. Urobilinogen is reabsorbed in the colon, and enters the enterohepatic circulation."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Obstructive jaundice, there is an increased build up of conjugated bilirubin, and a decrease in stercobilin.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> In <b>obstructive jaundice</b>, conjugated bilirubin builds up in blood and little reaches the gut, so <b>stercobilin (the brown stool pigment) falls</b>: pale stools.<br>A. Red cell lysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin; dark urine and light stool point to obstruction instead.<br>C. Jaundice is not always pathological: newborn (physiological) jaundice usually resolves on its own.<br>D. In the course figure, urobilinogen is reabsorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b> into the enterohepatic circulation; the colon makes urobilin and stercobilin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)","L54 slide 3 (Bile acids & bile salt formation & function)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":84,"f":"D024"}],"tier":2,"like":"G055-32-1","kind":"harder","n":"C9 extra 62"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":82,"label":"Stercobilin colors stool brown","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D013-24.jpg","cap":"Colon turns urobilinogen into urobilin and stercobilin, excreted in feces","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D015-214.jpg","cap":"Bile duct obstruction stops bilirubin reaching gut, so no stool pigment","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 214"}],"q":[{"id":"D060-118-1","s":"A patient presents to the ER with obstructive jaundice due to a gallstone. The patient is seen to have dark urine and “clay colored” stool. Which of the following is missing from the patient's stool?","o":["A. Biliverdin","B. Conjugated bilirubin","C. Urobilinogen","D. Stercobilin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stercobilin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stercobilin.</b> A blocked bile duct keeps bilirubin out of the gut, so bacteria cannot make <b>stercobilin</b>, the <b>brown stool pigment</b>: stools turn clay-colored. Conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and urine (dark urine).<br>A. Biliverdin: green pigment made from heme before bilirubin (bruise color); not a stool pigment.<br>B. Conjugated bilirubin: the form that backs up into blood and darkens urine.<br>C. Urobilinogen: a colorless intermediate, partly reabsorbed in the terminal ileum; the brown stool pigment is stercobilin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"Physiology exam 1 TA review","sl":118,"f":"D060"}],"tier":1,"n":"296"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":83,"label":"Liver metabolic functions: carbohydrate, protein, lipid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-14.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: clotting factors (not VIII, von Willebrand), albumin, gluconeogenesis, glycogen, lipids","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D007-183.jpg","cap":"Liver functions grouped: carbohydrate, protein, lipid metabolism, and detoxification","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 183"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-260-1","s":"The liver’s ability to perform gluconeogenesis, store glucose as glycogen, and release stored glucose into the bloodstream are all examples of?","o":["A. Protein Metabolism","B. Carbohydrate Metabolism","C. Lipid Metabolism","D. Bile Acid Synthesis","E. Beta-Oxidation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Carbohydrate metabolism.</b> <b>Gluconeogenesis</b> (making new glucose), <b>glycogen storage</b>, and releasing glucose into blood are the liver's carbohydrate jobs.<br>A. Protein metabolism: making albumin, clotting factors and nonessential amino acids; turning ammonia into urea.<br>C. Lipid metabolism: fatty acid oxidation; making lipoproteins, cholesterol and phospholipids.<br>D. Bile acid synthesis: making bile acids from cholesterol.<br>E. Beta-oxidation: breaking down fatty acids (part of lipid metabolism).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 14 (Liver functions)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":260,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":259,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-262-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 63"},{"id":"G066-262-1","s":"A patient reports to the ED after a car accident; lab work indicates a significantly decreased albumin level, increased ammonia level, and diminished clotting factors. Which metabolic function of the liver is in question here?","o":["A. Protein Metabolism","B. Carbohydrate Metabolism","C. Lipid Metabolism","D. Bile Acid Synthesis","E. Beta-Oxidation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Protein Metabolism","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Protein metabolism.</b> Low <b>albumin</b>, low <b>clotting factors</b> and high <b>ammonia</b> all point to the liver's protein jobs: making plasma proteins and turning ammonia into urea.<br>B. Carbohydrate metabolism: would show low blood glucose.<br>C. Lipid metabolism: would affect lipoproteins and cholesterol.<br>D. Bile acid synthesis: would cause fat malabsorption.<br>E. Beta-oxidation: fatty acid breakdown; these labs do not test it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 14 (Liver functions)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":262,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":261,"f":"D035"}],"tier":1,"n":"297"},{"id":"D036-196-1","s":"What important tasks does the liver perform in protein metabolism, and what issues can arise in case of liver failure?","o":["A. The liver breaks down amino acids, leading to increased protein levels; liver failure causes elevated plasma protein levels.","B. The liver synthesizes amino acids and plasma proteins; liver failure may result in low albumin and clotting problems.","C. Liver failure decreases nonessential amino acid synthesis and raises plasma protein levels.","D. The liver converts ammonia during protein catabolism, increasing urea levels; liver failure reduces urea excretion."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver synthesizes amino acids and plasma proteins; liver failure may result in low albumin and clotting problems.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The liver <b>makes nonessential amino acids and nearly all plasma proteins</b>: albumin and every clotting factor except factor VIII and von Willebrand factor. Liver failure gives <b>low albumin</b> (edema) and <b>bleeding problems</b> (long prothrombin time).<br>A. Failure lowers plasma proteins; it does not raise them.<br>C. Failure lowers, not raises, plasma proteins.<br>D. The liver does turn ammonia into urea, but the failure problem is ammonia buildup (confusion), not reduced urea excretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 14 (Liver functions)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":196,"f":"D036"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-262-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 64"},{"id":"G066-264-1","s":"Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD) is characterized by the body’s inability to synthesize nonessential amino acids. Which metabolic function of the liver has been interrupted?","o":["A. Protein Metabolism","B. Carbohydrate Metabolism","C. Lipid Metabolism","D. Bile Acid Synthesis","E. Beta-Oxidation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Protein Metabolism","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Protein metabolism.</b> Maple syrup urine disease is a disorder of <b>amino acid</b> handling, so it falls under protein metabolism. True fact: it is a defect of branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, so the body cannot <b>break down</b> leucine, isoleucine and valine; it is not a failure to make nonessential amino acids.<br>B. Carbohydrate metabolism: glucose and glycogen.<br>C. Lipid metabolism: fats and lipoproteins.<br>D. Bile acid synthesis: bile acids from cholesterol.<br>E. Beta-oxidation: fatty acid breakdown.","ex":"","fl":["Key letter is right but the stem premise is wrong: MSUD is a defect of branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, i.e. inability to break down the branched-chain (essential) amino acids leucine, isoleucine, valine, not inability to synthesize nonessential amino acids. Either way the liver function involved is protein (amino acid) metabolism.","Stem premise is wrong (answer A still right): MSUD is a defect of branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, i.e. inability to BREAK DOWN the branched-chain (essential) amino acids leucine, isoleucine and valine, not inability to synthesize nonessential amino acids."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":264,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":263,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"G066-262-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 65"},{"id":"D040-184-1","s":"A patient with chronic alcohol abuse disorder comes in to the clinic complaining of right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, esophageal varices, spider angiomas, and ascites. You diagnose them with liver failure. What functions of the liver may be deficient in this patient?","o":["A. Carbohydrate metabolism","B. Protein metabolism","C. Lipid metabolism","D. Bile production","E. Detoxification of substances","F. All the above"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"All the above","a":"F","why":"<b>F. All the above.</b> Cirrhosis knocks out every liver job: <b>carbohydrate</b> (glycogen, gluconeogenesis), <b>protein</b> (albumin, so ascites; clotting factors), <b>lipid</b> (lipoproteins, cholesterol), <b>bile</b> production (jaundice), and <b>detoxification</b> (cytochrome P450 enzymes, ammonia).<br>A to E: each one is true but incomplete alone; liver failure affects all of them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 14 (Liver functions)","L57 slide 15 (Liver Function)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Physio II exam 1 TA review","sl":184,"f":"D040"}],"tier":1,"n":"298"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":83,"label":"Liver detox: phase I P450, phase II conjugation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-15.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: liver detoxification, drug excretion, P450 system","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/D013-25.jpg","cap":"First-pass metabolism; phase I P450 oxidation, phase II conjugation","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G066-266-1","s":"Detoxification of substances by the liver requires…","o":["A. Phase 1 reaction ONLY","B. Phase 2 reaction ONLY","C. Both phase 1 and 2 reactions","D. Neither phase 1 or 2 reactions"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Both phase 1 and 2 reactions","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Both phase 1 and 2 reactions.</b> <b>Phase I</b> (cytochrome P450 oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) changes the molecule; <b>phase II</b> (conjugation with glucuronide, sulfate, glutathione or amino acids) makes it water-soluble for excretion in bile or urine.<br>A. Phase I alone often leaves products that are not soluble enough, and sometimes more toxic.<br>B. Many substances need phase I first to create a site for conjugation.<br>D. Detoxification depends on these reactions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 15 (Liver Function)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"TA review Spring 2024","sl":266,"f":"G066"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"Phys 2 GI review","sl":265,"f":"D035"}],"tier":2,"like":"D024-86-1","kind":"repeat","n":"C9 extra 66"},{"id":"D024-86-1","s":"All of the following are phase II reactions in the liver, except?","o":["A. Conjugation with gluconoride","B. Conjugation with sulfate","C. Catalyzed by cytochrome p-450","D. Conjugated with amino acids"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Catalyzed by cytochrome p-450","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Catalyzed by cytochrome P450.</b> P450 enzymes run <b>phase I</b> (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis). <b>Phase II</b> is <b>conjugation</b>: adding glucuronide, sulfate, glutathione or amino acids to make the substance water-soluble.<br>A. Glucuronide conjugation: phase II.<br>B. Sulfate conjugation: phase II.<br>D. Amino acid conjugation: phase II.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 15 (Liver Function)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Physiology II TA review exam 1","sl":86,"f":"D024"}],"tier":1,"n":"299"},{"id":"D036-194-1","s":"How does the liver safeguard the body from absorbed toxins?","o":["A. By promoting the absorption of toxins into systemic circulation for better utilization.","B. By preventing toxins from entering systemic circulation through \"first-pass metabolism.\"","C. By facilitating direct toxin entry into systemic circulation, bypassing \"first-pass metabolism.\"","D. By minimizing toxin absorption into systemic circulation.","E. By promoting toxin absorption into systemic circulation to enhance their excretion."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"By preventing toxins from entering systemic circulation through \"first-pass metabolism.\"","a":"B","why":"<b>B. First-pass metabolism.</b> Everything absorbed from the gut goes through the <b>portal vein to the liver first</b>, where toxins and drugs are modified (P450 enzymes, conjugation) <b>before reaching the systemic circulation</b>.<br>A. The liver does not promote toxin entry into blood.<br>C. First-pass metabolism is the protection; it is not bypassed.<br>D. The gut, not the liver, controls absorption; the liver acts after absorption.<br>E. The liver keeps toxins out of systemic blood and excretes them in bile or, after conjugation, urine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 15 (Liver Function)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Physio exam 1 TA review Spring 2024","sl":194,"f":"D036"}],"tier":1,"n":"300"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"PHYS-C1","h":"Layers of the wall","p":"<p>This chapter builds the gut wall layer by layer and then wires it: the nervous system inside the wall, the parasympathetic and sympathetic nerves that tune it, and the four ways gut cells deliver a chemical message.</p>\n<p>From the lumen outward the wall has four layers: <b>mucosa</b>, <b>submucosa</b>, <b>muscularis externa</b> and serosa. The mucosa itself has three parts. The epithelium faces the lumen and does the absorbing and secreting. The lamina propria beneath it is connective tissue carrying blood and lymph vessels and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. The <b>muscularis mucosae</b> is a thin sheet of smooth muscle that changes the shape and surface area of the epithelium; it does not move food along.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-15a045dd36.jpg\" alt=\"The wall in three dimensions. On the right, read the layers from the lumen outward: mucosa (epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae), submucosa with its glands, the circular and longitudinal muscle coats, and the serosa. The yellow nerve plexuses lie between the layers; arteries, veins and nerves arrive through the mesentery (left).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The wall in three dimensions. On the right, read the layers from the lumen outward: mucosa (epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae), submucosa with its glands, the circular and longitudinal muscle coats, and the serosa. The yellow nerve plexuses lie between the layers; arteries, veins and nerves arrive through the mesentery (left). <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>In the small intestine the mucosa rises into finger-like villi (Chapter 7). The submucosa holds collagen, elastin, blood vessels and glands. In the duodenum these are <b>Brunner's glands</b>, which open onto the surface through ducts and add HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) when secretin calls for it (Chapter 2). Large glands outside the tube, the pancreas and liver, empty into it through ducts too. The muscularis externa (tunica muscularis) does the moving. Its inner <b>circular muscle</b> is thick and densely innervated, and contracting it narrows the tube. Its outer <b>longitudinal muscle</b> is thin with few nerve fibers, and contracting it shortens the tube. The serosa is connective tissue (adventitia) covered by a mesothelial layer, and the mesentery brings nerves, arteries and veins to the gut. Nerves make no true synapses on gut smooth muscle; they release transmitter from swellings (varicosities) along their axons.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e5d01d82de.jpg\" alt=\"The layers from the lumen (top) to the serosa. Find the two nerve plexuses: the submucosal plexus under the mucosa and the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal muscle.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The layers from the lumen (top) to the serosa. Find the two nerve plexuses: the submucosal plexus under the mucosa and the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal muscle. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.1</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Two nerve plexuses lie between these layers (next part): the <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> between the submucosa and the circular muscle, and the <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> between the circular and the longitudinal muscle.</p>\n<p>Some regions vary the plan. The stomach adds a third, <b>oblique</b> muscle layer inside the circular one, and its mucosa lies in folds (rugae). Its regions run fundus, cardia, body, antrum and pyloric canal, ending at the <b>pyloric sphincter</b> before the duodenum. The lower esophageal sphincter is a 2–4 cm zone of smooth muscle at the diaphragm, where the esophagus's stratified squamous lining meets the stomach's simple columnar lining (Chapter 3). The colon runs cecum (with the appendix), ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, then rectum and anus. That order matters for its nerve supply.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-66a25670c7.jpg\" alt=\"The stomach&#x27;s regions and its three muscle layers. The antrum narrows into the pyloric canal and pyloric sphincter at the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The stomach's regions and its three muscle layers. The antrum narrows into the pyloric canal and pyloric sphincter at the duodenum. <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6ed635df68.jpg\" alt=\"The esophagus entering the stomach at the diaphragm. The lower esophageal sphincter is the 2–4 cm zone of smooth muscle (bracket, left); the arrow at upper right marks where the esophagus&#x27;s stratified squamous lining meets the stomach&#x27;s glandular, simple columnar lining.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">The esophagus entering the stomach at the diaphragm. The lower esophageal sphincter is the 2–4 cm zone of smooth muscle (bracket, left); the arrow at upper right marks where the esophagus's stratified squamous lining meets the stomach's glandular, simple columnar lining. <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The wall and its own nerves","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C1","h":"The enteric nervous system","p":"<p>The gut's own nerves form the <b>ENS (enteric nervous system)</b>: more than 100 million neurons that lie wholly inside the wall in two plexuses. The ENS can run motility, secretion and hormone release on its own, even with every outside nerve cut. Interneurons link all its ganglia, so everything is connected to everything. It takes sensory input from the mucosa and sends output to smooth muscle, secretory cells and endocrine cells. The parasympathetic and sympathetic nerves only turn its activity up or down.</p>\n<p>The <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> lies in the submucosa, just under the mucosa. It reads local conditions and controls local secretion, absorption and small mucosal movements. The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle. It sets the tone of the gut and the speed and strength of its contractions. A handy pairing: Meissner = mucosa side = secretion; Auerbach = muscle side = movement. The gut's pacemaker cells, the <b>ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal)</b>, sit along the myenteric plexus and within the circular muscle (Chapter 3).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-608265c882.jpg\" alt=\"The enteric nervous system. Mucosal receptors feed the submucosal and myenteric plexuses, which talk to each other and drive secretory cells, endocrine cells and muscle; outside nerves only modulate them.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The enteric nervous system. Mucosal receptors feed the submucosal and myenteric plexuses, which talk to each other and drive secretory cells, endocrine cells and muscle; outside nerves only modulate them. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5790cb6ca3.jpg\" alt=\"The same circuit as the 2026 discussion drew it, coloured by layer. The red arrows give each plexus its job: submucosal plexus → GI secretions, myenteric plexus → movements. The yellow stars are the interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), the pacemakers along the myenteric plexus; the ring at left shows them spread through the muscle wall.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The same circuit as the 2026 discussion drew it, coloured by layer. The red arrows give each plexus its job: submucosal plexus → GI secretions, myenteric plexus → movements. The yellow stars are the interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), the pacemakers along the myenteric plexus; the ring at left shows them spread through the muscle wall. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e3f12926a2.jpg\" alt=\"Auerbach&#x27;s plexus in a stained section. Its round ganglion cells (arrows) sit between the inner circular muscle (the dark band across the middle) and the outer longitudinal muscle (cut across, below). A lower-esophagus biopsy with no ganglion cells here is the lesion of achalasia (Chapter 3).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">Auerbach's plexus in a stained section. Its round ganglion cells (arrows) sit between the inner circular muscle (the dark band across the middle) and the outer longitudinal muscle (cut across, below). A lower-esophagus biopsy with no ganglion cells here is the lesion of achalasia (Chapter 3). <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Sensors and reflexes</h4>\n<p><b>Mechanoreceptors</b> in the mucosa sense stretch (distention). <b>Chemoreceptors</b> sense pH, osmolarity, and substrates and end products of digestion. Their signals start reflexes that switch digestive glands on or off and release hormones. In a <b>short reflex</b> the whole loop stays inside the wall: receptor → enteric neurons → muscle or gland. In a long reflex the signal travels to the central nervous system and back. When both limbs run in the vagus, afferent in and efferent out, it is a <b>vago-vagal reflex</b>. Two examples come later: receptive relaxation of the stomach (Chapter 3) and acid secretion when the stomach stretches (Chapter 5).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-98ba3a61e6.png\" alt=\"The same receptors start two kinds of reflex. Short reflexes stay inside the wall; long (vago-vagal) reflexes loop through the brainstem.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,512px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The same receptors start two kinds of reflex. Short reflexes stay inside the wall; long (vago-vagal) reflexes loop through the brainstem. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The wall and its own nerves","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C1","h":"Two divisions with opposite effects","p":"<p>The two outside divisions are neatly opposite in effect. Parasympathetic \"rest and digest\" activity <b>contracts</b> the wall and secretes. The gut is a long muscular tube, so constriction means motion, and ACh (acetylcholine) \"makes you juicy.\" It also opens the sphincters. Sympathetic \"fight or flight\" activity uses <b>NE (norepinephrine)</b>: it relaxes the wall, closes the sphincters, slows motility and cuts secretion (\"dilates and dries\"). Saliva is the one exception, because both divisions increase it (Chapter 4).</p>","s":"Extrinsic nerves","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C1","h":"Parasympathetic and sympathetic wiring","p":"<p><b>Parasympathetic supply</b>: <b>vagus</b> (cranial nerve X) → upper tract: upper third of the esophagus (striated muscle), stomach, small intestine, ascending colon. <b>Pelvic nerve</b> → lower tract, everything past the ascending colon: transverse, descending and sigmoid colon (the vagus also reaches the first part of the transverse colon).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c4b84f1ac8.jpg\" alt=\"The colon in order: cecum and ascending colon (left of the picture, the body&#x27;s right), transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum. The vagus supplies the gut down to the ascending colon and the start of the transverse colon; from there on, the pelvic nerve takes over.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The colon in order: cecum and ascending colon (left of the picture, the body's right), transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum. The vagus supplies the gut down to the ascending colon and the start of the transverse colon; from there on, the pelvic nerve takes over. <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The preganglionic fibers are long and synapse directly in the wall, on the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. The postganglionic neurons are cholinergic (ACh) or <b>peptidergic</b>, releasing VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide), GRP (gastrin-releasing peptide), substance P, enkephalins or NPY (neuropeptide Y). The vagus is mostly sensory: 75% afferent (mechanoreceptors for stretch, chemoreceptors for acid) and 25% efferent (smooth-muscle contraction, secretion, hormone release). Cutting it (vagotomy, once used to cut acid in peptic ulcer disease) removes all these outputs, so it can also bring dumping, delayed gastric emptying, gallstones and diarrhea (Chapter 3).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-46b6bfbf7a.jpg\" alt=\"Extrinsic nerves. Parasympathetic fibers (left) synapse inside the wall and act through ACh or peptides; sympathetic fibers (right) synapse in ganglia outside the gut and act through NE on the plexuses, the muscle and the mucosa.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Extrinsic nerves. Parasympathetic fibers (left) synapse inside the wall and act through ACh or peptides; sympathetic fibers (right) synapse in ganglia outside the gut and act through NE on the plexuses, the muscle and the mucosa. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The lecture states the target simply: the vagus synapses <b>directly onto the myenteric plexus</b>, which relays onward to the submucosal plexus (follow the left-hand fiber in the figure).</p>\n<p><b>Sympathetic supply</b>: short preganglionic fibers synapse <i>outside</i> the bowel in four ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric and hypogastric. Adrenergic postganglionic fibers then end on the myenteric and submucosal plexuses or directly on smooth muscle, endocrine and secretory cells. The fibers are 50% afferent and 50% efferent.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Parasympathetic</th><th>Sympathetic</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Route</td><td>Vagus to ascending colon; pelvic nerve beyond</td><td>Celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric, hypogastric ganglia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Synapse</td><td>In the wall</td><td>Outside the bowel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transmitter</td><td>ACh; peptides</td><td>NE</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Afferent : efferent</td><td>75 : 25 (vagus)</td><td>50 : 50</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wall muscle</td><td>Contracts; ↑ motility</td><td>Relaxes; ↓ motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sphincters</td><td>Open</td><td>Close</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secretion</td><td>↑</td><td>↓ (saliva ↑)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"Extrinsic nerves","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C1","h":"Neural messengers and the four ways to deliver a message","p":"<p>Gut neurons release <b>neurocrines</b>: substances made in the neuron and released by an action potential to act across a synapse. Most enteric neurons hold more than one and co-secrete them. Some act as transmitters, others as modulators of transmitters.</p>\n<p><b>ACh</b>: cholinergic neurons; contracts wall muscle; relaxes sphincters; ↑ salivary, gastric and pancreatic secretion.<br>\n<b>NE</b>: adrenergic neurons; relaxes wall muscle; contracts sphincters; ↑ salivary secretion.<br>\n<b>VIP</b>: relaxes smooth muscle, above all the sphincters (it opens the lower esophageal sphincter); ↑ water and electrolyte secretion from pancreas and intestine; ↑ pepsinogen secretion.<br>\n<b>NO (nitric oxide)</b>: relaxes smooth muscle; partners VIP in receptive relaxation (Chapter 3).</p>\n<p><b>GRP</b> (also called bombesin): vagal neurons of the gastric mucosa; ↑ gastrin secretion (Chapter 2).<br>\n<b>Enkephalins</b> (opiates): contract smooth muscle; ↓ intestinal secretion.<br>\n<b>NPY</b>: relaxes smooth muscle; ↓ intestinal secretion.<br>\n<b>Substance P</b>: co-secreted with ACh; contracts smooth muscle; ↑ salivary secretion.</p>\n<p>Two quick sorts help. Relaxers: VIP, NO, NPY, and NE on the wall. Contractors: ACh, substance P and enkephalins. VIP and NPY both relax muscle but push secretion in opposite directions.</p>\n<p>A <b>VIPoma</b>, a VIP-secreting tumor (usually of pancreatic non-β islet cells), floods the intestine with secretion: large-volume watery diarrhea, low K⁺ (hypokalemia) from the K⁺ lost in stool, and little stomach acid, because VIP also inhibits acid secretion (Chapter 2).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-615a998cdf.jpg\" alt=\"Hormones travel through portal and then systemic blood; paracrines diffuse locally; neurocrines cross a synapse. Each acts on a receptor (R) on its target cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Hormones travel through portal and then systemic blood; paracrines diffuse locally; neurocrines cross a synapse. Each acts on a receptor (R) on its target cell. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 343</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Four ways to deliver a message</h4>\n<p>GI (gastrointestinal) messengers control contraction and relaxation of the wall and sphincters, secretion of enzymes, fluid and electrolytes, and growth (trophic effects). They are classed by their source and their route. A <b>hormone</b> (endocrine) comes from an endocrine cell of the mucosa; these cells sit singly, not gathered into glands. It enters the portal blood, passes the liver, and reaches its targets through the systemic circulation. The target may be back in the gut (gastrin on parietal cells) or elsewhere (GIP, glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide, on pancreatic β cells). A <b>paracrine</b> comes from the same kind of cell but diffuses a short distance to its neighbors; somatostatin and histamine are the examples, and histamine is not a peptide. A <b>neurocrine</b> comes from a neuron, as above. An <b>exocrine</b> secretion goes into a duct or the lumen instead of the blood: saliva, gastric juice, pancreatic juice and bile.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: Thanksgiving dinner</h4>\n<p>A large meal fills the stomach; which messengers are released? <b>ACh</b>, <b>VIP</b> and <b>gastrin</b>. Stretch is sensed by <b>mechanoreceptors</b>, not chemoreceptors, and travels to the brain in vagal <i>afferents</i>; vagal efferents then release ACh and VIP, and stretch plus protein releases gastrin. The traps: filling releases no NE (sympathetic, it relaxes the wall); vagal input raises the force of gastric contractions, not their frequency (Chapter 3); and the muscularis mucosae shapes the absorptive surface but does not drive motility.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Meissner (submucosal) runs secretion; Auerbach (myenteric) runs movement. Parasympathetic fibers synapse inside the wall; sympathetic fibers synapse in ganglia outside it. The vagus is 75% sensory; sympathetic nerves are 50%. The muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa; the muscle that moves food is the muscularis externa.</p>","s":"Chemical messengers","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"The cast and where it lives","p":"<p>This chapter covers the gut's chemical messengers: the four official hormones, the incretins, the candidate hormones and the paracrines. For each one it asks the same questions: which cell makes it, what releases it, what it does, and what happens when there is too much or a drug blocks it.</p>\n<p>Each messenger comes from its own enteroendocrine cell, scattered singly among the epithelial cells. In the stomach, <b>G cells</b> of the antrum make gastrin (a few G cells also sit in the duodenum) and D cells make somatostatin. ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells beside the parietal cells of the body make histamine. In the duodenum, <b>S cells</b> make secretin. <b>I cells</b> (CCK, cholecystokinin) and <b>K cells</b> (GIP, glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) line the duodenum and jejunum. Mo cells (motilin cells) of the upper duodenum make motilin, and L cells of the small intestine make GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f99d2a31fe.jpg\" alt=\"Where each messenger is made, from the stomach body down to the duodenum. The circles mark the vagus&#x27;s two routes to acid: ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto G cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Where each messenger is made, from the stomach body down to the duodenum. The circles mark the vagus's two routes to acid: ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto G cells. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A substance counts as an official GI (gastrointestinal) hormone only if it passes three tests. A physiologic stimulus must release it into the blood, and it must act at a distant site. It must work independently of nerves. It must have been isolated, purified, chemically identified and synthesized. Only four pass: <b>gastrin, CCK, secretin and GIP</b>. <b>Candidate hormones</b> pass some tests but not all: motilin, pancreatic polypeptide, somatostatin, serotonin, ghrelin, leptin, GLP-1 and GLP-2, enteroglucagon, PYY (peptide YY) and neurotensin.</p>\n<p>The official four fall into two structural families. Gastrin and CCK form the <b>gastrin–CCK family</b>: their last five amino acids are identical, so CCK has weak gastrin activity. The CCK<sub>B</sub> receptor responds to both, while the CCK<sub>A</sub> receptor responds only to CCK. Secretin and GIP belong with glucagon in the <b>secretin–glucagon family</b>, so high, drug-level doses of GIP mimic most actions of secretin.</p>\n<p>Each family member needs only the end of its chain to act. The smallest active piece is the C-terminal <b>tetrapeptide</b> (last four amino acids) for gastrin and the C-terminal <b>heptapeptide</b> (last seven) for CCK; secretin, by contrast, needs all 27 of its amino acids.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-de51f43991.jpg\" alt=\"The gastrin–CCK family. Blue boxes: the smallest active piece, the last 4 amino acids of little gastrin (G17) and the last 7 of CCK. Green box: CCK&#x27;s last 5 amino acids, identical to gastrin&#x27;s end, which is why CCK has weak gastrin activity.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The gastrin–CCK family. Blue boxes: the smallest active piece, the last 4 amino acids of little gastrin (G17) and the last 7 of CCK. Green box: CCK's last 5 amino acids, identical to gastrin's end, which is why CCK has weak gastrin activity. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2785d8848b.png\" alt=\"What releases what. Thick arrows mark each hormone&#x27;s strongest stimulus: acid → secretin, fat → CCK, glucose → GIP, distention → gastrin, fasting → motilin. The dashed arrow is a weak stimulus.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,522px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">What releases what. Thick arrows mark each hormone's strongest stimulus: acid → secretin, fat → CCK, glucose → GIP, distention → gastrin, fasting → motilin. The dashed arrow is a weak stimulus. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The messengers and their cells","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"What releases gastrin and what it does","p":"<p><b>Gastrin</b>: G cells, gastric antrum; gastrin–CCK family. Stimuli: <b>distention</b> of the stomach (the main signal); small peptides and amino acids (phenylalanine and tryptophan most potent); vagal stimulation. Inhibited by: acid (H⁺, hydrogen ion; low antral pH), somatostatin, secretin. It comes in two forms, each with its own precursor. <b>G17 (\"little\") gastrin</b> is released by a meal and is the main driver of acid. <b>G34 (\"big\") gastrin</b> circulates at low basal levels between meals.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e2010446e7.jpg\" alt=\"A gastric gland and its cells. The enteroendocrine cell that makes gastrin (green, near the gland base) sits singly among the other cells; in the stomach these G cells lie in the antrum, and their gastrin enters the blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">A gastric gland and its cells. The enteroendocrine cell that makes gastrin (green, near the gland base) sits singly among the other cells; in the stomach these G cells lie in the antrum, and their gastrin enters the blood. <small>· GI hormones lecture, part 1, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The vagus reaches G cells through <b>GRP</b> (gastrin-releasing peptide), not ACh (acetylcholine). This matters because atropine blocks the vagus's ACh on parietal cells but cannot block vagal gastrin release (Chapter 5). The vagus also quiets the D cells, which lifts somatostatin's brake on the G cells.</p>\n<p>Gastrin's actions: ↑ <b>H⁺ secretion</b> by parietal cells; <b>trophic</b> growth of the gastric mucosa; ↑ gastric motility. Most of its acid effect is indirect. Gastrin releases histamine from ECL cells, and the histamine then acts on the parietal cell. That is why H₂ (histamine type 2) blockers lower acid even when gastrin is high (Chapter 5).</p>","s":"Gastrin","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Feedback inhibition and gastrin's signatures","p":"<h4>Feedback inhibition</h4>\n<p>Gastrin is the GI hormone with true <b>feedback inhibition</b>: its own product shuts it off. While food sits in the stomach it buffers the acid, so antral pH stays fairly high and gastrin keeps flowing. As the stomach empties, the acid is no longer buffered and the pH falls. Low pH stops the G cells, partly by releasing somatostatin from D cells. Gastrin's motility effect closes a second loop: by emptying the stomach it removes the distention and peptides that released it. CCK and secretin have no such direct brake; they fade once their stimulus is digested, absorbed or neutralized.</p>\n<p>The loop explains three clinical patterns. A <b>PPI (proton pump inhibitor)</b> such as omeprazole removes acid, so the brake lifts and gastrin rises. Removing the antrum removes the G cells, so acid falls and the gastric mucosa atrophies. A gastrin-secreting tumor (<b>gastrinoma</b>, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) ignores the brake, so gastrin and acid are both high (Chapter 5).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-96e2d91275.png\" alt=\"Gastrin&#x27;s two loops. Gastrin drives acid directly and through ECL histamine, and the low antral pH that results turns the G cells off. Gastrin also speeds the stomach, which empties it and removes the stretch and peptides. Dashed lines ending in a bar mean &quot;switches off&quot;.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,524px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Gastrin's two loops. Gastrin drives acid directly and through ECL histamine, and the low antral pH that results turns the G cells off. Gastrin also speeds the stomach, which empties it and removes the stretch and peptides. Dashed lines ending in a bar mean \"switches off\". <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: gastrin's signatures</h4>\n<p><b>Only feedback, only nerves</b>: of the four official hormones, gastrin alone is switched off by its own product (acid), and gastrin alone is released by a nerve (the vagus, via GRP).</p>\n<p><b>Only one that speeds the stomach</b>: gastrin is the only official hormone that ↑ gastric motility; CCK, secretin and GIP all slow emptying. Motilin is the tempting choice. It does stir the stomach, but only between meals, and it is only a candidate hormone.</p>\n<p><b>Major stimulus for gastrin</b>: <b>distention</b>. Fatty acids release CCK and secretin instead, somatostatin and secretin inhibit gastrin, and bicarbonate does nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Gastrin up after omeprazole</b>: a woman with a duodenal ulcer starts omeprazole, and two days later her gastrin is high. The cause is <b>loss of feedback inhibition</b>: less acid means no antral brake. It is not Zollinger–Ellison syndrome, drug failure or <i>H. pylori</i> (<i>Helicobacter pylori</i>, a stomach bacterium). If gastrin and acid are <i>both</i> high, as in ulcers that recur after <i>H. pylori</i> is cleared, suspect a gastrinoma: its gastrin <b>rises after IV (intravenous) secretin</b> (Chapter 5), which a urea breath test or a drug trial cannot show.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-efe6b8893b.jpg\" alt=\"(a) Typical sites of duodenal and gastric ulcers; the inset shows an ulcer eating through the mucosa toward the muscle. (b) A perforated gastric ulcer: the dark hole goes through the whole wall. A PPI lets ulcers heal by removing acid, and the same loss of acid lets gastrin rise.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">(a) Typical sites of duodenal and gastric ulcers; the inset shows an ulcer eating through the mucosa toward the muscle. (b) A perforated gastric ulcer: the dark hole goes through the whole wall. A PPI lets ulcers heal by removing acid, and the same loss of acid lets gastrin rise. <small>· GI hormones lecture, part 1, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Thick stomach, oily stools</b> from a hyperactive peptide: <b>gastrin</b>, whose trophic effect thickens the mucosa while its acid inactivates lipase (worked through in Chapter 5).</p>","s":"Gastrin","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"What releases CCK and its five actions","p":"<p><b>CCK</b>: I cells, duodenum and jejunum; gastrin–CCK family. Stimuli: fat digestion products, <b>fatty acids and monoglycerides</b> (not intact triglycerides), and small peptides and amino acids. Fat is the strongest. The name says what it does: <i>cholecysto</i> = gallbladder, <i>kinin</i> = movement. Every action helps digest a fatty, protein-rich meal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-95517cbc66.jpg\" alt=\"CCK&#x27;s two main targets. It contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi at the common bile duct opening, so bile enters the duodenum; it also makes the pancreas send enzymes down the pancreatic duct to the same opening.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">CCK's two main targets. It contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi at the common bile duct opening, so bile enters the duodenum; it also makes the pancreas send enzymes down the pancreatic duct to the same opening. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>First, CCK <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so bile enters the duodenum to emulsify fat. Second, it drives <b>pancreatic enzyme</b> secretion: lipase, amylase and proteases such as trypsin. Third, it adds a little pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) itself but mainly <b>potentiates</b> secretin's HCO₃⁻ effect (Chapter 6). Fourth, it is trophic to the exocrine pancreas and gallbladder. Fifth, it <b>inhibits gastric emptying</b>, which buys time for slow fat digestion; CCK is the main hormone that slows the stomach (Chapter 3).</p>","s":"CCK and secretin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"CCK's loop and gallstones after pizza","p":"<p>As the fat and protein are digested and absorbed, and less new chyme arrives, the stimulus fades and CCK falls. Minor actions: a small rise in insulin; release of somatostatin from D cells, which damps acid; lower LES (lower esophageal sphincter) tone; and more intestinal motility, including a share in the gastrocolic reflex through CCK<sub>A</sub> receptors.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f873b88348.png\" alt=\"CCK&#x27;s loop. Fat and protein products release CCK; CCK speeds their digestion and absorption and slows the arrival of new chyme, which removes its own stimulus.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">CCK's loop. Fat and protein products release CCK; CCK speeds their digestion and absorption and slows the arrival of new chyme, which removes its own stimulus. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: gallstones after pizza</h4>\n<p>An obese, pregnant 40-year-old woman has severe right-upper-quadrant pain 3 hours after a cheesy pizza; stones block the common bile duct. Fat released <b>CCK</b>, which squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, so more CCK would worsen the pain. She fits the classic \"female, forty, fat, fertile.\"</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6d427139a8.jpg\" alt=\"Gallstones form in the gallbladder and can lodge in the cystic duct or the common bile duct (red arrows). After a fatty meal, CCK squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, and that is the pain.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Gallstones form in the gallbladder and can lodge in the cystic duct or the common bile duct (red arrows). After a fatty meal, CCK squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, and that is the pain. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"CCK and secretin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Secretin","p":"<p><b>Secretin</b>: S cells, duodenum; secretin–glucagon family. Stimulus: <b>acid</b> in the duodenum, once chyme arrives at pH &lt; 4.5; fatty acids help a little. Secretin is the body's own antacid. Its main action is ↑ <b>HCO₃⁻ and water</b> from three sources: the pancreatic ducts, the liver's bile ducts and the duodenum's <b>Brunner's glands</b> (Chapter 1). This HCO₃⁻ neutralizes the acid so that pancreatic enzymes can work (Chapter 6).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e8344eefa1.jpg\" alt=\"Two of secretin&#x27;s three HCO₃⁻ sources drain here: the pancreatic duct and the bile duct both empty into the duodenum, whose own Brunner&#x27;s glands are the third.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Two of secretin's three HCO₃⁻ sources drain here: the pancreatic duct and the bile duct both empty into the duodenum, whose own Brunner's glands are the third. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Secretin also turns off the source of the acid. It ↓ gastric H⁺ in three ways: it releases somatostatin, it inhibits gastrin release from antral G cells, and it directly damps the parietal cells. It also blocks gastrin's trophic effect, so in effect it cancels everything gastrin does: acid, growth and motility. As duodenal pH rises, secretin release falls and the loop closes. Other actions: ↑ pancreatic growth; slows gastric emptying; slows small-intestinal motility; small ↑ in insulin. The one exception to \"secretin inhibits gastrin\" is the gastrinoma cell, which secretin stimulates.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a4c0d456b8.png\" alt=\"Secretin&#x27;s loop. Duodenal acid releases secretin; HCO₃⁻ from three sources neutralizes the acid and the stomach makes less, so duodenal pH rises and secretin falls.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,523px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Secretin's loop. Duodenal acid releases secretin; HCO₃⁻ from three sources neutralizes the acid and the stomach makes less, so duodenal pH rises and secretin falls. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"CCK and secretin","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"The incretins: GIP and GLP-1","p":"<p>An <b>incretin</b> is a gut hormone, released within minutes of eating, that makes the pancreatic β cells secrete more insulin. There are two.</p>\n<p><b>GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide)</b>, old name gastric inhibitory peptide: K cells, duodenum and jejunum; secretin–glucagon family. It is the only GI hormone released by all three nutrients: <b>glucose</b>, amino acids and fatty acids. In short, food. Actions: ↑ <b>insulin</b> (the major one); ↓ gastric H⁺; ↓ gastric motility and emptying. \"Glucose-dependent\" means GIP boosts insulin only while blood glucose is raised, so the insulin matches the meal. Each name describes one side: the new name its insulin effect, the old name its stomach effects.</p>\n<h4>The incretin effect</h4>\n<p>The incretins show up when an oral glucose load is compared with an IV infusion adjusted so blood glucose follows exactly the same curve. The oral load still releases far more insulin. Blood glucose was identical, so the extra insulin must come from the gut. Glucose in the intestine releases GIP and GLP-1, which add to glucose's direct effect on the β cell. IV glucose bypasses the gut and gets only the direct effect. So an oral load is cleared from the blood faster than the same dose given IV.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example: oral glucose cleared faster than IV</b>. The best explanation is <b>GIP-induced insulin release</b>. CCK raises insulin only slightly. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) neither raises insulin nor releases GIP. GIP does not release glucagon, which would raise glucose anyway.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-828b61b49a.png\" alt=\"Top: blood glucose is made identical for the oral load and the IV infusion. Bottom: insulin still rises far higher after oral glucose; the hatched gap is the incretin effect of GIP and GLP-1.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,466px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top: blood glucose is made identical for the oral load and the IV infusion. Bottom: insulin still rises far higher after oral glucose; the hatched gap is the incretin effect of GIP and GLP-1. <small>· Graph redrawn from GIP and GLP-1 slides of the GI hormones lecture, part 2 (slides 3, 6–8), discussion session slides 46–47, and Costanzo p. 346 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-354eab3b99.png\" alt=\"Incretins and the drugs built on them. Oral glucose adds the incretin route to the direct effect of blood glucose on β cells; DPP-4 ends both incretins within minutes, so gliptins block DPP-4 and the analogues resist it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,508px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Incretins and the drugs built on them. Oral glucose adds the incretin route to the direct effect of blood glucose on β cells; DPP-4 ends both incretins within minutes, so gliptins block DPP-4 and the analogues resist it. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1)</b>: L cells, small intestine; cut from proglucagon. The name misleads, because GLP-1 is not like glucagon: it <b>inhibits glucagon</b>. It ↑ insulin and β-cell sensitivity to glucose, lowers blood glucose, cuts glucose output by the liver, grows the pancreas, slows gastric emptying (so you feel full), ↓ acid and ↓ appetite. Its half-life is only 1–2 minutes.</p>\n<p>Both incretins are destroyed by the enzyme <b>DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4)</b>. Raising incretin activity treats type 2 diabetes in two ways. <b>DPP-4 inhibitors</b> (sitagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin: the \"-gliptins\") slow the breakdown of the body's own GIP and GLP-1; they are added on to other diabetes drugs. <b>GLP-1 analogues</b> (exenatide, liraglutide) resist breakdown, so a subcutaneous injection lasts about 12 hours instead of 1–2 minutes. Exenatide came from Gila monster saliva (John Eng, Bronx VA hospital, 1992; Rosalyn Yalow won her Nobel Prize for radioimmunoassay at the same hospital).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-feb821c21c.jpg\" alt=\"Exenatide&#x27;s story: the Gila monster, whose saliva holds a GLP-1-like peptide that DPP-4 cannot destroy; John Eng, who found it at the Bronx VA in 1992; and the exenatide (Byetta) injection pens made from it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">Exenatide's story: the Gila monster, whose saliva holds a GLP-1-like peptide that DPP-4 cannot destroy; John Eng, who found it at the Bronx VA in 1992; and the exenatide (Byetta) injection pens made from it. <small>· GI hormones lecture, part 2, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: incretins</h4>\n<p><b>Raises insulin the most</b>: <b>GIP</b>. CCK and secretin raise insulin only slightly.</p>\n<p><b>Degraded by DPP-4</b>: <b>GIP</b> (GLP-1 too, if offered).</p>\n<p><b>Cheeseburger, fries and a shake</b>: fat, carbohydrate <i>or</i> protein in the duodenum releases <b>GIP</b>. CCK needs fat or protein, gastrin needs protein (or stretch and vagus), and secretin needs acid or fat. Carbohydrate releases none of them.</p>","s":"Incretins and candidate hormones","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Motilin and the other candidates","p":"<p><b>Motilin</b>: Mo cells, upper duodenum; in neither family. It is released in cycles during <b>fasting</b>, about every 90 minutes, and eating suppresses it; duodenal acid and fat can release some during a meal, but its job is between meals. It ↑ gastric and intestinal motility and starts the <b>MMC (migrating myoelectric complex)</b>, the fasting housekeeping sweep (Chapter 3).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-60eabea998.png\" alt=\"Motilin is the fasting signal. It stays low after the meal; once fasting begins, a motilin peak about every 90 minutes sets off each MMC sweep (Chapter 3).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Motilin is the fasting signal. It stays low after the meal; once fasting begins, a motilin peak about every 90 minutes sets off each MMC sweep (Chapter 3). <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 32, and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Ghrelin</b>: gastric cells; rises before meals and with fasting or starvation, falls after eating; ↑ appetite. <b>Leptin</b>: fat cells, in proportion to fat stores; ↓ appetite over the long term.</p>\n<p><b>Pancreatic polypeptide</b>: pancreas, released by carbohydrate, protein or fat; inhibits pancreatic HCO₃⁻ and enzyme secretion; role uncertain. <b>Enteroglucagon</b>: intestinal cells, released when blood glucose falls; makes the liver ↑ glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. <b>Serotonin</b>: enterochromaffin (Kulchitsky) cells of the intestine; ↑ intestinal motility. Do not mix these up with the histamine-making ECL cells of the stomach.</p>\n<p><b>Inhibited by food</b>: <b>motilin</b>, the fasting hormone. Every official hormone rises with a meal.</p>","s":"Incretins and candidate hormones","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Somatostatin, histamine and the messengers side by side","p":"<p><b>Somatostatin</b>: D cells of the GI mucosa and pancreatic islets (δ cells), also the hypothalamus; acts as both paracrine and hormone. Released by H⁺ (low luminal pH), and also by CCK and secretin; inhibited by vagal stimulation. It <b>inhibits everything</b>: release of all GI hormones (gastrin included), gastric acid, pancreatic and gallbladder secretion, and insulin and glucagon release. On acid it acts directly on the parietal cell and indirectly by cutting gastrin and histamine (Chapter 5).</p>\n<p><b>Histamine</b>: ECL cells of the gastric body; an amine, not a peptide. It diffuses to H₂ receptors on nearby parietal cells and, with gastrin and ACh, stimulates acid. Gastrin and ACh both release it, so it amplifies their effects (potentiation, Chapter 5).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-00d29d0df1.jpg\" alt=\"Where the paracrines are made in a gastric gland: ECL cells (histamine) and D cells (somatostatin) sit among the parietal and chief cells, with G cells at the base. Red labels mark messengers that make acid; the green one marks the brake.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Where the paracrines are made in a gastric gland: ECL cells (histamine) and D cells (somatostatin) sit among the parietal and chief cells, with G cells at the base. Red labels mark messengers that make acid; the green one marks the brake. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>VIP and GRP</b> are neurocrines (Chapter 1) with hormone-like reach. Beyond relaxing sphincters, VIP relaxes the stomach and gallbladder, adds water to pancreatic juice and bile, and inhibits gastric acid secretion and intestinal absorption. GRP is the vagus's messenger to the G cells.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Messenger</th><th>Cell, site</th><th>Released by</th><th>Main actions</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Gastrin</td><td>G cells, antrum</td><td>Distention; peptides, amino acids; vagus (GRP). ↓ by acid, somatostatin</td><td>↑ H⁺ (mostly via histamine); mucosal growth; ↑ gastric motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CCK</td><td>I cells, duodenum, jejunum</td><td>Fatty acids, monoglycerides; peptides, amino acids</td><td>Gallbladder contracts, Oddi relaxes; ↑ pancreatic enzymes; potentiates secretin; trophic; ↓ gastric emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secretin</td><td>S cells, duodenum</td><td>Acid (pH &lt; 4.5); fatty acids</td><td>↑ HCO₃⁻ (pancreas, bile ducts, Brunner's glands); ↓ gastric H⁺; blocks gastrin's growth effect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GIP</td><td>K cells, duodenum, jejunum</td><td>Glucose, amino acids, fatty acids</td><td>↑ insulin; ↓ gastric H⁺; ↓ gastric emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLP-1</td><td>L cells, small intestine</td><td>Oral glucose (meal)</td><td>↑ insulin; ↓ glucagon; ↓ emptying; ↓ appetite</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Motilin</td><td>Mo cells, upper duodenum</td><td>Fasting (cyclic); ↓ by food</td><td>Starts MMC; ↑ motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Somatostatin</td><td>D cells, mucosa and islets</td><td>H⁺. ↓ by vagus</td><td>Inhibits all GI hormones, acid, pancreatic secretion, insulin, glucagon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Histamine</td><td>ECL cells, gastric body</td><td>Gastrin, ACh</td><td>↑ H⁺ via H₂ receptors</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Pairs worth knowing</h4>\n<p>Gastrin and secretin are opposites: gastrin brings acid into the stomach, while secretin stops it and neutralizes what reaches the duodenum. CCK and secretin are partners: CCK sends enzymes and bile, secretin sends HCO₃⁻ and water, and each strengthens the other's pancreatic effect (Chapter 6). GIP and GLP-1 are the two incretins. Motilin and ghrelin are the fasting signals. Somatostatin brakes them all.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: four stimuli, four hormones</h4>\n<p>Acid, sugar, fat and distention are the strongest stimuli, in order, for which hormones? <b>Secretin, GIP, CCK, gastrin</b>. Acid in the duodenum releases secretin, oral glucose GIP, fat CCK, and stretch gastrin. The same map settles single-stimulus questions: stimulated most by acid → secretin; most by fat → CCK.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>GIP and GLP-1 are both incretins broken down by DPP-4, but GIP comes from K cells and GLP-1 from L cells. GLP-1 suppresses glucagon despite its name. Secretin inhibits normal G cells but stimulates gastrinoma cells. CCK answers fat and protein; GIP answers all three nutrients. Motilin and ghrelin rise with fasting; the official hormones rise with a meal.</p>","s":"Paracrines and neural peptides","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Muscle layers, tone and sphincters","p":"<p>This chapter follows a meal as it moves: how gut muscle keeps time, and how the esophagus, stomach, intestines and colon carry food along.</p>\n<p><b>Motility</b> is contraction and relaxation of the gut wall and its sphincters; its jobs are to mix, grind and move. All of it is smooth muscle except three striated (skeletal) parts: the pharynx, the <b>upper third of the esophagus</b> and the <b>external anal sphincter</b>. GI (gastrointestinal) smooth muscle is <b>unitary</b>: gap junctions join the cells into one electrical syncytium that contracts as a unit, producing rhythmic, undulating movements. A few connections link the two muscle layers, so exciting one layer usually excites the other.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-aae288f4ec.jpg\" alt=\"Striated muscle (hatched pink) forms the pharynx, the upper sphincter and the upper esophagus; smooth muscle (tan) forms the rest of the esophagus and the lower sphincter.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Striated muscle (hatched pink) forms the pharynx, the upper sphincter and the upper esophagus; smooth muscle (tan) forms the rest of the esophagus and the lower sphincter. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Circular muscle</b>: shortens a ring → narrows the tube → mixing contractions; it also forms the sphincters. <b>Longitudinal muscle</b>: shortens the tube → peristalsis and mass movements. <b>Phasic</b> contractions (contract, then relax) occur where food is mixed and pushed: esophagus, antrum, small intestine. <b>Tonic</b> contractions (steady tone) occur in the orad (upper) stomach and in the lower esophageal, ileocecal and internal anal sphincters. At rest a sphincter holds a pressure higher than the organs on either side, so nothing passes until a reflex relaxes it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-98dadaf230.jpg\" alt=\"The wall from the lumen outward. The circular muscle (rings) lies inside the longitudinal muscle (lengthwise strands); together they form the muscularis.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The wall from the lumen outward. The circular muscle (rings) lies inside the longitudinal muscle (lengthwise strands); together they form the muscularis. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Each sphincter guards a border: the UES (upper esophageal sphincter) between pharynx and esophagus, the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) between esophagus and stomach, the <b>pyloric sphincter</b> between stomach and duodenum, the ileocecal sphincter between ileum and cecum, and the internal and external anal sphincters at the anus.</p>","s":"Gut smooth muscle and its electrical rhythm","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Slow waves: the pacemaker rhythm","p":"<h4>Slow waves set the rhythm; spikes set the force</h4>\n<p><b>Slow waves</b>, also called the <b>basic electrical rhythm</b>, are not action potentials. They are rhythmic swings of membrane potential of about 5–15 mV (millivolts). They start in the <b>interstitial cells of Cajal</b> (ICC), spindle-shaped pacemaker cells in the myenteric plexus and inside the circular muscle. The ICC fire on their own and pass each wave to the muscle through gap junctions, just as the SA (sinoatrial) node paces the heart. The rising phase is Ca²⁺ (calcium, with some Na⁺, sodium) flowing in through calcium channels; the falling phase is K⁺ (potassium) flowing out.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-53930e65fc.jpg\" alt=\"The ICC (yellow stars) sit at the level of the myenteric plexus, between the circular (orange) and longitudinal (green) muscle layers, and pace both.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The ICC (yellow stars) sit at the level of the myenteric plexus, between the circular (orange) and longitudinal (green) muscle layers, and pace both. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1b7ef8f21f.jpg\" alt=\"A cross-section of the gut: the interstitial cells of Cajal (yellow) form a network all around the muscular wall (pink), outside the villi (red).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A cross-section of the gut: the interstitial cells of Cajal (yellow) form a network all around the muscular wall (pink), outside the villi (red). <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Frequency</b> is fixed for each region: stomach 3/min (3–5/min in the antrum); duodenum 12/min, the fastest; terminal ileum and colon 8/min. Nerves and hormones never change it.</p>","s":"Gut smooth muscle and its electrical rhythm","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Spikes, contraction and what changes them","p":"<p>A slow wave alone gives at most a weak, tonic contraction. When a wave depolarizes the cell to threshold, a burst of <b>spike potentials</b> (true action potentials) rides on its peak. More Ca²⁺ enters, and a strong phasic contraction follows a moment later. The more spikes on a wave, the stronger the contraction; the spikes summate into one long contraction instead of separate twitches. Because every contraction needs a slow wave under it, the slow-wave frequency is the <b>maximum contraction rate</b> of that region. Not every wave reaches threshold: in the duodenum only about 30–40% carry spikes.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-085590d4a3.jpg\" alt=\"Top: a burst of spikes sits on the plateau of each slow wave. Bottom: tension (dotted line) rises just after each burst.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top: a burst of spikes sits on the plateau of each slow wave. Bottom: tension (dotted line) rises just after each burst. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 349</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Nerves and hormones act by shifting the baseline. <b>Stretch, ACh (acetylcholine) and parasympathetic</b> input depolarize the muscle, so more of each wave crosses threshold: more spikes, stronger contractions. <b>NE (norepinephrine) and sympathetic</b> input hyperpolarize it, so the waves stay below threshold and contractions fade.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what changes the basic electrical rhythm?</h4>\n<p>Does ACh, GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) or CCK (cholecystokinin) change slow-wave frequency? Is the stomach faster than the duodenum? None of these: the rhythm is intrinsic, and the stomach (3/min) is slower than the duodenum (12/min). Likewise, NE placed near the duodenal ICC leaves the frequency unchanged but <b>weakens contractions</b>, because it removes spikes.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a8021a6aea.jpg\" alt=\"Resting slow waves stay below threshold. Stretch, ACh and parasympathetic input raise the potential and add spikes; NE and sympathetic input hyperpolarize it and silence the spikes. The rhythm itself never changes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Resting slow waves stay below threshold. Stretch, ACh and parasympathetic input raise the potential and add spikes; NE and sympathetic input hyperpolarize it and silence the spikes. The rhythm itself never changes. <small>· Discussion summary (wall, hormones, motility), slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: nifedipine and constipation</h4>\n<p>A patient started on <b>nifedipine</b>, a CCB (calcium channel blocker), for hypertension becomes constipated. Spikes and contraction depend on Ca²⁺ entry, so blocking calcium channels weakens gut contractions and slows transit.</p>","s":"Gut smooth muscle and its electrical rhythm","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Swallowing, peristalsis and opening the LES","p":"<p><b>Chewing</b>: voluntary plus a brainstem reflex; mixes food with saliva to lubricate it, shrinks particles for swallowing, and mixes starch with salivary amylase (Chapter 4).</p>\n<p>Swallowing starts voluntarily, then becomes a reflex run by the <b>swallowing center</b> in the medulla. Signals arrive via the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves; the center drives the striated pharynx and upper esophagus. The <b>oral phase</b> is voluntary: the tongue pushes the bolus back onto touch receptors in the pharynx, which triggers the reflex. The <b>pharyngeal phase</b> is reflex. The soft palate seals the nasopharynx. The epiglottis tips down over the rising larynx, so food cannot enter the trachea. The UES (upper esophageal sphincter) relaxes, a wave pushes the bolus through, and breathing pauses. In the <b>esophageal phase</b>, also reflex, the UES closes behind the bolus so it cannot reflux into the pharynx.</p>\n<h4>The esophagus</h4>\n<p>The bolus moves by <b>peristalsis</b>, the reflex called the \"law of the intestine.\" Stretch from the bolus excites enteric nerves on both sides of it. Behind the bolus, excitatory neurons release ACh and substance P: circular muscle contracts and longitudinal muscle relaxes. In front, inhibitory neurons release VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide): circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle shortens. Each contracting segment makes high pressure behind the bolus and pushes it on. Gravity helps when sitting or standing.</p>\n<p>The <b>primary peristaltic wave</b> follows a swallow. It is set off by a conscious swallow but runs as part of the swallowing reflex, through the vagus. If food remains, local stretch starts a <b>secondary peristaltic wave</b>. It is automatic, run by the ENS (enteric nervous system), and begins at the site of distension. So vagotomy (cutting the vagus; Chapter 1) abolishes primary peristalsis, while secondary waves and slow waves survive. A secondary wave needs no swallow, yet the LES relaxes ahead of it just as it does for a primary wave.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0e22bb39c3.png\" alt=\"After a swallow the upper sphincter (UES) dips open, a pressure peak passes down the esophagus, and the lower sphincter (LES) falls from 20 mm Hg to zero until the wave arrives.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,488px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">After a swallow the upper sphincter (UES) dips open, a pressure peak passes down the esophagus, and the lower sphincter (LES) falls from 20 mm Hg to zero until the wave arrives. <small>· Graph redrawn from Costanzo Fig. 8.9 (p. 351), schematic</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-13e03a0d34.jpg\" alt=\"The same pressure record in colour. Skeletal muscle (hatched) runs from the pharynx through the UES; smooth muscle runs below. Follow the blue traces: the UES dips, a peak travels down segment by segment, and the LES falls from 20 mm Hg until the wave arrives.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">The same pressure record in colour. Skeletal muscle (hatched) runs from the pharynx through the UES; smooth muscle runs below. Follow the blue traces: the UES dips, a peak travels down segment by segment, and the LES falls from 20 mm Hg until the wave arrives. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 76</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>LES</b> (lower esophageal sphincter) is closed at rest at about 20 mm Hg (millimeters of mercury, the unit of pressure). As the wave starts, vagal inhibitory pathways release <b>VIP and NO</b>, and LES pressure falls to zero. It stays open until the wave arrives, then returns to its resting tone. At the same moment the orad stomach relaxes (receptive relaxation, below). Both sphincters are needed because the esophagus sits in the chest. Its pressure equals intrathoracic pressure, below both atmospheric and abdominal pressure. The UES keeps air out at the top; the LES keeps acid out at the bottom.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the sphincter you cannot dissect</h4>\n<p>The structure hardest to find in a cadaver is the <b>LES</b>: a functional zone of tonic smooth muscle without a distinct thickening. The upper sphincter, pylorus, ileocecal valve and duodenojejunal junction are visible landmarks.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5189bb9df8.jpg\" alt=\"The esophagus (teal) runs down the back of the thorax and through the diaphragm to the stomach. No visible thickening marks the LES.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The esophagus (teal) runs down the back of the thorax and through the diaphragm to the stomach. No visible thickening marks the LES. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Swallowing and the esophagus","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"GERD, achalasia and esophageal spasm","p":"<h4>GERD: a sphincter too loose</h4>\n<p>In <b>GERD</b> (gastroesophageal reflux disease) LES tone is too low, so acid refluxes into the esophagus. Symptoms: epigastric pain; \"heartburn\" rising under the sternum; a bad taste in the mouth. <b>Factors that lower LES tone</b>: CCK, progesterone (pregnancy), nitrates, CCBs, high-fat meals, chocolate, nicotine (smoking), caffeine, alcohol, spicy food, and a large meal stretching the stomach. Alcohol blocks Ca²⁺ entry into esophageal smooth muscle and weakens its contraction. Pregnancy and obesity also raise abdominal pressure, which pushes gastric contents upward. Most accurate test: <b>24-hour pH monitoring</b>. Treatment: a PPI (proton pump inhibitor; Chapter 5) works in 90–95%. If PPIs fail, surgery tightens the closure. In a <b>Nissen fundoplication</b> the top of the stomach is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, and the diaphragmatic hiatus is tightened.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c764565f9f.jpg\" alt=\"Nissen fundoplication: the hiatus is tightened, and the top of the stomach (fundus) is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, so the wrap squeezes the sphincter zone shut.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Nissen fundoplication: the hiatus is tightened, and the top of the stomach (fundus) is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, so the wrap squeezes the sphincter zone shut. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: choking at night and burning chest pain</h4>\n<p>A 250-lb man has burning chest pain and chokes in his sleep, worse after large meals, alcohol, spicy food and coffee, better with acid blockers; heart and lungs are normal. The cause is <b>decreased LES tone</b>. An LES that fails to relax, or a tight UES, would block food, not let acid up.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e043b974ac.jpg\" alt=\"Esophageal manometry during a swallow, normal person and achalasia patient. Compare the timing of the pressure peaks down the esophagus, and what LES pressure does during the swallow (the text below explains the difference).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Esophageal manometry during a swallow, normal person and achalasia patient. Compare the timing of the pressure peaks down the esophagus, and what LES pressure does during the swallow (the text below explains the difference). <small>· Supplemental problem set 2, page 2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Achalasia: a sphincter that will not open</h4>\n<p><b>Achalasia</b> means \"failure to relax.\" The <b>inhibitory neurons</b> (VIP and NO) of the LES myenteric plexus are lost, and peristalsis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus is impaired. The LES has high resting pressure and does not relax when the person swallows. The esophageal body contracts weakly and all at once instead of in sequence. Food therefore piles up above the sphincter and the esophagus dilates. Patients have <b>dysphagia</b> (difficulty swallowing) for solids <i>and</i> liquids, regurgitation, a feeling of food stuck in the chest, and weight loss. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a narrow \"bird's beak\" at the LES. <b>Manometry</b> (pressure measurement) makes the diagnosis. Treatment opens the sphincter: balloon dilation, botulinum toxin (Botox) injection, or cutting the muscle surgically. None restores normal function.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-610e99390b.jpg\" alt=\"Achalasia on barium swallow: a dilated esophagus full of contrast tapers to a narrow &quot;bird&#x27;s beak&quot; at the LES.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Achalasia on barium swallow: a dilated esophagus full of contrast tapers to a narrow \"bird's beak\" at the LES. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: food that \"sticks\"</h4>\n<p>A 49-year-old cannot swallow solids or liquids, regurgitates, feels food stuck, and has lost 10 lb. Manometry shows an LES pressure that is high at rest and stays high during the swallow, plus simultaneous, non-sequential contractions. This is achalasia from selective loss of the inhibitory (VIP/NO) neurons to the LES; the dilation is due to the high LES tone. Balloon dilation of the LES relieves it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a2fba5a679.jpg\" alt=\"Balloon (pneumatic) dilation. A: a guidewire crosses the tight LES below a dilated esophagus. B and C: the balloon is inflated across the sphincter and stretches it open. D: food flows into the stomach again.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:722px\">Balloon (pneumatic) dilation. A: a guidewire crosses the tight LES below a dilated esophagus. B and C: the balloon is inflated across the sphincter and stretches it open. D: food flows into the stomach again. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: the finding that does not fit achalasia</h4>\n<p>A 35-year-old has dysphagia for solids and liquids, a dilated upper esophagus and a tapered lower end. Uncoordinated peristalsis, only small pressure rises with swallowing, and slow passage of food into the stomach all fit. <b>Increased parasympathetic stimulation at the LES</b> does not: the nerves that open the LES are parasympathetic inhibitory neurons, and achalasia is their loss.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>GERD</th><th>Achalasia</th><th>Esophageal spasm</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Defect</td><td>LES too loose</td><td>LES too tight, fails to relax; weak lower peristalsis</td><td>Sudden uncoordinated contractions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main symptom</td><td>Heartburn, bad taste</td><td>Dysphagia for solids and liquids</td><td>Chest pain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best test</td><td>24-h pH monitor</td><td>Manometry</td><td>Manometry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Treatment</td><td>PPI; fundoplication</td><td>Balloon, Botox, cut LES</td><td>CCB, nitroglycerin</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Esophageal spasm</h4>\n<p>Sudden, unprovoked contractions of the esophagus cause chest pain. Manometry shows the spasms. Smooth-muscle relaxants treat it: CCBs and nitroglycerin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d5f48bdff8.jpg\" alt=\"Esophageal spasm on barium swallow: uncoordinated contractions pinch the barium column into beaded, &quot;corkscrew&quot; segments.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Esophageal spasm on barium swallow: uncoordinated contractions pinch the barium column into beaded, \"corkscrew\" segments. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Swallowing and the esophagus","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Two regions and receptive relaxation","p":"<p>The stomach's extra inner <b>oblique</b> muscle layer (Chapter 1) adds churning, and its wall thickens from the fundus down to the antrum and pylorus. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses coordinate the churning. Its <b>rugae</b> (mucosal folds) flatten as it fills, giving an enormous potential space. For motility the stomach has two regions. The <b>orad</b> region (fundus and upper body) is thin and tonic; it receives and stores. The <b>caudad</b> region (lower body and antrum) is thick and phasic; it grinds, mixes and empties.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-81f50da153.jpg\" alt=\"The orad region (fundus and upper body) relaxes to store food; the caudad region (lower body and antrum) contracts to grind it and empty it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The orad region (fundus and upper body) relaxes to store food; the caudad region (lower body and antrum) contracts to grind it and empty it. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 352</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1b7a29af93.jpg\" alt=\"The stomach&#x27;s three muscle layers peeled back: longitudinal outside, then circular, then the oblique layer innermost. Rugae line the inside; the pyloric sphincter opens into the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">The stomach's three muscle layers peeled back: longitudinal outside, then circular, then the oblique layer innermost. Rugae line the inside; the pyloric sphincter opens into the duodenum. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-65b2479fc9.jpg\" alt=\"An opened stomach: the rugae are deep mucosal folds that flatten out as the stomach fills.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">An opened stomach: the rugae are deep mucosal folds that flatten out as the stomach fills. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Receptive relaxation</h4>\n<p>When a person swallows, the orad stomach relaxes together with the LES. This <b>receptive relaxation</b> lets the stomach go from about 50 mL to 1,500 mL with little rise in pressure. It is a <b>vago-vagal reflex</b> (Chapter 1). Mechanoreceptors sense stretch of the esophagus and stomach and signal the brainstem through vagal afferents. Vagal efferents return: preganglionic fibers release ACh onto enteric inhibitory neurons, which release <b>VIP and NO</b> onto the muscle. The fundus and upper body relax, so pressure stays low as volume rises. <b>Vagotomy</b> abolishes the reflex, so pressure climbs steeply as the stomach fills.</p>\n<p>The balance matters. A stomach that relaxed too much would never sense stretch, a main trigger for gastrin, so acid secretion would fall. One that could not relax would allow only tiny meals, and the person would starve. Receptive relaxation is what lets a competitive eater hold so much.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-613f70ffba.png\" alt=\"With receptive relaxation, pressure stays low until about 1.5 L. After vagotomy every extra milliliter raises pressure.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,468px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">With receptive relaxation, pressure stays low until about 1.5 L. After vagotomy every extra milliliter raises pressure. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 26 (50 mL to 1,500 mL), and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which motility event is vagal?</h4>\n<p>Of secondary esophageal peristalsis, the MMC (migrating myoelectric complex), slowing of emptying by duodenal fat, receptive relaxation and vomiting, only <b>receptive relaxation</b> is mediated by the vagus. Secondary peristalsis runs on the ENS, the MMC on motilin, the fat brake on CCK, and vomiting on the medullary vomiting center.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a8cc0041bc.png\" alt=\"The vago-vagal reflex: stretch goes up the vagus, and the vagus sends back a relaxing signal carried by VIP and NO.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,464px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The vago-vagal reflex: stretch goes up the vagus, and the vagus sends back a relaxing signal carried by VIP and NO. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: the hot-dog eating champion</h4>\n<p>The agent that best helps someone eat a huge meal is <b>ACh</b>, the parasympathetic transmitter driving the vago-vagal reflex of receptive relaxation. NE is the sympathetic \"stop digesting\" signal and tightens sphincters; atropine blocks ACh; propranolol (a β-blocker) plays no part; motilin drives fasting contractions.</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Retropulsion: grinding in the antrum","p":"<h4>Mixing, grinding and retropulsion</h4>\n<p>The caudad stomach contracts at the basic electrical rhythm, about <b>3 per minute</b>. Each wave starts mid-body and strengthens toward the pylorus. Each wave pushes about 2–3 mL into the duodenum. But it also closes the pylorus, so most of the chyme is squirted back into the stomach. This backward flow is <b>retropulsion</b>: it grinds and mixes and keeps emptying slow. Only particles of 1 mm or less pass. Vagal stimulation raises the <b>force</b> of these contractions, not their frequency. Parasympathetic input, gastrin and motilin increase spikes and force; sympathetic input, secretin and GIP decrease them.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f76d9f09ee.jpg\" alt=\"The regions in colour: fundus, body, and the antrum (orange) ending at the pylorus. The thick-walled antrum does the grinding; each wave closes the pylorus and throws chyme back.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The regions in colour: fundus, body, and the antrum (orange) ending at the pylorus. The thick-walled antrum does the grinding; each wave closes the pylorus and throws chyme back. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: after pyloric surgery</h4>\n<p>After surgery on the pyloric sphincter, the movement most affected while it heals is <b>retropulsion</b>, which needs the antral wave to close the pylorus. Receptive relaxation, segmentation and haustration do not involve the pylorus.</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Gastric emptying, gastroparesis and vagotomy","p":"<h4>Gastric emptying and what slows it</h4>\n<p>After a meal the stomach holds up to about 1.5 L. Emptying takes 30 minutes to 3 hours. Liquids empty before solids; carbohydrate and protein before fat; <b>fat is slowest</b>. Isotonic contents empty faster than hypotonic or hypertonic ones, and an isotonic liquid without calories is fastest of all. Acid in the stomach also slows emptying. The rate must stay slow enough for the duodenum to neutralize acid with pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) and to digest and absorb fat.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-49919b1804.png\" alt=\"Schematic: a calorie-free isotonic liquid leaves within about 30 minutes; solids wait to be ground first; fatty or hypertonic meals take about 3 hours.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,503px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Schematic: a calorie-free isotonic liquid leaves within about 30 minutes; solids wait to be ground first; fatty or hypertonic meals take about 3 hours. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 28 (30 min to 3 h), and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The brakes sit in the duodenum, which senses four things: <b>stretch, acid, fat and high osmolarity</b>. <b>Fat</b> (fatty acids and monoglycerides) releases CCK from I cells; CCK is the main hormone that slows emptying (Chapter 2). <b>Acid</b> (H⁺) is sensed by H⁺ receptors in the duodenal mucosa. A reflex through myenteric interneurons then slows the stomach until pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes the acid. Stretch and pH changes also start the <b>enterogastric reflex</b>, which briefly inhibits gastrin, weakens gastric contractions and tightens the pyloric sphincter. GIP and secretin also inhibit emptying. Gastrin is the classic hormone that speeds the stomach.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c16abd2234.png\" alt=\"Four duodenal signals converge on one result: weaker antral contractions and a tighter pylorus.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,498px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Four duodenal signals converge on one result: weaker antral contractions and a tighter pylorus. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>In the intestine, gastrin, CCK, motilin and serotonin <b>increase intestinal motility</b>; secretin and glucagon decrease small-intestinal motility. So CCK slows the stomach but speeds the intestine.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the fat-and-protein brake</h4>\n<p>The hormone released by fat and protein in the small intestine that strongly inhibits gastric emptying is <b>CCK</b>. GIP is the tempting choice: it also slows the stomach, but it answers glucose too, is the weaker brake, and its main job is insulin release. Gastrin speeds the stomach; CCK never does.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: Coke, brownie and pizza</h4>\n<p>The emptying order is <b>Coke, brownie, pizza</b>: liquid, then carbohydrate solid, then fatty solid held back by CCK.</p>\n<h4>Diabetic gastroparesis and vagotomy</h4>\n<p><b>Diabetic gastroparesis</b> is slow emptying from autonomic neuropathy. Damaged vagal afferents no longer sense distension, so the reflexes that relax and drive the stomach fail. Symptoms: bloating, nausea, pain, constipation, sometimes alternating with diarrhea. <b>Vagotomy</b> has similar effects. Without receptive relaxation, intragastric pressure rises, which can push liquids out too fast (<b>dumping syndrome</b>), while weak antral grinding delays emptying of solids. Other effects: diarrhea, and a sluggish gallbladder with cholestasis and gallstones.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: treating diabetic gastroparesis</h4>\n<p>The best drug is <b>erythromycin</b>, an antibiotic that binds motilin receptors and so triggers strong, MMC-like contractions that speed emptying. Every distractor slows the gut: NE (sympathetic), metoprolol (β-blocker), tricyclic antidepressants (anticholinergic) and diltiazem (CCB).</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Segmentation and peristalsis","p":"<p>Parasympathetic (vagal) input stimulates small-intestinal contraction; sympathetic input inhibits it. Slow waves run at 12/min in the duodenum and fall to 8/min in the ileum, so segmentation occurs at most 8–12 times per minute. The ENS coordinates two patterns.</p>\n<p><b>Segmentation</b> mixes. A ring of <b>circular muscle</b> contracts, splits the chyme and sends it both up and down; the ring relaxes and the pieces merge again. This exposes chyme to enzymes and mucosa but moves it nowhere. <b>Peristalsis</b> propels, with the same reciprocal pattern as in the esophagus. It moves chyme one way, toward the colon. It starts when the bolus makes enterochromaffin cells release 5-HT (serotonin), which activates sensory enteric neurons. Shorthand: circular muscle mixes, longitudinal muscle propels; in fact peristalsis needs both layers.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e056e4c51f.jpg\" alt=\"Peristalsis (left): contraction behind and relaxation ahead push the bolus one way. Segmentation (right): rings of contraction chop and mix the chyme back and forth without moving it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Peristalsis (left): contraction behind and relaxation ahead push the bolus one way. Segmentation (right): rings of contraction chop and mix the chyme back and forth without moving it. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 31</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-766a76a4c2.jpg\" alt=\"A, segmentation: a ring contracts in the middle of the chyme, splits it up and down, then relaxes so the halves merge again; nothing moves on. B, peristalsis: the segment behind (orad) contracts while the segment ahead (caudad) relaxes, so the bolus moves down.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A, segmentation: a ring contracts in the middle of the chyme, splits it up and down, then relaxes so the halves merge again; nothing moves on. B, peristalsis: the segment behind (orad) contracts while the segment ahead (caudad) relaxes, so the bolus moves down. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.11</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The small intestine","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"The MMC and other intestinal reflexes","p":"<h4>The MMC and motilin</h4>\n<p>Between meals the gut runs a <b>housekeeping</b> program, the <b>MMC</b>. It begins about 90–120 minutes after eating and repeats about every 90 minutes while fasting. Each cycle is a band of intense contractions traveling from stomach to terminal ileum, mostly in the small bowel. It sweeps undigested residue and bacteria into the colon. The MMC is driven by <b>motilin</b>, released cyclically during fasting from Mo cells of the upper duodenum; each motilin peak sets off a sweep (motilin as a hormone: Chapter 2). Eating stops the MMC and restores the fed pattern of segmentation and peristalsis. Without MMCs, residue and bacteria stay behind, which favors bacterial overgrowth in the small intestine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-60eabea998.png\" alt=\"Schematic: after the meal, mixing contractions continue for 1.5–2 hours. Then the gut falls quiet except for intense sweeps about every 90 minutes, each preceded by a rise in motilin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,478px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Schematic: after the meal, mixing contractions continue for 1.5–2 hours. Then the gut falls quiet except for intense sweeps about every 90 minutes, each preceded by a rise in motilin. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 32, and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: contractions every 90 minutes between meals</h4>\n<p>Periodic contractions every 90 minutes during fasting are MMCs, mediated by <b>motilin</b>. Mass movements are the trap: colon, after meals, 1–3 times a day. Gastrin belongs to the fed state.</p>\n<p><b>Other intestinal reflexes</b>. Gastroileal: food in the stomach and gastric peristalsis → ileal peristalsis and the ileocecal valve opens. Intestino-intestinal: stretch of one segment → ↓ contraction of the intestine below it. Duodenocolic: duodenal stretch → myenteric plexus → faster colonic propulsion.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Muscle or driver</th><th>Where, when</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Circular only</td><td>Small intestine, fed, ≤ 8–12/min</td><td>Mix</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Peristalsis</td><td>Circular + longitudinal, reciprocal</td><td>Esophagus, small intestine</td><td>Propel one way</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retropulsion</td><td>Antral wave closes pylorus</td><td>Caudad stomach, 3/min</td><td>Grind; slow emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MMC</td><td>Motilin</td><td>Stomach → ileum, fasting, every 90 min</td><td>Sweep residue, bacteria</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Haustration</td><td>Circular (segmentation)</td><td>Colon, about every 30 min</td><td>Mix; absorb water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mass movement</td><td>Strong peristalsis</td><td>Transverse → sigmoid colon, 1–3/day</td><td>Move feces far</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"The small intestine","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"The vomiting reflex and retching","p":"<p><b>Vomiting</b> does not come from the stomach contracting. It is a reflex run by the <b>vomiting center</b> in the medulla, fed by the vestibular system, the back of the throat, the GI tract and the CTZ (chemoreceptor trigger zone) in the fourth ventricle. First, slow waves in the stomach and small intestine stop. Reverse peristalsis then carries contents from the small intestine up into the stomach, and the stomach and pylorus relax. A forced inspiration and contraction of the abdominal muscles raise abdominal pressure. The larynx moves up, the LES relaxes and the glottis closes. The contents are forcefully expelled, squeezed by the abdominal muscles, not the stomach. In <b>retching</b> the UES stays closed, so contents fall back into the stomach.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-610997e36e.png\" alt=\"The vomiting sequence, in order. The stomach stays relaxed throughout; abdominal pressure does the work.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The vomiting sequence, in order. The stomach stays relaxed throughout; abdominal pressure does the work. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Vomiting","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Colon movements, the gastrocolic reflex and defecation","p":"<p>Chyme enters the colon as a thin soup, as watery as urine; the colon absorbs water, so its contents become semisolid. Once chyme passes, the <b>ileocecal sphincter</b> contracts to stop reflux. <b>Haustration</b> is the colon's segmentation: circular contractions form saclike pouches, the <b>haustra</b>, which mix contents against the mucosa. In the cecum and proximal colon they occur about every 30 minutes and last about 1 minute each. <b>Mass movements</b> are strong peristaltic waves over 20 cm or more of the transverse and descending colon, 1–3 times a day. Triggers: stretch or irritation of the colon, local enteric reflexes, intense parasympathetic stimulation, and meals. A final mass movement fills the rectum, and that sudden stretch starts defecation.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5b26f37b2a.jpg\" alt=\"The colon&#x27;s outpouchings are the haustra. Mass movements sweep contents from the transverse colon through the descending and sigmoid colon toward the rectum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The colon's outpouchings are the haustra. Mass movements sweep contents from the transverse colon through the descending and sigmoid colon toward the rectum. <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>gastrocolic reflex</b> (\"eat, then defecate\"): stretch of the stomach by a meal increases colonic motility and mass movements. Its afferent limb is parasympathetic; its efferent limb is carried by CCK and gastrin.</p>\n<p><b>Defecation</b>. As the rectum fills, the <b>rectosphincteric reflex</b> contracts the rectal wall and briefly relaxes the internal anal sphincter (smooth, involuntary). The external anal sphincter (striated, voluntary) stays closed. At about 25% of rectal capacity the urge appears. When appropriate, the external sphincter is relaxed voluntarily and the rectum contracts. A <b>Valsalva maneuver</b> (breathing out against a closed glottis) raises abdominal pressure to help.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d90c168de2.png\" alt=\"From meal to defecation: the gastrocolic reflex brings a mass movement, rectal stretch relaxes the involuntary sphincter, and the voluntary sphincter has the last word.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">From meal to defecation: the gastrocolic reflex brings a mass movement, rectal stretch relaxes the involuntary sphincter, and the voluntary sphincter has the last word. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>IBS</b> (irritable bowel syndrome) is altered intestinal contraction. Contractions that are too strong and long cause gas, bloating and diarrhea; weak ones slow passage and give hard, dry stools. Other motility disorders: esophageal spasm, delayed gastric emptying, diarrhea.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: urge to defecate 20 minutes after a meal</h4>\n<p>Stomach stretch after a meal triggers the <b>gastrocolic reflex</b>, which increases mass movements. The rectosphincteric reflex is the trap: it relaxes the internal sphincter in response to rectal, not gastric, stretch.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the commonest movement in the descending colon</h4>\n<p>The most frequent colonic movement is <b>haustration</b>, slow segmentation about every 30 minutes. Mass movements are stronger but occur only 1–3 times a day; retropulsion belongs to the stomach.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Slow-wave frequency is fixed by the ICC; nerves, hormones and drugs change only spikes and force. Primary peristalsis needs the vagus; secondary peristalsis runs on the ENS. Receptive relaxation is vagal (VIP/NO) and happens while eating; the MMC is driven by motilin and happens only while fasting. GERD is a loose LES; achalasia is a tight LES that never relaxes. CCK slows the stomach but speeds the intestine.</p>","s":"The large intestine and defecation","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Glands, acini and ducts","p":"<p>Saliva is the first digestive secretion and the only one run purely by nerves. This chapter covers how it is made, why its make-up changes with flow rate, its control and its jobs.</p>\n<p><b>Volume</b>: 1–2 L/day, a huge output for such small glands; about as much as the daily urine, and 1–2 of the roughly 9 L of fluid that enter the gut each day (Chapter 9). Secretion goes on all day, whether or not a person eats.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: aspiration with nothing by mouth</h4>\n<p>A stroke patient has lost his gag reflex. Feeding is stopped (NPO, nothing by mouth), yet he keeps aspirating and gets pneumonia. The deciding fact is that stopping food does not stop <b>saliva formation</b>, which continues at 1–2 L/day and runs into the unprotected airway. Regurgitated stomach contents are the tempting answer, but saliva never stops.</p>\n<p><b>Submandibular glands</b>: floor of the mouth; 65–70% of saliva; mixed (serous + mucous); Wharton's duct. <b>Parotid glands</b>: in front of the ears; 25%; purely <b>serous</b>; Stensen's duct. <b>Sublingual glands</b>: under the tongue; 5%; mixed; many small ducts of Rivinus. Serous cells secrete a watery fluid of water, ions and enzymes. Mucous cells secrete <b>mucin</b> glycoproteins, which make saliva slippery.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-537892b3b5.jpg\" alt=\"The three paired salivary glands. The parotid, in front of the ear, is purely serous; the submandibular and sublingual glands lie under the jaw and tongue and are mixed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The three paired salivary glands. The parotid, in front of the ear, is purely serous; the submandibular and sublingual glands lie under the jaw and tongue and are mixed. <small>· Saliva lecture, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8de0f82a63.jpg\" alt=\"Each gland&#x27;s share of the daily saliva, with its duct: parotid 25% (Stensen&#x27;s duct), submandibular 65–70% (Wharton&#x27;s duct) and sublingual 5% (the small ducts of Rivinus).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each gland's share of the daily saliva, with its duct: parotid 25% (Stensen's duct), submandibular 65–70% (Wharton's duct) and sublingual 5% (the small ducts of Rivinus). <small>· Saliva lecture, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Acinus makes, duct modifies</h4>\n<p>Each gland looks like a bunch of grapes. Each grape is an acinus, the blind starting end of a branching duct. Acinar cells make the <b>primary saliva</b>: water, ions, enzymes and mucus, with the same ion make-up and strength as plasma. This fluid flows through a short intercalated duct and then a <b>striated duct</b>. The cells lining the striated duct are the <b>ductal cells</b> that modify saliva. Their \"striations\" are deep folds of the basal membrane packed with mitochondria, which supply ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency) for ion pumping. <b>Myoepithelial cells</b> wrap the acini and intercalated ducts; nerve signals make them contract and squeeze saliva out.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c7035a58fd.jpg\" alt=\"A purely serous acinus (dark serous cells) and a mixed acinus that also has mucous cells (lighter). Both drain into ducts lined by ductal cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,376px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A purely serous acinus (dark serous cells) and a mixed acinus that also has mucous cells (lighter). Both drain into ducts lined by ductal cells. <small>· Saliva lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4385989c99.jpg\" alt=\"One secretory unit, from its blind end (acinus, right) through the intercalated duct to the striated duct (left). The enlargements show the striations: deep folds of the basal membrane packed with mitochondria, which power ion pumping. The blue arrows mark where saliva is made (acinus) and where it is modified (striated duct).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">One secretory unit, from its blind end (acinus, right) through the intercalated duct to the striated duct (left). The enlargements show the striations: deep folds of the basal membrane packed with mitochondria, which power ion pumping. The blue arrows mark where saliva is made (acinus) and where it is modified (striated duct). <small>· Saliva lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ed5923a491.jpg\" alt=\"A stained salivary gland. Myoepithelial cells are the thin, flat cells hugging each acinus; serous demilunes are caps of serous cells on the mucous acini of a mixed gland.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A stained salivary gland. Myoepithelial cells are the thin, flat cells hugging each acinus; serous demilunes are caps of serous cells on the mucous acini of a mixed gland. <small>· Saliva lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"How saliva is made","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Two-step formation: why saliva ends up hypotonic","p":"<p>Saliva is <b>hypotonic</b>: its osmolarity (dissolved particles per liter) is lower than plasma's. Compared with plasma it has less Na⁺ (sodium) and Cl⁻ (chloride) and more K⁺ (potassium) and HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate). So it is not filtered plasma; it is made in two steps.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1a1a9a198a.jpg\" alt=\"Step 1: acinar cells secrete an isotonic fluid. Step 2: down the duct, ductal cells take out Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and add K⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so hypotonic saliva leaves. The small box marks the cell enlarged in the next figure.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,340px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Step 1: acinar cells secrete an isotonic fluid. Step 2: down the duct, ductal cells take out Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and add K⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so hypotonic saliva leaves. The small box marks the cell enlarged in the next figure. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Step 1, acinus</b>: acinar cells secrete an <b>isotonic</b> fluid, the same strength as plasma (and as normal saline). Its Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ concentrations are close to plasma values.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2, ducts</b>: ductal cells change the ions as the fluid passes. Their luminal membrane carries three exchangers: Na⁺–H⁺, Cl⁻–HCO₃⁻ and H⁺–K⁺. Their blood-side membrane has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase (the pump that moves Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ in) and Cl⁻ channels. Working together, these transporters <b>reabsorb Na⁺ and Cl⁻</b> and <b>secrete K⁺ and HCO₃⁻</b>. More NaCl leaves the saliva than KHCO₃ enters it, so the duct removes solute overall. Water would normally follow solute out, but the ducts are <b>impermeable to water</b>. The water stays behind, so the final saliva is hypotonic.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4627abee64.jpg\" alt=\"One ductal cell. Luminal exchangers swap Na⁺ for H⁺, Cl⁻ for HCO₃⁻ and H⁺ for K⁺; the blood side has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase and a Cl⁻ channel. Net result: NaCl leaves the saliva, KHCO₃ enters, and no water moves.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One ductal cell. Luminal exchangers swap Na⁺ for H⁺, Cl⁻ for HCO₃⁻ and H⁺ for K⁺; the blood side has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase and a Cl⁻ channel. Net result: NaCl leaves the saliva, KHCO₃ enters, and no water moves. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 357</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>mEq/L (milliequivalents per liter) measures how much ionic charge a fluid carries per liter. Plasma Na⁺ is about 140 mEq/L and Cl⁻ about 110; resting saliva has only about 15 of each. Resting salivary pH is about 6–7.</p>\n<p>Being dilute has a purpose: flavor molecules leave the food, dissolve in saliva and reach the taste buds.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why we can taste food</h4>\n<p>What mainly lets us taste food? <b>Low salivary osmolality</b>, for the reason just given. Saliva's Na⁺ is low, not high; its high K⁺ and its HCO₃⁻ are real but release no flavor; and it has no proteases.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what ductal cells do</h4>\n<p>Ductal cells produce saliva whose <b>K⁺</b> is the highest relative to plasma of any ion. Contracting to eject saliva is the myoepithelial cells' job; making the isotonic first fluid is the acinar cells' job; no cell adds proteolytic enzymes.</p>","s":"How saliva is made","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Why composition changes with flow rate","p":"<p>The key idea is <b>contact time</b>. Ductal reabsorption of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ needs time. When saliva flows slowly, each bit of fluid stays in the duct longer, so the ductal cells change it more. When it flows fast, the ductal cells have little time to change it. Flow rate is given in mL/min (milliliters per minute).</p>\n<p><b>Low flow</b> (under about 1 mL/min, at rest): long contact. The ducts remove most of the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and add the most K⁺. Saliva is at its most hypotonic and least like plasma.</p>\n<p><b>High flow</b> (about 4 mL/min, stimulated): short contact. Less Na⁺ and Cl⁻ is removed, so their concentrations climb toward plasma values, and K⁺ falls. Saliva now most resembles the primary fluid and plasma. It is still hypotonic, because the ducts always remove some salt.</p>\n<p><b>HCO₃⁻ breaks the rule.</b> Contact time predicts that a secreted ion should be highest at low flow. Instead, HCO₃⁻ is lowest at low flow and highest at high flow. The reason is that the stimulus that raises flow, mainly parasympathetic nerves, also selectively switches on ductal HCO₃⁻ secretion. So as flow rises, HCO₃⁻ rises too, to well above plasma.</p>\n<p>The short version: at low flow, Na⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ are all low and K⁺ is high; at high flow, Na⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ are all high and K⁺ is low.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7ba308beac.png\" alt=\"Contact time explains Na⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺. HCO₃⁻ rises with flow only because stimulation switches on its secretion.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Contact time explains Na⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺. HCO₃⁻ rises with flow only because stimulation switches on its secretion. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How to read the flow-rate graph</h4>\n<p>The x-axis is flow rate; the y-axis is concentration in saliva. Identify each curve by its shape. The only curve that <b>falls</b> is K⁺, and it stays above plasma K⁺ (4 mEq/L) at every flow. The tallest rising curve is Na⁺: it climbs steeply, then levels off, but never reaches plasma Na⁺ (140). The curve that rises fast and then goes flat is HCO₃⁻; beyond the lowest flows it sits above plasma HCO₃⁻ (24). The slow, nearly straight climb is Cl⁻, which stays far below plasma Cl⁻ (110). Because Na⁺ and Cl⁻ never reach plasma levels, total osmolarity stays below plasma too: saliva is <b>hypotonic at every flow rate</b>, least so at high flow.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7b792999c2.png\" alt=\"Saliva ion concentrations against flow rate, with plasma levels at right. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ stay below plasma and K⁺ stays above it at every flow; HCO₃⁻ climbs above plasma as flow rises.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,534px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Saliva ion concentrations against flow rate, with plasma levels at right. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ stay below plasma and K⁺ stays above it at every flow; HCO₃⁻ climbs above plasma as flow rises. <small>· Graph redrawn from Saliva lecture, slide 17, with values from Costanzo Fig. 8.13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0210b60118.jpg\" alt=\"Costanzo&#x27;s original, in colour, with plasma levels as bars at right: compare each curve with its bar. Na⁺ (purple) and Cl⁻ (green) never reach their plasma bars, K⁺ (blue) stays above its small bar, and HCO₃⁻ (orange) climbs well above its bar as flow rises.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Costanzo's original, in colour, with plasma levels as bars at right: compare each curve with its bar. Na⁺ (purple) and Cl⁻ (green) never reach their plasma bars, K⁺ (blue) stays above its small bar, and HCO₃⁻ (orange) climbs well above its bar as flow rises. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: parasympathetic stimulation</h4>\n<p>Which goes up most when the parasympathetic nerves to a salivary gland fire? <b>HCO₃⁻</b>, because stimulation selectively turns on its secretion. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ rise only passively (less time to reabsorb them), and K⁺ falls. These nerves run in cranial nerves VII and IX; the vagus does not supply the salivary glands.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Ductal cells</th><th>Saliva vs plasma</th><th>As flow rises</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Na⁺</td><td>reabsorb</td><td>lower (rest ≈ 15 vs 140)</td><td>↑ toward plasma</td><td>less contact time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>reabsorb</td><td>lower (rest ≈ 15 vs 110)</td><td>↑ toward plasma</td><td>less contact time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>K⁺</td><td>secrete</td><td>higher at every flow</td><td>↓, stays above plasma</td><td>less contact time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HCO₃⁻</td><td>secrete</td><td>higher, except lowest flow</td><td>↑</td><td>stimulation turns on secretion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Osmolarity</td><td>solute out, no water</td><td>always lower</td><td>↑ toward plasma</td><td>less salt removed</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"How saliva is made","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Nerves only, and both divisions stimulate","p":"<p>Saliva is the only GI (gastrointestinal) secretion under purely <b>neural</b> control: no hormone sets its rate. It is also the only GI secretion that <b>both</b> autonomic divisions increase, although the parasympathetic division dominates.</p>\n<p><b>Parasympathetic</b> (main control): CN (cranial nerve) VII, facial, and CN IX, glossopharyngeal → ACh (acetylcholine) → <b>muscarinic</b> receptors on acinar and ductal cells → IP₃ (inositol trisphosphate) → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ (calcium) → a large volume of watery saliva, more HCO₃⁻ and enzymes, and myoepithelial contraction. ↑ by food, smell, taste, nausea and conditioning (Pavlov's dog); <b>citric acid</b> is the strongest stimulant. ↓ by sleep, dehydration and fear. <b>Atropine</b> blocks the muscarinic receptor and cuts the high-volume secretion.</p>\n<p><b>Sympathetic</b>: T1–T3 spinal segments → superior cervical ganglion → postganglionic fibers release NE (norepinephrine) → <b>β receptors</b> → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → a small volume of thick, mucus-rich saliva. This is why the mouth goes dry when you are nervous: fear turns down the parasympathetic flow, and the sympathetic adds only a little thick saliva.</p>\n<p><b>Aldosterone</b> changes composition, not rate. It acts on the ducts as on the kidney: more Na⁺ reabsorbed and more K⁺ secreted, so salivary Na⁺ falls further. Because the ducts are water-impermeable, little water follows the extra Na⁺.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3384f45b0a.png\" alt=\"Both divisions increase secretion: the parasympathetic gives a large watery volume, the sympathetic a small mucus-rich one. Atropine blocks the parasympathetic arm at the muscarinic receptor.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,529px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Both divisions increase secretion: the parasympathetic gives a large watery volume, the sympathetic a small mucus-rich one. Atropine blocks the parasympathetic arm at the muscarinic receptor. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e82c65cad2.jpg\" alt=\"Costanzo&#x27;s version, colour-coded by arm. Pink, parasympathetic: CN VII and IX, ACh on muscarinic (M) receptors, IP₃ and Ca²⁺; atropine blocks it; food, smell and nausea turn it up, dehydration, fear and sleep turn it down. Blue, sympathetic: T1–T3, NE on β receptors, cAMP. Both arms end in more saliva.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Costanzo's version, colour-coded by arm. Pink, parasympathetic: CN VII and IX, ACh on muscarinic (M) receptors, IP₃ and Ca²⁺; atropine blocks it; food, smell and nausea turn it up, dehydration, fear and sleep turn it down. Blue, sympathetic: T1–T3, NE on β receptors, cAMP. Both arms end in more saliva. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which nerves</h4>\n<p>Salivary secretion rate rises with parasympathetic input through <b>CN VII and IX</b>. CN X is wrong; so is \"decreased by both divisions\" (both increase it) and \"sympathetic stronger than parasympathetic\" (the parasympathetic dominates).</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what lowers saliva</h4>\n<p>Of ACh, atropine, bradykinin, citric acid and facial-nerve stimulation, only <b>atropine</b> decreases salivary secretion, because it blocks ACh at the muscarinic receptor. Sleep and dehydration also lower it. Citric acid, ACh and CN VII stimulation raise it, and bradykinin raises gland blood flow.</p>","s":"Control of secretion","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Blood flow and what saliva carries","p":"<h4>Blood flow</h4>\n<p>Salivary glands have an unusually high blood flow: at its peak, about <b>10 times</b> that of exercising skeletal muscle per gram of tissue. This supplies the water for 1–2 L/day. Active acinar cells release <b>kallikrein</b>, an enzyme that splits the plasma protein kininogen into <b>bradykinin</b>. Bradykinin dilates the gland's blood vessels.</p>\n<h4>What saliva does and what it carries</h4>\n<p>Saliva lubricates food with mucin for chewing and swallowing, and dilutes it. It dissolves taste molecules: no liquid, no taste. It washes food off the teeth. Its HCO₃⁻ buffers the acid that mouth bacteria make, which would otherwise dissolve Ca²⁺ out of tooth enamel. No saliva means no teeth.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: mouth breather with cavities</h4>\n<p>A nasal bone spur makes a man breathe through his mouth at night, and his mouth dries out. He gets more dental caries (cavities) mainly from loss of the <b>mechanical removal of food</b> from his teeth. Increased acid production, more K⁺ secretion and less lingual lipase are not the reason.</p>\n<p><b>Enzymes</b>: <b>α-amylase</b> starts breaking starch and glycogen into maltose and dextrins (Chapter 7); not needed for life (pancreatic amylase suffices), but it is why bread tastes good; inactivated by gastric acid. <b>Lingual lipase</b> splits all three fatty acids off a triglyceride; not needed for life, but it is why we love fatty food; unlike amylase, it keeps working in the stomach (Chapter 8). <b>No protease</b>: protein is not broken down in the mouth, which is why plain tofu tastes of almost nothing. Saliva also carries <b>R protein</b>, which binds vitamin B₁₂ (Chapter 9).</p>\n<p><b>Immune contents</b>: IgA (immunoglobulin A) keeps bacteria from attacking the lining. <b>Lysozyme</b> (muramidase) breaks down the muramic acid of bacterial cell walls and dissolves staphylococci. <b>Lactoferrin</b> binds iron before bacteria can use it. Lactoperoxidase also kills microbes. Together they destroy bacteria and viruses before they can enter. Hypotonic saliva also kills HIV-infected (human immunodeficiency virus) mononuclear white cells, which swell and burst in the dilute fluid, so kissing does not spread HIV.</p>","s":"What saliva does","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Dry mouth: Sjögren's syndrome and drugs","p":"<p><b>Sjögren's syndrome</b> is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies attack the salivary and lacrimal (tear) glands. The result is dry mouth and dry eyes, tooth loss from caries, and a higher risk of lymphoma. Treatment replaces tears and saliva with artificial ones. <b>Cevimeline</b>, a muscarinic agonist, acts like ACh on the glands and raises saliva output. <b>Atropine</b> and other anticholinergic drugs cause the same dry mouth (<b>xerostomia</b>) by blocking muscarinic receptors.</p>\n<p>Many over-the-counter allergy and sleep pills, the first-generation antihistamines such as diphenhydramine, also block muscarinic receptors and dry the mouth the same way.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Saliva is hypotonic at every flow rate and approaches plasma as flow rises; pancreatic juice is isotonic at all flows (Chapter 6). In saliva, Na⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ all rise with flow while K⁺ falls; in pancreatic juice HCO₃⁻ rises as Cl⁻ falls. Salivary nerves are CN VII and IX, not the vagus. Sympathetic stimulation still increases saliva, just a small, thick volume.</p>","s":"What saliva does","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Gastric juice and the cells that make it","p":"<p>The stomach secretes acid, an enzyme precursor, intrinsic factor and mucus. This chapter follows how the parietal cell makes acid, how acid is switched on and off, and what happens when acid wins: peptic ulcers and Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.</p>\n<p>Gastric juice has four main parts: <b>HCl</b> (hydrochloric acid), <b>pepsinogen</b>, <b>intrinsic factor</b> and <b>mucus</b>. HCl and pepsinogen start protein digestion. Intrinsic factor is needed to absorb vitamin B₁₂. Mucus protects the lining from acid and lubricates the food.</p>\n<p><b>Body</b> (oxyntic glands): parietal (oxyntic) cells → HCl + intrinsic factor; chief (peptic) cells → pepsinogen (+ gastric lipase, Chapter 8); mucous neck cells → mucus, HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate), some pepsinogen; ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells → histamine. <b>Antrum</b> (pyloric glands, deeper pits): G cells → <b>gastrin, released into the blood</b>, not into the gland; mucous cells → mucus, HCO₃⁻. D cells → somatostatin. In a gland, mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells sit in the middle and chief cells lie near the base.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse ECL cells with <b>enterochromaffin (EC) cells</b>: enteroendocrine cells scattered along the gut lining that make <b>serotonin</b>, which acts on enteric neurons to adjust motility and secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7e075f2d35.jpg\" alt=\"One gastric gland. Mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells (purple) sit in the middle and chief cells crowd the base; a G cell and an ECL cell are drawn beside it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One gastric gland. Mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells (purple) sit in the middle and chief cells crowd the base; a G cell and an ECL cell are drawn beside it. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Parietal cells are packed with mitochondria, which supply ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency) for the acid pump. Their membrane folds into deep channels called canaliculi, where acid is poured into the gland.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-91873ace49.jpg\" alt=\"A parietal cell. Its apical membrane folds into deep canaliculi that pour acid into the gland, and its cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria to power the pump.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A parietal cell. Its apical membrane folds into deep canaliculi that pour acid into the gland, and its cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria to power the pump. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: matching cells to secretions</h4>\n<p>Only \"S cells → secretin\" is correct (duodenum; Chapter 2). Each wrong pair swaps a product: trypsinogen comes from pancreatic acinar cells, not mucous cells; intrinsic factor from parietal cells, not chief cells; gastrin from G cells; pepsinogen from chief cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bedb2cc440.jpg\" alt=\"Parietal and chief cells sit in the body; G cells and mucous cells sit in the antrum. Note that gastrin leaves the G cell into the circulation, not into the lumen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Parietal and chief cells sit in the body; G cells and mucous cells sit in the antrum. Note that gastrin leaves the G cell into the circulation, not into the lumen. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.15</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Gastric juice and acid","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"How the parietal cell makes HCl","p":"<p>Parietal cells acidify the gastric contents to <b>pH 1–2</b> (pH falls as H⁺, hydrogen ion, concentration rises). The job of this acid is to turn inactive pepsinogen into active pepsin. The cell does it in four steps.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1, inside the cell.</b> CO₂ (carbon dioxide) from the cell's metabolism and from blood joins water to form carbonic acid. The enzyme <b>carbonic anhydrase</b> speeds this up. Carbonic acid then splits into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻. In symbols: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2, apical (lumen) side.</b> The <b>H⁺/K⁺ ATPase</b>, or proton pump, splits ATP to push H⁺ into the lumen and pull K⁺ (potassium) into the cell. Both ions move against their gradients, so this is primary active transport. K⁺ leaks back to the lumen through K⁺ channels and is reused. Cl⁻ (chloride) follows H⁺ out through apical Cl⁻ channels, so the product is HCl.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3, basolateral (blood) side.</b> Every H⁺ pumped out leaves one HCO₃⁻ behind. A <b>Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger</b> (AE2, anion exchanger 2) moves that HCO₃⁻ into the blood and brings Cl⁻ in. This Cl⁻ is the supply for the apical Cl⁻ channel. A Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase (sodium–potassium pump) on the same side keeps cell Na⁺ low.</p>\n<p><b>Step 4, the net result.</b> HCl goes to the lumen and HCO₃⁻ goes to the blood. After a meal, gastric venous blood therefore turns alkaline: the <b>alkaline tide</b>. The pancreas later secretes this HCO₃⁻ into the duodenum to neutralize the same acid, and pancreatic venous blood turns acidic while it does (Chapter 6).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e8d10889f7.jpg\" alt=\"Follow one CO₂: carbonic anhydrase splits it into H⁺, pumped into the lumen in exchange for K⁺, and HCO₃⁻, swapped into the blood for Cl⁻. The Cl⁻ then leaves through the apical channel to pair with H⁺.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Follow one CO₂: carbonic anhydrase splits it into H⁺, pumped into the lumen in exchange for K⁺, and HCO₃⁻, swapped into the blood for Cl⁻. The Cl⁻ then leaves through the apical channel to pair with H⁺. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which blood has the highest pH?</h4>\n<p>Choices: cerebral artery, gastric vein, pancreatic vein, portal vein, renal artery. The <b>gastric vein</b> wins because it carries the alkaline tide. The pancreatic veins have the lowest pH, because duct cells send H⁺ into the blood as they secrete HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cd38413b62.jpg\" alt=\"The same steps in a simpler colour drawing: carbonic anhydrase inside the cell, the H⁺/K⁺ pump (blue) on the lumen side, and the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (pink) sending HCO₃⁻ toward the capillary. The label marks the pump that proton pump inhibitors block.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The same steps in a simpler colour drawing: carbonic anhydrase inside the cell, the H⁺/K⁺ pump (blue) on the lumen side, and the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (pink) sending HCO₃⁻ toward the capillary. The label marks the pump that proton pump inhibitors block. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Gastric juice and acid","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Three stimulants, one pump","p":"<p>Three messengers turn the parietal cell on. Each uses its own receptor, but all end at the same H⁺/K⁺ ATPase.</p>\n<p><b>ACh</b> (acetylcholine): neurocrine from vagal endings → <b>M₃</b> (muscarinic) receptor → Gq protein (a G protein: the membrane switch linking receptor to enzyme) → phospholipase C → IP₃ (inositol trisphosphate) + DAG (diacylglycerol) → Ca²⁺ (calcium) released from stores → protein kinases → H⁺ secretion. Blocked by <b>atropine</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Gastrin</b>: hormone from antral G cells; enters the blood and returns to the stomach → <b>CCKB</b> receptor → Gq → <b>IP₃/Ca²⁺</b>. The CCKB receptor binds gastrin and CCK (cholecystokinin) equally; the CCKA receptor is CCK-specific.</p>\n<p><b>Histamine</b>: paracrine from ECL cells; diffuses to nearby parietal cells → <b>H₂</b> receptor → Gs protein → adenylyl cyclase → <b>cAMP</b> (cyclic AMP) → protein kinase A. Blocked by H₂ blockers such as cimetidine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9df81cd7af.jpg\" alt=\"Two second-messenger paths inside one cell: histamine (purple H₂ receptor) raises cAMP, while gastrin and ACh raise Ca²⁺. Both paths end at the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, where PPIs act; atropine blocks only the ACh receptor.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Two second-messenger paths inside one cell: histamine (purple H₂ receptor) raises cAMP, while gastrin and ACh raise Ca²⁺. Both paths end at the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, where PPIs act; atropine blocks only the ACh receptor. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Switching acid on and off","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Indirect routes and the brakes","p":"<p>ACh and gastrin also act indirectly: both make ECL cells release histamine. A second nerve peptide, PACAP (pituitary adenylate cyclase–activating peptide), also stimulates ECL cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-229a270530.jpg\" alt=\"The three stimulants and their receptors: ACh on M₃, histamine on H₂, gastrin on CCKB. ACh, PACAP and gastrin also drive the ECL cell; somatostatin (Sst) from the D cell brakes the parietal cell, gastrin release and the ECL cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The three stimulants and their receptors: ACh on M₃, histamine on H₂, gastrin on CCKB. ACh, PACAP and gastrin also drive the ECL cell; somatostatin (Sst) from the D cell brakes the parietal cell, gastrin release and the ECL cell. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Inhibitors.</b> <b>Somatostatin</b> from D cells inhibits acid three ways. On the parietal cell it acts through a Gi (inhibitory G) protein, lowering cAMP and opposing histamine. It also cuts gastrin release from G cells and histamine release from ECL cells. <b>Prostaglandins</b> (PGE₂, prostaglandin E₂) use the same Gi route to oppose histamine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e28330e307.jpg\" alt=\"ACh and gastrin share the Gq → IP₃/Ca²⁺ path; histamine uses Gs → cAMP; somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit through Gi. Find where each drug acts: atropine, cimetidine and omeprazole.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">ACh and gastrin share the Gq → IP₃/Ca²⁺ path; histamine uses Gs → cAMP; somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit through Gi. Find where each drug acts: atropine, cimetidine and omeprazole. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-470735f7c0.jpg\" alt=\"Colour-coded receptors: the green ligands stimulate (histamine through Gs and cyclic AMP; ACh and gastrin through Ca²⁺), while the red one, PGE₂, acts through Gi to hold back adenylyl cyclase (Ac) and lower cAMP.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Colour-coded receptors: the green ligands stimulate (histamine through Gs and cyclic AMP; ACh and gastrin through Ca²⁺), while the red one, PGE₂, acts through Gi to hold back adenylyl cyclase (Ac) and lower cAMP. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Switching acid on and off","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Potentiation, H₂ blockers and atropine","p":"<h4>Potentiation: why one blocker cuts more than its share</h4>\n<p><b>Potentiation</b> means two stimuli together produce more than the sum of their separate effects. In the parietal cell it has two causes. First, the stimulants use different receptors and, for histamine, a different second messenger, so their signals reinforce each other inside the cell. Second, ACh and gastrin both release histamine, so part of their effect actually runs through histamine.</p>\n<p>Now look at the drugs. An H₂ blocker removes histamine's own effect <i>and</i> the boost histamine gives to ACh and gastrin. That is why <b>H₂ blockers cut about 65% of acid</b>, although they block only one of three stimulants. The cut is larger than histamine's share because the extra acid that existed only through potentiation disappears with it. Atropine works the same way from the other side: it removes ACh's effect plus the ACh-boosted effects of histamine and gastrin. Atropine still cannot stop acid completely. The vagal fibers to G cells release <b>GRP</b> (gastrin-releasing peptide), not ACh, so vagal gastrin release is untouched by atropine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a4a4bcc772.png\" alt=\"Left: together, two stimulants give more acid than their separate effects added up. Right: blocking histamine alone removes about two-thirds of stimulated acid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,466px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left: together, two stimulants give more acid than their separate effects added up. Right: blocking histamine alone removes about two-thirds of stimulated acid. <small>· Graph redrawn from Gastric secretions lecture, slide 18 (histamine potentiates ACh and gastrin; H2 blockers block 65% of acid) + Costanzo p. 363 definition of potentiation (qualitative bars)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cd7a3a0a53.jpg\" alt=\"A parietal cell with its three receptors (histamine, gastrin, acetylcholine) on the blood side. H⁺ and Cl⁻ leave together into the canaliculus as HCl while K⁺ is recycled; the insets show where the gastric gland sits in the stomach wall.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A parietal cell with its three receptors (histamine, gastrin, acetylcholine) on the blood side. H⁺ and Cl⁻ leave together into the canaliculus as HCl while K⁺ is recycled; the insets show where the gastric gland sits in the stomach wall. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Switching acid on and off","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Drugs that lower acid","p":"<h4>Drugs that lower acid</h4>\n<p><b>PPIs</b> (proton pump inhibitors), e.g., omeprazole (Prilosec, introduced 1988): block the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase itself, the final common step, so they stop acid whatever the stimulus; stronger than H₂ blockers; one dose works up to 24 h. <b>H₂ blockers</b>: cimetidine, ranitidine (Zantac), famotidine (Pepcid), nizatidine; block about 65%. Ranitidine was pulled from the market because of contamination with NDMA (N-nitrosodimethylamine), a probable human carcinogen.</p>\n<p>Lowering acid has a side effect. Acid normally brakes gastrin release, so a PPI <b>raises gastrin</b>. The extra gastrin acts on CCKB receptors on ECL cells and makes them multiply (gastrin-induced hyperplasia).</p>\n<p>Gastrin is also trophic to parietal cells, so long-term PPI use can enlarge the parietal cell mass as well.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-75604e91f8.jpg\" alt=\"A PPI lowers acid, which removes acid&#x27;s negative feedback (&quot;−ve&quot;) on the G cell. Gastrin rises and, through CCK-2R (the CCKB receptor), drives ECL cells to grow and release more histamine.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A PPI lowers acid, which removes acid's negative feedback (\"−ve\") on the G cell. Gastrin rises and, through CCK-2R (the CCKB receptor), drives ECL cells to grow and release more histamine. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Switching acid on and off","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Phases of acid secretion","p":"<p>Acid secretion during a meal falls into three phases. In the first two the vagus nerve acts by two routes. The <b>direct</b> route releases ACh onto parietal cells. The <b>indirect</b> route releases GRP onto G cells, whose gastrin travels in the blood to the parietal cells.</p>\n<p><b>Cephalic phase (~30%, \"anticipatory\")</b>: smell, taste, chewing, swallowing and conditioned reflexes act through the brain and the vagus, using both routes.</p>\n<p><b>Gastric phase (~60%, \"reactionary\")</b>: the largest, with four mechanisms. First and second, distention stretches the wall. Stretch receptors send signals up vagal afferents to the brain stem, which answers down vagal efferents. Both limbs run in the vagus, so this is a <b>vago-vagal reflex</b> (Chapter 1), and it again uses ACh on parietal cells and GRP on G cells. Third, stretch of the antrum triggers local ENS (enteric nervous system) reflexes that release gastrin. Fourth, <b>amino acids and small peptides</b> act directly on G cells. Alcohol and caffeine also stimulate acid.</p>\n<p>During a meal the same vago-vagal reflex also relaxes the orad stomach so the meal fits. That relaxation is carried by VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide), as Chapter 3 explains.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a56ca8ca73.png\" alt=\"Trace each phase to the parietal cell. The vagus feeds both the direct ACh route and the GRP → G cell → gastrin route; the dotted arrow is ACh releasing histamine. Low antral pH brakes the G cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Trace each phase to the parietal cell. The vagus feeds both the direct ACh route and the GRP → G cell → gastrin route; the dotted arrow is ACh releasing histamine. Low antral pH brakes the G cell. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Intestinal phase (~10%, \"finishing touches\")</b>: products of protein digestion in the duodenum add a little more acid.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8daf595812.jpg\" alt=\"The same two vagal routes on the stomach: ACh straight onto parietal cells, and GRP onto antral G cells, whose gastrin reaches the parietal cells through the circulation. Distention and amino acids feed in at the antrum; the table gives each phase&#x27;s share, stimuli and route.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The same two vagal routes on the stomach: ACh straight onto parietal cells, and GRP onto antral G cells, whose gastrin reaches the parietal cells through the circulation. Distention and amino acids feed in at the antrum; the table gives each phase's share, stimuli and route. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: a distended stomach</h4>\n<p>Which agents raise HCl secretion by acting on parietal cells during distention? <b>ACh, gastrin and histamine all do.</b> Stretch triggers vagal ACh release and vagal plus local gastrin release, and both then release histamine. Choosing \"gastrin and histamine, not ACh\" forgets the vago-vagal reflex.</p>\n<h4>Turning acid off</h4>\n<p>Food is itself a buffer. While a meal sits in the stomach, much of the secreted H⁺ is soaked up and the pH falls only partly. When chyme moves to the duodenum, buffering drops and the pH falls steeply. <b>Low pH then inhibits gastrin release</b>, directly and by making D cells release somatostatin. This negative feedback stops acid once it is no longer needed to activate pepsin. Vagal stimulation does the opposite: it reduces somatostatin release. From the duodenum, CCK stimulates somatostatin release, and secretin and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) also brake acid (Chapter 2).</p>\n<h4>Before PPIs: vagotomy</h4>\n<p>Ulcers were once treated surgically. A <b>truncal vagotomy</b> cut the anterior and posterior vagal trunks, removing vagal ACh and GRP drive. It was often combined with an <b>antrectomy</b>, removal of the antrum and its G cells, joining the stomach to the duodenum (Billroth I). Vagotomy also slows gastric emptying (Chapter 3).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5742643ec6.jpg\" alt=\"A: cutting the vagal trunks removes nerve drive; removing the antrum (dashed line) removes the G cells. B: the remaining stomach is joined to the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A: cutting the vagal trunks removes nerve drive; removing the antrum (dashed line) removes the G cells. B: the remaining stomach is joined to the duodenum. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: after complete vagotomy</h4>\n<p>Which function is fully abolished? <b>Primary esophageal peristalsis</b>, which swallowing triggers through vagal pathways (Chapter 3). Gastrin release survives (amino acids act on G cells directly; antral stretch works through local reflexes), and so does histamine release, because gastrin still reaches ECL cells. Slow waves start in the gut wall itself.</p>","s":"Phases, pepsin and intrinsic factor","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Pepsinogen and intrinsic factor","p":"<h4>Pepsinogen and pepsin</h4>\n<p><b>Pepsinogen</b>: chief cells (+ mucous neck cells); main stimulus = vagal ACh in the cephalic and gastric phases; H⁺ also triggers local reflexes that release it. Acid converts it to <b>pepsin</b>, a protease that starts protein digestion. Because H⁺ both releases and activates pepsinogen, it appears only when the pH is low enough to use it. Pepsin is <b>inactivated above pH 5</b>, so duodenal HCO₃⁻ shuts it off, and trypsin and the other pancreatic proteases take over (Chapter 7). Pepsin is not essential for life or for protein digestion. By itself it does not cause ulcers, but once the mucus barrier is damaged it helps acid dig one. Pepsi began as \"Brad's Drink\" (kola nuts, carbonic acid), sold to \"activate pepsinogen and aid digestion\".</p>\n<h4>Intrinsic factor: the only essential secretion</h4>\n<p><b>Intrinsic factor</b> is a mucoprotein made by parietal cells. It binds vitamin B₁₂ and is required for B₁₂ absorption in the ileum (steps in Chapter 9). Follow the logic for why it is the only essential gastric secretion. No food is absorbed in the stomach. Pepsin is not essential, so acid, whose job is to activate pepsin, is not essential. Without acid, mucus is not needed either. Without intrinsic factor, however, B₁₂ cannot be absorbed, and red blood cells cannot form normally: <b>pernicious anemia</b>. In pernicious anemia the immune system attacks parietal cells or intrinsic factor itself.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3e0758d756.jpg\" alt=\"Pernicious anemia. The parietal cell (circle) secretes intrinsic factor, which binds B₁₂ in the lumen and carries it to the distal ileum. Below, T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase lead an immune attack that destroys the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are both lost.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,607px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:607px\">Pernicious anemia. The parietal cell (circle) secretes intrinsic factor, which binds B₁₂ in the lumen and carries it to the distal ileum. Below, T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase lead an immune attack that destroys the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are both lost. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Phases, pepsin and intrinsic factor","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"The mucosal barrier and NSAIDs","p":"<p>Why does the stomach not digest itself? Mucous cells secrete <b>mucus</b>, a gel that covers the epithelium, and <b>HCO₃⁻</b>, which is trapped in the gel. H⁺ that enters the gel meets HCO₃⁻ and becomes CO₂ and water, and pepsin is inactivated in this alkaline zone. So the pH is about 2 in the lumen but about 7 at the cell surface. <b>Mucosal blood flow</b> supplies O₂ (oxygen) and HCO₃⁻ and carries away H⁺ and toxins. Growth factors speed repair. Part of the HCO₃⁻ that parietal cells send into the blood rides the mucosal capillaries up to the surface cells, which add it to the protective layer.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-15c1212ccc.jpg\" alt=\"HCO₃⁻ from the mucous cells is trapped in the mucus layer, so the pH climbs from about 2 in the lumen to about 7 at the cell surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">HCO₃⁻ from the mucous cells is trapped in the mucus layer, so the pH climbs from about 2 in the lumen to about 7 at the cell surface. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-238a4fb591.jpg\" alt=\"The barrier in colour, lumen at the top. H⁺ (green) in the lumen activates pepsinogen (1 → 2). HCO₃⁻ from the surface cells and from the blood below (blue arrows; the alkaline tide) meets H⁺ in the mucus and becomes H₂O + CO₂, so the pH scale at right climbs from 1.5 to 7.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">The barrier in colour, lumen at the top. H⁺ (green) in the lumen activates pepsinogen (1 → 2). HCO₃⁻ from the surface cells and from the blood below (blue arrows; the alkaline tide) meets H⁺ in the mucus and becomes H₂O + CO₂, so the pH scale at right climbs from 1.5 to 7. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Prostaglandins</b> (mainly PGE₂) hold the barrier together: ↑ mucus, ↑ HCO₃⁻, ↑ blood flow (vasodilation), ↓ acid (Gi blunts histamine's cAMP). Gastric <b>stretch</b> makes prostaglandins. So the same stretch that calls up acid and pepsin also makes the prostaglandins that make the mucus that protects against that acid and pepsin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: duodenal cell turnover</h4>\n<p>Why are duodenal enterocytes replaced every few days (Chapter 7)? <b>Acid, proteolytic enzymes and mechanical wear all damage them</b>, so \"all of the above\" is right.</p>\n<h4>NSAIDs</h4>\n<p><b>NSAIDs</b> (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, e.g., aspirin) block <b>COX</b> (cyclooxygenase), the enzyme that turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandins. COX-1 is \"constitutive\", always on; its prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and activate platelets. COX-2 is \"inducible\"; its prostaglandins cause pain, inflammation and fever. Ordinary NSAIDs block both, and COX-2 inhibitors block only COX-2. Without PGE₂, mucus, HCO₃⁻ and blood flow fall and the brake on acid is lost, so acid \"cooks\" the lining.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d3757f773a.png\" alt=\"NSAIDs hit both branches, so they relieve pain but also strip the stomach&#x27;s protection. COX-2 inhibitors spare the COX-1 branch.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,490px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">NSAIDs hit both branches, so they relieve pain but also strip the stomach's protection. COX-2 inhibitors spare the COX-1 branch. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-239a5c2576.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s colour version of the same pathway. COX-1 prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and support platelet hemostasis; COX-2 prostaglandins mediate pain, inflammation and fever. NSAIDs (green) block both enzymes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">The lecture's colour version of the same pathway. COX-1 prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and support platelet hemostasis; COX-2 prostaglandins mediate pain, inflammation and fever. NSAIDs (green) block both enzymes. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: back pain pills and stomach pain</h4>\n<p>A woman takes regular medication for back pain and develops abdominal pain. Mechanism: <b>decreased mucus production</b>, because NSAIDs cut prostaglandins and the mucosal barrier breaks down. \"Excess acid secretion\" is close, since losing PGE₂ does raise acid, but barrier loss is what exposes the epithelium.</p>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Peptic ulcers and Helicobacter pylori","p":"<p>A <b>peptic ulcer</b> is an erosion of gastric or duodenal mucosa by H⁺ and pepsin. The name misleads: pepsin does not cause ulcers by itself. An ulcer needs loss of the barrier, too much H⁺ and pepsin, or both: an imbalance between protective and damaging factors (\"no acid, no ulcer\"). <b>Protective</b>: mucus, HCO₃⁻, prostaglandins, blood flow, growth factors. <b>Damaging</b>: H⁺, pepsin, <i>Helicobacter pylori</i>, NSAIDs, stress (head trauma, burns, ventilators/intubation), smoking, alcohol, cancer, IBD (inflammatory bowel disease). Diagnosis: upper endoscopy (EGD, esophagogastroduodenoscopy) with biopsy.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-83c194d40d.jpg\" alt=\"The balance behind every peptic ulcer: H⁺ and pepsin attack a barrier of HCO₃⁻ and mucus. The columns list what protects the mucosa and what damages it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The balance behind every peptic ulcer: H⁺ and pepsin attack a barrier of HCO₃⁻ and mucus. The columns list what protects the mucosa and what damages it. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f8b1c546f8.jpg\" alt=\"A chronic peptic ulcer in a resected stomach: a punched-out crater with the mucosal folds radiating to its edge.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A chronic peptic ulcer in a resected stomach: a punched-out crater with the mucosal folds radiating to its edge. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Helicobacter pylori</h4>\n<p><b><i>H. pylori</i></b> is a gram-negative bacterium that lives in gastric mucus, often in the antrum. It survives the acid with <b>urease</b>. Urease splits urea into NH₃ (ammonia) and CO₂, and NH₃ grabs H⁺ to form NH₄⁺ (ammonium). This neutralizes the acid around the bacterium, so it can bind the epithelium instead of being shed. The ammonia is safe for the bug but harmful to the host: it impairs mitochondrial respiration and kills mucosal cells. Cytotoxins (CagA) and mucinase destroy the mucus and epithelium, and recruited leukocytes add inflammation.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-423a61c8cf.jpg\" alt=\"Infection in four steps: (1) the bacteria swim through the gastric fluid into the mucus; (2) urease protects them from the acid; (3) they multiply on the epithelium; (4) mucinase, cytotoxins and ammonia damage the mucosa, and leukocytes are drawn in.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">Infection in four steps: (1) the bacteria swim through the gastric fluid into the mucus; (2) urease protects them from the acid; (3) they multiply on the epithelium; (4) mucinase, cytotoxins and ammonia damage the mucosa, and leukocytes are drawn in. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>It causes ulcers by two routes. A <b>gastric ulcer</b> is direct: the barrier breaks down where the bacteria live. A <b>duodenal ulcer</b> is indirect. Infection suppresses somatostatin from antral D cells, so gastrin and acid rise (\"inhibition of inhibition\"). Infection that spreads to the duodenum also cuts duodenal HCO₃⁻ secretion. More acid arrives and less buffer meets it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e8b3496121.png\" alt=\"Left branch: direct damage gives a gastric ulcer. Right branches: less somatostatin raises acid while duodenal infection lowers HCO₃⁻, giving a duodenal ulcer.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left branch: direct damage gives a gastric ulcer. Right branches: less somatostatin raises acid while duodenal infection lowers HCO₃⁻, giving a duodenal ulcer. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Diagnosis</b>: biopsy, stool antigen, serology, or the <b>¹³C-urea breath test</b>. The patient drinks urea labeled with carbon-13, a heavy, nonradioactive carbon. Urease, if present, splits off the label as ¹³CO₂, which is absorbed, exhaled and measured. No <i>H. pylori</i> means no urease and no ¹³CO₂. <b>Treatment</b>: PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: surviving the acid</h4>\n<p>A curved organism in a gastric biopsy survives the acid by <b>ammonia neutralizing H⁺</b>. It does not inhibit proton pumps or raise prostaglandins, and it uses urease rather than inhibiting it.</p>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Gastritis, and gastric versus duodenal ulcers","p":"<h4>Worked example: chronic antral gastritis</h4>\n<p>A 45-year-old man has three years of vague pain and nausea, not relieved by antacids; endoscopy shows antral redness without ulcer or mass, and biopsy shows chronic nonspecific gastritis. Answer: <b><i>H. pylori</i> infection</b>. Zollinger–Ellison syndrome (ZES) would produce ulcers, and pernicious anemia is an autoimmune gastritis of the gastric body, not the antrum. Serum gastrin would be raised, because infection lowers somatostatin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: duodenal ulcer with a positive breath test</h4>\n<p>A 59-year-old woman has burning epigastric pain that eases with food or antacids. Serology and the ¹³C-urea breath test are positive, and endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer. She gets antibiotics to clear <i>H. pylori</i> plus omeprazole, which lowers the H⁺ load reaching the duodenum. Food helps because it buffers acid.</p>\n<h4>Gastric versus duodenal ulcers</h4>\n<p><b>Duodenal ulcers</b> are more common. They form because H⁺ secretion is high; gastrin rises more after meals, and its trophic effect enlarges the parietal cell mass. <b>Gastric ulcers</b> form because the barrier fails. Net H⁺ secretion is actually low, because H⁺ leaks back into the damaged mucosa, and gastrin is high because less acid means less feedback. A gastric ulcer can become cancer (about 4%), so it <b>must be biopsied</b> and rescoped to confirm healing.</p>\n<p>Where <i>H. pylori</i> inflames the acid-secreting body of the stomach, the inflammation also suppresses the parietal cells directly, which lowers acid further.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-923426dc46.jpg\" alt=\"Typical sites: gastric ulcers along the stomach&#x27;s curvatures, and a duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Typical sites: gastric ulcers along the stomach's curvatures, and a duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f3217e9824.jpg\" alt=\"Endoscopic view of a gastric ulcer: a sharply punched-out crater in reddened gastric mucosa. Because about 4% of gastric ulcers are cancers, every one is biopsied.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Endoscopic view of a gastric ulcer: a sharply punched-out crater in reddened gastric mucosa. Because about 4% of gastric ulcers are cancers, every one is biopsied. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Gastric ulcer</th><th>Duodenal ulcer</th><th>Zollinger–Ellison</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>H⁺ secretion</td><td>↓ (leaks back)</td><td>↑</td><td>↑↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gastrin</td><td>↑ (less H⁺ feedback)</td><td>↑ after meals</td><td>↑↑ from tumor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main cause</td><td>barrier loss: <i>H. pylori</i> (direct), NSAIDs</td><td>acid excess: <i>H. pylori</i> (indirect)</td><td>gastrinoma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key point</td><td>~4% cancer: biopsy</td><td>parietal mass ↑</td><td>large, multiple, distal ulcers</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"A gastrin tumor and its acid","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-731df8ec1b.png\" alt=\"Gastrin without feedback drives both acid secretion and parietal cell growth. The acid load in the duodenum explains the ulcers, the steatorrhea and the diarrhea.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,482px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Gastrin without feedback drives both acid secretion and parietal cell growth. The acid load in the duodenum explains the ulcers, the steatorrhea and the diarrhea. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>ZES</b> (Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) is caused by a <b>gastrinoma</b>, a gastrin-secreting tumor, usually of the pancreas (non-β islet cells). Normal G cells stop releasing gastrin when the stomach turns acidic. The tumor ignores that feedback, so gastrin stays <b>high despite high acid</b>. The gastrin drives H⁺ secretion and, being trophic, enlarges the parietal cell mass and thickens the gastric mucosa. Basal acid output is normally under 10 mEq/h (milliequivalents of H⁺ per hour); in ZES it is usually over 15 mEq/h.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-02fddc9a72.jpg\" alt=\"A gastrinoma in the pancreas, and the duodenal ulcers that its gastrin-driven acid produces.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">A gastrinoma in the pancreas, and the duodenal ulcers that its gastrin-driven acid produces. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The acid load causes three problems. First, it overwhelms pancreatic HCO₃⁻, so ulcers form that are large (&gt; 1 cm), multiple and distal, even near the ligament of Treitz. Second, the volume of gastric juice exceeds what the intestine can absorb, causing <b>diarrhea</b>. Third, low duodenal pH causes <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stool). Pancreatic lipase is inactivated (its best pH is about 6). Bile salts stay nonionized and are absorbed too early, before micelles finish their work. Acid also damages the intestinal mucosa, shrinking the absorbing surface (Chapter 8).</p>","s":"Zollinger–Ellison syndrome","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Suspecting, diagnosing and treating ZES","p":"<p><b>Suspect ZES</b> with ulcers that are &gt; 1 cm, multiple or distal, that recur after <i>H. pylori</i> treatment, or that come with diarrhea or steatorrhea.</p>\n<p>About one gastrinoma in four is part of <b>MEN1</b> (multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1), an inherited syndrome that also causes parathyroid tumors (high calcium, kidney stones) and pituitary tumors; those findings, or a family history, point to a gastrinoma.</p>\n<p><b>Diagnosis</b>: high acid AND high gastrin = ZES (fasting gastrin on at least three occasions; gastric acid output and pH). In the <b>secretin stimulation test</b>, IV (intravenous) secretin normally lowers gastrin or leaves it unchanged, because secretin inhibits G cells. Gastrinoma cells instead <b>release more gastrin</b>. Nobody knows why; this reversal is simply the tumor's signature. In a pentagastrin test (a gastrin analog), a healthy stomach roughly triples its acid output; in ZES acid does not rise, because the parietal cells are already maximally driven.</p>\n<p>Ultrasound, CT (computed tomography), MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) and endoscopic ultrasound locate the tumor and exclude metastases. <b>Treatment</b>: a localized tumor is removed surgically; metastases need lifelong PPI. H₂ blockers such as cimetidine help less but still help, because much of gastrin's effect runs through histamine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-484d60dbe4.jpg\" alt=\"Endoscopic ultrasound: an ultrasound probe on the tip of the endoscope (left) images the pancreas through the wall of the stomach and duodenum (right), which finds small gastrinomas.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">Endoscopic ultrasound: an ultrasound probe on the tip of the endoscope (left) images the pancreas through the wall of the stomach and duodenum (right), which finds small gastrinomas. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Zollinger–Ellison syndrome","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"ZES cases and look-alikes","p":"<h4>Worked example: oily diarrhea and a pancreatic mass</h4>\n<p>A 47-year-old man has abdominal pain eased by eating and antacids, oily diarrhea and a duodenal-bulb ulcer. Gastrin is 800 pg/mL (picograms per milliliter; normal 0–130), basal H⁺ is 100 mEq/h (normal 10), pentagastrin adds no acid, and secretin raises gastrin to 1,100: a <b>gastrinoma</b>, here a 3-cm pancreatic-head mass. Food eased his pain because it buffers acid. Omeprazole controls acid until surgery, and removing the tumor heals the ulcer.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: reading gastrin and acid together</h4>\n<p>The correct statement: <b>increased acid with elevated gastrin indicates ZES</b>. H₂ blockers are not preferred for metastatic gastrinoma (lifelong PPI is), and they do not lower gastrin. PPIs do affect gastrin: they <b>raise</b> it by removing acid feedback.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: a hyperactive gut peptide</h4>\n<p>A 51-year-old man has oily, foul-smelling stool and a hypertrophied gastric mucosa; a mutation makes one gut peptide tenfold more active. Answer: <b>gastrin</b>, because mucosal hypertrophy is its trophic signature; CCK, GIP, secretin and histamine do not thicken the gastric mucosa. The steatorrhea comes from acid <b>inactivating lipase</b> (the same result orlistat gets by blocking lipase), not from a faster colon or from amylase or trypsinogen.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>High gastrin with <b>high acid</b> = ZES. High gastrin with <b>low acid</b> = lost feedback: gastric ulcer, acid-suppressing drugs, or the atrophic gastritis of pernicious anemia. Gastrin reaches parietal cells through the blood; histamine diffuses locally. ACh and gastrin use IP₃/Ca²⁺, histamine uses cAMP, and somatostatin and PGE₂ use Gi. Atropine cannot block vagal gastrin release, because that synapse uses GRP.</p>","s":"Zollinger–Ellison syndrome","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Location, cells and nerves","p":"<p>Two juices enter the duodenum after a meal. Pancreatic juice brings digestive enzymes and bicarbonate; bile brings the bile salts that make fat digestible.</p>\n<p><b>Location</b>: a very posterior (retroperitoneal) organ lying against the spine, its head in the C-loop of the duodenum. The pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct, and both empty into the duodenum through the <b>sphincter of Oddi</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2338029697.jpg\" alt=\"Front view (left) and side view (right). The pancreas (P, yellow) lies deep behind the stomach, its head in the curve of the duodenum and its body pressed against the spine.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Front view (left) and side view (right). The pancreas (P, yellow) lies deep behind the stomach, its head in the curve of the duodenum and its body pressed against the spine. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Tissue mix</b>: exocrine tissue (acini + ducts) ≈ 85–90% of the gland; islets only ≈ 2% (β cells 60–75% → insulin; α 20–25% → glucagon; δ 5–10% → somatostatin; a few PP (pancreatic polypeptide) cells); the other 8–12% is connective tissue, arteries, veins and nerves. Diagrams that draw large islets mislead: the gland is mainly a digestive factory.</p>\n<p>In <b>type 1 diabetes</b> the immune system destroys the β cells, so no insulin is made and blood glucose rises; glucose spilling into the urine drags water with it, causing heavy urination and thirst.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ae24ac71b1.jpg\" alt=\"The kind of drawing the lecture warns about: a box on the pancreas is enlarged into one big islet (blue, with darker endocrine cells) beside a few small acini (red). Drawn this way the islet looks major, yet islets are only about 2% of the gland.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The kind of drawing the lecture warns about: a box on the pancreas is enlarged into one big islet (blue, with darker endocrine cells) beside a few small acini (red). Drawn this way the islet looks major, yet islets are only about 2% of the gland. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The exocrine pancreas is built like a bunch of grapes. Each grape is an <b>acinus</b>, the blind end of a branching duct. <b>Acinar cells</b> line the acinus and make the enzymes. Centroacinar cells, where the acinus opens into the intercalated duct, and the <b>ductal cells</b> beyond make the watery, bicarbonate-rich part. Inside an acinar cell, enzymes are built on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, packaged by the Golgi and stored in apical <b>zymogen granules</b>. A stimulus such as CCK (cholecystokinin) or ACh (acetylcholine) makes the granules fuse with the apical membrane and empty into the lumen.</p>\n<p>Centroacinar cells secrete the initial aqueous fluid, and the ductal cells then modify it (next section).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8c86477199.jpg\" alt=\"Acinar cells release enzymes into the acinus; centroacinar and ductal cells add an isotonic, bicarbonate-rich fluid. One duct cell is drawn in detail in the next section.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,340px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Acinar cells release enzymes into the acinus; centroacinar and ductal cells add an isotonic, bicarbonate-rich fluid. One duct cell is drawn in detail in the next section. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 367</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4dfc3c69d0.jpg\" alt=\"One acinus up close. The acinar cells (blue) are dotted with zymogen granules on the side facing the lumen; the centroacinar cells (orange) sit where the acinus opens into the intercalated duct.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One acinus up close. The acinar cells (blue) are dotted with zymogen granules on the side facing the lumen; the centroacinar cells (orange) sit where the acinus opens into the intercalated duct. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Nerves</b>: far less important than hormones. Parasympathetic (vagus) → secretes; sympathetic (celiac ganglion, splanchnic nerves) → \"dries\" (inhibits). In the salivary glands, by contrast, both divisions stimulate (Chapter 4).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-18efb416a4.jpg\" alt=\"The two nerve supplies: the right and left vagus nerves (parasympathetic) come down along the esophagus; sympathetic fibres leave the celiac ganglion and reach the pancreas in the splanchnic nerves.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The two nerve supplies: the right and left vagus nerves (parasympathetic) come down along the esophagus; sympathetic fibres leave the celiac ganglion and reach the pancreas in the splanchnic nerves. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The exocrine pancreas","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Acinar enzymes and acute pancreatitis","p":"<p>Acinar cells secrete enzymes for all three food groups, and these enzymes are essential for life. <b>Amylase</b> and <b>lipase</b> leave the cell already active. The proteases leave as inactive precursors called <b>zymogens</b>: trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase and procarboxypeptidases A and B. <b>Colipase</b> also leaves inactive, as procolipase, and so does phospholipase A₂ (their jobs in fat digestion: Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-767fd7b1bb.jpg\" alt=\"Inside an acinar cell (scanning electron micrograph, false colour, about 2200×): red zymogen granules hold the stored enzymes; the yellow stacks at the bottom are the Golgi that packed them.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Inside an acinar cell (scanning electron micrograph, false colour, about 2200×): red zymogen granules hold the stored enzymes; the yellow stacks at the bottom are the Golgi that packed them. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pancreas is made of protein, not of bread or butter. Active amylase and lipase find no starch or fat to attack in the gland, so they are safe. (Mnemonic: a gingerbread man could not secrete active amylase; it would digest itself.) Active proteases would digest the pancreas itself. So each zymogen carries an extra piece, a <b>negative regulatory domain</b>, that blocks its active site until it is cut off.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1f65e538fd.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s gingerbread man, bitten in half: a cookie made of starch could not hold active amylase. The pancreas is protein, so only its proteases must be kept inactive.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The lecture's gingerbread man, bitten in half: a cookie made of starch could not hold active amylase. The pancreas is protein, so only its proteases must be kept inactive. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three safeguards keep activation out of the gland. First, the zymogens are stored and shipped inactive. Second, acinar cells pack a <b>trypsin inhibitor</b> into the granules to neutralize any trypsin that forms early. Third, the trigger enzyme lives only in the duodenum. <b>Enterokinase</b> (enteropeptidase), a duodenal brush-border enzyme, cuts trypsinogen into <b>trypsin</b>. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens, procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ included, plus more trypsinogen (autocatalysis). Trypsin is the master switch and the most abundant pancreatic protease: without it, every protease acts as if missing.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bd9410d027.png\" alt=\"Enterokinase makes the first trypsin in the duodenum; trypsin switches on everything else, including more of itself. The dashed path is acute pancreatitis.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,510px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Enterokinase makes the first trypsin in the duodenum; trypsin switches on everything else, including more of itself. The dashed path is acute pancreatitis. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which enzyme has a negative regulatory domain?</h4>\n<p>Choices: chymotrypsinogen, salivary amylase, lingual lipase, pancreatic amylase, pancreatic lipase. Only <b>chymotrypsinogen</b> is a zymogen. The amylases and lipases are all secreted active.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: acute pancreatitis</h4>\n<p>After an alcohol binge, trauma or a gallstone blocking the duct, a patient has epigastric pain, vomiting and high serum amylase and lipase. The mechanism is <b>premature activation of trypsinogen</b> inside the pancreas: trypsin activates the other zymogens there, and they digest the gland and the tissue around it. Amylase and lipase are markers, not the cause (they are secreted active anyway), and chymotrypsin and elastase are activated by trypsin, not the reverse. <i>H. pylori</i> (<i>Helicobacter pylori</i>) causes ulcers, not pancreatitis (Chapter 5).</p>","s":"The exocrine pancreas","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"The duct cell and flow rate","p":"<h4>The duct cell: making bicarbonate</h4>\n<p>Ductal and centroacinar cells secrete <b>HCO₃⁻</b> (bicarbonate) to neutralize gastric acid. Follow it in five steps. First, CO₂ (carbon dioxide) diffuses in from blood. Second, <b>carbonic anhydrase</b> joins CO₂ and water into H₂CO₃ (carbonic acid), which splits into H⁺ (hydrogen ion) and HCO₃⁻. Third, an apical <b>Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger</b> sends HCO₃⁻ into the lumen and takes Cl⁻ (chloride) in. Fourth, that Cl⁻ leaks back to the lumen through <b>CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), an apical Cl⁻ channel. So the exchanger never runs out of luminal Cl⁻. Fifth, H⁺ leaves into blood on a basolateral sodium–hydrogen (Na⁺/H⁺) exchanger. The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase (sodium–potassium pump) drives it by keeping cell Na⁺ low; K⁺ recycles out through a K⁺ channel.</p>\n<p>The negative HCO₃⁻ in the lumen pulls <b>Na⁺</b> through the junctions between cells, and <b>water</b> follows by osmosis. Because the duct lets water through, the juice stays <b>isotonic</b>. Secretin raises cAMP (cyclic AMP, a second messenger) in these cells, which opens CFTR and speeds the whole system.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0fe23948e0.jpg\" alt=\"Duct cell. Carbonic anhydrase (CA) makes HCO₃⁻, which exits on the apical Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger; Cl⁻ returns through the apical Cl⁻ channel (CFTR); H⁺ goes to blood on the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,473px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Duct cell. Carbonic anhydrase (CA) makes HCO₃⁻, which exits on the apical Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger; Cl⁻ returns through the apical Cl⁻ channel (CFTR); H⁺ goes to blood on the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Acid tide</b>: every HCO₃⁻ sent to the lumen leaves one H⁺ in the blood, so pancreatic venous blood turns acidic during secretion. This offsets the stomach's alkaline tide. So during digestion the <b>pancreatic veins</b> carry the lowest-pH blood, the mirror image of the gastric vein (Chapter 5).</p>\n<h4>Pancreatic juice and flow rate</h4>\n<p>Pancreatic juice is <b>isotonic at every flow rate</b>. Its <b>Na⁺ and K⁺ stay at plasma levels</b>. Only the two anions change, and they move in opposite directions: as flow rises, <b>HCO₃⁻ rises and Cl⁻ falls</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-24cc8d9697.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic juice vs flow rate. Na⁺ and K⁺ stay flat; HCO₃⁻ climbs far above its plasma value while Cl⁻ falls as its mirror image. The bars on the right show plasma for comparison.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Pancreatic juice vs flow rate. Na⁺ and K⁺ stay flat; HCO₃⁻ climbs far above its plasma value while Cl⁻ falls as its mirror image. The bars on the right show plasma for comparison. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Read the graph step by step. The x-axis is flow in µL/min·g (microliters per minute per gram of gland). The y-axis is concentration in mEq/L (milliequivalents per liter, the charge an ion carries per liter). Na⁺ is flat near 150; K⁺ is flat and low. At the lowest flow, Cl⁻ is high and HCO₃⁻ low. As flow climbs, HCO₃⁻ rises steeply and levels off above 100 mEq/L, far above plasma, while Cl⁻ falls as its mirror image. Because the juice stays isotonic with constant Na⁺ and K⁺, a rise in one anion must be matched by a fall in the other.</p>\n<p>Why? There is no \"contact time\" story here. Unstimulated juice is mostly Na⁺, Cl⁻ and water. Secretin makes duct cells pour out much more fluid, and that extra fluid is rich in HCO₃⁻, because the exchanger swaps Cl⁻ for HCO₃⁻. Stronger stimulation therefore means both more flow and more HCO₃⁻: <b>higher flow = higher HCO₃⁻</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>As flow rises</th><th>Saliva (Chapter 4)</th><th>Pancreatic juice</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tonicity</td><td>Hypotonic → nearer isotonic</td><td>Isotonic throughout</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Na⁺, Cl⁻</td><td>Both ↑</td><td>Na⁺ flat; Cl⁻ ↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>K⁺</td><td>↓</td><td>Flat (plasma level)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HCO₃⁻</td><td>↑</td><td>↑, far above plasma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Why</td><td>Water-tight ducts; less contact time</td><td>Water follows; secretin drives exchange</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Do not carry the saliva story over. Salivary ducts reabsorb NaCl without water, so slow flow makes saliva more hypotonic. Pancreatic ducts let water follow, so only the HCO₃⁻:Cl⁻ ratio changes.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7b792999c2.png\" alt=\"For contrast, saliva&#x27;s curves (Chapter 4): Na⁺ (blue) and Cl⁻ (orange) climb together with flow and stay below plasma. Compare the pancreatic graph above, where Na⁺ is flat and HCO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ mirror each other.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">For contrast, saliva's curves (Chapter 4): Na⁺ (blue) and Cl⁻ (orange) climb together with flow and stay below plasma. Compare the pancreatic graph above, where Na⁺ is flat and HCO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ mirror each other. <small>· Graph redrawn from Saliva lecture, slide 17, with values from Costanzo Fig. 8.13</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Pancreatic juice and its control","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Regulation: phases, CCK, secretin and potentiation","p":"<p><b>Phases</b>: cephalic (smell, taste, conditioning) and gastric (distention) phases are minor; both work through the vagus, release mainly enzymes, and their ACh potentiates CCK and secretin. The <b>intestinal phase</b> gives 80–90% of pancreatic secretion and works through two hormones.</p>\n<p><b>CCK → acinar cells → enzymes.</b> I cells of the duodenum and jejunum release CCK in response to fatty acids, small peptides and amino acids (most potently phenylalanine, methionine and tryptophan). CCK binds CCK<sub>A</sub> receptors on acinar cells and acts through IP₃ (inositol trisphosphate) and Ca²⁺ (calcium). ACh uses the same pathway and potentiates CCK through vago-vagal reflexes.</p>\n<p><b>Secretin → duct cells → HCO₃⁻ and water.</b> Duodenal S cells release secretin when acid (pH &lt; 4.5) arrives. Secretin acts through cAMP. CCK and ACh potentiate it.</p>\n<p><b>Potentiation</b> means two stimuli together give more than the sum of each alone. It happens because secretin uses cAMP while CCK and ACh use Ca²⁺, and the two pathways amplify each other in the same cell. So a fatty, acidic meal, which releases both hormones, gets a far larger HCO₃⁻ flow than secretin alone could produce.</p>\n<p>The secretin loop adjusts itself. More acid in the duodenum → more secretin → more flow and more HCO₃⁻. So, in the duodenum, <b>more acid = more HCO₃⁻</b>. Once the acid is neutralized, secretin release falls. Neutralizing matters because acid inactivates pancreatic lipase (Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-12e0884e5f.jpg\" alt=\"Two hormones, two targets. CCK from I cells drives enzyme secretion through IP₃/Ca²⁺; secretin from S cells drives the aqueous HCO₃⁻ secretion through cAMP. The zigzag arrows mark potentiation.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Two hormones, two targets. CCK from I cells drives enzyme secretion through IP₃/Ca²⁺; secretin from S cells drives the aqueous HCO₃⁻ secretion through cAMP. The zigzag arrows mark potentiation. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 370</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: what does CCK do to pancreatic juice?</h4>\n<p>CCK <b>potentiates secretin</b>, raising the volume and HCO₃⁻ of the juice. It does not inhibit HCO₃⁻ secretion or speed gastric emptying. Secretin does not drive enzymes, so \"potentiates secretin to increase enzymes\" is wrong.</p>","s":"Pancreatic juice and its control","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Pancreatic insufficiency: chronic pancreatitis and cystic fibrosis","p":"<p><b>Chronic pancreatitis</b> is a dead, burnt-out pancreas (classically from alcohol). Fibrosis and cell death leave <b>no enzymes</b>. Serum amylase and lipase therefore drop (they rise only in acute pancreatitis). No trypsin is released, so every protease fails and protein digestion fails too. Duct cells are lost too, so secretin releases markedly <b>decreased HCO₃⁻</b>. Undigested fat gives oily, floating stools (<b>steatorrhea</b>), weight loss, and poor absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K.</p>\n<p>The <b>IV (intravenous) secretin stimulation test</b> uses this. Secretin is injected into a vein and duodenal juice is collected. A normal pancreas answers with a massive HCO₃⁻ release; a chronically damaged one releases little.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: oily floating stools, alcohol overuse, weight loss, equivocal celiac tests</h4>\n<p>The most specific test for chronic pancreatitis is the <b>secretin stimulation test</b>. Biopsy is too invasive; vitamin A, D, E, K levels fall with any fat malabsorption; CCK levels may be normal. Endoscopic ultrasound is the most sensitive imaging for early changes, and ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) images the ducts. Cystic fibrosis and pancreatic cancer can also blunt the secretin response.</p>\n<p><b>Cystic fibrosis (CF)</b> is a CFTR defect. Without CFTR, Cl⁻ cannot return to the duct lumen, the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger stalls, and little HCO₃⁻ or water is secreted. Thick secretions <b>clog the ducts</b>. The acini still make enzymes, but the enzymes cannot flow out, and the blocked gland becomes cystic and fibrotic. The result mimics chronic pancreatitis: fat and vitamin A, D, E, K malabsorption. CF is the <b>opposite of cholera</b>: cholera toxin keeps CFTR open and floods the gut with fluid (Chapter 9), while CF shuts it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f4efaf4adf.jpg\" alt=\"The 2026 discussion&#x27;s drawing of the whole system. CCK acts on the acinar cells (enzymes), secretin on the centroacinar and duct cells. In the duct cell, the red-circled CFTR returns Cl⁻ to the lumen for the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue); Na⁺ follows between the cells (red box). In CF this channel fails.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:722px\">The 2026 discussion's drawing of the whole system. CCK acts on the acinar cells (enzymes), secretin on the centroacinar and duct cells. In the duct cell, the red-circled CFTR returns Cl⁻ to the lumen for the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue); Na⁺ follows between the cells (red box). In CF this channel fails. <small>· Discussion session (saliva, stomach, pancreas), 2026, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Chronic pancreatitis</th><th>Cystic fibrosis</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Enzymes</td><td>Not made</td><td>Made, not released</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ducts</td><td>Dry, burnt out</td><td>Plugged, cystic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Image</td><td>No beer left</td><td>Beer tap blocked</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"Pancreatic juice and its control","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"What bile contains and how bile salts are made","p":"<p>Bile is indispensable for fat digestion and absorption, because fat does not dissolve in water. Hepatocytes secrete bile continuously. Its organic solids are <b>bile salts ≈ 50%</b>, <b>phospholipids ≈ 40%</b>, <b>cholesterol ≈ 4%</b> and <b>bilirubin ≈ 2%</b>; water and electrolytes make up the bulk of the fluid. Cells lining the bile ducts add a HCO₃⁻-rich fluid by the same mechanism as pancreatic duct cells, and secretin stimulates it.</p>\n<p><b>Bile acids</b> are made from cholesterol in the liver; the rate-limiting enzyme is <b>7α-hydroxylase</b>. The <b>primary bile acids</b> are cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid. Gut bacteria remove a hydroxyl group from some of them, forming the <b>secondary bile acids</b> deoxycholic and lithocholic acid. The liver conjugates bile acids with <b>glycine or taurine</b>, making bile salts (eight in all, for example glycocholic and taurocholic acid).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f241fd5d97.jpg\" alt=\"Read it top to bottom. In the liver, 7α-hydroxylase turns cholesterol into the two primary bile acids (green box); gut bacteria strip a hydroxyl to make the secondary acids (teal box); back in the liver, conjugation with glycine or taurine makes the bile salts (blue box).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Read it top to bottom. In the liver, 7α-hydroxylase turns cholesterol into the two primary bile acids (green box); gut bacteria strip a hydroxyl to make the secondary acids (teal box); back in the liver, conjugation with glycine or taurine makes the bile salts (blue box). <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Conjugation lowers the pK (the pH at which half the molecules carry a charge) from about 7 to 1–4. Duodenal contents run pH 3–5. At that pH an unconjugated bile acid is mostly uncharged and fat-soluble, so it would be absorbed too early. A bile salt is mostly ionized, so it stays water-soluble and in the lumen until the ileum reclaims it. Conjugation also serves excretion: bilirubin conjugated with glucuronic acid becomes water-soluble and leaves in bile (Chapter 9). If the duodenum stays too acidic (Zollinger–Ellison syndrome, Chapter 5), bile salts become uncharged and are absorbed early.</p>","s":"Bile and bile salts","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Bile salts at work","p":"<p>Bile salts are <b>amphipathic</b>: the fat-soluble side touches fat, while the charged glycine or taurine side faces water. So they do two jobs: they emulsify large fat blobs into small droplets for lipase, and they carry the digestion products, with vitamins A, D, E and K, in micelles (both in Chapter 8). <b>Phospholipids</b> are amphipathic too and help form micelles. <b>Cholesterol</b> in bile is the body's main route for excreting cholesterol; it does not aid digestion. <b>Bilirubin</b>, from hemoglobin breakdown, gives bile and stool their color (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4468d24f66.jpg\" alt=\"Bile salts at work, top to bottom: pancreatic lipase digests the dietary fat, and the products ride in micelles, each a ball of bile salts with their water-loving heads facing out, down through the unstirred layer to the brush border.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile salts at work, top to bottom: pancreatic lipase digests the dietary fat, and the products ride in micelles, each a ball of bile salts with their water-loving heads facing out, down through the unstirred layer to the brush border. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which statement about bile is true?</h4>\n<p>True: bile salts <b>emulsify</b> large triglyceride droplets. Bile is not an enzyme and catalyzes nothing. Emulsification <i>increases</i> droplet surface area. More than 95% of bile salts are recycled, not lost in feces.</p>","s":"Bile and bile salts","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"The gallbladder and sphincter of Oddi","p":"<p>The gallbladder <b>stores</b>, <b>concentrates</b> and ejects bile. Between meals it is relaxed and the sphincter of Oddi is closed, so continuously made bile fills it. Its epithelium absorbs ions and water isosmotically; the organic parts stay behind and become concentrated. Within about 30 minutes of a meal, fat and protein products reach the duodenum and I cells release CCK. CCK does two things at once: it <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> and <b>relaxes the sphincter of Oddi</b>. Bile then spurts into the duodenum whenever duodenal pressure is low.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-95517cbc66.jpg\" alt=\"The route bile takes: from the gallbladder down the common bile duct, through the head of the pancreas, to its opening in the duodenal wall, where the pancreatic duct empties too. The sphincter of Oddi guards this opening.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The route bile takes: from the gallbladder down the common bile duct, through the head of the pancreas, to its opening in the duodenal wall, where the pancreatic duct empties too. The sphincter of Oddi guards this opening. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ee0a83f47e.jpg\" alt=\"(1) The liver makes bile salts from cholesterol. (2) The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile. (3) CCK contracts it and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. (4) The ileum returns bile salts to portal blood. (5) The liver extracts them. Secretin adds ions and water in the ducts.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">(1) The liver makes bile salts from cholesterol. (2) The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile. (3) CCK contracts it and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. (4) The ileum returns bile salts to portal blood. (5) The liver extracts them. Secretin adds ions and water in the ducts. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.24</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Bile flow and recycling","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Enterohepatic circulation and losing the ileum","p":"<p>Bile salts are recycled between gut and liver. They work in the duodenum and jejunum, where most fat is absorbed by mid-jejunum, and stay in the lumen when the lipids enter the cells. In the <b>terminal ileum</b>, a <b>Na⁺–bile salt cotransporter</b> carries them out of the lumen, and they pass into portal blood. The liver extracts them and secretes them again. Bacteria deconjugate some on the way, so the circuit carries bile acids as well as bile salts. Uptake at the far end of the small intestine keeps bile salts present along its whole length.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d85bc3041d.jpg\" alt=\"Follow the red line: bile flows from the liver down the common bile duct (filling the gallbladder on the way) into the duodenum, travels the small intestine, leaves at the terminal ileum and returns in the hepatic portal vein to the liver.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Follow the red line: bile flows from the liver down the common bile duct (filling the gallbladder on the way) into the duodenum, travels the small intestine, leaves at the terminal ileum and returns in the hepatic portal vein to the liver. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pool is only about <b>2.5 g</b>, yet the liver secretes about <b>24 g/day</b>, so the pool cycles several times a day. More than <b>95%</b> is reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. About <b>600 mg/day</b> (also quoted as 0.4 g/day), roughly a quarter of the pool, is lost in stool, and new synthesis replaces exactly that. Returning bile salts inhibit 7α-hydroxylase, so less return means more synthesis. Returning bile salts also stimulate bile secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7e8533d286.png\" alt=\"The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day circulates from a 2.5 g pool; more than 95% is reclaimed in the ileum, and the ≈ 600 mg/day lost in stool is newly made.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,508px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day circulates from a 2.5 g pool; more than 95% is reclaimed in the ileum, and the ≈ 600 mg/day lost in stool is newly made. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which step belongs to the enterohepatic circulation?</h4>\n<p>Correct: bile salts move from the <b>intestinal lumen into blood on Na⁺–bile salt cotransporters</b> (terminal ileum). They are not taken up in the duodenum, and portal blood carries them from the ileum, not from the bile duct.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: distal ileum removed</h4>\n<p>Crohn disease or cancer surgery removes the terminal ileum (with or without the colon); the patient already gets B₁₂ injections. Bile acids escape in stool, so the liver <b>raises bile-acid synthesis</b>, and cholesterol synthesis to feed it, but cannot keep pace: the pool shrinks, too few micelles form, and the result is <b>steatorrhea</b> with fat-soluble-vitamin (e.g., vitamin A) deficiency, not toxicity. The pancreas is intact, so protein digestion is normal. Bile acids reaching an intact colon also trigger cAMP-driven Cl⁻ secretion, with Na⁺ and water following (<b>bile-acid diarrhea</b>), but this secretory diarrhea is a secondary effect, not the best answer.</p>\n<p>Normally no bile acids reach the colon. <b>Cholestyramine</b>, a resin that binds bile acids, stops bile-acid diarrhea but not the steatorrhea, since bound bile acids are lost too. The same resin lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol, because the liver uses cholesterol to replace the lost bile acids. Loss of the ileum also blocks vitamin B₁₂ uptake (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-51ec7bd1b3.png\" alt=\"Ileal resection. The solid line ends in steatorrhea, the best answer. Dashed boxes are side effects: bile-acid diarrhea (only while the colon remains) and B₁₂ loss (Chapter 9).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,513px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Ileal resection. The solid line ends in steatorrhea, the best answer. Dashed boxes are side effects: bile-acid diarrhea (only while the colon remains) and B₁₂ loss (Chapter 9). <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Acute pancreatitis: trypsin activated inside the gland; serum amylase and lipase ↑. Chronic pancreatitis: gland burnt out; enzymes, HCO₃⁻ and serum enzymes ↓. CF: enzymes made but trapped. Emulsion droplets (bile salts coating fat, before lipase) are not micelles (bile salts plus digestion products, after lipase). Bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, not the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The lecture slides use the word loosely: they call the emulsified fat droplets \"microdroplet micelles\" that lipase then digests. Strictly, micelles form only from lipase's products.</p>","s":"Bile flow and recycling","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Digestion, absorption and the absorptive surface","p":"<p>This chapter follows starch, sugars and protein from the lumen into the blood: how enzymes cut them into small pieces, and how the intestinal cells pull those pieces in.</p>\n<h4>Digestion and absorption</h4>\n<p><b>Digestion</b> is the chemical breakdown of food into molecules small enough to absorb. The enzymes come from saliva, the stomach (pepsin), the pancreas (amylase, lipase, trypsin and the other proteases) and the brush border of the small intestine. We need only <b>one amylase</b>: pancreatic amylase alone can digest all the starch, and salivary amylase is a bonus (Chapter 4). Fat likewise needs essentially one lipase, pancreatic lipase (Chapter 8). Protein is different: it needs a whole team of proteases, like a butcher who needs many knives for one cow.</p>\n<p><b>Absorption</b> is the movement of nutrients, water and electrolytes from the lumen into the blood. In the <b>cellular (transcellular) path</b>, a substance crosses the apical (luminal) membrane, passes through the cytoplasm, and leaves across the basolateral membrane into blood. It may cross each membrane by simple diffusion, on a transporter, or inside vesicles after binding a receptor (transcytosis). In the <b>paracellular path</b>, it slips between cells through the tight junctions and the lateral intercellular spaces into blood. Sugars and amino acids take the cellular path on transporters.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5ad141992d.jpg\" alt=\"Transcellular path: through SGLT1 or GLUT5, across the cell, out through GLUT2. Paracellular path: through the junction and the lateral space.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Transcellular path: through SGLT1 or GLUT5, across the cell, out through GLUT2. Paracellular path: through the junction and the lateral space. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-816d8d30dc.jpg\" alt=\"The ways across the lining. Through the cell (transcellular): simple diffusion and transcytotic vesicles (green) or a transporter (purple). Between the cells (paracellular): through the tight junction (orange).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The ways across the lining. Through the cell (transcellular): simple diffusion and transcytotic vesicles (green) or a transporter (purple). Between the cells (paracellular): through the tight junction (orange). <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The absorptive surface</h4>\n<p>The small-intestinal lining is built to give enzymes and transporters a huge surface. <b>Folds of Kerckring</b> (circular folds of the mucosa, named for a 17th-century Dutch anatomist) triple the area of a smooth tube. Fingerlike <b>villi</b> project from the folds and add another 10-fold. Villi are longest in the duodenum, where most digestion and absorption happen, and shortest in the terminal ileum. Each villus is covered by absorptive cells (<b>enterocytes</b>) with mucus-secreting goblet cells between them. Its core holds capillaries, which drain to the hepatic portal vein and the liver, and a central lymph vessel, the <b>lacteal</b>, which carries absorbed fat (Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ec707cb37e.jpg\" alt=\"Left: villi on the circular folds; the portal vein carries absorbed sugars and amino acids to the liver. Right: one villus with its capillary bed and central lacteal.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left: villi on the circular folds; the portal vein carries absorbed sugars and amino acids to the liver. Right: one villus with its capillary bed and central lacteal. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9956de01b0.jpg\" alt=\"The circular folds of Kerckring seen through an endoscope: ridges that ring the wall all the way around the lumen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The circular folds of Kerckring seen through an endoscope: ridges that ring the wall all the way around the lumen. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The apical membrane of each enterocyte is folded into tiny <b>microvilli</b>, which add another 20-fold. Under the microscope they look like bristles, so this surface is called the <b>brush border</b>. It carries the final digestive enzymes (disaccharidases, peptidases, enterokinase) and the nutrient transporters. Altogether 3 × 10 × 20 gives a <b>600-fold</b> increase: more than 200 m² (square meters) of absorptive surface.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-154140daea.jpg\" alt=\"Three magnifications: villi (left), the enterocytes covering one villus (middle), and the microvilli on their apical surface (right). The brush border is made of microvilli; villi are the much larger projections of the whole mucosa.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Three magnifications: villi (left), the enterocytes covering one villus (middle), and the microvilli on their apical surface (right). The brush border is made of microvilli; villi are the much larger projections of the whole mucosa. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2220f701e9.png\" alt=\"Each structure multiplies the area of the one before: folds ×3, villi ×10 more, microvilli ×20 more, for ×600 in all. The y-axis is logarithmic.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,501px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each structure multiplies the area of the one before: folds ×3, villi ×10 more, microvilli ×20 more, for ×600 in all. The y-axis is logarithmic. <small>· Graph redrawn from Digestion &amp; absorption discussion (2026), slides 23–24 (surface-area table: folds ×3, villi ×10, microvilli ×20)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-36fbaaeec6.jpg\" alt=\"The same steps as drawn in the 2026 discussion: the intestine as a smooth tube, then folds of Kerckring, villi and microvilli. The table gives the area each step reaches (×3, ×30, ×600).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">The same steps as drawn in the 2026 discussion: the intestine as a smooth tube, then folds of Kerckring, villi and microvilli. The table gives the area each step reaches (×3, ×30, ×600). <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Enterocytes are replaced every 3–5 days, one of the fastest turnovers in the body. In the duodenum the lining takes constant wear: acid arriving from the stomach, digestive enzymes and the mechanical rub of chyme all damage the cells, so crypt stem cells keep replacing them. Shed cells are one reason stool keeps forming even during fasting. Fast turnover also makes the mucosa vulnerable to radiation and chemotherapy. When villi are flattened, as in celiac disease, the surface collapses and absorption of everything suffers (Chapter 8).</p>","s":"The absorbing lining","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Digesting carbohydrates","p":"<p>Carbohydrates are about half of a typical diet. They arrive as starch (a long chain of glucose units), as the three food <b>disaccharides</b> (two-sugar molecules) lactose, sucrose and trehalose, and as a little free glucose and fructose. The key rule: <b>only monosaccharides</b> (single sugars) can be absorbed, namely glucose, galactose and fructose. Every larger carbohydrate must be cut down to these three.</p>\n<p><b>Starch → α-amylase.</b> Pancreatic α-amylase, secreted already active, cuts the interior α-1,4 bonds that join glucose units in a straight chain. It cannot cut the α-1,6 bonds at branch points. Its products are short pieces: <b>maltose</b> (2 glucoses), <b>maltotriose</b> (3 glucoses) and <b>α-limit dextrins</b> (short branched chains).</p>\n<p><b>Brush-border enzymes</b> finish the job on the cell surface. Maltase and sucrase both split maltose; maltase, sucrase and <b>α-dextrinase</b> (isomaltase) all trim maltotriose and α-limit dextrins, but only α-dextrinase cuts the α-1,6 branch bond (as in isomaltose). The end product is glucose.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-98ff6db288.jpg\" alt=\"Starch: α-amylase makes three short products, and brush-border enzymes turn each into glucose.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,376px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Starch: α-amylase makes three short products, and brush-border enzymes turn each into glucose. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The food disaccharides skip amylase, and each is hydrolyzed by its own enzyme. <b>Lactase</b>: lactose (galactose–glucose) → glucose + galactose. <b>Sucrase</b> (invertase): sucrose (table sugar, fruit; glucose and fructose joined by an α-glucosidic, β-fructosidic bond) → glucose + fructose. <b>Trehalase</b>: trehalose (glucose–glucose) → 2 glucose.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-869bb565df.jpg\" alt=\"Each food disaccharide has its own brush-border enzyme and yields two monosaccharides.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each food disaccharide has its own brush-border enzyme and yields two monosaccharides. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 376</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b0a630a927.jpg\" alt=\"Each substrate drawn as its sugar units, glucose (G), galactose (Ga) and fructose (F), next to the brush-border enzymes that split it. The row labelled &quot;Galactose&quot; is really lactose (galactose + glucose), split by lactase.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each substrate drawn as its sugar units, glucose (G), galactose (Ga) and fructose (F), next to the brush-border enzymes that split it. The row labelled \"Galactose\" is really lactose (galactose + glucose), split by lactase. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 and the sodium pump","p":"<p>Glucose and galactose enter the enterocyte through <b>SGLT1</b> (sodium–glucose linked transporter 1) in the brush border, by <b>secondary active transport</b>. Fructose enters through <b>GLUT5</b> (glucose transporter 5) by <b>facilitated diffusion</b>. All three leave across the basolateral membrane on <b>GLUT2</b>, by facilitated diffusion, into portal blood. None of these steps is affected by insulin.</p>\n<h4>How SGLT1 pulls glucose uphill</h4>\n<p>Glucose often has to enter against its gradient, because the enterocyte already holds more glucose than the lumen. The energy arrives in two steps. First, the <b>Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase</b> (sodium–potassium pump) on the basolateral membrane spends ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency). It pushes 3 Na⁺ (sodium ions) out into blood and brings 2 K⁺ (potassium ions) in. This keeps Na⁺ inside the cell low and the cell interior negative. So luminal Na⁺ has a steep downhill path into the cell, both chemical (more Na⁺ outside) and electrical (the negative interior attracts it).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-00c46fd389.jpg\" alt=\"One enterocyte. Glucose and galactose enter with Na⁺ on SGLT1, fructose enters alone on GLUT5, and all three leave on GLUT2. The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase (top right) is the energy source for SGLT1.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One enterocyte. Glucose and galactose enter with Na⁺ on SGLT1, fructose enters alone on GLUT5, and all three leave on GLUT2. The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase (top right) is the energy source for SGLT1. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Second, SGLT1 lets that Na⁺ in only together with sugar: <b>2 Na⁺ with each glucose</b> (or galactose). As the two Na⁺ run downhill, their energy drags glucose in uphill. SGLT1 itself uses no ATP. <b>Primary active</b> transport spends ATP directly, as the pump does; <b>secondary active</b> transport spends a gradient that a primary pump built. Think of the pump as a ski lift hauling Na⁺ up the slope, and SGLT1 as the run where Na⁺ skis down with glucose as its partner. If the pump stops, the gradient runs down and glucose uptake stops.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-65ff8efc96.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s ski run: on SGLT1, Na⁺ skis down its gradient and takes glucose along as its partner.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The lecture's ski run: on SGLT1, Na⁺ skis down its gradient and takes glucose along as its partner. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Glucose then piles up inside the cell and leaves through GLUT2, down its own gradient, into blood. The Na⁺ that came in is pumped out by the ATPase. Cl⁻ (chloride) follows the absorbed Na⁺ to balance charge, and <b>water follows</b> the absorbed solute by osmosis. This coupling, one glucose bringing two Na⁺ and then water, is why oral rehydration solution contains both glucose and salt (Chapter 9). The kidney's early proximal tubule uses the same Na⁺-coupled design.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-09820d6839.jpg\" alt=\"Glucose enters with 2 Na⁺ (red dots) on the apical symporter, SGLT1, and leaves on GLUT2 down its gradient. On the blood side the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ (green) in, keeping cell Na⁺ low.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">Glucose enters with 2 Na⁺ (red dots) on the apical symporter, SGLT1, and leaves on GLUT2 down its gradient. On the blood side the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ (green) in, keeping cell Na⁺ low. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5609dd7b30.png\" alt=\"The energy chain behind glucose absorption: the pump builds the Na⁺ gradient, SGLT1 spends it, GLUT2 lets glucose out, and water follows the absorbed solute.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,497px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The energy chain behind glucose absorption: the pump builds the Na⁺ gradient, SGLT1 spends it, GLUT2 lets glucose out, and water follows the absorbed solute. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Fructose and GLUT2: why both carriers","p":"<h4>Fructose: just a door</h4>\n<p>Fructose has no Na⁺ partner. GLUT5 is simply a door: a carrier protein that speeds fructose across the membrane but adds no energy. That is what facilitated diffusion means: carrier-assisted, but downhill only. So fructose, unlike glucose and galactose, <b>cannot be absorbed against its gradient</b>. It leaves on GLUT2, also downhill. Without any carrier, a sugar would have to cross by unassisted (simple) diffusion through the lipid membrane, which is far too slow to matter.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cc43e2d0c0.jpg\" alt=\"Fructose just gets a door: GLUT5 on the lumen side lets it in and GLUT2 on the blood side lets it out. No pump and no Na⁺, so it can only move downhill.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Fructose just gets a door: GLUT5 on the lumen side lets it in and GLUT2 on the blood side lets it out. No pump and no Na⁺, so it can only move downhill. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: why the enterocyte needs both SGLT1 and GLUT2</h4>\n<p>Glucose absorption needs both because of a division of labor: <b>SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against its gradient</b>, and <b>GLUT2 carries it out into blood with its gradient</b>. Wrong choices swap the jobs or say GLUT2 brings fructose in; fructose enters on GLUT5. GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane, not in the brush border.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8d05c06e55.jpg\" alt=\"Two doors in, one door out: glucose and galactose enter on SGLT1 with Na⁺, fructose on GLUT5, and all three leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Two doors in, one door out: glucose and galactose enter on SGLT1 with Na⁺, fructose on GLUT5, and all three leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral surface. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Lactase deficiency and α-glucosidase inhibitors","p":"<p><b>Lactase deficiency</b> (lactose intolerance) is the commonest failure of carbohydrate digestion. Brush-border lactase is low or absent, often because its activity fades after childhood (lactase non-persistence). Milk lactose is then not split into glucose and galactose. A disaccharide has no transporter, so lactose cannot be absorbed anywhere in the gut. It stays in the lumen, holds water there and feeds colon bacteria. The result is <b>osmotic diarrhea</b>, <b>flatus</b>, bloating and cramping after milk products (mechanism in Chapter 9). Only lactase is missing; the other brush-border enzymes work normally. Treatment: stop milk products, or take lactase tablets with them.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5bf92ada3c.jpg\" alt=\"Lactase splits lactose, with water, into glucose and galactose, two absorbable monosaccharides.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lactase splits lactose, with water, into glucose and galactose, two absorbable monosaccharides. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-aea4a5b19e.png\" alt=\"Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: lactose intolerance and frozen yogurt</h4>\n<p>A lactose-intolerant patient stops milk and cheese but keeps eating frozen yogurt. Expect <b>resolution of all symptoms in 1–2 days</b>. The symptoms are purely osmotic and fermentative, so they stop once the lactose load clears; no damaged mucosa needs 1–2 months to heal. Yogurt is tolerated because its live bacterial cultures carry their own lactase and digest much of its lactose.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why no weight loss</h4>\n<p>Lactose intolerance does not cause weight loss because <b>fat, protein and the other disaccharides are still absorbed</b>; only lactose is lost. \"Galactose has no caloric content\" is wrong: galactose yields as much energy as glucose.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what does not happen in lactose intolerance</h4>\n<p>Lactase non-persistence at the jejunal brush border, bloating and pain, and bacterial breakdown of lactose into osmotically active solutes all occur. What does <b>not</b> happen is absorption of undigested lactose by ileal cells: the intestine absorbs only monosaccharides.</p>\n<h4>α-Glucosidase inhibitors</h4>\n<p><b>Acarbose</b> and <b>miglitol</b> are diabetes drugs that inhibit the brush-border <b>α-glucosidases</b>, the maltase-like enzymes that split maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins and sucrose. Less glucose is freed and absorbed after a meal, so blood glucose rises less. But the undigested carbohydrate travels on to the colon, exactly like lactose in lactase deficiency, where it pulls water and feeds bacteria.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why acarbose and miglitol failed</h4>\n<p>These drugs do lower post-meal glucose, so \"no decrease in serum glucose\" is wrong. Patients abandon them because of <b>flatus and diarrhea</b>, the predictable result of drug-induced carbohydrate malabsorption.</p>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Protein digestion begins: pepsin","p":"<p>Protein can be absorbed as <b>amino acids, dipeptides and tripeptides</b>, so digestion only has to reach chains of 1–3 amino acids, not single units as for sugars. Digestion starts in the stomach with pepsin and is finished in the small intestine by pancreatic and brush-border proteases. Absorption is nearly complete in the duodenum and proximal jejunum. Not all of this protein is food: about 40% is the body's own (<b>autodigestion</b>), from pancreatic enzymes, which digest themselves and each other, and from shed intestinal cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-74ef5d3050.jpg\" alt=\"Top: pepsin in the stomach leaves amino acids and oligopeptides. Bottom: in the small intestine the pancreatic proteases make amino acids, di- and tripeptides and oligopeptides, and brush-border peptidases cut the oligopeptides down to absorbable size.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top: pepsin in the stomach leaves amino acids and oligopeptides. Bottom: in the small intestine the pancreatic proteases make amino acids, di- and tripeptides and oligopeptides, and brush-border peptidases cut the oligopeptides down to absorbable size. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Endopeptidases and exopeptidases</h4>\n<p><b>Endopeptidases</b> cut peptide bonds in the middle of a chain: pepsin, trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase. <b>Exopeptidases</b> break off one amino acid at a time from the end; the pancreatic ones, <b>carboxypeptidases A and B</b>, work from the C-terminal (carboxyl) end. Each enzyme prefers its own cutting site, so together they work fast. Trypsin cuts after the basic amino acids lysine (Lys) and arginine (Arg). Chymotrypsin cuts after aromatic ones: phenylalanine (Phe), tyrosine (Tyr), tryptophan (Trp). Elastase cuts after small neutral ones: alanine (Ala), glycine (Gly), serine (Ser). Carboxypeptidase B removes basic C-terminal residues; carboxypeptidase A removes most others (neutral and aromatic).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e6fbd0317d.png\" alt=\"Endopeptidases cut inside the chain and make many new ends; exopeptidases then nibble single amino acids off each C-terminal end.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,436px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Endopeptidases cut inside the chain and make many new ends; exopeptidases then nibble single amino acids off each C-terminal end. <small>· Graph redrawn from Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 44, and Costanzo p. 377 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Enzyme (zymogen)</th><th>Class</th><th>Cuts after</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Trypsin (trypsinogen)</td><td>endo</td><td>Lys, Arg (basic)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chymotrypsin (chymotrypsinogen)</td><td>endo</td><td>Phe, Tyr, Trp (aromatic)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Elastase (proelastase)</td><td>endo</td><td>Ala, Gly, Ser (small)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carboxypeptidase A (pro-A)</td><td>exo, C-end</td><td>neutral, aromatic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carboxypeptidase B (pro-B)</td><td>exo, C-end</td><td>Lys, Arg (basic)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Pepsin is not essential</h4>\n<p>Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which acid converts to <b>pepsin</b> (Chapter 5). Pepsin works only at pH 1–3 (strong acid) and is denatured above pH 5, so it stops in the duodenum once pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) neutralizes the acid. Pepsin starts protein digestion but is <b>not essential</b>. After total gastrectomy, or with no acid to activate pepsinogen, protein digestion and absorption stay normal, because pancreatic and brush-border proteases can do the whole job.</p>","s":"Digesting proteins","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Pancreatic proteases and the trypsin cascade","p":"<h4>Pancreatic proteases</h4>\n<p>The pancreas secretes its five proteases as inactive <b>zymogens</b>: trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase and procarboxypeptidases A and B. In the duodenal lumen, brush-border <b>enterokinase</b> turns trypsinogen into <b>trypsin</b>, and trypsin activates all the others, including more trypsinogen. Trypsin is the most abundant pancreatic protease. Activation and its diseases belong to Chapter 6. In chronic pancreatitis and cystic fibrosis the proteases never work in the lumen, so protein is poorly digested and lost in stool. <b>Acute pancreatitis</b> (alcohol, trauma, a gallstone blocking the duct) is premature trypsin activation inside the gland.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4a43d38193.jpg\" alt=\"Top: in the stomach, low pH turns pepsinogen into pepsin. Bottom: in the small intestine, brush-border enterokinase makes the first trypsin, and trypsin then activates all five zymogens, including more trypsinogen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Top: in the stomach, low pH turns pepsinogen into pepsin. Bottom: in the small intestine, brush-border enterokinase makes the first trypsin, and trypsin then activates all five zymogens, including more trypsinogen. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pancreatic proteases leave free amino acids, dipeptides, tripeptides and longer <b>oligopeptides</b>, which are too big to absorb. <b>Brush-border peptidases</b> (amino-oligopeptidases and dipeptidases) cut oligopeptides into amino acids, dipeptides and tripeptides. The last step is <b>intracellular</b>: most di- and tripeptides are absorbed whole and split into amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases inside the enterocyte.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-503d38c59c.jpg\" alt=\"Oligopeptides are trimmed by brush-border peptidases; small peptides are split by intracellular peptidases; free amino acids (neutral, basic, acidic) enter on carriers; all leave as amino acids into the capillary.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Oligopeptides are trimmed by brush-border peptidases; small peptides are split by intracellular peptidases; free amino acids (neutral, basic, acidic) enter on carriers; all leave as amino acids into the capillary. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 54</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: one enzyme missing, all missing</h4>\n<p>Deficiency of <b>trypsin</b> looks like deficiency of every pancreatic protease, because trypsin activates all the zymogens. Missing chymotrypsin, elastase or one carboxypeptidase removes only that enzyme; and chymotrypsin and elastase never activate trypsin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-663bcccc09.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic juice arrives with amylase and lipase already active but the proteases inactive. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal lining activates trypsin, and trypsin activates the other proenzymes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Pancreatic juice arrives with amylase and lipase already active but the proteases inactive. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal lining activates trypsin, and trypsin activates the other proenzymes. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which enzyme is an exopeptidase?</h4>\n<p><b>Carboxypeptidase B</b> (like A) clips single amino acids from the C-terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase are endopeptidases. Enterokinase is the tempting wrong answer: it is the brush-border activator of trypsinogen, not a digestive exopeptidase.</p>","s":"Digesting proteins","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Amino acids with Na⁺, peptides with H⁺","p":"<p>Protein absorption follows the carbohydrate pattern. Free <b>L-amino acids</b> (the natural form) enter on <b>Na⁺–amino acid cotransporters</b>: secondary active transport, again powered by the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase. There are four separate cotransporters, one each for <b>neutral, acidic, basic and imino</b> amino acids (imino: proline-type). Amino acids leave across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion, again on separate carriers, into portal blood.</p>\n<p>Most protein, though, is absorbed as <b>dipeptides and tripeptides</b>, which enter more easily than free amino acids. They ride an <b>H⁺-coupled cotransporter</b> (PepT1, peptide transporter 1) in the apical membrane. The H⁺ (hydrogen ion) gradient it uses comes from an apical Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger, which itself runs on the Na⁺ gradient. So peptide uptake is secondary active transport too. Inside the cell, cytoplasmic peptidases split most of them into amino acids; a few cross into blood intact. In short, Na⁺ drives sugars and amino acids, while H⁺ drives small peptides.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ff2f25190b.jpg\" alt=\"Amino acids enter with Na⁺; dipeptides and tripeptides enter with H⁺ and are mostly split by peptidase inside the cell (dotted arrows). Amino acids leave on basolateral carriers.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,510px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Amino acids enter with Na⁺; dipeptides and tripeptides enter with H⁺ and are mostly split by peptidase inside the cell (dotted arrows). Amino acids leave on basolateral carriers. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-897dcf6282.jpg\" alt=\"The route colour-coded: brush-border peptidases (1) cut large peptides; amino acids enter on Na⁺-coupled carriers (2, orange path) and small peptides on the H⁺-coupled carrier (3, green path); peptidases inside the cell (4) split most peptides; amino acids (5) and a few peptides (6) leave into blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The route colour-coded: brush-border peptidases (1) cut large peptides; amino acids enter on Na⁺-coupled carriers (2, orange path) and small peptides on the H⁺-coupled carrier (3, green path); peptidases inside the cell (4) split most peptides; amino acids (5) and a few peptides (6) leave into blood. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Absorbing proteins","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Cystinuria; carbohydrates and proteins compared","p":"<h4>Cystinuria</h4>\n<p><b>Cystinuria</b> is a genetic absence of the Na⁺-coupled transporter for the <b>basic (dibasic) amino acids</b>, in both the small intestine and the kidney. Remember <b>COAL</b>: cystine, ornithine, arginine, lysine. In the gut these amino acids are not absorbed and are lost in feces, yet the patient has no bowel symptoms, because they still enter as dipeptides and tripeptides. In the kidney they are not reabsorbed and spill into urine. Cystine is poorly soluble, so it forms <b>kidney stones</b>. These stones are only faintly radiopaque and are easily missed on a plain X-ray. Cystine dissolves better in alkaline urine, so <b>alkalinizing the urine</b> helps the stones resolve.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Carbohydrates</th><th>Proteins</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Lumen enzymes</td><td>α-amylase</td><td>pepsin; trypsin and other pancreatic proteases</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brush border</td><td>maltase, sucrase, α-dextrinase, lactase, trehalase</td><td>peptidases; enterokinase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Absorbed as</td><td>monosaccharides only</td><td>amino acids, di- and tripeptides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apical entry</td><td>SGLT1 (Na⁺); GLUT5 (fructose)</td><td>4 Na⁺–amino acid carriers; PepT1 (H⁺)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basolateral exit</td><td>GLUT2</td><td>facilitated diffusion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Classic defect</td><td>lactase deficiency</td><td>cystinuria; pancreatic insufficiency</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>GLUT5 brings fructose in; GLUT2 takes all three sugars out. SGLT1 uses no ATP itself: the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase does. Enterokinase (brush border) activates trypsinogen; trypsin activates everything else. Trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase are endopeptidases; only the carboxypeptidases are exopeptidases. Carbohydrates are absorbed only as monosaccharides, proteins also as di- and tripeptides. Na⁺ drives glucose, galactose and amino acids; H⁺ drives small peptides.</p>","s":"Absorbing proteins","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Why fat is hard, the stomach, and emulsification","p":"<p>Fat is the hardest nutrient to absorb because it does not dissolve in water. This chapter follows dietary fat from the stomach to the lymph, then shows how each broken step causes fatty stools and loss of vitamins A, D, E and K.</p>\n<p>Dietary lipids are <b>triglycerides</b> (TG), <b>cholesterol</b> (much of it as cholesterol ester) and <b>phospholipids</b>. All three are too big to enter the intestinal cell, and none dissolves in the watery gut contents. So the gut does four jobs: it breaks big fat drops into small ones, cuts each molecule into smaller pieces, ferries the pieces to the cell surface, and rebuilds them inside the cell for export. It is like moving house: pack the apartment into small boxes, truck them over, unpack in the new house. Almost every failure along the way ends the same way: fat in the stool.</p>\n<h4>Digestion starts in the stomach</h4>\n<p>The stomach churns and mixes fat into small droplets, kept apart by dietary proteins because no bile is present yet. Lingual lipase (Chapter 4) and <b>gastric lipase</b> from chief cells digest only about <b>10%</b> of dietary fat, to glycerol and free fatty acids (FFA). The stomach's larger contribution is to empty fat <b>slowly</b>. CCK (cholecystokinin), released when fat reaches the duodenum, slows emptying (Chapter 3), which gives pancreatic enzymes time to work.</p>\n<h4>Emulsification by bile salts</h4>\n<p><b>Bile salts</b>, bile acids conjugated with glycine or taurine (Chapter 6), stay charged at intestinal pH, so they remain in the lumen instead of leaking into cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-35c139f0cf.jpg\" alt=\"Cholic acid (a bile acid) joined to glycine makes glycocholic acid, a bile salt. The –OH groups and the charged COO⁻ end mix with water; the rest of the ring system mixes with fat.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Cholic acid (a bile acid) joined to glycine makes glycocholic acid, a bile salt. The –OH groups and the charged COO⁻ end mix with water; the rest of the ring system mixes with fat. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>They are <b>amphipathic</b>: one face mixes with water, the other with fat. In the duodenum they coat fat drops, and their negative charges repel each other, so the drops stay apart instead of clumping back together. This is <b>emulsification</b>. It digests nothing; it multiplies the surface that enzymes can reach.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cd4371cabb.jpg\" alt=\"Bile breaks clumped fat into separated droplets; enzymes then cut the droplets into fatty acids.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,340px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile breaks clumped fat into separated droplets; enzymes then cut the droplets into fatty acids. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bd607925f0.jpg\" alt=\"Bile salts (green) coat a big fat drop and break it into many small droplets (middle), which gives lipase far more surface. After digestion, the products end up in tiny micelles (right).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile salts (green) coat a big fat drop and break it into many small droplets (middle), which gives lipase far more surface. After digestion, the products end up in tiny micelles (right). <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Breaking fat down","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"The pancreatic enzymes","p":"<p>Most fat digestion happens in the small intestine. The pancreas supplies <b>pancreatic lipase</b>, its helper <b>colipase</b>, <b>cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> and <b>phospholipase A₂</b>. Each enzyme strips fatty acids off a big lipid, so the pieces become small enough to enter the cell.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ffc9b1f190.png\" alt=\"Each pancreatic enzyme cuts fatty acids off one kind of dietary lipid. The products on the right, plus free fatty acids (FFA), are what micelles carry.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,472px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each pancreatic enzyme cuts fatty acids off one kind of dietary lipid. The products on the right, plus free fatty acids (FFA), are what micelles carry. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Pancreatic lipase</h4>\n<p><b>Pancreatic lipase</b> is secreted already <b>active</b>. It cuts the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3 of the glycerol backbone, so each triglyceride yields two free fatty acids and one <b>2-monoglyceride</b> (the middle fatty acid stays on). Lipase works best at pH 6–8. Below about pH 5 acid inactivates it, and below pH 3 it is destroyed (\"acid cooks the lipase\"). So lipase depends on pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) to neutralize gastric acid. <b>Orlistat</b>, a weight-loss drug, blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase, so undigested fat passes into the stool.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a928e1fa3d.jpg\" alt=\"Lipase takes the two outer fatty acids (red chains) off the glycerol backbone (blue). The middle chain stays on, so one monoglyceride and two free fatty acids result.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lipase takes the two outer fatty acids (red chains) off the glycerol backbone (blue). The middle chain stays on, so one monoglyceride and two free fatty acids result. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ff68af01b2.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic lipase adds water across the ester bonds at positions 1 and 3, releasing two &quot;free&quot; fatty acids. The fatty acid at position 2 stays on glycerol as a 2-monoglyceride.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Pancreatic lipase adds water across the ester bonds at positions 1 and 3, releasing two \"free\" fatty acids. The fatty acid at position 2 stays on glycerol as a 2-monoglyceride. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why lipase needs colipase</h4>\n<p>Here is the catch. Lipase must sit on the surface of a fat droplet to reach its substrate. That surface is crowded with bile salts, and bile salts <b>push lipase off</b> the oil–water interface. So bile salts, which make digestion possible, also block the enzyme. <b>Colipase</b> solves this. It binds lipase and anchors it to the droplet, shoving the bile salts aside like bodyguards clearing a path. With colipase in place, lipase can touch the fat and do its job.</p>\n<p>Colipase is secreted inactive, as <b>procolipase</b> (101 amino acids). In the lumen, <b>trypsin</b> cuts off the first five residues, and the rest (residues 6–101) is active colipase. The small cut-off peptide is <b>enterostatin</b>, which <b>decreases hunger and fat intake</b>; it works whether given peripherally or into the brain. So the same cut that switches on fat digestion also sends a \"stop eating fat\" signal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cebf5d0bba.png\" alt=\"Trypsin splits procolipase into two useful pieces: colipase, which anchors lipase, and enterostatin, a satiety signal.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Trypsin splits procolipase into two useful pieces: colipase, which anchors lipase, and enterostatin, a satiety signal. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-69d0336279.jpg\" alt=\"The same cut on the lecture slide: trypsin cuts procolipase after residue 5. The short green piece (enterostatin, residues 1–5) signals to regulate fat intake; the long blue piece (colipase, residues 6–101) lets lipase digest triglyceride.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The same cut on the lecture slide: trypsin cuts procolipase after residue 5. The short green piece (enterostatin, residues 1–5) signals to regulate fat intake; the long blue piece (colipase, residues 6–101) lets lipase digest triglyceride. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: what lowers hunger?</h4>\n<p>The product that decreases the sense of hunger is <b>enterostatin</b>, cut from procolipase by trypsin. Amylase, lysolecithin, ileal bile-salt reabsorption and duodenal acid neutralization are real digestive events, but none is a satiety signal.</p>\n<h4>Cholesterol ester hydrolase</h4>\n<p><b>Cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> is secreted <b>active</b>. It pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol ester, giving free cholesterol plus a fatty acid, small enough to absorb. It can also split triglyceride ester bonds, releasing glycerol.</p>\n<h4>Phospholipase A₂</h4>\n<p>A phospholipid is glycerol with fatty acids at positions 1 and 2 and a phosphate head group at position 3. <b>Phospholipase A₂</b> (PLA₂) removes only the fatty acid at <b>position 2</b>, hence its name. What is left is a lysophospholipid; from lecithin (phosphatidylcholine), the main dietary phospholipid, it is <b>lysolecithin</b> (1-acylglycerophosphorylcholine). PLA₂ is secreted as an inactive proenzyme and is <b>activated by trypsin</b>. Other phospholipases cut elsewhere: A₁ at position 1, C on the glycerol side of the phosphate, D on the head-group side.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-91843ec0ad.jpg\" alt=\"Phospholipase A₂ adds water at the position-2 ester bond (red). The fatty acid there (R₂) leaves, and a lysophospholipid remains.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Phospholipase A₂ adds water at the position-2 ester bond (red). The fatty acid there (R₂) leaves, and a lysophospholipid remains. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>PLA₂ also works inside cells. It frees arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids; cyclo-oxygenase turns this into prostaglandins and lipoxygenase into leukotrienes. Mechanical strain stimulates this PLA₂, and lipocortin inhibits it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-fef17a75d8.jpg\" alt=\"Dashed arrows mark where each phospholipase cuts. Phospholipase A₂ cuts at position 2 (bold arrow), leaving lysolecithin plus a free fatty acid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Dashed arrows mark where each phospholipase cuts. Phospholipase A₂ cuts at position 2 (bold arrow), leaving lysolecithin plus a free fatty acid. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: is PLA₂ a zymogen?</h4>\n<p>Yes: PLA₂ is secreted inactive and <b>trypsin</b> activates it. The tempting answer is enterokinase, but enterokinase (a brush-border enzyme) activates only trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates the other zymogens (Chapter 6). PLA₂ does not simply wait for a basic pH.</p>","s":"Breaking fat down","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Micelles carry the products to the cell","p":"<p>The final digestion products are monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, lysolecithin and glycerol. All except glycerol are still insoluble in water. Next to the brush border lies the <b>unstirred water layer</b>, a thin film of still water that fat cannot cross on its own. Bile salts solve this second problem by forming <b>mixed micelles</b>. A micelle is a tiny disk, about 50 Å across (Å = angstrom, 10<sup>−10</sup> m). Its surface is a <b>monolayer</b> of bile salts and phospholipids, fat-loving side in, water-loving side out. Its core holds <b>monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, lysolecithin and the fat-soluble vitamins</b>. Glycerol dissolves in water and needs no micelle.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-195b8ec6f2.jpg\" alt=\"Two views of a micelle. Left: water-loving head groups cover the surface and fat-loving tails point inward. Right, in cross-section: the heads (green) face the watery lumen and the tails (yellow) fill the core, where the fat products ride.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Two views of a micelle. Left: water-loving head groups cover the surface and fat-loving tails point inward. Right, in cross-section: the heads (green) face the watery lumen and the tails (yellow) fill the core, where the fat products ride. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-302c719f37.jpg\" alt=\"A micelle is a single layer (monolayer) of amphipathic molecules around a fat core. A liposome and a cell membrane are bilayers.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A micelle is a single layer (monolayer) of amphipathic molecules around a fat core. A liposome and a cell membrane are bilayers. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Micelles diffuse through the unstirred layer to the brush border. There the lipids leave the micelle and enter the cell by <b>simple diffusion</b> down their concentration gradients, dissolving straight through the lipid membrane. No carrier and no energy are needed. The micelle itself does not enter: the <b>bile salts stay behind</b> in the lumen, pick up more lipid, and are reabsorbed later in the terminal ileum (Chapter 6). Most fat is absorbed by the <b>mid-jejunum</b>, so bile salts finish their work long before the ileum reclaims them.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: how is fat absorbed?</h4>\n<p>The main mechanism for lipid absorption is <b>diffusion</b>. SGLT1 (sodium–glucose linked transporter 1), GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2), secondary active transport and H⁺ (hydrogen ion) exchange move sugars and peptides (Chapter 7). Lipid products are fat-soluble, so they simply dissolve through the membrane.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-43e82bd8ad.jpg\" alt=\"Lipase products join micelles; in the unstirred layer next to the cells the micelles release their lipids, which diffuse into the epithelial cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,387px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lipase products join micelles; in the unstirred layer next to the cells the micelles release their lipids, which diffuse into the epithelial cells. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Absorbing fat","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Re-esterification and chylomicrons","p":"<p>Inside the enterocyte, the pieces are reassembled on the <b>smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b> (ER): monoglyceride + 2 FFA → triglyceride; cholesterol + FFA → cholesterol ester; lysolecithin + FFA → phospholipid. This <b>re-esterification</b> is the unpacking in the new house. It also keeps free products scarce inside the cell, so diffusion inward continues.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-db5aef540a.png\" alt=\"Inside the enterocyte: the digestion products diffuse in, are rebuilt in the smooth ER, packed into chylomicrons with apoB-48, and leave into the lacteal. FFA = free fatty acid; ER = endoplasmic reticulum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Inside the enterocyte: the digestion products diffuse in, are rebuilt in the smooth ER, packed into chylomicrons with apoB-48, and leave into the lacteal. FFA = free fatty acid; ER = endoplasmic reticulum. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The rebuilt lipids are packed into <b>chylomicrons</b>, the moving trucks: everything is inside, but nothing can be used until it is unloaded later. A chylomicron is about 1,000 Å across, twenty times a micelle. Its core holds triglycerides and cholesterol esters. Its covering is <b>80% phospholipid and 20% apoprotein</b>. Overall it is triglycerides 85–92%, phospholipids 6–12%, cholesterol 1–3% and protein 1–2%. The essential apoprotein is <b>apoB-48</b> (apoprotein B-48), made in the intestinal cell itself; apoproteins A, C-II, C-III and E also sit on the surface. <b>No apoB, no absorption.</b></p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-88acc31295.jpg\" alt=\"A chylomicron: a core of triglycerides and cholesteryl esters, a shell of phospholipids and cholesterol, and apoproteins (B-48, C-II, C-III) on the surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A chylomicron: a core of triglycerides and cholesteryl esters, a shell of phospholipids and cholesterol, and apoproteins (B-48, C-II, C-III) on the surface. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-78a9df4cd0.png\" alt=\"Composition of the lipoproteins (bars are approximate readings of the original graph). The chylomicron is mostly triglyceride with the least protein; HDL is about half protein. VLDL, LDL, HDL = very-low-, low- and high-density lipoprotein.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Composition of the lipoproteins (bars are approximate readings of the original graph). The chylomicron is mostly triglyceride with the least protein; HDL is about half protein. VLDL, LDL, HDL = very-low-, low- and high-density lipoprotein. <small>· Graph redrawn from Lipids lecture, slide 28 (composition of chylomicron, VLDL, LDL, HDL; values read off the bar graph)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The Golgi packs chylomicrons into secretory vesicles, which fuse with the basolateral membrane (<b>exocytosis</b>). Sugars and amino acids enter the villus blood capillaries, but chylomicrons are far too large for them. Instead they squeeze between the endothelial cells of the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the center of each villus. Lymph carries them to the cisterna chyli and up the <b>thoracic duct</b> (first described in humans by Thomas Bartholin), which empties into the great veins of the neck. So absorbed long-chain fat <b>bypasses the liver</b> and reaches the systemic blood first.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-45040c985f.jpg\" alt=\"Lymph (green) collects in the cisterna chyli in the abdomen and climbs the thoracic duct (white arrow) to empty into the great veins at the base of the neck (blue). This is the road every chylomicron takes to the blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lymph (green) collects in the cisterna chyli in the abdomen and climbs the thoracic duct (white arrow) to empty into the great veins at the base of the neck (blue). This is the road every chylomicron takes to the blood. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Short- and medium-chain fatty acids</b> (fewer than about 12 carbons) behave differently. They are water-soluble enough to skip re-esterification and pass straight from the enterocyte into <b>portal blood</b>, as glycerol does. That is why medium-chain fat is given to patients who cannot absorb ordinary fat.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1219c24997.jpg\" alt=\"The whole route in five numbered steps: (1) micelles hold the products in the lumen; (2) the lipids diffuse into the cell while bile salts stay behind; (3) re-esterification; (4) packing with apo B into chylomicrons; (5) exocytosis into lymph. Chol E = cholesterol ester; MG = monoglyceride; LysoPL = lysolecithin; PL = phospholipid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The whole route in five numbered steps: (1) micelles hold the products in the lumen; (2) the lipids diffuse into the cell while bile salts stay behind; (3) re-esterification; (4) packing with apo B into chylomicrons; (5) exocytosis into lymph. Chol E = cholesterol ester; MG = monoglyceride; LysoPL = lysolecithin; PL = phospholipid. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: where is fat re-esterified?</h4>\n<p>Re-esterification happens in the <b>intestinal epithelial cells</b>. Micelles only ferry digestion products, and lacteals and the thoracic duct only carry finished chylomicrons; villous capillaries never receive them.</p>\n<h4>Abetalipoproteinemia</h4>\n<p>In this rare autosomal recessive disease no <b>apoB</b>-containing lipoproteins are made: the protein that loads lipid onto apoB, MTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein), is missing. So enterocytes <b>cannot package chylomicrons</b> or export them into lymph. Fat is digested and taken up, but it piles up inside the intestinal cells, causing steatorrhea and poor absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Apolipoproteins, which bind lipids to form lipoproteins, are made mainly in the liver and partly in the small intestine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1e291491e5.jpg\" alt=\"The chylomicron coat with its apoproteins in colour. ApoB (magenta) is the one the intestinal cell must make; without it no chylomicron forms, and fat stays trapped in the cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The chylomicron coat with its apoproteins in colour. ApoB (magenta) is the one the intestinal cell must make; without it no chylomicron forms, and fat stays trapped in the cell. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: abetalipoproteinemia</h4>\n<p>The defining defect: intestinal cells <b>unable to package chylomicrons</b>. Fat and vitamin absorption are not normal, apoproteins are missing, not overexpressed, and apoB coats the chylomicron surface rather than enclosing lipids in the lumen.</p>","s":"Absorbing fat","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"No lipase, or lipase killed by acid","p":"<p><b>Steatorrhea</b> is fat in the stool: oily, greasy, floating, foul-smelling stools. Normally almost all dietary fat is absorbed, so fat in stool means a broken step. Because vitamins A, D, E and K ride the same route, steatorrhea usually brings their deficiency too. The causes group by the step they break.</p>\n<h4>No lipase, or lipase killed by acid</h4>\n<p><b>No lipase, no lipid absorption.</b> In pancreatic insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis; Chapter 6) the pancreas secretes too little lipase, colipase, cholesterol ester hydrolase and PLA₂. Undigested triglycerides cannot be absorbed, so they leave in the stool. Lipase also fails when duodenal contents stay acidic: without enough HCO₃⁻, or with too much acid arriving, pH falls below 5 and lipase is inactivated. The classic cause is <b>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome</b>, a gastrin-secreting tumor with huge acid output (Chapter 5). Its acid also damages the mucosa, shrinking the absorptive surface, and makes bile salts un-ionized, so they are absorbed too early.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5ae6c8016d.png\" alt=\"The whole route of dietary fat, top to bottom, and where each disease breaks it (dashed boxes); glycerol and short/medium-chain fatty acids take a shortcut to portal blood. TG = triglyceride; PL = phospholipid; MG = monoglyceride; FFA = free fatty acid; PLA₂ = phospholipase A₂; ER = endoplasmic reticulum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The whole route of dietary fat, top to bottom, and where each disease breaks it (dashed boxes); glycerol and short/medium-chain fatty acids take a shortcut to portal blood. TG = triglyceride; PL = phospholipid; MG = monoglyceride; FFA = free fatty acid; PLA₂ = phospholipase A₂; ER = endoplasmic reticulum. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: ulcers, high gastrin and oily stools</h4>\n<p>Greasy, floating, foul-smelling diarrhea with multiple distal duodenal ulcers, high acid and high gastrin is Zollinger–Ellison syndrome. The fat in the stool comes from <b>acid inactivation of lipase</b>. The large volume of gastric juice adds watery diarrhea, but oily stool points to lipase; amylase and trypsinogen activation are not the problem, and lipids are not secreted into the gut.</p>","s":"Steatorrhea: when a step fails","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Too few bile salts","p":"<h4>Too few bile salts</h4>\n<p><b>No bile = minimal fat absorbed</b>, because without bile salts there is no emulsification and no micelles. Bile-duct obstruction stops delivery. The pool also depends on recycling: more than 95% of bile salts return from the <b>terminal ileum</b> (Chapter 6). So <b>ileal resection</b> or ileal disease (for example, Crohn disease) shrinks the pool and causes steatorrhea, even though the liver raises bile-acid synthesis (worked example in Chapter 6). Losing the ileum also costs vitamin B₁₂ absorption (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-145767fe5c.jpg\" alt=\"The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day is secreted into the duodenum, and more than 95% returns from the ileum in portal blood. Only the small fecal loss (0.4 g/day) is replaced by new synthesis, so losing the ileum drains the pool faster than the liver can refill it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day is secreted into the duodenum, and more than 95% returns from the ileum in portal blood. Only the small fecal loss (0.4 g/day) is replaced by new synthesis, so losing the ileum drains the pool faster than the liver can refill it. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Removing the gallbladder does not cause fat malabsorption.</b> The gallbladder only stores and concentrates bile. Afterward the liver secretes bile continuously, and it still recirculates.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-af1bd3ef5f.jpg\" alt=\"Bile runs from the liver down the common bile duct to the duodenum, with the gallbladder only a side reservoir. Bile salts come back from the terminal ileum through the hepatic portal vein (red line), so the loop works with or without a gallbladder, but not without the terminal ileum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile runs from the liver down the common bile duct to the duodenum, with the gallbladder only a side reservoir. Bile salts come back from the terminal ileum through the hepatic portal vein (red line), so the loop works with or without a gallbladder, but not without the terminal ileum. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: no weight loss after gallbladder removal</h4>\n<p>Fat absorption continues because bile <b>continuously recirculates without loss</b>. Bile is essential for fat absorption, fat is not absorbed in the colon, vitamins A, D, E and K have no active-transport backup, and lingual lipase does not increase.</p>\n<h4>Bacterial overgrowth</h4>\n<p>Conjugation keeps bile salts ionized and trapped in the lumen. Overgrown bacteria in the small bowel <b>deconjugate</b> them back into bile acids. At intestinal pH, bile acids are mostly un-ionized and lipid-soluble, so they diffuse into cells <b>too early</b>, in the upper intestine. Micelles then fail before fat absorption is finished.</p>","s":"Steatorrhea: when a step fails","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Celiac disease, tropical sprue, and the causes compared","p":"<h4>Lost surface: celiac disease and tropical sprue</h4>\n<p>Fat enters cells by diffusion, and diffusion depends on surface area. Both diseases <b>destroy the villi</b> of the small bowel: no villi, no absorption.</p>\n<p><b>Celiac disease</b>: immune reaction to gluten (its gliadin protein) in wheat, rye, barley and oats (oats mostly through wheat contamination). Tissue transglutaminase chemically modifies gliadin peptides; antigen-presenting cells show them to CD4 (helper) T cells, which inflame the mucosa. Villi are stripped flat from duodenum to terminal ileum → fat malabsorption, iron-deficiency anemia, bloating, gas, fatigue, weight loss; ↑ bowel-wall lymphoma. Blood tests: <b>anti-endomysial</b> and <b>anti-tissue transglutaminase</b> antibodies; HLA-DQ2/DQ8 (human leukocyte antigen) typing can rule it out. <b>Biopsy is most accurate</b>: blunted villi, intraepithelial lymphocytes, crypt hyperplasia. Treatment: stop gluten; it resolves in a few weeks. Steroids help but are not a real solution.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b77a382427.jpg\" alt=\"Small-bowel biopsies. Normal mucosa has tall, finger-like villi (left); in celiac disease the villi are flattened and the crypts below them are deep (right), so the absorptive surface is gone.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Small-bowel biopsies. Normal mucosa has tall, finger-like villi (left); in celiac disease the villi are flattened and the crypts below them are deep (right), so the absorptive surface is gone. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Tropical sprue</b>: chronic infection of the small intestine (bacterial, viral, amebic or parasitic) → fewer epithelial cells and villi → fat malabsorption, and especially folate and B₁₂ malabsorption. Found on <b>biopsy</b>; treated with <b>antibiotics</b>. Workup for any malabsorption: bone density, complete blood count, folate, B₁₂ and vitamin D levels.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: flat villi in a young man</h4>\n<p>A 23-year-old has a year of low-volume, oily, foul diarrhea; biopsy shows absent villi, many intraepithelial lymphocytes and hyperplastic crypts. This is celiac disease, so the useful therapy is a <b>gluten-free diet</b>. Antibiotics treat tropical sprue, steroids are no lasting fix, and vagotomy or duodenal resection has no role.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: diagnosing tropical sprue</h4>\n<p>The most specific test is <b>biopsy</b>. A fecal fat stain (Sudan black) proves steatorrhea of any cause. Hypocalcemia, low bone density and a prolonged prothrombin time reflect vitamin D and K loss, seen in any fat malabsorption.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: tropical sprue and low calcium</h4>\n<p>Low serum calcium in tropical sprue comes from impaired absorption of <b>lipids and fat-soluble vitamins</b>: less vitamin D means less calcium (Ca²⁺) absorption. The organisms do not consume calcium or make calcium-pump toxins, and the lymphoma risk belongs to celiac disease.</p>\n<h4>The causes side by side</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Step that fails</th><th>Clue</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pancreatic insufficiency; orlistat</td><td>no lipase</td><td>chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome</td><td>lipase inactivated (pH &lt; 5)</td><td>ulcers, ↑ gastrin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bile obstruction; ileal resection</td><td>too few bile salts</td><td>↑ bile acid synthesis; B₁₂ loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bacterial overgrowth</td><td>bile salts deconjugated</td><td>bile acids absorbed early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Celiac disease</td><td>villi flattened</td><td>gluten; antibodies; biopsy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tropical sprue</td><td>villi lost</td><td>infection; antibiotics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Abetalipoproteinemia</td><td>no chylomicrons</td><td>no apoB; fat inside enterocytes</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"Steatorrhea: when a step fails","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"No fat absorption, no ADEK absorption","p":"<p>Vitamins <b>A, D, E and K</b> (ADEK) are absorbed exactly like fat: into micelles, by diffusion into the cell, into chylomicrons, and out through lymph. So <b>no fat absorption, no ADEK absorption</b>. Celiac disease, tropical sprue, bile insufficiency, ileal resection or Crohn disease, duodenal acid (Zollinger–Ellison) and lipase loss all lower ADEK absorption. Water-soluble vitamins, B₁₂ included, are absorbed differently (Chapter 9). Pancreatic insufficiency lowers B₁₂ absorption too, by another route: pancreatic proteases must free B₁₂ from R-protein before intrinsic factor can bind it (Chapter 9).</p>\n<p>Deficiency signs follow each vitamin's job. <b>A</b>: night blindness, dry eyes and skin. <b>D</b>: ↓ Ca²⁺ absorption → hypocalcemia, low bone density (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults). <b>E</b>: hemolytic anemia, nerve damage. <b>K</b>: ↓ clotting factors II, VII, IX, X → prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and bleeding. Liver disease with poor bile flow also lowers vitamin D absorption (vitamin D activation: Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a7d5771b1b.jpg\" alt=\"Vitamin D from skin or diet is hydroxylated in the liver, then the kidney, to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃, which makes the calcium carrier calbindin in intestinal cells. When dietary vitamin D is lost with the fat, calcium absorption falls.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Vitamin D from skin or diet is hydroxylated in the liver, then the kidney, to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃, which makes the calcium carrier calbindin in intestinal cells. When dietary vitamin D is lost with the fat, calcium absorption falls. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which vitamin is not fat-soluble?</h4>\n<p><b>Vitamin B₁₂</b>. It is water-soluble and absorbed in the ileum with intrinsic factor, while A, D, E and K travel with fat.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p><b>Micelle vs chylomicron</b>: micelles (≈ 50 Å, bile-salt monolayer) work in the lumen and never enter the cell; chylomicrons (≈ 1,000 Å, phospholipid and apoprotein coat) are built inside the cell and leave by lymph. <b>Emulsion droplets</b> (bile salts coating fat, before lipase) are not micelles. <b>Secreted active</b>: lipase, cholesterol ester hydrolase. <b>Activated by trypsin</b>: procolipase, PLA₂. <b>Gallbladder removal</b> spares fat absorption; <b>ileal resection</b> does not.</p>","s":"Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Nine liters a day and where each nutrient is absorbed","p":"<p>This chapter follows water and salt through the gut, maps nutrient absorption, explains diarrhea, and ends with the liver.</p>\n<p>The gut handles about <b>9 L</b> of fluid a day, close to the body's entire extracellular fluid volume. In: food and drink 2 L; saliva 1–2 L; gastric juice 2 L; pancreatic juice 2–3 L; plus bile and intestinal secretions. Out: the small intestine absorbs about 90% of the water (mainly jejunum and ileum) and 80% of the electrolytes. Less than 1 L reaches the colon, the colon absorbs most of it, and only 100–200 mL leaves in feces.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5b18675c74.jpg\" alt=\"Left bar: what enters the gut each day (diet plus four secretions). Right bar: where it is absorbed. Only the thin hatched strip leaves in feces.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left bar: what enters the gut each day (diet plus four secretions). Right bar: where it is absorbed. Only the thin hatched strip leaves in feces. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Absorption moves <b>solute first, water second</b>: villus cells carry Na⁺ (sodium) and other solutes to the blood, and water follows by osmosis. So the absorbed fluid is <b>isosmotic</b> (same particle concentration as plasma), as in the kidney's proximal tubule. Small-intestine tight junctions are <b>leaky</b>, so water and Na⁺ also cross between the cells. Colon tight junctions are <b>tight</b>, so there salt and water cross only through the cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-09a7d8dabb.jpg\" alt=\"Villi of the small intestine in a false-colour scanning electron micrograph. This folded surface absorbs about 90% of the 9 L that enter the gut each day.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Villi of the small intestine in a false-colour scanning electron micrograph. This folded surface absorbs about 90% of the 9 L that enter the gut each day. <small>· Regional absorption, diarrhea &amp; liver lecture, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where each nutrient is absorbed</h4>\n<p><b>Duodenum</b>: most divalent cations (ions with two positive charges): iron (Fe²⁺), calcium (Ca²⁺) and magnesium (Mg²⁺); Ca²⁺ and iron are absorbed according to the body's needs. <b>Jejunum</b>: most nutrients (sugars, amino acids and small peptides, Chapter 7; most lipid by the mid-jejunum, Chapter 8); the major site of Na⁺ absorption. <b>Ileum</b>: vitamin B₁₂ (terminal ileum) and bile salts (Chapter 6). <b>Colon</b>: water and Na⁺; secretes K⁺ (potassium) and HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate); absorbs no nutrients.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9f76e4f236.png\" alt=\"The gut from duodenum to colon. Each box lists what that segment absorbs, so losing a segment predicts the deficiency.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,501px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The gut from duodenum to colon. Each box lists what that segment absorbs, so losing a segment predicts the deficiency. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Water and salt along the gut","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"The sodium pump, jejunum and ileum","p":"<p>Every absorbing cell runs on one engine: the basolateral (blood-side) <b>Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase</b> pumps Na⁺ out and keeps cell Na⁺ low. Na⁺ then flows in from the lumen down that gradient, and carriers couple other solutes to its flow. This is <b>secondary active transport</b>: the pump spends ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency), and the carrier spends the Na⁺ gradient the pump built.</p>\n<h4>Jejunum: NaHCO₃ absorbed</h4>\n<p>The jejunum is the <b>major site of Na⁺ absorption</b> and works like the early proximal tubule of the kidney. Na⁺ enters across the apical (lumen-side) membrane with sugars and amino acids, and in exchange for H⁺ (hydrogen ion) on the <b>Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger</b>. The H⁺ comes from CO₂ and H₂O, which carbonic anhydrase turns into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻. H⁺ goes out to the lumen, and HCO₃⁻ goes to the blood. Net: <b>NaHCO₃ absorption</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b6e4757c12.jpg\" alt=\"Jejunal cell. Na⁺ enters with sugar or amino acid (orange) and on the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger (purple). Carbonic anhydrase makes H⁺ and HCO₃⁻: H⁺ goes to the lumen, HCO₃⁻ to blood, so the net is NaHCO₃ absorbed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">Jejunal cell. Na⁺ enters with sugar or amino acid (orange) and on the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger (purple). Carbonic anhydrase makes H⁺ and HCO₃⁻: H⁺ goes to the lumen, HCO₃⁻ to blood, so the net is NaHCO₃ absorbed. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 386</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Ileum: NaCl absorbed</h4>\n<p>The ileum has the same carriers plus an apical <b>Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger</b>, and its basolateral membrane passes Cl⁻ (chloride) instead of HCO₃⁻. Now both H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ leave into the lumen, while Na⁺ and Cl⁻ both enter. So the ileum absorbs <b>NaCl</b>, with <b>no net HCO₃⁻ absorption</b>, like the late proximal tubule. It also absorbs B₁₂ and bile salts.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9f43a9dd63.jpg\" alt=\"Ileal cell. The same carriers plus an apical Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue): HCO₃⁻ now goes out to the lumen and Cl⁻ comes in and exits to blood, so the net is NaCl absorbed and no HCO₃⁻.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">Ileal cell. The same carriers plus an apical Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue): HCO₃⁻ now goes out to the lumen and Cl⁻ comes in and exits to blood, so the net is NaCl absorbed and no HCO₃⁻. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-28c5e70d0f.png\" alt=\"Four epithelial cells, lumen on the left, blood on the right. Follow HCO₃⁻: the jejunum sends it to blood, the ileum trades it for Cl⁻. The colon swaps Na⁺ for K⁺; the crypt cell secretes Cl⁻ through CFTR. AA, amino acids; CA, carbonic anhydrase; R, receptor; AC, adenylyl cyclase.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Four epithelial cells, lumen on the left, blood on the right. Follow HCO₃⁻: the jejunum sends it to blood, the ileum trades it for Cl⁻. The colon swaps Na⁺ for K⁺; the crypt cell secretes Cl⁻ through CFTR. AA, amino acids; CA, carbonic anhydrase; R, receptor; AC, adenylyl cyclase. <small>· Graph redrawn from Costanzo Figs. 8.34A-B (jejunum, ileum), 8.35 (colon), 8.36 (crypt Cl- secretion); same figures on the 2026 digestion-and-absorption discussion slides 21, 42, 43</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Water and salt along the gut","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"The colon and the crypts","p":"<h4>Colon: Na⁺ in, K⁺ out, set by aldosterone</h4>\n<p>The colon works like the <b>principal cells</b> of the kidney's late distal tubule and collecting duct. Stool enters it watery; the colon absorbs Na⁺ and water but no nutrients. Its apical membrane has Na⁺ channels and K⁺ channels. <b>Aldosterone</b> induces synthesis of the Na⁺ channels. More Na⁺ enters, more is pumped out by the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, more K⁺ is pumped in, and more K⁺ leaks out to the lumen. So aldosterone raises Na⁺ absorption and, secondarily, <b>K⁺ secretion</b>. The colon also secretes HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<p>Colonic K⁺ secretion is also <b>flow-rate dependent</b>. Normally, secreted K⁺ builds up in the lumen and shrinks the gradient for more K⁺ to leave. Fast flow, as in diarrhea, flushes it away, keeps luminal K⁺ low, and lets K⁺ keep pouring out. That is why diarrhea causes <b>hypokalemia</b> (low blood K⁺).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b816944a0c.jpg\" alt=\"Left, the colon cell: aldosterone adds apical Na⁺ channels, and K⁺ leaves through apical K⁺ channels, faster at high flow (hence hypokalemia). Right, the kidney&#x27;s principal cell, which aldosterone runs the same way.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Left, the colon cell: aldosterone adds apical Na⁺ channels, and K⁺ leaves through apical K⁺ channels, faster at high flow (hence hypokalemia). Right, the kidney's principal cell, which aldosterone runs the same way. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Crypts: Cl⁻ secretion through CFTR</h4>\n<p>Crypt cells secrete. Their basolateral membrane has a <b>Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter</b>, like the one in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to drag Cl⁻ into the cell against its own gradient, another case of secondary active transport. Cl⁻ then exits to the lumen through apical <b>CFTR</b> Cl⁻ channels (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator; Chapter 6). Na⁺ follows Cl⁻ between the cells, and water follows the NaCl. CFTR is normally closed. <b>VIP</b> (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and <b>ACh</b> (acetylcholine) bind basolateral receptors → Gs (stimulatory G protein) → adenylyl cyclase → <b>cAMP</b> (cyclic AMP) → CFTR opens. So VIP increases secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3726dabd40.jpg\" alt=\"Crypt cell. The basolateral Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter loads Cl⁻; a hormone or neurotransmitter such as VIP acts on its receptor (R) → Gs → adenylyl cyclase (AC) → cAMP, which opens the apical Cl⁻ channel. Na⁺ and water follow between the cells. Circled steps 1–4 are cholera toxin locking this switch on (Diarrhea, below).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Crypt cell. The basolateral Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter loads Cl⁻; a hormone or neurotransmitter such as VIP acts on its receptor (R) → Gs → adenylyl cyclase (AC) → cAMP, which opens the apical Cl⁻ channel. Na⁺ and water follow between the cells. Circled steps 1–4 are cholera toxin locking this switch on (Diarrhea, below). <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: where does Cl⁻ enter cells by secondary active transport?</h4>\n<p>At the <b>basolateral membrane of crypt cells</b>, on the Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter. The thick ascending limb has the same kind of carrier \"flipped\" to its luminal membrane, where furosemide blocks it. Jejunal and colonic apical membranes lack it; pancreatic acinar cells carry it basolaterally.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3f338dc957.jpg\" alt=\"For comparison, a thick ascending limb cell: the same Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ carrier, but on the luminal side, where furosemide blocks it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">For comparison, a thick ascending limb cell: the same Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ carrier, but on the luminal side, where furosemide blocks it. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Water and salt along the gut","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Volume loss, acidosis and low K⁺","p":"<p>Diarrhea means the intestine fails to finish absorbing the fluid it receives.</p>\n<h4>What severe diarrhea does to the body</h4>\n<p><b>Volume</b>: stool carries extracellular-type fluid, so ECF (extracellular fluid) volume, blood volume and <b>blood pressure</b> fall. Baroreceptors and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system fight back but fail if the loss is too large or fast. <b>Acid–base</b>: saliva, pancreatic juice and intestinal juice are rich in HCO₃⁻, which is normally reabsorbed farther down the gut. In profuse diarrhea, fast transit carries it out in stool, and the pancreas and mucosa secrete still more. Losing base without losing acid causes <b>metabolic acidosis</b> (blood pH &lt; 7.35). As HCO₃⁻ falls, the kidney keeps Cl⁻ while saving salt and water, so blood Cl⁻ rises: <b>hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis</b>.</p>\n<p>That acidosis has a <b>normal anion gap</b>. In words, the anion gap is plasma Na⁺ minus the two main measured anions; in symbols, gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻). It estimates unmeasured anions, such as lactate. Diarrhea adds no new acid anion: each lost HCO₃⁻ is replaced by a Cl⁻, so Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻, and the gap, stay normal. <b>K⁺</b>: flow-dependent colonic secretion, boosted by aldosterone released in volume loss, empties K⁺ into stool. The rule: <b>diarrhea = acidosis + low K⁺</b>. Vomiting, which loses gastric H⁺, causes alkalosis instead.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: laxative abuse</h4>\n<p>A 17-year-old who abuses laxatives has acidosis and high blood Cl⁻. Mechanism: <b>increased loss of HCO₃⁻ and K⁺</b> in stool. Not \"like cholera\": no toxin, no cAMP surge, smaller volume. H⁺–peptide cotransport (Chapter 7) is irrelevant; acid made to replace gastric fluid describes vomiting.</p>","s":"Diarrhea","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Cholera and secretory diarrhea","p":"<h4>Secretory diarrhea: cholera</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-50738a9038.png\" alt=\"Cholera toxin hijacks the normal VIP/ACh switch and locks it on. Read down: one stuck G protein ends in volume loss, acidosis and hypokalemia.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,489px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Cholera toxin hijacks the normal VIP/ACh switch and locks it on. Read down: one stuck G protein ends in volume loss, acidosis and hypokalemia. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Secretory diarrhea is too much <b>secretion</b> by crypt cells, usually from bacterial toxins (<i>Vibrio cholerae</i>, some <i>Escherichia coli</i>). Cholera toxin enters the crypt cell across the apical membrane. Its A subunit moves to the basolateral membrane and <b>ADP-ribosylates</b> the αs subunit of Gs: it attaches an ADP-ribose (adenosine diphosphate–ribose) tag. This blocks the subunit's own GTPase, so its GTP (guanosine triphosphate, the \"on\" signal) is never split and Gs never switches off. <b>Adenylyl cyclase</b> stays on, cAMP stays high, and CFTR stays open. Cl⁻ pours out, Na⁺ and water follow, and secretion overwhelms the villi and colon: massive diarrhea.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-fd852562e3.jpg\" alt=\"Villi absorb; crypts (arrow), the pits between them, secrete. Cholera toxin acts on the crypt cells, and their secretion outruns what the villi and colon can absorb.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Villi absorb; crypts (arrow), the pits between them, secrete. Cholera toxin acts on the crypt cells, and their secretion outruns what the villi and colon can absorb. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: cholera</h4>\n<p>Watery diarrhea from <i>V. cholerae</i>: the toxin causes <b>adenylyl cyclase activation</b>, not inhibition, and it opens Cl⁻ channels rather than closing them. A toxin that irreversibly activates adenylyl cyclase in crypt cells causes <b>secretory diarrhea</b>. The tempting \"osmotic diuresis\" is a kidney term: extra urine driven by a filtered solute such as mannitol.</p>\n<p><b>Cystic fibrosis and cholera</b>: cystic fibrosis (CF) is loss of CFTR, the very channel cholera toxin forces open (Chapter 6). People who carry one CF allele have fewer working channels and lose less fluid in cholera, the classic explanation for why the CF allele is common.</p>","s":"Diarrhea","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Osmotic diarrhea and oral rehydration","p":"<h4>Osmotic diarrhea</h4>\n<p>Osmotic diarrhea comes from a <b>nonabsorbable solute</b> in the lumen that holds water there by osmosis. Classic case: <b>lactase deficiency</b>. Lactose is not split into glucose and galactose (Chapter 7), so it cannot be absorbed anywhere. It stays in the lumen and retains water. Colon bacteria then break it into more, smaller, osmotically active particles plus hydrogen and methane gas, which adds <b>bloating</b>, cramps and more diarrhea.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ab47e99e5a.png\" alt=\"Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,511px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Two tests separate them. <b>Fasting</b>: osmotic diarrhea stops (the solute came from food); secretory diarrhea continues (the toxin keeps cAMP high). <b>Stool osmotic gap</b>: stool osmolality is about 290 mOsm/kg (milliosmoles of dissolved particles per kilogram of water), the same as plasma. Na⁺ and K⁺, doubled to count their partner anions, should explain it. In symbols, gap = 290 − 2 × (stool Na⁺ + K⁺). Secretory stool is salt water, so the gap is small (&lt; 50). Osmotic stool holds an unmeasured solute such as lactose, so the gap is large (&gt; 125).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Secretory</th><th>Osmotic</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>crypt Cl⁻ secretion ↑ (cholera toxin, VIP)</td><td>unabsorbed solute (lactose)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Gs → cAMP ↑ → CFTR open</td><td>solute holds water in lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fasting</td><td>continues</td><td>stops</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stool osmotic gap</td><td>small (&lt; 50)</td><td>large (&gt; 125)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Oral rehydration</h4>\n<p><b>Oral rehydration solution</b> (glucose, Na⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ in water) is the major advance in treating diarrhea worldwide. It works even in cholera because absorption and secretion use different machinery. The toxin turns on crypt CFTR, but the villus <b>Na⁺–glucose cotransporter</b> (SGLT1, Chapter 7) is not run by cAMP and keeps working. Glucose drags Na⁺ in, water follows the absorbed solute, and the HCO₃⁻ offsets the acidosis. Absorption can then match the secretion.</p>\n<p>Toxins that raise cAMP or cGMP (cyclic GMP), such as cholera toxin and the <i>E. coli</i> enterotoxins, also shut down the villus cells' electroneutral NaCl absorption (the paired Na⁺/H⁺ and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchangers), but they leave SGLT1 working.</p>","s":"Diarrhea","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Water-soluble vitamins and the B₁₂ pathway","p":"<p>The water-soluble vitamins are B₁, B₂, B₆, B₁₂, C, biotin, folic acid, nicotinic acid and pantothenic acid. Most are absorbed in the small intestine by <b>Na⁺-dependent cotransport</b> or passively. (Vitamins A, D, E and K travel with fat, Chapter 8.)</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0a8d6db112.png\" alt=\"B₁₂ changes partners twice before the terminal ileum absorbs it. The dashed boxes show where each disease breaks the chain.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,475px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">B₁₂ changes partners twice before the terminal ileum absorbs it. The dashed boxes show where each disease breaks the chain. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>B₁₂</b> (cobalamin) is the exception and needs four steps. (1) In the stomach, <b>pepsin</b> and acid free B₁₂ from food protein. (2) Free B₁₂ binds <b>R-protein</b> (R-binder, haptocorrin), made in saliva. (3) In the duodenum, <b>pancreatic proteases</b> digest the R-protein, and B₁₂ moves onto <b>intrinsic factor</b> (IF), a glycoprotein from gastric parietal cells (Chapter 5). (4) The B₁₂–IF complex resists the proteases and travels to the <b>terminal ileum</b>, which has a specific uptake mechanism for it. Every step is a failure point: no IF, no pancreatic proteases, or no terminal ileum.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-06793ddf83.jpg\" alt=\"B₁₂ (blue) is freed from food in the stomach and rides R-protein (pink). In the duodenum it moves onto intrinsic factor (IF, yellow) from parietal cells, and only the ileal mucosal cell takes up the IF–B₁₂ complex.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">B₁₂ (blue) is freed from food in the stomach and rides R-protein (pink). In the duodenum it moves onto intrinsic factor (IF, yellow) from parietal cells, and only the ileal mucosal cell takes up the IF–B₁₂ complex. <small>· Water-soluble vitamins lecture (biochemistry), slide 49</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Vitamins, calcium and iron","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"B₁₂ deficiency: anemia, gastrectomy and ileal resection","p":"<p>Dividing erythroblasts (red cell precursors) need B₁₂ and folate to make DNA. Without them, purine and thymidine synthesis fails, many erythroblasts die in the marrow, and the survivors become oversized red cells: <b>megaloblastic anemia</b>. B₁₂ also maintains nerve myelin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a92e1a7f8a.jpg\" alt=\"Anemias sorted by red-cell size (MCV, mean cell volume). B₁₂ or folate deficiency gives large (macrocytic, megaloblastic) cells; iron deficiency gives small (microcytic) ones.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Anemias sorted by red-cell size (MCV, mean cell volume). B₁₂ or folate deficiency gives large (macrocytic, megaloblastic) cells; iron deficiency gives small (microcytic) ones. <small>· Water-soluble vitamins lecture (biochemistry), slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Pernicious anemia</b> is autoimmune loss of IF: CD4 (helper) T cells attack the parietal cell's H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, or antibodies target IF itself. Atrophic gastritis also cuts IF. Removing the stomach or the terminal ileum gives the same anemia, often also called pernicious anemia; a vegan diet lacking B₁₂ causes it with normal IF.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b3cb6cc7fe.jpg\" alt=\"Pernicious anemia. Parietal cells make intrinsic factor and acid (H⁺/K⁺ ATPase). CD4 T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase attack them, so no intrinsic factor binds B₁₂ and the distal ileum takes none up.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Pernicious anemia. Parietal cells make intrinsic factor and acid (H⁺/K⁺ ATPase). CD4 T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase attack them, so no intrinsic factor binds B₁₂ and the distal ileum takes none up. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: what is B₁₂ essential for?</h4>\n<p>The <b>maturation of red blood cells</b>; deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia, with red cells larger than normal. White-cell formation and the absorption of B₆ or folic acid are not its defining job.</p>\n<h4>Gastrectomy and ileal resection</h4>\n<p>After <b>gastrectomy</b>: no parietal cells → no IF → no B₁₂ absorption → eventual B₁₂ deficiency. Give B₁₂ by <b>injection</b>; oral B₁₂ in ordinary doses is not absorbed without IF. Gastrectomy can also cause <b>dumping syndrome</b>: food rushes into the small intestine, causing cramping and diarrhea. After <b>ileal resection</b>, IF is present but the uptake site is gone, so B₁₂ must again be injected; bile salts are lost too, causing steatorrhea (worked example in Chapter 6).</p>\n<h4>Worked example: total gastrectomy for cancer</h4>\n<p>A 60-year-old has his whole stomach removed. Expect <b>pernicious anemia</b> (B₁₂ deficiency). Not steatorrhea: pancreatic lipase and bile still digest fat. Not acute pancreatitis. Not fecal carbohydrate loss: pancreatic amylase and the brush-border enzymes still work.</p>","s":"Vitamins, calcium and iron","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Calcium and iron","p":"<p><b>Calcium</b> absorption needs active vitamin D, <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃). Sunlight converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin to cholecalciferol (vitamin D₃); diet supplies D₃ (fish, meat) and D₂ (supplements); all inactive. The <b>liver</b> hydroxylates it to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol, still inactive but the main circulating form. The <b>kidney</b> proximal tubule adds the second hydroxyl with <b>1α-hydroxylase</b>, making the active hormone. In the gut it induces <b>calbindin</b> (calbindin D-28K), a Ca²⁺-binding protein in intestinal cells, so more Ca²⁺ is absorbed. Deficiency, or failed activation (chronic renal failure), means poor Ca²⁺ absorption: <b>rickets</b> in children, <b>osteomalacia</b> in adults. Fat malabsorption also lowers vitamin D absorption (Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-612ea5995c.jpg\" alt=\"Rickets: a child&#x27;s X-ray with soft, bowed leg bones, from too little vitamin D and so too little Ca²⁺ absorbed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,319px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Rickets: a child's X-ray with soft, bowed leg bones, from too little vitamin D and so too little Ca²⁺ absorbed. <small>· Fat-soluble vitamins lecture (biochemistry), slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: hepatitis A</h4>\n<p>A 10-year-old has jaundice from hepatitis A (a fecal–oral virus). Expect <b>hypocalcemia</b>. The inflamed liver hydroxylates less vitamin D, and <b>cholestasis</b> (reduced or stopped bile flow) leaves less bile to absorb this fat-soluble vitamin. Less active vitamin D → less calbindin → less Ca²⁺ absorbed; vitamin D deficiency is also common in chronic hepatitis B and C. Hyperkalemia (impaired hepatic K⁺ uptake), hyponatremia and failing glucose output belong to advanced cirrhosis; protein absorption does not need the liver.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d084fa7dbe.jpg\" alt=\"Vitamin D needs two hydroxylations, first in the liver, then in the kidney, before it can induce calbindin in intestinal cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Vitamin D needs two hydroxylations, first in the liver, then in the kidney, before it can induce calbindin in intestinal cells. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-27055f664f.jpg\" alt=\"Nonheme iron (left) is reduced to Fe²⁺ before entry; heme iron (right) enters whole. Both leave the cell as Fe²⁺ and ride transferrin as Fe³⁺.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,483px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Nonheme iron (left) is reduced to Fe²⁺ before entry; heme iron (right) enters whole. Both leave the cell as Fe²⁺ and ride transferrin as Fe³⁺. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Iron</b> is eaten mostly as ferric <b>Fe³⁺</b> but absorbed as ferrous <b>Fe²⁺</b>, in the duodenum. On the brush border, Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺, and DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) carries it in. <b>Heme iron</b> (hemoglobin and myoglobin in meat) is easier to absorb: whole heme enters (via HCP1, heme carrier protein 1), and heme oxygenase frees Fe²⁺ inside the cell. Inside, Fe²⁺ binds <b>apoferritin</b> (forming ferritin, the storage form) or exits through ferroportin. Hephaestin re-oxidizes it to Fe³⁺ for <b>transferrin</b>, a β-globulin that carries iron to liver stores, then to bone marrow for hemoglobin. Duodenal damage, as in celiac disease (Chapter 8), causes iron-deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>Stomach acid helps keep dietary iron soluble and in the ferrous Fe²⁺ form, so removing the stomach also cuts iron absorption: after gastrectomy, iron-deficiency anemia can join B₁₂ deficiency.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6927c6af8a.jpg\" alt=\"Whole-body iron. Only 1–2 mg a day is absorbed and lost; plasma transferrin (Tf) holds just 3–4 mg, most of it headed for the marrow&#x27;s new red cells, and the liver stores the excess as ferritin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Whole-body iron. Only 1–2 mg a day is absorbed and lost; plasma transferrin (Tf) holds just 3–4 mg, most of it headed for the marrow's new red cells, and the liver stores the excess as ferritin. <small>· Iron metabolism lecture (biochemistry), slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Vitamins, calcium and iron","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Bilirubin: from old red cells to feces","p":"<p>Most liver blood is portal blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas and spleen, so the liver sees every absorbed nutrient, drug and toxin first.</p>\n<h4>Bilirubin: formation, conjugation, excretion</h4>\n<p>Macrophages of the <b>reticuloendothelial system</b> (RES) break down old red cells: hemoglobin → heme → <b>biliverdin</b> (green) → <b>bilirubin</b> (yellow). This unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble, so it rides on <b>albumin</b> to the liver. Hepatocytes conjugate it with glucuronic acid using <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (UDP, uridine diphosphate). <b>Conjugated bilirubin</b> is water-soluble: a small part enters urine, and the rest goes into bile, where bile pigments give body fluids their color. In the terminal ileum and colon, bacteria deconjugate it and make <b>urobilinogen</b>. Some returns to the liver (enterohepatic circulation); the rest becomes <b>urobilin</b> and <b>stercobilin</b>, excreted in feces.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-85027eb3cd.png\" alt=\"Bilirubin&#x27;s path from old red cells to feces. The dashed boxes mark where each kind of jaundice starts, and which bilirubin rises.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,513px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bilirubin's path from old red cells to feces. The dashed boxes mark where each kind of jaundice starts, and which bilirubin rises. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-192f76d23a.jpg\" alt=\"The course figure. Bilirubin rides albumin to the liver, is conjugated by UDP-glucuronyl transferase, and goes into bile; bacteria make urobilinogen in the terminal ileum (some returns to the liver), then urobilin and stercobilin in the colon.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The course figure. Bilirubin rides albumin to the liver, is conjugated by UDP-glucuronyl transferase, and goes into bile; bacteria make urobilinogen in the terminal ileum (some returns to the liver), then urobilin and stercobilin in the colon. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-78c7725616.jpg\" alt=\"The same path drawn in the body, in ten numbered steps. Follow the yellow line: B, unconjugated bilirubin; CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen (part returns to the liver, part goes to the kidney as yellow urobilin); S, the brown stercobilin of stool.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The same path drawn in the body, in ten numbered steps. Follow the yellow line: B, unconjugated bilirubin; CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen (part returns to the liver, part goes to the kidney as yellow urobilin); S, the brown stercobilin of stool. <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The liver","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Jaundice: which bilirubin rises","p":"<p><b>Jaundice</b> is yellow skin and sclera from excess bilirubin; ask which kind. Too much made (<b>hemolytic</b>, prehepatic jaundice) → <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises, because conjugation cannot keep up. Example: hemolytic disease of the newborn, where maternal antibodies against fetal ABO or Rh antigens cross the placenta and destroy fetal red cells. <b>Physiologic newborn jaundice</b> (day 2 or 3) is also unconjugated: UDP-glucuronyl transferase is made slowly after birth.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-79ee8efea8.jpg\" alt=\"Scleral icterus: the whites of the eyes turn yellow, often the first visible sign of excess bilirubin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Scleral icterus: the whites of the eyes turn yellow, often the first visible sign of excess bilirubin. <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Unconjugated bilirubin stays bound to albumin, so the kidney cannot filter it: hemolytic jaundice puts no bilirubin in the urine (only urine urobilinogen rises). Chronic hemolysis, as in sickle cell disease, also pours extra bilirubin into bile, where it can precipitate with calcium as pigment gallstones.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b929ce0932.jpg\" alt=\"Top (A): hemolysis makes more bilirubin (B) than the liver can conjugate, so unconjugated bilirubin rises. Bottom (B): in the newborn, too little UDP-glucuronosyl transferase does the same. CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen; S, stercobilin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,286px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top (A): hemolysis makes more bilirubin (B) than the liver can conjugate, so unconjugated bilirubin rises. Bottom (B): in the newborn, too little UDP-glucuronosyl transferase does the same. CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen; S, stercobilin. <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3833fdf05a.jpg\" alt=\"Newborn jaundice: yellow skin (right) beside a healthy baby (left).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Newborn jaundice: yellow skin (right) beside a healthy baby (left). <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Blocked outflow (<b>bile duct obstruction</b>: gallstones, tumors) or liver disease (hepatitis) → <b>conjugated</b> bilirubin cannot enter bile and backs up into blood. Being water-soluble, it darkens the urine; with little stercobilin, the stool turns pale (\"clay-colored\").</p>\n<h4>Worked example: jaundice with conjugated bilirubin</h4>\n<p>The cause is <b>common bile duct obstruction</b>: the liver conjugates normally but cannot excrete. Hemolytic and physiologic jaundice raise unconjugated bilirubin, so \"all of the above\" is wrong.</p>","s":"The liver","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Liver functions and detoxification","p":"<h4>The liver's other jobs</h4>\n<p><b>Bile acids</b>: made from cholesterol, conjugated to form bile salts (Chapter 6). <b>Protein</b>: nonessential amino acids; <b>albumin</b> (liver failure → low albumin → edema from low plasma oncotic pressure); all <b>clotting factors</b> except factor VIII and von Willebrand factor, so the <b>prothrombin time</b> (PT) is an essential liver function test. <b>Carbohydrate</b>: gluconeogenesis, glycogen storage, glucose release. <b>Lipid</b>: fatty acid oxidation; synthesis of lipoproteins, cholesterol and phospholipids. <b>Detoxification and drug excretion</b>: the <b>cytochrome P450</b> system runs phase I reactions; phase II conjugation (glucuronide, sulfate, amino acids, glutathione) then makes drugs and toxins water-soluble for bile or urine. Kupffer cells eat bacteria arriving from the colon.</p>\n<p>Because portal blood reaches the liver before the rest of the body, absorbed drugs and toxins are modified there first (first-pass metabolism), so little of many of them reaches the systemic circulation.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-51321ef7e1.jpg\" alt=\"Splanchnic circulation. Blood from the stomach, spleen, pancreas, small intestine and colon collects in the portal vein and passes through the liver before the hepatic veins, vena cava and heart: the route behind first-pass metabolism.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Splanchnic circulation. Blood from the stomach, spleen, pancreas, small intestine and colon collects in the portal vein and passes through the liver before the hepatic veins, vena cava and heart: the route behind first-pass metabolism. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Jejunum absorbs <b>NaHCO₃</b>, ileum <b>NaCl</b>; colon secretes K⁺ and HCO₃⁻. R-protein is salivary; IF is from parietal cells. Diarrhea → <b>acidosis</b>, vomiting → alkalosis. Osmotic <i>diuresis</i> is renal; osmotic <i>diarrhea</i> is intestinal. Hemolysis and newborn jaundice → <b>unconjugated</b>; obstruction → <b>conjugated</b>. Crypt Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ is basolateral; the kidney's is luminal.</p>","s":"The liver","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"}]};